window.ECON={"chapters":[{"id":"1","title":"Introduction","intro":"Macroeconomics is defined as that branch of economics which studies economic issues or economic problems at the level of an economy as a whole.","lessons":[{"title":"Meaning, origin and principal concerns","body":"Macroeconomics is defined as that branch of economics which studies economic issues or economic problems at the level of an economy as a whole. The term Macro comes from Makros, meaning large. Keynes’ The General Theory of Employment, Interest and Money (1936) established macroeconomics as a separate branch. Its principal concerns are employment, inflation, growth of output and depression. Keynes stressed government intervention when inadequate demand reduces output and employment.","formula":"","source":"IMG_0499","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"1.1 ABOUT MACROECONOMICS"},{"title":"Microeconomics versus macroeconomics","body":"Microeconomics studies scarcity and choice at the level of an individual, household, firm or industry; allocation of resources is its central issue. Macroeconomics studies these problems for the economy as a whole; determination of overall output and employment is its central issue. Micro uses partial equilibrium analysis, studying one market while holding other markets constant. Macro uses general equilibrium analysis, examining simultaneous equilibrium across markets. An industry is an aggregate, but studying one industry is still microeconomics.","formula":"","source":"IMG_0500","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"1.2 HOW MACROECONOMICS DIFFERS FROM MICROECONOMICS?"},{"title":"Micro–macro paradox and interdependence","body":"What is logical at the micro level may not be logical at the macro level. Saving may improve one person’s future prosperity; if everyone saves more by spending less, aggregate demand, investment, output and employment may fall. Macro affects micro when an RBI interest-rate change affects one producer’s borrowing. Micro affects macro when individual increases in spending together raise aggregate demand.","formula":"","source":"IMG_0500","kind":"Purple-box concept","textbookHeading":"Micro-Macro Paradox · Micro-Macro Interdependence"},{"title":"Scope and significance","body":"Macroeconomics studies national income, employment, general prices, growth, money, banking, government budgets and international transactions. It helps explain an economy’s functioning and formulate policies for growth with stability. Keep the level of analysis clear: the price of one product is micro; the general price level is macro.","formula":"","source":"IMG_0501","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"1.3 SCOPE AND SIGNIFICANCE OF MACROECONOMICS"}],"pages":[{"id":"IMG_0499","image":"pages/IMG_0499.jpg","text":"Introduction\n1\n86888 88888\nChapter\nSection\n1.1\nSection Title\nAbout Macroeconomics\nLAYOUT OF THE CHAPTER\nBasic Concepts Explained under each Section\n- Origin of the Term\n-Emergence of Macroeconomics\n-Meaning of Macroeconomics and its Principal Concerns\n-Microeconomics vs. Macroeconomics\n-Micro-Macro Paradox\n-Micro-Macro Interdependence\n-Area of Study (Scope) of Macroeconomics\n-Significance of Macroeconomics\nPg. No.\n3-4\n1.2\nHow Macroeconomics Differs\nfrom Microeconomics?\n1.3\nScope and Significance of\nMacroeconomics\nAbility Zone\nPower Points [Summary of the Chapter]\nWORK-BOOK [Question-answer Format]\nSelf Assessment Test\nDigital Library\n4-6\n6-8\n8-9\n9\n10-19\n20-22\n22\n1.1 ABOUT MACROECONOMICS\n• Origin of the Term: The term Macro in English has its origin in the Greek term Makros which\nmeans Large. In the context of macroeconomics, 'large' means economy as a whole.\n• Emergence of Macroeconomics\n• Macroeconomics emerged as a separate branch of economics in 1936 with the publication of\na celebrated book titled \"The General Theory of Employment, Interest and Money\" by John\nMaynard Keynes.\n• The book emphasised the role of the government when the economy is in a state of depression\n(when the level of output and employment tends to fall owing to lack of demand in the economy).\n• Keynes discarded the long-held belief that the market forces of supply and demand would\nautomatically tackle the problem of unemployment and low level of output.\n• Meaning of Macroeconomics: Macroeconomics is defined as that branch of economics which\nstudies economic issues or economic problems at the level of an economy as a whole.","file":"macro - 1 + 2/IMG_0499.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0500","image":"pages/IMG_0500.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\n• Principal Concerns of Macroeconomics\n• Employment for the residents,\n• The problem of price rise (inflation),\n• Growth of output in the economy,\n• The problem of depression.\n! CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS\nIn the words of M.H. spencer, Macroeconomics Is concerned with the economy as a whole or large\nthe rate of inflation, the nation's total output and other matters of economy-wide significance!\"\n1.2 HOW MACROECONOMICS DIFFERS FROM MICROECONOMICS?\nYou have already studied microeconomics at +1 level. Now that you have understood the meaning of\nmacroeconomics, you can draw a distinction between micro and macro economics, as under:\nTable 1.1: Microeconomics vs. Macroeconomics\nBasis of Difference\nMicroeconomics\nMacroeconomics\n1. Concept\n- Microeconomics\nstudies\nproblems\n- Macroeconomics studies\nproblems\nscarcity and choice\nat the level of an\nscarcity and choice at the level of an\nindividual, a household, a firm or an industry.\neconomy as a whole.\n2. Central Issue\n- Allocation of resources is the central issue\n- Determination of the overall level of output\nin microeconomics.\n(and employment) is the central issue in\n- It relates to:\nmacroeconomics.\n(i) maximisation of consumer's satisfaction\n- It relates to choice of such policies/strategies\nwhen he allocates his means (income)\nby the government and its institutions so\nto the purchase of different goods and\nthat economic growth is maximised along\nservices, and\nwith economic stability\n(ii) maximisation of profit by the producer\nwhen he allocates his resources to the\nproduction of different goods and services.\n3. Economic\n- Microeconomics\nuses\nmicroeconomic\n- Macroeconomics\nuses\nmacroeconomic\nVariables\nvariables such as\nvariables such as\n• consumer's demand or\n• aggregate demand (referring to demand for\n• producer's supply.\nall the goods and services in the economy),\nand\n• aggregate supply (referring to supply of all\nthe goods and services in the economy).\n4. Aggregation of\n- In microeconomics, there is a limited degree\n- In macroeconomics, there is a wider degree\nEconomic Units\nof aggregation of economic units.\nof aggregation of economic units.\n- Illustration:\nMicroeconomics\nstudies\n- This is because\nstudies\neconomic\nequilibrium of an industry; it is\nproblems/issues at the level of an economy\naggregation of all the firms producing a\nas a whole which is an aggregation of all\nparticular commodity.\neconomic units in the economy.\n5. Economic Agents\n- Here,\neconomic\ndecisions\nare\ntaken\n- Here, economic decisions are taken by\n[Individuals and\nlargely by individual economic agents' like\nthe 'institutional economic agents' like RBI\ninstitutions who\nconsumers and producers.\n(Reserve Bank of India), SEBI (Securities &\ntake economic\n- They focus on maximisation of personal\ndecisions]\nExchange Board of India) and TRAI (Telecom\ngains.\nRegulatory Authority of India).\n- They focus on growth with stability.\n1. Economic stability - implies the absence of deep fluctuations in the level of economic activity in the economy.\nIntroduction\n- Method of study in microeconomics is often - Method of study in macroeconomics is often\ndescribed as 'partial equilibrium analysis'.\ndescribed as 'general equilibrium analysis'.\nmicroeconomics,\ntotal output\nand\n- In macroeconomics, distribution of output/\nemployment are taken as constant while these\nincome is taken as constant, while it is an\nare important variables in macroeconomics.\nimportant variable in microeconomics.\n6. Method of Study\n7. Assumptions\nof Study\n2 AVLR 1.1\nMICROECONOMICS vs. MACROECONOMICS\nCRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS\nMicro-Macro Paradox\nWhat is logical at the micro level may not be logical at the macro level.\nIllustration\nSaving - A virtue at micro level\n• If an individual saves more, he adds to his future prosperity. This makes saving a virtue at micro level.\nSaving -A vice at macro level\n• If all the people in an economy save more (and spend less), demand for goods and services may\ndecline. Consequently, investment may decline; production and employment level may fall.\n• The economy will be driven towards future poverty rather than prosperity. This makes saving a vice at\nthe macro level.\nMicro-Macro Interdependence\n• Macro impacts Micro: For instance, increase in interest rate by the RBI (and consequently by the\ncommercial banks) will lower borrowings for investment by the individual producers.\n• Micro impacts Macro: For instance, increase in spending at the individual level (which is a\nmicroeconomic factor) will lead to a rise in AD (aggregate demand) in the economy (which is a\nmacroeconomic factor). Increase in AD induces higher investment, leading to a rise in the level of\noutput and employment in the economy (again the macroeconomic factors).\nAVLR 1.2\nMICRO-MACRO PARADOX\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\nQ. 1. Distinguish between partial equilibrium and general equilibrium.\nAns.\n(i) Partial equilibrium refers to equilibrium in one market (say, commodity market) on the\nassumption that there is no change in other markets (like labour market or capital market).\nIt is the method of study in microeconomics.\n(ii) General equilibrium refers to simultaneous equilibrium in all the markets in the economy.\nIt is the method of study in macroeconomics.","file":"macro - 1 + 2/IMG_0500.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0501","image":"pages/IMG_0501.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\nQ. 2. Categorise the following as microeconomic study or macroeconomic study:\n(i) Output produced by a sugar mill\n(ii) Inequalities of income in the economy\n(iv) Cement industry\n(i) General price level\n(vi) Allocation of resources\n(v) Aggregate demand\n(vili) Growth of resources in an economy\n(vii) Inflation in the economy\n(x) Price determination of gold jewellery (x) Consumption expenditure by a household\nAns. Microeconomic Study: (i), (iv), (vi), (ix), (x)\nMacroeconomic Study: (ii, ii, (v), (vii), (vili)\n! CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS\nMacroeconomic Variables\n• Macroeconomic variables are those economic variables which are studied at the level of an economy\nas a whole.\n• These variables are important components of the subject matter of macroeconomics. The important\nones are:\n- Level of employment in the economy\n- Aggregate demand\n- Consumption expenditure in the economy\n- National income\n- Aggregate supply\n- Investment expenditure in the economy\n1.3 SCOPE AND SIGNIFICANCE OF MACROECONOMICS\n1.3.1 Area of Study (Scope) of Macroeconomics\nScope of macroeconomics refers to the field of study of macroeconomics. It includes the following\nleading issues (in accordance with the CBSE syllabus for the +2 graders):\n(2) Estimation of National income and Related Aggregates: Macroeconomics starts with the\nconcept of national income.\nlike GDP (Gross Domestic Product) and NDP (Net Domestic Product).\n(2) Theory of Employment: Macroeconomics studies the theories related to employment\n(or unemployment) in the economy.\n• Keynesian theory of employment is of notable significance in this context.\n• I explains the causes of unemployment, and suggests the possible remedies to combat it.\n(3) Theory of Money: Creation of money or creation of credit) by the commercial banks is an\nimportant component of macroeconomics.\n• This relates to the rove of Central Bank of a country (RBl in India) in regulating the supply\nof money in the economy.\n(4) Theory of General Price Lever. It reveals the trend path of the general price level, including\nthe situations of inflation? and deflation in the economy.\n(5) Government Budget (indicating role of the government in managing the economy):\nMacroeconomics studies how government budget impacts the level of economic activity in\n2. Inflation - situation of a persistent increase in the general price level in the economy.\n3, Deflation - situation of a persistent decrease in the general price level in the economy.\nIntroduction\n(6) Exchange Rate and BoP (Balance of Payments): Determination of exchange rate and the\nway it is managed (in the international money market) is an important element of the scope\nof macroeconomics. BoP relates to the study of economic transactions of a country with rest\nof the world.\nBriefly, as a specialised branch of economics, macroeconomics focuses on such issues which explain:\n• how an economy functions, and\n• how can it be managed (or regulated), so that economic growth is maximised along with\neconomic stability.\n1.3.2 Significance of Macroeconomics\nThe following observations highlight the significance of macroeconomics:\n(1) Description of the Economy: Macroeconomics offers a deep description of the economy.\nHere are some key elements it covers and their associated benefits.\n• Estimation of national income (across different sectors) reveals the nature and level of\neconomic activity in the economy.\n• Study of unemployment reveals the extent of the problem and the way it can be handled.\n• Government budget reveals the way economy is regulated by the government.\n(2) Roadmap for Business Decisions: Description of the economy serves as a roadmap for\nbusiness decisions.\nExample:\nRising national income (and consequently rising consumer expenditure) over time will\ninduce the business houses to expand their investment, leading to expansion of output\nand employment.\n(3) Policy Formulation: Macroeconomic data helps in formulation of government policies.\nExample:\nData on 'people below poverty line' will facilitate an appropriate policy relating to DBT\n(direct benefit transfer) for poorer sections of the society.\n(4) Global Economic Issues: Study of macroeconomics brings to the fore the global economic\nissues such as 'exchange rate' in the international money market, and movement of FDI\n(Foreign Direct Investment) across different countries of the world.\n(5) Environmental Pollution and Sustainable Development: It is through macroeconomic\nanalysis that we can approach the issues related to environmental pollution, and formulate a\nstrategy of sustainable development\n(6) Structural Changes and Growth Path of the Economy: Study of macroeconomics helps\nunderstand structural changes in the economy. Changes in occupational structure of a\ncountry, for example, is an important macroeconomic information that helps trace the\nnature and pace of economic growth over time.\n4. DBT (Direct Benefit Transfer) Policy - the policy to transfer money (as a help) directly to the linked bank accounts\nof the beneficiaries.\n5. Sustainable development - a development process that does not lead to depletion of resources for the future\ngenerations.","file":"macro - 1 + 2/IMG_0501.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0502","image":"pages/IMG_0502.jpg","text":"in short, we can say that the knowledge or macroeconomic vartables and macroeconomic principles\nIntroductory Macroeconomics\nof policies and programmes for its growth and development.\nis extremely useful in understanding the performance of the economy, as well as in the formulatio\nABILITY ZONE.\nEmergence of Macroeconomics as a Separate Branch of Economics\n• Great Depression of 1930's is a landmark event that led to the emergence of macroeconomics as a\nseparate branch of economics. It does not mean that the concept of macroeconomics did not exist\nprior to this event. But, macroeconomics (prior to the Great Depression) was considered more like\nan extension of microeconomics: it did not exist as a separate (or specialised) branch of economics.\nOf course, there was a reason to it:\n• It was a long-held belief of the classical economists (economists prior to Keynes) that the principles of\nmicroeconomics were enough to explain the behaviour of the economy as a whole. The argument runs\nlike this:\n• Microeconomics teaches us that (in pursuit of self-interest) every individual (in a market economy\nor a free economy maximises his satisfaction with his given income.\n• Maximisation of individual satisfaction (or welfare) implies the maximisation of welfare of all the\nindividuals in the economy. It implies maximisation of social welfare.\n• If social welfare is maximised, the scarce resources in the economy must have been optimally\nutilised.\n• Microeconomics further teaches us that in a free economy (or under perfect competition), all\nmarkets tend to be in a state of equilibrium. Thus, in the labour market S_= D (supply of labour\n= demand for labour). It is a situation of full employment.\n• There could be unemployment when S, > DL. But, in such a situation wage rate would fall, leading\nto a fall in S, and rise in Dy. Eventually, the situation of full employment would automatically be\nrestored. Thus, full employment is a normal feature of a market economy.\n• Briefly, the principles of microeconomics explain that in a free economy:\n(i) social welfare is maximised,\n(i) resources are optimally utilised, and\n(i) full employment is a normal situation.\n• Accordingly, the classical economists concluded that the principles of microeconomics were enough\nto explain the behaviour of the economic activity at the macro level. There was no need to consider\nmacroeconomics as a separate branch of economics.\nGreat Depression of 1930's contradicted the Classical Thought\n• During the depression of 30's, economic events unfolded in such a manner that the classical thought\nwas totally contradicted. Following points are of notable significance in this regard:\n() The depression of 30's led to huge unemployment in the developed countries of the world (North\nAmerica and Europe.\n(i) In USA, unemployment shot up from 3% to 25% between the period 1929-33.\n(i lie mployment was accompanied with a fall in GDP, Between the period 1929-33, GDP in USA fell\nby about 33%.\nIntroduction\n(iv) Fall in GDP led to a fall in AD (aggregate demand). Accordingly, there were drastic cuts in output as\nplanned by the producers across all sectors of the economy.\n(v) Cut in planned output implied a further fall in employment and GDP in the economy.\n• Thus, during the depression of 30's, the western economies were driven into the vicious circle of low\ndemand and low GDP. This circle operated as under:\nLow AD → Low planned output Low level of employment → Low GDP → Low AD\n• This is called 'Low Level Equilibrium Trap'.\n• The classical economists failed to find any answer to this low level equilibrium trap. Market forces\nof supply and demand failed to break it. It is in such a situation that macroeconomics emerged as a\nseparate branch of economics. Prof. Keynes was the pioneer in the field of modern macroeconomics.\nHe invented several macroeconomic variables (like AD and AS) and formulated a macroeconomic model\nto break the vicious circle of 'low level equilibrium trap'. He diagnosed lack of AD as the root cause of the\nproblem, and suggested large scale expenditure by the government as a remedy.\nPower Points [Summary of the Chapter]\n• Macroeconomics is that branch of economics which studies economic problems (or economic\nissues) at the level of an economy as a whole.\n• Macroeconomics as different from Microeconomics\n• Microeconomics deals with the problem of choice and scarcity at the individual level, while\nmacroeconomics does it at the level of an economy as a whole.\n• Allocation of resources is the central issue in microeconomics, while in macroeconomics the\ncentral issue is the determination of national income and employment.\n• Microeconomics uses microeconomic variables (like consumer's demand and producer's supply),\nwhile macroeconomics uses macroeconomic variables (like AD and AS).\n• Microeconomics involves lesser degree of aggregation than macroeconomics.\n• Microeconomics and macroeconomics are based on different set of assumptions.\n• Scope and Significance of Macroeconomics\n• Scope\n(i) Estimation of national income and related aggregates\n(ii) Theory of employment\n(iii) Theory of money\n(iv) Theory of general price level\n(V) Government budget (indicating role of the government in managing the economy)\n(vi) Exchange rate and balance of payments\n• Significance:\n(i) Macroeconomics offers description of the economy.\n(ii) Macroeconomic data serves as a roadmap for business decisions.\n(iii) Macroeconomic data facilitates policy formulation by the government.\n(iv) Macroeconomics helps understand global economic issues.\n(v) Macroeconomics underlines the gravity of environmental\npollution and\nsignificance of\nsustainable development.\n(vi) Macroeconomics helps understand structural changes and growth path of the economy.","file":"macro - 1 + 2/IMG_0502.HEIC"}],"boxes":[{"title":"Ability Zone","page":"IMG_0499","text":"","image":"pages/IMG_0499.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS","page":"IMG_0500","text":"In the words of M.H. spencer, Macroeconomics Is concerned with the economy as a whole or large\nthe rate of inflation, the nation's total output and other matters of economy-wide significance!\"","image":"pages/IMG_0500.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS","page":"IMG_0500","text":"Micro-Macro Paradox\nWhat is logical at the micro level may not be logical at the macro level.\nIllustration\nSaving - A virtue at micro level\n• If an individual saves more, he adds to his future prosperity. This makes saving a virtue at micro level.\nSaving -A vice at macro level\n• If all the people in an economy save more (and spend less), demand for goods and services may\ndecline. Consequently, investment may decline; production and employment level may fall.\n• The economy will be driven towards future poverty rather than prosperity. This makes saving a vice at\nthe macro level.\nMicro-Macro Interdependence\n• Macro impacts Micro: For instance, increase in interest rate by the RBI (and consequently by the\ncommercial banks) will lower borrowings for investment by the individual producers.\n• Micro impacts Macro: For instance, increase in spending at the individual level (which is a\nmicroeconomic factor) will lead to a rise in AD (aggregate demand) in the economy (which is a\nmacroeconomic factor). Increase in AD induces higher investment, leading to a rise in the level of\noutput and employment in the economy (again the macroeconomic factors).\nAVLR 1.2\nMICRO-MACRO PARADOX","image":"pages/IMG_0500.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"IMG_0500","text":"Q. 1. Distinguish between partial equilibrium and general equilibrium.\nAns.\n(i) Partial equilibrium refers to equilibrium in one market (say, commodity market) on the\nassumption that there is no change in other markets (like labour market or capital market).\nIt is the method of study in microeconomics.\n(ii) General equilibrium refers to simultaneous equilibrium in all the markets in the economy.\nIt is the method of study in macroeconomics.","image":"pages/IMG_0500.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS","page":"IMG_0501","text":"Macroeconomic Variables\n• Macroeconomic variables are those economic variables which are studied at the level of an economy\nas a whole.\n• These variables are important components of the subject matter of macroeconomics. The important\nones are:\n- Level of employment in the economy\n- Aggregate demand\n- Consumption expenditure in the economy\n- National income\n- Aggregate supply\n- Investment expenditure in the economy","image":"pages/IMG_0501.jpg"},{"title":"ABILITY ZONE.","page":"IMG_0502","text":"Emergence of Macroeconomics as a Separate Branch of Economics\n• Great Depression of 1930's is a landmark event that led to the emergence of macroeconomics as a\nseparate branch of economics. It does not mean that the concept of macroeconomics did not exist\nprior to this event. But, macroeconomics (prior to the Great Depression) was considered more like\nan extension of microeconomics: it did not exist as a separate (or specialised) branch of economics.\nOf course, there was a reason to it:\n• It was a long-held belief of the classical economists (economists prior to Keynes) that the principles of\nmicroeconomics were enough to explain the behaviour of the economy as a whole. The argument runs\nlike this:\n• Microeconomics teaches us that (in pursuit of self-interest) every individual (in a market economy\nor a free economy maximises his satisfaction with his given income.\n• Maximisation of individual satisfaction (or welfare) implies the maximisation of welfare of all the\nindividuals in the economy. It implies maximisation of social welfare.\n• If social welfare is maximised, the scarce resources in the economy must have been optimally\nutilised.\n• Microeconomics further teaches us that in a free economy (or under perfect competition), all\nmarkets tend to be in a state of equilibrium. Thus, in the labour market S_= D (supply of labour\n= demand for labour). It is a situation of full employment.\n• There could be unemployment when S, > DL. But, in such a situation wage rate would fall, leading\nto a fall in S, and rise in Dy. Eventually, the situation of full employment would automatically be\nrestored. Thus, full employment is a normal feature of a market economy.\n• Briefly, the principles of microeconomics explain that in a free economy:\n(i) social welfare is maximised,\n(i) resources are optimally utilised, and\n(i) full employment is a normal situation.\n• Accordingly, the classical economists concluded that the principles of microeconomics were enough\nto explain the behaviour of the economic activity at the macro level. There was no need to consider\nmacroeconomics as a separate branch of economics.\nGreat Depression of 1930's contradicted the Classical Thought\n• During the depression of 30's, economic events unfolded in such a manner that the classical thought\nwas totally contradicted. Following points are of notable significance in this regard:\n() The depression of 30's led to huge unemployment in the developed countries of the world (North\nAmerica and Europe.\n(i) In USA, unemployment shot up from 3% to 25% between the period 1929-33.\n(i lie mployment was accompanied with a fall in GDP, Between the period 1929-33, GDP in USA fell\nby about 33%.\nIntroduction\n(iv) Fall in GDP led to a fall in AD (aggregate demand). Accordingly, there were drastic cuts in output as\nplanned by the producers across all sectors of the economy.\n(v) Cut in planned output implied a further fall in employment and GDP in the economy.\n• Thus, during the depression of 30's, the western economies were driven into the vicious circle of low\ndemand and low GDP. This circle operated as under:\nLow AD → Low planned output Low level of employment → Low GDP → Low AD\n• This is called 'Low Level Equilibrium Trap'.\n• The classical economists failed to find any answer to this low level equilibrium trap. Market forces\nof supply and demand failed to break it. It is in such a situation that macroeconomics emerged as a\nseparate branch of economics. Prof. Keynes was the pioneer in the field of modern macroeconomics.\nHe invented several macroeconomic variables (like AD and AS) and formulated a macroeconomic model\nto break the vicious circle of 'low level equilibrium trap'. He diagnosed lack of AD as the root cause of the\nproblem, and suggested large scale expenditure by the government as a remedy.","image":"pages/IMG_0502.jpg"},{"title":"Power Points [Summary of the Chapter]","page":"IMG_0502","text":"• Macroeconomics is that branch of economics which studies economic problems (or economic\nissues) at the level of an economy as a whole.\n• Macroeconomics as different from Microeconomics\n• Microeconomics deals with the problem of choice and scarcity at the individual level, while\nmacroeconomics does it at the level of an economy as a whole.\n• Allocation of resources is the central issue in microeconomics, while in macroeconomics the\ncentral issue is the determination of national income and employment.\n• Microeconomics uses microeconomic variables (like consumer's demand and producer's supply),\nwhile macroeconomics uses macroeconomic variables (like AD and AS).\n• Microeconomics involves lesser degree of aggregation than macroeconomics.\n• Microeconomics and macroeconomics are based on different set of assumptions.\n• Scope and Significance of Macroeconomics\n• Scope\n(i) Estimation of national income and related aggregates\n(ii) Theory of employment\n(iii) Theory of money\n(iv) Theory of general price level\n(V) Government budget (indicating role of the government in managing the economy)\n(vi) Exchange rate and balance of payments\n• Significance:\n(i) Macroeconomics offers description of the economy.\n(ii) Macroeconomic data serves as a roadmap for business decisions.\n(iii) Macroeconomic data facilitates policy formulation by the government.\n(iv) Macroeconomics helps understand global economic issues.\n(v) Macroeconomics underlines the gravity of environmental\npollution and\nsignificance of\nsustainable development.\n(vi) Macroeconomics helps understand structural changes and growth path of the economy.","image":"pages/IMG_0502.jpg"}],"subject":"macro","number":"1"},{"id":"2","title":"Some Basic Concepts of Macroeconomics","intro":"Final goods are those goods which have crossed the boundary line of production and are ready for use by their final users.","lessons":[{"title":"Final goods, intermediate goods and end use","body":"Final goods are those goods which have crossed the boundary line of production and are ready for use by their final users. They lead to final consumption expenditure or investment expenditure. Intermediate goods are purchased by one firm from another for resale or for use as raw material in producing other goods. Classification depends on end use, not the physical nature of the good: milk bought by a household is final; milk bought by an ice-cream producer is intermediate. Unsold additions to inventory count as investment.","formula":"","source":"IMG_0503","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"2.1 MEANING OF END-USE CLASSIFICATION OF GOODS"},{"title":"Consumption goods versus capital goods","body":"Consumption goods directly satisfy human wants. They include durable goods, non-durable goods, semi-durable goods and services. Capital goods are fixed assets of producers, repeatedly used in production: durable-use producer goods such as plant and machinery. A household car is a durable consumption good; a taxi used to produce transport services is a capital good. Both are final goods. A capital good is not intermediate merely because it helps produce something.","formula":"","source":"IMG_0504","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"2.1.1 Final Goods · Consumption Goods · Capital Goods"},{"title":"Consumption expenditure and investment","body":"Consumption expenditure includes expenditure by households, government and non-profit private institutions. Investment is a process of adding to the stock of capital. Fixed investment adds to fixed assets; inventory investment changes stocks of raw materials, semi-finished goods and finished goods. New residential construction is investment. Buying existing shares transfers financial ownership; it does not itself create new capital goods. Inventory investment can be negative when closing stock is less than opening stock.","formula":"Inventory investment = Closing stock − Opening stock\nGross investment = Fixed investment + Inventory investment","source":"IMG_0507","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"2.2 CONSUMPTION EXPENDITURE · 2.3 INVESTMENT"},{"title":"Gross investment, net investment and depreciation","body":"Net investment refers to the increase in the stock of capital during an accounting year. Gross investment includes replacement of worn-out capital. Depreciation, also called consumption of fixed capital, refers to loss of value of fixed assets in use through normal wear and tear, normal accidental damage and expected obsolescence. Unexpected destruction is a capital loss rather than normal depreciation. A depreciation reserve fund is kept to cope with depreciation losses.","formula":"Net investment = Gross investment − Depreciation\nDepreciation per year = (Capital value − Scrap value) ÷ Estimated life","source":"IMG_0508","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"2.3.3 Gross Investment, Net Investment and the Concept of Depreciation"},{"title":"Stocks and flows","body":"Stocks are quantities measured at a point of time; flows are quantities measured over a specified period of time. Wealth, money supply, capital and bank balances are stocks. Income, consumption, investment and saving are flows. Capital is a stock; investment is the flow adding to it. “Money supply on 31 March” is a stock even though money changes hands repeatedly.","formula":"","source":"IMG_0509","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"2.4 STOCKS AND FLOWS"},{"title":"Four sectors and circular flow of income","body":"The household sector supplies factor services and consumes goods. The producing sector produces goods and services. Government acts as a welfare agency and producer; the external sector links the domestic economy with the rest of the world. Real flows are flows of goods and factor services; money flows are payments and receipts in the opposite direction. Circular flow of income refers to the circularity of production, income and expenditure across sectors. In a simple two-sector model, production generates income and income generates expenditure.","formula":"Production → Income generation → Expenditure → Production\nValue of output = Factor income = Final expenditure","source":"IMG_0512","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"2.5 FOUR SECTORS · 2.6 INTERSECTORAL FLOWS · 2.7 CIRCULAR FLOW OF INCOME"},{"title":"Types of consumption goods","textbookHeading":"2.1.1 (a) Consumption Goods or Consumer Goods","body":"Durable-use consumer goods serve consumers repeatedly over a long period, such as cars and refrigerators. Semi-durable goods serve over a shorter period, such as clothes. Non-durable goods are used up in a single act of consumption, such as food. Services are intangible acts satisfying wants, such as teaching or medical treatment. Durability alone does not make an item a capital good: capital goods are used by producers in production.","source":"IMG_0504","kind":"Exam points","formula":""},{"title":"Why final goods are counted and intermediate goods are not","textbookHeading":"ABILITY ZONE · Significance of the Distinction between Intermediate Goods and Final Goods","body":"Intermediate inputs are embodied in final output. Counting both adds the same value more than once. If cloth worth ₹5,000 becomes shirts worth ₹7,000, final output is ₹7,000, not ₹12,000. Under the value-added method, the cloth producer’s value and the shirt producer’s additional ₹2,000 are each counted once. It is incorrect to say intermediate goods contribute nothing: their contribution is included in final value.","source":"IMG_0513","kind":"Ability Zone","formula":""}],"pages":[{"id":"IMG_0503","image":"pages/IMG_0503.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\n2.1 MEANING OF END-USE CLASSIFICATION OF GOODS\nExplanation of the Concept of End-use Classification of Goods\nvia an illustration\non mcrecons, we do not Classy goods on the basis or thel Prysical characteristics. Instead\nwe classify goods on the basis of their 'end-use'.\n• End-use of goods refers to the way' goods are primarily used.\nExample:\nPaper is broadly used in two ways:\n(i) by the households to satisfy their need for stationery, and\n(i) by the producers to make books/notebooks.\nIn case i): Paper is out of the production boundary!, and is ready for use by its final users\n(households.\n[Note: For details on production boundary refer Critical Terms and Concepts on page 26.]\nIn case (i): Paper is not out of the production boundary, and is not ready for use by its final\nusers (households). It is being used as a raw material and therefore continues to be within\nthe production boundary.\n• In economics, a good which has crossed the boundary line of production and is ready for use by\nits final users is called 'Final Good'.\n• On the other hand, a good which has not crossed the boundary line of production and is not ready\nfor use by its final users is called 'Intermediate Good'.\nExample:\nLet us assume that during the year 2024-25, all paper mills in the country have produced\n100 tonnes of paper. Also, assume that, out of 100 tonnes, 50 tonnes of paper is used by the\nhouseholds, and 50 tonnes is used by the producers as a raw material in the production of\nbooks and notebooks.\n- As used by the households, 50 tonnes of paper will be considered as 'Final Good' (Because\nit has reached its final users by crossing the boundary line of production).\n- As used by the producers, 50 tonnes of paper will be considered as Intermediate Good.\n(Because ithas not reached its final users and is yet to cross the boundary line of production).\n• Thus, we are classifying paper not on the basis of its physical characteristics, but on the basis of\n• Final Goods are further classified as:\n(i) Consumption Goods/Consumer Goods, and\n(i) Capital Goods, depending upon their end-users or final users.\ncase end-users of final goods are households, tdsse goods are classied as consumer goods\nis tend for the direct satisfaction of their wants. For example\nIce cream and milk as used by the households.\n1. Production boundary -an imaginary line around topold luction sector which depicts the nature of goods\nproduced in this sector.\nSome Basic Concepts of Macroeconomics\n• In case end-users of final goods are producers, these are classified as capital goods. These are\ngoods used by the producers as their capital assets. These are repeatedly used in the process of\nproduction over a long period of time. For example, Tractor used by a farmer in the cultivation\nof land.\nCRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS\nEnd-use Classification of Goods\n• End-use classification of goods is that classification which classifies goods on the basis of their\nend-use and end-users, rather than their physical characteristics.\n- Goods are classified as 'Final Goods' when goods have crossed the boundary line of production, and\nare ready for use by their final users.\n- Goods are classified as 'Intermediate Goods' when goods are within the boundary line of production\nand are not ready for use by their final users.\n- Good are classified as 'Consumer Goods' when goods are used for the direct satisfaction of households.\n- Goods are classified as 'Capital Goods' when goods are used as fixed assets by the producers.\nFlow Chart showing\nEnd-use Classification of Goods\nIntermediate Goods\n- within the boundary line of production\n- not ready for use by their final users\nFinal Goods\n- beyond the boundary line of production\n- ready for use by their final users\n(a) Consumption/\nConsumer Goods\n(used by the households\nfor the direct satistaction\n(b) Capital Goods\n(used as capital assets\nby the producers, ie. goods\nused in the production of\nother goods\n[Note: (i) End-user of the same good can be a producer or a consumer.\nExample:\n- a sewing machine used by a household. Here, machine is a consumer good.\n- a sewing machine used by a tailor. Here, machine is a capital good.\n(ii) All goods purchased by the government for defence services are treated as intermediate goods.]\n2.1.1 Final Goods\n• Final goods are those goods which have crossed the boundary line of production and are ready\nfor use by their final users.\n• Who are the final users?\n• These are (i) consumers, and (i) producers.\n• How are Final Goods classified?\n• Final goods are classified as: (i) final consumer goods, and (ii) final producer goods.\n(i) Final consumer goods are finally purchased by the consumers for the satisfaction of\ntheir wants.\n(ii) Final producer goods are finally purchased by the producers and are generally used as\nfixed assets in the process of production.\n25","file":"macro - 1 + 2/IMG_0503.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0504","image":"pages/IMG_0504.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\n• What is the expenditure on Final Goods?\nentre on i same boas Dy te noosenolds is Called Consumption expenditure\n• Expenditure on final producer goods by the producers is called investment expenditure. According\nExpenditure on Final Goods = Consumption expenditure\n+ Investment expenditure\nCRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS\nProduction Boundary\n• Production boundary is an imaginary line around the production sector which depicts the nature\nof goods produced in this sector.\n• It is used to distinguish final goods from intermediate goods.\nFLOW CHART DEPICTING PRODUCTION BOUNDARY\nWood supplier\ncuts timber and\nsells it to a\npulp manufacturer\n(T 500)\nPulp manufacturer\nproduces paper\npulp and sells it to\n(·900)\nPaper mill\nproduces paper sheets\nand sells them to\na notebook manufacturer\n(1,300)\nmanufacturer\nproduces notebooks\nand sells them\nto consumers\n(· 1,800)\nWhat do we mean when we say that final goods have crossed the boundary line of production?\n• We mean that these goods are out of the process of production or the process of value addition,\nand have reached their final users.\nA shirt in a retail showroom is all set for sale to its final user. No value is to be added to the\nshirt by way of stitching, ironing, packaging, etc.\nIs it to be treated as a final good?\n\"No' should be the answer. Because:\nThe final stage of value addition occurs only when the shirt is finally sold to the consumer\n(the final user), If the retailer buys the shirt for & 800 and sells to the consumer for 7 1,000,\nthere is value addition of & 200.\nTo repeat, the value addition process is completed only when the product reaches its final\nuser: it may be a shirt purchased by the consumer, or a tractor purchased by a farmer (Both,\nthe consumer and the farmer are the final users).\n• You may note that only final goods are included in the estimation of national product or\nnational income.\n2.1.1 (a) Consumption Goods or Consumer Goods\n• Consumption goods (or consumer goods) are those goods which are directly used for the\nsatisfaction of human wants.\nExample:\nIce cream and milk as used by the households. These goods are consumed by the households\nwhen purchased.\n26\nSome Basic Concepts of Macroeconomics\n• Consumption goods are broadly classified into four categories, as given in the flow chart below:\nConsumption Goods/Consumer Goods\nDurable\nGoods\n• Durable consumption\ngoods are those goods\nwhich can be used for\nseveral years and are of\nrelatively high value.\n• These goods are\nrepeatedly used before\nbeing discarded as\nuseless.\n• Examples: LED TV,\nlaptop, car, scooter,\nwashing machine, etc.\nGoods\n• Semi-durable\nconsumption goods are\nthose goods which can\nbe used for a period of\none year or slightly more.\n• These goods are not of\nvery high value.\n• Examples: Clothes,\nfurniture, crockery,\nelectric goods, etc.\nNon-durable\nGoods\n• Non-durable or single-use\nconsumption goods are\nthose goods which are\nused-up in a single act of\nconsumption.\n• For example, the bread\nthat you eat is used-up in a\nsingle act of consumption.\nThe same bread cannot be\nused again. Also these\ngoods are of relatively low\nNon-material\nGoods or Services\n• Services are those\nnon-material goods\nwhich directly satisfy\nhuman wants.\n• Examples: Services of a\ndoctor, lawyer, domestic\nhelp, etc.\n• Examples: Ink, ice cream,\nmilk, petrol, etc.\n2.1.1 (b) Capital Goods\n• Capital goods are fixed assets of the producers. Plant and machinery is an example of capital\ngoods. As fixed assets, capital goods are repeatedly used in the process of production for several\nyears and are of high value. Even nuts and bolts are used for several years, but these are not\ncapital goods as these are of low value. Only those fixed assets of the producers are taken as\ncapital goods which are used in the process of production for several years and which are of\nhigh value.\n• Also, capital goods involve depreciation. Depreciation refers to loss of value of fixed assets (in\nuse) owing to their wear and tear.\n• These goods are used by the producers either for:\n(i) the replacement of the existing capital stock, or for\n(ii) addition to the capital stock.\nDID YOU KNOW\nAll Machines are not Capital Goods, for example:\n• A sewing machine in a tailoring shop is a fixed asset of the tailor; it is a capital good.\n• But the same machine with a consumer household is not a capital good. It is simply a durable-use\nconsumer good.\n• Likewise, a car with a tourist company is a capital good. But the same car with a household is a\ndurable-use consumer good.\n• Thus, while identifying goods as capital goods, we must make sure about their end-user.\n- If the end-user of a durable good is a household consumer, it is durable-use consumer good.\n- On the other hand, if the end-user of a durable good is a producer, it is a capital good.\nFinally, capital goods are only those durable goods which are used as producer goods, not as\nconsumer goods.","file":"macro - 1 + 2/IMG_0504.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0505","image":"pages/IMG_0505.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\nAll Capital Goods are Producer Goods, But are all Producer Goods not Capital Goods?\n• Producer goods are those goods which are used in the production of other goods.\n• The classification of producer goods below substantiates these points further,\nTypes of Producer Goods\n(i) Goods used as raw material\nby the producers\n• Example: Wood used to make furniture\n- Non-durable use goods, i.e., single\nuse producer goods\n- Cannot be repeatedly used in\nproduction\n- The same wood cannot be used again\nto make turniture.\n(ii) Goods used as fixed assets\nby the producers\n• Examples: Plant and machinery,\n- Durable use producer goods\n- Repeatedly used in production\nprocess.\n• Thus, while all capital goods are producer goods, all producer goods are not capital goods.\nSimilarity and Difference between Consumption Goods and Capital Goods\n• Similarity: The similarity between consumption goods and capital goods is that these are final\ngoods. Both are included in the estimation of national income.\n• Difference:\nThere are many differences between consumption goods and capital\ngoods as\nhighlighted below:\nTable 2.1: Consumption Goods vs. Capital Goods\nBasis of Difference\nConsumption Goods\nCapital Goods\n1. Satisfaction of\n- Consumption\ngoods\n• Capital\ngoods\ndo\nlead\ndirect-satisfaction of human wants.\ndirect-satisfaction of human wants.\n2. Lifetime of Use\n- Most consumption goods have limited\nlifetime of use.\n- Capital goods generally have a lifespan of\nmore than one year.\n3. Nature of\nDemand\n- These goods are\nconsumed by the\n-These goods are not consumed by the\nhouseholds when purchased.\nhouseholds. Instead, these are used by\n- These are treated as 'Direct Demand'.\nthe producers for further production.\n- These are treated as Derived Demand'.\n4. Nature of\n- Expenditure on\nExpenditure\nconsumption goods is\ncalled Consumption Expenditure.\n- Expenditure on capital goods is called\nInvestment Expenditure.\n5. Economic\nImpact\n- Higher production of consumption goods\nleads to higher level of welfare of the people.\n- Higher production of capital goods leads to\n- It raises their quality of life.\nhigher production capacity in the economy.\n- It propels GDP growth.\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\nQ. 1. All producer goods are not capital goods. Why?\nAns. Producer goods include:\n(i) goods used as fixed assets, like plant and machinery, and\n(ii) goods used as raw material, like wood used to make furniture.\nCapital goods include only fixed assets of the producers. These are durable-use producer goods.\nOn the other hand, goods used as raw material are single-use producer goods. These are not\nrepeatedly used in the process of product\ndingly, all producer goods are not capital goods.\n28\nSome Basic Concepts of Macroeconomics\nQ. 2. Classify the following as capital good or consumer good:\n(i) Car purchased by a driver to be used as a taxi\n(i) Cloth lying unsold with a trader at the end of an year\n(ili) Sugar used by households\n(iv) Services of a doctor or a lawyer used by a household\n(v) Laptop purchased by a household\n(vi) Machine purchased by a sugar mill\nAns. Capital Good: (i), (ii) [as a part of capital stock], (vi)\nConsumer Good: (iii), (iv), (v)\n2.1.2 Intermediate Goods\n• Intermediate goods are those goods\n(i) which have yet not crossed the boundary line of production,\n(ii) value is still to be added to these goods, and\n(iii) which are yet not ready for use by their final users.\n• In other words, intermediate goods are those goods which are purchased by one firm from the\nother firm: (i) as raw material, or (ii) as goods for resale.\nExample:\n(i) Wood purchased by a carpenter (from a timber merchant) for making chairs is an\nintermediate good. Because, wood is used as a raw material for making chairs. Value is\nto be added to wood by converting it into chairs.\n(ii) Shirts purchased by firm X from firm Y for resale are intermediate goods. Because, value\nis to be added to the shirts through resale.\n• Non-inclusion in National Income: Value of intermediate goods ultimately becomes a part of the\nvalue of final goods.\n• Accordingly, intermediate goods are not included in the\nestimation of national product or\nnational income.\n• Otherwise, there would be duplication in the estimation of national product, called 'Double\nCounting' (counting the value of a good more than once).\nExample:\nWhen a carpenter buys wood worth 10,000 and converts it into chairs worth & 20,000,\nthen the value of chairs (final goods) includes the value of wood (intermediate good). If we\nadd R 10,000 (wood) + R 20,000 (chairs), the total becomes & 30,000. Thus, leading to double\ncounting ofF 10,000.\n• Expenditure on Intermediate Goods: Expenditure on intermediate goods by the producers\nduring an accounting year is called intermediate consumption or intermediate cost.\nSame Good May be Final or Intermediate\n• It is not possible to name one set of goods as final goods and another set as intermediate goods.\nThe same good may be final or intermediate good. The distinction depends on the end-use of the\ngoods.\n29","file":"macro - 1 + 2/IMG_0505.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0506","image":"pages/IMG_0506.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\nanana to be to pra sIs to be treated as an intermediate good.\n• Also, if it is purchased and resold by the producers, it is to be treated as an intermediate goo\n• But if it is used by the producer as a fixed asset (like a tractor used by the farmer), it is to b\ntreated as a final good.\n• And, of course, goods purchased by the households for final consumption are to be treated as\nfinal goods.\nExample:\nsugar used as a raw material in the production of biscuits is an intermediate good.\nBut, sugar used by the households in milk or tea is a final good.\nPaper purchased by a student is a final good. But when purchased by a publisher (for making\nbooks), it is an intermediate good.\nTable 2.2 highlights the difference between intermediate goods and final goods.\nTable 2.2: Intermediate Goods vs. Final Goods\nBasis of Difference\n1. Meaning\nIntermediate Goods\nFinal Goods\n- Intermediate goods are those goods which - Final goods are those goods which are\n- purchased\nconsumers\nfor\n-used as raw material for the production of\nother goods during the accounting year.\n-purchased by producers for investment.\n2. Production\nBoundary\n3. Value Addition\n4. National Income\nEstimation\n- resold by the firms for profit during the\naccounting year.\n- These goods remain within the boundary\nline of production, and are not ready for use\nby their final users.\n- Value is yet to be added to these goods.\n• These goods are not included in the estimation\nof national product or national income.\n- These goods are outside the boundary line\nof production, and are ready for use by their\nfinal users.\n- Value is not to be added to these goods.\n- These goods are included in the estimation\nof national product or national income.\nAVLR 2.1\nFINAL GOODS vs. INTERMEDIATE GOODS\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\nQ. 3. How would you find out whether a particular expenditure is an expenditure on intermediate\ngoods or on final goods?\nAns. Expenditure on final goods must lead to:\n(i) final consumption expenditure (C), or\n(il) investment expenditure (I).\nThe expenditure which does not lead to C and I (like the expenditure on raw material) is to be\ntreated as an expenditure on intermediate goods. Expenditure on intermediate goods leads to\nintermediate consumption or intermediate cost.\n30\nSome Basic Concepts of Macroeconomics\nQ. 4. Purchase of a car always means the purchase of a final good. Do you agree?\nAns. No. It depends on the end-use of the car. If it is purchased by a household, it is a final good. It is\nlike a consumer durable.\nIf it is purchased by taxi-operators, then again it is a final good, as it is to be finally used by the\nproducer as a fixed asset.\nHowever, if the car is purchased by a retail dealer from the factory for the purpose of resale, it is\nto be treated as intermediate good.\nQ. 5. Classify the following as final good or intermediate good (apply end-use as the basis of\nclassification):\n(i) Paper purchased by a publisher\n(i) Coal purchased by a factory\n(iii) Books purchased by a bookseller\n(iv) Furniture purchased by a school\n(v) Use of electricity to make products\n(vi) Car purchased by a household\n(vii) Seeds purchased by a farmer\n(viii) LED TV purchased by a dealer\n(ix) Milk purchased by a hotel\n(x) Machine purchased for installation in a factory\n(xi) Exhaust fans used for making water coolers\n(xii) Postage, stamps purchased by a consumer\n(xili) Sewing machine purchased by a homemaker\n(xiv) Soft drinks purchased by a school canteen\n(xv) Microwave oven purchased by a consumer household\nAns. Final Good: (iv), (vi), (x), (xii), (xiii), (xv)\nIntermediate Good: (i), (i), (iii), (v), (vi), (viii), (ix), (x), (xiv)\n2.2 CONCEPT AND COMPONENTS OF CONSUMPTION EXPENDITURE\n2.2.1 What is Consumption Expenditure?\n• In macroeconomics, consumption expenditure refers to aggregate consumption expenditure in\nthe economy.\n2.2.2 Components of Consumption Expenditure\n• The consumers of an economy are broadly classified as:\n(i) Households: Households buy consumer goods for the satisfaction of their wants.\n(i) The Government: The government buys consumer goods for distribution among defence\nforces, for mid-day meals in the government schools, and such other purposes.\n(iii) Non-profit Private Institutions (like NGOs, temples, mosques, gurudwaras, and others):\nNon-profit private institutions buy consumer goods for charity.\n• If we add up expenditure on the purchase of consumer goods by the households, government\nand non-profit private institutions, we get an estimate of total consumption expenditure in the\nAggregate Consumption Expenditure = Consumption expenditure by the households\n+ Consumption expenditure by the government\n+ Consumption expenditure by the non-profit\nprivate institutions (NGO, temples, mosques,\ngurudwaras and others)","file":"macro - 1 + 2/IMG_0506.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0507","image":"pages/IMG_0507.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\n2.3 CONCEPT AND COMPONENTS OF INVESTMENT\n2.3.1 What is Investment?\n• Investment refers to increase in the stock of capital. Thus:\nI = ДК\nHere, I = Investment; K = Capital stock; AK = Change in capital stock during the year.\nas capital formation. From the viewpoint of the economy as a whole, investment refers to total\nproduction of capital goods during an accounting year.\n• There are two components of investment:\n(a) Fixed investment, and\n(b) Inventory investment.\n2.3.2 Components of Investment\n2.3.2 (a) Fixed Investment\n• Fixed investment refers to increase in the stock of fixed assets (like plant and machinery) of the\nproducers during an accounting year.\nExample:\nIf at the beginning of the year 2025, a producer has a stock of 8 machines and at the end\nof 2025, he has a stock of 10 machines, then the stock of his fixed assets increases by\n2 machines during the year 2025.\n• Therefore, fixed investment of the producer during the year 2025 = 2 machines.\n• Fixed investment is also called fixed capital formation. This implies increase in the stock of capital\nin terms of fixed assets (or capital goods) which are repeatedly used in the process of production\nfor several years.\nFixed Investment = Stock of fixed assets with the producers at the end of the accounting year\n- Stock he producers at the beginning of the accounting year\n= Increase in the stock of fixed assets with the producers during an\naccounting year\nSignificance of Fixed Investment\nFixed investment raises production capacity of the producers. By raising production capacity o\nthe producers, fixed investment leads to higher level of output in the economy.\n• Higher level of output (because of fixed investment) leads to higher rate of economic growth,\npopularly known as GDP growth.\ncapital, increase in tixed vestment leads Burease in the stock of fixed capital, and therefore, increase in\nproduction capacity of the producer.\n32\nSome Basic Concepts of Macroeconomics\n2.3.2 (b) Inventory Investment\n• At a point of time, producers hold the stock of\n• finished goods (unsold goods),\n• semi-finished goods (goods which are in the process of production), and\n• raw material.\n• This is called \"inventory stock'. Change in inventory stock during the year is called inventory\ninvestment of the producers.\nInventory Investment = Inventory stock at the end of the accounting year\n- Inventory stock at the beginning of the accounting year\n= Change in inventory stock during an accounting year\nSignificance of Inventory Investment\n• Inventory investment primarily consists of investment in terms of the stock of (i) raw material, and\n(ii) finished goods.\n• The stock of raw material is significant because:\n(i) It ensures uninterrupted supply of inputs to the producers.\n(ii) With enough stock of raw material, the producers can avoid day-to-day purchases from the\nmarket. Accordingly, uncertainties of the market (relating to price and availability of the raw\nmaterial) are avoided.\n• The stock of finished goods is significant because it enables the producers to meet the potential\n(future) demand for their product. Further, actual inventory stock at a time may not be the desired\ninventory stock.\nCRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS\n• Desired inventory stock refers to planned inventory stock. This is maintained by the producers to meet\nthe future demand.\n• Undesired inventory stock, on the other hand, refers to unplanned inventory stock. It arises because\ndemand for the product turns out to be lower than expected. Unplanned inventory stock leads to losses.\n& AVLR 2.2\nFIXED INVESTMENT vs. INVENTORY INVESTMENT\n2.3.3 Gross Investment, Net Investment and The Concept of Depreciation\n• Gross investment refers to total production of capital goods during the year.\n• This includes\n(i) capital goods used for the replacement of existing\ncapital stock (which is worn-out), and\n+ Depreciation\n- Depreciation\n(ii) capital goods used as a net addition to the existing\nInvestment\ncapital stock.","file":"macro - 1 + 2/IMG_0507.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0508","image":"pages/IMG_0508.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\n• Capital goods used for the replacement of existing capital stock refers to 'depreciation, as\ncomponent of gross investment. apital goods used as net addition to the existing capital stod\nis called 'net investment. Only net investment leads to addition to the stock of capital.\n• Depreciation (a part of gross investment) only replaces the worn-out fixed assets. It helps t\nmaintain the existing stock of capital.\nSignificance of Net Investment\n(i) It raises the stock of capital in the economy.\n(ii) It helps generate opportunities of employment.\n(i) Net investment is a net rise in production capacity of the economy. Accordingly, GDP growth\nis accelerated.\nSimilarity and Difference between Gross Investment and Net Investment\n• Similarity: Both gross investment as well as net investment include: (i) fixed investment, and\n(il) inventory investment.\n• Difference: There are many differences between gross investment and net investment as\nhighlighted below:\nTable 2.3: Gross Investment vs. Net Investment\nBasis of Difference\n1. Meaning\nGross Investment\n- It includes expenditure by the producers on\nthe purchase of new assets.\nalso\nincludes\nexpenditure\nthe\nreplacement of existing assets during an\naccounting year.\n2. Addition to the\nIt does not show net addition to the existing\nCapital Stock\ncapital stock.\n3. Economic Impact\n- Increase in\ngross investment need not\nnecessarily lead to increase in production\ncapacity.\n- Therefore, it does not necessarily cause\nincrease in GDP.\nNet Investment\n- It includes expenditure by the producers on\nthe purchase of new assets only.\n- More\nspecifically, it does not include\nexpenditure by the\nproducers on the\nreplacement of existing assets.\n- It shows net addition to the existing capital\nstock.\n- Increase in net investment always leads to\nincrease in production capacity.\n- Therefore it always causes increase in GDP.\nConcept of Depreciation\n• While fixed assets (like plant & machinery) are in use, they go down in value owing to\n(i) normal wear and tear,\n(ii) accidental damages (beyond their routine repairs and maintenance).\n(i) expected obsolescence: when fixed assets become obsolete (or outdated) due to change in\ntechnology or change in demand (which the producers normally expect to happen).\n• Depreciation is the loss of value of fixed assets in use on account of: (i) normal wear and teaf,\n(il) accidental damages, (ili) expected obsolescence.\n• Depreciation is also called consumption of fixed capital. Because of depreciation, fixed assets\nneed to be replaced from time to time. Replacement of fixed assets requires funds.\n34\nSome Basic Concepts of Macroeconomics\n• Provision for the funds is made on annual basis.\nExample:\nIf a machine is purchased for & 10,00,000 and its expected lifetime of use is 10 years.\nThen the annual provision for funds (to replace the machine after 10 years - 3 10,00,000\n= 1,00,000.\nThis is called Depreciation Reserve Fund.\n• Depreciation reserve fund is a fund to replace the worn-out fixed assets. Depreciation reserve\nfund fulfills the need for replacement investment.\n• Lack of depreciation reserve fund implies the lack of replacement investment. Accordingly,\noverall investment (gross investment) in the economy tends to fall. This leads to a fall in the level\nof output. The level of income and employment will also fall. The economy will slip into a state of\n'economic slowdown'. Finally, due to the lack of depreciation reserve fund, the economy might be\ncaught into a low level equilibrium trap.\nCRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS\nA low level equilibrium trap is a situation when:\n• low income causes low demand,\n• low demand, in turn, causes low output;\n• low output, once again causes low income.\nirap\nExpected and Unexpected Obsolescence\n• Expected obsolescence has two components:\n(i) Loss of value of fixed assets when these become obsolete/outdated owing to change in\ntechnology.\nA plant producing black and white TVs becomes obsolete when technology is\ndiscovered to produce colour TVs.\n(ii) Loss of value of fixed assets when these become obsolete/outdated owing to change in demand.\nA plant producing rubber shoes becomes obsolete when demand shifts from rubber\nshoes to leather shoes.\n• Expected obsolescence is estimated by the producers on the basis of their knowledge and\nexperience of the market conditions.\n• Unexpected obsolescence occurs owing to\n(i) natural calamities (like earthquake, floods or fire), and\n(ii) fall in market value of the assets when there is economic recession.\n• Loss of value of fixed assets owing to unexpected obsolescence is called 'capital loss'. These\nlosses are not a part of depreciation or depreciation reserve fund.\n[Note: Only expected obsolescence is considered for the estimation of depreciation, not the unexpected","file":"macro - 1 + 2/IMG_0508.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0509","image":"pages/IMG_0509.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\nte the dience been age Ousescence ana unexpected obsolescence.\nTable 2.4: Expected Obsolescence vs. Unexpected Obsolescence\nExpected Obsolescence\nUnexpected Obsolescence\nBasis of Difference\n1. Meaning\n- It refers to a fall in the value of fixed assets\ndue to change in technology or change in\n- It refers to a fall in the value of fixed assets\ndue to natural\ncalamities\ndemand.\nrecession.\nor economic\n2. Nature\n- It is a part of depreciation.\n- It is not a part of depreciation. It is deemed\nas capital loss.\n3. Management\n- Expected obsolescence is managed through\nDepreciation Reserve Fund.\n- Unexpected\nobsolescence\nthrough insurance of the fixed assets.\nis managed\nConsumption of Fixed Capital and Capital Loss\n• Consumption of fixed capital is also known as Depreciation. Loss of value of fixed assets owing to\nunexpected obsolescence is called 'capital loss'.\nTable 2.5 highlights the difference between consumption of fixed capital and capital loss.\nBasis of Difference\n1. Meaning\nTable 2.5: Consumption of Fixed Capital vs. Capital Loss\nConsumption of Fixed Capital\nCapital Loss\n- It refers to loss of value of fixed assets due to\n- It refers to loss of value of fixed assets due to\n(i) normal wear and tear,\n(i) natural calamities (earthquake, floods,\n(i) accidental damages, and\nfire, etc.), and\n(il) expected obsolescence.\n(i) fall in the market value of the assets during\nperiods of economic recession.\n- Loss of value of fixed assets (capital goods) - Loss of value of fixed assets occurs while\noccurs while these are being continuously\nthese are not in use.\nused in the process of production.\n- It is managed through depreciation reserve\nfund.\n- It is managed through insurance of the fixed\nassets.\n2. Cause of\nOccurrence\n3. Management\n& AVLR 2.3\nDEPRECIATION vs. CAPITAL LOSS\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\nQ. 6. How does higher rate of net capital formation lead to higher level of productivity/efficiency of\nAns. Higher rate of net capital formation implies greater availability of capital (in terms of machines)\nper unit of labour. Aided by machines, efficiency of labour definitely increases. This precisely is\nthe reason why labour in developed countries (like USA) is more efficient than in less developed\ncountries like India.\nQ. 7. Distinguish between depreciation and depreciation reserve fund.\nAns. Depreciation is the loss of fixed assets in use on account of: (i) normal wear and tear,\n(ii) accidental damages, and (iii) expecte\nseen obsolescence.\n36\nSome Basic Concepts of Macroeconomics\nOn the other hand, depreciation reserve fund is a provision of funds to cope with depreciation\nlosses. These funds are used for the replacement of fixed assets when these are worn-out or\nwhen these become obsolete/outdated.\nQ. 8. What is current replacement cost?\nAns. It refers to the estimated value of depreciation for all the producing units in the economy, during\nthe period of an accounting year.\n2.4 STOCKS AND FLOWS\n2.4.1 Meaning of Stock\n• A stock is a quantity measured at a particular point of time. Lets take an example of money in a\nbank account.\nExample:\nSuppose, on January 1, 2026, there were & 20,000 in your bank account.\nSimilarly, on January 10, 2026, there were & 25,000 in your bank account.\nAll such values are stock values-as these are measured at a specific point of time.\n• Capital and quantity of money are notable examples of stock variables.\n2.4.2 Meaning of Flow\n• A flow is a quantity measured over a specified period of time.\nExample:\nYou may receive & 1,500 per month as pocket allowance.\nYou may spend & 50 everyday in the school canteen.\nYou may earn 4 per cent annual interest on your bank deposits of & 1,00,000.\nAll these values/quantities are 'flows' as these are measured per unit of time period (an hour,\na day, a month, an year, etc.).\n• Income, expenditure, production, consumption and interest are notable examples of flow\nvariables.\n2.4.3 Difference and Interdependence between Stock and Flow\nTable 2.6 shows the difference between stock and flow.\nTable 2.6: Stock vs. Flow\nBasis of Difference\nStock\nStock refers to the value of a variable at a\n- Flow refers to the value of a variable during\npoint of time.\na period of time.\n2. Time Dimension\nStock is not time dimensional.\nFlow is time dimensional.\n- It is measured at a specific point of time.\nIt is measured per hour, per month or per\nyear.\n3. Mutual\nDependence\n- Stock impacts the flow.\n- Greater the stock of capital, greater is the\nflow of goods and services.\n- Flow impacts the stock.\n- Greater the flow of income, greater is the\nstock of wealth with the people.\n37","file":"macro - 1 + 2/IMG_0509.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0510","image":"pages/IMG_0510.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\n• Fig. 2.1 shows deposits of * 20,000 in your saving bank account on January 1, 2026, This is a\nInterdependence between Stock and Flow\nLikewise, deposits of & 2,000 per month is a flow concept.\nFLOW\nSTOCK\nFLOW\nDeposits of ‹ 2,000\nper month\nWithdrawal of R1,000\nper month\nFig. 2.1: Interdependence between Stock and Flow\n• A point to be noted is that your stock of savings depends upon your flow of deposits into\nyour saving account. Likewise, your flow of withdrawals depends upon your stock of savings.\nThus, there is a mutual dependence between stocks and flows.\nAVLR 2.4\nSTOCK AND FLOW\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\nQ. 9. Classify the following as stock or flow variables:\n(i) Wealth\n(ii) Expenditure of money\n(ill) Number of births\n(iv) Income\n(v) Interest on capital\n(vi) Water in the overhead tank\n(vii) Capital formation\n(vili) Quantity/Supply of money in a country\n(ix) Bank deposits\n(x) Capital\n(xi) Rice stored in a godown\n(xii) Sale of rice as on January 1, 2026\n(xili) Distance between Delhi and Mumbai\n(xiv) Change in the supply of money as on January 1, 2026\n(xv) Labour force in India as on March 31, 2025\n(xvi) Leakage of water from the overhead tank\n(xvii) Population of a country at a point of time\n(xviii) Speed of a car going from Delhi to Mumbai\nAns. Stock: (i), (vi), (viii), (ix), (x), (xi), (xii), (xili), (xiv), (xv), (xvii)\nFlow: (ii), (iii), (iv), (v), (vii), (xvi), (xviii)\n38\nSome Basic Concepts of Macroeconomics\nQ. 10. Are the following Stocks or Flows? Explain with a reason.\n(i) Investment, (ii) Monetary Expenditure, (iii) A Hundred Rupee Note, (iv) A Family's\nConsumption of Sugar, (v) Services of a Tutor, (vi) Production of Cement, (vii) Machinery of a\n(i) Investment: It is a flow concept because it is related to a period of time.\n(ii) Monetary Expenditure: It is a flow concept because it is related to a period of time.\n(ii) A Hundred Rupee Note: It is a stock concept because it is a component of supply of money.\n(iv) A Family's Consumption of Sugar: It is a flow concept because consumption relates to a\nperiod of time.\n(v) Services of a Tutor: It is a flow concept because it is related to a period of time.\n(vi) Production of Cement: It is a flow concept because it is related to a period of time.\n(vii) Machinery of a Sugar Mill: It is a stock concept because it relates to a point of time.\nQ. 11. Based on the following headlines, identify whether each item is a stock or flow variable:\n(a) \"Gross GST revenue collections reached & 2.1 lakh crore in April 2025, reflecting strong\neconomic activity and better compliance under GST 2.0.\"\n(b) \"The GST Council has approved a rationalised two-rate structure under GST 2.0-5% and\n18%—to simplify taxation and reduce the burden on consumers.\"\nAnS.\n(a) Flow variable-Because GST revenue is collected over a period of time (one month, April\n2025).\n(b) Stock variable-Because the GST rate structure exists at a specific point in time and does\nnot depend on a time period.\nQ. 12. Are exports and imports stock variables or flow variables?\nAns. Certain concepts in economics are studied only as flow variables, not as stock variables. Exports\nand imports are always flow variables.\n2.5 FOUR SECTORS OF THE ECONOMY\nFrom the macro point of view, the economy is often divided into four sectors, as under:\nFour Sectors of the Economy\nHousehold Sector\n• It includes consumers of\ngoods and services.\n• Households are also the\nowners of the factors of\nproduction.\nProducer Sector\n• It includes all producing\nunits (firms) in the\neconorry.\n• For the production of\ngoods and services,\nthe firms hire/purchase\nfactors or production\n(land, labour, capital and\nentrepreneurial skill) from\nthe households.\nGovernment Sector\n• It includes:\n(i) Government as a\nwelfare agency\n(performing functions\nof law & order and\ndefence), and\n(ii) Government as a\nproducer.\nExternal Sector\n(or Rest of the World Sector)\n• It includes all such\nactivities which are\nrelated to export and\nimport of goods, and the\nflow of capital between\nthe domestic economy\nand rest of the world.","file":"macro - 1 + 2/IMG_0510.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0511","image":"pages/IMG_0511.jpg","text":"40\nIntroductory Macroeconomics\n2.6 INTERSECTORAL FLOWS\n2.6.1 Concept of Intersectoral Interdependence\n• Each sector of the economy depends on the other in one way or the other.\n• This is called intersectoral interdependence.\n• Following observations highlight the intersectoral interdependence:\n• The household sector depend aule producer sector for the supply or goods and service:\nneeded for consumption.\n• The producer sector depends on the household sector for the supply of factors of production\n(also called factor services). These are needed for the production of goods and services.\n• The government sector depends on the producer and household sectors for its tax and non-ta.\nrevenue.\n• Producers and households depend on the government for administrative services, besides law\n& order and defence.\n• Intersectoral interdependence leads to intersectoral flows, either in the form of goods and\nservices or in the form of money. Intersectoral flows are of two types as shown in the flow chart:\nIntersectoral Flows\nReal Flow\nIntersectoral flow in the form\nof goods and services\nMoney Flow\nIntersectoral flow in the\nform of money\n2.6.2 Concept of Real Flows\n• Real flows refer to the flow of goods and services among different sectors of the economy. Some\nexamples of real flows are as follows:\nFlow of factor services from household sector to the producer sector.\nFlow of goods and services from the producer sector to the household sector.\n• Fig. 2.2 illustrates real flows in case of a simple 2-sector economy, including\n(i) household sector, and\n(ii) producer sector.\nGoods Produced and Sold by the Firms\nHOUSEHOLD\nSECTOR\nPRODUCER\nSECTOR or FIRMS\nFactor Services\n(Land, Labour, Capital and Entrepreneurship)\nrendered by the Household Sector\n• Fig. 2.2 shows real flows in\nterms of:\n-flow of goods sold by the\nfirms to the households, and\n-flow of factor services\nrendered by the households\nto the producers.\n• Both these flows are real as\nthese involve the movement of\ngoods and services from one\nsector to the other. Money does\nnot come into the picture.\nFig. 2.2: Real Flows in a Simp\nSome Basic Concepts of Macroeconomics\n2.6.3 Concept of Money Flows\n• Money flows refer to the flow of money across different sectors of the economy. Some examples\nof money flows are as follows:\nFlow of factor payments by the producer sector to the household sector (on account of the\npurchase of factor services).\nFlow of money from the household sector to the producer sector (on account of the purchase\nof goods and services).\n• It needs to be noted that the money flows are just reciprocals of the real flows.\n• Fig. 2.3 illustrates money flows in case of a simple 2-sector economy.\nExpenditure on the Purchase of Goods\nHOUSEHOLD\nSECTOR\nPRODUCER\nSECTOR or FIRMS\n• Fig. 2.3 shows money flows in\nterms of:\n-flow of money from\nhouseholds to the producers\n(firms) for the purchase of\ngoods, and\n-flow of money from the\nproducers to the households\nfor the purchase of factor\nPayments for Factor Services\n(Rent, Wages, Interest and Profit)\nFig. 2.3: Money Flows in a Simple 2-sector Economy\nRelationship and Difference between Real Flows and Money Flows\n• Each money flow is a reciprocal of the real flow.\n• Thus, money flow from households to the producers (for the purchase of goods) is a reciprocal\nof the real flow of goods from the producers to the households.\n• Likewise, money flow from producers to the households (as payments for factor services) is a\nreciprocal of the real flow of factor services from the households to the producers.\n• Some important differences between real flows and money flows are as follows:\nBasis of Difference\n1. Meaning\n2. Nature\n3. Impact\nTable 2.7: Real Flows vs. Money Flows\nReal Flows\n- Real flows refer to the flow of goods and\nservices among different sectors\nof the\neconomy.\n- It is flow of physical output or services\namong sectors. Accordingly,\nalso called\n'Physical Flow'.\n- Higher level of real flows always leads to\nhigher level of output in the economy.\nMoney Flows\n- Money\nflows refer to the flow of money\nacross different sectors of the economy.\n- It is flow of monetary transactions among\nsectors. Accordingly, also called Nominal\nFlow.\n- Higher level of money\nflows need not\nnecessarily lead to higher level of output in\nthe economy.\ni lie","file":"macro - 1 + 2/IMG_0511.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0512","image":"pages/IMG_0512.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\nQ. 13. Money flows are opposite to real flows. How?\nAns. Money flows are opposite to real flow: a decause money flows are in response to the real fu\nFor example, there is a real flow of goods and services tom the producers to the househ\nIt is in response to it, that the nousentias make pant te producers. So that, man\nflows from the housenolds to producers in terms of consumption expenditure. Likewise, th\nis a real flow of factor services from the housenolds to the producers. It is in response tot,\nthe producers make payments to the noused olds. so that, money flows from producers to\nhouseholds in terms of factor payments.\n[Note: Students are advised to draw Fig. 2.2 and 2.3 in support of their answer.]\n0. 14. Classify the following transactions into Real Flow or Money Flow. Give reasons for your ansie\n(i) A firm pays 750,000 as wages to its workers.\n(i) Workers render their labour services to the firm.\ni) A household buys groceries worth R5,000 from a supermarket.\n(iv) The supermarket delivers groceries to the household.\n(v) The government pays subsidies to farmers for purchasing fertilizers.\nAns. Real Flow: (i, (iv)\nMoney Flow: (i), (iii), (v)\n2.7 CIRCULAR FLOW OF INCOME\n• in every economy, three activities never stop:\n(i) production of goods and services,\n(i) generation of income (in terms of wages, interest, rent and profit), and\n(ile penditure (in terms of consumption expenditure and investment expenditure.\n• in fact, these activities are the liteline of an economythe concept of economy does not exist\nwithout these activities. It is difficult to trame the beginning of these activities and it is impossible\nto predict their end.\n• Production, income and expenditure are flow variables. These have been in existence like a circle\nwithout a beginning or an end.\n2.7.1 Meaning of Circular Flow of Income\nCircular flow of income refers to the unending flow of the activities of production, income\nhouseholds in particular.\ngeneration and penanture involving different sectors of the economy, the producers and the\n2.7.2 Three Phases of Circular Flow\n• Three phases of circular flow are as follows:\n(i) Phase of Production,\n(ii) Phase of Income Generation (also called the Phase of Distribution), and\n(i) Phase of Disposition/Expenditure\nSome Basic Concepts of Macroeconomics\n• Fig. 2.4 describes the three phases of circular flow in an economy.\n3. Phase of\nDisposition/\nExpenditure\n1. Phase of\nProduction\n2. Phase of\nGeneration\n• Production of goods and services\n(under Phase-1) causes generation\n(or distribution) of income.\n• Income causes expenditure (or disposition).\n• By generating demand, expenditure\nonce again causes production.\n• Consequently, there is generation of income\nand disposition (or expenditure) of income.\n• The flows of production, income and\nexpenditure form a circularity with no\nbeginning or an end. Which is why it is\ncalled circular flow.\nFig. 2.4: Three Phases of Circular Flow of Income\nPhase of Production\n• Production refers to 'value addition. When wood worth 75,000 is converted into chairs worth\n· 10,000, there is value addition worth 7 5,000 (F 10,000 - 75,000). This is what production\nmeans.\n• Phase of production in the circular flow means the process of value addition by the producing\nsector.\n• The producer adds value by hiring or purchasing factors of production from the households who\nare the owners of these factors (land, labour, capital and entrepreneurship).\n• The factor inputs are used along with the non-factor inputs (raw material, etc.) for the production\nof goods and services. Goods and services are produced for the satisfaction of human wants.\nThere is no end to human wants. Accordingly, there is no end to the process of production.\nPhase of Income Generation (also called the Phase of Distribution)\n• For rendering their factor services to the producers, the households get factor payments.\nThe payments can be in the following forms:\n• rent for land,\n• interest for capital,\n• wages/salaries for labour, and\n• profit for entrepreneurship.\n• From the viewpoint of the households, these are factor incomes. Thus, in phase-2 of circular flow,\nthere is generation of income (or distribution of income) as a consequence of the production of\ngoods and services in phase-1. Now value addition is converted into factor income.\nPhase of Disposition/Expenditure\n• Purchase of final goods by the households leads to consumption expenditure. Purchase of final\ngoods by the producers leads to investment expenditure. Thus, in phase-3, there is disposition\n(or expenditure) of income as a consequence of the generation of income in phase-2.\n43","file":"macro - 1 + 2/IMG_0512.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0513","image":"pages/IMG_0513.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\n2.7.3 Summing-up the Three Phases\n• The three phases of circular flow assume an un-ending circularity as under:\n• Production or goods and services leads to income generation; income generation leads\ndisposition of income; disposition of income generates demand for goods and services wh\nonce again leads to the production of goods and services.\n• Thus, there is a non-stop flow of production of goods and services, generation of income a\ndisposition of income. Each flow keeps chasing the other flow, which is why it is called crue\nflow.\n• Considering the three phases together, we find that in a two sector economy:\nProduction (the value of goods and services) = Income generated = Expenditure (in terms of Conl\nThis is called triple identity. This is illustrated through a 2-sector circular flow model as in fig 21\nSale of Goods and Services (Value Addition) (R 10 crore)\nExpenditure on Goods\nand Services (R 10 crore)\nPRODUCER\nSECTOR or FIRMS\nHOUSEHOLD\nSECTOR\nSale of Factor Services: Land, Labour,\nCapital, Entrepreneurship (R 10 crore)\nFactor Payments: Rent Wages, Interest, Profit (2 10 crore)\nFig. 2.5: Circular Flow Model in a Two Sector Economy\nAssumptions of the Model\n* The circular flow model, as in Fig. 2.5, is based on the following assumptions:\n(i) There are only two sectors in the economy namely, households and producers.\n(i) The households spend their entire income, so that there are no savings.\n(ill) ne domestic economy is a closed ecceomy, so that there are no exports and import\n(iv) There is no government in the domestic economy.\nObservations\n• This model offers the following observations:\n() Factor payments by firms = Value addition by the firms.\nThus, value adition is converted into factor incomes (=R 10 crore).\n(i Total production of goods and services by firms = Total expenditure on goods and services by\nthe household sector.\nhus, all income is converted into expenditure on goods and services (= & 10 crore)\n(il) Value addition = Income generate)d = Expenditure on goods and services\nHence, the triple identity.\nSome Basic Concepts of Macroeconomics\n• Money flows are just the monetary expression of real flows. Briefly, one can observe from the\nCircular Flow Model that:\n• Value added is converted into income and income is converted into expenditure.\n• It is this conversion process which keeps the circular flow always in a state of circularity.\n2.7.4 Significance of Circular Flow Model\n• Study of the circular flow of income is important due to the following reasons:\n(1) Estimation of National Income: Circular flow model facilitates the estimation of national\nincome. National income is the sum total of factor incomes (rent + profit + wages + interest)\nflowing from producers to households of a country.\nIt may also be defined as the market value of the goods and services flowing from producers\nto other sectors of the economy. Further, it may be defined as the sum total of expenditure\non the goods and services produced by the producer sector.\n(2) Knowledge of Intersectoral Interdependence: A circular flow model helps understand\ninterdependence among different sectors of the economy. We learn how consumers are\ndependent on producers and vice versa.\n[Note: Circular flow of income is also called circular flow of money.]\n3 AVLR 2.5\nCIRCULAR FLOW OF INCOME (TWO SECTOR ECONOMY)\nABILITY ZONE\nSignificance of the Distinction between Intermediate Goods and Final Goods\n• From the viewpoint of national income estimation, the difference between intermediate goods and final\ngoods is very significant.\n• We know, intermediate goods are those goods which are purchased by one firm from the other either\nfor resale or for use as raw material in the production of other goods and services. When these goods\nare used as raw material in the production of final goods, the value of intermediate goods is reflected\nin the value of final goods.\n• To illustrate, firm A bought cloth for * 5,000 to be used as raw material in the production of shirts.\n• The shirts were sold for ‹ 7,000. This value of? 7,000 includes the value of cloth, viz., 7 5,000.\nTherefore, in the calculation of national income, the value of only final goods, that is, shirts of\n* 7,000 would be included.\n• The value of cloth (<5,000) is the value of intermediate goods (= intermediate cost or intermediate\nconsumption) and hence, will not be included in national product/national income. If we include\nthe value of intermediate goods also, the value of national product would add up to * 12,000\n(* 7,000 + F 5,000). It causes double counting. Because, the value of cloth * 5,000) is already\nincluded in the value of shirts (<7,000).\nThus, in the calculation of national product/national income, the value of only final goods is taken into\naccount.","file":"macro - 1 + 2/IMG_0513.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0514","image":"pages/IMG_0514.jpg","text":"46\nIntroductory Macroeconomics\nExpansion and contraction oCircular Flow: Injections and Leakage\n•A circular flow as cale circular tow or income may expand or contract. Expansion of crois\nthe other hand, implies a fall in the level of income/output in the economy.\nof income implies a rise in the lever of in theme/output in the economy. Contraction of circular fl\nExpansion of circular flow occurs owing tar injections', while contraction occurs owing to 'withdra\nInjections\nThese are those macro variables, a rise in the level of which leads to a rise in the level of outin,\neconomy. These include:\n) government consumption expenditure,\n(ii) government investment expenditure, and\n(il) exports (expenditure on domestic product by the foreigners).\n• A rise in the level of any of these variables implies a rise in the level of demand for the goods produce,\nin the domestic economy. This is expected to cause a rise in the level of output in the economy. This\ncalled expansion of circular flow.\nLeakages/Withdrawals\nThese are those macro variables, a rise in the level of which leads to a fall in the level of output in the\neconomy. These include:\n) saving (opposite of consumption),\n(il) government taxes (opposite of government investment), and\n(il) imports (opposite of exports).\n• A rise in the level of any of these variables implies a fall in the level of demand for the domestically produced\ngoods. This leads to a fall in the level of output in the economy. This is called contraction of circulario.\nThe circular flow of income remains stable if 'leakages' are exactly equal to 'injections.\nWhy does the Circular Flow of Money never Stop?\nThis may be explained in terms of the twin demand-supply relationship' between producers and the\nhouseholds. Households will always demand goods and services from the producers, and will avays\nsupply them their factor services.\n• It is by selling their factor services that the households earn money to buy goods and services.\n• On the other hand, producers will always demand factor services from the households, and will alvays\nsupply them the goods and services.\n• it is only by selling the goods and services to the households that the producers earn money to\nbuy factor services.\nhuman beings. Accordingly, the circular flow of money never stops.\nPower Points [Summary of the Chapter]\n• Classification of Goods\n• Final Goods are those goods which have crost ed the boundary line of production, and are read\nTor use by their final users. For example shoes used by the households, or tractor used by th\nTarmers, Final goods must lead to either final consumption expenditure or investment expenditure\nin the economy. Final goods are of two types:\n() Consumption Goods: Consumption goods are those goods which are directly used for the\nsatisfaction of human wants. Example: Milk and ice cream used by the households.\nSome Basic Concepts of Macroeconomics\n(ii) Capital Goods: Capital goods are fixed assets of the producers and are repeatedly used in\nthe process of production. These are durable-use producer goods and are of high value.\nExample: Plant and machinery.\n• Intermediate Goods are those goods which are purchased by one firm from the other for resale or\nfor use as raw material in the production of other goods. Example: Wood used in the production\nof chairs.\n• Consumption Expenditure is the aggregate consumption expenditure in the economy. It has three\ncomponents: (i) Consumption expenditure by the households, (ii) Consumption expenditure by the\ngovernment, (iii) Consumption expenditure by the non-profit private institutions.\n• Investment is a process of adding to the stock of capital. It has two components:\n(i) Fixed Investment: Fixed Investment is addition to the stock of fixed assets of the producers\nduring an accounting year.\n(i) Inventory Investment: Inventory Investment is addition to the stock of inventory with the\nproducers during an accounting year.\n• Gross Investment is the expenditure on the purchase of fixed assets and expenditure on the inventory\nstock of the producers during the accounting year.\n• Net Investment refers to increase in the stock of capital during an accounting year.\nNet Investment = Gross investment - Depreciation\nDepreciation (also called consumption of fixed capital) refers to loss of value of fixed assets in use,\non account of: (i) normal wear and tear, (ii) accidental damages, and (il) expected (or foreseen)\nobsolescence.\n• Depreciation Reserve Fund refers to that fund which the producers keep to cope with depreciation\nlosses in the process of production.\n• Stocks are the quantities which are measured at a point of time. Example: Your balance in the bank\naccount as on January 1, 2026.\nFlows are the quantities which are measured over a specified period of time. Example: Your income\nper month.\nL Four Sectors of the Economy\n• Household Sector refers to consumers engaged in the consumption of goods and services.\n• Producing Sector refers to all producing units (or firms) in the economy.\n• Government Sector refers to government as a welfare agency (engaged in maintaining law and\norder, defence, and other services of public welfare), It also refers to government as a producer.\n• The External Sector also called 'Rest of the World Sector', is engaged in the export and import of\ngoods and the flow of capital between the domestic economy and rest of the world.\n• Circular Flow of Income refers to the circularity of the flows of production, income and expenditure\nacross different sectors of the economy.\n• Real Flows refer to the flow of goods and services across different sectors of the economy.\n• Money Flows refer to the flow of money (in terms of receipts and payments) across different\nsectors of the economy.\n• Significance: Circular flow models are significant as they; (i) facilitate estimation of national\nincome, and (i) show interdependence among different sectors highlighting circularity of\nintersectoral flows.","file":"macro - 1 + 2/IMG_0514.HEIC"}],"boxes":[{"title":"[Note: For details on production boundary refer Critical Terms and Concepts on page 26.]","page":"IMG_0503","text":"In case (i): Paper is not out of the production boundary, and is not ready for use by its final\nusers (households). It is being used as a raw material and therefore continues to be within\nthe production boundary.\n• In economics, a good which has crossed the boundary line of production and is ready for use by\nits final users is called 'Final Good'.\n• On the other hand, a good which has not crossed the boundary line of production and is not ready\nfor use by its final users is called 'Intermediate Good'.\nExample:\nLet us assume that during the year 2024-25, all paper mills in the country have produced\n100 tonnes of paper. Also, assume that, out of 100 tonnes, 50 tonnes of paper is used by the\nhouseholds, and 50 tonnes is used by the producers as a raw material in the production of\nbooks and notebooks.\n- As used by the households, 50 tonnes of paper will be considered as 'Final Good' (Because\nit has reached its final users by crossing the boundary line of production).\n- As used by the producers, 50 tonnes of paper will be considered as Intermediate Good.\n(Because ithas not reached its final users and is yet to cross the boundary line of production).\n• Thus, we are classifying paper not on the basis of its physical characteristics, but on the basis of\n• Final Goods are further classified as:\n(i) Consumption Goods/Consumer Goods, and\n(i) Capital Goods, depending upon their end-users or final users.\ncase end-users of final goods are households, tdsse goods are classied as consumer goods\nis tend for the direct satisfaction of their wants. For example\nIce cream and milk as used by the households.\n1. Production boundary -an imaginary line around topold luction sector which depicts the nature of goods\nproduced in this sector.\nSome Basic Concepts of Macroeconomics\n• In case end-users of final goods are producers, these are classified as capital goods. These are\ngoods used by the producers as their capital assets. These are repeatedly used in the process of\nproduction over a long period of time. For example, Tractor used by a farmer in the cultivation\nof land.","image":"pages/IMG_0503.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS","page":"IMG_0503","text":"End-use Classification of Goods\n• End-use classification of goods is that classification which classifies goods on the basis of their\nend-use and end-users, rather than their physical characteristics.\n- Goods are classified as 'Final Goods' when goods have crossed the boundary line of production, and\nare ready for use by their final users.\n- Goods are classified as 'Intermediate Goods' when goods are within the boundary line of production\nand are not ready for use by their final users.\n- Good are classified as 'Consumer Goods' when goods are used for the direct satisfaction of households.\n- Goods are classified as 'Capital Goods' when goods are used as fixed assets by the producers.\nFlow Chart showing\nEnd-use Classification of Goods\nIntermediate Goods\n- within the boundary line of production\n- not ready for use by their final users\nFinal Goods\n- beyond the boundary line of production\n- ready for use by their final users\n(a) Consumption/\nConsumer Goods\n(used by the households\nfor the direct satistaction\n(b) Capital Goods\n(used as capital assets\nby the producers, ie. goods\nused in the production of\nother goods\n[Note: (i) End-user of the same good can be a producer or a consumer.\nExample:\n- a sewing machine used by a household. Here, machine is a consumer good.\n- a sewing machine used by a tailor. Here, machine is a capital good.\n(ii) All goods purchased by the government for defence services are treated as intermediate goods.]\n2.1.1 Final Goods\n• Final goods are those goods which have crossed the boundary line of production and are ready\nfor use by their final users.\n• Who are the final users?\n• These are (i) consumers, and (i) producers.\n• How are Final Goods classified?\n• Final goods are classified as: (i) final consumer goods, and (ii) final producer goods.\n(i) Final consumer goods are finally purchased by the consumers for the satisfaction of\ntheir wants.\n(ii) Final producer goods are finally purchased by the producers and are generally used as\nfixed assets in the process of production.\n25","image":"pages/IMG_0503.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS","page":"IMG_0504","text":"Production Boundary\n• Production boundary is an imaginary line around the production sector which depicts the nature\nof goods produced in this sector.\n• It is used to distinguish final goods from intermediate goods.\nFLOW CHART DEPICTING PRODUCTION BOUNDARY\nWood supplier\ncuts timber and\nsells it to a\npulp manufacturer\n(T 500)\nPulp manufacturer\nproduces paper\npulp and sells it to\n(·900)\nPaper mill\nproduces paper sheets\nand sells them to\na notebook manufacturer\n(1,300)\nmanufacturer\nproduces notebooks\nand sells them\nto consumers\n(· 1,800)\nWhat do we mean when we say that final goods have crossed the boundary line of production?\n• We mean that these goods are out of the process of production or the process of value addition,\nand have reached their final users.\nA shirt in a retail showroom is all set for sale to its final user. No value is to be added to the\nshirt by way of stitching, ironing, packaging, etc.\nIs it to be treated as a final good?\n\"No' should be the answer. Because:\nThe final stage of value addition occurs only when the shirt is finally sold to the consumer\n(the final user), If the retailer buys the shirt for & 800 and sells to the consumer for 7 1,000,\nthere is value addition of & 200.\nTo repeat, the value addition process is completed only when the product reaches its final\nuser: it may be a shirt purchased by the consumer, or a tractor purchased by a farmer (Both,\nthe consumer and the farmer are the final users).\n• You may note that only final goods are included in the estimation of national product or\nnational income.\n2.1.1 (a) Consumption Goods or Consumer Goods\n• Consumption goods (or consumer goods) are those goods which are directly used for the\nsatisfaction of human wants.\nExample:\nIce cream and milk as used by the households. These goods are consumed by the households\nwhen purchased.\n26\nSome Basic Concepts of Macroeconomics\n• Consumption goods are broadly classified into four categories, as given in the flow chart below:\nConsumption Goods/Consumer Goods\nDurable\nGoods\n• Durable consumption\ngoods are those goods\nwhich can be used for\nseveral years and are of\nrelatively high value.\n• These goods are\nrepeatedly used before\nbeing discarded as\nuseless.\n• Examples: LED TV,\nlaptop, car, scooter,\nwashing machine, etc.\nGoods\n• Semi-durable\nconsumption goods are\nthose goods which can\nbe used for a period of\none year or slightly more.\n• These goods are not of\nvery high value.\n• Examples: Clothes,\nfurniture, crockery,\nelectric goods, etc.\nNon-durable\nGoods\n• Non-durable or single-use\nconsumption goods are\nthose goods which are\nused-up in a single act of\nconsumption.\n• For example, the bread\nthat you eat is used-up in a\nsingle act of consumption.\nThe same bread cannot be\nused again. Also these\ngoods are of relatively low\nNon-material\nGoods or Services\n• Services are those\nnon-material goods\nwhich directly satisfy\nhuman wants.\n• Examples: Services of a\ndoctor, lawyer, domestic\nhelp, etc.\n• Examples: Ink, ice cream,\nmilk, petrol, etc.\n2.1.1 (b) Capital Goods\n• Capital goods are fixed assets of the producers. Plant and machinery is an example of capital\ngoods. As fixed assets, capital goods are repeatedly used in the process of production for several\nyears and are of high value. Even nuts and bolts are used for several years, but these are not\ncapital goods as these are of low value. Only those fixed assets of the producers are taken as\ncapital goods which are used in the process of production for several years and which are of\nhigh value.\n• Also, capital goods involve depreciation. Depreciation refers to loss of value of fixed assets (in\nuse) owing to their wear and tear.\n• These goods are used by the producers either for:\n(i) the replacement of the existing capital stock, or for\n(ii) addition to the capital stock.\nDID YOU KNOW\nAll Machines are not Capital Goods, for example:\n• A sewing machine in a tailoring shop is a fixed asset of the tailor; it is a capital good.\n• But the same machine with a consumer household is not a capital good. It is simply a durable-use\nconsumer good.\n• Likewise, a car with a tourist company is a capital good. But the same car with a household is a\ndurable-use consumer good.\n• Thus, while identifying goods as capital goods, we must make sure about their end-user.\n- If the end-user of a durable good is a household consumer, it is durable-use consumer good.\n- On the other hand, if the end-user of a durable good is a producer, it is a capital good.\nFinally, capital goods are only those durable goods which are used as producer goods, not as\nconsumer goods.","image":"pages/IMG_0504.jpg"},{"title":"DID YOU KNOW","page":"IMG_0504","text":"All Machines are not Capital Goods, for example:\n• A sewing machine in a tailoring shop is a fixed asset of the tailor; it is a capital good.\n• But the same machine with a consumer household is not a capital good. It is simply a durable-use\nconsumer good.\n• Likewise, a car with a tourist company is a capital good. But the same car with a household is a\ndurable-use consumer good.\n• Thus, while identifying goods as capital goods, we must make sure about their end-user.\n- If the end-user of a durable good is a household consumer, it is durable-use consumer good.\n- On the other hand, if the end-user of a durable good is a producer, it is a capital good.\nFinally, capital goods are only those durable goods which are used as producer goods, not as\nconsumer goods.","image":"pages/IMG_0504.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"IMG_0505","text":"Q. 1. All producer goods are not capital goods. Why?\nAns. Producer goods include:\n(i) goods used as fixed assets, like plant and machinery, and\n(ii) goods used as raw material, like wood used to make furniture.\nCapital goods include only fixed assets of the producers. These are durable-use producer goods.\nOn the other hand, goods used as raw material are single-use producer goods. These are not\nrepeatedly used in the process of product\ndingly, all producer goods are not capital goods.\n28\nSome Basic Concepts of Macroeconomics\nQ. 2. Classify the following as capital good or consumer good:\n(i) Car purchased by a driver to be used as a taxi\n(i) Cloth lying unsold with a trader at the end of an year\n(ili) Sugar used by households\n(iv) Services of a doctor or a lawyer used by a household\n(v) Laptop purchased by a household\n(vi) Machine purchased by a sugar mill\nAns. Capital Good: (i), (ii) [as a part of capital stock], (vi)\nConsumer Good: (iii), (iv), (v)\n2.1.2 Intermediate Goods\n• Intermediate goods are those goods\n(i) which have yet not crossed the boundary line of production,\n(ii) value is still to be added to these goods, and\n(iii) which are yet not ready for use by their final users.\n• In other words, intermediate goods are those goods which are purchased by one firm from the\nother firm: (i) as raw material, or (ii) as goods for resale.\nExample:\n(i) Wood purchased by a carpenter (from a timber merchant) for making chairs is an\nintermediate good. Because, wood is used as a raw material for making chairs. Value is\nto be added to wood by converting it into chairs.\n(ii) Shirts purchased by firm X from firm Y for resale are intermediate goods. Because, value\nis to be added to the shirts through resale.\n• Non-inclusion in National Income: Value of intermediate goods ultimately becomes a part of the\nvalue of final goods.\n• Accordingly, intermediate goods are not included in the\nestimation of national product or\nnational income.\n• Otherwise, there would be duplication in the estimation of national product, called 'Double\nCounting' (counting the value of a good more than once).\nExample:\nWhen a carpenter buys wood worth 10,000 and converts it into chairs worth & 20,000,\nthen the value of chairs (final goods) includes the value of wood (intermediate good). If we\nadd R 10,000 (wood) + R 20,000 (chairs), the total becomes & 30,000. Thus, leading to double\ncounting ofF 10,000.\n• Expenditure on Intermediate Goods: Expenditure on intermediate goods by the producers\nduring an accounting year is called intermediate consumption or intermediate cost.\nSame Good May be Final or Intermediate\n• It is not possible to name one set of goods as final goods and another set as intermediate goods.\nThe same good may be final or intermediate good. The distinction depends on the end-use of the\ngoods.\n29","image":"pages/IMG_0505.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"IMG_0506","text":"Q. 3. How would you find out whether a particular expenditure is an expenditure on intermediate\ngoods or on final goods?\nAns. Expenditure on final goods must lead to:\n(i) final consumption expenditure (C), or\n(il) investment expenditure (I).\nThe expenditure which does not lead to C and I (like the expenditure on raw material) is to be\ntreated as an expenditure on intermediate goods. Expenditure on intermediate goods leads to\nintermediate consumption or intermediate cost.\n30\nSome Basic Concepts of Macroeconomics\nQ. 4. Purchase of a car always means the purchase of a final good. Do you agree?\nAns. No. It depends on the end-use of the car. If it is purchased by a household, it is a final good. It is\nlike a consumer durable.\nIf it is purchased by taxi-operators, then again it is a final good, as it is to be finally used by the\nproducer as a fixed asset.\nHowever, if the car is purchased by a retail dealer from the factory for the purpose of resale, it is\nto be treated as intermediate good.\nQ. 5. Classify the following as final good or intermediate good (apply end-use as the basis of\nclassification):\n(i) Paper purchased by a publisher\n(i) Coal purchased by a factory\n(iii) Books purchased by a bookseller\n(iv) Furniture purchased by a school\n(v) Use of electricity to make products\n(vi) Car purchased by a household\n(vii) Seeds purchased by a farmer\n(viii) LED TV purchased by a dealer\n(ix) Milk purchased by a hotel\n(x) Machine purchased for installation in a factory\n(xi) Exhaust fans used for making water coolers\n(xii) Postage, stamps purchased by a consumer\n(xili) Sewing machine purchased by a homemaker\n(xiv) Soft drinks purchased by a school canteen\n(xv) Microwave oven purchased by a consumer household\nAns. Final Good: (iv), (vi), (x), (xii), (xiii), (xv)\nIntermediate Good: (i), (i), (iii), (v), (vi), (viii), (ix), (x), (xiv)","image":"pages/IMG_0506.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS","page":"IMG_0507","text":"• Desired inventory stock refers to planned inventory stock. This is maintained by the producers to meet\nthe future demand.\n• Undesired inventory stock, on the other hand, refers to unplanned inventory stock. It arises because\ndemand for the product turns out to be lower than expected. Unplanned inventory stock leads to losses.\n& AVLR 2.2\nFIXED INVESTMENT vs. INVENTORY INVESTMENT\n2.3.3 Gross Investment, Net Investment and The Concept of Depreciation\n• Gross investment refers to total production of capital goods during the year.\n• This includes\n(i) capital goods used for the replacement of existing\ncapital stock (which is worn-out), and\n+ Depreciation\n- Depreciation\n(ii) capital goods used as a net addition to the existing\nInvestment\ncapital stock.","image":"pages/IMG_0507.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS","page":"IMG_0508","text":"A low level equilibrium trap is a situation when:\n• low income causes low demand,\n• low demand, in turn, causes low output;\n• low output, once again causes low income.\nirap\nExpected and Unexpected Obsolescence\n• Expected obsolescence has two components:\n(i) Loss of value of fixed assets when these become obsolete/outdated owing to change in\ntechnology.\nA plant producing black and white TVs becomes obsolete when technology is\ndiscovered to produce colour TVs.\n(ii) Loss of value of fixed assets when these become obsolete/outdated owing to change in demand.\nA plant producing rubber shoes becomes obsolete when demand shifts from rubber\nshoes to leather shoes.\n• Expected obsolescence is estimated by the producers on the basis of their knowledge and\nexperience of the market conditions.\n• Unexpected obsolescence occurs owing to\n(i) natural calamities (like earthquake, floods or fire), and\n(ii) fall in market value of the assets when there is economic recession.\n• Loss of value of fixed assets owing to unexpected obsolescence is called 'capital loss'. These\nlosses are not a part of depreciation or depreciation reserve fund.\n[Note: Only expected obsolescence is considered for the estimation of depreciation, not the unexpected","image":"pages/IMG_0508.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"IMG_0509","text":"Q. 6. How does higher rate of net capital formation lead to higher level of productivity/efficiency of\nAns. Higher rate of net capital formation implies greater availability of capital (in terms of machines)\nper unit of labour. Aided by machines, efficiency of labour definitely increases. This precisely is\nthe reason why labour in developed countries (like USA) is more efficient than in less developed\ncountries like India.\nQ. 7. Distinguish between depreciation and depreciation reserve fund.\nAns. Depreciation is the loss of fixed assets in use on account of: (i) normal wear and tear,\n(ii) accidental damages, and (iii) expecte\nseen obsolescence.\n36\nSome Basic Concepts of Macroeconomics\nOn the other hand, depreciation reserve fund is a provision of funds to cope with depreciation\nlosses. These funds are used for the replacement of fixed assets when these are worn-out or\nwhen these become obsolete/outdated.\nQ. 8. What is current replacement cost?\nAns. It refers to the estimated value of depreciation for all the producing units in the economy, during\nthe period of an accounting year.","image":"pages/IMG_0509.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"IMG_0510","text":"Q. 9. Classify the following as stock or flow variables:\n(i) Wealth\n(ii) Expenditure of money\n(ill) Number of births\n(iv) Income\n(v) Interest on capital\n(vi) Water in the overhead tank\n(vii) Capital formation\n(vili) Quantity/Supply of money in a country\n(ix) Bank deposits\n(x) Capital\n(xi) Rice stored in a godown\n(xii) Sale of rice as on January 1, 2026\n(xili) Distance between Delhi and Mumbai\n(xiv) Change in the supply of money as on January 1, 2026\n(xv) Labour force in India as on March 31, 2025\n(xvi) Leakage of water from the overhead tank\n(xvii) Population of a country at a point of time\n(xviii) Speed of a car going from Delhi to Mumbai\nAns. Stock: (i), (vi), (viii), (ix), (x), (xi), (xii), (xili), (xiv), (xv), (xvii)\nFlow: (ii), (iii), (iv), (v), (vii), (xvi), (xviii)\n38\nSome Basic Concepts of Macroeconomics\nQ. 10. Are the following Stocks or Flows? Explain with a reason.\n(i) Investment, (ii) Monetary Expenditure, (iii) A Hundred Rupee Note, (iv) A Family's\nConsumption of Sugar, (v) Services of a Tutor, (vi) Production of Cement, (vii) Machinery of a\n(i) Investment: It is a flow concept because it is related to a period of time.\n(ii) Monetary Expenditure: It is a flow concept because it is related to a period of time.\n(ii) A Hundred Rupee Note: It is a stock concept because it is a component of supply of money.\n(iv) A Family's Consumption of Sugar: It is a flow concept because consumption relates to a\nperiod of time.\n(v) Services of a Tutor: It is a flow concept because it is related to a period of time.\n(vi) Production of Cement: It is a flow concept because it is related to a period of time.\n(vii) Machinery of a Sugar Mill: It is a stock concept because it relates to a point of time.\nQ. 11. Based on the following headlines, identify whether each item is a stock or flow variable:\n(a) \"Gross GST revenue collections reached & 2.1 lakh crore in April 2025, reflecting strong\neconomic activity and better compliance under GST 2.0.\"\n(b) \"The GST Council has approved a rationalised two-rate structure under GST 2.0-5% and\n18%—to simplify taxation and reduce the burden on consumers.\"\nAnS.\n(a) Flow variable-Because GST revenue is collected over a period of time (one month, April\n2025).\n(b) Stock variable-Because the GST rate structure exists at a specific point in time and does\nnot depend on a time period.\nQ. 12. Are exports and imports stock variables or flow variables?\nAns. Certain concepts in economics are studied only as flow variables, not as stock variables. Exports\nand imports are always flow variables.","image":"pages/IMG_0510.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"IMG_0512","text":"Q. 13. Money flows are opposite to real flows. How?\nAns. Money flows are opposite to real flow: a decause money flows are in response to the real fu\nFor example, there is a real flow of goods and services tom the producers to the househ\nIt is in response to it, that the nousentias make pant te producers. So that, man\nflows from the housenolds to producers in terms of consumption expenditure. Likewise, th\nis a real flow of factor services from the housenolds to the producers. It is in response tot,\nthe producers make payments to the noused olds. so that, money flows from producers to\nhouseholds in terms of factor payments.\n[Note: Students are advised to draw Fig. 2.2 and 2.3 in support of their answer.]\n0. 14. Classify the following transactions into Real Flow or Money Flow. Give reasons for your ansie\n(i) A firm pays 750,000 as wages to its workers.\n(i) Workers render their labour services to the firm.\ni) A household buys groceries worth R5,000 from a supermarket.\n(iv) The supermarket delivers groceries to the household.\n(v) The government pays subsidies to farmers for purchasing fertilizers.\nAns. Real Flow: (i, (iv)\nMoney Flow: (i), (iii), (v)","image":"pages/IMG_0512.jpg"},{"title":"ABILITY ZONE","page":"IMG_0513","text":"Significance of the Distinction between Intermediate Goods and Final Goods\n• From the viewpoint of national income estimation, the difference between intermediate goods and final\ngoods is very significant.\n• We know, intermediate goods are those goods which are purchased by one firm from the other either\nfor resale or for use as raw material in the production of other goods and services. When these goods\nare used as raw material in the production of final goods, the value of intermediate goods is reflected\nin the value of final goods.\n• To illustrate, firm A bought cloth for * 5,000 to be used as raw material in the production of shirts.\n• The shirts were sold for ‹ 7,000. This value of? 7,000 includes the value of cloth, viz., 7 5,000.\nTherefore, in the calculation of national income, the value of only final goods, that is, shirts of\n* 7,000 would be included.\n• The value of cloth (<5,000) is the value of intermediate goods (= intermediate cost or intermediate\nconsumption) and hence, will not be included in national product/national income. If we include\nthe value of intermediate goods also, the value of national product would add up to * 12,000\n(* 7,000 + F 5,000). It causes double counting. Because, the value of cloth * 5,000) is already\nincluded in the value of shirts (<7,000).\nThus, in the calculation of national product/national income, the value of only final goods is taken into\naccount.","image":"pages/IMG_0513.jpg"},{"title":"Power Points [Summary of the Chapter]","page":"IMG_0514","text":"• Classification of Goods\n• Final Goods are those goods which have crost ed the boundary line of production, and are read\nTor use by their final users. For example shoes used by the households, or tractor used by th\nTarmers, Final goods must lead to either final consumption expenditure or investment expenditure\nin the economy. Final goods are of two types:\n() Consumption Goods: Consumption goods are those goods which are directly used for the\nsatisfaction of human wants. Example: Milk and ice cream used by the households.\nSome Basic Concepts of Macroeconomics\n(ii) Capital Goods: Capital goods are fixed assets of the producers and are repeatedly used in\nthe process of production. These are durable-use producer goods and are of high value.\nExample: Plant and machinery.\n• Intermediate Goods are those goods which are purchased by one firm from the other for resale or\nfor use as raw material in the production of other goods. Example: Wood used in the production\nof chairs.\n• Consumption Expenditure is the aggregate consumption expenditure in the economy. It has three\ncomponents: (i) Consumption expenditure by the households, (ii) Consumption expenditure by the\ngovernment, (iii) Consumption expenditure by the non-profit private institutions.\n• Investment is a process of adding to the stock of capital. It has two components:\n(i) Fixed Investment: Fixed Investment is addition to the stock of fixed assets of the producers\nduring an accounting year.\n(i) Inventory Investment: Inventory Investment is addition to the stock of inventory with the\nproducers during an accounting year.\n• Gross Investment is the expenditure on the purchase of fixed assets and expenditure on the inventory\nstock of the producers during the accounting year.\n• Net Investment refers to increase in the stock of capital during an accounting year.\nNet Investment = Gross investment - Depreciation\nDepreciation (also called consumption of fixed capital) refers to loss of value of fixed assets in use,\non account of: (i) normal wear and tear, (ii) accidental damages, and (il) expected (or foreseen)\nobsolescence.\n• Depreciation Reserve Fund refers to that fund which the producers keep to cope with depreciation\nlosses in the process of production.\n• Stocks are the quantities which are measured at a point of time. Example: Your balance in the bank\naccount as on January 1, 2026.\nFlows are the quantities which are measured over a specified period of time. Example: Your income\nper month.\nL Four Sectors of the Economy\n• Household Sector refers to consumers engaged in the consumption of goods and services.\n• Producing Sector refers to all producing units (or firms) in the economy.\n• Government Sector refers to government as a welfare agency (engaged in maintaining law and\norder, defence, and other services of public welfare), It also refers to government as a producer.\n• The External Sector also called 'Rest of the World Sector', is engaged in the export and import of\ngoods and the flow of capital between the domestic economy and rest of the world.\n• Circular Flow of Income refers to the circularity of the flows of production, income and expenditure\nacross different sectors of the economy.\n• Real Flows refer to the flow of goods and services across different sectors of the economy.\n• Money Flows refer to the flow of money (in terms of receipts and payments) across different\nsectors of the economy.\n• Significance: Circular flow models are significant as they; (i) facilitate estimation of national\nincome, and (i) show interdependence among different sectors highlighting circularity of\nintersectoral flows.","image":"pages/IMG_0514.jpg"}],"subject":"macro","number":"2"},{"id":"3","title":"National Income and Related Aggregates","intro":"National income is the sum total of factor incomes earned by normal residents of a country during the period of an accounting year.","lessons":[{"title":"National income, factor incomes and transfer incomes","body":"National income is the sum total of factor incomes earned by normal residents of a country during the period of an accounting year. Factor incomes are rewards for factor services: compensation of employees, rent, interest and profit. Transfer incomes such as gifts, charity and old-age pensions are unilateral receipts without a current productive service in return; they are excluded. An employer’s retirement pension earned through service differs from an old-age welfare pension.","formula":"","source":"IMG_0515","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"3.1 CONCEPT OF NATIONAL INCOME"},{"title":"Normal residents and domestic territory","body":"A normal resident ordinarily resides in a country and has a centre of economic interest there. Citizenship alone does not determine residence. Tourists and visitors for medical treatment do not become residents merely through travel. Diplomats remain residents of their home countries; border workers are residents where they live. Domestic territory is economic territory: Indian embassies abroad belong to India’s domestic territory; foreign embassies in India do not. Check the Ability Zone for ships, aircraft, fishing vessels and other special cases.","formula":"","source":"IMG_0516","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"3.1.3 Normal Residents · 3.2.2 Domestic Territory"},{"title":"Domestic income versus national income","body":"Domestic income is the sum total of factor incomes generated within the domestic territory, whether earned by residents or non-residents. National income belongs to normal residents, wherever earned. Net factor income from abroad is factor income received from the rest of the world minus factor income paid to the rest of the world. Remittances that are gifts are transfers, not NFIA.","formula":"NNP at FC = NDP at FC + NFIA\nNFIA = Factor income from abroad − Factor income to abroad","source":"IMG_0517","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"3.2 DOMESTIC AND NATIONAL CONCEPTS OF INCOME"},{"title":"The three conversion bridges","body":"Use three independent adjustments. Gross to net: subtract depreciation. Domestic to national: add NFIA. Market price to factor cost: subtract net indirect taxes, which equal indirect taxes minus subsidies. Reverse the sign when travelling backwards. A negative NFIA reduces national income; negative NIT means subsidies exceed indirect taxes. Do not deduct indirect taxes again if NIT is already supplied.","formula":"NNPFC = GDPMP − Depreciation − NIT + NFIA\nGDPMP = NNPFC + Depreciation + NIT − NFIA","source":"IMG_0519","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"3.3 GROSS AND NET · 3.4 MARKET PRICE AND FACTOR COST"},{"title":"Eight related aggregates","body":"GDPMP, GDPFC, NDPMP and NDPFC are domestic measures; GNPMP, GNPFC, NNPMP and NNPFC are national measures. Gross measures include consumption of fixed capital; net measures exclude it. Market-price measures include net indirect taxes; factor-cost measures exclude them. National income means NNPFC; domestic income means NDPFC. Identify all three labels before substituting a number.","formula":"","source":"IMG_0520","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"3.5 AGGREGATES RELATED TO NATIONAL INCOME"},{"title":"Nominal GDP, real GDP and GDP deflator","body":"Nominal GDP is valued at current-year prices; real GDP is valued at base-year prices. Real GDP helps isolate changes in physical production from changes in prices. Nominal GDP may rise with unchanged output simply because prices rise. The GDP deflator is the ratio of nominal GDP to real GDP multiplied by 100. Use the price index as a percentage index, not as a decimal, in the formula below.","formula":"Nominal GDP = Σ(Current price × Current quantity)\nReal GDP = Σ(Base-year price × Current quantity)\nGDP deflator = Nominal GDP ÷ Real GDP × 100\nReal GDP = Nominal GDP ÷ GDP deflator × 100","source":"IMG_0523","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"3.6 NOMINAL AND REAL GDP"},{"title":"GDP and welfare: why output is an incomplete measure","body":"A rise in real GDP can expand available goods and services, but it does not ensure an equal rise in welfare. The textbook highlights distribution of income, composition of GDP, non-monetary transactions and externalities. Unequal distribution can leave most people without gains; more military goods may not improve consumption; unpaid household work is omitted; pollution imposes an unpriced cost. Positive externalities are benefits not paid for; negative externalities are costs not compensated.","formula":"","source":"IMG_0525","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"3.7 GDP AND WELFARE"},{"title":"Special cases of residents and territory","textbookHeading":"ABILITY ZONE · Normal Residents of a Country · Components of Domestic Territory","body":"Normal Residents: citizens of another country can be residents here when their centre of economic interest is here; officials and diplomats serving abroad remain residents of their home country; border workers are residents where they live. Domestic Territory: include a country’s embassies, consulates and military establishments abroad, ships and aircraft operated by its residents between countries, and fishing vessels and floating platforms operated by residents in international waters under the accounting conventions. Exclude foreign embassies and international organisations located inside its political borders. Residence and territory are separate tests.","source":"IMG_0526","kind":"Ability Zone","formula":""}],"pages":[{"id":"IMG_0515","image":"pages/IMG_0515.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\n3.1 CONCEPT OF NATIONAL INCOME\n3.1.1 Meaning of National Income\n• National income is the sum total of factor incomes earned by normal residents of a country\nduring the period of an accounting year. This dehniton of national income conveys two\nimportant points:\n(i) national income includes factor incomes only, and\ni) national income includes income of only the normal residents of a country.\nLet us go deeper into the concepts of (i) factor incomes, and (i) normal residents.\n3.1.2 Concept of Factor Incomes\n• Factor incomes are the payments made by the producing units (firms) to the households (owners\nof the factors of production) for the use of their factor services.\n• Factor incomes (or factor payments) are broadly classified as under:\n(i) Compensation of employees (received by the households for rendering their services as\nemployees of the producing units).\n(i) Rent (received by the households for the use of their land by the producing units).\n(i) Interest (received by the households for the use of their capital by the producing units).\n(iv) Profit (received by the households for the use of their entrepreneurial skills by the producing\n• In the estimation of national income, we include only these factor incomes or factor\npayments).\nCRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS\nFactor Payment = Factor Income\n• Factor payments and factor incomes are two ways of looking at the same concept. Hence, these are\nused interchangeably.\n• Factor payments refer to payments made by the producers to the households for the use of their factor\n• Factor incomes on the other hand refer to the income (payment) received by the households for\nrendering their factor services to the producers.\nFactor Incomes are Different from Transfer Incomes\n• Transfer incomes are those incomes which are received by a person as help, donation, charity\n* As transter incomes are unearne a countrye, i.e., not earned as rewards for rendering facto\nservices, these are not included in the estimation of national income.\nNational Income and Related Aggregates\nTable 3.1: Factor Incomes vs. Transfer Incomes\nBasis of Difference\n1. Definition\nFactor Incomes\nTransfer Incomes\n- It refers\nincome\nreceived\nby the\nhouseholds from the producers for providing\nIt refers to those incomes which are received\nby a person in the form of help, donation or\ntheir factor services.\ncharity.\n2. Nature\n- It is a reward for rendering factor services.\n- It is not a reward for rendering factor services.\n3. Concept\n- It is a bilateral (two-sided) payment involving\n- It is a unilateral (one-sided) payment without\nmutual benefit from the transaction.\nanything in return.\n4. Component of\n- These are included in national income.\n- These are not included in national income.\nNational Income\n5. Components\n- Rent, wages, interest, profits.\n- Gifts, donation, charity, etc.\n3.1.3 Concept of Normal Residents\nWho are normal residents of a country?\nA normal resident is said to be one:\n• who ordinarily resides in the country concerned (for one year or more), and\n• whose centre of economic interest lies in that country.\nHere, note these observations carefully:\n(i) A person residing in a country for a period of one year (or more) is taken as 'ordinarily\nresiding' in that country. This person may or may not be the citizen of that country.\n(ii) A person is said to have his economic interest in a country when he considers that country\nas a central place of all his economic activities (production, consumption and investment).\nExamples:\nIndians working in foreign embassies in India.\nIndians employed in World Health Organisation located in India.\nThe foreign citizens living in India for a period of more than one year (other than\nthose for studies or medical treatment).\n[Note: National income includes income of only the normal residents of a country. It does not include\nincome of the non-residents, even when they happen to be the citizens of our country.\n(Check Ability Zone for more detail on the concept of normal residents of a country.)]\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\nQ. 1. Profits earned by a company in India, which is owned by a non-resident is included in national\nincome of India? Is it true?\nAns. No. Because this is the income which does not belong to the normal residents of India.\nNote:\n• Profits earned by a company in India, which is owned by a non-resident is a part of domestic factor\nincome of India. This is because the company is generating profit within the domestic territory of India.\n• However, these profits are considered a part of income from domestic product accruing to rest of the\nworld. Therefore, these are not reflected in the estimation of national income.\n• These are deducted from domestic income to find national income.\n1. Economic interest - interest related to such activities as of production, consumption and investment.","file":"macro 3/IMG_0515.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0516","image":"pages/IMG_0516.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\n9. 2. Which of the following are to be treated as normal residents of India?\n(i) Indian officials working in Indian embassy in China.\n(ii) A Chinese tourist who stays in India for two months.\niii) Indians going to Sri Lanka for watching a cricket match.\n(iv) Foreigners working in World Health Organisation located in India.\n(v) Foreigners visiting India for medical treatment.\n(vi) An American citizen visiting India for attending a business conference.\n(vii) Foreigners working in Indian embassies in Canada and Japan.\n(vil) Indian workers crossing the borders regularly to work in Nepal.\n(ix) Ambassador of India in rest of the world.\n(x) A German working as the Director of the office of International Monetary Fund located in hoa\n(xi) Some foreign technical experts working in India for a period of less than one year.\n(xii) Local people of India working in the office of International Monetary Fund in India.\nAns. Normal Residents of India: (i), (iii, (viii), (ix), (xii)\nDID YOU KNOW\n• Domestic income of a country includes factor incomes of both the residents and non-residents\nworking within the domestic territory of a country.\n• Net factor income from abroad is the difference between\nFactor income earned by our residents who are temporarily residing abroad, and\nFactor income earned by non-residents who are temporarily residing in our country.\n3.2 DOMESTIC AND NATIONAL CONCEPTS OF INCOME\n• At the macro level, the concept of income is used both as a domestic concept and a nationa\nconcept. When used as a domestic concept, we call it domestic income, and when used as t\n• Both domestic income and national income include the four basic elements of factor income, viz.\n(ili) interest, and\n(iv) profit.\n3.2.1 Difference between the Concepts of\nDomestic Income and National Income\nThe difference between the two concepts is as follows:\n• Domestic income NDPrd Is the sum total of factor incomes (compensation of employees + rer\n+ interest + profit generated within the domestic territory of a country (no matter who generate\nit: normal residents or non-residents).\n• National Income (NNfo IS the sum total of factor incomes (compensation of employees + res\n+ interest + profit) earned by normal residents of a country (no matter where it is generate\nwithin the domestic territory or outside).\nHere, it becomes important to understand the concept of domestic territory of a country.\n74\nNational Income and Related Aggregates\n3.2.2 Concept of Domestic Territory of a Country\n• In common language, the domestic territory of a country is understood to mean political territory\nof a nation. But, in economics, it refers to 'economic territory' which is a much wider concept than\npolitical territory.\n• According to United Nations, \"Economic territory is the geographical territory administered by a\ngovernment within which persons, goods and capital circulate freely!\"\n• The above definition raises two critical questions:\nDo we have this freedom of circulation (of persons, goods and capital) in the embassies of foreign\ncountries located in India?\nThe answer is 'No'.\nDo we have this freedom of circulation (of persons, goods and capital) in Indian embassies located\nabroad?\nThe answer is \"Yes'.\nAccordingly, we conclude that foreign embassies located in India are not a part of domestic/\neconomic territory of India. On the other hand, Indian embassies located abroad are a part of\ndomestic/economic territory of India.\nThus, while defining the term domestic territory, the focus is NOT to be on political boundaries of a\nnation but on the geographical area where our persons, our goods and our capital enjoy the freedom\nof circulation.\n[Check Ability Zone for the components of domestic territory.]\n3.2.3 Conversion of Domestic Income into National Income\nBefore we understand how to convert domestic income into national income and vice versa, it is\nimportant to note that:\n• our domestic income includes factor income earned by non-residents within the domestic\nterritory of our country,\n• our domestic income does not include factor income earned by our residents from the domestic\nterritories of other countries.\nAccordingly, domestic income becomes national income provided:\n• We exclude from domestic income that part of factor income which belongs to non-residents within\nour domestic territory, and\n• We add to domestic income that part of factor income which our residents earn from rest of the\nworld (or from the domestic territories of other countries).\n- NFIA\nNational Income\n(NNPFc)\nDomestic Income\n(NDPFC)\nImplications:\n• National income = Domestic income + NFIA\n• Domestic income = National income - NFIA\n• NFIA\n= National income\n- Domestic income\n+ NFIA\nFig. 3.1: Conversion between National Income and Domestic Income\n75","file":"macro 3/IMG_0516.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0517","image":"pages/IMG_0517.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\n2Ý TACKLING CONVERSICIN BETWEEN DOMESTIC INCOME AND NATIONAL INCOME\nillustration 1. Given: Nahonal Income = 3,400, NFIA = ‹ 400. Find Domestic Income.\nDomestic Income = $ 5,400 - $ 400\nlustration 2. Given: Domestic Income = 7 4,300, NFIA = 7 200. Find National Income.\nNational Income = 74,300 + 7 200\nlustration 3. Given: National Income = 7 5,000, Domestic Income = F 4,000. Find NFIA.\nNFIA = · 5,000 - 2 4,000\nThe Concept of NFIA\nNet Factor Income from Abroad (NFIA) refers to the difference between factor income received fro\nthe rest of the world and factor income paid to the rest of the world. The conversion of domes\nincome into national income and vice versa is based on this concept.\n• Formula of NFIA:\nNet Factor Income from Abroad\n= Factor income earned by our residents from rest of the world\n- Factor income earned by non-residents in our domestic territory\n3 AVLR 3.1\nDOMESTIC INCOME vS. NATIONAL INCOME\nThe following block enlists various illustrations related to the calculation of NFIA.\nS TACKLING NFIA\nlustration 4. Given: Factor income trom abroad = 1,000, Factor income to abroad = R 700. Find NFIA.\nNFIA = R 1,000 - K700 = 8 300.\nWiustration 5. Given: Factor income from abroad = R 550, Factor income to abroad = ‹ 750. Find NFIA.\nNFIA = R 550 - 2750 = (-) ≤ 200.\nIllustration 6. Given: Factor income to abroad = R 320. Find NFIA.\nNFIA =0 - R 320 = (-) R 320.\n(lustration 7. Given: Factor income to abroad = (-) R 570. Find NFIA.\n3.2.4 Domestic Income and Domestic Product are Identical Concepts\n• There is no difference between the concepts of 'domestic income' and 'domestic product. In fac\n(Domestic Income = Domestic Product]\nthe two terms are identical to each other. Because, all production is ultimately converted int\nfactor incomes.\nNational Income and Related Aggregates\n• Since, domestic income (the sum total of factor incomes) is identical to domestic product. We can\nwrite that:\nDomestic Income = Domestic Product\n• Accordingly, the equation that:\n• can also be written as:\nDomestic income + Net factor income from abroad = National Income\nDomestic product + Net factor income from abroad = National Product\n3 AVLR 3.2\nTREATMENT OF ITEMS IN INDIA'S DOMESTIC AND NATIONAL INCOME\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\nQ. 3. Which of the following are the elements of domestic territory of India?\n(i) A French Company in India.\n(ii) A branch of Microsoft Office in India.\n(iii) A company in India owned by a Chinese.\n(iv) Office of Reliance Industries in USA.\n(v) Tata rented its building to Microsoft in New York.\n(vi) Branch of a foreign bank in India.\n(vii) Branch of Punjab National Bank in China.\n(viii) Indian embassy in Russia.\nAns. Domestic Territory of India: (i), (ii), (iii), (vi), (viii)\nQ. 4. Write two observations indicating the difference between domestic income and national\nincome.\nBasis of\nDifference\n(i) Meaning\nAns. Following observations indicate the difference between domestic income and national income:\nDomestic Income\nNational Income\n(NDP Fc)\n(NNPFc)\n• It is the sum total of factor incomes\n• It is the sum total of factor incomes\ngenerated within the\ndomestic\naccruing to normal residents of a\nterritory of a country, no matter who\ncountry, no matter where this income\ngenerates this income-residents or\nis generated-within the domestic\nnon-residents.\nterritory or in rest of the world.\n(ii) Inclusion of NEIA\n• It does not include net factor income\n• It includes net factor income from\nfrom abroad.\nabroad.\nQ. 5. An ambassador in US embassy in India stays in his job for a period exceeding one year.\nWould he be treated as a resident or non-resident of India? Give reason.\nAns. (i) Resident of a country is defined as a person who normally resides in the country (or does\nnot leave the country for a period exceeding one year) and whose centre of interest lies in\nthe country concerned.","file":"macro 3/IMG_0517.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0518","image":"pages/IMG_0518.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\nwe, terre delta rules of thumb in and me accounting sea\npractices rather than any logic). One such rule of thumb is that the foreign diplomat\nambassadors continue to be treated as non-residents even if their stay exceeds one yea\n(il) Moreover, centre of interest of such diplomats continues to be in the country they be\nto. Accordingly, Us ambassador in India would be treated as non-resident of India, t\nwhen his stay in India exceeds the period of one year.\n3.3 GROSS AND NET CONCEPTS OF DOMESTIC PRODUCT\n• Domestic product is measured as\n(i) Gross Domestic Product (GDP) or\n(i) Net Domestic Product (NDP).\n3.3.1 Understanding about Depreciation as the Reason for Difference.\nbetween the Two Aggregates\n• Depreciation (Consumption of Fixed Capital) causes the difference between the two. While cal\nincludes depreciation, NDP does not.\nThus,\n- Depreciation\nGross Domestic Product\n(GDP)\nNet Domestic Product\n(NDP)\nImplications:\n• GDP = NDP + Depreciation\n• NDP = GDP - Depreciation\n• Depreciation = GDP - NDP\n+ Depreciation\nFig. 3.2: Conversion between GDP and NDP\n2S TACKLING CONVERSION BETWEEN GDP AND NDP\nlustration 8. Given: GDP = R 2,300, Depreciation = 7 300. Find NDP.\nNDP=72,300 - 2300 = 22,000.\nWustration 9. Given: NDP = 7 3,300, Depreciation = 7 200. Find GDP.\nGDP = R +R = =R 3,500.\nWustration 10. Given: GDP - 7 5,500, NDP = Z 5,000. Find Depreciation.\nDepreciation = 7 5,500 - R 5,000 = 8 500.\nLewise, depreciation Causes the difference between GNP and NP, as illustrated below\n2S TACKLING CONVERSION BETWEEN GNP AND NNP\nWustration 1. Given: GP = 2 3,300, Depreciation = R 400. Find NP.\nNN = 7 3,300 - 2 400 = 7 2,900.\nNuration 12. Given: NP = 7 4,200, Depreciation = 7 500. Find GNP,\nGNP - 2 4,200 + 7 500 = 2 4,700.\nMustration 13. Given: GIP - 2 6,500, NP = 2 6.000. Find Depreciation.\nDepreciation - 7 6,500 - R 6,000 = R 500.\nNational Income and Related Aggregates\n[Note: The term 'depreciation' has been used in several ways like:\n(i) Consumption of fixed capital.\n(ii) Current replacement cost.\n(iii) Replacement cost of fixed capital.\n(iv) Capital consumption allowance.\nBut, irrespective of the term used, depreciation always refers to the 'loss of value of fixed assets in\nThe illustrations given below present two cases associated with the calculation of depreciation when\nit is not given directly.\nF TACKLING DEPRECIATION\nIllustration 14. Calculate 'Depreciation on Capital Asset' from the following data:\nItems\n(i) Capital value of the asset\n(ii) Estimated life of the asset\n· 2,000 crore\n20 years\n(iii) Scrap value\nCapital value of the asset - Scrap value\nSol.\nDepreciation on Capital Asset =\nEstimated life of the asset\n· 2,000 crore -0 =\n· 2,000 crore\n20 years\n20 years\n= · 100 crore.\nIllustration 15. Calculate 'Depreciation on Capital Asset' from the following data:\nSol.\n(i) Estimated life of the asset\n(ii) Capital value of the asset\n(iii) Scrap value\n20 years\n· 3,500 crore\n3 500 crore\nCapital value of the asset - Scrap value\nDepreciation on Capital Asset =\nEstimated life of the asset\nF 3,500 crore - & 500 crore\n20 years\n‹ 3,000 crore\n= * 150 crore.\n20 years\n3.4 DOMESTIC PRODUCT AT MARKET PRICE AND AT FACTOR COST\n3.4.1 Understanding about NIT as the Reason for Difference between\nthe Two Aggregates\n• There is no difference between domestic product at market price and domestic product at factor\ncost in a two sector economy, which includes:\n(i) producer sector, and (i) household sector.\n• No 'government sector' in the economy implies that there are no taxes or subsidies related to the\nproduction of goods and services.","file":"macro 3/IMG_0518.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0519","image":"pages/IMG_0519.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\n• Once, the government sector is introcaced, taxes and subsidies affect domestic product at\nprice and domestic product at factor cost differently. his is how it happens:\n• Taxes on goods (called indirect taxes) tend to raise the market price of the goods. Accorda\ndomestic product at market price is increased.\n• Subsidies tend to lower the market price of the goods. Accordingly, domestic product ma\nprice is reduced.\n• To restore ine parity between domestic product at market price and domestic product at f\ncost:\ni) We deduct the value of indirect taxes from domestic product at market price, and\n(i) we add the value of subsidies to domestic product at market price.\n• It is with this adjustment that the two aggregates are equated.\nMarket Price\nFactor Cost\nImplications:\n• MP = FC + NIT\n• FC = MP - NIT\n• NIT = MP - FC\n*NIT = Net Indirect Taxes,\ni.e., Indirect Taxes - Subsidies\n+ NIT\nFig. 3.3: Conversion between MP and FC\nThe same rule of conversion between 'market price' and factor cost' applies with respect to i\naggregates of Domestic Product (gross or net) and National Product (gross or net).\nDomestic Product (gross/net) at market price - Net indirect taxes\n= Domestic Product (gross/net) at factor cost\nNational Product (gross/net) at market price - Net indirect taxes\n= National Product (gross/net) at factor cost\nGiven below are some illustrations related to conversion between MP and FC.\n2 TACKLING CONVERSION BETWEEN MP AND FC\nIllustration 16. Given: MP = R 500, NIT = R 300. Find FC.\nFC = R 500 - R 300\n=· 200.\nIllustration 17. Given: FC = R 200, NIT = R 150. Find MP.\nMP =‹ 200+2 150\n- 7 350.\nIllustration 18. Given: FC - 7 200, MP = 7 300. Find NIT.\nNIT = K 300 - R 200\nHere, it becomes important to understand the concept of Nil and Its estimation.\nNational Income and Related Aggregates\nConcept of NIT\n• Net Indirect Tax (NIT) refers to the difference between indirect taxes and subsidies?.\n• Formula for NIT\nNet Indirect Taxes (NIT) = Indirect taxes (IT) - Subsidies\n[Note: If NIT and IT both are given in the numerical question, use NIT directly.]\nGiven below are some illustrations where NIT needs to be estimated given IT and Subsidies.\nỮ TACKLING NIT\nIllustration 19. Given: IT = 0, Subsidies = 7 500. Find NIT.\nSol.\nNIT = 0 - 7500 = (-) 7 T500.\nIllustration 20. Given: IT= 350, Subsidies = 0. Find NIT.\nSol.\nNIT = 7 350 - 0 = 7 350.\nIllustration 21. Given: IT = 300, Subsidies = & 150. Find NIT.\nNIT = 7 300 - 7 150 = 7 150.\n3.5 AGGREGATES RELATED TO NATIONAL INCOME\n• Economic performance of a country is measured on the basis of goods and services it produces in\none financial year. This estimation is based on a host of aggregates that depict this value in money\nterms.\nGiven below is a brief description about these aggregates.\n(1) Gross Domestic Product at Market Price [GDP Mp]: Gross domestic product at market price\nis the market value of final goods and services produced within the domestic territory of a\ncountry during the period of an accounting year, inclusive of depreciation.\nCRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS\nGDP MP-Break up of the Aggregate\nGROSS\nDOMESTIC\nPRODUCT\nMARKET PRICE\nTotal Value\ninclusive of\ndepreciation\nWithin the domestic\nterritory of the\nFinal goods\nand services\nproduced\nPrice at which\ngoods are sold in the\nmarket, It includes\nthe impact of indirect\ntaxes and subsidies\n(2) Net Domestic Product at Market Price [NDP Mp]: Net domestic product at market price is\nthe market value of the final goods and services produced within the domestic territory of a\ncountry during the period of an accounting year, exclusive of depreciation.\n2. Indirect taxes - sales tax, excise duty, GST are some of the alternative terms used in place of indirect taxes.\nBasically, indirect tax refers to the tax on the production or sale of goods and services by the producers.\n3. Subsidies - financial assistance or financial compensation to the producers in lieu of their commitment to sell\ntheir product at the 'reduced price. Price is reduced to facilitate purchase of a particular product by poorer\nsections of the society.\n81","file":"macro 3/IMG_0519.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0520","image":"pages/IMG_0520.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\n• Relating GDP Mp and NDPMp, we can write that:\nGDP MP = NDPMP\n+ Depreciation\nand\nNDP MP = GDP MP\n- Depreciation\n(3) Gross National Product at Market Price [GNP Mp): Gross national product at market pio-\nthe sum total of gross domestic product at market price and net factor income from alone\nGNP MP = GDP MP\n+ Net factor income from abroad\n(4) Net National Product at Market Price [NN Mp]: Net national product at market price ste\nsum total of net domestic product at market price and net factor income from abroad.\nNNP MP = NDP MP\n+ Net factor income from abroad\n(5) Gross Domestic Product at Factor Cost [GDP c]: Gross domestic product at factor cost is te\nsum total of factor cost incurred on the production of final goods and services within the\ndomestic territory of a country (during an accounting year), inclusive of depreciation.\nGDP Fc = Compensation of employees\n+ Interest\n+ Profit\n+ Depreciation\n(6) Net Domestic Product at Fact aN (9) [NDPc or Net Domestic Income: Net domestic product\nat factor cost is the sum total of factorcost incurred on the production of final goods a\nservices with the domestic territory of a country, during an accounting year.\nNDP Fc = Compensation of employees\n+ Rent\n+ Interest\n+ Profit\nComponents\nof Factor Cost\n• Relating GDPrc and NDPro We can write that:\nGDP FC = NDP FC\nand\n+ Depreciation\nNDPFC = GDPFC\n- Depreciation\n(7) Gross National Product at Factor Cost [GNPrc): Gross national product at factor cost is the\nsum total of gross domestic product at factor cost and net factor income from abroad\nGNPFC = GDPFO\n+ Net factor income from abroad\nNational Income and Related Aggregates\n(8) Net National Product at Factor Cost [NNPrc]: Net national product at factor cost is the sum\ntotal of net domestic product at factor cost and net factor income from abroad.\nNNPFC = NDPFC\n+ Net factor income from abroad\n• We know that,\nNDPFc = Compensation of employees\n+ Rent\n+ Interest\n+ Profit\n• Accordingly,\nNNPFc = Compensation of employees\n+ Rent\n+ Interest\n+ Profit\n+ Net factor income from abroad\nSteps involved in Conversion of Aggregates:\nConversion of Aggregates\nFIND OUT\nGIVEN\nGDPMP\nGDPMP - Depreciation + NFIA - NIT\ni.e.,\nNNPFC = GDP MP - Depreciation + NFIA - NIT\nStep 1: Write the aggregate whose value is given on right hand side and the aggregate whose value\nis to be found on the left hand side.\nLet us say given GDPMP, we need to estimate NNPFC-\nIn our example, gross needs to converted to net, domestic to national and market price to\nfactor cost.\nStep 3: Use the three basic conversion formulae given below and then make necessary adjustments\nto find the respective aggregate.\nThree Basic Conversion Formulae\nNet\n= Gross - Depreciation\nNational = Domestic + NFIA\nFC\n= MP - NIT\n83","file":"macro 3/IMG_0520.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0521","image":"pages/IMG_0521.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\nThe equation in our example will be:\nNNPFC = GDP MP\n- Depreciation\n+ NFIA\n- NIT\nGiven below is a list of cases related to conversion of aggregates.\nBasic Conversion of Aggregates\nCase 1: Given: GDP Mp find NDP MP-\nCase 2: Given:\nGDP MP, find GNP MP.\nNDP MP = GDP MP - Depreciation\nGNPMP = GDP MP + NFIA\nCase 3: Given: GNP Mp find NP MP-\nCase 4: Given: GDP Mp, find GDPFC.\nNNP MP = GNP MP- Depreciation\nGDPFC = GDP MP - NIT\nCase 5: Given:\nGDP Fo. find NDP FC.\nCase 6:\nGiven:\nGDPFC, find GNPFC •\nNDP FC = GDP Fc - Depreciation\nGNPFC = GDPFC + NFIA\nCase 7: Given:\nGNPFc, find NNFFC -\nCase 8:\nGiven:\nGNP MP. find GNPFC.\nNNFFC = GNPFc - Depreciation\nGNPFC = GNP MP - NIT\nLet us understand the conversion between aggregates with the help of the following examples.\nEXAMPLES\nExample 1.\nS.No.\nFrom the following data, calculate Gross Domestic Product at Market Price:\nItems\nNet domestic product at factor cost\nDepreciation\nIndirect taxes\nSubsidies\n( in crore)\n13,500\n1,000\n1,300\n1,200\nSolution:\nGross Domestic Product at Market Price\n= Net domestic product at factor cost + Depreciation + Net indirect taxes\n= R 13,500 crore +* crore crore + (R crore crore - R1,200 crore)\n= * 13,500 crore + * 1,000 crore + ‹ 100 crore\n= · 14,600 crore\nAns. Gross domestic product at market price = * 14,600 crore.\n[Note: Net indirect taxes = Indirect taxes - Subsidies\n= R 1,300 crore - & 1,200 crore\n= ‹ 100 crore]\nNational Income and Related Aggregates\nExample 2.\nFrom the following data, calculate Net Domestic Product at Factor Cost:\nrems\nS.No.\n(ili)\n(iv)\nDepreciation\nGross national product at factor cost\nFactor income from abroad\nFactor income to abroad\n(R in crore)\n16,000\n42,000\n600\n400\nSolution:\nNet Domestic Product at Factor Cost\n= Gross national product at factor cost - Depreciation - Net factor income from abroad\n= · 42,000 crore - F 16,000 crore - (R 600 crore - 7 400 crore)\n= · 42,000 crore - F 16,000 crore - 7 200 crore\n= · 25,800 crore\nAns. Net domestic product at factor cost = 7 25,800 crore.\n[Note: Net factor income from abroad\n= Factor income from abroad - Factor income to abroad\n= F 600 crore - 7 400 crore\n= · 200 crore]\nExample 3.\nFrom the following data, estimate Gross Domestic Product at Factor Cost:\nS.No.\n(& in crore)\n910\n(iii)\n(iv)\nGross national product at market price\nConsumption of fixed capital\nNet indirect taxes\nSubsidies\nNet factor income from abroad\n8\n(-) 70\nSolution:\nGross Domestic Product at Factor Cost\n= Gross national product at market price - Net indirect taxes - Net factor income from\nabroad\n= R 910 crore - F 130 crore - (-) * 70 crore\n= F 910 crore - * 130 crore + 7 70 crore\n= T 850 crore\nAns. Gross domestic product at factor cost = 7 850 crore.","file":"macro 3/IMG_0521.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0522","image":"pages/IMG_0522.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\nCalculate Gross Domestic Product at Market Price with the help of the following data:\nItems\nS.No.\n(iv)\nGross national product at factor cost\nConsumption of fixed capital\nFactor income received from abroad\nFactor income paid to abroad\nNet indirect taxes\nR incrore,\nSolution:\nGross Domestic Product at Market Price\n= Gross national product at factor cost - Net factor income from abroad + Net indirecte.-\n= · 600 crore - (R 400 crore - 7 200 crore) + 7 40 crore\n= · 600 crore - & 200 crore + F 40 crore\n= R 440 crore\nAns. Gross domestic product at market price = 7 440 crore.\nExample 5.\nFind NNPMp from the following data:\nS.No.\nItems\n(R in crore)\n3,850\n(iii)\n(iv)\n(vi)\n(vil)\nGDP FC\nNet investment\nFactor income from abroad\nTaxes on production\nFactor income to abroad\nGross investment\nSubsidies\n320\n525\nSolution:\nNNP MP\n= GDPc Depreciation (Gross Hagg=ment - Net investment) + NIT (Taxes on productio\n-Subsidies) + Net factor income from abroad (Factor income from abroad - Factor income\n=K 3,850 crore - R 525 crore - ‹ 325 crore) + (R 720 crore - 7 80 crore) + ( 720 cror\n- R 320 crore)\n= { 3,850 crore - ‹ 200 crore + R 640 crore + & 400 crore\n= R 4,690 crore\nAns. NPMP =R4,690 crore.\n86\nNational Income and Related Aggregates\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\nQ. 6. Identify the missing terms in the following equations:\n(i) NNPFc = NNPMP -\n(iii) NDPFC =\n(iv) GNPMP =\n= National Income - Domestic Income.\n- Depreciation.\n+ GDPMP-\n= NDP MP + Depreciation + NFIA.\nAns.\n(i) Net indirect taxes\n(ii) Net factor income from abroad\n(iii) GDPFc\n(iv) Net factor income from abroad\n(v) GNPMP\nQ. 7. Fill in the blanks using >, ‹ or = symbol for the following situations:\nSituation\n(i) NFIA =0\n(ii) Subsidies > Indirect Taxes\n(ili) Factor income to rest of the world\nResult\n: National Income\n: GDPFC\n: NNPFC\nGDP MP -\nNDPFC•\n> Factor income from rest of the world\n(iv) NIT = 0\n(v) - ve NFIA\n: GDPFC.\n: GDP FC.-\nGDP MP -\nGNP FC.\nAns.\n(i) =, (i) >, (i) <, (iv) =, (v) >\nDomestic Income\n3.6 NOMINAL AND REAL GDP\n3.6.1 Concept of Nominal GDP\n• Nominal GDP refers to GDP at current prices. It is the market value of the final goods and services\nproduced within the domestic territory of a country during an accounting year, as estimated\nusing the current year prices*.\n*Current year prices are the prices prevailing during the year of estimation.\n• Thus:\nNominal GDP = Q × P\nHere, Q= Quantity of final goods and services\nproduced during an accounting year\nP = Prices prevailing during the accounting year.\n• The above equation shows that nominal GDP can increase when there is increase in either Q or P.\nIf it increases owing to increase in Q (P remaining constant), it shows increase in nominal GDP\nowing to increase in output.","file":"macro 3/IMG_0522.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0523","image":"pages/IMG_0523.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\n• On the other hand, if it increases owing to increase in P (Q remaining constant), it shows incre\nin nominal GDP owing to increase in the general price level. Caused by increase in 'p'\nGOP is merely a monetary increase in GDP, and is of little significance.\nincreas\n• It does not cause any increase in the flow of goods and services in the economy. It only care\n\"money illusion'- the illusion of a higher market value of the given output.\n3.6.2 Concept of Real GDP\n• Real GDP refers to GDP at constant prices. It is the marker value of the final goos and ser\nproduced within the domestic territory panpod try auring an accounting year, as estima\nusing the base year* prices.\n* Base year is the year of comparison. It is the year when macro variables like production-\ngeneral price level) are believed to be within their normal range.\n• Thus:\n| Here,\nReal GDP = Q× P*\nQ = Quantity of final goods and services produce\nduring an accounting year\np* = Prices prevailing during the base year.\n• The above equation shows that real GDP increases only when Q increases. Simply because P\nconstant. This implies that when real GDP increases, there is an increase in the flow of good sar\nservices in the economy.\n• Other things remaining constant, higher the level of real GDP, greater should be the availin\nof goods and services to the residents of a country. Implying that the quality of lite so\nimprove.\n• Table 3:2 on the estimation of nominal GDP illustrates how nominal GDP can rise even wis\noutput is constant.\nTable 3.2: Estimation of Nominal GDP\n[Assumptions: (f) The economy produces wheat, cloth and sugar only, and\n(il) Output remains constant]\nCommodity\nQuantity\nPrice\n(R in crot\nImplication\n2011-12\nGDPMP\n(Nominal)\nWheat\nCloth\nSugar\n20 tonnes\n100 metres\n5 tonnes\n100 per tonne\n5 per metre\n500 per tonne\n2,000\n500\n+ 2,500\n2024-25\nWheat\nCloth\nSugar\n= 5,000\n20 tonnes\n100 metres\n5 tonnes\n1,000 per tonne\n20 per metre\n1,600 per tonne\n20,000\n+ 2,000\n+ 8,000\nThus, nominal\nGDP rises from\n· 5,000 crore to\n· 30,000 crore\neven when output\nis constant. Here,\nrise in nominal\nGDP is driven\nexclusively by the\nrise in price level.\n88\n= 30,000\nNational Income and Related Aggregates\n• Table 3.3 on the estimation of real GDP shows that real GDP rises only when output rises.\nTable 3.3: Estimation of Real GDP\n[Assumptions:\n0) The economy produces wheat, cloth and sugar only, and\n(ii) Prices remain constant)\nCommodity\nPrice\n(in crore)\nImplication\n2011-12\nWheat\nCloth\nSugar\n20 tonnes\n100 metres\n5 tonnes\n100 per tonne\n5 per metre\n500 per tonne\n2024-25\nWheat\nCloth\nSugar\n30 tonnes\n200 metres\n10 tonnes\n100 per tonne\nGDP MP\n(Real)\n2,000\n500\n+ 2,500\n= 5,000\n3,000\nThus, real GDP\nrises from 75,000\ncrore to 79,000\ncrore, even when\nprice level is\nconstant. Here,\nrise in real GDP is\ndriven exclusively\nby the rise in\n500 per tonne\n+ 5,000\n= 9,000\n[Note: The prices of the year 2011-12 are taken as base year prices while estimating the real GDP for the year\n2024-25]\n• The index of real GDP always reflects a change in the level of output, while the index of nominal\nGDP may or may not. Which is why real GDP is considered as a better index of economic growth\nthan the nominal GDP.\nDID YOU KNOW\n• When nominal GDP rises, the flow of goods and services in the economy may or may not rise during\nan accounting year.\n• When real GDP rises, the flow of goods and services in the economy must rise during an accounting\nyear.\nConversion of Nominal GDP into Real GDP\n• If GDP at current prices is divided by the price index of the current year and multiplied by the price\nindex of base year (which is always equal to 100), we obtain GDP at constant prices or real GDP.\nGDP at Current Prices × 100\nReal GDP Or GDP at Constant Prices =\nPrice Index\nIllustration\n• If in 2011, GDP is * 100 crore, and in 2025 it is & 200 crore at the current prices.\n• If price index rises from 100 to 400 within the same period, then GDP at current prices is\nconverted into real GDP (or GDP at constant prices) as in Table 3.4.\nTable 3.4: Conversion of Nominal GDP into Real GDP\nGDP at Current Prices\nIndex Number\nGDP at Constant Prices\n(or Base Year Prices)\n( in crore)\nCurrent Price Index\n(in crore)\n400\n200\n× 100 = 50\n89","file":"macro 3/IMG_0523.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0524","image":"pages/IMG_0524.jpg","text":"introductory Macroeconomics\n• It may be noted that but at current prices in 2025 is twice the GDP in 2011. But, GDP at co\nprices has reduced from 100 in 2010, to 50 in 2025. This is because the index of price in the\n2025 has risen to 400 (compared to 100 in 2011).\n• We may also estate nominal but, real ran price index are known to us, using\nfollowing formula:\nReal GDP x Price Index\nNominal GDP =\n100\nLet us see some examples related to this concept.\nExample 1.\nFind Nominal GDP if Real GDP = 240 and Price Index = 120.\nSolution:\nNominal GDP = Real GDP × Price Index\n100\n= 240 × 120\n- = 288\n100\nAns. Nominal GDP = 288.\nExample 2.\nIf Nominal GDP is 600 and Price Index is 120, calculate the Real GDP.\nSolution:\nReal GDP = Nominal GDP\nPrice Index\n× 100\n× 100 = 500\nAns. Real GDP = 500.\nCRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS\nGDP Deflator\n• It refers to the ratio between GDP at current prices and GDP at constant prices. It is expressed as under.\nGDP Deflator =\nGDP at Current Prices\nGDP at Constant Prices\n-x 100\nGDP Deflator =\nNominal GDP\nReal GDP\n• It shows change in GDP due to change in price level. It is the same as price index.\nExample. If Real GDP = 600 and Nominal GDP = 660, find GDP Deflator (Price Index).\nSolution: GDP Deflator (Price Index) = Nominal GDP × 100 = 660\nReal GDP\n600 ×100=110\nIt shows increase in the general price level by 10%.\n[Note: - GDP deflator should not be confused with Consumer Price Index, even when both indicate change\nin the price level. The basic difference is as under:\n- GDP deflator indicates change in price index or change in price level related to all goods and\nservices produced within the domestic territory of a country.\nNational Income and Related Aggregates\n- Consumer Price Index indicates change in price index or change in price level related to only a\nspecific basket of goods bought by the consumers, and these may be produced within the\ndomestic territory or in rest of the world.]\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\nQ. 8. Write observations indicating the difference between GDP at current prices and GDP at\nconstant prices.\nAns. Following observations indicate the difference between GDP at current prices and GDP at\nconstant prices:\nBasis of Difference\n(i) Meaning\nGDP at Current Prices\nGDP at Constant Prices\n• It is the market value of the final\n• It is the market value of the final\ngoods and services produced within\ngoods and services produced within\nthe domestic territory of a country\nthe domestic territory of a country\nduring an accounting year,\nduring an\naccounting year,\nestimated at current year prices.\nestimated at base year prices.\n(ii) Impact of Price\nChange\n• It is impacted by the change in price\n• It is not impacted by the change in\nlevel. It increases when the price\nprice level. It increases only when\nlevel increases, even when output is\noutput increases, no matter what the\nconstant.\nprice level Is.\n(iii) Measure of\nWelfare\n• It is not a good measure of welfare of\n• It is a good measure of welfare of\npeople, as it may or may not reflect\npeople, as it always reflects a change\nchange in the level of output.\nin the level of output.\n(iv) Alternate Name • It is also known as nominal GDP.\n• It is also known as Real GDP.\nQ. 9. Why is Real GDP considered a better estimate of national income as compared to Nominal\nAns.\nGDP?\n(i) Real GDP shows the change in the actual level of output, as it is adjusted for inflation. In\ncontrast, Nominal GDP may rise or fall even without any change in output, because it is\naffected by changes in the general price level (inflation or deflation).\n(ii) Real GDP provides a more accurate measure of true economic growth, as it reflects the\nactual increase in goods and services produced, rather than changes caused only by price\nmovements.\nAVLR 3.3\nNOMINAL GDP AND REAL GDP\n3.7 GDP AND WELFARE\n• Real GDP is considered as an index of welfare of the people. Welfare of the people is measured in\nterms of the availability of goods and services per person. Accordingly, increase in real GDP means\nincrease in the level of output in the economy. Other things remaining constant, this means\ngreater availability of goods per person implying higher level of welfare (popularly known as social\nwelfare),","file":"macro 3/IMG_0524.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0525","image":"pages/IMG_0525.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\n• It is because of this reason that 4g planners and politicians in a country always lay\non the growth rate of Gur Higher thegrowin of GDP, greater is the flow of goods and s\nGreater is the availability of goods and services per person.\n3.7.1 Limitations of GDP as an Index of Welfare\nThe positive relationship between Gur and social welfare suffers from certain limitations,\ncan say that there are certain imitations relat, til to GDP as an index of social welfare. These\nunder:\n(1) Distribution of Income: If the distribution of income becomes unequal, GDP growth al\nreflect a rise in social welfare. India is facing this situation at present. While the nure.\nbillionaires is rising, a significant segment of population is struggling for the necessie.\nlife.\nReason: Distribution of income is becoming increasingly unequal.\n(2) Composition of GDP: Composition of GDP may not be welfare-oriented.\nExample:\nIncrease in the production of defence goods does not lead to any direct increase in wea\nof the people. Of course, strong defence offers a peaceful environment in the country. Butt\ncontributes to social welfare only indirectly.\n(3) Non-monetary Exchanges: Non-monetary exchanges refer to economic transaction\nwhere goods, services or resources are exchanged without the use of money as a media.\nof exchange. In rural economies, barter system of exchange still prevails to some exten\nPayments for farm labour are often made in kind rather than in cash.\n• All such transactions remain unrecorded. This causes underestimation of GDP. To te\nextent GDP remains underestimated, it remains an inappropriate index of welfare.\n(4) Externalities: Externalities refer to good and bad impact of an economic activity wits.\npaying the price or penalty for that. There are both positive and negative externalities.\n• Positive externalities occur when, for example, Mr. X maintains a beautiful garden 2c,\nMr. Y (neighbour of Mr. X) enjoys it. It adds to welfare of Mr. Y but he does not pay to\nit. Negative externalities occur when, for example, smoke emitted by factories cause i\npollution or industrial waste is driven Is rivers causing water pollution. It causes a los\nsocial welfare. But, most factories do not pay the penalty.\n• GDP fails to account for the impact of poduce and negative externalities on social welfar\nHence, it is an inappropriate index of welfare.\nThus, there are serious limitations of GDP as an index of welfare. These limitations reduce\nsignificance of GDP as an index of welfare.\nAVLR 3.4\nLIMITATIONS OF GDP AS AN INDEX OF WELFARE\nNational Income and Related Aggregates\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\nQ. 10. What lowers the significance of GDP as an index of welfare?\nAns. The following observations explain how the significance of GDP as an index of welfare is lowered:\n(i) Distribution of Income: GDP as an index of welfare loses significance if the distribution of\nincome turns unequal.\n(ii) Composition of GDP: If luxuries are produced for richer sections of the society and the poor\nsuffer deprivation, GDP growth becomes meaningless.\n(ili) Non-monetary Exchanges/Transactions: Larger the non-monetary transactions, greater the\nunderestimation of GDP as an index of welfare.\n(iv) Externalities: GDP index does not account for externalities: the good and bad impact\nof economic activities without the price or penalty. Environmental pollution related to\nproduction activity is an important example. This also lowers the significance of GDP as an\nindex of welfare.\nQ. 11. \"GDP growth as an index of welfare loses its significance if there is a deep economic divide in\nthe economy.\" State whether true or false and provide a reason for the same.\nAns. True. Economic divide indicates the gulf between the rich and the poor. If the gulf increases, GDP\ngrowth loses its significance.\nABILITY ZONE\nNormal Residents of a Country\n• It is not necessary that normal resident of a country is also a citizen of that country. A person may be a\nnormal resident of one country even when he is a citizen of other country.\n• Normal Residents of a country include:\n• Citizens (and institutions) of the country who normally reside in the country and whose centre\nof economic interest lies in the country.\n• Citizens of other nations who continue to live in the country beyond a period of one year and\nwhose centre of economic interest lies in the country.\n• For example, if an Indian is living in USA for more than one year and his centre of economic\ninterest lies in that country, he would be deemed as a normal resident of USA even when he\ncontinues to be a citizen of India.\n• Citizens of a country working in international organisations (like World Bank and IMF) or foreign\nembassies located in that country.\n• For example, an Indian working in the World Bank office located in New Delhi would be\nconsidered as a normal resident of India.\n• Citizens of a country who are living abroad, but not for a period exceeding one year and whose\ncentre of economic interest lies in their home country.\n• Border workers or persons who cross the border between two countries daily or regularly in\norder to work in one country are the residents of the country in which they live, not of the country\nin which they are working.\n• Officials, diplomats, and members of the armed forces of a foreign country are treated as the\nnormal residents of the country to which they belong, and not of the country in which they are\nemployed.\n93","file":"macro 3/IMG_0525.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0526","image":"pages/IMG_0526.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\n• Normal Residents of a country do not include:\nThe foreighers who visit Country to Havelling, recreation, holidays, medical treatment, su\nconferences, sports, etc.\n• The officials, dipromats and members of armed forces of a foreign country posted in the\n• International organisations located in the country.\n• Crew members of foreign vessels.\n• Foreigners who are employees of non-resident enterprises and who have come to the co\nfor purpose of installing machinery in these enterprises.\n• All these people will be treated as non-residents as generally they stay for less than one yes tr\nconcerned country and their centre of economic interest does not lie in it.\nComponents of Domestic Territory\n• Territory lying within the political frontiers including territorial waters of a country.\n• Ships and aircrafts operated by residents of the country across different parts of the world.\n• For example, Indian ships moving between Japan and Korea regularly or passenger plane\noperated by Air India between England and Canada are a part of the domestic territory of india.\n• Fishing vessels, oil and natural gas rigs and floating platforms operated by the residents of the coun\nin the international waters or engaged in extraction in areas in which the country has the exclusive igh\nof exploitation.\n• For example, the fishing boats operated by Indian fishermen in the international waters ofte\nIndian ocean are a part of the domestic territory of India.\n• Embassies, consulates and military establishments of the country located abroad.\n• For example, the Indian embassy in the United States of America is a part of the domest.\nterritory of India and the embassy of USA in India is a part of the domestic territory of Unite,\nStates of America.\n[Note: It may be noted in this context that domestic territory does not refer to the areas of ownership beyon\npolitical frontiers of a nation. It only refers to areas of operation where our persons, our good ent\nour capital can circulate freely to serve our economic interest. Thus, factor income generated withe\nthe domestic territory of a nation amounts to domestic income.]\nComponents of Net Factor Income from Abroad\n• Net Compensation of Employees\n• It is the difference between compensation (of employees) received by resident worker.\ntemporarily employed abroad and a similar payment made to non-resident workers who are.\nemployed temporarily within the domestic territory of a country.\n• Compensation of employees refers to payments made by the employers to the employees bot\nin cash and kind. It also includes payments made by the employers on behalf of the employees\n(like contribution to employees' provident fund).\n• Net Income from Property and Entrepreneurship\n(other than Retained Earnings of Resident Companies Abroad)\n• It is the difference between the income in the form of rent, interest and profit received by the\nresidents of a country and similar payments made to the rest of the world.\n• Net Retained Earnings of Resident Companies Abroad\n• It is the difference between the retained earnings of resident companies located abroad and\nretained earnings of foreign companies located within the domestic territory of a country.\nNational Income and Related Aggregates\nEstimation of GNP Deflator\n• Using hypothetical figures, GNP deflator is calculated as under:\nNominal GNP\nYear\nQuantities\nYear\nPrices\n(Current Year)\n(2) * (3)\nBase Year\nReal GNP\n(Current Year)\n(2) × (5)\n®\nShirts\nShoes\nBricks\n10\nSum: 192\nSum: 81\nGNP Deflator for Current Year = Nominal GNP for Current Year × 100 = 192 × 100 = 237.04\nReal GNP for Current Year\nConcept of Green GNP\n• GNP (at current or constant prices) is estimated with no regard to (i) environmental pollution, and\n(ii) exploitation of natural resources.\n• If GNP increases along with increase in environmental pollution, the quality of life would be far less\nthan indicated by the index of GNP. Likewise, if GNP increases along with excessive exploitation of the\nnatural resources (so excessive that it reduces availability of resources for the future generations), the\nincrease in GNP would only be misleading as it cannot be sustained in the years to come.\n• Accordingly, it is suggested that GNP index should account for (i) cost in terms of environmental\npollution, and (i) cost in terms of excessive exploitation of natural resources. Estimation of GNP that\naccounts for these parameters (environmental pollution and excessive exploitation of natural resources)\nis called Green GNP.\nNew Concepts Introduced by CSO (Central Statistics Office)\n• In India, national income and related aggregates are defined and estimated by CSO. Since 2015,\nCSO has introduced some new concepts as under:\n• Net Production Taxes:\nNet Production Taxes = Production taxes - Production subsidies\n› Important it is to note that the receipt and payment of production taxes and production\nsubsidies are related to the product, not the volume of production. Land revenue and\nregistration fee may be cited as examples.\n• Net Product Taxes:\nNet Product Taxes = Product taxes - Product subsidies\n> To be noted, product taxes and product subsidies are paid and received per unit of product.\nExcise tax, service tax may be cited as examples.\n• Basic Price\n> Basic price includes production taxes and production subsidies only. Categorically, basic\nprice does not include product taxes and product subsidies.\n• GVA at Basic Price\n> CSO now estimates GVA (gross value added) at basic price. To be noted, GVA at basic price\naccounts only for production taxes and production subsidies. It does not account for product\ntaxes and product subsidies.\n• Difference among GVA at Factor Cost, GVA at Basic Price and GVA at Market Price\nGVA at factor cost + Net production taxes = GVA at Basic Price\nGVA at basic price + Net product taxes = GVA at Market Price","file":"macro 3/IMG_0526.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0527","image":"pages/IMG_0527.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\nPower Points [Summary of the Chapter]\n• National Income is the sum total of factor incomes accruing to normal residents of a con\nnot account for transfer incomes. Important terms associated with this concept:\n• Factor Incomes are the rewards of the factor of production, viz., compensation of ene\nrent, interest and profit.\n• Transfer Incomes are unearned incomes. These include gifts in cash, scholarships to thes\nold-age pensions to the senior, etc. These are not included in the estimation of the atra.\n• Domestic Income is the sum total of factor incomes generated within the domestic ter.\ncountry (no matter it is the income accruing to residents or non-residents of the country.\n• Conversion of Domestic Income into National Income:\nDomestic income + Net factor income from abroad = National Income\n• Net Factor Income from Abroad (NFIA) is the difference between the factor income eared\nresidents from abroad and factor income earned by non-residents in our country.\n• Domestic Income does not include net factor income from abroad.\n• National Income includes net factor income from abroad.\n• Gross and Net Concepts\n• Gross Domestic Product is the market value of final goods and services produced with\ndomestic territory of the country during an accounting year, inclusive of depreciation.\n• Net Domestic Product is the market value of final goods and services produced with\ndomestic territory of the country during an accounting year, exclusive of depreciation.\n• Conversion of GDP into NDP:\nGDP - Depreciation = NDP\n• Market Price and Factor Cost\n• Market Price includes the impact of indirect taxes (taxes on goods and services) and subside.\n• Indirect Tax raises the market price, subsidies tend to lower it.\n• Factor Cost is the cost of factors of production. It is equal to factor payments.\n• Conversion of GDPMp into GDPFC:\nGDPMP - Net indirect taxes = GDPFc\n• Nominal GDP and Real GDP\n• Nominal GDP is the market value of goods and services produced within the domestic trity\na country during an accounting year, as estimated using the current year prices.\n• Real GDP is the market value of goods and services produced within the domestic territory de\ncountry during an accounting year, as estimated using the base year prices.\n• Conversion of Nominal GDP into Real GDP:\nReal GDP = Nominal GDP × 100\n• GDP Deflator is the ratio between nominal GDP and real GDP, It shows change in GDP owing to\nchange in the price level. It is also called price index.\nGDP Deflator = Nominal GDP\nReal GDP\n× 100\n• GDP and Welfare\n• Real GDP is a widely used index of welfare. But, it suffers from certain limitations as it ignores:\n(i) distribution of income\n(il) composition of GDP\n(ill) non-monetary transactions\n(iv) externalities\n96\nWORK\nCompetency Based Leam\n1. Multiple Choice Questi\nChoose the correct option:\n1. \"Real Gross Domestic P\n2023-24. The correspon\nSource: https://pib.go\nOn the basis of the gi\n(a) 295.36\n(c) 169.92\n2. To arrive at the value\nto/1\n(a) depreciation, ade\n(c) net indriect taxe\n3. Identify, which of tr\nIndia.\n(a) Salary to Indian\n(b) Income generat\n(c) Payments to far\n(d) Interest paid by\n4. When goods and se\nas:\n(a) real GDP\n(c) constant prices\n5. To arrive at the val\nadded to Gross Vali\n(a) Product taxes\n(c) Production tax\n6. Identify the correc\n(a) GDPMP = NNPR\n(b) NDPFc = NNPFC\n(c) GNP MP = GDP~\n(d) NNPFc = NDPM\n7. Identify which of t\n(a) Excess of Gros\n(b) Excess of Gros\n(c) Equality betw\n(d) Sum of Gross i","file":"macro 3/IMG_0527.HEIC"}],"boxes":[{"title":"CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS","page":"IMG_0515","text":"Factor Payment = Factor Income\n• Factor payments and factor incomes are two ways of looking at the same concept. Hence, these are\nused interchangeably.\n• Factor payments refer to payments made by the producers to the households for the use of their factor\n• Factor incomes on the other hand refer to the income (payment) received by the households for\nrendering their factor services to the producers.\nFactor Incomes are Different from Transfer Incomes\n• Transfer incomes are those incomes which are received by a person as help, donation, charity\n* As transter incomes are unearne a countrye, i.e., not earned as rewards for rendering facto\nservices, these are not included in the estimation of national income.\nNational Income and Related Aggregates\nTable 3.1: Factor Incomes vs. Transfer Incomes\nBasis of Difference\n1. Definition\nFactor Incomes\nTransfer Incomes\n- It refers\nincome\nreceived\nby the\nhouseholds from the producers for providing\nIt refers to those incomes which are received\nby a person in the form of help, donation or\ntheir factor services.\ncharity.\n2. Nature\n- It is a reward for rendering factor services.\n- It is not a reward for rendering factor services.\n3. Concept\n- It is a bilateral (two-sided) payment involving\n- It is a unilateral (one-sided) payment without\nmutual benefit from the transaction.\nanything in return.\n4. Component of\n- These are included in national income.\n- These are not included in national income.\nNational Income\n5. Components\n- Rent, wages, interest, profits.\n- Gifts, donation, charity, etc.\n3.1.3 Concept of Normal Residents\nWho are normal residents of a country?\nA normal resident is said to be one:\n• who ordinarily resides in the country concerned (for one year or more), and\n• whose centre of economic interest lies in that country.\nHere, note these observations carefully:\n(i) A person residing in a country for a period of one year (or more) is taken as 'ordinarily\nresiding' in that country. This person may or may not be the citizen of that country.\n(ii) A person is said to have his economic interest in a country when he considers that country\nas a central place of all his economic activities (production, consumption and investment).\nExamples:\nIndians working in foreign embassies in India.\nIndians employed in World Health Organisation located in India.\nThe foreign citizens living in India for a period of more than one year (other than\nthose for studies or medical treatment).\n[Note: National income includes income of only the normal residents of a country. It does not include\nincome of the non-residents, even when they happen to be the citizens of our country.","image":"pages/IMG_0515.jpg"},{"title":"(Check Ability Zone for more detail on the concept of normal residents of a country.)]","page":"IMG_0515","text":"","image":"pages/IMG_0515.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"IMG_0515","text":"Q. 1. Profits earned by a company in India, which is owned by a non-resident is included in national\nincome of India? Is it true?\nAns. No. Because this is the income which does not belong to the normal residents of India.\nNote:\n• Profits earned by a company in India, which is owned by a non-resident is a part of domestic factor\nincome of India. This is because the company is generating profit within the domestic territory of India.\n• However, these profits are considered a part of income from domestic product accruing to rest of the\nworld. Therefore, these are not reflected in the estimation of national income.\n• These are deducted from domestic income to find national income.\n1. Economic interest - interest related to such activities as of production, consumption and investment.","image":"pages/IMG_0515.jpg"},{"title":"DID YOU KNOW","page":"IMG_0516","text":"• Domestic income of a country includes factor incomes of both the residents and non-residents\nworking within the domestic territory of a country.\n• Net factor income from abroad is the difference between\nFactor income earned by our residents who are temporarily residing abroad, and\nFactor income earned by non-residents who are temporarily residing in our country.","image":"pages/IMG_0516.jpg"},{"title":"The above definition raises two critical questions:","page":"IMG_0516","text":"Do we have this freedom of circulation (of persons, goods and capital) in the embassies of foreign\ncountries located in India?\nThe answer is 'No'.\nDo we have this freedom of circulation (of persons, goods and capital) in Indian embassies located\nabroad?\nThe answer is \"Yes'.\nAccordingly, we conclude that foreign embassies located in India are not a part of domestic/\neconomic territory of India. On the other hand, Indian embassies located abroad are a part of\ndomestic/economic territory of India.\nThus, while defining the term domestic territory, the focus is NOT to be on political boundaries of a\nnation but on the geographical area where our persons, our goods and our capital enjoy the freedom\nof circulation.","image":"pages/IMG_0516.jpg"},{"title":"[Check Ability Zone for the components of domestic territory.]","page":"IMG_0516","text":"3.2.3 Conversion of Domestic Income into National Income\nBefore we understand how to convert domestic income into national income and vice versa, it is\nimportant to note that:\n• our domestic income includes factor income earned by non-residents within the domestic\nterritory of our country,\n• our domestic income does not include factor income earned by our residents from the domestic\nterritories of other countries.\nAccordingly, domestic income becomes national income provided:\n• We exclude from domestic income that part of factor income which belongs to non-residents within\nour domestic territory, and\n• We add to domestic income that part of factor income which our residents earn from rest of the\nworld (or from the domestic territories of other countries).\n- NFIA\nNational Income\n(NNPFc)\nDomestic Income\n(NDPFC)\nImplications:\n• National income = Domestic income + NFIA\n• Domestic income = National income - NFIA\n• NFIA\n= National income\n- Domestic income\n+ NFIA\nFig. 3.1: Conversion between National Income and Domestic Income\n75","image":"pages/IMG_0516.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"IMG_0517","text":"Q. 3. Which of the following are the elements of domestic territory of India?\n(i) A French Company in India.\n(ii) A branch of Microsoft Office in India.\n(iii) A company in India owned by a Chinese.\n(iv) Office of Reliance Industries in USA.\n(v) Tata rented its building to Microsoft in New York.\n(vi) Branch of a foreign bank in India.\n(vii) Branch of Punjab National Bank in China.\n(viii) Indian embassy in Russia.\nAns. Domestic Territory of India: (i), (ii), (iii), (vi), (viii)\nQ. 4. Write two observations indicating the difference between domestic income and national\nincome.\nBasis of\nDifference\n(i) Meaning\nAns. Following observations indicate the difference between domestic income and national income:\nDomestic Income\nNational Income\n(NDP Fc)\n(NNPFc)\n• It is the sum total of factor incomes\n• It is the sum total of factor incomes\ngenerated within the\ndomestic\naccruing to normal residents of a\nterritory of a country, no matter who\ncountry, no matter where this income\ngenerates this income-residents or\nis generated-within the domestic\nnon-residents.\nterritory or in rest of the world.\n(ii) Inclusion of NEIA\n• It does not include net factor income\n• It includes net factor income from\nfrom abroad.\nabroad.\nQ. 5. An ambassador in US embassy in India stays in his job for a period exceeding one year.\nWould he be treated as a resident or non-resident of India? Give reason.\nAns. (i) Resident of a country is defined as a person who normally resides in the country (or does\nnot leave the country for a period exceeding one year) and whose centre of interest lies in\nthe country concerned.","image":"pages/IMG_0517.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS","page":"IMG_0519","text":"GDP MP-Break up of the Aggregate\nGROSS\nDOMESTIC\nPRODUCT\nMARKET PRICE\nTotal Value\ninclusive of\ndepreciation\nWithin the domestic\nterritory of the\nFinal goods\nand services\nproduced\nPrice at which\ngoods are sold in the\nmarket, It includes\nthe impact of indirect\ntaxes and subsidies\n(2) Net Domestic Product at Market Price [NDP Mp]: Net domestic product at market price is\nthe market value of the final goods and services produced within the domestic territory of a\ncountry during the period of an accounting year, exclusive of depreciation.\n2. Indirect taxes - sales tax, excise duty, GST are some of the alternative terms used in place of indirect taxes.\nBasically, indirect tax refers to the tax on the production or sale of goods and services by the producers.\n3. Subsidies - financial assistance or financial compensation to the producers in lieu of their commitment to sell\ntheir product at the 'reduced price. Price is reduced to facilitate purchase of a particular product by poorer\nsections of the society.\n81","image":"pages/IMG_0519.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"IMG_0522","text":"Q. 6. Identify the missing terms in the following equations:\n(i) NNPFc = NNPMP -\n(iii) NDPFC =\n(iv) GNPMP =\n= National Income - Domestic Income.\n- Depreciation.\n+ GDPMP-\n= NDP MP + Depreciation + NFIA.\nAns.\n(i) Net indirect taxes\n(ii) Net factor income from abroad\n(iii) GDPFc\n(iv) Net factor income from abroad\n(v) GNPMP\nQ. 7. Fill in the blanks using >, ‹ or = symbol for the following situations:\nSituation\n(i) NFIA =0\n(ii) Subsidies > Indirect Taxes\n(ili) Factor income to rest of the world\nResult\n: National Income\n: GDPFC\n: NNPFC\nGDP MP -\nNDPFC•\n> Factor income from rest of the world\n(iv) NIT = 0\n(v) - ve NFIA\n: GDPFC.\n: GDP FC.-\nGDP MP -\nGNP FC.\nAns.\n(i) =, (i) >, (i) <, (iv) =, (v) >\nDomestic Income","image":"pages/IMG_0522.jpg"},{"title":"DID YOU KNOW","page":"IMG_0523","text":"• When nominal GDP rises, the flow of goods and services in the economy may or may not rise during\nan accounting year.\n• When real GDP rises, the flow of goods and services in the economy must rise during an accounting\nyear.\nConversion of Nominal GDP into Real GDP\n• If GDP at current prices is divided by the price index of the current year and multiplied by the price\nindex of base year (which is always equal to 100), we obtain GDP at constant prices or real GDP.\nGDP at Current Prices × 100\nReal GDP Or GDP at Constant Prices =\nPrice Index\nIllustration\n• If in 2011, GDP is * 100 crore, and in 2025 it is & 200 crore at the current prices.\n• If price index rises from 100 to 400 within the same period, then GDP at current prices is\nconverted into real GDP (or GDP at constant prices) as in Table 3.4.\nTable 3.4: Conversion of Nominal GDP into Real GDP\nGDP at Current Prices\nIndex Number\nGDP at Constant Prices\n(or Base Year Prices)\n( in crore)\nCurrent Price Index\n(in crore)\n400\n200\n× 100 = 50\n89","image":"pages/IMG_0523.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS","page":"IMG_0524","text":"GDP Deflator\n• It refers to the ratio between GDP at current prices and GDP at constant prices. It is expressed as under.\nGDP Deflator =\nGDP at Current Prices\nGDP at Constant Prices\n-x 100\nGDP Deflator =\nNominal GDP\nReal GDP\n• It shows change in GDP due to change in price level. It is the same as price index.\nExample. If Real GDP = 600 and Nominal GDP = 660, find GDP Deflator (Price Index).\nSolution: GDP Deflator (Price Index) = Nominal GDP × 100 = 660\nReal GDP\n600 ×100=110\nIt shows increase in the general price level by 10%.\n[Note: - GDP deflator should not be confused with Consumer Price Index, even when both indicate change\nin the price level. The basic difference is as under:\n- GDP deflator indicates change in price index or change in price level related to all goods and\nservices produced within the domestic territory of a country.\nNational Income and Related Aggregates\n- Consumer Price Index indicates change in price index or change in price level related to only a\nspecific basket of goods bought by the consumers, and these may be produced within the\ndomestic territory or in rest of the world.]","image":"pages/IMG_0524.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"IMG_0524","text":"Q. 8. Write observations indicating the difference between GDP at current prices and GDP at\nconstant prices.\nAns. Following observations indicate the difference between GDP at current prices and GDP at\nconstant prices:\nBasis of Difference\n(i) Meaning\nGDP at Current Prices\nGDP at Constant Prices\n• It is the market value of the final\n• It is the market value of the final\ngoods and services produced within\ngoods and services produced within\nthe domestic territory of a country\nthe domestic territory of a country\nduring an accounting year,\nduring an\naccounting year,\nestimated at current year prices.\nestimated at base year prices.\n(ii) Impact of Price\nChange\n• It is impacted by the change in price\n• It is not impacted by the change in\nlevel. It increases when the price\nprice level. It increases only when\nlevel increases, even when output is\noutput increases, no matter what the\nconstant.\nprice level Is.\n(iii) Measure of\nWelfare\n• It is not a good measure of welfare of\n• It is a good measure of welfare of\npeople, as it may or may not reflect\npeople, as it always reflects a change\nchange in the level of output.\nin the level of output.\n(iv) Alternate Name • It is also known as nominal GDP.\n• It is also known as Real GDP.\nQ. 9. Why is Real GDP considered a better estimate of national income as compared to Nominal\nAns.\nGDP?\n(i) Real GDP shows the change in the actual level of output, as it is adjusted for inflation. In\ncontrast, Nominal GDP may rise or fall even without any change in output, because it is\naffected by changes in the general price level (inflation or deflation).\n(ii) Real GDP provides a more accurate measure of true economic growth, as it reflects the\nactual increase in goods and services produced, rather than changes caused only by price\nmovements.\nAVLR 3.3\nNOMINAL GDP AND REAL GDP","image":"pages/IMG_0524.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"IMG_0525","text":"Q. 10. What lowers the significance of GDP as an index of welfare?\nAns. The following observations explain how the significance of GDP as an index of welfare is lowered:\n(i) Distribution of Income: GDP as an index of welfare loses significance if the distribution of\nincome turns unequal.\n(ii) Composition of GDP: If luxuries are produced for richer sections of the society and the poor\nsuffer deprivation, GDP growth becomes meaningless.\n(ili) Non-monetary Exchanges/Transactions: Larger the non-monetary transactions, greater the\nunderestimation of GDP as an index of welfare.\n(iv) Externalities: GDP index does not account for externalities: the good and bad impact\nof economic activities without the price or penalty. Environmental pollution related to\nproduction activity is an important example. This also lowers the significance of GDP as an\nindex of welfare.\nQ. 11. \"GDP growth as an index of welfare loses its significance if there is a deep economic divide in\nthe economy.\" State whether true or false and provide a reason for the same.\nAns. True. Economic divide indicates the gulf between the rich and the poor. If the gulf increases, GDP\ngrowth loses its significance.","image":"pages/IMG_0525.jpg"},{"title":"ABILITY ZONE","page":"IMG_0525","text":"Normal Residents of a Country\n• It is not necessary that normal resident of a country is also a citizen of that country. A person may be a\nnormal resident of one country even when he is a citizen of other country.\n• Normal Residents of a country include:\n• Citizens (and institutions) of the country who normally reside in the country and whose centre\nof economic interest lies in the country.\n• Citizens of other nations who continue to live in the country beyond a period of one year and\nwhose centre of economic interest lies in the country.\n• For example, if an Indian is living in USA for more than one year and his centre of economic\ninterest lies in that country, he would be deemed as a normal resident of USA even when he\ncontinues to be a citizen of India.\n• Citizens of a country working in international organisations (like World Bank and IMF) or foreign\nembassies located in that country.\n• For example, an Indian working in the World Bank office located in New Delhi would be\nconsidered as a normal resident of India.\n• Citizens of a country who are living abroad, but not for a period exceeding one year and whose\ncentre of economic interest lies in their home country.\n• Border workers or persons who cross the border between two countries daily or regularly in\norder to work in one country are the residents of the country in which they live, not of the country\nin which they are working.\n• Officials, diplomats, and members of the armed forces of a foreign country are treated as the\nnormal residents of the country to which they belong, and not of the country in which they are\nemployed.\n93","image":"pages/IMG_0525.jpg"},{"title":"Power Points [Summary of the Chapter]","page":"IMG_0527","text":"• National Income is the sum total of factor incomes accruing to normal residents of a con\nnot account for transfer incomes. Important terms associated with this concept:\n• Factor Incomes are the rewards of the factor of production, viz., compensation of ene\nrent, interest and profit.\n• Transfer Incomes are unearned incomes. These include gifts in cash, scholarships to thes\nold-age pensions to the senior, etc. These are not included in the estimation of the atra.\n• Domestic Income is the sum total of factor incomes generated within the domestic ter.\ncountry (no matter it is the income accruing to residents or non-residents of the country.\n• Conversion of Domestic Income into National Income:\nDomestic income + Net factor income from abroad = National Income\n• Net Factor Income from Abroad (NFIA) is the difference between the factor income eared\nresidents from abroad and factor income earned by non-residents in our country.\n• Domestic Income does not include net factor income from abroad.\n• National Income includes net factor income from abroad.\n• Gross and Net Concepts\n• Gross Domestic Product is the market value of final goods and services produced with\ndomestic territory of the country during an accounting year, inclusive of depreciation.\n• Net Domestic Product is the market value of final goods and services produced with\ndomestic territory of the country during an accounting year, exclusive of depreciation.\n• Conversion of GDP into NDP:\nGDP - Depreciation = NDP\n• Market Price and Factor Cost\n• Market Price includes the impact of indirect taxes (taxes on goods and services) and subside.\n• Indirect Tax raises the market price, subsidies tend to lower it.\n• Factor Cost is the cost of factors of production. It is equal to factor payments.\n• Conversion of GDPMp into GDPFC:\nGDPMP - Net indirect taxes = GDPFc\n• Nominal GDP and Real GDP\n• Nominal GDP is the market value of goods and services produced within the domestic trity\na country during an accounting year, as estimated using the current year prices.\n• Real GDP is the market value of goods and services produced within the domestic territory de\ncountry during an accounting year, as estimated using the base year prices.\n• Conversion of Nominal GDP into Real GDP:\nReal GDP = Nominal GDP × 100\n• GDP Deflator is the ratio between nominal GDP and real GDP, It shows change in GDP owing to\nchange in the price level. It is also called price index.\nGDP Deflator = Nominal GDP\nReal GDP\n× 100\n• GDP and Welfare\n• Real GDP is a widely used index of welfare. But, it suffers from certain limitations as it ignores:\n(i) distribution of income\n(il) composition of GDP\n(ill) non-monetary transactions\n(iv) externalities\n96\nWORK\nCompetency Based Leam\n1. Multiple Choice Questi\nChoose the correct option:\n1. \"Real Gross Domestic P\n2023-24. The correspon\nSource: https://pib.go\nOn the basis of the gi\n(a) 295.36\n(c) 169.92\n2. To arrive at the value\nto/1\n(a) depreciation, ade\n(c) net indriect taxe\n3. Identify, which of tr\nIndia.\n(a) Salary to Indian\n(b) Income generat\n(c) Payments to far\n(d) Interest paid by\n4. When goods and se\nas:\n(a) real GDP\n(c) constant prices\n5. To arrive at the val\nadded to Gross Vali\n(a) Product taxes\n(c) Production tax\n6. Identify the correc\n(a) GDPMP = NNPR\n(b) NDPFc = NNPFC\n(c) GNP MP = GDP~\n(d) NNPFc = NDPM\n7. Identify which of t\n(a) Excess of Gros\n(b) Excess of Gros\n(c) Equality betw\n(d) Sum of Gross i","image":"pages/IMG_0527.jpg"}],"subject":"macro","number":"3"},{"id":"4","title":"Measurement of National Income","intro":"Value added is value of output minus intermediate consumption.","lessons":[{"title":"Value added method: measure production once","body":"Value added is value of output minus intermediate consumption. Value of output equals sales plus change in stock, with own-account production added where applicable. Sum GVA at market price across producing units to obtain GDPMP. Deduct depreciation and NIT, then add NFIA for national income. Alternatively, sum sectoral NVAFC and add NFIA.","formula":"Value of output = Sales + Closing stock − Opening stock\nGVA MP = Value of output − Intermediate consumption\nNNPFC = ΣGVA MP − Depreciation − NIT + NFIA","source":"IMG_0528","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"4.2 VALUE ADDED METHOD (OR PRODUCT METHOD)"},{"title":"Double counting and value-added precautions","body":"Double counting means counting the value of the same output more than once. Avoid it by counting final goods only or summing value added at each stage. If wheat sells for 100, flour for 160 and bread for 250, total value added is 100 + 60 + 90 = 250, not 510. Include own-account construction and imputed rent of owner-occupied houses. Exclude resale value of second-hand goods, but include the current broker’s service. Do not count intermediate goods separately from the final product.","formula":"","source":"IMG_0530","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"4.2.2 Precautions · 4.2.3 Problem of Double Counting"},{"title":"Income method: compensation, surplus and mixed income","body":"Income method measures domestic income as the sum total of factor incomes generated within domestic territory. Compensation of employees includes wages and salaries in cash and kind and employers’ social-security contributions. Operating surplus comprises rent and royalty, interest and profit. Profit includes corporate tax, dividends and retained earnings; these are components, so do not add them again if total profit is given. Mixed income of the self-employed combines labour and property income that cannot be separated.","formula":"NDPFC = Compensation of employees + Operating surplus + Mixed income\nOperating surplus = Rent and royalty + Interest + Profit\nNational income = NDPFC + NFIA","source":"IMG_0534","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"4.3 INCOME METHOD"},{"title":"Income-method precautions","body":"Include only incomes earned from current productive services. Exclude transfer receipts, capital gains and windfall gains. Exclude interest on consumption loans and interest on public debt when it represents a transfer rather than payment for a productive service, following the textbook treatment. Include imputed rent and production for self-consumption where measured. Do not count sale proceeds of shares or second-hand goods as factor income; the current commission or brokerage is a service income.","formula":"","source":"IMG_0535","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"4.3.2 Precautions Regarding Income Method"},{"title":"Expenditure method and its precautions","body":"Expenditure method measures final expenditure on domestically produced output. Add private final consumption expenditure, government final consumption expenditure, gross domestic capital formation and net exports. Gross domestic capital formation includes fixed capital formation and change in stocks. Exclude financial-asset purchases, transfers and second-hand purchases; include current brokerage. Subtract imports because imported content may already be included in consumption or investment. Do not add change in stock twice if gross domestic capital formation already includes it.","formula":"GDPMP = PFCE + GFCE + GDCF + (X − M)\nNNPFC = PFCE + GFCE + GDCF + (X − M) − Depreciation − NIT + NFIA","source":"IMG_0538","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"4.4 EXPENDITURE METHOD"},{"title":"Reconciliation and treatment of tricky items","body":"The three methods measure the same current production from different sides: value added creates factor income, and income finances final expenditure. In an exam, classify each item before calculating. Government services are counted even when provided free; employer provident-fund contributions belong in compensation. Scholarships and welfare pensions are transfers. Foreign factor earnings require a residence/territory check; the purchase of a newly produced house is investment. Read the supplied transaction table for item-by-item domestic and national treatment.","formula":"","source":"IMG_0542","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"4.5 RECONCILIATION OF THREE METHODS · 4.6 TREATMENT OF DIFFERENT ITEMS"},{"title":"Steps under the three measurement methods","textbookHeading":"4.2.1 Steps · 4.3.1 Steps · 4.4.1 Steps","body":"Value Added Method: identify and classify producing units; estimate each unit’s output and intermediate consumption; obtain sectoral value added; aggregate and make depreciation, tax and NFIA adjustments to national income. Income Method: identify producing enterprises; classify factor incomes into compensation, operating surplus and mixed income; sum them to obtain domestic income; add NFIA. Expenditure Method: identify final expenditure by sectors; classify consumption, investment and net exports; sum to GDPMP; convert to NNPFC. Keep the starting aggregate clear because the adjustments depend on whether it is gross/net and market price/factor cost.","source":"IMG_0538","kind":"Exam points","formula":""}],"pages":[{"id":"IMG_0528","image":"pages/IMG_0528.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\n4.1 METHODS OF CALCULATING NATIONAL INCOME\n• Circular How Model (chapter 4) reveals that national income can be viewed from three dith\nangles:\n(i) as the sum total of value addition in the economy,\ni) as the sum total of income generated in the economy, and\n(i) as the sum total of expenditure on the final goods and services produced in the econom\n• Corresponding to the three different ways of looking at national income, the economists.\nsuggested three methods of calculating national income. These are:\n• Value Added Method,\n• Income Method, and\n• Expenditure Method.\nFollowing is a brief description of these methods.\n4.2 VALUE ADDED METHOD (OR PRODUCT METHOD)\n• Value Added Method measures national income in terms of value addition by each producing\nenterprise in the economy during an accounting year. It is also known as Industrial origi\nMethod or Net Output Method.\n• Estimation of the contribution of all producing enterprises to production in the domestic teriy\nof the country during the year is equal to the market value of GP, called GDP. It is adjustin.,\nfind out NP Fc or national income.\nConcept of Value Added\n• Value added refers to the value created over and above the value of raw material (intermediate\ngoods) used by a firm in the process of production.\n• Value added is the difference ben value of output of an enterprise and the value of it\nintermediate consumption.\nValue Added = Value of output - Intermediate consumption\nConcept of Value of Output\n* Value of output refers to market value of the goods for services) produced by a firm during an\naccounting year.\nEstimating Value of Output\nCase A: When entire output of the year is sold during the year, value of output = sales\nValue of Output = Sales + Goods produced for self-consumption\nImputed value?\noutput.\nof goods produced for self-consumption is taken to be a part of value of\n1. Imputed value - a proxy value when true value is not known.\n130\nMethods of Calculating National Income\nEstimation of sales under different situations is explained through three illustrations as under:\n' TACKLING SALES\nIllustration 1. Firm X manufactures 500 watches annually and sells them @ * 1,000 per piece. Find Sales.\nQuantity = 500 units, Price = 7 1,000\nSales = 500 x · 1,000\n= · 5,00,000. [Sales = Quantity × Price]\nIllustration 2. Given: Domestic Sales = 7 3,000, Exports = 7 2,000. Find Sales.\nSales = 7 3,000 + $ 2,000\n= · 5,000.\n[Exports are included as these are sales to rest of the world.]\nIllustration 3. Given: Sales = 7 5,000, Exports = 7 500. Find Sales.\nSales = 7 5,000. [Exports are a part of total sales.]\nCase B: When some output remains unsold, it is added to the firm's inventory stock.\n> It is expressed as change in stock (Astock) during the year.\n> In such a situation, value of output is measured as the sum total of 'sales during the year\"\nand 'change in stock during the year'.\nValue of Output = Sales + AStock\nThe illustrations given below relate to the estimation of GVO.\nLT TACKLING GO (GROSS VALUE OF OUTPUT)\nIllustration 4. Given: Sales = 7 100, Change in Stock = & 50. Find GVO.\nGVO = Sales + Change in Stock\n= · 100 + 7 50\n= · 150.\nIllustration 5. Given: Sales = & 100, Goods produced for Self-consumption = 7 20,\nChange in Stock = 7 50. Find GVO.\nSol.\nGVO= Sales + Change in Stock + Goods produced for Self-consumption\n= · 100 + 750 + 7 20\n= · 170.\n[Note: All that is produced may not be sold. A part of it could be kept by the producer for self-use (called production\nfor self-consumption). Goods produced for self-consumption ought to be included while estimating GVO.]\nIllustration 6. Given: Value of Output of Primary Sector = R 100\nValue of Output of Secondary Sector = 7 50\nValue of Output of Service Sector = 7 20\nFind GVO (i.e., Total Value of Output).\nGVO = F 100 + 7 50 + F 20\n= T 170.\n131","file":"macro 4/IMG_0528.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0529","image":"pages/IMG_0529.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\n! CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS\nA Standard Precaution Relating to the Use of Value Added Method\n• Value added should not be contused with the value of output. Value of output simply refers try\n• On the other hand, value auded reters to the market value of the goods produced minus market val\nmarket value of the goods produced.\nthe goods used as inputs/taw materar in the process of production. Goods used as inputs/raw m\nare called intermediate goods. Thus,\nValue Added = Value of output - Value of intermediate goods used in production\n• Bretty, wille value or output includeshan f value of intermediate goods used in production, vi\nadded does not.\nConcept of Change in Stock (4Stock)\n• Change in stock is measured as the difference between 'closing stock of the accounting ear\nand 'opening stock of the accounting year'\nAStock = Closing Stock - Opening Stock\nThe illustrations given\nbelow show various situations dealing with the estimation o\nchange in stock.\nTACKLING CHANGE IN STOCK\nIllustration 7. Given: Closing Stock = 7 2,000, Opening Stock = F 1,500. Find Change in Stock.\nChange in Stock = 7 2,000 - 7 1,500\n= · 500.\nIllustration 8. Given: Closing Stock = 7 4,500, Opening Stock = F 5,000. Find Change in Stock.\nChange in Stock = 7 4,500 - · 5,000\n= (-)R 500.\nIllustration 9. Given: Closing Stock = & 1,500. Find Change in Stock.\nSol.\nChange in Stock = 7 1,500 - 0\n= · 1,500.\nlustration 10. Given: Opening Stock = 7 2,000. Find Change in Stock.\nChange in Stock = 0 - R 2,000\n[Note: (i) Change in stock can be written as At (1) aon) et change in stock or increase/decrease in unsold sto\n(i) Sign given along with it (AStock) is not be ignored.]\nConcept of Intermediate Consumption\n• Intermediate consumption refers to value of non-factor inputs (all inputs other than fac\ninputs of land, labour, capital and entreareneurship). Primarily, it includes value of raw mater\nused in the process of production.\n132\nMethods of Calculating National Income\nWhile solving numerical illustrations to estimate intermediate consumption one may encounter\nvarious situations. The following illustrations show those situations.\nỸ TACKLING INTERMEDIATE CONSUMPTION (IC)\nIllustration 11. Given: IC = 7 1,500, Import of Raw Material = 7 300. Find IC.\nIC = 7 1,500 (The term IC includes import of raw material).\nIllustration 12. Given:Purchase of Raw Material from Domestic Firm = 7 2,000, Import of Raw Material = 7 500.\nFind IC.\nSol.\nIC = R2,000 + 7500 = 72,500.\nIllustration 13. Given:Domestic Purchase of Raw Material = 7 500, Import of Raw Material = 71,200, Find IC.\nSol.\nIC = 7 500+71,200 = 7 1,700.\nIllustration 14. Given:Purchase of Raw Material = 7 2,000, Import of Raw Material - 7 500. Find IC.\nIC = · 2,000.\n[Note: We are not including import of raw material (R 500) in intermediate consumption as it is assumed to be\nincluded in the purchase of raw material R 2,000).]\nIllustration 15. Given: Purchase of oil to be used to lubricate the machines = 7 2,000, Purchase of gas = 7 500,\nPurchase of machinery = 75,000. Find IC.\nIC = R 2,000 + 7 500 = 7 2,500.\n[Note: Purchase of machinery will not be included in estimating intermediate consumption as it is considered to be\na final capital good used by the producer as a fixed asset.]\nIllustration\nTable 4.1 illustrates how 'value added' is estimated.\nTable 4.1: Estimating Value Added (or Value Addition)\nProducing Enterprise\nValue of\nCost of Intermediate\nOutput\nGoods [Intermediate\nConsumption]\n3\n1. Farmer\n600\n200\n2. Flour Mill\n800\n600\n200\n3. Baker\n1,000\n800\n4. Shopkeeper\n1,200\n1,000\nTotal\n3,600\n2,600\n1,000\n• In Table 4.1, it is assumed that the production of wheat involves cost of intermediate consumption\nof T 200 (it may include cost of inputs like seeds, fertilizers, irrigation expenses, etc.). Accordingly,\nvalue added by the farmer is equal to 7 600 - 7 200 = 7 400.\n•The flour mill buys wheat for & 600 and sells flour for 7 800. Value added by the flour mill, therefore.\nis equal to & 800 - F 600 = 7 200.\n• Further, the baker buys flour for ‹ 800 and sells the bread for * 1,000 to the shopkeeper. The value\nadded by the baker is * 1,000 - 7 800 = 7 200.\n• The shopkeeper buys the bread for * 1,000 and sells the bread to the households for & 1,200.\nThe value added by the shopkeeper is * 1,200 - 1,000 = 7 200.\n• Thus, the gross value added (GDP Mp) by all the producing enterprises is 7 400 + 7 200 + 7 200\n+· 200 = · 1,000.\n133","file":"macro 4/IMG_0529.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0530","image":"pages/IMG_0530.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\nCRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS\nGDP MP (GVAMP)\nperiod of one year.\n= Market value of final goods and services produced in the economy during the period of one year.\nHaving estimated GDP Mp, We will find NPrc (national income) in terms of the following equation.\nNNPFC\n= GDP MP Or GVAMp - Depreciation - Net indirect taxes+ Net factor income from abroad\n[Note: Domestic income is identified with NDP Fc and national income is identified with NPFc.\n4.2.1 Steps involved in Estimating National Income by\nValue Added Method\nFollowing steps are involved in estimating national income by value added method:\nStep 1: Identification of production units across all sectors of\nValue Added Method\nproduction, viz., primary, secondary and tertiary sector.\nThis step involves identification and classification of\nProduct Method\nproduction units in the domestic economy into primary\nsector (involving exploitation of natural resources-fishing,\nlogging, etc.), secondary sector (involving conversion of\none type of commodity into another), and tertiary sector\n(rendering services like banking, teaching, medical care,\nGVAMp in the Primary Sector\nGVAMp in the Secondary Sector\nGVAMP in the Tertiary Sector\netc.).\nStep 2: Estimation of GDP MP Or GVAMp by summation of GVAMP\nby all the sectors identified in Step 1, i.e.,\nGross Domestic Product\nat Market Price (GDPMP)\nGDP MP/GVAMP\n(-) Depreciation\n(- Net Indirect Taxes\n= GVAMp of Primary Sector\n+ GVAMP of Secondary Sector\nDomestic Income\n+ GVAMp of Tertiary Sector\n(NDPFC)\n(+) Net Factor\nStep 3: Calculation of Domestic Income, viz., NDPFc\nIncome from Abroad\nThis involves conversion of GDP Mp into NDP FC-\nNational Income\n(NNPFc)\nEstmation or domestic income require ssois = ton of depreciation and net indirect taxes\nfrom GDP MP.\nNDP FC\n= GDP MP\n- Depreciation\n- Net indirect taxes\n134\nMethods of Calculating National Income\nStep 4: Calculation of National Income, viz., NPFC\nConversion of NDPc into NNPFc in order to estimate national income requires addition of\nnet factor income from abroad, i.e., NFIA into NDP+c-\nNNP+C= NDPFC + Net factor income from abroad\n4.2.2 Precautions Regarding Value Added Method\nFollowing are some important precautions regarding value added method:\nItems to be included\nCommission earned on account of the sale and\npurchase of second-hand goods is included in the\nestimation of value added.\n• Reason: It is a reward for the services rendered.\n- Own account production of goods of the producing\nunits is taken into account while estimating value added.\n• Reason: These goods are like those produced for the\nmarket but are not sold owing to their need by the\nproducers themselves.\n• Example: Cars\nused by the car producers\ntransporting their employees.\n- Imputed value of production for self-consumption is\ntaken into account.\n• Reason: These goods are like those produced for the\nItems not to be included\n- Value of the sale and purchase of second-hand goods\nis not included in the estimation of'value added or GDP.\n• Reason: It is already accounted for during the year\nthey were produced.\n- Value of intermediate goods is not included in the\nestimation of value added.\n• Reason: Its value is reflected in the value of final\ngoods.\n- Services for self-consumption are not considered\nwhile estimating value added.\n• Reason: It is difficult to estimate their market value,\nlike, for example, services of housewives.\n• Example: Wheat produced and consumed by the\nfarming families themselves.\n- Imputed rent on the owner occupied house is to be\ntaken into account.\n• Reason: All houses have rental value, no matter these\nare self-occupied or rented out.\n- Value addition related to illegal activities is not\nincluded.\n• Reason: No specific data input is available.\n4.2.3 Problem of Double Counting and the Ways to Tackle it\nMeaning of Double Counting\n• Double counting refers to counting of the value of same product more than once.\nWhy does the problem of Double Counting occur?\n• The problem arises when in the estimation of GDP or GVA the value of certain goods is counted\nmore than once.\n135","file":"macro 4/IMG_0530.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0531","image":"pages/IMG_0531.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\nTabular Illustration on the Problem of Double Counting\nTable 4.2: Double Counting and Over-estimation of GDP\nProducing Enterprise\nValue of Output/Sales\n(Stage-1) Farmer (produces wheat and sells it to a mill owner)\n(Stage-2) Mill owner (converts wheat into flour and sells it to the baker)\n(Stage-3) Baker (converts flour into bread and sells it to the shopkeeper)\n(Stage-4) Shopkeeper (sells the entire stock of bread to the final consumers)\n400\n600\n800\n900\n2,700\nValue of Output/Sales =72,701\nExplanation\n• Table 4.2 shows that in stage 1, a farmer produces one tonne of wheat and sells it for 7 400 in the\nmarket to a flour mill.\n• As far as the farmer is concerned, the sale of wheat for 400 is a final sale.\n• In stage 2, the flour mill buys wheat from the farmer and converts it into flour.\n• It sells flour for 7 600 to a baker. The flour mill treats the flour as a final good.\n• In stage 3, the baker buys flour from the flour mill and converts it into bread.\n• The baker sells the bread to the shopkeeper for 7 800. For the baker, the bread is a final good.\n• In stage 4, the shopkeeper buys bread from the baker for further sale. He sells bread to\nits final users (consumers) for 7 900. Like other producers, he treats his sale as a final good worth\n· 900.\n• It is an intermediate good because it is redeant for further sale (to the consumers). He treats his\nentire sale (R 900) as the final good.\n• The shopkeeper sells the entire stock of bread to the final consumers for 7 900 (final stage).\nImplications\n+· 900 = 7 2,700.\n• If we treat this as the production or hinal goods (GDP or GVA) in the economy, we are making\na grave mistake of double counting. Because goods sold by one producer are becoming\nintermediate consumption of the next producer.\n• And, we know that intermediate consumption is not a part of GDP or GVA. Only, final goods are a\npart of GDP. Then, what are the final goods in the present illustration?\n• These are those goods which are not used as intermediate goods but are sold to the final user\n• in the above illustration, it is the value of bread sold by the shopkeeper to the consume\n• Consumers are the final users of bread. Goods are finally consumed by them. Implying that GDP\n=$ 900, not ? 2,700, as estimated above.\n136\nMethods of Calculating National Income\n• Double counting occurs\n• when we fail to draw the distinction between intermediate goods and final goods.\n• when we keep on adding sales of all firms, ignoring the fact that goods sold by one firm may\nhave been used as intermediate consumption.\nTwo Ways to Avoid the Problem of Double Counting\nThe problem of double counting is avoided in case either of the following two methods is adopted\nfor the estimation of GDP:\n(i) Final Output Method: According to this method, only final goods and services (in terms of\ntheir end-use) are to be considered in the estimation of GDP. Intermediate goods are not to\nbe considered.\n* We know (in terms of their end-use), final goods are those goods which have crossed the\nboundary line of production and which are ready for use by their final users who may be\nconsumers or producers. Intermediate goods are not to be considered, as these are within\nthe boundary line of production and are yet not ready for use by their final users.\n> In the above example, the bread sold to the consumers is the final good. Only the value of\nfinal good (bread sold to the consumers, in the present case), i.e., & 900 will be included\nin the estimation of GDP.\n(ii) Value Added Method: According to this method, sum total of value added (Not value of\noutput or sales) by all the producing units is to be considered in the estimation of GDP.\n> Value added refers to the difference between value of output and the value of intermediate\nconsumption of each producing unit in the country.\n* Table 4.3 shows summation of value added at each stage of production, i.e., & 400 + 7 200\n+* 200+ ≤ 100 = 7 900 will be included in the estimation of GDP.\nTable 4.3 illustrates value added by each producer in the given illustration.\nTable 4.3: Estimation of GDP through Value Added Approach\nProducing Enterprise\nValue of Output\nIntermediate Consumption\nValue Added\nFarmer\nMill Owner\nBaker\nShopkeeper\n400\n600\n800\n900\n600\n400(400 - 0)\n200(600 - 400)\n200(800-600)\n100(900 - 800)\nValue Added = 900\nGDP or GVA (Gross Value Added) = 7 900\nAVLR 4.1\nPROBLEM OF DOUBLE COUNTING AND WAYS TO AVOID IT\n137","file":"macro 4/IMG_0531.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0532","image":"pages/IMG_0532.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\nGiven below are some examples regapupto the estimation of national income, via, value at\nmethod.\nEXAMPLES\nOn the basis of the following data about an economy which consists of only two firms, find ou\n(a) Value Added by firm A and B, and\n(b) Gross Value Added or Gross Domestic Product at Factor Cost.\nItems\n(R in lakh)\nS.No.\nSales by firm A\nPurchases from firm B by firm A\nPurchases from firm A by firm B\nSales by firm B\nClosing stock of firm A\nClosing stock of firm B\nOpening stock of firm A\nOpening stock of firm B\nIndirect taxes paid by both firms\nSolution:\n(a) Value Added by firm A\n= Sales by firm A - Purchases from firm B + Change in stock (Closing stock - Opening 50o,\n= R 100 lakh - 7 40 lakh + (R 20 lakh - < 25 lakh)\n= R 100 lakh - * 40 lakh - 7 5 lakh\n= * 55 lakh\nValue Added by firm B\n= Sales by hrm B - Purchases fo sis firm A + Change in stock (Closing stock - Opening stock\n= R200 lakh - R 60 lakh + (R 35 lakh - R 45 lakh)\n= R 200 lakh - < 60 lakh - < 10 lakh\n= < 130 lakh\nAns. Value Added by firm A = & 55 lakh.\nValue Added by firm B = R 130 lakh.\n(b) Gross Value Added or Gross Domestic Product at Factor Cost\n= Value added by firm A + Value added by firm B - Indirect taxes\n= R 55 lakh + R 130 lakh - & 30 lakh\n= R 185 lakh - < 30 lakh\n= R 155 lakh\nAns. Gross domestic product at factor cost = & 155\n(Note: Value added by firm A and firm B here implie\nMethods of Calculating National Income\nExample 2.\nFrom the following data, calculate:\n(a) Gross Value Added at Market Price, and\n(b) National Income.\nS.No.\nItems\nR in lakh)\n(vi)\n(vii)\nValue of output:\n(a) Primary sector\n(b) Secondary sector\n(c) Tertiary sector\nValue of intermediate inputs purchased by:\n(a) Primary sector\n(b) Secondary sector\n(c) Tertiary sector\nIndirect taxes paid by all sectors\nConsumption of fixed capital of all sectors\nFactor income received by the residents from rest of the world\nFactor income paid to non-residents\nSubsidies received by all sectors\n800\n300\n400\n100\nSolution:\n(a) Gross Value Added at Market Price\n= Value of output of different sectors - Value of intermediate inputs purchased by\ndifferent sectors\n= 7 800 lakh + * 200 lakh + * 300 lakh - 7 400 lakh - * 100 lakh - 7 50 lakh\n= 7750 lakh\nAns. Gross value added at market price = 7 750 lakh.\n(b) National Income\n= Gross value added at market price - Consumption of fixed capital - Indirect taxes\n+ Subsidies + Factor income received by the residents from rest of the world - Factor\nincome paid to non-residents\n= 7 750 lakh - 7 80 lakh - * 50 lakh + * 20 lakh + R 10 lakh - R 20 lakh\n= R 630 lakh\nAns. National income = 7 630 lakh.\nAVLR 4.2\nCALCULATION OF NATIONAL INCOME USING VALUE ADDED METHOD\ndded at market price.)\n139","file":"macro 4/IMG_0532.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0533","image":"pages/IMG_0533.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\nQ. 1. \"Value addition occurs even when goods do not undergo any material transtormation.\ntrue or false and give a reason for the same.\nnel level when goods are arrased tor resale, Without any mate\ntransformation.\nQ. 2. Imputed rent on owner occupied houses does not involve any payment to others. According\nit should not be included in the estimation of national income. \" State true or false and i-\nreason for the same.\nAns. False. Imputed rent is included in the estimation of national income as all houses have res\nvalue, no matter these are self-occupied or rented out.\nQ.3. What do you mean by the error of double counting?\nAns. Error of double counting occurs when the value of some goods produced in the economi\ncounted more than once in the estimation of GDP.\nExample: Entire value of wheat sold by the farmers is considered along with the value of whet\npurchased by the miller who uses wheat as an input for producing wheat flour.\n4.3 INCOME METHOD\n• income method measures national income in terms of factor payments (compensation\nemployees, rent, interest and profit) made to the owners (households) of factors of producie\nDistributed Share Method or Factor Payment Method.\n• Adding the sum total of factor incomes generated within the domestic territory of a county\nfactor income from abroad to find national income.\nWhat are Factor Incomes?\n• A factor income refers to income earned by a person as a reward for rendering\nfactor service.\n• It may be in the form of wage/salary for his labour, rent for his land, interest for his capital or pro\nfor his entrepreneurship.\n\" it must be noted that factor incomes are only earned' incomes. It does not include any in\nwhich is 'not earned' or for which a factor service has not been rendered.\nExample:\nOld-age pension received by the senior citizens.\nis not their earned income. It is just a help by the government without anything in ret\nincluded in the estimation of national income.\nSuch receipts or payments are called transfer receipts or transfer payments. These aren\n140\nMethods of Calculating National Income\nClassification (or Types) of Factor Incomes\nFactor incomes are broadly classified as follows:\n(1) Compensation of Employees: Compensation of employees refers to the sum total of\nbenefits (cash or in kind) received by the employees as a reward for their labour-service\nrendered to their employers.\nIt includes the following components:\n• Wages and Salaries in Cash: It refers to cash paid (or transferred) to the salary account\nof the employees by the employers. It includes all monetary benefits like basic pay.\nconcession, bonus, etc., given as a reward for the work done during the period of an\naccounting year.\n• Payments in Kind: It refers to benefits in kind, i.e., all non-monetary benefits (like rent-free\naccommodation) given to the employees by the employers.\n• Employers' Contribution to Social Security: It refers to such payments as provident fund\ncontributions by the employers on behalf of the employees.\n• Pension on Retirement: To be specific, it does not refer to old-age pensions. It only refers\nto pension payments as a part of the 'Service-Contract' between the employer and the\nemployees.\nAssertion and Reason\nReason\n-Do not include money received by employees in form of It is included under intermediate consumption of the firm.\nreimbursement for business expenses.\n- Do not include any facility which is necessary for work,\nIt is treated as intermediate consumption.\ni.e., employees have no discretion for personal use.\nExample: Uniform to be worn during work.\n- Do not include any financial transfer from third party say\ninsurance companies.\n- Do not include any contribution made by the employees\ntowards social security schemes.\n- Do not include non-factor incomes.\nThe third party, insurance company in this case is not the\nemployer of the injured workers.\nSuch contributions are paid out of compensation of\nemployees.\nThese incomes do not correspond to any value addition\nin the economy.\n(2) Operating Surplus: Operating surplus refers to income from property and entrepreneurship.\nIt includes the following components:\n• Rent: It is a factor income earned by leasing land, building. It includes both actual rent and\nimputed rent.\n• Royalty: It refers to income earned by lending rights of using intellectual property or\nsub-soil assets like rights over mineral deposits of coal, natural gas, etc.\n• Interest: It refers to the price for funds borrowed. In other words, it refers to the payment\nwhich the borrower makes to the lender (supplying money capital for using capital for\nproductive purposes.\n141","file":"macro 4/IMG_0533.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0534","image":"pages/IMG_0534.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\n• Profit: it is a factor income palerated through entrepreneurial-service rendered in\nprocess of production. Front earned by a production unit is divided into three categ\n(i) Dividend: That part of the profit which is distributed among the shareholder\nalso called 'distributed profit.\n(il) Corporate/Corporation Pront Tax: That part of the profit which is paid to\ngovernment by way of 'profit tax'.\n(i) Undistributed Profit: That part of the profit which is retained by the time for to,\nuse, particularly to meet some contingent expenses.\n[Note: Undistributed profit is also called corporate saving, net retained earnings, accumulas\nprofits, undivided profits.)\nAssertion and Reason\nAssertion\n- Dorit include interest paid by government on public\nReason\nFunds raised through public debt are assumed to be uses.\nfor consumption purposes only.\n- Don't include interest paid by consumers on loans.\nThe loan raised by the consumers is assumed to be used\nfor consumption purposes only.\n- Don't include interest paid by one firm to another.\nIt represents a transfer of income rather than production di\ngoods and services.\n- Do include interest paid on loan in national income (or\ndomestic income) (in case it is used for productive\npurposes).\nInterest paid on loans is included in the estimation of\nnational income only when the loan amount is used for\nproduction purposes.\n(3) Mixed Income: Mixed income refers to the incomes of the self-employed persons using\ntheir own labour, land, capital and entrepreneurship in their household enterprises.\n• These incomes are a mixture of wagesing rent, interest and profit. That is why it is called\nmixed income.\n• Separate estimation of wages, rent, interest and profit is not possible. Because, factors of\nproduction are not hired/purchased from the market.\nComponents of Domestic Income (NDPFc)\nCompensation of Employees\nWages and Salaries\nin Cash\nOperating Surplus\n(OS)\nMixed Income of Self-employed\n(MI)\nPayments in Kind\nE\nEmployers' Contribution\nto Social Security on\nbehalf of the Employees\nPension on Retirement\nIncome from\nPropery\nRent\nRoyalty\nInterest\nIncome from\nEntrepreneurship\nProfit\n[Dividend\n(+)\nCorporate Profit Tax\nUndistibuted Profit]\nMethods of Calculating National Income\nCRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS\nNDPFC = Sum total of factor incomes generated within the domestic teritory of a country during an accounting year.\nIt is briefly called domestic income.\n• National income (NPFc) is estimated by adding net factor income from abroad to NDP Fc-\nNNPFC\n= NDPFC\n+ Net factor income from abroad\n4.3.1 Steps involved in Estimating National Income by Income Method\nFollowing steps are involved in estimating national income by\nIncome Method\nincome method:\nStep 1: Identify income generating units and classify them across\ndifferent sectors of the economy (primary sector, secondary\nsector and tertiary sector).\nStep 2: Classification and Estimation of Factor Incomes/Payments\nFactor incomes are classified as:\nCompensation of Employees\nOperating Surplus.\n(Rent + Royalty + Interest + Profit)\nMixed Income of\nSelf-employed\n(i) Compensation of Employees (COE),\n(i) Rent and Royalty,\nDomestic Income\n(NDP+c)\n(iii) Interest, and\n(iv) Profit.\nRent, royalty, interest and profit are clubbed together,\nand\ncalled\nOperating Surplus (OS). Mixed Income of\ncome from Abroad\nNational Income\n(NNPFc)\nSelf-employed (MI) is considered as a separate category\nin case of self-employed individuals. These individuals often use self-owned factors of\nproduction, and therefore generation of factor income occurs as a mixture of rent, interest,\nwages and profit.\nStep 3: Calculation of Domestic Income, viz., NDPFc\nSum total of factor income of all sectors provides us domestic income.\nNDPFC = COE + OS + MI\nStep 4: Calculation of National Income, viz., NNPFC\nConversion of NDP Fc into NP c in order to estimate national income requires addition of net\nfactor income from abroad, i.e., NFIA into NDPFC.\nNNPFC\n= NDPFC\n+ NFIA\n143","file":"macro 4/IMG_0534.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0535","image":"pages/IMG_0535.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\n4.3.2 Precautions Regarding Income Method\nThe following precautions are to be taken while using the income method:\nItems not to be included\nItems to be included\n- Retirement pension\n- Transfer earnings like old-age pension, unemploymen\n• Reason: These are a part of compensation of\nallowance, scholarships, pocket expenses, etc.\nemployees.\n• Reason: Corresponding to transfer payments ther\nno value addition in the economy.\n- Commissions paid on the sale and purchase of\n- Income from illegal activities like theft and gaming\netc.\nsecond-hand goods.\n• Reason: These are a reward for providing factor\n• Reason: This income is generated in terms of blat\nmoney and no estimates\nare\navailable for such\nservices.\nincomes.\n- Brokerage on the sale/purchase of shares and bonds.\n- Income in terms of windfall gains (like from lotteries) or\n• Reason: These are a reward for providing factor\ncapital gains.\nservice.\n• Reason: There is no value addition corresponding to\nwindfall gains.\n- Imputed rent of owner occupied houses.\n- Payments made out of accumulated savings.\n• Reason: House is an income generating asset.\n• Reason: These do not add to the current flow of goods\nAccordingly, imputed value of market rent of owner\nand services.\noccupied houses is taken account of.\n- Income from production for self-consumption.\n• Reason: It leads to generation of income in the\neconomy.\n- Interest paid by production units on capital borrowed.\n• Reason: It is used for investment purposes.\n- Indirect taxes\n• Reason: These are included in NP Mp as these reflect\nmarket price of goods and services but not in national\nincome as national income is estimated at factor cost.\n- Interest paid by consumers on the loans.\n• Reason: The loan amount is used for consumption\npurposes.\nAssertion and Reason\nAssertion\n- Do not separately add corporate tax, dividends and\nReason\nundistributed profits in case profits are collectively given.\nCorporate tax, dividends and undistributed profits are the\ncomponents of profits hence there is no need to include\nthese separately. Otherwise, it will lead to over-estimation\n- Do not include income tax while estimating national\nof national income.\nincome.\nIt is paid out of compensation of employees.\n- Do not separately add wages in cash and kind as well\nas social security contribution by the employer in\ncase compensation of employees is collectively given.\nThese are the components of compensation of employe\nand there is no need to include these separately.\n- Do not include taxes received by government in the\nestimation of national income.\nthese are compulsory transter payments and hence ar\n- Do not include any transaction that corresponds to no\nvalue addition in the economy.\nnot to be included in national income.\nIncome method accounts for only factor incomes\ncorresponding to which there is a flow of goods and\nservices.\n144\nMethods of Calculating National Income\nFollowing are some examples related to the estimation of national income via income method.\nEXAMPLES\nExample 3.\nGiven the following data and using income method, calculate:\n(a) Net Domestic Income, (b) Gross Domestic Income, (c) Net National Income, and (d) Net National\nProduct at Market Price.\nS.No.\nItems\n(iii)\n(iv)\n(vi)\n(vii)\nIndirect taxes\nSubsidies\nDepreciation\nMixed income of self-employed\nOperating surplus\nNet factor income from abroad\nCompensation of employees\nR in crore)\n9,000\n1,800\n28,000\n10,000\n(-) 300\n24,000\nSolution:\n(a) Net Domestic Income\n= Mixed income of self-employed + Operating surplus + Compensation of employees\n= R 28,000 crore + * 10,000 crore + 7 24,000 crore\n= R 62,000 crore\nAns. Net domestic income = R 62,000 crore.\n(b) Gross Domestic Income\n= Net domestic income + Depreciation\n= · 62,000 crore + * 1,700 crore\n= · 63,700 crore\nAns. Gross domestic income = F 63,700 crore.\n(c) Net National Income\n= Net domestic income + Net factor income from abroad\n= R 62,000 crore + (-) R 300 crore\n= R 62,000 crore - F 300 crore\n= 7 61,700 crore\nAns. Net national income = 7 61,700 crore.\nd) Net National Product at Market Price\n= Net national income + Indirect taxes - Subsidies\n= R 61,700 crore + R 9,000 crore - & 1,800 crore\n= 768,900 crore\nAns. Net national product at market price = 7 68,900 crore.\n145","file":"macro 4/IMG_0535.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0536","image":"pages/IMG_0536.jpg","text":"146\nIntroductory Macroeconomics\nThe following information is available for an economy. On the basis of this information thing\nincome method, calculate: (a) Domestic Income, and (b) National Income.\nItems\nR in crore)\n10,000\n5,000\n(vii)\nInterest\nDividend\nMixed income\nUndistributed profit\nSocial security contribution\nCorporate profit tax\nNet factor income from abroad\n3,000\n1,000\nSolution:\n(a) Domestic Income\n= Wages + Rent + Interest + Dividend + Mixed income + Undistributed profit + Social\nsecurity contribution + Corporate profit tax\n= R 10,000 crore + & 5,000 crore + & 400 crore + F 3,000 crore + * 400 crore + R 200 crore\n+* 400 crore + & 400 crore\n= = 19,800 crore\nAns. Domestic income = 7 19,800 crore.\n(b) National Income\n= Domestic income + Net factor income from abroad\n= $ 19,800 crore + R 1,000 crore\n= · 20,800 crore\nAns. National income = R 20,800 crore.\nAVLR 4.3\nCALCULATION OF NATIONAL INCOME USING INCOME METHOD\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\nQ. 4. How do you distinguish between old-age persions and retirement pensions in the context of\nestimation of national income?\nAns. Old-age pensions are unilateral payments or transfer payments. These are not included in the\nestimation of national income. On the other hand, retirement pensions are like a deferred\nwage. These are related to factor services rendered by the recipients prior to their retirement.\nAccordingly, these are included in the estimation of national income,\nMethods of Calculating National Income\nQ. 5. Operating surplus does not arise in (i) subsistence sector, and (i) general government sector of\nthe economy. Why?\nAns. Operating surplus does not arise in the subsistence sector and in the general government sector\nbecause of the following reasons:\n(i) In the subsistence sector, production is meant only for subsistence of the producing families.\nProduction is not meant for sale in the market. There is no marketable surplus. Accordingly,\nthere is no operating surplus.\n(ii) In the general government sector, production is meant only for collective consumption (or\nconsumption by the general public). Goods and services are produced not for sale in the\nmarket, but for general welfare of the people.\nExample: Services of law & order and defence are available to the people free of charge.\nAccordingly, there is no operating surplus in the 'general government\" sector.\nQ. 6. How are dividends, corporate taxes and undistributed profits treated in national income?\nAns. (i) Dividends: These are included in the estimation of national income as these are a part of\nfactor payments.\n(ii) Corporate Taxes: These are not included, as all taxes are transfer payments.\n(iii) Undistributed Profits: These are retained earnings of the firms and are a part of factor\npayments. Therefore, these are included in the estimation of national income.\n4.4 EXPENDITURE METHOD\n• Expenditure method measures national income in terms of expenditure on the purchase of final\ngoods and services produced in the economy during an accounting year.\n• Since final expenditure comprises consumption and investment, it is also called Consumption and\nInvestment Method, or Income Disposal Method.\n• Sum total of expenditure on the final goods produced during the year (within the domestic\nterritory of a country) gives us the market value of GDP, called GDPMP. It is adjusted to find NNPFc\nor national income.\nClassification of Final Expenditure\nFinal expenditure (as expenditure on the purchase of domestically produced final goods and services\nduring an accounting year) is broadly classified as follows:\nComponents of Final Expenditure\nPrivate Final\nConsumption\nExpenditure\n(PFCE)\nGoverment Final\nGross Domestic\nCapital\nNet\nExports\n= GDPMP\n(GFCE)\n(GĐCF)\nFinal\nConsumption\nExpenditure by\nHouseholds\nFinal\nConsumption\nExpenditure by\n+ Non-profit Private\nInstitutions serving\nDomestic\nFixed Capital\nFormation\nInventory\nInvestment\nExports\nImports\nClosing stock\nNet Domestic + Depreciation\nOpening stock\nFixed Capital\nFormation\n147","file":"macro 4/IMG_0536.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0537","image":"pages/IMG_0537.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\n(1) Private Final Consumption Expenditure (PFCE): Private final consumption expende\nrefers to expenditure on final goods and services by the individuals, households\nnon-profit private institutions serving society (like Help-age).\n• It includes:\n• consumer services.\n• consumer non-durable good, that is, goods which are not repeatedly used like but\nor milk.\n> consumer durable goods which are repeatedly used for several years, like tried\nand washing machines.\nAssertion and Reason\nAssertion\nReason\n- Do not include expenditure incurred on purchase or It is indicated under GDF as investment on residente\nconstruction of own-occupied house under PFCE.\nconstruction by households.\n(2) Government Final Consumption Expenditure (GFCE): Government final consumption\nexpenditure refers to expenditure on final goods and services by the government.\n• Forinstance, expenditure on the purchase of goods for consumption by the defence persone,\nexpenditure on maintenance of administrative services like law and order, education, etc.\n(3) Gross Domestic Capital Formation (GDCF): Gross domestic capital formation res t\nexpenditure on the purchase of final goods by the producers. It is also known as investment.\n• These goods are to be further be used in the process of production.\nExample:\nExpenditure by the farmers on the purchase of tractors.\n• Gross domestic capital formation can further be classified as under:\n(i) Gross Domestic Fixed Capitalapirmation or Fixed Investment (GDFCF): It refer\nto expenditure by the producers on the purchase of fixed assets like plant and\nmachinery. Economists often classify fixed investment as:\n> business fixed investment\n- fixed investment by the households in terms of construction of houses, anc\n* public fixed investment or fixedy vestment by the government, like expenditure\nthe government on the construction of roads, dams and bridges.\nAssertion and Reason\nAssertion\n- Do include net purchase of fixed assets from abroad\nunder this category.\nReason\nIt adds to the existing stock of capital in the economy.\n[Note: When a country acquires new or second-hand fixed assets from abroad, it adds to its capital stoc\n148\nThese assets whether new or second-hial strontribute to the productive capacity of the economy\nMethods of Calculating National Income\n(ii) Inventory Investment or Change in Stock: It refers to change in stock during the\nyear. The stock includes:\n> Stock of finished goods,\n› Stock of semi-finished goods, and\n› Stock of raw material.\nAs noted earlier, change in stock is estimated as the difference between 'closing stock\nof the year' and 'opening stock of the year\".\nWhile solving numerical illustrations to estimate GDF, one may encounter various situations. The\nillustrations given below capture those situations.\nÝ TACKLING GROSS DOMESTIC CAPITAL FORMATION (GDCF)\nIllustration 16. Given: NDF =7 500, Depreciation = 7 100. Find GDCF.\nSol.\nGDCF = Z 500 + ≤ 100\n= 7600.\n[Since NDCF is given, we add depreciation to find GDCF.]\nIllustration 17. Given: GDFCF = 7 600, Change in Stock = 7 100. Find GDCF.\nGDF = F 600 + = 100\n= 7700.\n[Since GDFCF is given, we add change in stock to find GDCF.]\nIllustration 18. Given: NDFCF = 7 700, Depreciation = 7 200, Change in Stock = 7 100. Find GDCF.\nSol.\nGDF = 7 700 + R 200 + R 100\n= · 1,000.\n[Since NDFCF is given, we add both depreciation and change in stock to find GDCF.]\n[Note: GDFCF refers to gross domestic fixed capital formation.\nNDFCF refers to net domestic fixed capital formation.]\n(4) Net Exports (X - M): Net exports refer to the difference between exports and imports during\nan accounting year.\n• Exports are an expenditure by the foreigners on the domestically produced final goods\nand services. On the other hand, imports are an expenditure on the goods and services\nproduced abroad.\n• Net of Exports (X - M) or 'net export adjustment' is an essential component of expenditure\non the domestically produced goods and services.\n[Note: Exports are included in GDP because exports are a part of domestically produced goods and services.]\nWhile solving numerical illustrations to estimate net exports, one may encounter various situations.\nThe illustrations given below capture those situations.\nTACKLING NET EXPORTS\nIllustration 19. Given: Exports (x) = 7 500, Imports (M) = R 200. Find Net Exports.\nSol.\nNet Exports = 7 500 - F 200 = F 300.\nIllustration 20. Given: Exports (X) = * 500, Imports (M) = 7 800. Find Net Exports.\nSol.\nNet Exports = P 500 - 7 800 = (-) R 300.\n149","file":"macro 4/IMG_0537.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0538","image":"pages/IMG_0538.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\nIllustration 21. Given:Imports (M) = & 500. Find Net Exports.\nNet Exports = 0 - 7 500 = (-) R 500.\nIllustration 22. Given:Exports (X) = 7 500. Find Net Exports.\nSol.\nNet Exports = 7 500 - 0 = R 500.\nIllustration 23. Given:Net Imports = (-)R 450. Find Net Exports.\nNet Exports = 7 450,\n[Note: Net Exports are the negative of Net Imports; thus, if Net Imports are (-) negative then Net Expot ar\n(+) positive.]\n• Sum total of expenditure on the domestically produced goods and services during ar\naccounting year is called GDP Mp (gross domestic product at market price).\n• Having estimated GDP Mp, we find out NPFc (national income) in terms of the following\nequation:\nBasis of Difference\n1. Meaning\n2. Formula\n3. Variant\nNNPFC\n= GDP MP\n- Depreciation\n- Net indirect taxes\n+ Net factor income from abroad\nTable 4.4: Net Exports vs. Net Factor Income from Abroad\nNet Exports\nNet Factor Income from Abroad\n- Net exports refer to the difference\nbetween exports and imports of\n- Net factor income from abroad refers to the excess of\nfactor income earned by our residents from rest of the\nworld over factor income earned by non-residents from\nthe domestic territory of our country.\n- Exports- Imports (X- M).\n- Factor income received by our residents from rest of\nthe world - Factor income paid to non-residents in the\ndomestic territory of our country.\n- It is a part of domestic income.\n- It is a part of national income.\nAVLR 4.4\nNET FACTOR INCOME FROM ABROAD (NFIA) AND NET EXPORTS (NX)\n4.4.1 Steps involved in Estimating National Income by\nExpenditure Method\nFollowing steps are involved in estimating national income via expenditure method:\nStep 1: Identification of economic units incurring final expenditure\nThis step involves identification of economic units that incur final expenditure in the domestic\neconomy. These include household sector, producer sector, government sector and rest of\nthe world sector.\n150\nMethods of Calculating National Income\nStep 2: Estimation of Final Expenditure\nThis step involves estimation of final expenditure by the\nExpenditure Method\npreviously identified economic units under four heads, i.e.,\n• Private final consumption expenditure\n> Government final consumption expenditure\n* Gross domestic capital formation\n> Net exports\nPrivate Final\nConsumption Expenditure\nGovernment Final\nConsumption Expenditure\nGross Domestic Capital Formation\nNet Exports\nSum total of these components give GDPMp.\nStep 3: Calculation of Domestic Income, viz., NDPFc\nConversion of GDPMp into NDPFc in order to estimate\ndomestic income requires subtraction of depreciation and\nnet indirect taxes.\nNDPFC = GDPMP - Depreciation - Net indirect taxes\nStep 4: Calculation of National Income, viz., NNPFc\nGross Domestic Product\nat Market Price (GDPMP)\n(-) Depreciation\n(- Net Indirect Taxes\nDomestic Income\n(NDPFc)\n(+) Net Factor\nIncome from Abroad\nNational Income\n(NNPFc)\nConversion of NDPFc into NNPFc in order to calculate\nnational income requires addition of net factor income from abroad, i.e., NFIA to NDPFC-\nNNPFC = NDPFC + NFIA\n4.4.2 Precautions Regarding Expenditure Method\nThe following precautions are to be taken while using expenditure method:\nItems to be included\nItems not to be included\n- Final expenditure\n- Expenditure on intermediate goods and services.\n• Reason: It is to be interpreted as expenditure on final\n• Reason: Its value is included in the value of final\ngoods and services and hence included in national\nexpenditure. If included again it will lead to double\nincome accounting.\ncounting of expenditure.\n- Expenditure on commission/brokerage paid for sale/ - Expenditure on second-hand goods.\npurchase of second-hand goods.\n• Reason: Their value has already been accounted\n• Reason: Commission paid for sale/purchase\nduring the year when they were initially produced and\nsecond-hand goods involves new services provided in\npurchased by the final users.\nthe economy.\n- Expenditure on brokerage paid for sale/purchase of\nshares and bonds.\n• Reason: Brokerage paid for sale/purchase of shares\nand bonds involve new services provided in the\n- Expenditure on sale of financial assets, shares,\ndebenture and bonds.\n• Reason: These are mere paper claims and are not\nrelated to the production of final goods and services.\neconomy.\n- Imputed value of expenditure on goods produced for\nself-consumption, imputed rent of owner occupied\nhouses.\n- Expenditure on transfer payments by the government\n(like old-age pension, scholarships).\n• Reason: These payments do not cause any value\naddition in the economy.\n• Reason: Own-account production is considered as a\nproduced activity. Also, imputed rent of houses should\nalso be taken into account.\n151","file":"macro 4/IMG_0538.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0539","image":"pages/IMG_0539.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\nGiven below are some examples related to the estimation or national income via expenditure metho\nEXAMPLES\nCalculate Net National Product mopctor Cost by Expenditure Method from the following data:\nItems\nS.No.\n(R in lakh)\nPrivate final consumption expenditure\nGovernment final consumption expenditure\nIndirect taxes\nConsumption of fixed capital\nChange in stock\nGross domestic capital formation\nNet exports\nNet factor income from abroad\n(-) 10\n(-) 10\nSubsidies\nSolution:\nNet National Product at Factor Cost\n= Private final consumption expenditure + Government final consumption expenditure\n+ Gross domestic capital formation + Net exports - Consumption of fixed capital - Indirect\ntaxes + Subsidies + Net factor income from abroad\n= R lakh + 7 50 lakh+7 lakh lakh+ (-) lakh+ 10 lakh - 7 30 lakh - 7 60 lakh + +R lakh + (-) * F lah\n= * 450 lakh + 7 50 lakh + 7 330 lakh - 7 10 lakh - 7 30 lakh - 7 60 lakh + & 10 lakh - 7 10 lakh\nAns. Net national product at factor cost = < 730 lakh.\nExample 6.\nFrom the following data, calculate the GDP at both (a) Market Price, and (b) Factor Cost.\nS.No.\nGross investment\nItems\nNet exports\nNet indirect taxes\nDepreciation\nNet factor income from abroad\nPrivate consumption expenditure\nGovernment purchases of goods and services\nSolution:\n(R in crore)\n(-) 5\n100\n(a) GUMp* Private consumption expenditure + Government purchases of goods and services\nGross investment + Net exports\n= ‹ 350 crore + 7 100 crore + & 90 crore + R 10 crore\n= ‹ 550 crore\nAns. GDP MP = R 550 crore.\n152\nMethods of Calculating National Income\n(b) GDPFC\n= GDPMP - Net indirect taxes\n= 7 550 crore - 7 5 crore\n= 7545 crore\nAns. GDPFc = R545 crore.\nExample 7.\nFrom the following data, find NDPFC\nItems\nS.No.\n(iii)\n(vii)\nGross domestic fixed investment\nInventory investment\nDepreciation\nIndirect taxes\nSubsidies\nConsumption expenditure\nResidential construction investment\n(® in crore)\n10,000\n5,000\n2,000\n1,000\n2,000\n20,000\nSolution:\nGDPMP = Consumption expenditure + Gross domestic fixed investment + Inventory investment\n= F 20,000 crore + R 10,000 crore + 7 5,000 crore\n= · 35,000 crore\nNDPFC\n= GDPMP - Depreciation - Indirect taxes + Subsidies\n= 7 35,000 crore - R 2,000 crore - & 1,000 crore + & 2,000 crore\n= T 34,000 crore\nAns. NDPFc = 734,000 crore.\nExample 8.\nFrom the following data, calculate Net National Product at Market Price.\nS.No.\nItems\n(R in crore)\n(-) 5\nNet factor income from abroad\n100\nPrivate final consumption expenditure\nPersonal tax\nGovernment final consumption expenditure\nCorporation tax\n(vi)\nGross domestic capital formation\n(-) 10\n(vii)\nNet exports\nConsumption of fixed capital\nSolution:\nNet National Product at Market Price\n= Private final consumption expenditure + Government final consumption expenditure + Gross\ndomestic capital formation + Net exports - Consumption of fixed capital + Net factor income\nfrom abroad\n153","file":"macro 4/IMG_0539.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0540","image":"pages/IMG_0540.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\n= * 100 crore + ‹ 20 crore + R 30 crade + (-) R 10 crore - 7 25 crore + (-) R 5 crore\n= R 100 crore + 7 20 crore +7 30 crore - F 10 crore - 7 25 crore - F 5 crore\n= = 110 crore\nAns. Net national product at market price = F 110 crore.\nFrom the following data, find out (a) Gross Domestic Product at Market Price, and ( to hoe,\nto Abroad:\nS.No.\nItems\n(R in crore)\n3\n(vii)\n(viii)\nFactor income from abroad\nNet indirect tax\nPrivate final consumption expenditure\nGovernment final consumption expenditure\nNet domestic fixed capital formation\nNet imports\nGross national product at factor cost\nDepreciation\nChange in stock\n140\n(-) 60\n1,260\nSolution:\n(a) Gross Domestic Product at Market Price\n= Private final consumption expenditure + Government final consumption expenditure\n+ Net domestic fixed capital formation + Change in stock + Depreciation - Net impons\n= R 770 crore +& 270 crore + & 220 crore + R 30 crore + R 70 crore - (-) R 60 crore\n= R 770 crore + & 270 crore + & 220 crore + & 30 crore + & 70 crore + R 60 crore\n= 71,420 crore\nAns. Gross domestic product at market price = & 1,420 crore.\n(b) Gross National Product at Factor Cost\n= Gross product at market price - Net indirect tax + Factor income from abroad\n- Factor income to abroad\nOr, Factor Income to Abroad\n= Gross domestic product at market price - Net indirect tax + Factor income from abroac\n- Gross national product at factor cost\n= * 1,420 crore - & 140 crore + & 40 crore - R 1,260 crore\n= 7 60 crore\nAns. Factor income to abroad = R 60 crore.\nAVLR 4.5\nCALCULATION OF NATIONAL INCOME USING EXPENDITURE METHOD\n154\nMethods of Calculating National Income\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\nQ. 7. What causes increase in inventory stock?\nAns. Increase in inventory stock is caused by two factors:\n(i) Unexpected Fall in Demand in the Current Year: Example: Producers may have expected\ndemand to the tune of 50,000 umbrellas but, owing to the failure of monsoon, only 10,000\numbrellas are sold during an year. Accordingly, 40,000 umbrellas are added to the existing\nstock.\n(ii) Expected Rise in Demand in the Near Future: Producers may expect a spurt (rise) in demand\n(and therefore, increase in price) in the near future. Accordingly, they pile up stocks during\nthe current year.\nQ.8. Export receipts are not a part of net factor income from abroad. Why?\nAns. This is because of two reasons:\n(i) Exports refer to the purchase of domestically produced goods by the rest of the world.\nGoods produced within the domestic territory of a country are to be treated as a part of\nGDP.\n(ii) Export receipts refer to revenue of the firms from the sale of its output. These are not the\nreceipts of factor incomes from abroad which are available in the form of rent, interest,\nprofit and wages.\nQ. 9. Exports are just sales yielding revenue, not income. Comment.\nAns. It is true that exports are just sales and yield revenue, not income. Exports are just sales because\nthese are purchases of rest of the world from the domestic economy. Accordingly, exports yield\nrevenue (receipts from sales). These do not yield income. Because, income is in the form of rent,\ninterest, profit or wages. Exports do not yield any of these directly.\n[Note: It is out of revenue that a firm distributes incomes to the owners of factors of production.]\nQ. 10. Is net factor income from abroad zero in case exports = imports?\nAns. No. Net factor income from abroad is the difference between the factor income earned by\nnormal residents of a country from abroad and the factor income earned by non-residents in\nour country. It has nothing to do with exports and imports.\n4.5 RECONCILIATION OF THREE METHODS\nTriple Identity\n• The flow chart given on the next page shows how estimation of national income using different\nmethods is reconciled.\n• We get triple identity in terms of the equality of estimation of nationall income using\n(i) product or value added method,\n(ii) income method, and\n(iii) expenditure method.\n• This happens because all the three methods measure the value of same physical output produced\nin the economy via different angles.\n155","file":"macro 4/IMG_0540.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0541","image":"pages/IMG_0541.jpg","text":"156\nIntroductory Macroeconomics\nThree Methods of Calculating National Income\nValue Added Method\nProduct Method\nIncome Method\nGVAMP\nin the Primary Sector\nGVAMP\nin the Secondary Sector\nin the Tertiary Sector\nCompensation of\nEmployees\nOperating Surplus\n(Rent + Royalty + Interest + Profit)\nMixed Income of the\nSelf-employed\nGross Domestic Product\nat Market Price (GDPMP)\n(-) Depreciation\n(-) Net Indirect Taxes\nDomestic Income\n(NDP Fc)\nNational Income\n(NNPFC)\nDomestic Income\n(NDPFC)\n(+) Net Factor\ny Income from Abroad\nNational Income\n(NNPFC)\nIllustration showing Triple Identity\nGiven the following data, calculate national income by:\n(a) Value Added Method, (b) Income Method, and (c) Expenditure Method.\n(хії)\n(xiii)\n(xiv)\n(xv)\nGross value added in the primary sector at market price\nGross value added in the secondary sector at market price\nGross value added in the tertiary sector at market price\nCompensation of employees\nRent and Royalty\nInterest\nMixed income of the self-employed\nProfit\nPrivate final consumption expenditure\nGovernment final consumption expenditure\nGross domestic capital formation\nNet exports\nNet indirect taxes\nDepreciation\nNet factor income from abroad\nExpenditure Method\nPrivate Final\nConsumption Expenditure\nGovernment Final\nConsumption Expenditure\nGross Domestic Capital Formation\nNet Exports\nGross Domestic Product\nat Market Price (GDPMP)\n- Depreciation\n(- Net Indirect Taxes\nDomestic Income\n(NDPFc)\n(+) Net Factor\nIncome from Abroad\nNational Income\n(NNPFc)\nR in crore)\n1,500\n2,400\n1,100\n1,400\n1,000\n450\n500\n650\n3,400\n550\n1,200\n(-) 150\n600\n400\nMethods of Calculating National Income\nThree Methods of Calculating National Income\nValue Added Method\nIncome Method\nProduct Method\nExpenditure Method\n1,500\nGVAMP in the\nPrimary Sector\n1,400\n(+) 2,400\nGVAMP in the\nSecondary Sector\n(+) 1,100\nGVAMP in the\nTertiary Sector\n(+) 1,000\n(+) 450\n(+) 650\n(+) 500\nCo Employees\nOperating Surplus\n(Rent and Royalty\n+ Interest\n+ Profit)\nMixed Income of the\nSelf-employed\n3,400\nConsumption Expenditure\nGovernment Final\n(+) 550 Consumption Expenditure\nGross Domestic\n(+1,200\n(+) -150\nCapital Formation\nNet Exports\n5,000\nGDPMP\n5,000\n-400 (→) Depreciation\ny-600 (-) Net Indirect Taxes\n4,000\n4,090\nNDPFC\nNNPFC\n4,000\n1+90 come from Abroad\n4,090\nNDPFC\n+90 ((n2 Net Facto Abroad\nNNPFC\n4,000\n4,090\nGDPMP\n-400 | (-) Depreciation\n-600, (-) Net Indirect Taxes\nNDPFC\n190 income on Abroad\nNNPFC\n4.6 TREATMENT OF DIFFERENT ITEMS WHILE\nESTIMATING NATIONAL INCOME\nDo not include the following items in the estimation of national income:\nS.No.\n'Not to be Included' Items\nGifts from Abroad\n- These are transfer payments and, therefore, not included in national\nUnemployment Allowance\n(iii)\n(iv)\nFinancial Help to Tsunami Victims\nPurchase of Vegetables by a\nRestaurant\nExpenses on Electricity by a Factory\nincome.\n- This is available to those persons who are not employed. This is,\ntherefore, only a transfer payment not included in national income.\n- It is not included in national income since it is a transfer payment.\n- It is not included in national income since it is an intermediate\nconsumption.\n- It is not included in national income since it is a part of intermediate\nconsumption.\n- By convention, value added through such activities is not accounted\nfor in the estimation of national income/product.\n(Vii)\nLeisure-time Activities like Growing\nVegetables\nby Household\nin his\nKitchen Garden\nServices rendered by the Housewives\n(vili)\nMoney Received by an Individual\nfrom his Son Working Abroad\n- These are not included in national income because it is difficult to\nfind their market value, and these are not rendered for the purpose\nof earning income.\n- It is not included in national income of India because it is a kind of\ntransfer income.\n157","file":"macro 4/IMG_0541.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0542","image":"pages/IMG_0542.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\nInterest Received from a Friend\nproduction purpose.\non Loans offered to him for the\nPurchase of a Motorbike\nCorporate Profit Tax\n- Itis not included in the estimation of national income as it forsat\nof profits as a transfer payment to the government.\n[Note: If pront is not known in any numerical question) we can find its value by adding up: (a) dividen\n(b) undistributed pronts, and (C) corpoth gopront tax. However, if the question is: now do we tr\ncorporate tax in the estimation of national income then the answer is as in point (x) above.l\nDo include the following items in the estimation of national income:\nS.No.\n\"To be Included' Items\nReason\nDefence and Security Services (For\n- The services of these persons may be taken as intermediary or final.\nmaintaining law & order and defence\nThese are final services so far as they provide security and peaceful\nof the country- the services of defence\nexistence to the households. On the other hand, they may be!\npersonnel, policemen, judges and\ntreated as intermediary services in so far as they provide peaceful\nothers)\nenvironment to the productive process. However, it is very difficult\nto assess the extent to which these services are intermediate or\nfinal. Accordingly, as a matter of convention, these are treated as\nfinal services and are, therefore, included in national income.\nFree Services by the Government\n- Free services by the government like free education, free medical\nfacilities or street lighting. These are a part of government final\nconsumption expenditure.\n(11)\nEmployer's Contribution to Provident\n- It is included in national income, since it is paid by the employers on\nbehalf of the employees.\nRent Received by Indian Residents\n- It is income from the rest of the world and forms a part of net factor\non Buildings Rented out to Foreign\nEmbassies in India\nincome from abroad. It is included in national income.\nProfits Earned by a Branch of an Indian\nBank in London\n- It is included in the national income since it is a part of net factor\nincome from abroad.\nWages Received by the Indian Employees\nWorking in Pakistan Embassy\n- It is included in national income since Indian employees of Pakistan\n(vii)\nSalary to Foreign Technical Specialists\nEmbassy are normal residents of India.\n- As a payment of factor income to the 'non-resident\", it reduces\nDividend Received by an Indian\nnational income.\nResident from his Investment in a\n- this included in national income of India because it is a part of net.\nForeign Financial Firm\nfactor income from abroad.\nCRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS\nA Fundamental Question\nestimation of national product income which flow from producers to the consumers through the market.\n• According to the authors of the present text, this is a wrong notion. To understand this lets take a\nexample of an economy where farming is the only production activity.\nparte mare because produces barely enough tor consumption by the farmin\nfamilies themselves.\nwithout production? Perhaps never.\nwhich are routed through the markful to be recorded, such an economy where production is only fo\nLet it be clearly understood that the imputed value of pr\nalways included in the estimation of national income.\n158\nthe farmers) for self-consumption is\nMethods of Calculating National Income\nABILITY ZONE\nA. Included in both Domestic Income and\nNational Income\n1. Domestic manufacturing output\n2. Wages paid to employees (within India)\n3. Government provision of defence & security services\n4. Free services by Government (e.g., free schooling,\npublic hospitals, street-lighting)\n5. Employer's contribution to Provident Fund\nB. Included in Domestic Income but NOT in\nNational Income (= factor payments to non-\nresidents for production occurring in India)\n1. Salary paid to foreign technical specialists working in\nIndia\n2. Profits of a foreign company earned in India and\nremitted to its parent abroad\n3. Interest paid by an Indian firm to a foreign bank on a\nproduction loan\n4. Rent paid by an Indian firm to a foreign owner of\ncommercial property in India\n5. Royalty/technology fee paid by an Indian manufacturer\nto a foreign patent holder\nC. Included in National Income but NOT in\nDomestic Income (= factor income from abroad\naccruing to Indian residents)\n1. Rent received by an Indian resident from a building\nrented to a foreign embassy in India\n2. Profits earned by a branch of an Indian bank in London\n3. Wages received by Indian employees working for the\nPakistan Embassy (in India)\n4. Dividend received by an Indian resident from shares in\na foreign company\n5. Interest received by an Indian resident on foreign\ngovernment/corporate bonds\nTable of Transactions\nReason\nNew production within India adds to value added →\nFactor income generated domestically → included in\nboth.\nTreated (by convention) as final services → counted in\nboth.\nGovernment final consumption expenditure → included\nin both.\nPart of compensation of employees→ included in both.\nReason\nFactor income to abroad → included in domestic,\nexcluded from national.\nOperating surplus accrues to non-residents → domestic\nYES, national NO.\nFactor payment to non-resident → domestic YES,\nnational NO.\nFactor income accrues abroad → excluded from\nnational.\nFactor income to non-resident → reduces national\nReason\nEmbassies are foreign territory; receipt is from abroad\n→ national YES, domestic NO.\nIncome earned overseas by residents → included only\nin national.\nPaid by a foreign entity (foreign territory) → factor\nincome from abroad.\nFactor income from abroad → national YES, domestic\nNO.\nEarnings from foreign financial assets → counted only\nin national.","file":"macro 4/IMG_0542.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0543","image":"pages/IMG_0543.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\nPower Points [Summary of the Chapter]\n• Three Different Methods of Measuring National Income\n(i Product Method (or Value Added Method),\n(il) Income Method,\n(iii) Expenditure Method.\n• Product Method (or Value Added Method)\n• Meaning: It is that method which measures the gross value added or gross domestic prod\nat market price as the sum total of value added by all the producing units within the dome\nterritory of the country, during the period of an accounting year.\n• It is adjusted to find national income (NNPFc).\n• Precaution: Add only the market value of final goods and services, do not include market value\nintermediate goods and services.\nOr\nAvoid double counting.\n• Double Counting and Avoiding it: Double counting occurs if output of all the producers is adde\nup without considering the fact that output of one producer may have been used as an input i\nthe other.\nIt can be best avoided by adding up value added, rather than output of different firms.\nValue Added = Value of output\n- Intermediate consumption (referring to expenditure on intermediate goods\nand services)\n• Income Method\n• Meaning: It is that method which measures domestic income as the sum total of factor incomes\n(rent + interest + profit + wages) generated within the domestic territory of a country during the\nperiod of an accounting year.\n• Net factor income from abroad is added to domestic income to find national income (NNPF).\n• Precaution: Add only factor incomes, do not include transfer payments. Also, do not include\nincome generated through illegal activities (like, gambling).\n• Expenditure Method\n• Meaning: It is that method which measures the gross domestic product at market price as the sun\ntotal of the expenditure (consumption expenditure and investment expenditure on the purchas\nperiod of an accounting year.\nof final goods and services produced within the domestic territory of the economy during the\n• It is adjusted to find national income (NNPFc).\n• Precaution: Consider only the expenditure on final goods that causes:\n(i) final consumption, or\n(i) capital formation (also called investment);\n• Do not include expenditure on intermediate goods and services.\n• Also, do not consider expenditure on second-hand goods or on shares and bonds.\nWORK-BO\nCompetency Based Learning: Obje\n1. Multiple Choice Questions\nChoose the correct option:\n1. Value Addition =\n(Choose the correct option(s) t\n(i) Domestic Sales\n(iii) Value of output\nOptions:\n(a) (i) and (ii)\n(c) (ii),(ili) and (iv)\n2. Operating surplus does not i\n(a) interest\n(c) profit\n3. Identify the correct sequen\nA. Add NFIA to calculate N\nB. Add sales and change it\nC. Value of intermediate\nGVA at MP\nD. Deduct value of depre\nChoose the correct answe\n(a) C, D, A, B\n(c) D, C, B, A\n4. Sales for a firm is calculat\n(a) Value of output + Inte\n(b) Value of output - Int\n(c) Value of output - Ch\n(d) Closing stock + Value\n5. Value added method m\nterritory of a country?\n(a) Household consume\n(b) The producing ente\n(c) The producing ente\n(d) Both (b) and (c)\n6. Difference between clo\n(a) increase in stock\n(c) decrease in stock\n160","file":"macro 4/IMG_0543.HEIC"}],"boxes":[{"title":"CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS","page":"IMG_0529","text":"A Standard Precaution Relating to the Use of Value Added Method\n• Value added should not be contused with the value of output. Value of output simply refers try\n• On the other hand, value auded reters to the market value of the goods produced minus market val\nmarket value of the goods produced.\nthe goods used as inputs/taw materar in the process of production. Goods used as inputs/raw m\nare called intermediate goods. Thus,\nValue Added = Value of output - Value of intermediate goods used in production\n• Bretty, wille value or output includeshan f value of intermediate goods used in production, vi\nadded does not.\nConcept of Change in Stock (4Stock)\n• Change in stock is measured as the difference between 'closing stock of the accounting ear\nand 'opening stock of the accounting year'\nAStock = Closing Stock - Opening Stock\nThe illustrations given\nbelow show various situations dealing with the estimation o\nchange in stock.\nTACKLING CHANGE IN STOCK\nIllustration 7. Given: Closing Stock = 7 2,000, Opening Stock = F 1,500. Find Change in Stock.\nChange in Stock = 7 2,000 - 7 1,500\n= · 500.\nIllustration 8. Given: Closing Stock = 7 4,500, Opening Stock = F 5,000. Find Change in Stock.\nChange in Stock = 7 4,500 - · 5,000\n= (-)R 500.\nIllustration 9. Given: Closing Stock = & 1,500. Find Change in Stock.\nSol.\nChange in Stock = 7 1,500 - 0\n= · 1,500.\nlustration 10. Given: Opening Stock = 7 2,000. Find Change in Stock.\nChange in Stock = 0 - R 2,000\n[Note: (i) Change in stock can be written as At (1) aon) et change in stock or increase/decrease in unsold sto\n(i) Sign given along with it (AStock) is not be ignored.]\nConcept of Intermediate Consumption\n• Intermediate consumption refers to value of non-factor inputs (all inputs other than fac\ninputs of land, labour, capital and entreareneurship). Primarily, it includes value of raw mater\nused in the process of production.\n132\nMethods of Calculating National Income\nWhile solving numerical illustrations to estimate intermediate consumption one may encounter\nvarious situations. The following illustrations show those situations.\nỸ TACKLING INTERMEDIATE CONSUMPTION (IC)\nIllustration 11. Given: IC = 7 1,500, Import of Raw Material = 7 300. Find IC.\nIC = 7 1,500 (The term IC includes import of raw material).\nIllustration 12. Given:Purchase of Raw Material from Domestic Firm = 7 2,000, Import of Raw Material = 7 500.\nFind IC.\nSol.\nIC = R2,000 + 7500 = 72,500.\nIllustration 13. Given:Domestic Purchase of Raw Material = 7 500, Import of Raw Material = 71,200, Find IC.\nSol.\nIC = 7 500+71,200 = 7 1,700.\nIllustration 14. Given:Purchase of Raw Material = 7 2,000, Import of Raw Material - 7 500. Find IC.\nIC = · 2,000.\n[Note: We are not including import of raw material (R 500) in intermediate consumption as it is assumed to be\nincluded in the purchase of raw material R 2,000).]\nIllustration 15. Given: Purchase of oil to be used to lubricate the machines = 7 2,000, Purchase of gas = 7 500,\nPurchase of machinery = 75,000. Find IC.\nIC = R 2,000 + 7 500 = 7 2,500.\n[Note: Purchase of machinery will not be included in estimating intermediate consumption as it is considered to be\na final capital good used by the producer as a fixed asset.]\nIllustration\nTable 4.1 illustrates how 'value added' is estimated.\nTable 4.1: Estimating Value Added (or Value Addition)\nProducing Enterprise\nValue of\nCost of Intermediate\nOutput\nGoods [Intermediate\nConsumption]\n3\n1. Farmer\n600\n200\n2. Flour Mill\n800\n600\n200\n3. Baker\n1,000\n800\n4. Shopkeeper\n1,200\n1,000\nTotal\n3,600\n2,600\n1,000\n• In Table 4.1, it is assumed that the production of wheat involves cost of intermediate consumption\nof T 200 (it may include cost of inputs like seeds, fertilizers, irrigation expenses, etc.). Accordingly,\nvalue added by the farmer is equal to 7 600 - 7 200 = 7 400.\n•The flour mill buys wheat for & 600 and sells flour for 7 800. Value added by the flour mill, therefore.\nis equal to & 800 - F 600 = 7 200.\n• Further, the baker buys flour for ‹ 800 and sells the bread for * 1,000 to the shopkeeper. The value\nadded by the baker is * 1,000 - 7 800 = 7 200.\n• The shopkeeper buys the bread for * 1,000 and sells the bread to the households for & 1,200.\nThe value added by the shopkeeper is * 1,200 - 1,000 = 7 200.\n• Thus, the gross value added (GDP Mp) by all the producing enterprises is 7 400 + 7 200 + 7 200\n+· 200 = · 1,000.\n133","image":"pages/IMG_0529.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS","page":"IMG_0530","text":"GDP MP (GVAMP)\nperiod of one year.\n= Market value of final goods and services produced in the economy during the period of one year.\nHaving estimated GDP Mp, We will find NPrc (national income) in terms of the following equation.\nNNPFC\n= GDP MP Or GVAMp - Depreciation - Net indirect taxes+ Net factor income from abroad\n[Note: Domestic income is identified with NDP Fc and national income is identified with NPFc.\n4.2.1 Steps involved in Estimating National Income by\nValue Added Method\nFollowing steps are involved in estimating national income by value added method:\nStep 1: Identification of production units across all sectors of\nValue Added Method\nproduction, viz., primary, secondary and tertiary sector.\nThis step involves identification and classification of\nProduct Method\nproduction units in the domestic economy into primary\nsector (involving exploitation of natural resources-fishing,\nlogging, etc.), secondary sector (involving conversion of\none type of commodity into another), and tertiary sector\n(rendering services like banking, teaching, medical care,\nGVAMp in the Primary Sector\nGVAMp in the Secondary Sector\nGVAMP in the Tertiary Sector\netc.).\nStep 2: Estimation of GDP MP Or GVAMp by summation of GVAMP\nby all the sectors identified in Step 1, i.e.,\nGross Domestic Product\nat Market Price (GDPMP)\nGDP MP/GVAMP\n(-) Depreciation\n(- Net Indirect Taxes\n= GVAMp of Primary Sector\n+ GVAMP of Secondary Sector\nDomestic Income\n+ GVAMp of Tertiary Sector\n(NDPFC)\n(+) Net Factor\nStep 3: Calculation of Domestic Income, viz., NDPFc\nIncome from Abroad\nThis involves conversion of GDP Mp into NDP FC-\nNational Income\n(NNPFc)\nEstmation or domestic income require ssois = ton of depreciation and net indirect taxes\nfrom GDP MP.\nNDP FC\n= GDP MP\n- Depreciation\n- Net indirect taxes\n134\nMethods of Calculating National Income\nStep 4: Calculation of National Income, viz., NPFC\nConversion of NDPc into NNPFc in order to estimate national income requires addition of\nnet factor income from abroad, i.e., NFIA into NDP+c-\nNNP+C= NDPFC + Net factor income from abroad\n4.2.2 Precautions Regarding Value Added Method\nFollowing are some important precautions regarding value added method:\nItems to be included\nCommission earned on account of the sale and\npurchase of second-hand goods is included in the\nestimation of value added.\n• Reason: It is a reward for the services rendered.\n- Own account production of goods of the producing\nunits is taken into account while estimating value added.\n• Reason: These goods are like those produced for the\nmarket but are not sold owing to their need by the\nproducers themselves.\n• Example: Cars\nused by the car producers\ntransporting their employees.\n- Imputed value of production for self-consumption is\ntaken into account.\n• Reason: These goods are like those produced for the\nItems not to be included\n- Value of the sale and purchase of second-hand goods\nis not included in the estimation of'value added or GDP.\n• Reason: It is already accounted for during the year\nthey were produced.\n- Value of intermediate goods is not included in the\nestimation of value added.\n• Reason: Its value is reflected in the value of final\ngoods.\n- Services for self-consumption are not considered\nwhile estimating value added.\n• Reason: It is difficult to estimate their market value,\nlike, for example, services of housewives.\n• Example: Wheat produced and consumed by the\nfarming families themselves.\n- Imputed rent on the owner occupied house is to be\ntaken into account.\n• Reason: All houses have rental value, no matter these\nare self-occupied or rented out.\n- Value addition related to illegal activities is not\nincluded.\n• Reason: No specific data input is available.\n4.2.3 Problem of Double Counting and the Ways to Tackle it\nMeaning of Double Counting\n• Double counting refers to counting of the value of same product more than once.\nWhy does the problem of Double Counting occur?\n• The problem arises when in the estimation of GDP or GVA the value of certain goods is counted\nmore than once.\n135","image":"pages/IMG_0530.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"IMG_0533","text":"Q. 1. \"Value addition occurs even when goods do not undergo any material transtormation.\ntrue or false and give a reason for the same.\nnel level when goods are arrased tor resale, Without any mate\ntransformation.\nQ. 2. Imputed rent on owner occupied houses does not involve any payment to others. According\nit should not be included in the estimation of national income. \" State true or false and i-\nreason for the same.\nAns. False. Imputed rent is included in the estimation of national income as all houses have res\nvalue, no matter these are self-occupied or rented out.\nQ.3. What do you mean by the error of double counting?\nAns. Error of double counting occurs when the value of some goods produced in the economi\ncounted more than once in the estimation of GDP.\nExample: Entire value of wheat sold by the farmers is considered along with the value of whet\npurchased by the miller who uses wheat as an input for producing wheat flour.","image":"pages/IMG_0533.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS","page":"IMG_0534","text":"NDPFC = Sum total of factor incomes generated within the domestic teritory of a country during an accounting year.\nIt is briefly called domestic income.\n• National income (NPFc) is estimated by adding net factor income from abroad to NDP Fc-\nNNPFC\n= NDPFC\n+ Net factor income from abroad\n4.3.1 Steps involved in Estimating National Income by Income Method\nFollowing steps are involved in estimating national income by\nIncome Method\nincome method:\nStep 1: Identify income generating units and classify them across\ndifferent sectors of the economy (primary sector, secondary\nsector and tertiary sector).\nStep 2: Classification and Estimation of Factor Incomes/Payments\nFactor incomes are classified as:\nCompensation of Employees\nOperating Surplus.\n(Rent + Royalty + Interest + Profit)\nMixed Income of\nSelf-employed\n(i) Compensation of Employees (COE),\n(i) Rent and Royalty,\nDomestic Income\n(NDP+c)\n(iii) Interest, and\n(iv) Profit.\nRent, royalty, interest and profit are clubbed together,\nand\ncalled\nOperating Surplus (OS). Mixed Income of\ncome from Abroad\nNational Income\n(NNPFc)\nSelf-employed (MI) is considered as a separate category\nin case of self-employed individuals. These individuals often use self-owned factors of\nproduction, and therefore generation of factor income occurs as a mixture of rent, interest,\nwages and profit.\nStep 3: Calculation of Domestic Income, viz., NDPFc\nSum total of factor income of all sectors provides us domestic income.\nNDPFC = COE + OS + MI\nStep 4: Calculation of National Income, viz., NNPFC\nConversion of NDP Fc into NP c in order to estimate national income requires addition of net\nfactor income from abroad, i.e., NFIA into NDPFC.\nNNPFC\n= NDPFC\n+ NFIA\n143","image":"pages/IMG_0534.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"IMG_0536","text":"Q. 4. How do you distinguish between old-age persions and retirement pensions in the context of\nestimation of national income?\nAns. Old-age pensions are unilateral payments or transfer payments. These are not included in the\nestimation of national income. On the other hand, retirement pensions are like a deferred\nwage. These are related to factor services rendered by the recipients prior to their retirement.\nAccordingly, these are included in the estimation of national income,\nMethods of Calculating National Income\nQ. 5. Operating surplus does not arise in (i) subsistence sector, and (i) general government sector of\nthe economy. Why?\nAns. Operating surplus does not arise in the subsistence sector and in the general government sector\nbecause of the following reasons:\n(i) In the subsistence sector, production is meant only for subsistence of the producing families.\nProduction is not meant for sale in the market. There is no marketable surplus. Accordingly,\nthere is no operating surplus.\n(ii) In the general government sector, production is meant only for collective consumption (or\nconsumption by the general public). Goods and services are produced not for sale in the\nmarket, but for general welfare of the people.\nExample: Services of law & order and defence are available to the people free of charge.\nAccordingly, there is no operating surplus in the 'general government\" sector.\nQ. 6. How are dividends, corporate taxes and undistributed profits treated in national income?\nAns. (i) Dividends: These are included in the estimation of national income as these are a part of\nfactor payments.\n(ii) Corporate Taxes: These are not included, as all taxes are transfer payments.\n(iii) Undistributed Profits: These are retained earnings of the firms and are a part of factor\npayments. Therefore, these are included in the estimation of national income.","image":"pages/IMG_0536.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"IMG_0540","text":"Q. 7. What causes increase in inventory stock?\nAns. Increase in inventory stock is caused by two factors:\n(i) Unexpected Fall in Demand in the Current Year: Example: Producers may have expected\ndemand to the tune of 50,000 umbrellas but, owing to the failure of monsoon, only 10,000\numbrellas are sold during an year. Accordingly, 40,000 umbrellas are added to the existing\nstock.\n(ii) Expected Rise in Demand in the Near Future: Producers may expect a spurt (rise) in demand\n(and therefore, increase in price) in the near future. Accordingly, they pile up stocks during\nthe current year.\nQ.8. Export receipts are not a part of net factor income from abroad. Why?\nAns. This is because of two reasons:\n(i) Exports refer to the purchase of domestically produced goods by the rest of the world.\nGoods produced within the domestic territory of a country are to be treated as a part of\nGDP.\n(ii) Export receipts refer to revenue of the firms from the sale of its output. These are not the\nreceipts of factor incomes from abroad which are available in the form of rent, interest,\nprofit and wages.\nQ. 9. Exports are just sales yielding revenue, not income. Comment.\nAns. It is true that exports are just sales and yield revenue, not income. Exports are just sales because\nthese are purchases of rest of the world from the domestic economy. Accordingly, exports yield\nrevenue (receipts from sales). These do not yield income. Because, income is in the form of rent,\ninterest, profit or wages. Exports do not yield any of these directly.\n[Note: It is out of revenue that a firm distributes incomes to the owners of factors of production.]\nQ. 10. Is net factor income from abroad zero in case exports = imports?\nAns. No. Net factor income from abroad is the difference between the factor income earned by\nnormal residents of a country from abroad and the factor income earned by non-residents in\nour country. It has nothing to do with exports and imports.","image":"pages/IMG_0540.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS","page":"IMG_0542","text":"A Fundamental Question\nestimation of national product income which flow from producers to the consumers through the market.\n• According to the authors of the present text, this is a wrong notion. To understand this lets take a\nexample of an economy where farming is the only production activity.\nparte mare because produces barely enough tor consumption by the farmin\nfamilies themselves.\nwithout production? Perhaps never.\nwhich are routed through the markful to be recorded, such an economy where production is only fo\nLet it be clearly understood that the imputed value of pr\nalways included in the estimation of national income.\n158\nthe farmers) for self-consumption is\nMethods of Calculating National Income","image":"pages/IMG_0542.jpg"},{"title":"ABILITY ZONE","page":"IMG_0542","text":"A. Included in both Domestic Income and\nNational Income\n1. Domestic manufacturing output\n2. Wages paid to employees (within India)\n3. Government provision of defence & security services\n4. Free services by Government (e.g., free schooling,\npublic hospitals, street-lighting)\n5. Employer's contribution to Provident Fund\nB. Included in Domestic Income but NOT in\nNational Income (= factor payments to non-\nresidents for production occurring in India)\n1. Salary paid to foreign technical specialists working in\nIndia\n2. Profits of a foreign company earned in India and\nremitted to its parent abroad\n3. Interest paid by an Indian firm to a foreign bank on a\nproduction loan\n4. Rent paid by an Indian firm to a foreign owner of\ncommercial property in India\n5. Royalty/technology fee paid by an Indian manufacturer\nto a foreign patent holder\nC. Included in National Income but NOT in\nDomestic Income (= factor income from abroad\naccruing to Indian residents)\n1. Rent received by an Indian resident from a building\nrented to a foreign embassy in India\n2. Profits earned by a branch of an Indian bank in London\n3. Wages received by Indian employees working for the\nPakistan Embassy (in India)\n4. Dividend received by an Indian resident from shares in\na foreign company\n5. Interest received by an Indian resident on foreign\ngovernment/corporate bonds\nTable of Transactions\nReason\nNew production within India adds to value added →\nFactor income generated domestically → included in\nboth.\nTreated (by convention) as final services → counted in\nboth.\nGovernment final consumption expenditure → included\nin both.\nPart of compensation of employees→ included in both.\nReason\nFactor income to abroad → included in domestic,\nexcluded from national.\nOperating surplus accrues to non-residents → domestic\nYES, national NO.\nFactor payment to non-resident → domestic YES,\nnational NO.\nFactor income accrues abroad → excluded from\nnational.\nFactor income to non-resident → reduces national\nReason\nEmbassies are foreign territory; receipt is from abroad\n→ national YES, domestic NO.\nIncome earned overseas by residents → included only\nin national.\nPaid by a foreign entity (foreign territory) → factor\nincome from abroad.\nFactor income from abroad → national YES, domestic\nNO.\nEarnings from foreign financial assets → counted only\nin national.","image":"pages/IMG_0542.jpg"},{"title":"Power Points [Summary of the Chapter]","page":"IMG_0543","text":"• Three Different Methods of Measuring National Income\n(i Product Method (or Value Added Method),\n(il) Income Method,\n(iii) Expenditure Method.\n• Product Method (or Value Added Method)\n• Meaning: It is that method which measures the gross value added or gross domestic prod\nat market price as the sum total of value added by all the producing units within the dome\nterritory of the country, during the period of an accounting year.\n• It is adjusted to find national income (NNPFc).\n• Precaution: Add only the market value of final goods and services, do not include market value\nintermediate goods and services.\nOr\nAvoid double counting.\n• Double Counting and Avoiding it: Double counting occurs if output of all the producers is adde\nup without considering the fact that output of one producer may have been used as an input i\nthe other.\nIt can be best avoided by adding up value added, rather than output of different firms.\nValue Added = Value of output\n- Intermediate consumption (referring to expenditure on intermediate goods\nand services)\n• Income Method\n• Meaning: It is that method which measures domestic income as the sum total of factor incomes\n(rent + interest + profit + wages) generated within the domestic territory of a country during the\nperiod of an accounting year.\n• Net factor income from abroad is added to domestic income to find national income (NNPF).\n• Precaution: Add only factor incomes, do not include transfer payments. Also, do not include\nincome generated through illegal activities (like, gambling).\n• Expenditure Method\n• Meaning: It is that method which measures the gross domestic product at market price as the sun\ntotal of the expenditure (consumption expenditure and investment expenditure on the purchas\nperiod of an accounting year.\nof final goods and services produced within the domestic territory of the economy during the\n• It is adjusted to find national income (NNPFc).\n• Precaution: Consider only the expenditure on final goods that causes:\n(i) final consumption, or\n(i) capital formation (also called investment);\n• Do not include expenditure on intermediate goods and services.\n• Also, do not consider expenditure on second-hand goods or on shares and bonds.\nWORK-BO\nCompetency Based Learning: Obje\n1. Multiple Choice Questions\nChoose the correct option:\n1. Value Addition =\n(Choose the correct option(s) t\n(i) Domestic Sales\n(iii) Value of output\nOptions:\n(a) (i) and (ii)\n(c) (ii),(ili) and (iv)\n2. Operating surplus does not i\n(a) interest\n(c) profit\n3. Identify the correct sequen\nA. Add NFIA to calculate N\nB. Add sales and change it\nC. Value of intermediate\nGVA at MP\nD. Deduct value of depre\nChoose the correct answe\n(a) C, D, A, B\n(c) D, C, B, A\n4. Sales for a firm is calculat\n(a) Value of output + Inte\n(b) Value of output - Int\n(c) Value of output - Ch\n(d) Closing stock + Value\n5. Value added method m\nterritory of a country?\n(a) Household consume\n(b) The producing ente\n(c) The producing ente\n(d) Both (b) and (c)\n6. Difference between clo\n(a) increase in stock\n(c) decrease in stock\n160","image":"pages/IMG_0543.jpg"}],"subject":"macro","number":"4"},{"id":"5","title":"Money","intro":"Money is generally defined as a thing that is commonly accepted as a medium of exchange.","lessons":[{"title":"Money, barter and the evolution of money","body":"Money is generally defined as a thing that is commonly accepted as a medium of exchange. Barter exchanges goods directly for goods. Its difficulties include lack of double coincidence of wants, lack of a common measure of value, difficulty storing wealth and difficulty making deferred payments. Money separates selling from buying: the seller need not want the precise good the buyer offers. Evolution moves through commodity money, metallic money, paper money and bank/credit money.","formula":"","source":"IMG_0545","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"5.1 MEANING AND EVOLUTION OF MONEY"},{"title":"Fiat, fiduciary and legal-tender money","body":"Fiat money is money issued by authority of the government. Fiduciary money is accepted on trust between payer and payee. Legal tender must be accepted in discharge of debts within the applicable legal conditions; a cheque depends on acceptance and is not legal tender. Full-bodied money has face value equal to intrinsic commodity value; credit money has face value greater than intrinsic value. Follow the textbook distinctions, not the everyday use of the word “cash”.","formula":"","source":"IMG_0547","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"5.2 FORMS OF MONEY"},{"title":"Primary and secondary functions of money","body":"The primary functions are medium of exchange and measure of value (unit of account). Secondary functions are standard of deferred payments, store of value and transfer of value. Buying a book illustrates medium of exchange; quoting its price illustrates measure of value; paying a loan next year illustrates deferred payment; keeping purchasing power for later illustrates store of value. Inflation reduces the purchasing power stored in money.","formula":"","source":"IMG_0548","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"5.3 FUNCTIONS OF MONEY"},{"title":"Money supply and its components","body":"Money supply is the stock of money held by the public at a particular point of time. Interbank deposits and money held by the government/banking system are excluded from money held by the public. M1 comprises currency with the public, demand deposits with commercial banks and other deposits with the RBI. Demand deposits are withdrawable on demand. A cheque transfers a deposit; it is not an additional independent stock of money.","formula":"M1 = Currency with public + Demand deposits with banks + Other deposits with RBI","source":"IMG_0549","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"5.4 SUPPLY OF MONEY"},{"title":"Ability Zone: broader measures and liquidity","body":"The textbook also gives M2, M3 and M4 for reference. M2 adds post-office savings-bank deposits to M1. M3 adds net time deposits with commercial banks to M1 and is called aggregate monetary resources. M4 adds total post-office deposits, excluding National Savings Certificates, to M3. Liquidity means convertibility into cash: money is the most liquid asset. The textbook’s monetary-system notes discuss paper currency, limited/unlimited legal tender and the minimum reserve system; the original page is available to read exactly.","formula":"","source":"IMG_0551","kind":"Ability Zone","textbookHeading":"ABILITY ZONE · Measures of Money Supply · Liquidity"},{"title":"Why money solves barter’s difficulties","textbookHeading":"5.1.3 Barter System of Exchange · 5.3 Functions of Money","body":"Lack of Double Coincidence of Wants: money is accepted as an intermediary, so you can sell to one person and buy from another. Lack of a Common Measure of Value: prices expressed in money make values comparable. Difficulty in Storing Wealth: money offers a convenient store, though inflation can erode it. Difficulty in Deferred Payments: future payments can be fixed in monetary units. Difficulty in Transfer of Value: purchasing power can be transferred without moving bulky goods. In theory answers, state the barter difficulty first and then the function that addresses it.","source":"IMG_0546","kind":"Exam points","formula":""}],"pages":[{"id":"IMG_0544","image":"pages/IMG_0544.jpg","text":"\" Income.\nian company.\nof the given text and\nwheat producers (the\nneat and they do ng,\nraw materials besides\nneat that the farmer,\nhe bakers have used\nvorth of bread. Thus,\nf intermediate good,\n. Therefore, the find\na baker) +z50 (fina\nmption expenditure\nure (I: Spending by\novernment on final\nnents.\nMoney\n5\nlating GDP through\nenditure method.\nRARY\n000\nLAYOUT OF THE CHAPTER\nChapter\nSection\nSection Title\n5.1\nMeaning and Evolution of\nMoney\n5.2\nForms of Money\n5.3\nFunctions of Money\n5.4\nSupply of Money\nBasic Concepts Explained under each Section\n-Concept of Money\n-Evolution of Money: A Journey from 'no money' Economy\n(Barter System of Exchange) to 'e-money' Economy\n-Flow Chart Presentation of Evolution of Money\n-How Evolution of Money led to Change in the Role of Money?\n-Barter System of Exchange\n-Drawbacks of the Barter System and their Elimination\n-Fiat Money and Fiduciary Money\n-Full Bodied Money and Credit Money\n-Primary Functions of Money\n-Secondary or Subsidiary Functions of Money\n-Concept of Supply of Money\n-Measures of Money Supply\n• My Measure of Money Supply\n-Suppliers of Money or Who Supplies Money?\n-Bank Money and High Powered Money\nPg. No.\n223-228\n228-230\n230-232\n232-235\n• Ability Zone\n236-237\n• Power Points [Summary of the Chapter]\n237\n• WORK-BOOK [Question-answer Format]\n238-254\n• Self Assessment Test\n255-256\n• Digital Library\n256\n5.1 MEANING AND EVOLUTION OF MONEY\n5.1.1 Concept of Money\n• Origin (and evolution) of money is related to the need to facilitate exchange. Therefore, money is\nsenerally defined as a thing that is commonly accepted as a medium of exchange.\nExample:\nA rupee in India is money, as it is a commonly accepted medium of exchange here.\nLikewise, a dollar in USA is money, as it is a commonly accepted medium of exchange there.","file":"macro 5/IMG_0544.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0545","image":"pages/IMG_0545.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\n5.1.2 Evolution of Money\n• In olden days, gooas were exchangeabir goods. There was no money. Thus, a cobbler would\nmake shoes in return for whea ofom the tarmer; a farm worker would get grains as a reward fo\nhis labour, and so on. This system of exchange was known as Darter system. With the multiplicit\nof wants (and greater need for exchaure 9, Warter system a system where goods were exchange\nfor goods) proved to be an inefficient system of exchange.\nfor goods. Instead, goods were sold for money. Initially, coins of gold and silver were introduced\nas money. Subsequently, alloy metal was used for coinage, along with paper money. And, now is\nthe age of plastic money (in the form of cash cards), or e-money in the form of 'electronic transfer\nof money' by way of credit/debit entries in the bank accounts.\n• Thus, evolution of money unfolds a journey from 'no-money' economy to 'e-money' economy.\nThe flow chart given below shows various stages of the evolution of money, along with merits and\ndemerits related to each stage of evolution.\nFlow Chart Presentation of Evolution of Money\n(with merits and demerits related to different stages of the evolutionary process)\nMERITS\nBARTER SYSTEM\n(Stage-1)\n(Goods exchanged\nfor goods)\nDEMERITS\n• Exchange value of goods and\nservices remained constant.\n- Because money and market price\nwere not invoived\n• Economic order remains immune\nfrom uncertainties of the market.\n• Separated the acts of sale and\npurchase, which led to expansion of\nexchange\n• Ease of exchange led to expansion of\nthe market.\n• Expansion of market is a\n'sine-qua-non' of growth. It means\neconomic growth is not possible\nwithout expansion of the market.\n• Now, supply of money becomes\nmore elastic.\n- Bean sleetal money,.\n• Elastic suppined money led to\nnational and international expansion\nof the market\n• Money emerged to be an instrument of\ninvestment, which escalated the\nprocess of economic growth.\n• Cost of printing paper currency is\nsubstantially reduced.\n• e-money promotes digital banking\n(on-line banking).\n• On-line banking promotes financial\ninclusion, leading to expansion of\nthe banking system and expansion\nof economic activity.\nMETALLIC CURRENCY\n(Stage-2)\n• Gold and silver coins\n• Alloy meta\n• Under barter system acts of sale and\npurchase must co-exist.\nExample: If you intend to sell Good-X,\nyou must at the same time be ready\nto buy Good-Y.\n• Exchange (and therefore trade)\nremained limited only to the\nnecessities of life. It symbolised a\nprimitive economy.\n• Use of metallic money was found to\nbe difficult in making large\n• It was hard to split metallic money\ninto smaller denominations, and\ntherefore, hard to cope with smaller\ntransactions.\nPAPER MONEY\n(Stage-3)\n• Elastic money supply is associated\nwith inflationary and deflationary\n• Inflationary and deflationary spirals\nare a challenge to the stability of the\ngrowth-process.\nPLASTIC MONEY and e-MONEY\n(Stage-4)\n• 'Cyber crime' is an emerging\nchallenge related to e-money\ntransactions.\n224\nMoney\n! CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS\nFrom Plastic Money to Digital Currency: How CBCs are Shaping the Future of Payments?\nthe transition from plastic money (credit/debit cards) to e-money (digital wallets, online payments)\nmarked the move from physical to digital transactions, enhancing convenience. Bitcoin (a type of digital\ncurrency that operates without a central authority, such as a government or financial institution, and is\nalued based on its supply and demand in the market) introduced decentralised, block chain-based digital\ncurrencies, challenging traditional financial systems with its lack of central control. Now, Central Bank\nDigital Currencies (CBCs) offer a state-backed, centralised digital currency, combining the efficiency of\ne-money with government regulation, alming to modernise payment systems and ensure stability in the\ndigital economy.\nJAVLR 5.1\nEVOLUTION OF MONEY\nHow Evolution of Money Led to Change in the Role of Money?\nEvolution of money led to a significant change in the role of money in the economy.\nFollowing observations highlight this point:\n• Though initially invented as a medium of exchange, gradually money found its other uses as well.\nPeople started using money as a store of value.\n• Savings are no longer done by way of storage of goods. Money is used as an instrument of saving.\nFurther, money is used as a measure of value. People now express value of goods and services in\nterms of money.\n• Besides, money is serving as a standard for deferred payments (deferred payments are those\npayments which are made sometimes in the future). Accordingly, money has acquired a wider\ndefinition than merely a medium of exchange.\n• Money is defined as an instrument that serves as a medium of exchange, store of value, measure\nof value and standard for deferred payments. Briefly, it is said that 'money is what money does.\nMoney is:\nMedium of Exchange\nMeasure of Value\nStore of Value\nStandard for Deferred Payments\nGoods and services can be exchanged because of money.\nPrice of different goods and services is determined in terms of money.\nMoney can be saved and stored for future use.\nMoney serves as a standard for deferred (future) payments.\n5.1.3 Barter System of Exchange\n• Barter system of exchange is a syster lots which goods are exchanged for goods.\nExample:\nit (as a parmer) you have surpluse paducton ot rice, you are to look for a person wito neeas\nrice, and at the same time has (say) cloth, which you need for yourself.\n225","file":"macro 5/IMG_0545.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0546","image":"pages/IMG_0546.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\nIt means 'double coincidence of wants: your want for cloth must coincide with somebody's\nwant for rice, and you must have surf and nice and somebody must have the surpius of cloth\nHow difficult it is! What do you do these days?\nAs a farmer, you sell rice for money, and as a cloth merchant, you sell your cloth for mone\nWith money in hand, you buy Wiquaver you wish to buy. hus, rice is exchanged for money,\ncloth is exchanged for money. Money acts as a common medium of exchange.\nsuch common medium of exchangy fists in the economy my for commity\nDrawbacks of the Barter System and their Elimination\nFollowing are the principal drawbacks of barter system of exchange. It is with the introduction of\nmoney that these drawbacks have been eliminated.\n(1) Double Coincidence of Wants: Double coincidence of wants is a core characteristic of the\nbarter system of exchange.\n• Double coincidence of wants implies that (at a point of time), the two individuals are in\npossession of such goods which they are willing to exchange for the satisfaction of their\nwants.\n• But, it is not so simple to find a person who wants your horse and at the same time has\na cow that you want to buy. Accordingly, under the barter system, exchange remained\nextremely limited.\n• With the emergence of money (as a medium of exchange), the problem of double\ncoincidence of wants has vanished. Money as a medium of exchange has separated the\nacts of sale and purchase.\nPossesses - Wheat\nNeeds\n- Pulses\nPossesses - Rice\nNeeds\n- Gold Jewellery\nGold Smith\nPossesses - Gold Jewellery\n- Wheat\nPerson A\nPossesses - Wheat\nNeeds\n- Gold Jewellery\nFig. 5.1: Difficulty in exchange as it requires simultaneous satisfaction of wants of buyers and sellers\nDID YOU KNOW\n• Introduction of Money has led to the expansion of exchange.\n• Expansion of exchange has led to expansion of the markets for goods and services.\n• Expansion of market has led to expansion of the scale of output.\n• Expansion of the scale of output has led to GDP growth. Implying growth of the economy.\n226\n2) Lack of a Common Unit of Value: What is the value\nMoney\nof your car? You can reply: 7 5 lakh. Can you give\nthe same answer in a barter system of exchange?\nCertainly not.\n• Under such a system, your car would be valued in\nterms of horses, cows or buffaloes, simply because\nthere is no money (or a common unit of value).\n• Evolution of money has given us a common unit\nof value and, therefore, a system of accounting.\n13) Difficulty of Future Payments or Contractual\nFig. 5.2: Under barter system, value of\none commodity has to be determined\nin context of other commodities\nPayments: These days you hire a worker and\nstrike a contract to pay him (say)R R10,000 p.m.\n• What do you do in a barter system? Would you\ndecide to pay him in terms of tables or chairs,\nin terms of rice or wheat, in terms of drugs or\nchocolates?\n• Contractual payments or future payments would\ncertainly be very difficult under barter system of\nFig. 5.3: Both parties (debtor and\ncreditor) run at a risk of fluctuation in\nthe value of goods exchanged\nexchange.\n• Evolution of money has facilitated contractual payments.\n(4) Difficulty of Storage of Value (Saving) and Transfer\nof Value: In the C-C economy, saving is possible only\nby way of storage of goods. It involves substantial\nstorage cost as well as the fear of capital loss (owing\nto natural disasters).\n• Further, what happens if you are to transfer your\nsaving from one place to the other? Obviously,\nyou are to transfer the goods. Which again is a\ndifficult task, besides being expensive.\n• Evolution of money has made storage and transfer\nof value much easier.\nFig. 5.4: Storing commodities is not as\neasy as storing paper notes\nor coins in a locker\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\n4. 1. Introduction or money il car separated the acts of sale and purchase. How!\nat the same point of time. To buy a thing, an individual must at the same time sell something\nneeded by the other person. Also, sale and purchase by an individual must be of equal value.\nWith the introduction of money (as a medium of exchange, an individual can buy a thing wit\nmoney without selling anything at the same time. Likewise. he can sell a thing for money without\nuying anything at the same time. Thus, with the introduction of money, acts of sale and purchas\nhave been separated.\n227","file":"macro 5/IMG_0546.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0547","image":"pages/IMG_0547.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\nQ. 2. \"Double coincidence of wants is a typical erature or monetary system of exchange\". State\nwhether true or false and give a reason for the same.\nAns. False. Double coincidence of wants is a typical feature of barter system of exchange.\nQ. 3. \"There is no medium of exchal ose in the parter system • State whetner true or raise and give\nAns False under rer st, goods there wore the medium of exchange for goous, Ur cours\nreason for the same.\nthere is no common medium of exchange like money.\n5.2 FORMS OF MONEY\nSome important forms of money are described as under:\n• Fiat money and fiduciary money, and\n• Full bodied money and credit money.\n5.2.1 Fiat Money and Fiduciary Money\n•Fiat money refers to that money which is issued by order/authority of the government.\n• It includes all notes and coins which the people in a country are legally bound to accept as a\nmedium of exchange.\n• Fiduciary money is that money which is accepted as a medium of exchange because of the trust\nbetween the payer and the payee.\nExample:\nCheques are fiduciary money as these are accepted as a means of payment on the basis of\ntrust, not on the basis of any order of the government.\nCRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS\nFiat Money/ Legal Tender Money\nLegal tender is a coin or a banknote that is legally tenderable for discharge of debt or obligation.\nIt is divided into two parts:\n(i) Limited Legal Tender\n-Coins issued by the Government of India under the Coinage Act, 2011, are legal tender if they are not\ndefaced and weigh at least the prescribed minimum weight.\n- Coins of one rupee or more are legal tender for amounts up to ? 1,000.\n- Fifty paise coins are legal tender for amounts up to 710.\n- Acceptance of coins beyond these limits is voluntary, not mandatory.\n(i) Unlimited Legal Tender\n- All Reserve Bank of India banknotes (R2, 25, 710, 820, 850, 7100, 7200, 7500) are legal tender\nthroughout India, unless withdrawn from circulation.\n- The Central Government guarantees these banknotes under Section 26(2) of the RBI Act, 1934.\n21 notes issued by Government of India are also Legal Tender.\n- 3500 and 71,000 banknotes of Mahatma Gandhi series issued up to November 08, 2016 have ceased\nto be Legal Tender with effect from the midnight of November 8, 2016.\nFiduciary Money\n• It is a form of money whose acceptance is based on trust between two parties.\n• Examples include instruments like cheques, credit cards, etc.\n228\nMoney\nCAVLR 5.2,\nFAT MONEY AND FIDUCIARY MONEY\n5,..2 Full Bodied Money and Credit Money\n• Full bodied money refers to money in terms of coins whose commodity value is equal to the\nmoney value as and when these are issued.\nExample:\nA rupee coin during the British period in India was made of silver. Commodity value of the\ncoin was equal to its money value at the time of its issuing.\nThe market value of the silver contained in the coin was equal to 71.\n„ Credit money refers to that money of which money value is more than commodity value.\nExample:\nWhat is the market value of the metal that the rupee coin is made of in India? Obviously,\nmuch lower than the money value of the rupee coin.\nIt it weren't, people would have melted the coins and sold the metal in the market at a price\ngreater than one rupee.\n• CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS\nFull Bodied Money:\nMoney Value = Commodity Value\nCredit Money:\nMoney Value > Commodity Value\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\nQ. 4. Distinguish between money value of money and commodity value of money.\na Mere be one more mere to l was inside on a coin or written on d paper\nnote. Thus, money value of a paper note is what is written on it: one hundred rupees, two\nhundred rupees, etc. With a two hundred rupee note, you can buy goos and services worn two\nhundred rupees in the market.\nCommoditv value of money refers to value of the commodity (like metal) that the money is\nmade of. Thus, if coins are made of gold or silver (as was the practice in olden days), commodit\nvalue of money refers to the market value of the gold or silver contained in the coin\nQ. 5. Can you think of a situation when money plays no role whatsoever?\nAns. Money plays no role in a situation when there is no exchange. Example: An individual or a family\nurviving on an island without anv exchange (or sale and purchase of goods and services\n229","file":"macro 5/IMG_0547.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0548","image":"pages/IMG_0548.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\n4. 0. Why people hold notes and coins when it is clear that the intrinsic value commodity value) of\nAns. Because notes and coins are legai tenders. These are hat money or the money backed with\nnotes and coins is almost negligible?\nauthority of the government. It explawful not to accept notes and coins for receipts/payments\nQ. 7. Commodity value of money his a unit of been greater than the race value (or money value)\nAns. No, it is not true.!! good old odys when coins were made or gold and silver, commodity value\nmoney (referring to the value of metal contained in the coins) would sometimes exceed the face\nvalue of coins which is why coins were sometimes melted and sold as a metal.\nQ. 8. Do you think money acts as a store of value only when it is in the form of gold and silver coins?\nAns. No. Money acts as a store of value even when it is not in the form of gold or siver coins.\nExample: Deposits with the banks are a store of value.\n5.3 FUNCTIONS OF MONEY\nFunctions of money are placed in two categories:\n• Primary functions, and\n• Secondary (or Subsidiary) functions.\n5.3.1 Primary Functions of Money\nMoney performs two primary functions, as under:\n(1) Medium of Exchange: Money acts as a medium for the sale and purchase of goods and\nservices.\n• In the absence of money, goods were exchanged for goods. This required double\ncoincidence of wants. Implying that, exchange was difficult, and therefore, limited.\n• Introduction of money has separated the acts of sale and purchase: double coincidence of\nwants is no longer a limitation.\nCRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS\nSignificance of 'Medium of Exchange' Function of Money\n• Exchange has become much simpler, and therefore, expanded considerably. Expansion of exchange\nleads to expansion of value addition (or GDP growth).\n• Exchange function of money has changed the nature of production activity. Production now is done\nmore for the market and less for self-consumption.\n- Example: Farmers now produce wheat for sale in the market, not merely for self-consumption.\nAccordingly, the level of production has substantially risen.\n• Exchange function of money has separated the acts of sale and purchase. Accordingly, size of the\nmarket has considerably expanded. This has also led to GDP growth.\n(2) Measure of Value or Unit of value: Money serves as a measure of value or as a unit of\naccount, which implies that the value of each good or service is measured in the monetar\n230\n• Measurement of value was very difficult in the barter system: one good was valued in terms\nof the other. There was no common unit of value. Introduction of money has removed this\ndifficulty. Now, each good is valued in terms of money.\n• It is because of the existence of money as a common unit of value that we are now able to\nconstruct consumer price index and wholesale price index. Consumer price index reflects\ncost of living of the people. Wholesale price index shows the rate of inflation in the country.\nCRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS\nSignificance of 'Unit of Value' Function of Money\n• Common unit of value (in terms of money) has facilitated comparison of market value of different\nassets.\n-Accordingly, the market for sale and purchase of assets has considerably expanded. Expansion of\nmarket has led to expansion of investment in the economy.\n- Example: Investment in real estate has shown a significant growth over time, because the value of\ndifferent properties can be expressed in terms of a common unit of value.\n• Common unit of value has facilitated the estimation of national income and related aggregates.\n-Accordingly, it has become easy to assess performance of the economy over time. Without a common\nunit of value, estimation of GDP (Index of Growth) was not possible.\n5.3.2 Secondary or Subsidiary Functions of Money\nFollowing three functions are secondary or subsidiary functions of money:\n(1) Standard of Deferred Payments: Deferred payments refer to those payments which are\nmade sometimes in the future.\n• Money has made deferred payments much easier than before. When we borrow money\nfrom somebody, we have to return both the principal as well as interest amount, sometime\n• Money is a convenient mode of these payments. It is difficult to make such payments in\nterms of goods and services. Imagine that you have taken a loan from somebody in terms\nof wheat. How difficult it is to return this loan in terms of wheat of the same quality?\nCRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS\nSignificance of 'Standard of Deferred Payments' Function of Money\n• This function of money has led to the emergence of 'financial market'-the market that deals in\nborrowing and lending of money.\n• Availability of credit through financial market has led to a multiple increase in consumption expenditure\nas well as investment expenditure. Accordingly, there has been a multiple rise in the level of production\nactivity in the economy.\n2) Store of Value: Store of value implies store of wealth.\n• Storing wealth has become considerably easy with the introduction of money. Wealth can\nbe stored just in terms of paper titles like FDR (fixed deposit receipt.\n• It was not convenient to store value in the barter system of exchange, Because, goods tenc\nto wear-out or perish.","file":"macro 5/IMG_0548.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0549","image":"pages/IMG_0549.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\nCRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS\nSignificance of Store of Value' Function of Money\n• Stored wealth is a source of future investment, and investment is the principal determinant of GDP growt\n• Convenient storage or wealth in terms of money has induced people to save more. saving is a virtue whe\nconverted into investment. However, it may be noted that idle saving leads to the deficiency of demand\nIt hinders the process of GDP growth.\n(3) Transfer of Value: Money serves as a convenient mode of transfer of value (wealth). Money\nvalue can be conveniently transferred (or can be e-transferred) to any part of the world.\n• It is the transfer of value, which has led to the emergence of MINCS (Multinational\nCorporations). Accordingly, the concept of global economy has come into existence.\nCRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS\nSignificance of \"Transfer of Value' Function of Money\n• It has led to the emergence of Global Financial Market. Funds are easily available not only from\nwithin the economy, but from across the borders as well.\n-Accordingly, less developed countries find wider opportunities of investment through borrowing.\n• Transfer of value has encouraged the growth of multinational corporations. This has raised the level of\nglobal economic activity.\n• Transfer of value has facilitated Direct Foreign Investment. This has accelerated the process of growth\nin less developed economies.\n• It has enhanced the mobility of capital across different regions of the country. Accordingly, balanced\nregional growth has become an achievable target.\nStatic and Dynamic Functions\nFunctions of money are also viewed as: static functions and dynamic functions.\n• Static Functions: Static functions of money refer to conventional functions of money.\n- These basically include the primary and secondary functions of money: medium of exchange,\nmeasure of value, store of value, transfer of value, and standard for deferred payments.\n- Performing these functions, money helps in regulating the economic system.\n• Dynamic Functions: Dynamic functions of money refer to those functions of money which impart\n(a) stability to the economy, and (b) accelerate the pace of GDP growth.\n- RBI (in India) tries to tackle the problem of inflation by lowering the supply of money.\n- Further, it tries to tackle the problem of deflation (or economic slowdown) by increasing the supply\n5.4 SUPPLY OF MONEY\n5.4.1 Concept of Supply of Money\n• Supply of money is a stock concept. It refers to total stock of money (of all types) held by the\npeople of a country at a point of time.\n• It is important to note that the supply of money does not include:\n• stock of money held by the government, and\n• stock of money held by the banking system of a country.\n232\n• The stock of money held by the suppliers of money is never treated as a part of the supply of\nmoney in the country as government and banking system of a country are the suppliers of money\n5.4.2 Measures of Money Supply\n• In India, there are four alternative measures of money supply, popularly known as My, M2, Mz, and M..\n(Note: Only My measure is discussed here, as prescribed in the syllabus. Mz, My and My measures are\ngiven in 'Ability Zone' of the chapter for general reference.]\nM, Measure of Money Supply\n• According to My measurement, money supply includes the following components:\nM, = C + DD + OD\nHere,\n(i) C: It refers to currency held by the public. It includes coins as well as paper notes.\n(i)DD: It refers to demand deposits of the people with the commercial banks. These are\nchequeable deposits which can be withdrawn or transferred on demand.\n(i) OD: These are other deposits which include:\n• Demand deposits with RBI of public financial institutions like NABARD (National Bank for\nAgriculture and Rural Development).\n• Demand deposits with RBI of foreign central banks and of the foreign governments.\n• Demand deposits of international financial institutions like IMF and World Bank.\n• Specifically, OD does not include:\n(i) deposits of the government of the country with RBI,\n(ii) deposits of the country's banking system with RBI.\nCRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS\nNet Demand Deposits and Gross Demand Deposits\n• Gross demand deposits include inter-banking claims: claims of one bank against the other.\n• Net demand deposits do not include inter-banking claims. Inter-banking claims are not a part of\ndemand deposits of the people.\nNote: Only net demand deposits are taken as a part of money supply.]\nTerm Deposits vs. Demand Deposits\nBasis of Difference\n1. Time\nDemand Deposits\nTerm Deposits\n- Term deposits are always for a specific period\n- Demand deposits are not for any specific\nof time, like fixed deposits for a period of one\nperiod of time.\n2. Withdrawal of\nyear or two years.\nMoney\n- Depositors\ncannot\nwithdraw\nmoney\n- Money in demand deposits\ncan be\n3. Nature of\n(in fixed deposits) as and when needed.\nwithdrawn as and when needed.\nDeposits\n- Term deposits are not chequeable deposits.\n- Demand deposits are chequeable deposits.\nDepositors cannot sign a cheque against\nThese are:\nthese deposits.\n• saving account deposits, and\n• current account deposits.\n- Depositors can sign a cheque and withdraw/\ntransfer money from these accounts.\nNote: In the context of M, measurement of money supply, only demand deposits are considered as a part of money supply, NOT the\nterm denosits (Demand Deposits = Saving Account Deposits + Current Account Deposits)\n233","file":"macro 5/IMG_0549.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0550","image":"pages/IMG_0550.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\nMeasure of Money Supply (M,)\nCurrency\nwith the Public\nDemand Deposits\nof the People with\nthe Commercial Banks\nOther Deposits\nwith Reserve Bank\nDemand Deposits with\nDemand Deposits with\nReserve Bank of Public\nReserve Bank of\nDemand Deposits of\nFinancial Institutions (like\nForeign Central Banks\nInternational Financial\nNABARD)\nand Governments\nInstitutions (like IMF and\nWorld Bank)\n[Note: The standard practice is to consider (i) Currency with the public, and i) Demand deposits of the\npeople with the commercial banks as the two principal components of money supply in the economy.\nIllustration:\nGiven, currency with the public = 7 85,000, demand deposits of the people with the commercial\nbanks = ‹ 65,000, other deposits with Reserve Bank = 7 3,500. Find M1.\nSolution:\nMy = 7 85,000 + F 65,000 + R 3,500\n= · 1,53,500.\n5.4.3 Suppliers of Money or Who Supplies Money?\nFollowing are the principal suppliers of money in an economy:\n(1) Central Bank of the Country: The central bank of a country, like 'RBl' in India, 'The Federal\nReserve System in USA', and 'Bank of England' in UK.\n• In India, RBI is the principal supplier of money. RBl issues currency on the basis of Minimum\nReserve System.\n• Under this system, Reserve Bank maintains a minimum reserve of & 200 crore in the form\nof gold and foreign securities. Of this reserve, value of the gold must be & 115 crore.\n(2) Commercial Banks: Commercial banks are the second significant source of money supply.\nUnlike the central bank, commercial banks do not have the authority of issuing currency nor\ncan they issue notes and coins. Yet, they are the suppliers of money as they create money by\nway of demand deposits.\n• These deposits serve as supply of money because these are chequeable deposits.\nMoney created by the commercial banks by way of demand deposits is called Bank\nMoney.\n(3) The Government: Government is the third source of money supply in the country. In India,\nthe Ministry of Finance issues one rupee notes and coins of all denomination.\n& AVLR 5.3\nM, - MEASURE OF MONEY SUPPLY\n234\nCRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS\nMoney\nBank Money and High Powered Money\n• Bank money refers to demand deposits (or chequeable deposits) of the people with the commercia\nbanks.\n• High powered money is the sum total of:\n(i) currency held by the people,\n(i) vault cash of the commercial banks (cash reserves held by the bank itselt, over and above the\nmandated CRR amount, and\n(il) ash reserves of the commercial banks with the RBI.\n- This is called 'Monetary Base' or \"Base Money' in the economy.\n- This base money is referred to as high powered money because it is on the basis of this money\nthat banks are able to create credit (or demand deposits) which serves as money. High powered\nmoney thus determines the total flow of money in the economy which includes (besides other things)\ndemand deposits of the banks.\n(Note: While currency held by the people is a part of money supply, vault cash of the commercial banks as well as cash reserves of the\ncommercial banks with RBl are NOT a part of money supply.\nAs already noted, stock of money held by the suppliers of money is never treated as a part of money supply J\n3 AVLR 5.4\nCONCEPT OF HIGH POWERED MONEY\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\nQ.9. Who are the producers of money?\nAns. Producers of money refer to suppliers of money. They include:\n(i) the government of the country, and\n(ii) the banking system of a country, including both the central bank (which is the note- issuing\nauthority) and the commercial banks (who add to the supply of money through demand\ndeposits).\n9.10. State the principal components of money supply.\nAns. The principal components of money supply are:\n(i) Currency (notes + coins) held by the public.\n(i) Demand deposits of the people with the commercial banks, and\n(i) Other deposits (demand deposits with RBl of domestic and foreign institutions other than\nof the government of the country and commercial banks within the country.\nQ. 11. Why are cash deposits of the government and of the commercial banks with the RBI no\ntreated as a part of money supply?\nAns. Because government and commercial banks are creators/suppliers of money. And, money held\nby the creators/suppliers of money is never treated as a part of money supply.\n0.12. What is meant by an ideal supply of money?\nAns. Ideal money supply is that amount of money supply which keeps the total purchasing power in\na state of balance with the supply of goods and services in the economy, so that the economy\ndoes not slip into inflationary or deflationary situations.\n235","file":"macro 5/IMG_0550.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0551","image":"pages/IMG_0551.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\nABILITY ZONE\nM2, Mz, M4 Measures of Money Supply\n• M_ Measurement\n• M2 is a boarder concept of money Siverted compared to My. Besides all the components or M,\nit also includes savings of the people with the post offices. Thus,\nM2 = M, + Deposits with Post Office Saving Bank Account\n• M Measurement\n• My is also a broader concept of money supply compared to My. Besides all the components of\nM, it also includes (net) time deposits (or fixed deposits/term deposits) of the people with the\ncommercial banks. Thus,\nM3 = M, + Net Time Deposits with the Commercial Banks\n• M Measurement\n• My concept of money supply is still broader-it is broader than even M3. Besides all the\ncomponents of M3, it also includes total deposits with the post offices (other than in the form of\nNational Saving Certificate. Thus,\nM4 = Mz + Total Deposits with Post Offices\n(other than in the form of National Saving Certificate)\nAggregate Monetary Resources of the Country\nIf money supply in the country is measured using M3 measure, it is called 'aggregate monetary\nresources' of the country.\n'Narrow Money' and 'Broad Money' Concepts of Money Supply\n• The distinction between narrow money and broad money is sometimes drawn with reference to the\nmeasure used for estimating total money supply in the country.\n•IfM, or M, measures are used, then it is known as 'narrow money' concept of money supply. If M3 or M4\nmeasures are used, then it is known as \"broad money' concept of money supply.\nIndian Monetary System\n• A monetary system refers to the form of money which circulates in the economy by way of order/authority\nof the government. India is using paper currency. Accordingly, Indian monetary system is described as\na Paper Currency Standard.\nPaper currency does not exclude the use of metal coins. Both metal coins and paper notes are credit\nmoney in india. The only difference is that while coins are of small denomination, paper notes are of\nlarge denomination.\nNote Carefully\n- Coins in India are limited legal tender.\n- Paper notes in India are unlimited legal tender.\n• It means that coins can be used to settle payments only of limited value; paper notes can be used to\nsettle payments of unlimited value.\nWhat Governs Note Issuing in India?\n• Note issuing in India is governed by Minimum Reserve System. The entire currency issued has the\nbacking of minimum gold reserves. Two points must be carefully understood in this context:\n(i) Gold reserves are just the minimum reserves (2 115 crore). These are not proportionate to the\ncurrency in circulation.\n236\n(i) Money with the people is entirely credit money. It cannot be converted into bullion-silver or gold\nMoney\nby the issuing authority. Gold reserves are just a backing, not the stock to convert paper notes into gold.\nThe Concept of Liquidity\n• Liquidity of an asset refers to its convertibility into money/cash. Faster an asset can be converted\ninto cash, more liquid it is. Obviously, money itself is the most liquid asset.\n• Chequeable deposits/demand deposits are highly quid assets. Accordingly, we can state that M\nincludes only those components of money supply which are most liquid.\n• Term deposits/time deposits/fixed deposits are not chequeable deposits. These cannot be withdrawi\nby issuing a cheque. These deposits are, therefore, less liquid than the demand deposits.\nAccordingly, M3 and M4 measures of money supply include such components of money supply which\nare less liquid.\nPower Points [Summary of the Chapter]\n• Money is anything which is commonly accepted as a medium of exchange.\n• It has been invented to overcome the difficulties of barter system of exchange.\n• Barter System of Exchange is the system in which commodities are exchanged for commodities.\n• This is also called commodity for commodity exchange economy or 'C-C economy.\n• Drawbacks:\n) It requires double coincidence of wants which is hard to find.\n(i) It lacks a common unit of exchange.\n(i) It lacks the system of future payments or deferred payments.\n(iv) It lacks the system of storage and transfer of value.\n• Forms of Money\n• Fiat Money is that money which is issued by order (authority) of the government.\n• Fiduciary Money is that money which is accepted as a medium of exchange because of the trust\nbetween the payer and the payee.\n• Full Bodied Money: Money value = Commodity value of money.\n• Credit Money: Money value of coins and notes > Commodity value of coins and notes.\n• Functions of Money\n• Primary Functions:\n- It serves as a medium of exchange.\n- It is a standard unit of value.\n• Secondary Functions:\n- It is a standard of deferred payments.\n- It is a store of value.\n- It facilitates transfer of value.\n* Supply of Money is a stock concept. It refers to stock of money available with the public/people at\na point of time.\n• Stock of Money with the government and the banking system of the country is not considered\nas a part of money supply.\n• Components of Money Supply:\nCurrency with Public + Demand Deposits with Commercial Banks + Other Deposits with the\nReserve Bank.\n237","file":"macro 5/IMG_0551.HEIC"}],"boxes":[{"title":"Ability Zone","page":"IMG_0544","text":"236-237","image":"pages/IMG_0544.jpg"},{"title":"Power Points [Summary of the Chapter]","page":"IMG_0544","text":"237\n• WORK-BOOK [Question-answer Format]\n238-254\n• Self Assessment Test\n255-256\n• Digital Library\n256","image":"pages/IMG_0544.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS","page":"IMG_0545","text":"From Plastic Money to Digital Currency: How CBCs are Shaping the Future of Payments?\nthe transition from plastic money (credit/debit cards) to e-money (digital wallets, online payments)\nmarked the move from physical to digital transactions, enhancing convenience. Bitcoin (a type of digital\ncurrency that operates without a central authority, such as a government or financial institution, and is\nalued based on its supply and demand in the market) introduced decentralised, block chain-based digital\ncurrencies, challenging traditional financial systems with its lack of central control. Now, Central Bank\nDigital Currencies (CBCs) offer a state-backed, centralised digital currency, combining the efficiency of\ne-money with government regulation, alming to modernise payment systems and ensure stability in the\ndigital economy.\nJAVLR 5.1\nEVOLUTION OF MONEY\nHow Evolution of Money Led to Change in the Role of Money?\nEvolution of money led to a significant change in the role of money in the economy.\nFollowing observations highlight this point:\n• Though initially invented as a medium of exchange, gradually money found its other uses as well.\nPeople started using money as a store of value.\n• Savings are no longer done by way of storage of goods. Money is used as an instrument of saving.\nFurther, money is used as a measure of value. People now express value of goods and services in\nterms of money.\n• Besides, money is serving as a standard for deferred payments (deferred payments are those\npayments which are made sometimes in the future). Accordingly, money has acquired a wider\ndefinition than merely a medium of exchange.\n• Money is defined as an instrument that serves as a medium of exchange, store of value, measure\nof value and standard for deferred payments. Briefly, it is said that 'money is what money does.\nMoney is:\nMedium of Exchange\nMeasure of Value\nStore of Value\nStandard for Deferred Payments\nGoods and services can be exchanged because of money.\nPrice of different goods and services is determined in terms of money.\nMoney can be saved and stored for future use.\nMoney serves as a standard for deferred (future) payments.\n5.1.3 Barter System of Exchange\n• Barter system of exchange is a syster lots which goods are exchanged for goods.\nExample:\nit (as a parmer) you have surpluse paducton ot rice, you are to look for a person wito neeas\nrice, and at the same time has (say) cloth, which you need for yourself.\n225","image":"pages/IMG_0545.jpg"},{"title":"DID YOU KNOW","page":"IMG_0546","text":"• Introduction of Money has led to the expansion of exchange.\n• Expansion of exchange has led to expansion of the markets for goods and services.\n• Expansion of market has led to expansion of the scale of output.\n• Expansion of the scale of output has led to GDP growth. Implying growth of the economy.\n226\n2) Lack of a Common Unit of Value: What is the value\nMoney\nof your car? You can reply: 7 5 lakh. Can you give\nthe same answer in a barter system of exchange?\nCertainly not.\n• Under such a system, your car would be valued in\nterms of horses, cows or buffaloes, simply because\nthere is no money (or a common unit of value).\n• Evolution of money has given us a common unit\nof value and, therefore, a system of accounting.\n13) Difficulty of Future Payments or Contractual\nFig. 5.2: Under barter system, value of\none commodity has to be determined\nin context of other commodities\nPayments: These days you hire a worker and\nstrike a contract to pay him (say)R R10,000 p.m.\n• What do you do in a barter system? Would you\ndecide to pay him in terms of tables or chairs,\nin terms of rice or wheat, in terms of drugs or\nchocolates?\n• Contractual payments or future payments would\ncertainly be very difficult under barter system of\nFig. 5.3: Both parties (debtor and\ncreditor) run at a risk of fluctuation in\nthe value of goods exchanged\nexchange.\n• Evolution of money has facilitated contractual payments.\n(4) Difficulty of Storage of Value (Saving) and Transfer\nof Value: In the C-C economy, saving is possible only\nby way of storage of goods. It involves substantial\nstorage cost as well as the fear of capital loss (owing\nto natural disasters).\n• Further, what happens if you are to transfer your\nsaving from one place to the other? Obviously,\nyou are to transfer the goods. Which again is a\ndifficult task, besides being expensive.\n• Evolution of money has made storage and transfer\nof value much easier.\nFig. 5.4: Storing commodities is not as\neasy as storing paper notes\nor coins in a locker","image":"pages/IMG_0546.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"IMG_0546","text":"4. 1. Introduction or money il car separated the acts of sale and purchase. How!\nat the same point of time. To buy a thing, an individual must at the same time sell something\nneeded by the other person. Also, sale and purchase by an individual must be of equal value.\nWith the introduction of money (as a medium of exchange, an individual can buy a thing wit\nmoney without selling anything at the same time. Likewise. he can sell a thing for money without\nuying anything at the same time. Thus, with the introduction of money, acts of sale and purchas\nhave been separated.\n227","image":"pages/IMG_0546.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS","page":"IMG_0547","text":"Fiat Money/ Legal Tender Money\nLegal tender is a coin or a banknote that is legally tenderable for discharge of debt or obligation.\nIt is divided into two parts:\n(i) Limited Legal Tender\n-Coins issued by the Government of India under the Coinage Act, 2011, are legal tender if they are not\ndefaced and weigh at least the prescribed minimum weight.\n- Coins of one rupee or more are legal tender for amounts up to ? 1,000.\n- Fifty paise coins are legal tender for amounts up to 710.\n- Acceptance of coins beyond these limits is voluntary, not mandatory.\n(i) Unlimited Legal Tender\n- All Reserve Bank of India banknotes (R2, 25, 710, 820, 850, 7100, 7200, 7500) are legal tender\nthroughout India, unless withdrawn from circulation.\n- The Central Government guarantees these banknotes under Section 26(2) of the RBI Act, 1934.\n21 notes issued by Government of India are also Legal Tender.\n- 3500 and 71,000 banknotes of Mahatma Gandhi series issued up to November 08, 2016 have ceased\nto be Legal Tender with effect from the midnight of November 8, 2016.\nFiduciary Money\n• It is a form of money whose acceptance is based on trust between two parties.\n• Examples include instruments like cheques, credit cards, etc.\n228\nMoney\nCAVLR 5.2,\nFAT MONEY AND FIDUCIARY MONEY\n5,..2 Full Bodied Money and Credit Money\n• Full bodied money refers to money in terms of coins whose commodity value is equal to the\nmoney value as and when these are issued.\nExample:\nA rupee coin during the British period in India was made of silver. Commodity value of the\ncoin was equal to its money value at the time of its issuing.\nThe market value of the silver contained in the coin was equal to 71.\n„ Credit money refers to that money of which money value is more than commodity value.\nExample:\nWhat is the market value of the metal that the rupee coin is made of in India? Obviously,\nmuch lower than the money value of the rupee coin.\nIt it weren't, people would have melted the coins and sold the metal in the market at a price\ngreater than one rupee.","image":"pages/IMG_0547.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS","page":"IMG_0547","text":"Full Bodied Money:\nMoney Value = Commodity Value\nCredit Money:\nMoney Value > Commodity Value","image":"pages/IMG_0547.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"IMG_0547","text":"Q. 4. Distinguish between money value of money and commodity value of money.\na Mere be one more mere to l was inside on a coin or written on d paper\nnote. Thus, money value of a paper note is what is written on it: one hundred rupees, two\nhundred rupees, etc. With a two hundred rupee note, you can buy goos and services worn two\nhundred rupees in the market.\nCommoditv value of money refers to value of the commodity (like metal) that the money is\nmade of. Thus, if coins are made of gold or silver (as was the practice in olden days), commodit\nvalue of money refers to the market value of the gold or silver contained in the coin\nQ. 5. Can you think of a situation when money plays no role whatsoever?\nAns. Money plays no role in a situation when there is no exchange. Example: An individual or a family\nurviving on an island without anv exchange (or sale and purchase of goods and services\n229","image":"pages/IMG_0547.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS","page":"IMG_0548","text":"Significance of 'Medium of Exchange' Function of Money\n• Exchange has become much simpler, and therefore, expanded considerably. Expansion of exchange\nleads to expansion of value addition (or GDP growth).\n• Exchange function of money has changed the nature of production activity. Production now is done\nmore for the market and less for self-consumption.\n- Example: Farmers now produce wheat for sale in the market, not merely for self-consumption.\nAccordingly, the level of production has substantially risen.\n• Exchange function of money has separated the acts of sale and purchase. Accordingly, size of the\nmarket has considerably expanded. This has also led to GDP growth.\n(2) Measure of Value or Unit of value: Money serves as a measure of value or as a unit of\naccount, which implies that the value of each good or service is measured in the monetar\n230\n• Measurement of value was very difficult in the barter system: one good was valued in terms\nof the other. There was no common unit of value. Introduction of money has removed this\ndifficulty. Now, each good is valued in terms of money.\n• It is because of the existence of money as a common unit of value that we are now able to\nconstruct consumer price index and wholesale price index. Consumer price index reflects\ncost of living of the people. Wholesale price index shows the rate of inflation in the country.","image":"pages/IMG_0548.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS","page":"IMG_0548","text":"Significance of 'Unit of Value' Function of Money\n• Common unit of value (in terms of money) has facilitated comparison of market value of different\nassets.\n-Accordingly, the market for sale and purchase of assets has considerably expanded. Expansion of\nmarket has led to expansion of investment in the economy.\n- Example: Investment in real estate has shown a significant growth over time, because the value of\ndifferent properties can be expressed in terms of a common unit of value.\n• Common unit of value has facilitated the estimation of national income and related aggregates.\n-Accordingly, it has become easy to assess performance of the economy over time. Without a common\nunit of value, estimation of GDP (Index of Growth) was not possible.\n5.3.2 Secondary or Subsidiary Functions of Money\nFollowing three functions are secondary or subsidiary functions of money:\n(1) Standard of Deferred Payments: Deferred payments refer to those payments which are\nmade sometimes in the future.\n• Money has made deferred payments much easier than before. When we borrow money\nfrom somebody, we have to return both the principal as well as interest amount, sometime\n• Money is a convenient mode of these payments. It is difficult to make such payments in\nterms of goods and services. Imagine that you have taken a loan from somebody in terms\nof wheat. How difficult it is to return this loan in terms of wheat of the same quality?","image":"pages/IMG_0548.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS","page":"IMG_0548","text":"Significance of 'Standard of Deferred Payments' Function of Money\n• This function of money has led to the emergence of 'financial market'-the market that deals in\nborrowing and lending of money.\n• Availability of credit through financial market has led to a multiple increase in consumption expenditure\nas well as investment expenditure. Accordingly, there has been a multiple rise in the level of production\nactivity in the economy.\n2) Store of Value: Store of value implies store of wealth.\n• Storing wealth has become considerably easy with the introduction of money. Wealth can\nbe stored just in terms of paper titles like FDR (fixed deposit receipt.\n• It was not convenient to store value in the barter system of exchange, Because, goods tenc\nto wear-out or perish.","image":"pages/IMG_0548.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS","page":"IMG_0549","text":"Significance of Store of Value' Function of Money\n• Stored wealth is a source of future investment, and investment is the principal determinant of GDP growt\n• Convenient storage or wealth in terms of money has induced people to save more. saving is a virtue whe\nconverted into investment. However, it may be noted that idle saving leads to the deficiency of demand\nIt hinders the process of GDP growth.\n(3) Transfer of Value: Money serves as a convenient mode of transfer of value (wealth). Money\nvalue can be conveniently transferred (or can be e-transferred) to any part of the world.\n• It is the transfer of value, which has led to the emergence of MINCS (Multinational\nCorporations). Accordingly, the concept of global economy has come into existence.","image":"pages/IMG_0549.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS","page":"IMG_0549","text":"Significance of \"Transfer of Value' Function of Money\n• It has led to the emergence of Global Financial Market. Funds are easily available not only from\nwithin the economy, but from across the borders as well.\n-Accordingly, less developed countries find wider opportunities of investment through borrowing.\n• Transfer of value has encouraged the growth of multinational corporations. This has raised the level of\nglobal economic activity.\n• Transfer of value has facilitated Direct Foreign Investment. This has accelerated the process of growth\nin less developed economies.\n• It has enhanced the mobility of capital across different regions of the country. Accordingly, balanced\nregional growth has become an achievable target.\nStatic and Dynamic Functions\nFunctions of money are also viewed as: static functions and dynamic functions.\n• Static Functions: Static functions of money refer to conventional functions of money.\n- These basically include the primary and secondary functions of money: medium of exchange,\nmeasure of value, store of value, transfer of value, and standard for deferred payments.\n- Performing these functions, money helps in regulating the economic system.\n• Dynamic Functions: Dynamic functions of money refer to those functions of money which impart\n(a) stability to the economy, and (b) accelerate the pace of GDP growth.\n- RBI (in India) tries to tackle the problem of inflation by lowering the supply of money.\n- Further, it tries to tackle the problem of deflation (or economic slowdown) by increasing the supply","image":"pages/IMG_0549.jpg"},{"title":"given in 'Ability Zone' of the chapter for general reference.]","page":"IMG_0549","text":"M, Measure of Money Supply\n• According to My measurement, money supply includes the following components:\nM, = C + DD + OD\nHere,\n(i) C: It refers to currency held by the public. It includes coins as well as paper notes.\n(i)DD: It refers to demand deposits of the people with the commercial banks. These are\nchequeable deposits which can be withdrawn or transferred on demand.\n(i) OD: These are other deposits which include:\n• Demand deposits with RBI of public financial institutions like NABARD (National Bank for\nAgriculture and Rural Development).\n• Demand deposits with RBI of foreign central banks and of the foreign governments.\n• Demand deposits of international financial institutions like IMF and World Bank.\n• Specifically, OD does not include:\n(i) deposits of the government of the country with RBI,\n(ii) deposits of the country's banking system with RBI.","image":"pages/IMG_0549.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS","page":"IMG_0549","text":"Net Demand Deposits and Gross Demand Deposits\n• Gross demand deposits include inter-banking claims: claims of one bank against the other.\n• Net demand deposits do not include inter-banking claims. Inter-banking claims are not a part of\ndemand deposits of the people.\nNote: Only net demand deposits are taken as a part of money supply.]\nTerm Deposits vs. Demand Deposits\nBasis of Difference\n1. Time\nDemand Deposits\nTerm Deposits\n- Term deposits are always for a specific period\n- Demand deposits are not for any specific\nof time, like fixed deposits for a period of one\nperiod of time.\n2. Withdrawal of\nyear or two years.\nMoney\n- Depositors\ncannot\nwithdraw\nmoney\n- Money in demand deposits\ncan be\n3. Nature of\n(in fixed deposits) as and when needed.\nwithdrawn as and when needed.\nDeposits\n- Term deposits are not chequeable deposits.\n- Demand deposits are chequeable deposits.\nDepositors cannot sign a cheque against\nThese are:\nthese deposits.\n• saving account deposits, and\n• current account deposits.\n- Depositors can sign a cheque and withdraw/\ntransfer money from these accounts.\nNote: In the context of M, measurement of money supply, only demand deposits are considered as a part of money supply, NOT the\nterm denosits (Demand Deposits = Saving Account Deposits + Current Account Deposits)\n233","image":"pages/IMG_0549.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS","page":"IMG_0550","text":"Money\nBank Money and High Powered Money\n• Bank money refers to demand deposits (or chequeable deposits) of the people with the commercia\nbanks.\n• High powered money is the sum total of:\n(i) currency held by the people,\n(i) vault cash of the commercial banks (cash reserves held by the bank itselt, over and above the\nmandated CRR amount, and\n(il) ash reserves of the commercial banks with the RBI.\n- This is called 'Monetary Base' or \"Base Money' in the economy.\n- This base money is referred to as high powered money because it is on the basis of this money\nthat banks are able to create credit (or demand deposits) which serves as money. High powered\nmoney thus determines the total flow of money in the economy which includes (besides other things)\ndemand deposits of the banks.\n(Note: While currency held by the people is a part of money supply, vault cash of the commercial banks as well as cash reserves of the\ncommercial banks with RBl are NOT a part of money supply.\nAs already noted, stock of money held by the suppliers of money is never treated as a part of money supply J\n3 AVLR 5.4\nCONCEPT OF HIGH POWERED MONEY","image":"pages/IMG_0550.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"IMG_0550","text":"Q.9. Who are the producers of money?\nAns. Producers of money refer to suppliers of money. They include:\n(i) the government of the country, and\n(ii) the banking system of a country, including both the central bank (which is the note- issuing\nauthority) and the commercial banks (who add to the supply of money through demand\ndeposits).\n9.10. State the principal components of money supply.\nAns. The principal components of money supply are:\n(i) Currency (notes + coins) held by the public.\n(i) Demand deposits of the people with the commercial banks, and\n(i) Other deposits (demand deposits with RBl of domestic and foreign institutions other than\nof the government of the country and commercial banks within the country.\nQ. 11. Why are cash deposits of the government and of the commercial banks with the RBI no\ntreated as a part of money supply?\nAns. Because government and commercial banks are creators/suppliers of money. And, money held\nby the creators/suppliers of money is never treated as a part of money supply.\n0.12. What is meant by an ideal supply of money?\nAns. Ideal money supply is that amount of money supply which keeps the total purchasing power in\na state of balance with the supply of goods and services in the economy, so that the economy\ndoes not slip into inflationary or deflationary situations.\n235","image":"pages/IMG_0550.jpg"},{"title":"ABILITY ZONE","page":"IMG_0551","text":"M2, Mz, M4 Measures of Money Supply\n• M_ Measurement\n• M2 is a boarder concept of money Siverted compared to My. Besides all the components or M,\nit also includes savings of the people with the post offices. Thus,\nM2 = M, + Deposits with Post Office Saving Bank Account\n• M Measurement\n• My is also a broader concept of money supply compared to My. Besides all the components of\nM, it also includes (net) time deposits (or fixed deposits/term deposits) of the people with the\ncommercial banks. Thus,\nM3 = M, + Net Time Deposits with the Commercial Banks\n• M Measurement\n• My concept of money supply is still broader-it is broader than even M3. Besides all the\ncomponents of M3, it also includes total deposits with the post offices (other than in the form of\nNational Saving Certificate. Thus,\nM4 = Mz + Total Deposits with Post Offices\n(other than in the form of National Saving Certificate)\nAggregate Monetary Resources of the Country\nIf money supply in the country is measured using M3 measure, it is called 'aggregate monetary\nresources' of the country.\n'Narrow Money' and 'Broad Money' Concepts of Money Supply\n• The distinction between narrow money and broad money is sometimes drawn with reference to the\nmeasure used for estimating total money supply in the country.\n•IfM, or M, measures are used, then it is known as 'narrow money' concept of money supply. If M3 or M4\nmeasures are used, then it is known as \"broad money' concept of money supply.\nIndian Monetary System\n• A monetary system refers to the form of money which circulates in the economy by way of order/authority\nof the government. India is using paper currency. Accordingly, Indian monetary system is described as\na Paper Currency Standard.\nPaper currency does not exclude the use of metal coins. Both metal coins and paper notes are credit\nmoney in india. The only difference is that while coins are of small denomination, paper notes are of\nlarge denomination.\nNote Carefully\n- Coins in India are limited legal tender.\n- Paper notes in India are unlimited legal tender.\n• It means that coins can be used to settle payments only of limited value; paper notes can be used to\nsettle payments of unlimited value.\nWhat Governs Note Issuing in India?\n• Note issuing in India is governed by Minimum Reserve System. The entire currency issued has the\nbacking of minimum gold reserves. Two points must be carefully understood in this context:\n(i) Gold reserves are just the minimum reserves (2 115 crore). These are not proportionate to the\ncurrency in circulation.\n236\n(i) Money with the people is entirely credit money. It cannot be converted into bullion-silver or gold\nMoney\nby the issuing authority. Gold reserves are just a backing, not the stock to convert paper notes into gold.\nThe Concept of Liquidity\n• Liquidity of an asset refers to its convertibility into money/cash. Faster an asset can be converted\ninto cash, more liquid it is. Obviously, money itself is the most liquid asset.\n• Chequeable deposits/demand deposits are highly quid assets. Accordingly, we can state that M\nincludes only those components of money supply which are most liquid.\n• Term deposits/time deposits/fixed deposits are not chequeable deposits. These cannot be withdrawi\nby issuing a cheque. These deposits are, therefore, less liquid than the demand deposits.\nAccordingly, M3 and M4 measures of money supply include such components of money supply which\nare less liquid.","image":"pages/IMG_0551.jpg"},{"title":"Note Carefully","page":"IMG_0551","text":"- Coins in India are limited legal tender.\n- Paper notes in India are unlimited legal tender.\n• It means that coins can be used to settle payments only of limited value; paper notes can be used to\nsettle payments of unlimited value.\nWhat Governs Note Issuing in India?\n• Note issuing in India is governed by Minimum Reserve System. The entire currency issued has the\nbacking of minimum gold reserves. Two points must be carefully understood in this context:\n(i) Gold reserves are just the minimum reserves (2 115 crore). These are not proportionate to the\ncurrency in circulation.\n236\n(i) Money with the people is entirely credit money. It cannot be converted into bullion-silver or gold\nMoney\nby the issuing authority. Gold reserves are just a backing, not the stock to convert paper notes into gold.\nThe Concept of Liquidity\n• Liquidity of an asset refers to its convertibility into money/cash. Faster an asset can be converted\ninto cash, more liquid it is. Obviously, money itself is the most liquid asset.\n• Chequeable deposits/demand deposits are highly quid assets. Accordingly, we can state that M\nincludes only those components of money supply which are most liquid.\n• Term deposits/time deposits/fixed deposits are not chequeable deposits. These cannot be withdrawi\nby issuing a cheque. These deposits are, therefore, less liquid than the demand deposits.\nAccordingly, M3 and M4 measures of money supply include such components of money supply which\nare less liquid.","image":"pages/IMG_0551.jpg"},{"title":"Power Points [Summary of the Chapter]","page":"IMG_0551","text":"• Money is anything which is commonly accepted as a medium of exchange.\n• It has been invented to overcome the difficulties of barter system of exchange.\n• Barter System of Exchange is the system in which commodities are exchanged for commodities.\n• This is also called commodity for commodity exchange economy or 'C-C economy.\n• Drawbacks:\n) It requires double coincidence of wants which is hard to find.\n(i) It lacks a common unit of exchange.\n(i) It lacks the system of future payments or deferred payments.\n(iv) It lacks the system of storage and transfer of value.\n• Forms of Money\n• Fiat Money is that money which is issued by order (authority) of the government.\n• Fiduciary Money is that money which is accepted as a medium of exchange because of the trust\nbetween the payer and the payee.\n• Full Bodied Money: Money value = Commodity value of money.\n• Credit Money: Money value of coins and notes > Commodity value of coins and notes.\n• Functions of Money\n• Primary Functions:\n- It serves as a medium of exchange.\n- It is a standard unit of value.\n• Secondary Functions:\n- It is a standard of deferred payments.\n- It is a store of value.\n- It facilitates transfer of value.\n* Supply of Money is a stock concept. It refers to stock of money available with the public/people at\na point of time.\n• Stock of Money with the government and the banking system of the country is not considered\nas a part of money supply.\n• Components of Money Supply:\nCurrency with Public + Demand Deposits with Commercial Banks + Other Deposits with the\nReserve Bank.\n237","image":"pages/IMG_0551.jpg"}],"subject":"macro","number":"5"},{"id":"6","title":"Banking","intro":"Commercial banks accept deposits and advance loans with the objective of earning profit.","lessons":[{"title":"Commercial banks and their functions","body":"Commercial banks accept deposits and advance loans with the objective of earning profit. Deposits include current, savings and fixed deposits. Primary functions are accepting deposits and lending; other functions include agency services and general-utility services. Banks mobilise saving and create demand deposits through lending. A central bank is the apex institution regulating the monetary and financial system; its objective is growth with stability rather than commercial profit.","formula":"","source":"IMG_0552","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"6.1 COMMERCIAL BANKS"},{"title":"Credit creation: follow every round","body":"Suppose an initial cash deposit of 1,000 enters the banking system and the reserve ratio is 20%. The first bank keeps 200 and lends 800. If 800 is redeposited, the next bank keeps 160 and lends 640. Repeated lending and redepositing expand total deposits to 5,000. These results assume no cash leakage, no excess reserves and loans redeposited in the banking system. Total deposits include the initial deposit; additional credit excludes it.","formula":"Deposit multiplier = 1 ÷ Reserve ratio\nTotal deposits = Initial deposit ÷ Reserve ratio\nAdditional credit = Total deposits − Initial deposit","source":"IMG_0554","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"6.2 MONEY CREATION BY THE COMMERCIAL BANKS"},{"title":"Central bank: seven functions","body":"The textbook lists issuer of currency, banker to the government, bankers’ bank and supervisor, lender of last resort, custodian of foreign exchange, clearing-house function and control of credit. As government banker it holds accounts, receives money, makes payments and manages borrowing. As bankers’ bank it holds bank reserves and supports banks. Clearing settles interbank claims. Lender of last resort provides emergency assistance when normal sources cannot meet a bank’s needs.","formula":"","source":"IMG_0555","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"6.3 THE CENTRAL BANK"},{"title":"Quantitative instruments: rates and open market operations","body":"Bank rate, repo rate and reverse repo rate influence the cost and placement of funds. The textbook distinguishes bank-rate lending without collateral from repo lending against securities under a repurchase agreement. Raising lending rates tends to raise borrowing cost and reduce credit demand; lowering them works in the opposite direction. RBI sales of securities absorb liquidity and reduce credit creation; RBI purchases release liquidity and increase it. These are textbook mechanisms, not a claim about today’s policy rates.","formula":"","source":"IMG_0557","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"6.4.1 Quantitative Instruments of Credit Control"},{"title":"Reserve ratios and qualitative instruments","body":"CRR is the percentage of deposits banks must keep with the RBI. SLR requires banks to maintain prescribed liquid assets such as cash, gold and approved securities. Higher reserve requirements constrain lending; lower requirements release lending capacity. Margin requirement is the difference between collateral value and loan value. Higher margins reduce permitted loans. Rationing fixes credit quotas, while moral suasion uses persuasion and pressure to influence bank lending. Quantitative instruments affect overall credit; qualitative instruments target its allocation.","formula":"","source":"IMG_0558","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"6.4.1 Quantitative Instruments · Qualitative Instruments"},{"title":"Ability Zone: when monetary tools succeed","body":"Repo-rate policy depends on banks’ reliance on RBI loans, responsiveness of credit demand, the wider structure of interest rates and availability of non-bank funds. Open market operations require a functioning securities market and an effective link between reserve changes and lending. Explaining a policy requires the transmission chain: policy change → bank funds or borrowing cost → credit → expenditure → aggregate demand.","formula":"","source":"IMG_0559","kind":"Ability Zone","textbookHeading":"ABILITY ZONE · Success of Repo Rate · Success of Open Market Operations"},{"title":"Issuer of currency and banker to government","textbookHeading":"6.3 THE CENTRAL BANK · Functions","body":"Issuer of Currency: the central bank has the principal note-issuing authority; in India, the ₹1 note and coins are issued by the Government of India. Centralised issue promotes uniformity and control. Banker to Government: the RBI keeps government deposits, receives payments, makes disbursements, advances funds within the applicable arrangements and manages public debt. Financial adviser is a related role. Distinguish issuing authority from circulation: government-issued coins enter circulation through the monetary system.","source":"IMG_0555","kind":"Exam points","formula":""},{"title":"Bankers’ bank, lender of last resort and clearing house","textbookHeading":"6.3 THE CENTRAL BANK · Functions","body":"Bankers’ Bank and Supervisory Role: commercial banks maintain required cash balances with the central bank, which regulates their functioning. Lender of Last Resort: the central bank offers financial accommodation in emergencies when other sources are unavailable. Clearing House: it settles interbank claims by adjusting banks’ accounts and facilitates fund transfers. Custodian of Foreign Exchange: it manages the country’s foreign-exchange reserves and can intervene in the exchange market. Control of Credit: it regulates the cost and availability of credit to support growth with stability.","source":"IMG_0556","kind":"Exam points","formula":""}],"pages":[{"id":"IMG_0552","image":"pages/IMG_0552.jpg","text":"dit Money:\nqual to its face (money) value\n© value\nall bodied money\n(d) (i), (ii), and (iii)\ne the correct option from\ngovernment.\nxplanation of Assertion (A)\nhe correct explanation of\nSanking\n8888888838\nive them?\ntal payments reflects a\nnce on cash. However,\nearly 65% of the total\nraceable transactions,\nectors.\ned)\nexcerpt?\nin India?\nI LIBRARY\nLAYOUT OF THE CHAPTER\nSection Title\nChapter\nsection\nCommercial Banks\n6.1\nMoney Creation by the\nCommercial Banks\nThe Central Bank\n6.3\nBasic Concepts Explained under each Section\n-Meaning of Commercial Bank\n-All Financial Institutions are not Banks\n-Process of Money Creation by the Commercial Banks\n-Factors affecting Money Creation by the Commercial Banks\n-CRR and Credit Multiplier\n-Concept of Central Bank\n-Functions of the Central Bank\n-The Central Bank vs. Commercial Bank\nPg. No.\n257-258\n258-262\n263-266\n6.4\nControl of Money Supply (or\nCredit Supply) by the Central\n-Quantitative Instruments of Credit Control\nBank (RBl in India)\n-Qualitative Instruments of Credit Control\n266-270\n• Ability Zone\n271\n• Power Points [Summary of the Chapter]\n272\n• WORK-BOOK [Question-answer Format]\n273-293\n• Self Assessment Test\n294-296\n• Digital Library\n296\n6.1 COMMERCIAL BANKS\nCommercial banks are an important component of banking system of a country. Let us first understand\nwhat a commercial bank means.\nMeaning of Commercial Bank\n* A commercial bank is that financial institution which accepts deposits from the people and gives\nloans for the purpose of consumption or investment.\n• Besides, commercial banks perform various other functions such as credit creation, transfer of\nfunds, agency jobs and general services.\n• According to Indian Banking Companies Act, \"Banking company is one which transacts the\nbusiness of banking which means the accepting (for the purpose of lending or investment) of\ndeposits of money from the public repayable on demand or otherwise and withdrawable by\ncheque, draft, order or otherwise.\"","file":"macro 6/IMG_0552.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0553","image":"pages/IMG_0553.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\n! CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS\nA financial institution is not a banking institution if it does not perform the two primary functions:\nAll Financial Institutions are not Banks\n- accepting chequeable deposits, and\n-making advances or offering loans.\n• LIC is a financial institution but not a bank, as it offers loans but does not accept chequeable deposits from the\n• Post offices are not banks, even though they accept deposits from the people. Because they do not offer\nloans.\nAVLR 6.1\nFINANCIAL INSTITUTIONS vs. BANKS\n6.2 MONEY CREATION BY THE COMMERCIAL BANKS\n• In the previous chapter, we noted that the commercial banks are an important source of money\nsupply in the economy.\n• Unlike the central bank, the commercial banks do not have the authority of issuing currency: they\ncannot issue notes or coins. Yet, they are the suppliers of money as they create money by way of\ndemand deposits.\nHere, we discuss the process of money creation by the commercial banks: how exactly the commercial\nbanks create money?\n6.2.1 Process of Money Creation by the Commercial Banks\nFollowing observations explain the process of money creation/credit creation by the commrcial\n• Banks receive cash deposits trom the people. These are called 'primary deposits'. Banks len\nmoney many times more than their cash reserves.\n• The borrowers can issue cheque sits from thet credit' (loans) in their accounts. The cheques arcae\nin the economy as money.\nPrimary deposits + Secondary furposits = Demand deposits of the people with the\ncommercial banks\n1. Demand deposits - bank deposits of till not offle which can be withdrawn by writing a cheque, without gin\nany advance notice. Demand deposits are also called chequeable deposits.\n258\n! CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS\nBanking\nPrimary and Secondary Deposits\n• Primary deposits are cash deposits with the commercial banks by the people. These are a part of total\ndemand deposits of the banks.\n• Secondary deposits are those deposits which arise on account of loans by the banks to the people.\nThese are also a part of total demand deposits of the banks.\n• Important to note it is, that while primary deposits indicate savings of the depositors with the banks,\nsecondary deposits indicate borrowings of the depositors from the banks. Secondary deposits are also\ncalled Derivative Deposits.\nTotal Demand Deposits of the People with the Commercial Banks\n= Primary Deposits of the Commercial Banks + Secondary/Derivative Deposits of the\nCommercial Banks\n• Total demand deposits with the banks are many times more than the cash reserves of the\ncommercial banks. This is because the commercial banks know by way of their historical\nexperience that all the depositors would not show up in the banks to withdraw all their deposits\n* If experience shows that withdrawals are generally around 10 per cent of demand deposits, the\nbanks need to keep only 10 per cent of deposits as cash reserves. All demand deposits (held by\nthe people) serve as money supply in the economy, just like cash held by the people.\n• Demand deposits serving as money supply is called bank money. This is money created by the\nbanks. Because this is circulating in the system not in the form of cash, but in the form of cheques\nissued by the banks to the holders of demand deposits.\nIllustration:\n• Let us illustrate the process of credit creation with the help of an example.\n• For the sake of simplicity, we assume that:\n• There is a 'single banking system' in the economy and initially, the bank receives deposits of 7 1,000.\n• CRR (Cash Reserve Ratio) = 10% and it does not change. [In the Indian banking system, CRR refers\nto that fraction (percentage) of total deposits of the people which commercial banks are obliged\nto hold as reserves (of cash) with the RBl. It is set and varied by the RBl from time to time.)\n• Table 6.1 shows how the system would work for the creation of money:\nTable 6.1: Creation of Money in a Single Banking System\nRound\nDeposits\nLoans\nCash Reserves\n® (CRR = 10%)\n1st Round\n2nd Round\n1,000\n3rd Round\n900 *\n810+\n(and so on till all excess reserves? are exhausted)\nTotal\n900\n810\n729\n9,000\n1,000\n2. Excess reserves - reserves held by the commercial banks after meeting the mandatory (legal) requirement of CRR\n259","file":"macro 6/IMG_0553.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0554","image":"pages/IMG_0554.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\n• In the first round, bank receives deposits of 7 1,000.\n• The cash reserves to tackle th formbility of 1,000 is equal to 100 (because cash reserve ratio\n= · 900 which they can use for the purpose of lending.\nis = 10% of total deposits) impiying that the banks have excess reserves = 1,000 - 10\n• By offering loans of 7900, deposits of the banks are raised by F 900.\n• In the second round, the banks need to hold cash reserves as 10% of ‹ 900 which die exces\nreserves of the bank.\n• So that 90(10% of 900) are retained as cash reserves, and 810(900 - 90) are loaned ou\nby way of another demand deposit.\n• Thus, loans increase by 7 810 and demand deposits also increase by 7 810. Now, excess reserves\nof the bank is 7 900 - 7 90 = 7 810 which can be given as loans. Consequently, demand deposits\nwould increase by & 810.\n• This process continues till total demand deposits are & 10,000, cash reserves are F 1,000, and\n• Thus, if cash reserve ratio is equal to 10%, initial deposits of & 1,000 (subsequently shifted to RBI\nas reserves) allows the bank to create demand deposits up to & 10,000. So that,\nDemand Deposits =\nCRR X Cash Reserves\n-X· 1,000\n10%\n= 10 x· 1,000 = 7 10,000\nNote: Loans are never offered in cash. Every loan is reflected as demand deposit of the borrower.\n(The borrower can use cheque to make payments out of his loan account).]\nFactors affecting Money Creation by the Commercial Banks\nMoney creation or credit creation by the commercial banks depends on these factors:\n(i) Cash Balances with Commercial Banks: Higher these cash balances, greater the money\ncreation (or credit creation) capacity of the commercial banks.\n(i) Cash Reserve Ratio (CRR): Higher the CRR, lower the capacity to create money.\n(i) Vault Cash: If banks are risk-averse, they try to keep higher 'excess reserves\", called vault.\ncash. Higher the vault cash, lower the money creation.\n(iv) Deposits by the Public: In countries like India where cash transactions are still very significant,\nbank deposits are low. Owinglane low can rlow into the banking system, credit creation\ncapacity of the banks also remains low.\n(V)- Business Expectations: \"Bullish and 'bearish' business expectations also influence credit\ncreaton by the commercial banks. Bullidin business expectations (When entrepreneurs are\nexpecting high profits) raises the demand sir credit, even at a higher interest rate. According!\nbanks are induced to create more credit by using their excess reserves. Conversely, 'bearish\nbusiness expectations (when profits are expected to be low) lowers the demand for credi\neven at a low rate of interest. Intemch a situation, banks are reluctant to create more cred\n260\neven when they have the excess reserves.\nI CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS\nBanking\n• SLR is Statutory Liquidity Ratio, and is detined as the minimum percentage of total deposits (demand\ndeposits + term deposits) of the people which the banks are required to hold in the form of liquid\nCRR VS. SLR, following are some critical observations:\nassets'. (We have already explained the concept of CRk, and are avoiding repetition). In the context of\n-Both CRR and SLR are legal reserve ratios. Both are set by the central bank (RBl in India) and are varied\nfrom time to time to manage the flow or liquidity in the economy.\n-Compliance of both CRR and SLK is mandatory for the commercial banks.\nBut, both are significantly different from each other, because,\n() while CRR-reserves include cash only, 'SLR-reserves' include:\n(a) cash reserves, (b) gold reserves, and (c) reserves of approved securities\n) while CRR reserves are deposited with the RBI, SLR reserves are held by the banks themseives.\nA Note of Caution\n• Never add up SLR and CRR to find LRR (legal reserve ratio). Both SLR and CRR are legal reserve ratios,\nbut independent of each other. Some +2 graders are found to believe that SLR + CRR = LRR. This is not\ncorrect.\n• If you consider that SLR + CRR = LRR, then, in a situation when LRR is constant, any increase in CRR\nmust imply a corresponding decrease in SLR, which is technically wrong.\n6.2.2 CRR and Credit Multiplier (also called Money Multiplier)\n• In India, CRR is determined not by the commercial banks themselves but by the RBI (Reserve Bank\nof India). Therefore, it is also called LRR (Legal Reserve Ratio).\n• The commercial banks are required to keep the stipulated (legally required) cash reserves not\nwith themselves, but with the RBI (of course, the banks can keep excess reserves as 'vault cash\"\nwith themselves).\n• Accordingly, credit multiplier indicates the maximum amount of money that the commercial\nbanks can create; given their cash reserves with the RBI. Once CRR is known (as fixed by the RBI),\nwe can find out credit multiplier\", or the number of times the commercial banks can create credit,\nper unit of their cash reserves with the RBI.\n\" Credit multiplier is found in terms of the following equation:\nk=\nHere, k = Credit multiplier; CRR = Cash reserve ratio.)\nIllustration:\n• If CRR = 10%, then\n• It implies that if CRR = 10% then the commercial banks can credit money 10 times of their cash\nк = 10%\n100\n261","file":"macro 6/IMG_0554.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0555","image":"pages/IMG_0555.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\nIf cash reserves are = T 10,000, the corsomercial banks can create credit, as per the following\nequation:\nCredit Creation or Money Creation = 710,000 x-\n10%\n= · 10,000 × 100\n10\n=·1,00,000\ncash reserves. This is because CRR IS legally dys wmined by the RBI, and the commercial banks\nmust comply with it.]\nThe illustrations given below deal with numerical problems on credit creation.\nL TACKLING CREDIT CREATION\nIllustration 1. Given: k = 5. Find CRR.\nk= CRR → 5= CRR\nCR = 5 = 0.2 or 20%.\nIllustration 2. Given: Primary deposits = R 2,000 and CRR = 12.5%. Find the total credit that the commercial banks\ncan create, when all primary deposits are kept with the RBl as reserves.\nCAR 125% 100\nTotal Credit Creation or Money Creation = Initial Deposits (subsequently shifted to RBl as reserves) × k\n= · 2,000 x 8=7 16,000.\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\nQ. 1. \"Demand deposits are equal to cash deposits with the commercial banks\". State whether true\nor false and give a reason for the same.\nAns. False. Cash deposits are only primary deposits with the commercial banks. Deposits created by\nway of loans are secondary deposits.\nDemand Deposits = Primary deposits + Secondary deposits\nQ.2. \"Higher CRR implies higher capacity to create credit\". State whether true or false and give a\nreason for the same.\nAns. False. Higher CRR implies lower capacity of the commercial banks to create credit. Because,\ncredit multiplier is the reciprocal of CRR.\nQ. 3. What role does CRR play in the creation of credit by the commercial banks?\nAns. CRR (cash reserve ratio) sets a limit up to which commercial banks can legally create credit.\nExample: 1f CRR = 4%, it implies that the commercial banks can create credit (by way of loans)\nmaximum up to 25 times\nof their cash reserves with the RBI.\nPROCESS OF CREDIT CREATION BY COMMERCIAL BANKS\n262\n6.3 THE CENTRAL BANK\nBanking\n6.3.1 Concept of Central Bank\n• The central bank is an apex bank that controls the entire banking system of a country.\n• It is the sole agency of note-issuing and controls the supply of money in the economy. It serves as\na banker to the government and manages fore (foreign exchange reserves of the country. The\nReserve Bank of India (RBI), establisnea in 1935, Is the central bank of India. It plays a crucial role\nin ensuring financial Stability and smooth funcaoning or the country ionetary system.\n6,3,2 Functions of the Central Bank\nFig. 6.1 displays the tunctions of the central bank or a country (with special reference to RBI in\nIndia).\nIssuer of Currency\nControl of Credit\nBanker to the\nGovernment\nFunctions of\nCentral Bank\nClearing\nHouse Function\nBanker's Bank and\nSupervisory Role\nCustodian of\nLender of the\nForeign Exchange\nFig. 6.1: Functions of Central Bank\n(1) Issuer of Currency: RBl in India has the exclusive right (monopoly right) of issuing currency.\nThis is called currency authority function of the central bank. The currency notes issued by\nthe central bank are an unlimited legal tender.\n• CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS\nCental bank of the country enjoys monopoly right of note-issuing. This has the following merts.\n) Monopoly rignt or note-ssuing by the central bank will impart untommy to the monetary system.\n(ii) Peoples contidence in the ci of the country grows stronger because the bank enjoys the\npatronage (support or backing) of the government.\n(lIt pies a central check on the supply of money in the country.\n(iv) Monopoly right of note-issuing imparts elasticity to the monetary system. Supply of money can be\nconveniently increased or decreased to tackle inflationary or deflationary situations in the countly.\n(v) Issuing of notes is a profitabntry. The ness. When it is done by the central bank, the protit so\nearned goes to the state excheduer (aovernment's treasury, or the place where government mone\n263","file":"macro 6/IMG_0555.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0556","image":"pages/IMG_0556.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\n(2) Banker to the Government: Central bank is a banker, agent, and financial advisor to the\ngovernment.\n(i) Banker to the Govenmer tot As the banker, the central bank manages the accoun\nof the government. This includeses naintaining government deposits, facilitating th\ntransfer of funds for varioussupblic expenditures, and handling transactions related t\nthe government's day-to-day hirratecial operations. The central bank also manages the\nissuance and redemption of government securities and treasury bills.\n(i) Agent to the Government: In its capacity as an agent, the central bank buys and sels\ngovernment securities on behalf of the government. This helps in managing government\ndebt and meeting financing needs. The central bank may also manage the countrys\nforeign exchange reserves and perform currency exchange operations in line with the\ngovernment's monetary policy.\n(i) Financial Advisor to the Government: The central bank acts as an advisor to the\ngovernment by providing expert advice on monetary policy, economic stability,\nand financial regulation. It helps in formulating policies related to interest rates,\ninflation control, and the regulation of the money market. The central bank's advice\nis critical in shaping policies that maintain price stability and promote sustainable\neconomic growth.\n(3) Bankers' Bank and Supervisory Role: As a Bankers' Bank, it has almost the same relation with\nother banks in the country as a commercial bank has with its customers. Three observations\nneed to be noted in this context:\n(i) The central bank accepts deposits from the commercial banks, and offers them loan.\n(ii) The central bank provides 'Clearing House' facility to the commercial banks. It is a\ncheque clearing facility provided at one centre to all the banks.\n(iii) In its supervisory role, the central bank ensures that the commercial banks show\ncompliance to its directives, particularly relating to CRR and SLR. The central bank\nchanges CRR, SLR as and when required. It ensures that the commercial banks show\ncompliance to these changes so that the desired targets are achieved.\n(4) Lender of the Last Resort: It means that if a commercial bank fails to get financial\naccommodation from anywhere, it approaches the central bank as a last resort.\n• Central bank advances loan to such a bank against approved securities. By offering loans to\nthe commercial banks in situations of emergency, the central bank ensures:\n* that the banking system of the country does not suffer any set-back, and\n> that money market remains stable.\n(5) Custodian of Foreign Exchange: Central bank is the custodian of nation's foreign exchange\nreserves. It also exercises \"Tuasy suy d floating to ensure stability of exchange rate in the\ninternational money market.\n3. Managed Roating - the sale and purchase wew gin exchange with a view to achieving stability of exchang\nrate for the domestic currency.\n264\n(6) Clearing House Function: Central bank performs the function of a clearing house\nBanking\na cheque of ? 15,000 drawn on Bank A. Both, Banks A and B have their accounts with\nthe central bank. The cheques of both the banks are cleared through their accounts with\nthe central bank. This is how the central bank acts as a clearing house. It avoids transfer\nof cash between the banks and reduces requirement of cash.\n(7) Control of Credit: The principal unction of the central bank is to control the supply of credit\nin the economy.\n• It implies increase or decrease in the supply of money in the economy by regulating the\n'creation of credit' by the commercial banks.\n• The central bank needs to control the supply of money to cope with the situations of inflation\nand deflation. During inflation, the supply of money is reduced and during deflation, it is\n(Note: Section 6.4 of the chapter gives a detailed description of how the central bank controls supply of\nmoney in the economy]\nPerforming all these functions, the central bank focuses on growth with stability. (Growth refers to\na sustained rise in GDP. Stability refers to the elimination of inflationary and deflationary situations\nin the economy.)\nAfter understanding the meaning and role of commercial banks and central bank let us look at the\ndifference between the two which is expressed in the Table 6.2.\nTable 6.2: The Central Bank vs. Commercial Bank\nBasis of Difference\n1. Meaning\nThe Central Bank\nCommercial Bank\n- The central bank is the apex bank-the bank\n- A commercial bank is that financial institution\nof all banks in the country. All commercial banks\nfunction under the control of the central bank.\n• accepts deposits from the general public\n- It accepts deposits from the commercial\nbanks and advances loans to them. But, it\ndoes not deal with the general public.\n2. Role in Money\nSupply\n- The central bank has the monopoly right of\nnote-issuing and is therefore, the principal\nsource of money supply.\n3. Role in Forex\nReserves\n- The central bank is a custodian of forex\nreserves\nof\ncountry.\nIt conducts\nmanaged floating' to regulate exchange rate\nof the domestic currency.\n- The central bank focuses on growth and\nstability of the economy.\nand\n• offers loans to the people for purpose of\nconsumption and investment.\n- A commercial bank contributes to the supply\nof money by way of credit creation. Unlike\ncentral bank, commercial bank is not a\nnote-issuing authority.\n- A commercial bank is not a custodian of\nforex reserves of the country. However, it\ndeals in the sale and purchase of foreign\nexchange for purpose of profit.\ncommercial\nbank focuses on\nprofit\nmaximisation.\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\nQ.4. What is a central bank?\nAns. A central bank is an apex instyasad n of a country that controis and regulates the monetary anc\nfinancial system of the country.\n265","file":"macro 6/IMG_0556.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0557","image":"pages/IMG_0557.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\nQ. 5. What is the significance of centralised cash reserves with central bank?\nAns. Two observations need to be noted in this context:\n(i) Centralised cash reserves enable the lysi to ofter nancial help to the commercial banks\nduring emergencies. It is called \"finde cal accommodation' by the RBI. Banks get financia\naccommodation (or financial help) in times of emergency.\n(il) Centralised can reserves enable thy to pi to exercise control over the commercial bank\nbecause these reserves depend the-kk (nea by kBl In india) and by varying the CRR, the RBI\ncan increase or uecredse tarkeredit creation capacity or the commercial panks. Accordingly\nmoney supply in the economy is regulated.\n6.4 CONTROL OF MONEY SUPPLY (OR CREDIT SUPPLY)\nBY THE CENTRAL BANK (RBI IN INDIA)\n• The central bank adopts various measures to control the supply of money in the economy. Largely.\nthese measures relate to credit supply by the commercial banks. These are broadly classified as:\n6.4.1 Quantitative Instruments, and\n6.4.2 Qualitative Instruments.\n• The following flow chart shows instruments of credit control in detail.\nInstruments of Credit Control\nQuantitative Instruments\nQualitative Instruments\nBank\nRate\nRepo\nRate\nReverse\nRepo\nRate\nReserve\nRatio\nStatutory\nLiquidity\nRatio\nOpen\nMargin\nMarket\nRequirement\nOperations\n→\nRationing\nof Credit\nMoral\nSuasion\nThree Policy Rates used\nTwo Policy Ratios used\nby the RBl as instruments\nby the RBl as instruments\nof credit control\nof credit control\n• Following is a brief description of these instruments. It may be noted that these instruments are\nused to decrease the supply of money when there is inflationary spiral in the economy and to\nincrease the supply of money when there is deflationary spiral in the economy.\n6.4.1 Quantitative Instruments of Credit Control\n• Quantitative instruments are those instruments of credit control which focus on the overall supply\nof money in the economy. Supply of money is lowered to tackle inflation, and it is raised to tackle\nFollowing is a brief description of these instruments:\n(1) Bank kate: Bank rate refers to the rate of interest at which the RBI lends money to the\nCommercial banks without any colluritral (security for purpose of loans). Often, it applies to\ninstant (immediate) loan requirement of the commercial banks.\n• The increase (or decrease) in bank rate is often followed by increase (or decrease) in the marke\nrate of interest (the interest rate charged by the commercial banks from the general public\n266\n• Accordingly, the cost of credit (also called the cost of capital changes in the market.\nWhen bank rate Is increased, market rate of interest is also increased, As a result, the cos\nof capital increases. This lowers the demand for credit and therefore, the supply of mone\ntends to fall. Consequently, inflation is corrected.\n• on the other hand, when bank rate is decreased, market rate of interest is also decreased.\nThe cost of capital also ded lases. This increases demand tor credit and thererore, supply\nof money tends to rise. Accordingly, deflation is corrected.\nRise in Bank Rate\n→ Rise in market rate of interest\ndemand for credit\n→ Fall in the supply of money → Inflation is controlled.\n> Rise in cost of capital → Fall in\nFall in Bank Rate\n→ Fall in market rate of interest → Fall in cost of capital\ndemand for credit\n→ Rise in the supply of money → Deflation is controlled.\n→ Rise in\n(2) Open Market Operations (OMOs): Open market operations refer to the sale and purchase\nof securities in the open market by the RBI on behalf of the government.\n• By selling the securities (like, National Saving Certificates-NSCs) in the open market,\nthe RBI soaks liquidity (cash) from the economy. And, by buying the securities, the RBI\nreleases liquidity.\n• When liquidity is soaked (as during inflation), cash reserves of the commercial banks are\nsqueezed. Implying a cut in their credit creation capacity.\n• On the other hand, when liquidity is released (as during recession/deflation), cash reserves\nof the banks tend to rise. Implying a rise in credit creation capacity of the commercial\nbanks.\n• Thus, inflation is corrected by selling the securities and soaking liquidity, while deflation is\ncorrected by buying the securities and releasing liquidity.\nSale of Securities by the RBI Soaks liquidity and leads to a fall in cash reserves of the\ncommercial banks — Fall in credit creation capacity of the commercial banks -\n→ Fall in\nmoney supply Inflation is controlled.\nPurchase of Securities by the RBI → Releases liquidity and leads to a rise in cash reserves of\nthe commercial banks -\n→ Rise in credit creation capacity of the commercial banks\n→ Rise\nin money supply\n> Deflation is controlled.\nTwo Types of Open Market Operations\nOutright\n• Outright open market operations\nare permanent in nature\n(as discussed above, the sale and\npurchase of securities is outright\nRepo\n• Under repo open market\noperations, there is a promise of\nrepurchase and resale of\nsecurities (as discussed below).\nor permanent).\n(3) Repo Rate: The rate at which the RBI (central bank) offers short period loans to the\ncommercial banks by buying the government securities in the open market is called Repo\nRate'. In fact, it is a Repurchase Rate.\n• A repurchase agreement is signed by both the parties stating that the securites will be\nrepurchased by the commercial banks on a given date at a predetermined price.\n267","file":"macro 6/IMG_0557.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0558","image":"pages/IMG_0558.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\n• In other words, the RBl issues a loan cheque to the commercial banks by buying from them\nthe government securities. B of it carries the agreement of repurchase of securities by the\ncommercial banks at the predetermined date and at a predetermined price.\n• During inflation, the cost of capital is increased by incredsing the repo rate. Ihis lowers\nthe demand for credit and accordingly, the supply of money in the economy, as desired.\n• During deflation, the cost Oaticapital is reduced by reducing the repo rate. This increases\nthe demand for credit and accordingly, the supply or money in the economy, as desired\nRise in Repo Rate → Rise in cost of capital → rall in demand for credit → Fall in supply\nof money by the commercial banks Inflation is controlled.\nFall in Repo Rate → Fall in cost of capital → Rise in demand for credit → Rise in supply\nof money by the commercial banks → Deflation is controlled.\n(4) Reverse Repo Rate: The rate at which the RBI (central bank) accepts deposits from the\ncommercial banks (through government securities) is called 'Reverse Repo Rate' It is also\ncalled Reverse Repurchase Rate.\n• In this case, a reverse repurchase agreement is signed by both the parties stating that the\nsecurities will be repurchased on a given date at a predetermined price. Reverse repo rate\nallows the commercial banks to generate interest income.\n• When reverse repo rate is lowered, banks are discouraged to park their surplus funds\nwith the RBI. Instead, the banks may use these funds as CRR-funds with the RBI. This leads\nto a rise in credit supply (money supply) by the commercial banks. Accordingly, supply of\nmoney is enhanced in the economy, as desired to control deflation.\n• On the other hand, a rise in reverse repo rate may induce the commercial banks to park\nmore funds with the RBI to generate interest income. This lowers their capacity to offer\nCRR-funds to the RBI for the creation of credit. Accordingly, supply of money is reduced in\nthe economy, as desired to control inflation.\nFall in Reverse Repo Rate → Less funds are parked by the commercial banks with the RBI to\ngenerateinterestincome→ Morefundsareusedas CRR-fundswiththeRB/forthecreationofcredit\n→ Supply of money increases\n→ Deflation is controlled.\nRise in Reverse Repo Rate → More funds are parked by the commercial banks with the RB\nto generate interest income Less funds are used as CRR-funds with the RBI for the creation\nof credit\n> Supply of money decreases\n> Inflation is controlled.\n(5) Cash Reserve Ratio (CRR): Cash reserve ratio refers to a percentage of a bank's total\ndeposits to be kept with the RBl as a legal requirement. It is fixed by the RBI and is varied\nfrom time to time to regulate the supply of money in the economy.\n• When the supply of money is to be increased, CRR is lowered, and when the supply of\nmoney is to be reduced, CRR is raised.\nRise in CRR -\n→ Rise in cash reserves for a given amount of demand deposits\n> Fall in\nmoney supply of the commercial banks → Inflation is controlled.\nFall in CRR → Fall in cash reserves for a given amount of demand deposits\nsupply of the commercial banks\n- Rise in money\n→ Deflation is controlled.\n268\n(6) Statutory Liquidity Ratio (SLR): Every bank is required to maintain a fixed percentage of its\nassets in the form of liquid assets, called SLR.\n• The liquid assets include: cash, gold, and unencumbered approved securities.\n• The rate of SLR (like that of CRR) is fixed by the RBI and is varied from time to time.\nTo decrease the supply of money (as during inflation), the central bank increases SLR.\nAccordingly, funds available for CRR-deposits (for the creation of credit) are reduced.\n• Conversely, SLR is reduced to increase the supply of money (as during deflation) in the\neconomy. Accordingly, funds available for CRR-deposits (for the creation of credit) are\nRise in SLR → Rise in liquid assets to be held by the commercial banks with themselves\n→ Fall in the availability of funds for CRR-deposits with the RBI → Fall in money supply of\nthe commercial banks → Inflation is controlled.\nFall in SLR → Fall in liquid assets to be held by the commercial banks with themselves\n→ Rise in the availability of funds for CRR-deposits with the RBI → Rise in money supply of\nthe commercial banks → Deflation is controlled.\nKAVLR 6.3\nINSTRUMENTS OF CREDIT CONTROL (QUANTITATIVE INSTRUMENTS) -\nPARTI\n• Qualitative instruments are those instruments of credit control which focus on select sectors of\nthe economy.\n• These instruments are used to increase or decrease the supply of money to select sectors of the\neconomy (these are those sectors which are the principal sources of instability in the economy).\nBroadly, the qualitative instruments are placed in three categories, as under:\n(1) Margin Requirement: The margin requirement refers to the difference between the current\nvalue of the security offered for loan (called collateral) and the value of loan granted.\n• Suppose, a person mortgages his house worth & 1 crore with the bank for a loan of 280 lakh.\nThe margin requirement in this case would be 7 20 lakh. The margin requirement is raised\nWhen the supply of money needs to be reduced. The margin requirement is lowered when\nthe supply of money is to be increased. Often the margin requirement is kept high for\nspeculative (trading) activities.\nRise in Margin Requirement → Fall in demand for credit → Fall in supply of credit by the\ncommercial banks → Fall in money supply → Inflation is controlled.\nFall in Margin Requirement → Rise in demand for credit → Rise in supply of credit by the\ncommercial banks → Rise in money supply\n→ Deflation is controlled.\nUnencumbered approved securities - are those securities which are free of claims, or tree of any debt agains\n269","file":"macro 6/IMG_0558.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0559","image":"pages/IMG_0559.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\n(2) Rationing of Credit: Rationi per? crealt reters to fixation of crealt quotas for different\nbusiness activities. Rationing or credvet is introduced when the supply of credit is to be\nchecked particularly for speculative act irites in the economy. This Is also called selective\ncredit control.\n• The RBI fixes credit quota for different businey activities. he commercial banks cannot\neconomy, and intanon is controlled. Unren Oner hand, ratoning of credit if already in\npractice) is withdrawn to increase the supply of money. This controls deflation.\nIntroduction of Credit Rationing → Decreases the supply of credit by the commercial banks\n→ Decreases the supply of money → Inflation is controlled.\nWithdrawal of Credit Rationing → Increases the supply of credit by the commecial banks\n→ Increases the supply of money Deflation is controlled.\n(3) Moral Suasion: Moral suasion is like rendering an advice to the commercial banks by the\nRBI to follow its directives.\n• Moral suasion is a combination of both 'persuasion' and 'pressure'. The RBI tries to\npersuade the commercial banks to follow its directives. If persuasion does not work, it\nuses the required pressure as an apex bank of the country. If pressure also does not work,\nthe RBI can use direct action which includes derecognition of the concerned bank. As an\ninstrument of monetary policy, 'moral suasion' works both as a quantitative instrument as\nwell as a qualitative instrument. However, often it is classified as a qualitative instrument.\nThe banks are advised to restrict loans during inflation, and be liberal in lending during\n3JAVLR 6.4\nINSTRUMENTS OF CREDIT CONTROL (QUALITATIVE INSTRUMENTS) -\nPART II\n270\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\nQ. 6. If the commercial banks buy government securities, their capacity to create credit is reduced.\nDo you agree?\nAlls, Tes, trie sven statement is correy allowing or inducing the commercial banks to buy\nsovernment securities, the central bank soaks 'cash balances' or 'excess reserves' of the banks\nwhich they could use to create credit. Accordingly, tuy: credit creation capacity of the commerci\nbanks is reduced.\nQ. 1. How would you interpret a fall in repo rate fof›m 5% to 4% in the context of money supply ir\nAns.) innere a tamin repo rate from 5% to 4%of he central bank is giving a signal of easy mone\npolicy. Funds will be easily available fipply the central bank in case commercial banks nee\ninem. Accordingly, commercial banks need not maintain high reserves of vault cash\ni Creation of credit by the commercial bankient ikely to be more responsive to the demand to\ncredit. Accordingly, supply of money is likely to increase,\nBanking\nABILITY ZONE\nsuccess of Repo Rate as an Instrument of Credit Control\n• Success of repo rate policy as an instrument of credit control depends on the following factors:\n• Degree of Dependence of the Commercial Banks upon the Central Bank for Loans:\n> If commercial banks have their own surplus funds which they can utilise during periods of\nhigh credit needs, their dependence on the central bank is reduced.\n• Degree of Sensitivity of Bank's Demand for Funds from the Central Bank:\n› Depending on business conditions, commercial banks may not be very sensitive to small\nvariations in repo rate. In such situations, repo rate policy may not be a big success.\n• Structure of Interest Rates in the Money Market:\n› Ifnon-banking financial institutions in the market do not vary their interest rates in accordance\nwith what the central bank expects from the commercial banks, the repo rate policy may not\nsucceed.\n• Overall Supply of Funds in the Market:\n> Repo rate policy may not be a success if non-banking sources of funds are more popular\nthan the banking sources.\nSuccess of Open Market Operations as an Instrument of Credit Control\n• Success of open market operations as an instrument of credit control depends on the following factors:\n• Existence of Securities Market:\n›There must be a well organised and well functioning market for the sale and purchase of\nsecurities. In the absence of it, open market operations would be of little significance as an\ninstrument of monetary policy.\n• Excess Reserves with the Banks:\n• If commercial banks have healthy excess reserves as Vault Cash' they need not buy\nsecurities. This is the standard practice in developed countries like USA. Accordingly, open\nmarket operations fail to be a successful instrument of credit control.\nTable 6.3: Bank Rate vs. Repo Rate\nBank Rate\nRepo Rate\n- Bank rate relates to the loans offered by the RBI to the\n- Repo rate relates to the loans offered by the RBI\ncommercial banks without any collateral (security for\nto the commercial banks NOT without collateral.\npurpose of loans).\nThe securities are pledged as a security for the loans.\n- Bank rate does not allow any facility of repurchase\n- Repo rate allows repurchase of securities. The holder of\nof securities. The bank rate is simply the Rate of\nDiscount.\nsecurities can repurchase them at a later date. Therefore,\nrepo rate is also called Repurchase Rate.\n- Bank rate relates to borrowings by the commercial\n- Repo rate relates to short-term borrowings by the\nbanks to cope with their immediate cash-crunch.\ncommercial banks.\nte bank ta ger tan te repo rate as it (bank rate) relates to instant loan requirement of\nthe commercial banks.]\n271","file":"macro 6/IMG_0559.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0560","image":"pages/IMG_0560.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\nPower Points [Summary of the Chapter]\n• Money Creation by the Commercial Banks:\n• Commercial banks contribute to money supply by creating credit. They do it by adring de,\n(in terms of demand deposits) many times more than their cash reserves.\n• They do it on the basis oftheir historical experience that the deposit holders never tum up an a\n• That, the liability towards the deposit holders can be managed by keeping only a trig\n(all together) to withdraw their deposits.\npercentage of deposits as cash reserves.\n• Credit Mutilier = CAR. It shows the number of times the comeril as a ey\nunit of their cash reserves with the RBI. In India, CRR is fixed by the RBI.\n• The Central Bank is an apex bank of the entire banking system of a country. RBI is the central ta\n• Functions:\n• Issuer of currency\n> Banker to the government\n› Bankers' bank and supervisory role\n• Lender of the last resort\n• Custodian of foreign exchange\n> Clearing house function\n• Control of credit\n• Control of Money Supply by the Centrai Bank\n• Monetary Policy is the policy to control the supply of credit/money in the econom, tait.\ncorrecting the situations of inflation and deflation in the economy.\n• Instruments of monetary policy are broadly classified as:\n• Quantitative instruments, and\n• Qualitative instruments.\nThese are also called 'instruments of credit control.\n• Quantitative Instruments of Monetary Policy:\n* Three policy rates: (i) Bank rate, ii) Repo rate, and ii Reverse repo rate.\n• Two policy ratios: (1) CRR, and (i) SLR.\n* Open market operations.\n• Qualitative Instruments of Monetary Policy:\n• Moral suasion,\n• Rationing of credit, and\n• Margin requirement.\n272\nCompetenc\n1. Multiple CI\nChoose the co\n1. ReservE\n(a) 19:\n(c) 19\n2. In the c\n(1) Det\n(I1) The\n(II1) The\n(IV) The\nOptions\n(a) (I),\n(c) (1) a\n3. As the E\n(a) Pur\n(b) Mai\n(c) Clea\n(d) Faci\n4. If the to\ndeposits\n(a) 7 20\n(c) 7 10\n5.\n(a) Cort\n(c) Con\n6. In an im\n· 1,000,\n2) 5,00\n(c) 4,00\n7. The inter\nthe borr\n(a) Bant\n(c) ResE\n[\" Key: In!\nat","file":"macro 6/IMG_0560.HEIC"}],"boxes":[{"title":"Ability Zone","page":"IMG_0552","text":"271","image":"pages/IMG_0552.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS","page":"IMG_0553","text":"A financial institution is not a banking institution if it does not perform the two primary functions:\nAll Financial Institutions are not Banks\n- accepting chequeable deposits, and\n-making advances or offering loans.\n• LIC is a financial institution but not a bank, as it offers loans but does not accept chequeable deposits from the\n• Post offices are not banks, even though they accept deposits from the people. Because they do not offer\nloans.\nAVLR 6.1\nFINANCIAL INSTITUTIONS vs. BANKS","image":"pages/IMG_0553.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS","page":"IMG_0553","text":"Banking\nPrimary and Secondary Deposits\n• Primary deposits are cash deposits with the commercial banks by the people. These are a part of total\ndemand deposits of the banks.\n• Secondary deposits are those deposits which arise on account of loans by the banks to the people.\nThese are also a part of total demand deposits of the banks.\n• Important to note it is, that while primary deposits indicate savings of the depositors with the banks,\nsecondary deposits indicate borrowings of the depositors from the banks. Secondary deposits are also\ncalled Derivative Deposits.\nTotal Demand Deposits of the People with the Commercial Banks\n= Primary Deposits of the Commercial Banks + Secondary/Derivative Deposits of the\nCommercial Banks\n• Total demand deposits with the banks are many times more than the cash reserves of the\ncommercial banks. This is because the commercial banks know by way of their historical\nexperience that all the depositors would not show up in the banks to withdraw all their deposits\n* If experience shows that withdrawals are generally around 10 per cent of demand deposits, the\nbanks need to keep only 10 per cent of deposits as cash reserves. All demand deposits (held by\nthe people) serve as money supply in the economy, just like cash held by the people.\n• Demand deposits serving as money supply is called bank money. This is money created by the\nbanks. Because this is circulating in the system not in the form of cash, but in the form of cheques\nissued by the banks to the holders of demand deposits.\nIllustration:\n• Let us illustrate the process of credit creation with the help of an example.\n• For the sake of simplicity, we assume that:\n• There is a 'single banking system' in the economy and initially, the bank receives deposits of 7 1,000.\n• CRR (Cash Reserve Ratio) = 10% and it does not change. [In the Indian banking system, CRR refers\nto that fraction (percentage) of total deposits of the people which commercial banks are obliged\nto hold as reserves (of cash) with the RBl. It is set and varied by the RBl from time to time.)\n• Table 6.1 shows how the system would work for the creation of money:\nTable 6.1: Creation of Money in a Single Banking System\nRound\nDeposits\nLoans\nCash Reserves\n® (CRR = 10%)\n1st Round\n2nd Round\n1,000\n3rd Round\n900 *\n810+\n(and so on till all excess reserves? are exhausted)\nTotal\n900\n810\n729\n9,000\n1,000\n2. Excess reserves - reserves held by the commercial banks after meeting the mandatory (legal) requirement of CRR\n259","image":"pages/IMG_0553.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS","page":"IMG_0554","text":"Banking\n• SLR is Statutory Liquidity Ratio, and is detined as the minimum percentage of total deposits (demand\ndeposits + term deposits) of the people which the banks are required to hold in the form of liquid\nCRR VS. SLR, following are some critical observations:\nassets'. (We have already explained the concept of CRk, and are avoiding repetition). In the context of\n-Both CRR and SLR are legal reserve ratios. Both are set by the central bank (RBl in India) and are varied\nfrom time to time to manage the flow or liquidity in the economy.\n-Compliance of both CRR and SLK is mandatory for the commercial banks.\nBut, both are significantly different from each other, because,\n() while CRR-reserves include cash only, 'SLR-reserves' include:\n(a) cash reserves, (b) gold reserves, and (c) reserves of approved securities\n) while CRR reserves are deposited with the RBI, SLR reserves are held by the banks themseives.\nA Note of Caution\n• Never add up SLR and CRR to find LRR (legal reserve ratio). Both SLR and CRR are legal reserve ratios,\nbut independent of each other. Some +2 graders are found to believe that SLR + CRR = LRR. This is not\ncorrect.\n• If you consider that SLR + CRR = LRR, then, in a situation when LRR is constant, any increase in CRR\nmust imply a corresponding decrease in SLR, which is technically wrong.\n6.2.2 CRR and Credit Multiplier (also called Money Multiplier)\n• In India, CRR is determined not by the commercial banks themselves but by the RBI (Reserve Bank\nof India). Therefore, it is also called LRR (Legal Reserve Ratio).\n• The commercial banks are required to keep the stipulated (legally required) cash reserves not\nwith themselves, but with the RBI (of course, the banks can keep excess reserves as 'vault cash\"\nwith themselves).\n• Accordingly, credit multiplier indicates the maximum amount of money that the commercial\nbanks can create; given their cash reserves with the RBI. Once CRR is known (as fixed by the RBI),\nwe can find out credit multiplier\", or the number of times the commercial banks can create credit,\nper unit of their cash reserves with the RBI.\n\" Credit multiplier is found in terms of the following equation:\nk=\nHere, k = Credit multiplier; CRR = Cash reserve ratio.)\nIllustration:\n• If CRR = 10%, then\n• It implies that if CRR = 10% then the commercial banks can credit money 10 times of their cash\nк = 10%\n100\n261","image":"pages/IMG_0554.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"IMG_0555","text":"Q. 1. \"Demand deposits are equal to cash deposits with the commercial banks\". State whether true\nor false and give a reason for the same.\nAns. False. Cash deposits are only primary deposits with the commercial banks. Deposits created by\nway of loans are secondary deposits.\nDemand Deposits = Primary deposits + Secondary deposits\nQ.2. \"Higher CRR implies higher capacity to create credit\". State whether true or false and give a\nreason for the same.\nAns. False. Higher CRR implies lower capacity of the commercial banks to create credit. Because,\ncredit multiplier is the reciprocal of CRR.\nQ. 3. What role does CRR play in the creation of credit by the commercial banks?\nAns. CRR (cash reserve ratio) sets a limit up to which commercial banks can legally create credit.\nExample: 1f CRR = 4%, it implies that the commercial banks can create credit (by way of loans)\nmaximum up to 25 times\nof their cash reserves with the RBI.\nPROCESS OF CREDIT CREATION BY COMMERCIAL BANKS\n262","image":"pages/IMG_0555.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS","page":"IMG_0555","text":"Cental bank of the country enjoys monopoly right of note-issuing. This has the following merts.\n) Monopoly rignt or note-ssuing by the central bank will impart untommy to the monetary system.\n(ii) Peoples contidence in the ci of the country grows stronger because the bank enjoys the\npatronage (support or backing) of the government.\n(lIt pies a central check on the supply of money in the country.\n(iv) Monopoly right of note-issuing imparts elasticity to the monetary system. Supply of money can be\nconveniently increased or decreased to tackle inflationary or deflationary situations in the countly.\n(v) Issuing of notes is a profitabntry. The ness. When it is done by the central bank, the protit so\nearned goes to the state excheduer (aovernment's treasury, or the place where government mone\n263","image":"pages/IMG_0555.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"IMG_0556","text":"Q.4. What is a central bank?\nAns. A central bank is an apex instyasad n of a country that controis and regulates the monetary anc\nfinancial system of the country.\n265","image":"pages/IMG_0556.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"IMG_0559","text":"Q. 6. If the commercial banks buy government securities, their capacity to create credit is reduced.\nDo you agree?\nAlls, Tes, trie sven statement is correy allowing or inducing the commercial banks to buy\nsovernment securities, the central bank soaks 'cash balances' or 'excess reserves' of the banks\nwhich they could use to create credit. Accordingly, tuy: credit creation capacity of the commerci\nbanks is reduced.\nQ. 1. How would you interpret a fall in repo rate fof›m 5% to 4% in the context of money supply ir\nAns.) innere a tamin repo rate from 5% to 4%of he central bank is giving a signal of easy mone\npolicy. Funds will be easily available fipply the central bank in case commercial banks nee\ninem. Accordingly, commercial banks need not maintain high reserves of vault cash\ni Creation of credit by the commercial bankient ikely to be more responsive to the demand to\ncredit. Accordingly, supply of money is likely to increase,\nBanking","image":"pages/IMG_0559.jpg"},{"title":"ABILITY ZONE","page":"IMG_0559","text":"success of Repo Rate as an Instrument of Credit Control\n• Success of repo rate policy as an instrument of credit control depends on the following factors:\n• Degree of Dependence of the Commercial Banks upon the Central Bank for Loans:\n> If commercial banks have their own surplus funds which they can utilise during periods of\nhigh credit needs, their dependence on the central bank is reduced.\n• Degree of Sensitivity of Bank's Demand for Funds from the Central Bank:\n› Depending on business conditions, commercial banks may not be very sensitive to small\nvariations in repo rate. In such situations, repo rate policy may not be a big success.\n• Structure of Interest Rates in the Money Market:\n› Ifnon-banking financial institutions in the market do not vary their interest rates in accordance\nwith what the central bank expects from the commercial banks, the repo rate policy may not\nsucceed.\n• Overall Supply of Funds in the Market:\n> Repo rate policy may not be a success if non-banking sources of funds are more popular\nthan the banking sources.\nSuccess of Open Market Operations as an Instrument of Credit Control\n• Success of open market operations as an instrument of credit control depends on the following factors:\n• Existence of Securities Market:\n›There must be a well organised and well functioning market for the sale and purchase of\nsecurities. In the absence of it, open market operations would be of little significance as an\ninstrument of monetary policy.\n• Excess Reserves with the Banks:\n• If commercial banks have healthy excess reserves as Vault Cash' they need not buy\nsecurities. This is the standard practice in developed countries like USA. Accordingly, open\nmarket operations fail to be a successful instrument of credit control.\nTable 6.3: Bank Rate vs. Repo Rate\nBank Rate\nRepo Rate\n- Bank rate relates to the loans offered by the RBI to the\n- Repo rate relates to the loans offered by the RBI\ncommercial banks without any collateral (security for\nto the commercial banks NOT without collateral.\npurpose of loans).\nThe securities are pledged as a security for the loans.\n- Bank rate does not allow any facility of repurchase\n- Repo rate allows repurchase of securities. The holder of\nof securities. The bank rate is simply the Rate of\nDiscount.\nsecurities can repurchase them at a later date. Therefore,\nrepo rate is also called Repurchase Rate.\n- Bank rate relates to borrowings by the commercial\n- Repo rate relates to short-term borrowings by the\nbanks to cope with their immediate cash-crunch.\ncommercial banks.\nte bank ta ger tan te repo rate as it (bank rate) relates to instant loan requirement of\nthe commercial banks.]\n271","image":"pages/IMG_0559.jpg"},{"title":"Power Points [Summary of the Chapter]","page":"IMG_0560","text":"• Money Creation by the Commercial Banks:\n• Commercial banks contribute to money supply by creating credit. They do it by adring de,\n(in terms of demand deposits) many times more than their cash reserves.\n• They do it on the basis oftheir historical experience that the deposit holders never tum up an a\n• That, the liability towards the deposit holders can be managed by keeping only a trig\n(all together) to withdraw their deposits.\npercentage of deposits as cash reserves.\n• Credit Mutilier = CAR. It shows the number of times the comeril as a ey\nunit of their cash reserves with the RBI. In India, CRR is fixed by the RBI.\n• The Central Bank is an apex bank of the entire banking system of a country. RBI is the central ta\n• Functions:\n• Issuer of currency\n> Banker to the government\n› Bankers' bank and supervisory role\n• Lender of the last resort\n• Custodian of foreign exchange\n> Clearing house function\n• Control of credit\n• Control of Money Supply by the Centrai Bank\n• Monetary Policy is the policy to control the supply of credit/money in the econom, tait.\ncorrecting the situations of inflation and deflation in the economy.\n• Instruments of monetary policy are broadly classified as:\n• Quantitative instruments, and\n• Qualitative instruments.\nThese are also called 'instruments of credit control.\n• Quantitative Instruments of Monetary Policy:\n* Three policy rates: (i) Bank rate, ii) Repo rate, and ii Reverse repo rate.\n• Two policy ratios: (1) CRR, and (i) SLR.\n* Open market operations.\n• Qualitative Instruments of Monetary Policy:\n• Moral suasion,\n• Rationing of credit, and\n• Margin requirement.\n272\nCompetenc\n1. Multiple CI\nChoose the co\n1. ReservE\n(a) 19:\n(c) 19\n2. In the c\n(1) Det\n(I1) The\n(II1) The\n(IV) The\nOptions\n(a) (I),\n(c) (1) a\n3. As the E\n(a) Pur\n(b) Mai\n(c) Clea\n(d) Faci\n4. If the to\ndeposits\n(a) 7 20\n(c) 7 10\n5.\n(a) Cort\n(c) Con\n6. In an im\n· 1,000,\n2) 5,00\n(c) 4,00\n7. The inter\nthe borr\n(a) Bant\n(c) ResE\n[\" Key: In!\nat","image":"pages/IMG_0560.jpg"}],"subject":"macro","number":"6"},{"id":"7","title":"Aggregate Demand, Aggregate Supply and Related Concepts","intro":"Aggregate demand is the sum total of demand for all goods and services during an accounting year, measured as planned expenditure.","lessons":[{"title":"Aggregate demand and aggregate supply","body":"Aggregate demand is the sum total of demand for all goods and services during an accounting year, measured as planned expenditure. In a two-sector economy AD = C + I; a closed economy with government adds G; an open economy adds X − M. Aggregate supply refers to production planned by producers. In the textbook’s short-run model with constant prices and spare capacity, AS is identified with income/output and represented by the 45° line.","formula":"AD = C + I + G + (X − M)\nAS = Y = C + S","source":"IMG_0562","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"7.1 CONCEPT OF AD · 7.2 COMPONENTS OF AD · 7.3 CONCEPT OF AS"},{"title":"Consumption function: autonomous and induced consumption","body":"Consumption function is the functional relationship between consumption and income. Autonomous consumption is consumption when income is zero; induced consumption is the part that varies with income. In C = C̄ + bY, the vertical intercept is C̄ and the slope b is MPC. Consumption rises with income, but normally by less than the rise in income. A change in income moves along a given function; a change in autonomous consumption shifts it.","formula":"C = C̄ + bY\nMPC = ΔC ÷ ΔY\nInduced consumption = bY","source":"IMG_0564","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"7.4 CONSUMPTION FUNCTION"},{"title":"APC and MPC: totals versus increments","body":"Average propensity to consume is total consumption divided by total income; marginal propensity to consume is additional consumption divided by additional income. APC may exceed one when consumption exceeds income. MPC is the slope of the consumption curve; APC is not. With positive autonomous consumption and constant MPC below one, APC falls as income rises. At zero income APC is undefined.","formula":"APC = C ÷ Y\nMPC = ΔC ÷ ΔY","source":"IMG_0566","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"APC vs. MPC"},{"title":"Saving function and the break-even point","body":"Saving is income not consumed. Derive S by subtracting the entire consumption function from Y. The saving function has a negative intercept equal to autonomous consumption and a slope equal to MPS. At break-even income C = Y and S = 0: APC is one and APS is zero. Below it saving is negative; above it saving is positive. Break-even is not necessarily equilibrium, because equilibrium also depends on investment.","formula":"S = Y − C = −C̄ + (1 − b)Y\nBreak-even income = C̄ ÷ (1 − b)","source":"IMG_0567","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"SAVING FUNCTION · Break-even Point"},{"title":"APS, MPS and the two identities","body":"Average propensity to save is S/Y; marginal propensity to save is ΔS/ΔY. APS can be negative when households dissave. MPS is the slope of the saving function. Since income is split between consumption and saving, APC + APS = 1 and MPC + MPS = 1. Do not confuse ΔS (change in saving) with AS (aggregate supply): similar letters represent different concepts.","formula":"APS = S ÷ Y; MPS = ΔS ÷ ΔY\nAPC + APS = 1; MPC + MPS = 1","source":"IMG_0568","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"APS vs. MPS · Relationship between Propensity to Consume and Propensity to Save"},{"title":"Investment and deriving one function from the other","body":"In the simple model autonomous investment is independent of income, so its curve is horizontal. Induced investment responds to changes in income or activity. The expected profitability of investment and rate of interest influence investment decisions. To obtain C from S, use C = Y − S; to obtain S from C, use S = Y − C. The two slopes add to one and their vertical intercepts have equal magnitudes with opposite signs.","formula":"","source":"IMG_0572","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"INVESTMENT · ABILITY ZONE: Derivation of C-function from S-function"},{"title":"Ranges of APC, MPC, APS and MPS","textbookHeading":"Comparative Table of APC, MPC, APS and MPS · Critical Questions","body":"APC can exceed one when C exceeds Y; it equals one at break-even and is below one when saving is positive. APS is negative when saving is negative, zero at break-even and positive when saving is positive; with positive consumption it remains below one. MPC and MPS range from zero to one in the textbook model and add to one. At the endpoints, MPC = 1 implies MPS = 0 and all additional income is consumed; MPC = 0 implies MPS = 1 and all additional income is saved. Do not infer a marginal ratio from a single total-income observation.","source":"IMG_0571","kind":"Exam points","formula":""}],"pages":[{"id":"IMG_0561","image":"pages/IMG_0561.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\n7.1 CONCEPT OF AD (AGGREGATE DEMAND)\n7.1.1 Meaning and Measurement of AD\n•AD is defined as the sum total of expenditure that the people plan to incur on the purchase\n• AD is measured with reference to (aggregate income. Since there is a positive relationship\ncorresponding to their different levels of income.\nof goods and services produced in the economy (during the period of an accounting year)\nbetween AD and Y (higher theY, higher the AD), AD curve tends to slope upward.\n[Note: AD can also be measured with respect to the general price level. In such a case, AD curve will slope\ndownward: higher the general price level, lower the AD. However, at the +2 level, we study AD on\nwith respect to income.]\n•AD is measured not as the sum total of physical quantities of goods demanded in the economy.\nIn fact, it is not possible to do so. AD is always measured in terms of total expenditure on the\ngoods and services.\nAD is always expressed as what people wish to spend or what people plan to spend at different levels\nof income. Never interpret AD as what people actually spend on the purchase of goods and services\nin the economy.\nDID YOU KNOW\n• AD is never zero even when Y (income) = 0.\nReason: Some expenditure is always essential for survival. This is incurred even when income is\nzero. This is done either by using the past saving or by borrowing.\n7.1.2 Behaviour of AD with reference to AD Schedule\n• Since AD is measured in terms of aggregate expenditure (on the purchase of goods and services)\nin the economy, behaviour of AD is studied in terms of the behaviour of aggregate expenditure at\ndifferent levels of Y (income).\n• Behaviour of AD means how aggregate expenditure (AE) responds to different levels of Y in the\neconomy. This is expressed by way of a schedule, known as AD schedule or AE schedule.\n• AD schedule is a table showing AD (or aggregate expenditure) corresponding to different levels\nof income (Y) in the economy. Table 7.1 is an example of this schedule.\nTable 7.1: AD Schedule showing relationship between AD and Y\n(Income)\nObservations:\n(= AE or Planned Expenditure)\n- There is always some minimum level of expenditure in\nthe economy even when Y = 0. Thus, AD = 30 when Y =0.\nThe reason is simple: you must incur some expenditure to\nbuy goods for your survival, even when your income (y) is\nabsolutely zero. You may do it through borrowing, called\nnegative saving.\n- AD increases as Y increases, Thus, AD is positively\nrelated to Y.\n100\n120\n55\n- After a certain level of Y is reached, AD lags behind y.\nThus, when Y = 60,\nAD = 45. Likewise, when Y = 80,\nAD = 50. This happens because, at higher levels of y\npeople start saving a part of their income.\n298\n1.1.3 Diagrammatic Presentation Of AD Schedule\nAggregate Demand, Aggregate Supply and Related Concepts\n• Fig. 7.1 shows AD curve. AD curve is a diagrammatic presentation of AD schedule, showing\nAD corresponding to different levels of income (Y) in the economy.\n• It tends to slope upward, showing positive relationship between AD and Y\nObservations:\nregate Expenarture\n• 45° line' indicates the level of Y, as shown on the\nX-axis. It is a reference line which shows all\npossible points of equilibrium where planned\nC + I (= AD or AE)\nexpenditure equals income.\n• AD = Y at point E, when Y = 40, and that is the\nactual equilibrium point of the economy.\n• Prior to it, AD > Y. This is because of the impact of\nminimum level of AD.\n• Beyond it, AD < Y. This is because at higher levels\nof Y, people start saving a part of their income.\n210+\n45°\n• Minimum level of AD = 30.\n100 120\nThis indicates expenditure independent of the level\nof income in the economy.\nY (Income)/GDP\nFig. 7.1: AD in relation to Y (income)\n• AD starts from 30 on the Y-axis. It suggests minimum level of expenditure, or expenditure which\nis independent of the level of Y in the economy.\n•Eis the point where AD = Y. Prior to it, AD > Y. This is because of the impact of minimum level of AD.\nBeyond point E, AD < Y. This is because at higher levels of income, people tend to save a part of\ntheir income. Generally, AD increases as Y increases.\nCRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS\nObservations related to AD Curve\n• AD=C+ I.\n• The first component of AD: Consumption (C) has an upward slope.\n• Thesecond componentofAD: Investment (I) is assumedto be independent ofthelevel ofincome. Hence,\na horizontal straight line.\n• AD shoots from +ve Y-axis owing to autonomous consumption (i.e., consumption at zero level of\nincome and autonomous investment.\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\nQ. 1. What is aggregate demand? How it is different from market demand?\nAns. Aggregate demand refers to demand for all goods and services in the economy. It is measured\nin terms of total expenditure on goods and services produced in the economy during an\naccounting year.\nWhereas, market demand is the sum total of demand for one commodity by all the buyers\nthe market.\nMarket demand is a micro concept while aggregate demand is a macro concept.\nQ. 2. Do you think higher the level of Y, higher should be the level of autonomous consumption i\nC-function?\nAns. This is not correct because autonomous consumption is not related to the level of Y.\n45° line - a straiaht line where the value of every point on the line is the same for both the horizontal and vertical axes\n299","file":"macro 7/IMG_0561.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0562","image":"pages/IMG_0562.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\n7.2 COMPONENTS OF AD (AGGREGATE DEMAND)\nclosed economy, and (7.2.2) open economy, as under:\nThe various components of AD (or aggregate expenditure) are presented with reference to (7.2.1)\nComponents of Aggregate Demand (AD)\nHousehold\nConsumption\nExpenditure\nProducers\nInvestment\nExpenditure\nGovernment Expenditure\n(including both\nconsumption as well as\ninvestment expenditure\nNet Exports\n(X - M)\n(Components of AD in a two sector closed economy)\n(1 + 2)\n(Components of AD in a three sector closed economy)\n(1+2+3)\n(Components of AD in a four sector open economy)\n(1+2+3+4)\n7.2.1 Components of AD in a Closed Economy\n• A closed economy may be: (a) two sector closed economy, or (b) three sector closed economy.\n(a) A two sector closed economy includes: household sector, and producer sector.\n> Specifically, there is no government sector in this economy. Households purchase goods\nfor purpose of consumption. Accordingly, their expenditure is called consumption\n> Producers, on the other hand, purchase goods for purpose of investment. Accordingly,\ntheir expenditure is called investment expenditure (I).\nThus, in a two sector closed economy, AD has two components as these:\nAD = C + I\n(b) A threesector closed economy includes: household sector, producer sector, and government\nsector.\n> The government makes 'collective consumption expenditure. Collective consumption\nexpenditure refers to public consumption expenditure or consumption expenditure on\nbehalf of the society as a whole.\nExample:\nGovernment expenditure on the purchase of food and clothes for the defence\nforces. Also, the government makes investment expenditure, such as expenditur\non roads and bridges.\n* The total government expenditure (including government consumption expenditure and\ngovernment investment expenditure) is indicated by G.\nIn a three sector closed economy, there are three components of AD, as these\nAD = C+ I + G\n300\n1.2.2 Components of AD in an Open Economy\nAggregate Demand, Aggregate Supply and Related Concepts\nsector, and (iv) rest of the world sector.\nAn open economy includes tour sectors: (i) household sector, (il) producer sector, (iii) government\n• Rest of the world sector generates demand for our goods and services in terms of our exports.\nThus, exports add to Ab in the domestic economy, out, we also make imports trom rest of the\nworld. Imports (M) are opposite to exports (X).\n• While exports increase AD in the domestic economy, imports decrease it. Accordingly, we find\nthe value of net exports (X - M). It is this value which contributes to AD in the domestic economy\nAccordingly, we have four components of AD in an open economy, as these:\nAD=C+I+G+ (X- M)\n• In a two sector closed economy model, AD is derived by adding Consumption\nInvestment (I, i.e., AD = C + I.\nTable 7.2: Schedule showing Calculation of AD by adding C and I\nY (Income)\nC (Consumption)\nI (Investment)\nAD (= C+1)\nand\n50\n75\n100\n200\n100\n200\n300\n200\n100\n100\nGiven below are some numerical illustrations related to the calculation of AD.\nL TACKLING CALCULATION OF AD\n(lustration 1. Given:\nC = 200, I = 50. Find AD.\nAD = 200 + 50\n= 250.\nlustration 2. Given:\nAD = 350, C = 200. Find I.\nAD = C + I\n350 = 200 + I\nI = 350 - 200\n= 150.\n7.3 CONCEPT OF AS (AGGREGATE SUPPLY)\n1.3.1 Concept of AS\n• AS (aggregate supply) refers to aggregate production as planned by the producers during an\naccounting year. It implies the flow of goods and services in the economy during an accounting\n301","file":"macro 7/IMG_0562.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0563","image":"pages/IMG_0563.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\nProduction of goods and services implies value addition', and value addition implies income\ngeneration, these are identical to each other. AS refers to flow of goods and services as planned\nby the producers during an accounting year. AS and Y, therefore, may be treated as identical to\n• It needs emphasis that in the context of macroeconomic equilibrium, As reters only to planned\nproduction' or 'desired production' during an accounting year. It does not refer to actual output.\nDesired production is what the producers desire to produce or plan to produce during an\naccounting year.\n7.3.2 Behaviour of AS with Reference to AS Schedule\nAS schedule is a table showing the behaviour or As, corresponding to alterent levels of Y\nTable 7.3 is an example.\nTable 7.3: AS Schedule showing relationship between AS and y\n(Income)\n(Aggregate Supply)\n1.3.3 Diagrammatic Presentation of AS Schedule\nAggregate Demand, Aggregate Supply and Related Concepts\n• Fig. 7,2 shows AS curve. AS curve is a diagrammatic presentation of AS schedule.\n• It shows that AS and Y are equal to each other. AS is indicated by a 45° line from the origin.\nY = AS\nObservations:\n• AS line happens to be a 45*\nline.\n• This is because of the fact that\nAS on the Y-axis is identical\nwith Y on the X-axis.\nObservation:\nThis table shows perfect identity\nbetween AS and Y.\n20 40 60\n80 100 120\nY (Income)/GDP\nFig. 7.2: AS curve\n• It must be noted that this behaviour of AS (as 45° line from the origin) is true only with respect to\nsuch economies where there is excess capacity, and AS can proportionately rise whenever there\nis any rise in AD.\n• Aggregate supply is derived by adding Consumption (C) and Saving (S), i.e..,\nAS = C + S\n100\nImplications of the Perfect Identity between AS and Y\n• Identity between Y and AS shows that the producers in the economy are ready to supply (or sell)\nall that they wish to produce.\nWhy do the producers wish to sell/supply all that they wish to produce?\n• This is because, there is excess capacity in the economy. Accordingly, whenever there is a rise in\ndemand, the producers respond to it by planning an equal rise in supply (so that their excess capacity\n[Note: A proportionate increase in AS (corresponding to an increase in AD) leads to a constant price level\nin the economy. So that price has no role to play as a determinant of AS.]\nWhy should we presume that there is excess capacity in the economy?\n• This, in fact, is the basic assumption of macroeconomic model as propounded by Keynes\n• Keynes made this basic assumption because he was dealing with the economies facing sever\nunemployment in the wake of Great Depression of 1930's. Such economies must have excess\ncapacity or unutilised capacity (because of the lack of demand).\nThus, we can identify AS with Y.\n302\n! CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS\nObservations related to AS Curve\n• AS shoots from the origin and forms a 45° angle. This is on the assumption that there is excess capacity\nin the economy.\n• Because of excess capacity in the economy, AS rises proportionately to AD.\nY (Income)\nTable 7.4: Schedule showing Calculation of AS by adding C and S\nAS (= C+S)\nS (Savings)\nC (Consumption)\n200\n50\n75\n100\n150\n300\n200\n303","file":"macro 7/IMG_0563.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0564","image":"pages/IMG_0564.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\nGiven below are some numerical illustrations related to the calculation of AS.\n27 TACKLING CALCULATION OF AS\nIllustration 3. Given:\nC= 200, 5 = -25. Find AS.\nAS = 200 + (-25)\n= 200 - 25 = 175.\nIllustration 4. Given:\nAS = 500, C = 275. Find S.\nAS = C+ S\n500 = 275 + S\nS = 500 - 275 = 225.\n7.4 CONSUMPTION FUNCTION\n7.4.1 Concept of Consumption Function\n• Household consumption expenditure (C) is an important component of aggregate demand in\nthe economy. It is directly related to the level of income (Y). Higher Y leads to higher C, and\nvice versa.\n•The functional relationship (or the algebraic relationship) between CandY is called Consumption\nFunction. It reveals the behaviour of household consumption expenditure with respect to the\nlevel of income in the economy.\n• Studying the behaviour of C with respect to Y, the economists have identified the following points\nas of central significance:\n• There is always some minimum level of C, even when Y = 0. This is called autonomous\nconsumption. This leads to negative saving (-S).\n• Consumption is positively related to income: rise in Y causes a rise in C, and vice versa.\n• The entire increase in Y during a particular period is not converted into C. A part of it is saved\nas well. So that the rate at which C increases often lags behind the rate at which Y increases.\n• Accounting for these observations, the behaviour of C with respect to Y is specified as in\n150\nTable 7.5: Consumption Function: A Tabular Presentation\nObservations:\nThis table offers a simple tabular presentation\nof consumption function. It reveals that:\n• 20 is the minimum level of consumption.\nThis is the level of C even when Y =0.\n60\n• C rises in response to a rise in Y. So that C is\npositively related to Y.\n100\n• The rate at which C increases is lower than\nthe rate at which Y increases. Thus, while\nY increases each time by 50, C increases by\n140\n40 only. (The difference between Y and C\nindicates saving out of income.)\n304\nDiagrammatic Presentation of Consumption Function\nAggregate Demand, Aggregate Supply and Related Concepts\nDiagrammatically, when the data related to consumption and income are plotted on a graph\npaper, we get consumption function as in Fig. 7.3.\nBreak-even Point\nDATA-SET\n50\n100\n150\n100\n140\nObservations:\n• C-line starts from 20 indicating that\nC = 20, when Y = 0.\n(This is the minimum level of consumption.)\n• Initially C-line is above the Y-line. But\neventually it is below the Y-line. It indicates\nthat the rate at which C increases is lower\nthan the rate at which Y increases.\n• C-line crosses Y-line at point B. It is called\n\"break-even point?. Here, total consumption\nis equal to total income.\n145°\nY (Income)/GDP\nFig. 7.3: Consumption Function: A Diagrammatic Presentation\n• In Fig. 7.3, consumption is measured on Y-axis, and income on X-axis. A line making an angle of\n45° and shooting from the origin is drawn as a line of reference. It indicates the equality between\nconsumption and income.\n• C-line represents consumption function, indicating the behaviour of C with respect to Y. Now, note\nthe following observations carefully:\n• C-line starts from 20 (on Y-axis). It is the minimum level of consumption (value of C when Y = 0).\n• C-line is positively sloped, moving upward from left to right. It indicates that C is positively\nrelated to Y: higher Y leads to higher C.\n• Though initially C-line is above the Y-line (45° line), eventually it remains below the Y-line.\nImplying that the rate at which C increases is lower than the rate at which Y increases owing to\nKeynes' Psychological Law of Consumption.\n• CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS\nObservations related to Consumption Curve\n• Consumption curve shoots from +ve Y-axis owing to autonomous consumption or minimum level o\nconsumption.\n• The slope of consumption curve is positive.\n• The point where C = Y is called break-even point.\nAutonomous Consumption vs. Induced Consumption\nAutonomous consumption is the value of C* when Y =0.\n• Induced consumption is the value of C corresponding to different values of Y, when Y is rising. We are\ninduced to spend more when income is rising.\n2. Break-even point - the point where total consumption is equal to the total income or the point where tota\nsaving is equal to zero.\n305","file":"macro 7/IMG_0564.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0565","image":"pages/IMG_0565.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\n• Keynes' Psychological Law of Consumption suggests that as income Increases, consumption also\nKeynes' Psychological Law of Consumption\nincreases, but at a diminishing rate. In other words, when peoples income rise, they spend a portion of\nthe extra income, but the increase in consumption is less than the increase in income. This is because\npeople tend to save more as their income grows, leading to a smaller proportion of income being used\nfor consumption.\n7.4.2 Slope of C-line (Consumption Function):\nMarginal Propensity to Consume\n• Slope of C-line (indicating consumption function) needs some elaboration. Slope of C-line refers\nto the rate at which increases in response to a given increase n. It cates the promo\nof additional income that goes to consumption.\n• This is measured as the ratio between 4c (increase in C) and AY (increase in Y). ThIS IS known as\nmarginal propensity to consume (MPC).\n• Fig. 7.4 shows the estimation of MPC as the slope of C-line.\nC (Consumption)\nДС = 40\nДY = 50\nObservation:\nSlope of C-line\n- 30 = 0.8\nY (Income)/GDP\nFig. 7.4: Slope of C-line and Estimation of MPC\n• The figure shows that when:\nY increases from 100 to 150,\nC increases from 100 to 140.\nSo that, AC = 40 and AY = 50.\nMPC (the slope of C-line) =\n40 = 0.8\n• It shows that out of every additional rupee of income, 80% is spent as consumption expenditure\nAPC VS. MPC\n• It needs to be noted that APC (Average Propensity to Consume) and MPC (Marginal Propensity to\nincome. The difference is as under:\nConsume) are different concepts, even when both indicate the ratio between consumption ana\n• APC is the ratio between totabpensity tomption and total income, while MPC is the ratio between\nadditional consumption and additional income.\n306\nAggregate Demand, Aggregate Supply and Related Concepts\nAPC = -\nC (Consumption)\nThus:\nY (Income)\nAC (Change in Consumption)\n• In Fig. 7.4, when Y= 150 and C = 140, then:\nAY (Change inIncome)\nAPC = 140 = 0,933\n• In Fig. 7.4 again, when AY = 50 and AC = 40, then:\nMPC =\n50 = 0.8\nBriefly, APC shows consumption per unit of total income, whereas MPC shows consumption per unit\nof additional income.\n(Note: Slope of C-function is indicated by MPC, NOT by APC.]\nSlope of a Straight Line C-function (as in Fig. 7.4) is Constant,\nso that MPC is Constant\n• We know that a straight line has a constant slope. So that a straight line C-function as in Fig. 7.4\nindicates constant slope of C-function. Implying that MPC (as indicated by the slope of C-line or\n(function) must be constant corresponding to all levels of income.\n• Such a C-function is called linear consumption function. It suggests that every time there is a rise\nin Y, a constant proportion of it is converted into C.\n• In Fig. 7.4, consumption function is linear and MPC = 0.8, it suggests that every time there is a rise\nin income, 80% of it is converted into consumption.\n• Table 7.6 represents values of APC and MPC with respect to change in income and consumption\n(Consumption)\nTable 7.6: Understanding APC and MPC\nAC (Change in\nAY (Change in\nConsumption)\nMPC =\nAPC - y\n= 0.5\n75\n= 0.5\n100\n= 1\n100\n= 0.75\n100\n200\n150\n= 0.5\n0.67\n300\n200\n= 0.5\n= 0.625\n400\n250\n= 0.5\n= 0.6\n500\n300\n307","file":"macro 7/IMG_0565.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0566","image":"pages/IMG_0566.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\nCRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS\nObservations related to APC\n• APC > 1, when C> Y.\n• APC = 1, when C= Y.\n• APC < 1, when C < Y.\n• APC tends to fall with increase in income\n(generally people tend to save\nmore with\nincrease in income.\n• APC can never be zero, because C is never zero.\nObservations related to MPC\n• MPC = 4C\n4Y •\n• Value of MPC ranges between 0 and 1.\n• If entire additional income is spent, AC = AY,\nMPC = 1.\n• If entire\nMPC = 0.\nadditional income is saved, AC = 0,\n• MPC is the slope of consumption curve.\n• If MPC is constant, consumption curve is linear.\nGiven below are some numerical illstrations related to the calculation of APC and MPC.\nÝ TACKLING APC AND MPC\nIllustration 5. Given: Y= 300, C= 180, Find APC.\n= 0.6.\nIllustration 6.\nGiven: 4Y = 100, 4C = 30. Find MPC.\nMPC =\n= 0.3.\n100\nIllustration 7. Find APC and MPC from the following data:\nIncome (Y)\nConsumption (C)\n200\n110\n300\n200\nSol.\nAPC\n18\n= 0.6\nMPC\n110\n= 0.55\n200\n110 - 60\n200 - 100\n50\n= 0.5\n100\n200\n= 0.67\n300\n200 - 110\n300 - 200\n100\nIllustration 8.\nGiven: MPC = 0.5, AY = 500. Find AC.\nMPC =\n0.5 =\nAC = 0.5 × 500 = 250.\nIllustration 9. Given: APC = 0.95, 0.94 at income levels of 300 and 350 respectively. Find the value of MP\nAPC\nC = APC × Y\n(as APC = y)\n0.95 × 300 = 285\nMPC * AV\n300\n0.95\n350\n0.94\n0,94 × 350 = 329\n44 = 0.88\n329 - 285 - 50\n350 - 300\n1.4.3 Algebraic Presentation of C-function\nAggregate Demand, Aggregate Supply and Related Concepts\nAlgebraically, consumption function is expressed as in the following equation:\nC= C + bY\n• ¿ in this equation refers to the minimum level of consumption. It is the value of consumption\n• The parameter 'b' refers to the rate at which C increases in response to an increase in V. It is =\nIt is the slope of C-function, also called 'marginal propensity to consume\".\n• The parameter 'Y' refers to income.\n• Fig. 7.4 shows that C= 20 and MPC = 0.8. Using these values, we can write our consumption\nfunction equation as under:\nC = 20 + 0.8Y\n• This equation enables us to estimate the values of C corresponding to different values of Y. Using\nvalues of Y as 50, 100 and 150 respectively (as in Table 7.5), we can estimate the corresponding\nvalues of C, as under:\nC = 20 + 0.8Y (as given)\nC when Y = 0,\n= 20 + 0.8 (0) = 20\nC when Y = 50,\n= 20 + 0.8 (50) = 20 + 40 = 60\nC when Y = 100,\n= 20 + 0.8 (100) = 20 + 80 = 100\nC when Y = 150,\n= 20 + 0.8 (150) = 20 + 120 = 140\n• We find that the estimated values of consumption are exactly the same as in Table 7.5. Thus, we\ncan find the values of Conce we know the values of C, b and Y.\nGiven below are some numerical illustrations related to the calculation of consumption function.\n2 TACKLING CONSUMPTION FUNCTION\nlustration 10. Find C, when C = 200, MPC = 0.5 and Y = 1,000.\nC = C + MPC (Y)\n= 200 + 0.5 (1,000)\n= 200 + 500 = 700.\nlustration 11. Find C, if MPC = 0.6, Y = 500 and C = 50.\nC= C + MPC (Y)\n= 50 + 0.6 (500)\n= 50 + 300 = 350.\nIllustration 12. Given that: C = 75 + 0.75Y, calculate the level of consumption if income level is 500.\nC = 75 + 0.75Y\n= 75 + 0.75 (500)\n= 75 + 375 = 450.\n#lustration 13. Calculate the income level, if consumption = 2,000 and the consumption function, C = 750 + 0.5Y.\nC= C + MPC (Y)\n2,000 = 750 + 0.5Y\n2,000 - 750 = 0.5Y\n1,250 = 0.5Y\n1,250\nY=\n0.5\n= 2,500.\n309\n308","file":"macro 7/IMG_0566.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0567","image":"pages/IMG_0567.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\nAns. Yes. Consumption function would be linear in case MPC is constant.\nQ. 3. Would consumption function be linear in case MPC is constant? Give reason in support of your\nA straight line C-line means that the consumption function is linear.\nQ. 5. Increase in MPC implies increase in the slope of C-function. Comment.\nAns. The given statement is correct. Because MPC shows the rate at which consumption increases in\nresponse to increase in income. Or, MPC is the slope of C-function.\n7.5 SAVING FUNCTION\n7.5.1 Concept of Saving Function\n• Income is either consumed or saved, so that Y = C + S. We have already discussed how C behaves\nwith respect to Y (consumption function). We now discuss how S behaves with respect to (saving\nfunction). The functional relationship between S and Y is called saving function.\n• Once C-function is specified, -function can easily be derived from it. Because C and S are the only\ntwo components of income and because both C and S are determined by Y.\nLet us get back to the tabular presentation of C-function and see how S-function can be derived from it,\nC-function (as in Table 7.5)\ng\n100\n20\n60\n100\n140\nTable 7.7: S-function (derived from C-function as stated above)\nY - C\n0-20\n-10\n50 - 60\n100 - 100\n+10\n150-140\n• The S-function as specified in Table 7.7 reveals that:\n• S= -20 when Y = 0. This is because C = 20 when Y = 0. We know that C + S = Y. So that, if C=20\nand Y = 0, then S must be = -20.\n• S increases as Y increases. Implying that S is positively related to Y.\n• S remains lower than Y. It is never greater than Y. Because, S is only a part of Y.\n310\nDiagrammatic Presentation of saving Function\nAggregate Demand, Aggregate Supply and Related Concepts\nFig. 7.5 shows diagrammatic presentation of S-function. It is drawn using the data-set of Table 7.7.\nDATA-SET\nObservations:\n• S-line starts from - 20. This is because\nwhen Y= 0 and C= 20, then S must be\nequal to - 20.\n-10\n100\n• S-line crosses X-axis when Y = 100. It is\n+10\nhere that S= 0, because Y = C= 100.\nIt is called 'break-even' point. Because\n'S' is no longer negative. Implying that\nborrowing is not needed to meet the\nminimum C.\n• S-line is positively sloped, indicating\n-201\nY (Income)/GDP\nthat S is positively related to Y.\nHigher Y leads to higher S.\nFig. 7.5: Saving Function\n• In Fig. 7.5, saving is measured on Y-axis, and income on X-axis. S-line represents saving function,\nindicating the behaviour of S with respect to Y. It offers the following observations:\n• S-line starts from - 20 (on Y-axis). It indicates the value of S when Y = 0.\n[Note that S = -20, because C = 20 when Y = 0].\n• S-line is positively sloped, moving upward from left to right. It indicates that S is positively\nrelated to Y: higher Y causes higher S.\n• S-line crosses the X-axis when Y = 100. It indicates that S = 0 when Y = 100. (Here, consumption\nmust be equal to income.) So that, S = 0 when Y = 100, S is negative when Y < 100, S is positive\n• CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS\nObservations related to Saving Curve\n• Saving curve shoots from -ve Y-axis. Because S is negative when C> Y.\n• The slope of saving curve is positive (ST as Y 1).\n• S= 0 at break-even point, i.e., when C = Y, S = 0.\n• After break-even point, saving is positive.\nAutonomous Saving vs. Induced Saving\n• Autonomous saving is independent of the level of income.\nAutonomous saving is the value of 'S' when Y = 0. Thus, if autonomous C = 20, then autonomous S=-20\n• Induced saving is the value of corresponding to different values of Y, when Y is rising. We are induced\nto save more when income is rising.\n7.5.2 Slope of S-line (Saving Function): Marginal Propensity to Save\n• Slope of S-line (indicating saving function) refers to the rate at which S increases in response to\na given increase in Y.\n311","file":"macro 7/IMG_0567.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0568","image":"pages/IMG_0568.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\n• It indicates the proportion of add seiny, income that goes to saving. It is measured as the ratio\nto save (MPS).\nFig. 7.6 shows the estimation of MPS as the slope of S-line or S-function.\nbetween 4S (additional saving) and 4f (additional income). This is called marginal propensity\nObservation:\nSlope of S-line\n= 4S\n4y =\n= 0.2\nAS =10\n100ДY= 50150\nY (Income)/GDP\nFig. 7.6: Slope of S-line or Estimation of MPS\n• Fig. 7.6 show that when:\nY increases from 100 to 150,\nS increases from 0 to 10.\nSo that, AS = 10 and AY = 50.\nMPS (the slope of S-line) =\n• It shows that out of every additional rupee of income, 20% is saved. [This exactly matches\nwith our C-function which shows that out of every additional rupee of income, 80% is spent as\nconsumption expenditure.]\nAPS vs. MPS\n• APS (Average Propensity to Save) and MPS (Marginal Propensity to Save) are different concepts,\neven when both indicate the ratio between saving and income.\n• APS is the ratio between total saving and total income, while MPS is the ratio between addition\nsaving and additional income.\nThus:\nS (Saving)\nAPS= Y (Income)\nMPS =\nAS (Change in Saving)\n• In Fig. 7.6, when Y = 150 and S = 10, then:\nAY (Change in Income)\nAPS =\n= 0.067\n• In Fig. 7.6 again, when AS = 10 and AY = 50, then:\nMPS =\n= 0.2\n312\nAggregate Demand, Aggregate Supply and Related Concepts\nBriefly, APS shows saving per unit of total income, whereas MPS shows saving per unit of additional\n/Note: Slope of S-function is indicated by MPS, NOT by APS.]\nso that MPS is Constant\nslope of a Straight Line S-function (as in Fig. (.o) Is Constant,\n. A straight line has a constant slope. So, a straight line S-function (as in Fig. 7.6) indicates that it\nhas a constant stope. Imply nates that it MPS (as indicated by the slope of S-line or S-function) must be\nconstant corresponding to all levels of income.\nSuch an S-function is called linear saving unction. It suggests that every time there is a rise in Y,\na constant proportion of it is converted into s.\n• in hg./.o, saving function Is inear and MPS = 0.4, It suggests that every time there is a rise in\nincome, 20% of it is saved.\nTable 7.8: Understanding APS and MPS\n(Income)\n50\n-50\n75\n- 25=-0.5\n- = 0.5\n100\n100\n200\n150\n50\n100\n= 0.25\n25 - 0.5\n-= 0.5\n300\n200\n100\n= 0.33\n300\n- = 0.5\n250\n150\n- = 0.375\n500\n300\n100\n200\n= 0.4\n= 0.5\n200\n! CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS\nObservations related to APS\n• APS canneverbe 1 or> 1 assavingscanneverbezY.\n(There is always some minimum level of C.)\n• APS = 0 when S = 0.\n• APS is negative when S is negative or C> Y.\n• APS tends to rise as the level of income rises in\nthe economy.\nObservations related to MPS\n• MPS = 45\n• Value of MPS ranges between 0 and 1.\n• MPS will be 0 if entire additional income is\nconsumed, i.e., 4S = 0.\n• MPS will be 1 if entire additional income is saved,\ni.e., AS = 4Y.\n• MPS is the slope of saving curve.\n• If MPS is constant, saving curve is linear.\n313","file":"macro 7/IMG_0568.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0569","image":"pages/IMG_0569.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\nGiven below are some numerical illustrations related to the APS and MPS.\nI TACKLING APS AND MPS\nIllustration 14. Given: Y = 400, 5 = 150. Find APS.\nAPS = 400\n150 = 0.375.\nIllustration 15. Given: ДY = 200, 45 = 150. Find MPS.\nMPS = 200\n150 = 0.75-\nIllustration 16. Find APS and MPS from the following data:\nIncome (Y)\n200\n100\nSaving (S)\n300\nSol.\n18\n= 0.5\nAPS\nMPS\n100\n= 0.5\n200\n100 - 50\n50\n200 - 100 100=0.5\n300 = 0.6\n180 - 100\n300 - 200 =-\n100 - 0.8\nlustration 17. Given: APS = 0.25, 0.45 at income levels of 500 and 700 respectively. Find the value of MPS.\nSol.\nAPS\nS= APS × Y (as APS = 5)\nMPS = A5)\n0.25 × 500 = 125\n500\n700\n0.25\n0.45\n0.45 × 700 = 315\n315 - 125\n700 - 500\n190\n• = 0.95\n200\n7.5.3 Algebraic Presentation of S-function\nAlgebraically, S-function is specified as under:\nS=-C + (1 - b)Y\n• -E indicates the value of 5 when Y = 0. [C indicates the value of C when Y = 0. Accordingy,\n-E indicates the value of 5 when Y = 0.] So that, when C = 20, -č = - 20 (which is the value of s\nwhen Y = 0).\n• 1 - b = MPS. It indicates the rate at which 's' changes in response to a change in Y: Why is\nMPS = 1 - b (where b = MPC)? This is because MPC + MPS = 1 see the next section for details).\n• The parameter Y indicates income.\n• This unction on then the quation for function can be derived from Crudin, a\nC = 20 + 0.8Y\n(this is C-function)\n5 = - 20 + (1 - 0.8)Y (this is S-function as derived from C-function)\nS= -20 + 0.2Y\nThis is the value of S when Y = 0\nand corresponds to the value\nof C (= 20) when Y = 0\nThis is the value of MPS when\nthe value of MPC = 0.8\n314\nAggregate Demand, Aggregate Supply and Related Concepts\n7.5.4 Derivation ofS-functionfrom C-function: DiagrammaticIllustration\nFig. 7.7 shows derivation of S-function from C-function.\n(A) C-function\nDATA-SET\nC (Consumpton)\nC = Y\n[Break-even\nPoint]\nMPC = 0.8\n20 A\n>X\n› Y (Income)/GDP\n(B) S-function\n(Saving)\nDATA-SET\nS (= Y - C)\n-20 (=0 - 20)\n-10 (=50 - 60)\n0 (= 100- 100)\n+10 (= 150 - 140)\nObservaions:\n• S-function starts from -20\nbecause C-function starts\nfrom +20. It indicates that\nwhen Y = 0 and C = 20, then\nS must be equal to -20.\n• S = 0 when Y = 100. This is\nbecause C= Y when Y = 100.\n• S-function is a linear\nfunction. Because S-line is a\nstraight line, moving upward\nfrom left to right.\nS= 0\n[Break-even Point] s\nMPS = 1 - MPC\n= 1 - 0.8 = 0.2\nA = Autonomous consumption\nA' = Dissavings, i.e., savings at\nzero level of income\n-20€\nY (Income)/GDP\nFig. 7.7: Derivation of S-function from C-function\nSAVLR 7.1\nDERIVATION OF THE SAVING CURVE FROM THE CONSUMPTION CURVE\nWe need to focus on three points:\n(i) Sine starts from the point which indicates the value of 5. And the value of 5 is equal to the\nvalue of C, but with a negative sign. Thus in Fig. 7.7(B), S-line starts from - 20 because C-line\nstarts from +20.\n(ii) S-line crosses the X-axis when C = Y, so that S = 0. Thus in Fig. 7.7(B), S-line crosses X-axis when\nC= Y = 100, and therefore, S= 0.\n(i) The slope of S-line is equal to '1 - slope of C-line'. Thus in Fig. 7.7(A) and Fig. 7.7(B), while the\nSlope of C-line = 0.8 that of S-line = 1 - 0.8 = 0.2.\n[Note that: MPS = 1 - MPC.]","file":"macro 7/IMG_0569.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0570","image":"pages/IMG_0570.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\nGiven below are some numerical illustrations related to calculation or saving function.\nTACKLING SAVING FUNCTION\nSlope of C-function = MPC\nSlope of S-function = MPS\nMPC + MPS = 1\nWe know,\nslope of S-function = 1 - MPC\n= 1 - 0.6 = 0.4.\nIllustration 19. Find S, when C = 200, MPS = 0.4 and Y = 1,000.\nS = -C + MPS (Y)\nWe know that,\nWe as know ta, saving is negauve; fure extent consumption is posite when 1 - v. so the\nSubstituting the given values, we get:\nS=-200+0.4(1,000)\n= -200 + 400 = 200.\nIllustration 20. Find savings, if Y = 1,000 and saving function = -50 + 0.6Y\nS = -50 + 0.6Y\n= -50 + 0.6 (1,000)\n= -50 + 600 = 550.\nIllustration 21. Find income level, if saving function = -25 + 0.65Y and savings = 100.\nS= -25 + 0.65Y\n100 = -25 + 0.65Y\n100 + 25 = 0.65Y\n125 = 0.65Y\nY=\n125 = 192.3.\n0.65\nIllustration 22. Find savings, if autonomous consumption = 85, MPC = 0.6 and level of income = 1,000.\nS= -C + (1 - MPC)Y\n= -85 + (1 - 0.6) 1,000\n= -85 + 0.4(1,000)\n= -85 + 400 = 315.\n7.6 RELATIONSHIP BETWEEN PROPENSITY TO\nCONSUME AND PROPENSITY TO SAVE\nUsing Equations to Express the Relationship between Propensity to\nConsume and Propensity to Save\naspects, as noted earlier:\nPropensity to consume refers to the ratio between consumption (C) and income (Y). It has two\nAPC =\nAverage propensity\nto consume\nand\nMPC =•\nAY\nMarginal propensity\nto consume\n316\nPropensity to save refers to the ratio between saving (5) and income (Y). Its two aspects are:\nAggregate Demand, Aggregate Supply and Related Concepts\nMPS = 4s\nAverage propensity\nto save\nMarginal propensity\nto save\n• Relationship between propensity to consume and propensity to save is as expressed in the\nfollowing equations:\n(i) APC + APS = 1\n(Because Y is either consumed or saved.)\ni) MPC + MPS = 1 (Because additional Y is either consumed or saved.)\nThese equations are explained (and proved) as under:\n• APC + APS = 1, always. Because if (say) → of total income is spent, then the other half (- of\ntotal income must be saved. Or, if 4 of total income is spent, then - of total income must be\nsaved. Simply because, Y = C + S.\nAlgebraically, it can be proved as under:\nWe know that,\nAPC = f, and APS = 5,\nWe also know that, Y = C+ S\nSo that,\nHence, APC + APS = 1, always.\n• MPC + MPS = 1, always. it are is because, if (say) → of additional income is spent, then the other\nhalf (ź) of additional income must be saved. Or, if 3 of additional income is spent, then 4.\nof additional income must be saved. Simply because, AY = AC + 4S.\nAlgebraically, it can be proved as under:\nWe know that,\nMPC = Av: and MPS - AV\nWe also know that, AC + 4S = AY\nSo that,\nY = 1\nMPC + MPS =\nHence, MPC + MPS = 1, always.\n317","file":"macro 7/IMG_0570.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0571","image":"pages/IMG_0571.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\nTable 7.9: Comparative Table of APC, MPC, APS and MPS\nMPC\nAPS\nBasis of\nDifference\n1. Meaning\nAPC\nIt refers to the\nratio between total\nconsumption and total\nincome.\n2. Formula\n3. Numerical\nExample (via\nschedule)\nCase\n100\n200\n300\n200\nCase V:\n200\nAPC =\n= 0.67\n300\n4. Diagram\nINCOME (R in crore)\n5. Range of\nReason: There is always\nsome minimum level of C\neven when Y = 0\nNo, C is never equal to 0\nYes, when C= y\nYes, when C> Y\nIt refers to the ratio\nbetween change in\nconsumption (additional\nconsumption) and change\nin income (additional\nincome).\nIt refers to the ratio\nbetween total saving\nand total income.\nMPC =\n-50\n50\n50\n100\n100\n50\n50\n100\nCase V:\n(200 - 150)\nMPC =\n(300 - 200)\n= 100 = 0.5\n(t in crore)\nINCOME (T in crore)\nNo.\nReason: AC is always a\n+ve component of AY\nYes, when AS = AY\nAPS =\nAPC\nMPC\n1.5\n50\n50\nCase V:\n0.75\n0.67\n0.5\n0.5\n0.5\n0.5\n100\nAPS =\n= 0.33\n300\nAPS • S- DE,\nSAVING\nin crore\nINCOME (R in crore)\nYes.\nReason: When C > Y\nYes, when C = Y\nYes, when AC = AY\nNo, as AC can never be\ngreater than AY\nNo. Because total S is\nnever = total Y\nNo, as S can never be\ngreater than Y\n[Note: Here, A = Autonomous consumption, and -A = Dissavings]\nMPS\nIt refers to the\nratio between\nchange in saving\n(additional saving)\nand change in\nincome (additional\nincome.\nMPS = AS\nAPS\nMPS\n- 0.5\n0.25\n0.33\n0.5\n0.5\nCase V:\n(100-50)\nMPS= (300 - 200)\n100=0.5\nINCOME (R in crore)\nNo.\nReason: AS is\nalways a tve\ncomponent of AY\nYes, when AC = AY\nYes, when AS = AY\nNo, as AS can\nnever be greater\nthan AY\n318\nAggregate Demand, Aggregate Supply and Related Concepts\nPAVLR 7.2\nRANGE OF VALUES FOR APC, APS, MPC AND MPS\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\nQ.6. How would you specify the saving function, given the consumption function as under?\nAns. S = (1 - b)Y.\n[Note: In the C-function, there is no constant term like . Accordingly, there is no constant term like - in\nthe saving function. Such a C-function (or S-function) indicates that = 0 (or S= 0) when Y = =0.]\na.7. If personal disposable income is 7 1,000 crore and consumption expenditure is ? 750 crore,\nfind out average propensity to save.\nAns. S= Y - C=R 1,000 crore - & 750 crore = & 250 crore\nAPS =\n250\n- 1,000 = 0.25.\nQ. 8. Can APS ever be negative? If yes, give an example.\nAns. Yes. APS can be negative in situations when S is negative (or when C> Y).\nExample: Y = 50, C = 60, S = -10\nAPS =\n= 50\n-10 = -0.2.\nQ. 9. APC and MPC are two parameters. The value of which parameter can be greater than one, and\nwhen?\nAns. Value of APC can be greater than one. It happens when the level of income is low and C> Y.\nValue of MPC cannot be greater than one. MPC is the ratio between additional consumption\nand additional income\n). Since additional consumption (AC) is only a part of additional\nincome (AY), Ay cannot be greater than one.\nQ.10, Can MPS or MPC ever be negative? Give reasons in support of your answer.\nAns. No. Neither MPS nor MPC can ever be negative. Because: MPS is the ratio between additional\nsaving (AS) and additional income (4Y). Likewise, MPC is the ratio between additionall\nconsumption (AC) and additional income (AY). The ratio aY\n- refers to slope of S-function which\nis always positive because of positive relationship between S and Y). Likewise, the ratio\nrefers to slope of C-function which is always positive (because of positive relationship between\nC and Y).\n319","file":"macro 7/IMG_0571.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0572","image":"pages/IMG_0572.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\nABILITY ZONE\nDerivation of C-function (C-curve) from S-function (S-curve):\nDiagrammatic Illustration\n• We know,\nC = C + bY\nS = -C + (1 - b)Y\n• Fig. 7.8 lustrates the derivation of C-function (C-curve) from S-function (S-curve).\n[AJ S-function\nS (Saving)\nY (Income/GDP *X\nBreak-even point\n[B] C-function\nC (Consumption)\nY= C\nC=C+ bY\nObservations:\n• C-function [Fig. 7.8(B)] is derived from\nS-function [Fig. 7.8(A)], taking the\nfollowing steps:\n• Step 1: -C indicates negative saving\nwhen Y = 0. This is equal to (-) OP.\nCorresponding to it, C would be equal\nto OP (consumption when Y = 0).\nThus, P becomes the starting point of\nC-function (when Y = 0).\n• Step 2: When S= 0 [at point L in\nFig. 7.8(A)], C = OP* [at point Q in\nFig. 7.8(B)]. This corresponds to\nincome level OL in both the figures.\nThus, between O to OL level of Y, C> Y.\n• Step 3: Starting from P and passing\nthrough point Q, we stretch the\nC-function as a straight line. The line\nshows constant slope (= b). This\ncorresponds to the constant slope of\nS-function = (1 - b).\n• Note that:\nb + (1 -b) = 1\nY (Income//GDP\nSlope of Slope\nC-line\nof S-line\nFig. 7.8: Derivation of C-function (C-curve) from S-function (S-curve)\n320\nPower Points (Summary of the Chapter)\nAggregate Demand, Aggregate Supply and Related Concests\n, Aggregate Demand (AD) is the sum total of demand for all goods and services in the economy during\nand services in the economy during an accounting year.\nthe period of an accounting year. It is measured in terms of total (planned) expenditure on the good\n• Components:\nAD = C+I+G+ (X-M)\n= Planned expenditure on the domestically produced goods and services during an\naccounting year.\nAggregate Supply (AS) reters to the production of goods and services in the economy as planner\nby the producers during an accounting year.\n• It sidentical with GDP in the economy where: ) prices are constant, and (i) supply responds\nproportionately to increase in demand, owing to excess capacity. Being identical with GDP, His\nindicated by a 45° line.\n, Consumption Function is the functional relationship between consumption and income. tis specified\nas under:\nC= C+ bY\n• Slope of C-line (consumption function) is the rate at which C (consumption) increases in response\nto a given increase in Y (income). It is measured as the ratio between additional consumption and\nadditional income.\nSlope of C-function = MPC = AC\nI Saving Function is the functional relationship between saving and income. It is usually specified as\nunder.\nS=-C + (1 - b)Y\n• Slope of S-line (saving function) is measured as the ratio between additional saving and additio\nincome.\nSlope of S-function = MPS = AS\n* Average Propensity to Consume is the ratio between total consumption and total income\nAPC = Y\n• Average Propensity to Save is the ratio between total saving and total income.\nAPS = S\n• Relationship between Propensity to Consume and Propensity to Save\n• APC + APS = 1\nMPC + MPS = 1\nalways","file":"macro 7/IMG_0572.HEIC"}],"boxes":[{"title":"DID YOU KNOW","page":"IMG_0561","text":"• AD is never zero even when Y (income) = 0.\nReason: Some expenditure is always essential for survival. This is incurred even when income is\nzero. This is done either by using the past saving or by borrowing.\n7.1.2 Behaviour of AD with reference to AD Schedule\n• Since AD is measured in terms of aggregate expenditure (on the purchase of goods and services)\nin the economy, behaviour of AD is studied in terms of the behaviour of aggregate expenditure at\ndifferent levels of Y (income).\n• Behaviour of AD means how aggregate expenditure (AE) responds to different levels of Y in the\neconomy. This is expressed by way of a schedule, known as AD schedule or AE schedule.\n• AD schedule is a table showing AD (or aggregate expenditure) corresponding to different levels\nof income (Y) in the economy. Table 7.1 is an example of this schedule.\nTable 7.1: AD Schedule showing relationship between AD and Y\n(Income)\nObservations:\n(= AE or Planned Expenditure)\n- There is always some minimum level of expenditure in\nthe economy even when Y = 0. Thus, AD = 30 when Y =0.\nThe reason is simple: you must incur some expenditure to\nbuy goods for your survival, even when your income (y) is\nabsolutely zero. You may do it through borrowing, called\nnegative saving.\n- AD increases as Y increases, Thus, AD is positively\nrelated to Y.\n100\n120\n55\n- After a certain level of Y is reached, AD lags behind y.\nThus, when Y = 60,\nAD = 45. Likewise, when Y = 80,\nAD = 50. This happens because, at higher levels of y\npeople start saving a part of their income.\n298\n1.1.3 Diagrammatic Presentation Of AD Schedule\nAggregate Demand, Aggregate Supply and Related Concepts\n• Fig. 7.1 shows AD curve. AD curve is a diagrammatic presentation of AD schedule, showing\nAD corresponding to different levels of income (Y) in the economy.\n• It tends to slope upward, showing positive relationship between AD and Y\nObservations:\nregate Expenarture\n• 45° line' indicates the level of Y, as shown on the\nX-axis. It is a reference line which shows all\npossible points of equilibrium where planned\nC + I (= AD or AE)\nexpenditure equals income.\n• AD = Y at point E, when Y = 40, and that is the\nactual equilibrium point of the economy.\n• Prior to it, AD > Y. This is because of the impact of\nminimum level of AD.\n• Beyond it, AD < Y. This is because at higher levels\nof Y, people start saving a part of their income.\n210+\n45°\n• Minimum level of AD = 30.\n100 120\nThis indicates expenditure independent of the level\nof income in the economy.\nY (Income)/GDP\nFig. 7.1: AD in relation to Y (income)\n• AD starts from 30 on the Y-axis. It suggests minimum level of expenditure, or expenditure which\nis independent of the level of Y in the economy.\n•Eis the point where AD = Y. Prior to it, AD > Y. This is because of the impact of minimum level of AD.\nBeyond point E, AD < Y. This is because at higher levels of income, people tend to save a part of\ntheir income. Generally, AD increases as Y increases.","image":"pages/IMG_0561.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS","page":"IMG_0561","text":"Observations related to AD Curve\n• AD=C+ I.\n• The first component of AD: Consumption (C) has an upward slope.\n• Thesecond componentofAD: Investment (I) is assumedto be independent ofthelevel ofincome. Hence,\na horizontal straight line.\n• AD shoots from +ve Y-axis owing to autonomous consumption (i.e., consumption at zero level of\nincome and autonomous investment.","image":"pages/IMG_0561.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"IMG_0561","text":"Q. 1. What is aggregate demand? How it is different from market demand?\nAns. Aggregate demand refers to demand for all goods and services in the economy. It is measured\nin terms of total expenditure on goods and services produced in the economy during an\naccounting year.\nWhereas, market demand is the sum total of demand for one commodity by all the buyers\nthe market.\nMarket demand is a micro concept while aggregate demand is a macro concept.\nQ. 2. Do you think higher the level of Y, higher should be the level of autonomous consumption i\nC-function?\nAns. This is not correct because autonomous consumption is not related to the level of Y.\n45° line - a straiaht line where the value of every point on the line is the same for both the horizontal and vertical axes\n299","image":"pages/IMG_0561.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS","page":"IMG_0563","text":"Observations related to AS Curve\n• AS shoots from the origin and forms a 45° angle. This is on the assumption that there is excess capacity\nin the economy.\n• Because of excess capacity in the economy, AS rises proportionately to AD.\nY (Income)\nTable 7.4: Schedule showing Calculation of AS by adding C and S\nAS (= C+S)\nS (Savings)\nC (Consumption)\n200\n50\n75\n100\n150\n300\n200\n303","image":"pages/IMG_0563.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS","page":"IMG_0564","text":"Observations related to Consumption Curve\n• Consumption curve shoots from +ve Y-axis owing to autonomous consumption or minimum level o\nconsumption.\n• The slope of consumption curve is positive.\n• The point where C = Y is called break-even point.\nAutonomous Consumption vs. Induced Consumption\nAutonomous consumption is the value of C* when Y =0.\n• Induced consumption is the value of C corresponding to different values of Y, when Y is rising. We are\ninduced to spend more when income is rising.\n2. Break-even point - the point where total consumption is equal to the total income or the point where tota\nsaving is equal to zero.\n305","image":"pages/IMG_0564.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS","page":"IMG_0566","text":"Observations related to APC\n• APC > 1, when C> Y.\n• APC = 1, when C= Y.\n• APC < 1, when C < Y.\n• APC tends to fall with increase in income\n(generally people tend to save\nmore with\nincrease in income.\n• APC can never be zero, because C is never zero.\nObservations related to MPC\n• MPC = 4C\n4Y •\n• Value of MPC ranges between 0 and 1.\n• If entire additional income is spent, AC = AY,\nMPC = 1.\n• If entire\nMPC = 0.\nadditional income is saved, AC = 0,\n• MPC is the slope of consumption curve.\n• If MPC is constant, consumption curve is linear.\nGiven below are some numerical illstrations related to the calculation of APC and MPC.\nÝ TACKLING APC AND MPC\nIllustration 5. Given: Y= 300, C= 180, Find APC.\n= 0.6.\nIllustration 6.\nGiven: 4Y = 100, 4C = 30. Find MPC.\nMPC =\n= 0.3.\n100\nIllustration 7. Find APC and MPC from the following data:\nIncome (Y)\nConsumption (C)\n200\n110\n300\n200\nSol.\nAPC\n18\n= 0.6\nMPC\n110\n= 0.55\n200\n110 - 60\n200 - 100\n50\n= 0.5\n100\n200\n= 0.67\n300\n200 - 110\n300 - 200\n100\nIllustration 8.\nGiven: MPC = 0.5, AY = 500. Find AC.\nMPC =\n0.5 =\nAC = 0.5 × 500 = 250.\nIllustration 9. Given: APC = 0.95, 0.94 at income levels of 300 and 350 respectively. Find the value of MP\nAPC\nC = APC × Y\n(as APC = y)\n0.95 × 300 = 285\nMPC * AV\n300\n0.95\n350\n0.94\n0,94 × 350 = 329\n44 = 0.88\n329 - 285 - 50\n350 - 300\n1.4.3 Algebraic Presentation of C-function\nAggregate Demand, Aggregate Supply and Related Concepts\nAlgebraically, consumption function is expressed as in the following equation:\nC= C + bY\n• ¿ in this equation refers to the minimum level of consumption. It is the value of consumption\n• The parameter 'b' refers to the rate at which C increases in response to an increase in V. It is =\nIt is the slope of C-function, also called 'marginal propensity to consume\".\n• The parameter 'Y' refers to income.\n• Fig. 7.4 shows that C= 20 and MPC = 0.8. Using these values, we can write our consumption\nfunction equation as under:\nC = 20 + 0.8Y\n• This equation enables us to estimate the values of C corresponding to different values of Y. Using\nvalues of Y as 50, 100 and 150 respectively (as in Table 7.5), we can estimate the corresponding\nvalues of C, as under:\nC = 20 + 0.8Y (as given)\nC when Y = 0,\n= 20 + 0.8 (0) = 20\nC when Y = 50,\n= 20 + 0.8 (50) = 20 + 40 = 60\nC when Y = 100,\n= 20 + 0.8 (100) = 20 + 80 = 100\nC when Y = 150,\n= 20 + 0.8 (150) = 20 + 120 = 140\n• We find that the estimated values of consumption are exactly the same as in Table 7.5. Thus, we\ncan find the values of Conce we know the values of C, b and Y.\nGiven below are some numerical illustrations related to the calculation of consumption function.\n2 TACKLING CONSUMPTION FUNCTION\nlustration 10. Find C, when C = 200, MPC = 0.5 and Y = 1,000.\nC = C + MPC (Y)\n= 200 + 0.5 (1,000)\n= 200 + 500 = 700.\nlustration 11. Find C, if MPC = 0.6, Y = 500 and C = 50.\nC= C + MPC (Y)\n= 50 + 0.6 (500)\n= 50 + 300 = 350.\nIllustration 12. Given that: C = 75 + 0.75Y, calculate the level of consumption if income level is 500.\nC = 75 + 0.75Y\n= 75 + 0.75 (500)\n= 75 + 375 = 450.\n#lustration 13. Calculate the income level, if consumption = 2,000 and the consumption function, C = 750 + 0.5Y.\nC= C + MPC (Y)\n2,000 = 750 + 0.5Y\n2,000 - 750 = 0.5Y\n1,250 = 0.5Y\n1,250\nY=\n0.5\n= 2,500.\n309\n308","image":"pages/IMG_0566.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"IMG_0567","text":"Ans. Yes. Consumption function would be linear in case MPC is constant.\nQ. 3. Would consumption function be linear in case MPC is constant? Give reason in support of your\nA straight line C-line means that the consumption function is linear.\nQ. 5. Increase in MPC implies increase in the slope of C-function. Comment.\nAns. The given statement is correct. Because MPC shows the rate at which consumption increases in\nresponse to increase in income. Or, MPC is the slope of C-function.","image":"pages/IMG_0567.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS","page":"IMG_0567","text":"Observations related to Saving Curve\n• Saving curve shoots from -ve Y-axis. Because S is negative when C> Y.\n• The slope of saving curve is positive (ST as Y 1).\n• S= 0 at break-even point, i.e., when C = Y, S = 0.\n• After break-even point, saving is positive.\nAutonomous Saving vs. Induced Saving\n• Autonomous saving is independent of the level of income.\nAutonomous saving is the value of 'S' when Y = 0. Thus, if autonomous C = 20, then autonomous S=-20\n• Induced saving is the value of corresponding to different values of Y, when Y is rising. We are induced\nto save more when income is rising.\n7.5.2 Slope of S-line (Saving Function): Marginal Propensity to Save\n• Slope of S-line (indicating saving function) refers to the rate at which S increases in response to\na given increase in Y.\n311","image":"pages/IMG_0567.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS","page":"IMG_0568","text":"Observations related to APS\n• APS canneverbe 1 or> 1 assavingscanneverbezY.\n(There is always some minimum level of C.)\n• APS = 0 when S = 0.\n• APS is negative when S is negative or C> Y.\n• APS tends to rise as the level of income rises in\nthe economy.\nObservations related to MPS\n• MPS = 45\n• Value of MPS ranges between 0 and 1.\n• MPS will be 0 if entire additional income is\nconsumed, i.e., 4S = 0.\n• MPS will be 1 if entire additional income is saved,\ni.e., AS = 4Y.\n• MPS is the slope of saving curve.\n• If MPS is constant, saving curve is linear.\n313","image":"pages/IMG_0568.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"IMG_0571","text":"Q.6. How would you specify the saving function, given the consumption function as under?\nAns. S = (1 - b)Y.\n[Note: In the C-function, there is no constant term like . Accordingly, there is no constant term like - in\nthe saving function. Such a C-function (or S-function) indicates that = 0 (or S= 0) when Y = =0.]\na.7. If personal disposable income is 7 1,000 crore and consumption expenditure is ? 750 crore,\nfind out average propensity to save.\nAns. S= Y - C=R 1,000 crore - & 750 crore = & 250 crore\nAPS =\n250\n- 1,000 = 0.25.\nQ. 8. Can APS ever be negative? If yes, give an example.\nAns. Yes. APS can be negative in situations when S is negative (or when C> Y).\nExample: Y = 50, C = 60, S = -10\nAPS =\n= 50\n-10 = -0.2.\nQ. 9. APC and MPC are two parameters. The value of which parameter can be greater than one, and\nwhen?\nAns. Value of APC can be greater than one. It happens when the level of income is low and C> Y.\nValue of MPC cannot be greater than one. MPC is the ratio between additional consumption\nand additional income\n). Since additional consumption (AC) is only a part of additional\nincome (AY), Ay cannot be greater than one.\nQ.10, Can MPS or MPC ever be negative? Give reasons in support of your answer.\nAns. No. Neither MPS nor MPC can ever be negative. Because: MPS is the ratio between additional\nsaving (AS) and additional income (4Y). Likewise, MPC is the ratio between additionall\nconsumption (AC) and additional income (AY). The ratio aY\n- refers to slope of S-function which\nis always positive because of positive relationship between S and Y). Likewise, the ratio\nrefers to slope of C-function which is always positive (because of positive relationship between\nC and Y).\n319","image":"pages/IMG_0571.jpg"},{"title":"ABILITY ZONE","page":"IMG_0572","text":"Derivation of C-function (C-curve) from S-function (S-curve):\nDiagrammatic Illustration\n• We know,\nC = C + bY\nS = -C + (1 - b)Y\n• Fig. 7.8 lustrates the derivation of C-function (C-curve) from S-function (S-curve).\n[AJ S-function\nS (Saving)\nY (Income/GDP *X\nBreak-even point\n[B] C-function\nC (Consumption)\nY= C\nC=C+ bY\nObservations:\n• C-function [Fig. 7.8(B)] is derived from\nS-function [Fig. 7.8(A)], taking the\nfollowing steps:\n• Step 1: -C indicates negative saving\nwhen Y = 0. This is equal to (-) OP.\nCorresponding to it, C would be equal\nto OP (consumption when Y = 0).\nThus, P becomes the starting point of\nC-function (when Y = 0).\n• Step 2: When S= 0 [at point L in\nFig. 7.8(A)], C = OP* [at point Q in\nFig. 7.8(B)]. This corresponds to\nincome level OL in both the figures.\nThus, between O to OL level of Y, C> Y.\n• Step 3: Starting from P and passing\nthrough point Q, we stretch the\nC-function as a straight line. The line\nshows constant slope (= b). This\ncorresponds to the constant slope of\nS-function = (1 - b).\n• Note that:\nb + (1 -b) = 1\nY (Income//GDP\nSlope of Slope\nC-line\nof S-line\nFig. 7.8: Derivation of C-function (C-curve) from S-function (S-curve)\n320","image":"pages/IMG_0572.jpg"},{"title":"Power Points (Summary of the Chapter)","page":"IMG_0572","text":"Aggregate Demand, Aggregate Supply and Related Concests\n, Aggregate Demand (AD) is the sum total of demand for all goods and services in the economy during\nand services in the economy during an accounting year.\nthe period of an accounting year. It is measured in terms of total (planned) expenditure on the good\n• Components:\nAD = C+I+G+ (X-M)\n= Planned expenditure on the domestically produced goods and services during an\naccounting year.\nAggregate Supply (AS) reters to the production of goods and services in the economy as planner\nby the producers during an accounting year.\n• It sidentical with GDP in the economy where: ) prices are constant, and (i) supply responds\nproportionately to increase in demand, owing to excess capacity. Being identical with GDP, His\nindicated by a 45° line.\n, Consumption Function is the functional relationship between consumption and income. tis specified\nas under:\nC= C+ bY\n• Slope of C-line (consumption function) is the rate at which C (consumption) increases in response\nto a given increase in Y (income). It is measured as the ratio between additional consumption and\nadditional income.\nSlope of C-function = MPC = AC\nI Saving Function is the functional relationship between saving and income. It is usually specified as\nunder.\nS=-C + (1 - b)Y\n• Slope of S-line (saving function) is measured as the ratio between additional saving and additio\nincome.\nSlope of S-function = MPS = AS\n* Average Propensity to Consume is the ratio between total consumption and total income\nAPC = Y\n• Average Propensity to Save is the ratio between total saving and total income.\nAPS = S\n• Relationship between Propensity to Consume and Propensity to Save\n• APC + APS = 1\nMPC + MPS = 1\nalways","image":"pages/IMG_0572.jpg"}],"subject":"macro","number":"7"},{"id":"8","title":"Short Run Equilibrium Output","intro":"The textbook’s short run assumes constant technology and a given capital stock; changes in employment change output.","lessons":[{"title":"Short run and equilibrium output","body":"The textbook’s short run assumes constant technology and a given capital stock; changes in employment change output. Equilibrium output is the level where intended production equals intended expenditure. Producers have no unplanned change in inventories and no incentive to change output. In the two-sector economy Y = C + I and Y = C + S, so the same equilibrium condition can be written S = I.","formula":"Y = AD = C + I\nC + S = C + I ⇒ S = I\nY* = (C̄ + I) ÷ (1 − MPC)","source":"IMG_0573","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"8.1 CONCEPT OF SHORT RUN · 8.2 CONCEPT OF EQUILIBRIUM OUTPUT"},{"title":"How inventories restore equilibrium","body":"When AD is below AS, goods remain unsold and actual inventories exceed desired inventories. Producers cut output and employment until planned saving equals planned investment. When AD exceeds AS, inventories fall unexpectedly and producers expand output, provided spare capacity exists. The direction is easy to reverse by mistake: excess supply causes inventory accumulation; excess demand causes inventory depletion.","formula":"","source":"IMG_0574","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"Equilibrium GDP · Inventory Adjustment"},{"title":"Ex-ante versus ex-post saving and investment","body":"Ex-ante means planned; ex-post means realised. Planned saving and planned investment need not be equal away from equilibrium. Actual saving and actual investment are equal by accounting identity because realised investment includes unintended inventory changes. Equilibrium requires equality of planned S and planned I, not merely the ex-post identity.","formula":"","source":"IMG_0575","kind":"Purple-box concept","textbookHeading":"Ex-ante Saving and Ex-ante Investment"},{"title":"Effective demand and underemployment equilibrium","body":"Effective demand is aggregate demand at the equilibrium level of income/output. Equilibrium can occur below full employment: firms’ production plans can match spending plans while workers and productive capacity remain idle. An increase in autonomous expenditure shifts AD upward and raises equilibrium output in the model with spare capacity; a fall lowers it.","formula":"","source":"IMG_0576","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"Effective Demand · Underemployment Equilibrium"},{"title":"Investment multiplier: size and derivation","body":"Investment multiplier is the ratio of change in income to change in investment. Starting with ΔY = ΔC + ΔI and ΔC = MPC × ΔY gives ΔY(1 − MPC) = ΔI. Higher MPC means more income is respent, so the multiplier is larger. Higher MPS means more income leaks into saving, so the multiplier is smaller. With MPC zero, k = 1; as MPC approaches one, k becomes very large.","formula":"k = ΔY ÷ ΔI = 1 ÷ (1 − MPC) = 1 ÷ MPS\nΔY = k × ΔI","source":"IMG_0579","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"8.5 INVESTMENT MULTIPLIER"},{"title":"Multiplier mechanism, assumptions and reverse operation","body":"An investment injection is someone’s income. They consume a fraction, which becomes someone else’s income; successive rounds generate ΔI, MPC × ΔI, MPC² × ΔI and so on. Saving reduces each next round. The simple multiplier assumes constant MPC, available spare capacity, constant prices, and no additional leakages such as imports or taxes. A fall in investment works in reverse, producing a multiplied fall in income. The final increase in saving equals the initial increase in investment.","formula":"","source":"IMG_0580","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"Multiplier Mechanism"},{"title":"Break-even income versus equilibrium income","textbookHeading":"Consumption Function · Equilibrium Output","body":"Break-even Income: consumption equals income, so saving is zero; use C = Y. Equilibrium Income: planned output equals planned expenditure, so saving equals investment; use Y = C + I or S = I. With positive autonomous investment and positive MPS, equilibrium income exceeds break-even income. The two coincide only when autonomous investment is zero in the simple model. If you are asked for dissaving at zero income, solve for autonomous consumption and report saving as its negative.","source":"IMG_0578","kind":"Exam points","formula":"Break-even Y = C̄/MPS\nEquilibrium Y = (C̄ + I)/MPS"}],"pages":[{"id":"IMG_0573","image":"pages/IMG_0573.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\n8.1 CONCEPT OF SHORT RUN\nUnderstanding the Concept of Short Run\n• Output is determined exclusively by the level of emproyment in the economy. \"Higher level of\nto remain constant.\nemployment leads to higher level of output, and vice versa.\" Technology remaining constant, there\nis one-to-one relationship between output and employment. Thus, if employment is doubled,\noutput will also be doubled.\n• In other words, the level of employment in the economy measures the level of output (GDP) in\nthe economy. Accordingly, output cannot increase once there is full employment in the economy.\n8.2 CONCEPT OF EQUILIBRIUM OUTPUT (GDP)\nTwo Approaches of Equilibrium\n(i) AS = AD Approach\n• Equilibrium output (also called equilibrium GDP or equilibrium income) refers to that level of\noutput in the economy where:\nAD\n[Aggregate Supply = Aggregate Demand]\nCRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS\nWhy is there an equilibrium in the economy when AS = AD?\n• Because in such a situation, intended production in the economy is equal to intended purchase in the\neconomy. The producers do not suffer:\n- The burden of unwanted supplies (or unsold stocks), or\n- The loss of unfulfilled demand (due to lack of stocks).\n• When AS = AD, actual stocks with the producers = desired stocks with the producers.\nWe have already learnt in the previous chapter that:\n• AS refers to the desired level of output in the economy. It is the level of GDP that the producers\nwish to produce (or plan to produce) during an accounting year (also called ex-ante AS).\n• AD, on the other hand, refers to the level of GDP that the buyers wish to buy during an accountin\nyear (also called ex-ante AD).\n• The equilibrium GDP means that level of GDP where what the producers wish to produce (or\naccounting year.\nplan to produce) is exactly equal to what the buyers wish to buy (or plan to buy) during a\n• Equality between AS and AD implies that there is no excess production (or unwanted stocks with\nand AD implies the equality between Y and AD.\nthe producers) or there is no shortage of output in relation to its demand. Equality between As\n1. Full employment - a situation in the economy when AS = AD (or S = I) along with fuller utilisation of resources.\nSo that there is no excess capacity or unemployment in the economy.\n350\n• Further, as Y = AS we can write that the equilibrium is struck when:\nShort Run Equilibrium Output\nY = AD\n(Here, Y denotes income, and Y = AS, as already discussed in the previous chapter\n! CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS\nEquilibrium GDP\nEquilibrium GDP implies a situation, when:\nAS = AD or Y = AD\nIn such a state,\nActual stocks of the producers = Required (or Desired) stocks of the producers\nIn case AS > AD (or Y> AD),\nActual stocks > Required stocks\nThe producers suffer losses because of excessive stocks or unsold stock of goods.\nIn case AS < AD (or Y<AD),\nActual stocks < Required stocks\nThe producers suffer losses on account of unfulfilled demand in the economy.\n(i)S = I Approach\n• Now, we know that in a simple two sector economy (producer sector and household sector), the\nequilibrium is struck when:\nY = AD\nSince,\nY= C + S, and\nAD = C + I (as discussed in the previous chapter)\n• The equilibrium equation can be written like this:\nC+S = C+1\nOr that, equilibrium is struck when:\nS= I (as C is common on both sides of the equation)\n! CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS\nEx-ante Saving and Ex-ante Investment\nIn the context of equilibrium GDP, we should focus only on ex-ante saving and ex-ante investment\nEquilibrium is struck when:\nex-ante saving (S) = ex-ante investment (I)\n• Ex-ante Saving: It refers to 'desired saving' or planned saving during the period of one year. (These are\nthe savings which people intend to make in the economy during the period or one yea\n• Ex-ante Investment: It refers to 'desired investment' or planned investment during the period of one\nyear. This is the investment expenditure which is intended to be made in the economy during the period\nundesired inventory'.)\n(Unplanned investment is like unsold stock of goods which is treated as inventory investment. But this is\na one year. It does not include unplanned investment\n351","file":"macro 8/IMG_0573.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0574","image":"pages/IMG_0574.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\nRelated concepts are: ex-post saving and ex-post investment.\n• Ex-post Saving: It reters to actual saving the economy during the period of one year. his aspect of\nsaving is considered in the context of 'National Income Accounting'.\n• Ex-post investment: It reters actual investment in the economy during the period of one year. Like\nincludes both planned as well as unplanned investment.\n[Note: Equilibnum GDP is struck only when De, emper Planned saving = wesired or Planned Investment\nEquilibrium GDP has nothing to do with actual saving and actual investment.]\n8.3 DETERMINATION OF EQUILIBRIUM OUTPUT (GDP)\nOR EQUILIBRIUM INCOME\nTwo Approaches to Study the Determination of\nEquilibrium Output (GDP) or Equilibrium Income\nAS = AD Approach\nS= 1 Approach\nIn what follows, we discuss how equilibrium is determined using each of these approaches:\n8.3.1 AS = AD Approach and Equilibrium GDP or Equilibrium Income\nConcept of AS, AD (A Recap)\n• According to this approach, equilibrium GDP or equilibrium income is achieved when AS = AD.\nWe have already discussed the concepts of AS and AD in the previous chapter. Only a brief description\nis repeated here as required in the context of equilibrium GDP.\nAS\n• AS refers to planned output in the economy. As described in the previous chapter, it is indicated\nby a 45° line from the origin. The 45° line indicates that AS and GDP are identical to each other.\n• AS is not related to price, as we are considering an economy where price remains constant.\nIt is an economy with excess capacity where AS responds proportionately to AD and price remains\nunaffected.\nThus, AS-function is drawn as in Fig 8.1.\nAS (or Y)\nDATA-SET\nY/GDP\nObservations:\n• The figure shows that Y (or GDP)\nand AS are identical to each other.\n• Thus, when Y = 30, AS is also\n• Likewise, when Y = 50, AS is also\n= 50. And so on.\nY (Income)/GDP\nFig. 8.1: AS-function\n352\nShort Run Equilibrium Output\n, AD refers to desired expenditure (or planned expenditure) in the economy during an accounting\near. It has two components: desired consumption expenditure and desired investment\nrelated to the level of income in the economy.\nexpenditure. Desired investment expenditure is assumed to be autonomous, so that it is no\n, Desired consumption expenditure is related to the level of income: there is a positive relationshis\nbetween consumption (C) and income (Y); C rises as income rises. However, there is always some\nminimum level of C, independent or Y. Thus, C may be 30 when Y = 0. (This is just an assumption\nthat C = 30 when Y = 0.)\n• Based on this description, I-function, C-Tunction and combined C and I function are drawn as in\nFig. 8.2, Fig. 8.3 and Fig. 8.4 respectively. The combined C and I function is called AD-function.\n(Investment Expenditure\nAutonomous I = 20\nat all levels of income\nC (Consumption Expenditure)\nDATA-SET\nAutonomous C = 30\n20 40 60\nObservations:\n• Fig. 8.2\n- All investment is treated as\nautonomous investment.\n- I is not related to Y. So\nthat, I is drawn as a\nhorizontal straight line.\n• Fig. 8.3\n- OK is autonomous\nconsumption (= 30).\n- Crises as Y rises.\n- C-function is a straight\nline, implying that it is a\nlinear function.\nY (Income)/GDP\nY (Income)/GDP\nFig. 8.2: I-function\nFig. 8.3: C-function\nC+I(=AD)\nExpenditure (Cal\n•C+1 (= AD)\n8\ng\ng\n100\n120\nAutonomous C = 30\nAutonomous I = 20\n→ x Autonomous Expenditure\n= 30 + 20 = 50\nObservations:\n• Cand I functions are\ncombined to get AD-function.\n• Constant value of I (= 20) is\nadded to different values of C\nto get C+ I.\n• Example: 1 = 20,\nC = 30 so that C+ 1 = 50.\nThis is when Y = 0.\nLikewise, when Y= 100, then\nC= 80, 1 = 20 and C+1 = 100.\n80 100\nY (Income)/GDP\nFig. 8.4: C and 1 Functions Combined (AD-function)\n353","file":"macro 8/IMG_0574.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0575","image":"pages/IMG_0575.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\nEquilibrium GDP or Equilibrium Income: Tabular Presentation\n• Table 6.1 illustrates the equilibriu epression Gur in terms or the equality between aggregate\nsupply and aggregate demand.\nTable 8.1: Equilibrium GDP in terms of Equality between Aggregate Supply and\nAggregate Demand\nIncome (GDP)\n(Y) ( in crore)\n(& in crore)\n< in crore)\n20\n20\nC+ 1 (= AD)\n(& in crore)\n100\n100\n20\n20\n20\n100\n110\n120\n• Equilibrium is struck when planned output (AS) = planned expenditure (AD) = 7 100 crore. It need\nnot necessarily correspond to full employment.\nEquilibrium GDP or Equilibrium Income: Graphic Presentation\nFig. 8.5 illustrates the equilibrium level of GDP or equilibrium level of income in the economy.\nAD (C+I)\nDATA-SET\nC+I(=AD)\n50\nObservations:\n• AD = C + I.\n• OA: Minimum level of\nexpenditure, including\nminimum C and minimum I.\n• 45° line: Y = AS. It is called\n'Line of Reference.\n• Equilibrium is struck at point E,\nwhere AS = AD.\n• EL is the desired AD as well as\ndesired AS.\n140|100\nY (Income)/GDP\nFig. 8.5: Equilibrium GDP/Income: AS = AD or Y = C+ I\n• AD-line intersects the 45° AS-ine at point E, so that E is the point of equilibrium where AS = AD.\n• OL is the equilibrium GDP in the economy.\n• Now, what the producers wish to produce is exactly equal to what the households wish to buy in\nthe economy. Or, that the desired expenditure on output is equal to desired level of output = 100.\n• All output as planned by the producers is purchased by the buyers in the economy. There are no\nunwanted or undesired stocks with the producers.\n354\nShort Run Equilibrium Output\nDID YOU KNOW\n• Keynes related his discussion on equilibrium GDP to a situation when there is a severe depression\nin the economy.\n- In such a situation, there is a lack of AD, and production capacity remains unutilised. The economy\nwould emerge out of the state of depression only when AD rises\n-AS would automatically respond to AD because there Is excess capacity (unutilised capacity\n-Thus, Keynes considers AD as the principal determinant of equilibrium GDP (as AS is perfectly\nelastic owing to excess capacity.\nGiven below are some numerical illustrations related to the estimation of equilibrium income\nEY TACKLING EQUILIBRIUM INCOME\nlustration 1. Find equilibrium Y when: C = 100 + 0.5Y and I = 1,000.\nEquilibrium is struck when:\nY = AD\nOr, when\nY =C+ I\nSubstituting the values of C and I, we get:\nY = 100 + 0.5Y + 1,000\nY - 0.5Y = 100 + 1,000\n0.5Y = 1,100\n1,100\nY = -\n= 2,200.\n0.5\nIllustration 2. Find equilibrium C when: Y = 6,000 and C = 100 + 0.75Y.\nC = 100 + 0.75Y\nOr,\nC = 100+0.75(6,000)\n= 100 + 4,500 = 4,600.\nIllustration 3. Estimate the value of ex-ante AD, when autonomous investment and autonomous consumption\nexpenditure are & 10 crore and & 50 crore respectively, with MPS = 0.2 and the level of income is\n· 500 crore.\nMPC = 1 - MPS = 1 - 0.2 = 0.8\nWe know that,\nAD = C+ I\nAD = C + MPC (Y) + I\n= 50 + 0.8 (500) + 10\n= 50 + 400 + 10 = 7 460 crore.\nlustration 4. On the basis of the given information, identify whether the economy is in equilibrium or not?\nS.No.\nItems\nAutonomous consumption and investment expenditure\n· 600 crore\nMarginal propensity to save\n· 6,000 crore\n(ili)\nNational income\nMPC = 1 - MPS = 1 - 0.4 = 0.6\nEquilibrium is struck when:\nAD = AS (Y)\nC+I = C+S\nC + MPC (Y) + I = Y\n600+0.6(6,000) = 6,000\n600 + 3,600 = 6,000\n4,200 < 6,000\nSince, AD (R4,200 crore) < AS R 6,000 crore), we conclude that the economy is not in equilibrium.\n355","file":"macro 8/IMG_0575.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0576","image":"pages/IMG_0576.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\nWhat happens if AS > AD?\nAccumulation of Undesired/Unplanned Inventory\nSituation:\nto exceed their demand. As a result, some of the goods would remain unsold. Implying\nincrease in undesired/unplanned inventory.\nAdjustment: To clear unwanted stocks, ang whencers would plan a cut in production. consequenti\nAS would reduce to become equal tomise. This is how As adjusts itself to Ab. Briefly\nequilibrium is restored through a change in output or a change in Y.\nWhat happens if AS < AD?\nFall in Undesired/Unplanned Inventory\nSituation:\nWhen AS is less than AD (AS < AD), supply of goods and services in the economy tends\nto be less than their demand. The existing stocks of the producers would be sold out\nand the producers would suffer the loss of unfulfilled demand. Lack of stock (to cope\nwith the demand) causes loss to the producers.\nAdjustment: To avoid the loss of unfulfilled demand, the producers would plan greater production.\nAS would increase to become equal to AD. This is how AS converges with AD. Thus,\nequilibrium is restored through a change in output or a change in Y.\nCRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS\nThree Basic Assumptions related to Equilibrium GDP\nThree basic assumptions of the Keynesian theory must be noted:\n• Short Period Analysis: Equilibrium GDP according to Keynesian theory is discussed only with reference\nto short period of time.\n• Two Sector Closed Economy: Initially, Keynes discusses the theory of equilibrium GDP in the context\nof a two sector closed economy. Accordingly, AD = C+ I (when only household sector and producer\nsector are considered).\n• AS is Perfectly Elastic: Keynes assumes that AS is perfectly elastic.\n- It means that we are studying an economy in which there is an 'excess capacity': production capacity\nis lying idle or there is unemployment of resources. So that, whenever there is a rise in AD, there is a\ncorresponding rise in AS (as excess capacity begins to be utilised).\n- Thus, AS always aligns itself with AD, without causing any change in the price level.\n8.3.2 S = I Approach and Equilibrium GDP or Equilibrium Income\nAccording to this approach, equilibrium GDP or equilibrium income is achieved when S = I. We have\nalready discussed S and I functions in the previous chapter.\nA brief repetition is given below.\nConcept of S and I Functions (A Recap)\nS-function\nWe know, 5 = f (Y). S is positively related to Y. However at the lower level of income, S can be negative.\nBecause, at lower level of income C may be greater than Y. S becomes negative to the extent C > Y. We\nare considering a straight line S-function called a linear function.\n356\nShort Run Equilibrium Output\nAs regards I-function, we are considering only autonomous 1. It is independent of the level of Y.\nAccordingly, it is a horizontal straight line (shooting from the Y-axis). It may be categorically noted\nthat in the context of his equilibrium analysis, Keynes does not consider induced investment' which\nchanges in response to changes in the level of income in the economy.\nEquilibrium GDP/Income: Tabular Presentation\ntable 82 shows equilibrium GOP in terms of the equality between S and I.\nTable 8.2: Equilibrium GDP/Income in terms of Equality between S and I\nIncome (GDP)\nConsumption\nSaving\nInvestment\n(S = Y - C)\n(R in crore)\n(in crore)\n( in crore)\n( in crore)\n-30\n90\n140\nIn Table 8.2, equilibrium is struck when:\nS = I = = 20 crore\nEquilibrium Income (GDP) = * 100 crore.\nEquilibrium GDP/Income : Graphic Presentation\nFig. 8.6 shows S and I functions, and the equilibrium GDP/income.\nObservations:\n• Ol is constant investment, independent\nof the level of Y.\n• OK is -ve saving and is equal to C when\nY = 0.\n• E is the point of equilibrium where S= 1.\n• OL is the equilibrium income or\nequilibrium GDP.\n100 120 140\nY (Income)/GDP\nFig. 8.6: S and I functions depicting equilibrium GDP/Income.\n* Eis the point of equilibrium where 5 = I. OL is the equilibrium GDP = 100.\n• Now, planned expenditure on output (AD) = planned output (AS/ in the economy, even whe\nowing to saving) entire income of the households is not converted into expenditure. This is\n357","file":"macro 8/IMG_0576.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0577","image":"pages/IMG_0577.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\nbecause loss of expenditure by way as is equally compensated by the gain of expenditure by\n• Income and expenditure being equal for planned output and expenditure on planned output\nbeing equal), there are no unwanted or undesired stocks with the producers, Level of income/\nGDP is in a state of equilibrium.\nWhat happens if S> 1?\nAccumulation of Undesired/Unplanned Inventory\nSituation:\nIn case 5 > 1, it implies a situation when a fall in expenditure through 'S is more than\nthe rise in expenditure through 'T: Accordingly, aggregate expenditure in the economy\nwould be less than what is needed to buy the planned output. Some output would\nremain unsold, and producers will have undesired stocks.\nAdjustment: To clear their stocks, the producers would now plan lesser output. Lesser output would\nmean lesser income. Lesser income would mean lesser saving. The process would\ncontinue till S = 1. Thus, the equality between S and I is restored through change in the\nlevel of Y.\nWhat happens if 5 < I?\nFall in Undesired/Unplanned Inventory\nSituation:\nIn case S < 1, it implies a situation when a fall in expenditure through 'S' is less than\nthe rise in expenditure through T'. Accordingly, aggregate expenditure in the economy\nwould be greater than what is required to buy the planned output. It is a situation of\nhigher AD than AS. The producers would suffer the loss of unfulfilled demand due to\nlack of stocks.\nAdjustment: This will prompt the producers to plan higher output. Higher output would mean higher\nincome, and higher income would mean higher saving. The process would continue till\nS= I. Here again, the equality between S and I is restored through change in the level\nof Y.\nCRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS\nSimultaneous Equality between AS and AD as well as between S and I\nIncome\nConsumption\nSavings\nInvestment\nAD (= C + I)\nAS (= C+ S)\n200\n300\n400\n500\n-50\n150\n175\n100\n200\n200\n100\n250\n100\n300\n250\n300\n300\n400\n500\n200\n100\n400\n• At equilibrium level of income, S = 1(100 = 100), AD = AS AS = 300).\n• The following figure shows simultaneous equality between AS and AD, as well as between S and I.\n• Equilibrium is attained at point E when AS = AD, as in Fig. 8.7(A), and at point E* when S = 1, as in Fig. 8.7(B\n358\nShort Run Equilibrium Output\nAD = C + I\nY (Income)/GDP\n(B)\nObservation:\n• Equality between AS and AD at point E\n[in Fig. 8.7(A)] implies the equality betweer\nSand I at point E* [in Fig. 8.7(B)].\n• There is one and only one equilibrium GDP\nwhen AS = AD or when S = 1. In either case,\nthe equilibrium GDP = OL\nY (Income)/GDP\nFig. 8.7: Simultaneous Equality between AS and AD as well as between S and I\n• OL is the equilibrium level of income. It is to be noted that the equality between AS and AD implies the\nequality between Sand I; and equality between Sand I implies the equality between AS and AD.\n• Accordingly, there is one and only one level of equilibrium income when AS = AD, and S = I.\nAVLR 8.1\nEX-ANTE AND EX-POST ASPECTS OF EQUALITY BETWEEN AD & AS\nAND BETWEEN S & I\nGiven below are some numerical illustrations related to the estimations of C, S, I andY.\nTACKLING LIST OF C, S, I AND Y\nlustration 5. Find equilibrium S and equilibrium I when: Y = 4,400, MPC = 0.75, and C = 100.\nEquilibrium is struck when:\nY = C+ I\nOr, when\nCat equilibrium:\nS = I\nC = C + MPC (Y)\n=100+0.75(4,400)\n= 100 + 3,300 = 3,400\nWe know,\nMPS = 1 - MPC = 1 - 0.75 = 0.25\nThus, S at equilibrium: S = -100 + 0.25 (4,400)\n(Note that, 5 = -C= - 100), i.., Dissavings = (Negative of autonomous consumption).]\n= -100 + 1,100 = 1,000\nAlternatively:\nY = C+S\nS = Y - C= 4,400 - 3,400 = 1,000\nIn equilibrium,\nWe know,\nAccordingly,\nS = 1\nS = 1,000\nI = 1,000.\n359","file":"macro 8/IMG_0577.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0578","image":"pages/IMG_0578.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\nIllustration 6. Given that, S = - 25 + 0.5Y and 1 = 5,000, find equilibrium Y and equilibrium c.\nIn equilibrium,\nS = 1\nSo that,\n-25 + 0.5Y = 5,000\n0.5Y = 5,000 + 25\n→ 0.5Y = 5,025\n5,025\nY=\n= 10,050\n0.5\nS at equilibrium:\nS= -25 + 0.5 (10,050)\n= -25 + 5,025=5,000\nWe know,\ny= C+ S\nC = Y - S\nThus,\nequilibrium C = 10,050 - 5,000\n= 5,050.\nIllustration 7. Given that, C = 400 + 0.9Y and I = 4,000, find: (i) equilibrium Y, (i)S and C at equilibrium y.\nEquilibrium Y is found when:\ny= C+ I\nSubstituting the values, we get:\nY = 400 + 0.9Y + 4,000\nY - 0.9Y = 400 + 4,000\n0.1Y = 4,400\nY = 4,400\nThus,\nC at equilibrium:\n= 44,000\n0.1\nC=C+ MPC (Y) = 400+0.9 (44,000)\n= 400 + 39,600 = 40,000\nS at equilibrium:\nS = -C + MPS (Y)\n= -400 + 0.1 (44,000)\n= -400 + 4,400\nAlternatively, in equilibrium, S = I\nSince I = 4,000 (given), S must be equal to 4,000.\n8.4 SHIFT IN EQUILIBRIUM: IMPACT OF ADDITIONAL\nINVESTMENT (AI)\n• Increase in investment causes increase in the level of AD. Accordingly, AD function shifts upward.\nFig. 8.8 illustrates how it impacts the equilibrium GDP.\n= AS\nAD, = C+ I + AI\nAD = C+ I\nAY\nBecause of Al\nequilibrium GDP\nincreases from OJ to OK.\nObservations:\n• Point 'A' indicates initial\nequilibrium when AD = C+ I and\nequilibrium GDP = OJ.\n• Point 'B' indicates equilibrium\nwhen AD=C+I+ AI and\nequilibrium GDP = OK.\nIt shows the impact of AL.\n• AY > AI; due to multiplier effect.\nY (Income)/GDP\nFig. 8.8: Impact of Increase in Investment (AI) on Equilibrium GDP\n360\n• o/is the initial level of equilibrium GDP.\nShort Run Equilibrium Output\nDue to additional investment (Al), the AD-function shifts upward. It is now indicated by C+1 + Al.\nIt causes an increase in equilibrium GDP from Of to OK.\nAditional Investment has Multiplier Effect\n• in Fig. 8.8, owing to an additional investment (4), level of income increases from Of to OK.\nIncrease in income = JK. Compare it with the size of additional investment (AT) = EF. You will find\nincrease in income is more than the increase in investment (IK > EF).\n• This offers the conclusion that additional investment carries a multiplier effect on the level of GDP\n(income or output). This brings us to the concept of multiplier.\n8,5 INVESTMENT MULTIPLIER AND ITS MECHANISM\n8.5.1 The Concept of Multiplier\n• We have seen how additional investment (AI) causes additional output (AY) in the economy.\nWe have noted that increase in output/income (AY) is many times more than the increase in\ninvestment (AT).\n• The factor by which the increase in output/income is greater than the increase in investment is\ncalled investment multiplier or output multiplier (K). It is measured as the ratio between increase\nin output/income and increase in investment.\nCRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS\nInvestment Multiplier or Output Multiplier\nInvestment multiplier or output multiplier refers to the 'number of times by which the increase in output/\nincome (AY) exceeds the increase in investment (AI)'. It is measured as the ratio between change in\noutput/income and change in investment.\n\"\nHere, K= Multiplier; AY = Change in output/income;\nAI = Change in investment.\nIllustration.\nIfinvestment increases by * 15 crore and as a consequence, income increases by & 60 crore, it implies\nthat increase in income (AY) is 4 times the increase in investment (AL). Accordingly, multiplier (K) = 4.\nK=\n= 4\n8.5.2 Relationship between Multiplier and\nMPC (Marginal Propensity to Consume)\n• There is a direct relationship between multiplier and MPC. Higher the value of MPC, higher the\nmultiplier and vice versa.\nin fact, multiplier is often estimated with reference to MPC, as under:\nK= 1 - MPC\n361","file":"macro 8/IMG_0578.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0579","image":"pages/IMG_0579.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\nThis equation establishes a direct relationship between MPC and K.\nIllustration.\nIf MPC = 0.5,\nK= 1-0.5 0.5 = 2\nAnd, if MPC is 0.8,\nK= 1 - 0.8 0.2 = 5\nThus, increase in MPC implies increase in the value of multiplier.\nCRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS\nHow do we find that K= 1-MPC?\nWe know that,\nK=-\nWe also know that,\nAccordingly,\nY= C+I\nAY = C + AI\n(in a state of equilibrium)\n(Here, AI = Change in investment; AY = Change in income; and 4C = Change in consumption.)\nPutting the value of Al in equation (i), we get\nAY - AC\nDividing right hand side of the equation by AY,\nor K= 1 - MPC\n(MPC - AN\nSince MPC + MPS = 1, or MPS = 1 - MPC, we can also write that,\nK = MPS\nImplying that multiplier is the reciprocal of marginal propensity to save. Higher the value of MPS, lower\nthe value of K.\nLogic behind the Direct Relationship between MPC and Multiplier\n• Additional investment (AI) means additional expenditure in the economy; additional expenditure\nmeans additional income (AY) in the economy.\n• Thus, if AI = 100, then AY = 100, when investment expenditure is incurred. Let us take this AY as\nincrease in income in the round-1.\n• This AY (= 100) would be split into AC and 1S. Because a part of income is spent and a part of it\nis saved.\n• In round-2, AC would be converted into AY (because AC is expenditure and expenditure causes\nincome).\nHere, comes an important point:\nHigher value of MPC would mean higher AC.\n362\nShort Run Equilibrium Output\nExample:\n1FMPC = 0.4, then AC = 0.4 100) = 40.\nIFMPC=0.6, then AC=0.6(100) = 60.\nwould mean higher AY.\nAccordingly, AY in round-2 (which is equal to AC) would depend on the value of MPC. Higher MPC\n, Conversion of 4C into AY continues in various rounds. And, in all the rounds, higher MPC would\ncause higher AC and therefore, higher AY.\n• Hence the conclusion that, higher the value of MPC, higher is the generation of income caused by a\ngiven increase in investment. Or that, higher the MPC, higher is the value of investment multiplier.\nNote: The section on multiplier mechanism should explain this point further.]\n8.5.3 Relationship between Multiplier and MPS\n(Marginal Propensity to Save)\n• There is an indirect relationship between multiplier and MPS. Higher the value of MPS, lower the\nmultiplier and vice versa.\n• Multiplier is estimated with reference to MPS, as under:\nK=•\nMPS\nIllustration.\nIf MPS = 0.4,\nK=\n= 2.5\nAnd if MPS = 0.8,\nK=\n0.8\n=1.25\nThus, increase in MPS implies fall in the value of multiplier.\nFormulae of Multiplier (K)\nK=•\nK=1 - MPC\nK = MPS\nMPC\nMPS\nMultiplier (K) = T-MPC * MPS\n0.25\n0.75\n1-0.25 * 0.75 = 1.33\n0.50\n0.50\n0.75\n0.25\n1-0.50 0.50 * 2\n1-0.75 0.25 * 4\n11 070\n363","file":"macro 8/IMG_0579.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0580","image":"pages/IMG_0580.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\nObservations:\n• Multiplier (K) is directly related to MPC.\n• Multiplier (K) is inversely related to MPS.\n• Maximum value of multiplier = uw MPC = 1, i.e., when entire additional income is\nMmm value or mutiler uxew n MP = O, l.e., when enure additonal income is saved\nGiven below are some numerical ilustrations related to the estimation of investment multiplier.\nY TACKLING INVESTMENT MULTIPLIER\nlustration 8. In an economy, government makes some additional investment. Find its value when MPC = 0.5 and\nincrease in income = · 1,000.\nSol.\nWe know,\nK (Multiplier) =\nWe also know that,\nSubstituting the value of MPC,\nK = 1 -MPC\nK= 1 - 0.5\n0.5\nWe know,\n*\nand AY = 1,000\nThus,\n1,000\nДІ\n1,000\nДІ =\n= 500\nAdditional investment by the government = 7 500.\nIllustration 9. If MPC = 0.75, how much additional investment is required to increase income by & 600? Also, find\nthe multiplier.\nSol.\nWe know,\nK (Multiplier) = I - MPC\n= 1-0.75 0.25 = 4\nK=\nSo that,\nAI =\n-600 = 150\nThus, additional investment of & 150 is required to increase income by 7 600 and the value of\nmultiplier = 4.\nIllustration 10.\nFind MPC when investment multiplier = 1.\nSol.\nGiven that,\nK (Multiplier) = 1 - MPC\n1 - MPC\n= =1\n1 = 1 - MPC\nMPC = 1- 1=0\nThus, when K = 1, MPC = 0.\n364\nShort Run Equilibrium Output\nlustration 11.\nlustration 12.\nFind the value of multiplier when MPC = MPS.\nWe know,\nMPC + MPS = 1\nWhen MPC = MPS, each parameter must be equal to 0.5.\nNoW,\nK= MPS 0.5-2.\nIn an economy investment expenditure increased by 7 400 crore and marginal propensity to\nconsume is 0.8. Calculate increase in income and increase in saving.\nK (Multiplier) = 1- MPC 1-0.8 0.2=5\nMPS = 1 - MPC\n= 1 - 0.8 = 0.2\nIncrease in Income =K× AI\n= 5x7 x7 crore = 7 2,000 crore.\nIncrease in Saving = MPS × AY\n= 0.2 x* 2,000 crore = 7 400 crore.\n8.5.4 Multiplier Mechanism\nInvestment Multiplier Works through Change in Consumption\nInitial increase in income due to initial investment expenditure causes increase in consumption\nwhich (because it is an expenditure becomes somebody's income again.\n• So greater the additional consumption (AC) out of additional income (AY), greater is the generation\nof income. Or, greater the marginal propensity to consume (AC/AY), greater will be the value of\n\"ONE PERSON'S EXPENDITURE IS ANOTHER PERSON'S INCOME.\"\nTable 8.3 illustrates the multiplier mechanism. It is based on the assumption that MPC = 0.5.\nTable 8.3: Multiplier Process\n(Assumption: MPC = 0.5)\nRound\nIncrease in\nInvestment\nExpenditure (AI)\n(‹ in crore)\n100\nChange in Income\nInduced Change in\nLeakage or Saving\n(AY)\nConsumption\n(in crore)\n( in crore)\n(MPC = 0.5)\nR in crore)\n50.00\n25.00\n12.50\n6.25\n3.12\nTotal\n12.50\n6.25\n3.12\n1.56\n0.78\n0.39\n0.20\n200\n0.78\n0.39\n0.20\n0.10\n50.00\n25.00\n12.50\n6.25\n3.12\n1.56\n0.78\n0.39\n0.20\n0.10\n100\n365","file":"macro 8/IMG_0580.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0581","image":"pages/IMG_0581.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\n•Table 8.3 shows that as a result of initar increase in investment by cre tere is increase\nExplanation\n•Our assumption is that MPC IS U.5. Hence, because of the increase in income by R100 crore\nin income by ? 100 crore in round-1.\nconsumption will increase by & 50 crore and remaining & 50 crore will be saved.\nincome by & 50 crore.\n• Now, AY (=850 crore) would be split into AC = R 25 crore and AS = 7 25 crore.\non nt is consum up hunture by so crore, there will de increase in incom\nby & 25 crore in round-3.\n• NoW, 4T = 1 25 crore would be spilt be • = ‹ 12.5 crore and As = Y 12.5 crore. Accordingly\nin round-4 income will increase by F 12.5 crore.\n• In different time periods, as shown in the table, income will go on increasing as a result of increase\nin consumption expenditure.\n• Total increase in income = 7 200 crore. Since, increase in investment (AI) = F 100 crore, and\nincrease in income (AY) = 7 200 crore, it follows that multiplier = 2.\n200\nK=\nДІ\n= 2\n100\nK= 1- MPC 1 - 0.5 0.5\n= 2\nObviously, higher MPC would have caused greater increase in income, implying a higher value of\nmultiplier.\n8.5.5 Forward Action and Backward Action of Multiplier\nMultiplier Action\nForward Action\n• Multiplier action is 'forward' when there is\na multiple increase in income caused by\nan increase in investment.\n• If investment increases by & 100 crore and\nMPC = 0.5, there will be increase in\nincome 2 times the increase in\ninvestment. This is forward action\nmultiplier.\nBackward Action\n• Multiplier action is 'backward' when there\nis a multiple decrease in income caused\nby decrease in investment.\n• If investment decreases by & 100 crore\nand MPC = 0.5, there will be decrease in\nincome 2 times the decrease in\ninvestment. This is backward action\nAY = K. AI\n= 2(100)\n= 200\nAY = K. (-AI)\n= 2(-100)\n=-200\nK=T - MPC\n*T-0.5\n0.5 * 2\n• In case of forward action multiplie\nequilibrium level of income incre\nK = 1 -MPC\n* 1 - 0.5\n2=90\n• In case of backward action multiplier, the\nequilibrium level of income decreases.\nShort Run Equilibrium Output\nCAVLR 8.2,\nIVESTMENT MULTIPLIER AND ITS MECHANISM\ninvestment multiplier.\nGiven below is a numerical illustration related to the estimation of missing values in the process of\n3Ý TACKLING MISSING VALUES RELATED TO THE PROCESS OF INVESTMENT MULTIPLIER\n(lustration 13. The following table shows a multipller process initiated after an initial additional investment o\n· 2,000. Fill the missing values.\nRound\nAll other rounds\nTotal\nRound\n2,000\n1,600\n1,280\nAll other rounds\nTotal\nRound\n5,120\n10,000\n1,600\n1,280\n1,024\n4,096\n8,000\nAY\n2,000\n1,600\n1,600\n1,280\nAll other rounds\nTotal\n5,120\n10,000\n4,096\n8,000\nHow to find Missing Values?\n(i) Initially, AI = AY, i.e., 2,000 = 2,000 in round-1\n(ii) AY = AC + AS\n2,000 = 4C + 400\nAC = 2,000 - 400 = 1,600 (Round-1)\n(iii) AC of round-1 becomes 4Y of the next round.\n(:: Additional C = Additional Expenditure = Additional Y)\n(iv) AY is split between AC and AS on the basis of MPC [MPC =\nMPS = 1 - MPC\n= 1 - 0.8 = 0.2\nAY (2,000)\n400\n320\n1,024\nAS\n256\n1,024\n2,000\n1,600\n= 0.8]\n= 2,000\nAC = 0.8 × 2,000 = 1,600\nAS = 0.2 × 2,000 = 400\nMPS\nMPC AY\n367\n366","file":"macro 8/IMG_0581.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0582","image":"pages/IMG_0582.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\n(v) K= 1 - MPC\n= 1 - 0.8\n* 0.2\n(vi) Total increase in income = Additional investment × K\n= 2,000 × 5 = 10,000\n(vii) Total increase in consumption = Totall increase in income × MPC\n= 10,000 × 0.8 = 8,000\n(vil) Total increase in saving = Total increase in income - Total increase in consumption\n= 10,000 - 8,000 = 2,000.\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\nQ. 1. Briefly explain the multiplier process.\nAns. The working of the multiplier assumes the following process:\nChange in\nInvestment\nChange in\nIncome\nChange in\nConsumption\nChange in\nincome\n»ДУ -\n(i) Change in investment implies additional expenditure which leads to change in income. As\na result, there is change in consumption. Consumption expenditure of one person is an\nincome of the other. Hence, change in consumption leads to change in income. This process\ncontinues till AC reduces to zero.\n(ii) MPC is the core factor in the process of income generation. Higher the MPC, greater is the\nconversion of income into consumption expenditure.\n(i) Accordingly, greater is the generation of income. Because, ultimately it is expenditure which\nis converted into income. Expenditure is an injection into the income generation process,\nsaving is a leakage.\nQ. 2. What will be the value of multiplier if entire additional income is converted into additional\nconsumption?\nAns. In such a situation, AC = AY (or change in consumption = change in income or that\nAccordingly, K (or multiplier) would be:\nK=1- MPC*1-1-1=∞\nThe multiplier value would tend towards infinity.\nABILITY ZONE\nConcept of AED (Aggregate Effective Demand)\n• AED refers to that level of AD where AS = AD. Thus, AED always corresponds to the equilibrium level\nof income in the economy.\n• It is called 'effective' as it is this level of AD which actually determines the equilibrium between AS and Al\nAS just coincides with AD, because AS is assumed to be perfectly elastic.\n368\nCheck Fig. 8.9 for further illustration.\n• Observations:\n• Eis the point of equilibrium where AS = AD.\n• EL is the level of demand where AS coincides with AD,\nand the equilibrium GDP is achieved.\n• EL, therefore, is AED (aggregate effective demand).\nAD function shows different levels of aggregate demand.\nBut it is only at point E that AS = AD. At point E, level of\naggregate demand = EL. Hence, EL is aggregate effective\ndemand, which is effective in striking an equilibrium between\nAS and AD. As regards a schedule indicating AED, students\nare advised to see Table 8.1, page 354. In this table,\nAED = 100, where AD = C + I and the economy is in a state\nof equilibrium.\nShort Run Equilibrium Output\nAggregate Demand (AD)\n(= AS\nY (Income)/GDP\nFig. 8.9: Aggregate Effective Demand\nPower Points [Summary of the Chapter]\n• Equilibrium Output implies equilibrium income as well as equilibrium employment. It also implies\nequilibrium GDP when we are considering a closed economy\n• Equilibrium is Struck when AS = AD (implying that planned output = planned demand). Or, when S = 1\n(implying that planned saving = planned investment).\n• Equilibrium Situation is that situation when what is planned to be produced is exactly equal to what\nplanned to be purchased during a given period of time. The producers do not suffer:\n> the loss due to excess stocks, or\n› the loss due to lack of stocks. When AS = AD: actual stocks (with producers) = desired stocks (with\nproducers).\n• Adjustment Mechanism: When AS > AD or when AD > AS, the equilibrium is restored through changes\nin AS (planned output), as it is assumed to be perfectly elastic, owing to the existence of excess\ncapacity in the economy.\n• Shift in Equilibrium occurs when additional investment causes increase in the level of income through an\nupward shift in AD.\n• Additional investment has a multiplier effect on the level of income. Additional investment causes a\npositive multiplier effect on the level of income.\n• Investment Multiplier is the ratio between change in income and change in investment.\nK=\n(Here, K= Multiplier; AY = Change in income; AI = Change in investment.)\n• Relation between Multiplier and MPC: Multiplier is positively related to MPC (or inversely related to MPS).\nHigher the MPC, higher the multiplier and smaller the MPC, smaller the multiplier will be. Thus,\nK= 1-MPC MPS\n• Forward and Backward Action of the Multiplier: Investment multiplier works both ways, positive\nand negative.\n* Additional investment causes multiple increase in income. This is torward action of the multiplie\n* Decrease in investment causes a multiple decrease in income. This is backward action of the multiplier\n369","file":"macro 8/IMG_0582.HEIC"}],"boxes":[{"title":"CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS","page":"IMG_0573","text":"Why is there an equilibrium in the economy when AS = AD?\n• Because in such a situation, intended production in the economy is equal to intended purchase in the\neconomy. The producers do not suffer:\n- The burden of unwanted supplies (or unsold stocks), or\n- The loss of unfulfilled demand (due to lack of stocks).\n• When AS = AD, actual stocks with the producers = desired stocks with the producers.\nWe have already learnt in the previous chapter that:\n• AS refers to the desired level of output in the economy. It is the level of GDP that the producers\nwish to produce (or plan to produce) during an accounting year (also called ex-ante AS).\n• AD, on the other hand, refers to the level of GDP that the buyers wish to buy during an accountin\nyear (also called ex-ante AD).\n• The equilibrium GDP means that level of GDP where what the producers wish to produce (or\naccounting year.\nplan to produce) is exactly equal to what the buyers wish to buy (or plan to buy) during a\n• Equality between AS and AD implies that there is no excess production (or unwanted stocks with\nand AD implies the equality between Y and AD.\nthe producers) or there is no shortage of output in relation to its demand. Equality between As\n1. Full employment - a situation in the economy when AS = AD (or S = I) along with fuller utilisation of resources.\nSo that there is no excess capacity or unemployment in the economy.\n350\n• Further, as Y = AS we can write that the equilibrium is struck when:\nShort Run Equilibrium Output\nY = AD\n(Here, Y denotes income, and Y = AS, as already discussed in the previous chapter","image":"pages/IMG_0573.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS","page":"IMG_0573","text":"Equilibrium GDP\nEquilibrium GDP implies a situation, when:\nAS = AD or Y = AD\nIn such a state,\nActual stocks of the producers = Required (or Desired) stocks of the producers\nIn case AS > AD (or Y> AD),\nActual stocks > Required stocks\nThe producers suffer losses because of excessive stocks or unsold stock of goods.\nIn case AS < AD (or Y<AD),\nActual stocks < Required stocks\nThe producers suffer losses on account of unfulfilled demand in the economy.\n(i)S = I Approach\n• Now, we know that in a simple two sector economy (producer sector and household sector), the\nequilibrium is struck when:\nY = AD\nSince,\nY= C + S, and\nAD = C + I (as discussed in the previous chapter)\n• The equilibrium equation can be written like this:\nC+S = C+1\nOr that, equilibrium is struck when:\nS= I (as C is common on both sides of the equation)","image":"pages/IMG_0573.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS","page":"IMG_0573","text":"Ex-ante Saving and Ex-ante Investment\nIn the context of equilibrium GDP, we should focus only on ex-ante saving and ex-ante investment\nEquilibrium is struck when:\nex-ante saving (S) = ex-ante investment (I)\n• Ex-ante Saving: It refers to 'desired saving' or planned saving during the period of one year. (These are\nthe savings which people intend to make in the economy during the period or one yea\n• Ex-ante Investment: It refers to 'desired investment' or planned investment during the period of one\nyear. This is the investment expenditure which is intended to be made in the economy during the period\nundesired inventory'.)\n(Unplanned investment is like unsold stock of goods which is treated as inventory investment. But this is\na one year. It does not include unplanned investment\n351","image":"pages/IMG_0573.jpg"},{"title":"DID YOU KNOW","page":"IMG_0575","text":"• Keynes related his discussion on equilibrium GDP to a situation when there is a severe depression\nin the economy.\n- In such a situation, there is a lack of AD, and production capacity remains unutilised. The economy\nwould emerge out of the state of depression only when AD rises\n-AS would automatically respond to AD because there Is excess capacity (unutilised capacity\n-Thus, Keynes considers AD as the principal determinant of equilibrium GDP (as AS is perfectly\nelastic owing to excess capacity.\nGiven below are some numerical illustrations related to the estimation of equilibrium income\nEY TACKLING EQUILIBRIUM INCOME\nlustration 1. Find equilibrium Y when: C = 100 + 0.5Y and I = 1,000.\nEquilibrium is struck when:\nY = AD\nOr, when\nY =C+ I\nSubstituting the values of C and I, we get:\nY = 100 + 0.5Y + 1,000\nY - 0.5Y = 100 + 1,000\n0.5Y = 1,100\n1,100\nY = -\n= 2,200.\n0.5\nIllustration 2. Find equilibrium C when: Y = 6,000 and C = 100 + 0.75Y.\nC = 100 + 0.75Y\nOr,\nC = 100+0.75(6,000)\n= 100 + 4,500 = 4,600.\nIllustration 3. Estimate the value of ex-ante AD, when autonomous investment and autonomous consumption\nexpenditure are & 10 crore and & 50 crore respectively, with MPS = 0.2 and the level of income is\n· 500 crore.\nMPC = 1 - MPS = 1 - 0.2 = 0.8\nWe know that,\nAD = C+ I\nAD = C + MPC (Y) + I\n= 50 + 0.8 (500) + 10\n= 50 + 400 + 10 = 7 460 crore.\nlustration 4. On the basis of the given information, identify whether the economy is in equilibrium or not?\nS.No.\nItems\nAutonomous consumption and investment expenditure\n· 600 crore\nMarginal propensity to save\n· 6,000 crore\n(ili)\nNational income\nMPC = 1 - MPS = 1 - 0.4 = 0.6\nEquilibrium is struck when:\nAD = AS (Y)\nC+I = C+S\nC + MPC (Y) + I = Y\n600+0.6(6,000) = 6,000\n600 + 3,600 = 6,000\n4,200 < 6,000\nSince, AD (R4,200 crore) < AS R 6,000 crore), we conclude that the economy is not in equilibrium.\n355","image":"pages/IMG_0575.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS","page":"IMG_0576","text":"Three Basic Assumptions related to Equilibrium GDP\nThree basic assumptions of the Keynesian theory must be noted:\n• Short Period Analysis: Equilibrium GDP according to Keynesian theory is discussed only with reference\nto short period of time.\n• Two Sector Closed Economy: Initially, Keynes discusses the theory of equilibrium GDP in the context\nof a two sector closed economy. Accordingly, AD = C+ I (when only household sector and producer\nsector are considered).\n• AS is Perfectly Elastic: Keynes assumes that AS is perfectly elastic.\n- It means that we are studying an economy in which there is an 'excess capacity': production capacity\nis lying idle or there is unemployment of resources. So that, whenever there is a rise in AD, there is a\ncorresponding rise in AS (as excess capacity begins to be utilised).\n- Thus, AS always aligns itself with AD, without causing any change in the price level.\n8.3.2 S = I Approach and Equilibrium GDP or Equilibrium Income\nAccording to this approach, equilibrium GDP or equilibrium income is achieved when S = I. We have\nalready discussed S and I functions in the previous chapter.\nA brief repetition is given below.\nConcept of S and I Functions (A Recap)\nS-function\nWe know, 5 = f (Y). S is positively related to Y. However at the lower level of income, S can be negative.\nBecause, at lower level of income C may be greater than Y. S becomes negative to the extent C > Y. We\nare considering a straight line S-function called a linear function.\n356\nShort Run Equilibrium Output\nAs regards I-function, we are considering only autonomous 1. It is independent of the level of Y.\nAccordingly, it is a horizontal straight line (shooting from the Y-axis). It may be categorically noted\nthat in the context of his equilibrium analysis, Keynes does not consider induced investment' which\nchanges in response to changes in the level of income in the economy.\nEquilibrium GDP/Income: Tabular Presentation\ntable 82 shows equilibrium GOP in terms of the equality between S and I.\nTable 8.2: Equilibrium GDP/Income in terms of Equality between S and I\nIncome (GDP)\nConsumption\nSaving\nInvestment\n(S = Y - C)\n(R in crore)\n(in crore)\n( in crore)\n( in crore)\n-30\n90\n140\nIn Table 8.2, equilibrium is struck when:\nS = I = = 20 crore\nEquilibrium Income (GDP) = * 100 crore.\nEquilibrium GDP/Income : Graphic Presentation\nFig. 8.6 shows S and I functions, and the equilibrium GDP/income.\nObservations:\n• Ol is constant investment, independent\nof the level of Y.\n• OK is -ve saving and is equal to C when\nY = 0.\n• E is the point of equilibrium where S= 1.\n• OL is the equilibrium income or\nequilibrium GDP.\n100 120 140\nY (Income)/GDP\nFig. 8.6: S and I functions depicting equilibrium GDP/Income.\n* Eis the point of equilibrium where 5 = I. OL is the equilibrium GDP = 100.\n• Now, planned expenditure on output (AD) = planned output (AS/ in the economy, even whe\nowing to saving) entire income of the households is not converted into expenditure. This is\n357","image":"pages/IMG_0576.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS","page":"IMG_0577","text":"Simultaneous Equality between AS and AD as well as between S and I\nIncome\nConsumption\nSavings\nInvestment\nAD (= C + I)\nAS (= C+ S)\n200\n300\n400\n500\n-50\n150\n175\n100\n200\n200\n100\n250\n100\n300\n250\n300\n300\n400\n500\n200\n100\n400\n• At equilibrium level of income, S = 1(100 = 100), AD = AS AS = 300).\n• The following figure shows simultaneous equality between AS and AD, as well as between S and I.\n• Equilibrium is attained at point E when AS = AD, as in Fig. 8.7(A), and at point E* when S = 1, as in Fig. 8.7(B\n358\nShort Run Equilibrium Output\nAD = C + I\nY (Income)/GDP\n(B)\nObservation:\n• Equality between AS and AD at point E\n[in Fig. 8.7(A)] implies the equality betweer\nSand I at point E* [in Fig. 8.7(B)].\n• There is one and only one equilibrium GDP\nwhen AS = AD or when S = 1. In either case,\nthe equilibrium GDP = OL\nY (Income)/GDP\nFig. 8.7: Simultaneous Equality between AS and AD as well as between S and I\n• OL is the equilibrium level of income. It is to be noted that the equality between AS and AD implies the\nequality between Sand I; and equality between Sand I implies the equality between AS and AD.\n• Accordingly, there is one and only one level of equilibrium income when AS = AD, and S = I.\nAVLR 8.1\nEX-ANTE AND EX-POST ASPECTS OF EQUALITY BETWEEN AD & AS\nAND BETWEEN S & I\nGiven below are some numerical illustrations related to the estimations of C, S, I andY.\nTACKLING LIST OF C, S, I AND Y\nlustration 5. Find equilibrium S and equilibrium I when: Y = 4,400, MPC = 0.75, and C = 100.\nEquilibrium is struck when:\nY = C+ I\nOr, when\nCat equilibrium:\nS = I\nC = C + MPC (Y)\n=100+0.75(4,400)\n= 100 + 3,300 = 3,400\nWe know,\nMPS = 1 - MPC = 1 - 0.75 = 0.25\nThus, S at equilibrium: S = -100 + 0.25 (4,400)\n(Note that, 5 = -C= - 100), i.., Dissavings = (Negative of autonomous consumption).]\n= -100 + 1,100 = 1,000\nAlternatively:\nY = C+S\nS = Y - C= 4,400 - 3,400 = 1,000\nIn equilibrium,\nWe know,\nAccordingly,\nS = 1\nS = 1,000\nI = 1,000.\n359","image":"pages/IMG_0577.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS","page":"IMG_0578","text":"Investment Multiplier or Output Multiplier\nInvestment multiplier or output multiplier refers to the 'number of times by which the increase in output/\nincome (AY) exceeds the increase in investment (AI)'. It is measured as the ratio between change in\noutput/income and change in investment.\n\"\nHere, K= Multiplier; AY = Change in output/income;\nAI = Change in investment.\nIllustration.\nIfinvestment increases by * 15 crore and as a consequence, income increases by & 60 crore, it implies\nthat increase in income (AY) is 4 times the increase in investment (AL). Accordingly, multiplier (K) = 4.\nK=\n= 4\n8.5.2 Relationship between Multiplier and\nMPC (Marginal Propensity to Consume)\n• There is a direct relationship between multiplier and MPC. Higher the value of MPC, higher the\nmultiplier and vice versa.\nin fact, multiplier is often estimated with reference to MPC, as under:\nK= 1 - MPC\n361","image":"pages/IMG_0578.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS","page":"IMG_0579","text":"How do we find that K= 1-MPC?\nWe know that,\nK=-\nWe also know that,\nAccordingly,\nY= C+I\nAY = C + AI\n(in a state of equilibrium)\n(Here, AI = Change in investment; AY = Change in income; and 4C = Change in consumption.)\nPutting the value of Al in equation (i), we get\nAY - AC\nDividing right hand side of the equation by AY,\nor K= 1 - MPC\n(MPC - AN\nSince MPC + MPS = 1, or MPS = 1 - MPC, we can also write that,\nK = MPS\nImplying that multiplier is the reciprocal of marginal propensity to save. Higher the value of MPS, lower\nthe value of K.\nLogic behind the Direct Relationship between MPC and Multiplier\n• Additional investment (AI) means additional expenditure in the economy; additional expenditure\nmeans additional income (AY) in the economy.\n• Thus, if AI = 100, then AY = 100, when investment expenditure is incurred. Let us take this AY as\nincrease in income in the round-1.\n• This AY (= 100) would be split into AC and 1S. Because a part of income is spent and a part of it\nis saved.\n• In round-2, AC would be converted into AY (because AC is expenditure and expenditure causes\nincome).\nHere, comes an important point:\nHigher value of MPC would mean higher AC.\n362\nShort Run Equilibrium Output\nExample:\n1FMPC = 0.4, then AC = 0.4 100) = 40.\nIFMPC=0.6, then AC=0.6(100) = 60.\nwould mean higher AY.\nAccordingly, AY in round-2 (which is equal to AC) would depend on the value of MPC. Higher MPC\n, Conversion of 4C into AY continues in various rounds. And, in all the rounds, higher MPC would\ncause higher AC and therefore, higher AY.\n• Hence the conclusion that, higher the value of MPC, higher is the generation of income caused by a\ngiven increase in investment. Or that, higher the MPC, higher is the value of investment multiplier.\nNote: The section on multiplier mechanism should explain this point further.]\n8.5.3 Relationship between Multiplier and MPS\n(Marginal Propensity to Save)\n• There is an indirect relationship between multiplier and MPS. Higher the value of MPS, lower the\nmultiplier and vice versa.\n• Multiplier is estimated with reference to MPS, as under:\nK=•\nMPS\nIllustration.\nIf MPS = 0.4,\nK=\n= 2.5\nAnd if MPS = 0.8,\nK=\n0.8\n=1.25\nThus, increase in MPS implies fall in the value of multiplier.\nFormulae of Multiplier (K)\nK=•\nK=1 - MPC\nK = MPS\nMPC\nMPS\nMultiplier (K) = T-MPC * MPS\n0.25\n0.75\n1-0.25 * 0.75 = 1.33\n0.50\n0.50\n0.75\n0.25\n1-0.50 0.50 * 2\n1-0.75 0.25 * 4\n11 070\n363","image":"pages/IMG_0579.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"IMG_0582","text":"Q. 1. Briefly explain the multiplier process.\nAns. The working of the multiplier assumes the following process:\nChange in\nInvestment\nChange in\nIncome\nChange in\nConsumption\nChange in\nincome\n»ДУ -\n(i) Change in investment implies additional expenditure which leads to change in income. As\na result, there is change in consumption. Consumption expenditure of one person is an\nincome of the other. Hence, change in consumption leads to change in income. This process\ncontinues till AC reduces to zero.\n(ii) MPC is the core factor in the process of income generation. Higher the MPC, greater is the\nconversion of income into consumption expenditure.\n(i) Accordingly, greater is the generation of income. Because, ultimately it is expenditure which\nis converted into income. Expenditure is an injection into the income generation process,\nsaving is a leakage.\nQ. 2. What will be the value of multiplier if entire additional income is converted into additional\nconsumption?\nAns. In such a situation, AC = AY (or change in consumption = change in income or that\nAccordingly, K (or multiplier) would be:\nK=1- MPC*1-1-1=∞\nThe multiplier value would tend towards infinity.","image":"pages/IMG_0582.jpg"},{"title":"ABILITY ZONE","page":"IMG_0582","text":"Concept of AED (Aggregate Effective Demand)\n• AED refers to that level of AD where AS = AD. Thus, AED always corresponds to the equilibrium level\nof income in the economy.\n• It is called 'effective' as it is this level of AD which actually determines the equilibrium between AS and Al\nAS just coincides with AD, because AS is assumed to be perfectly elastic.\n368\nCheck Fig. 8.9 for further illustration.\n• Observations:\n• Eis the point of equilibrium where AS = AD.\n• EL is the level of demand where AS coincides with AD,\nand the equilibrium GDP is achieved.\n• EL, therefore, is AED (aggregate effective demand).\nAD function shows different levels of aggregate demand.\nBut it is only at point E that AS = AD. At point E, level of\naggregate demand = EL. Hence, EL is aggregate effective\ndemand, which is effective in striking an equilibrium between\nAS and AD. As regards a schedule indicating AED, students\nare advised to see Table 8.1, page 354. In this table,\nAED = 100, where AD = C + I and the economy is in a state\nof equilibrium.\nShort Run Equilibrium Output\nAggregate Demand (AD)\n(= AS\nY (Income)/GDP\nFig. 8.9: Aggregate Effective Demand","image":"pages/IMG_0582.jpg"},{"title":"Power Points [Summary of the Chapter]","page":"IMG_0582","text":"• Equilibrium Output implies equilibrium income as well as equilibrium employment. It also implies\nequilibrium GDP when we are considering a closed economy\n• Equilibrium is Struck when AS = AD (implying that planned output = planned demand). Or, when S = 1\n(implying that planned saving = planned investment).\n• Equilibrium Situation is that situation when what is planned to be produced is exactly equal to what\nplanned to be purchased during a given period of time. The producers do not suffer:\n> the loss due to excess stocks, or\n› the loss due to lack of stocks. When AS = AD: actual stocks (with producers) = desired stocks (with\nproducers).\n• Adjustment Mechanism: When AS > AD or when AD > AS, the equilibrium is restored through changes\nin AS (planned output), as it is assumed to be perfectly elastic, owing to the existence of excess\ncapacity in the economy.\n• Shift in Equilibrium occurs when additional investment causes increase in the level of income through an\nupward shift in AD.\n• Additional investment has a multiplier effect on the level of income. Additional investment causes a\npositive multiplier effect on the level of income.\n• Investment Multiplier is the ratio between change in income and change in investment.\nK=\n(Here, K= Multiplier; AY = Change in income; AI = Change in investment.)\n• Relation between Multiplier and MPC: Multiplier is positively related to MPC (or inversely related to MPS).\nHigher the MPC, higher the multiplier and smaller the MPC, smaller the multiplier will be. Thus,\nK= 1-MPC MPS\n• Forward and Backward Action of the Multiplier: Investment multiplier works both ways, positive\nand negative.\n* Additional investment causes multiple increase in income. This is torward action of the multiplie\n* Decrease in investment causes a multiple decrease in income. This is backward action of the multiplier\n369","image":"pages/IMG_0582.jpg"}],"subject":"macro","number":"8"},{"id":"9","title":"Problem of Deficient Demand and Excess Demand","intro":"Full employment means those able and willing to work at the existing wage obtain work.","lessons":[{"title":"Full employment and unemployment","body":"Full employment means those able and willing to work at the existing wage obtain work. Voluntary unemployment reflects unwillingness to work at the existing wage; involuntary unemployment means willing workers cannot find jobs. The textbook also distinguishes natural unemployment: frictional unemployment during job transitions and structural unemployment caused by changing industries, skills or production techniques. Underemployment equilibrium has AD = AS with unutilised capacity.","formula":"","source":"IMG_0583","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"9.1 SOME CRITICAL CONCEPTS"},{"title":"Deficient demand and deflationary gap","body":"Deficient demand occurs when aggregate demand is less than the aggregate supply corresponding to full employment. The deflationary gap is the shortfall of AD from the level required to maintain full employment; measure it vertically at full-employment output. It reduces output and employment and creates involuntary unemployment. The gap in expenditure is not the same as the shortfall in equilibrium income: the multiplier links the two.","formula":"Deflationary gap = Yf − AD at Yf\nRequired increase in autonomous expenditure = (Yf − Y*) ÷ k","source":"IMG_0584","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"DEFICIENT DEMAND · Deflationary Gap"},{"title":"Excess demand and inflationary gap","body":"Excess demand occurs when AD exceeds the demand required to sustain full employment. The inflationary gap is the excess of AD over full-employment AS, measured at Yf. Once resources are fully employed, real output cannot expand in the simple model; excess spending raises the general price level. A calculated equilibrium above Yf is a signal of inflationary pressure, not evidence that the economy can actually produce beyond its capacity.","formula":"Inflationary gap = AD at Yf − Yf","source":"IMG_0585","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"EXCESS DEMAND · Inflationary Gap"},{"title":"Fiscal measures: expenditure, taxation and borrowing","body":"To correct deficient demand, raise government expenditure, reduce taxes, reduce public borrowing and increase borrowing from the central bank, following the textbook framework. These measures raise spending power or public expenditure. For excess demand, reverse the direction. Explain the chain: lower taxes → higher disposable income → more consumption → higher AD. Borrowing from the public can withdraw purchasing power; borrowing from the central bank can expand it.","formula":"","source":"IMG_0586","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"Fiscal Policy"},{"title":"Monetary measures: cost and availability of credit","body":"For deficient demand, lower bank/repo/reverse-repo rates, lower CRR and SLR, buy securities, lower margins, relax credit rationing and encourage lending. For excess demand, raise rates and reserve requirements, sell securities, raise margins and restrict lending. Monetary policy is the central bank’s policy; fiscal policy is the government’s revenue and expenditure policy. Always connect the instrument to credit and then to aggregate demand.","formula":"","source":"IMG_0592","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"Monetary Policy"}],"pages":[{"id":"IMG_0583","image":"pages/IMG_0583.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\n9.1 SOME CRITICAL CONCEPTS\nproblem of deficient demand and excess demand. These concepts are:\nKnowledge of some critical cong towork essential before we take up a detailed description of the\n9.1.1 Full employment & underemployment equilibrium,\n9.1.2 Voluntary & involuntary unemployment, and\n9.1.3 Full employment & natural unemployment.\n9.1.1 Full Employment and Underemployment Equilibrium\n• Equilibrium is struck when AD = AS or when S = l. But, It need not necessarily correspond to\nmay be equal even when resources are not tury utilised or when there is unemploy tell in the\neconomy.\nLet us assume that fuller utilisation of the resources leads to output worth 10 billion US dollars\nduring an accounting year. But, the producers may plan output and the households may\nplan expenditure on output worth 8 billion US dollars only. So that, resources worth 2 billion\nUS dollars of output remain unutilised. But, the equilibrium is struck simply because planned\noutput (AS) and planned expenditure on output (AD) are equal (= 8 billion US dollars).\nThus, we can think of two possible situations of equilibrium:\n(i) Full Employment Equilibrium\n• Full employment equilibrium refers to that situation in the economy when AS = AD (or S = 1)\nalong with fuller utilisation of resources. So that there is no excess capacity or unemployment\nin the economy.\n• With reference to the above illustration, full employment equilibrium is struck when\nplanned AS = planned AD = 10 billion US dollars.\n(ii) Underemployment equilibrium\n• Underemployment equilibrium refers to that situation in the economy when AS = AD (or S= 1) but\nwithout fuller utilisation of resources. Accordingly, there is unutilised capacity or excess capacity\n(or unemployment) in the economy even in a state of equilibrium.\n• With reference to the above illustration, underemployment equilibrium is struck when\nplanned AS = planned AD = 8 billion US dollars.\n9.1.2 Voluntary and Involuntary Unemployment\n• Voluntary unemployment refers to a situation when people choose to remain unemployed, even\nwhen jobs are available. They may not be willing to work at all, or not willing to work at the\nexisting wage rate.\n• involuntary unemployment refers to a situation when some people are not getting work, even\nwhen they are willing to work at the existing wage rate.\nProblem of Deficient Demand and Excess Demand\n• It is a situation when AD is not enough to induce fuller utilisation of the existing resources.\npeople are forced to remain unemployed\npotential output (full employment output). There is excess capacity in the economy, and some\n• Problem of unemployment refers to the problem of involuntary unemployment, not the problem\nof voluntary unemployment.\n•If all those who are willing to supply of (and able to work) at the existing wage rate are getting\nvoluntary unemployment in the economy.\nwork, the economy would be deerk, evto be in a state of full employment even when there is\n9.1.3 Full Employment and Natural Unemployment\n, Full employment refers to a situation when all those who are able to work and are willing to work\n(at the existing wage rate) are getting work.\n• It is a situation when, corresponding to a given wage rate, demand for labour (D) = supply of\nlabour (s), and the labour market is cleared it is in a state of equilibrium).\nSituation of Full Employment does not mean\na Situation of Zero (Involuntary) Unemployment\n• Students of economics must carefully note that in the economy, a situation of full employment\nnever implies a situation of 'zero (involuntary) unemployment'.\n• There always exists some degree of unemployment, called 'natural unemployment\". It is the\nminimum rate of unemployment that must always exist in the economy.\n• Natural unemployment occurs owing to the fact that there are constant changes in the\nsupply-demand parameters in the economy, and adjustment to these changes takes time. While\nadjustments are occurring, some people continue to remain unemployed.\n• Natural unemployment is a situation of 'frictional and structural' unemployment in the\neconomy.\n• Frictional Unemployment: In the words of Gardner,\n\"Frictional unemployment is the\nunemployment associated with the changing of jobs in dynamic economy.\"\n• It arises due to immobility of labour, shortage of raw material, lack of information regarding\nopportunities of employment, shortage of power, wear and tear of machines, tendency of the\nworkers to move from one job to the other, etc.\n• Structural Unemployment: In the words of Gardner,\n\"Structural unemployment is the\nunemployment that results from the long-term decline of certain industries.\"\nIt is sociated with the structural changes in the economy due to these situations.\n• when other factors of production (other than labour) are in short supply.\n• when labourers lack expertise for new emerging industries, and\n• when there is change in the production technique.\n423","file":"macro 9/IMG_0583.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0584","image":"pages/IMG_0584.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\nQ. 1. What is natural rate of unemployment?\nAns. It refers to the rate of unemplature is it which always exists in the economy even when labou\nmarket is in a state of equilibrium. It occurs due to:\n(i) rictional changes (changes related to shifting from one job to the other), and\n(il) structural changes (changes related to new production technology).\nFrictional changes lead to triconal unemployment, while structural changes lead to structura\nunemployment.\n(Note: Frictional unemployment and structural unemployment are the two variants\nunemployment.]\nof natural\n9.2 PROBLEM OF DEFICIENT DEMAND\n9.2.1 Concept of Deficient Demand\n• Deficient demand means deficiency of AD. Demand is said to be 'deficient' when it is lower than\nwhat is required for the fuller utilisation of resources.\n• AD is deficient when it does not permit fuller utilisation of production capacity. Or, AD is deficient\nwhen there is excess capacity in the economy (implying underutilisation of resources). According\nto Keynes, AD is deficient when it is less than AS corresponding to full employment in the economy.\nDeficient Demand = AD < AS (corresponding to full employment in the economy)\nAD falls short of its full employment level\nFig. 9.1 reflects measurement of deficient demand.\nY = AS\nAD- (Full\nEmployment AD)\nDeficient Demand\nADp (Planned AD)\nAggregate Demand (AD)\n45°\nObservations:\nIn Fig. 9.1:\n• AD_ line shows the required\nlevel of AD for full employment\nin the economy.\n• ADp line shows planned AD\nwhich is lower than the full\nemployment AD.\n• The vertical difference between\nADp and ADp\n= FE\n= ADF -ADp\n= Deficient demand.\nY (Income)/GDP\n* X\nFig. 9.1: Measurement of Deficient Demand\n424\n9.2.3 Causes of Deficient Demand\nProblem of Deficient Demand and Excess Demand\nin a two sector closed economy, deficiency of aggregate demand (AD) occurs largely due to two\n(1) Reduction in Private Consumptiousinessenditure (C): Private consumption expenditure is an\nimportant component of AD. Kilowction in private consumption expenditure causes a serious\ndeficiency in AD.\n• Private consumption expenditure may reauce owing to several reasons.\n• However, the most implendant is reduction in propersity io consume or increase i\npropensity to save. In davald isa economies, MPs tends to rise wiel consumption reaches\nits peak.\n(2) Reduction in Private Investment Expenditure (I): Private investment expenditure is\nthe otner important component of AD. It may reauce in situations or poor business\nexpectations.\n• It has happened in all market economies of the world in the year 2019-20 during COVID-19\npandemic.\nIn four sector open economy, the following factors may also contribute to the deficiency of AD:\n(1) Reduction in Government Expenditure (G): The government may cut its consumption\nexpenditure or investment expenditure. This may be because of the budgetary constraints\nof the government.\n• A cut in government consumption expenditure or investment expenditure leads to a cut\nin AD.\n(2) Decline in Exports (X): We know, exports are an important component of AD. A fall in exports\nimplies a fall in expenditure on the domestically produced goods and services. This leads to\na fall in AD.\n(3) Rise in Imports (M): Imports may rise when international prices are lower than the domestic\nprices. Imports are a negative component of AD. Accordingly, a rise in imports leads to a\nfall in AD.\n(4) Increase in Tax Rates: Increase in tax rates leaves lesser disposable income with the people.\nIt reduces their capacity to spend, even when their propensity to spend remains the same.\nLower disposable income means lower level of AD.\nBriefly, we can say that the deficiency of demand is a situation when planned AD is lower than its ful\nemployment level. It is indicated by a downward shift in AD function.\nThe possible reasons are:\n(i) a reduction in C,\n(i) a reduction in G,\n(v) a rise in M, and\n(ii) a reduction in I,\n(iv) a reduction in X,\n(vi) increase in taxation.\n425","file":"macro 9/IMG_0584.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0585","image":"pages/IMG_0585.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\n9.2.4 Consequences of Deficient Demand:\nFour Critical Situations in the Economy\nDeficient demand leads to four critical situations in the economy, as under:\n(1) Underemployment Equilibrium\n• Deficient demand leads to underemployment equilibrium.\n• Owing to the denciency or Au, promo•s are not able to fully unlise their resources\n(production capacity). Planored output remains less than the potential output output\ncorresponding tofuller utilisation sources). Accordingly, a situation of underemployment\nequilibrium arises in the ereciomy. In this situanon, equilibrium between AS and AD is\nstruck at a lower level of GDP, lower than the full employment level.\nFig. 9.2 illustrates this situation.\nAggregate Demand (AD)\nAD- (Full\nEmployment AD)\nDeficient\nDemand, ADp (Planned AD)\nPoint of\nUnderemployment\nEquilibrium\n,Underemployment\nEquilibrium GDP\nObservations:\nIn Fig. 9.2:\n• Deficient demand = FE.\n• Because of the deficiency of\ndemand, equilibrium is struck at\npoint Q where ADp line intersects\nthe 45° AS line. This is\nunderemployment equilibrium.\n• Equilibrium GDP (corresponding\nto underemployment equilibrium)\n= ON.\nFull Employment\nEquilibrium GDP\nUnderemployment\nequilibrium GDP\nFull employment\n(or potential GDP)\nM\nY (Income)/GDP\nFig. 9.2: Underemployment Equilibrium\n(2) Deflationary Gap\n• Deficiency of demand creates 'deflationary gap' in the economy. Deflationary gap is\na situation when lack of demand leads to deflationary pressures in the economy.\nInducement to invest is hurt. Low investment leads to low output. Implying low income,\nand low demand once again. The economy starts slipping into 'deflationary spiral\", or low\nlevel equilibrium trap'.\nMeasurement of Deflationary Gap\n• Deflationary gap is measured in terms of deficiency of demand. We can say that:\nDeflationary Gap = Deficiency of AD\n• Deflationary gap is measured as the difference between 'AD corresponding to full\nemployment and 'planned AD which is lower than the full employment AD'.\nas we dent ats tw demand leading to low investment\nand low level of GDP) tends to repeat itself. This is also called a situation of 'low level equilibrium trap.\n426\nFig. 9.3 illustrates this situation.\nProblem of Deficient Demand and Excess Demand\nAD: (Full\nEmployment AD)\nAD, (Planned AD)\nAggregate Demand LAD)\nObservation:\nIn Fig. 9.3.\n• Deflationary Gap\n= Deficient demand\nDeficient Demand\n= Deflationary Gap\nY (Income)/GDP\nFig. 9.3: Measurement of Deflationary Gap\n(3) Undesired Stocks\nmpact of Deficient Di\n• In a situation of low AD, producers are not able to sell all that • On Output: Planned\nthey plan to sell (or wish to sell). Accordingly, undesired stocks, on Employment:\noutput tends to decrease.\ntend to pile up.\nInvoluntary unemployment\n• Clearance of undesired stocks often lead to:\ntends to rise.\n(i) fall in prices, and (ii) fall in planned output for the year\n• On General Price Level:\nPrice level tends to fall.\nahead. Both these factors contribute to deflationary spiral • On TradeCycle*: Deficient\nin the economy.\ndemand generally lead to\n(4) Loss of Profits\na deflatinary gap in the\nесопоту.\n• Deficiency of AD causes loss of profits. This happens because: (i) producers are not able to\nclear their stocks, and (i) undesired stocks lead to 'price crash'\nBriefly, deficiency of demand hurts business expectations. Low business expectations (or bearish\nbusiness expectations) lead to low investment, and low level of income and employment.\nOverall level of economic activity reduces and the economy is caught in a low level equilibrium trap,\nwhere low demand leads to low income and low income leads to low demand.\n9.3 PROBLEM OF EXCESS DEMAND\n9.3.1 Concept of Excess Demand\n• Excess demand means excess of AD. Demand is said to be 'excess' when it is more than what is\nrequired for the fuller utilisation of resources. According to Keynes, AD is excess when it is more\nthan AS corresponding to full employment in the economy.\n2. Trade cycle (also called business cycle) - a situation of recurring 'up & down' in the level of economic activity\nso that, there is a phase of 'expansion of economic activity, leading to the phase of peak of economic actvity.\nAnd subsequently, there is a phase of 'contraction of economic activity' leading to the phase of trough' (which\nis the lowest level of economic activity). This is a repetitive process, and therefore called 'cycle","file":"macro 9/IMG_0585.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0586","image":"pages/IMG_0586.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\nExcess Demand = AD > AS (corresponding to full employment in the economy)\nAD crosses its full employment level\n9.3.2 Measurement of Excess Demand: Diagrammatic Illustration\nFig. 9.4 reflects measurement of excess demand.\nADp (Planned AD)\n•-AD= (Full\nEmployment AD)\nExcess Demand\nAggregate Demand (AD)\nObservations:\nIn Fig. 9.4:\n• AD line shows the required level\nof AD for full employment in the\neconomy.\n• ADp line shows planned AD\nwhich is higher than the full\nemployment AD.\n• The vertical difference between\nADp and ADF = EF\n= ADp - AD\n= Excess demand\nM\nY (Income)/GDP\nFig. 9.4: Measurement of Excess Demand\n9.3.3 Causes of Excess Demand\nCauses of excess demand are almost opposite to those of deficient demand. Briefly, there are two\nprincipal causes of excess AD in a two sector closed economy:\n(1) Increase in Private Consumption Expenditure (C): This may occur owing to increase in\npropensity to consume or decrease in propensity to save. In countries like India, propensity\nto consume has tended to rise owing to a high degree of demonstration effect?\n(2) Increase in Private Investment Expenditure (I): This may occur owing to bullish business\nexpectations. (Business expectations are 'bullish' when the producers expect high returns\nfrom their investment. Business expectations are 'bearish' when the producers do not expect\nhigh returns from their investment.)\nIn an open economy with government, there are following additional causes of excess AD:\n(1) Increase in Government Expenditure (G): The government may increase its consumption\nexpenditure owing to its expanding commitments for the development as well as welfare\nprojects in the economy.\n(2) Increase in Exports (X): This is majorly owing to lower domestic prices in relation to\ninternational prices.\n(3) Decrease in Imports (M): Fall in imports due to higher international prices compared with\ndomestic prices.\n(4) A Cut in Tax Rates: Fall in tax rates leaves higher disposable income with the people.\n3. Demonstration effect - refers to urge of the poor to adopt the expensive lifestyle of the rich. Consequently.\npropensity to consume rises.\n428\n9,3.4 Consequences of Excess Demand:\nProblem of Deficient Demand and Excess Demand\nFour Critical Situations in the Economy\nExcess demand leads to four critical situations in the economy, as under:\n(1) Inflationary Gap\n• Excess demand causes inflationary sap in the economy. In a situation of excess demand\nthe level or output aoes er frise. In tact, it cannot rise because factors are already full\nemployed (and technology is assumed to remain constant).\nexcess demana generates pressure or demand on the exisung resources\n• Accordingly, COST Or pi4 GDP doesenas to rise, which leads to a rise in the general pric\nlevel in the economy. keynes identifies this situation as a situanon or intationary gap'\nin the economy.\nExcess AD → Pressure of demand on the existing resources → Rise in cost of production →\nRise in general price level → Output level remains constant because resources are already\nfully employed\nMeasurement of Inflationary Gap\n• Inflationary gap is measured in terms of 'excess demand\". We can say that:\nInflationary Gap = Excess demand (or Excess AD)\n• Inflationary gap is measured as the difference between 'planned AD which is beyond full\nemployment level' and 'AD that corresponds to full employment.\nFig. 9.5 illustrates this situation.\nAggregate Demand (AD)\n•ADp (Planned AD)\n•AD= (Full\nEmployment AD)\nExcess Demand\n= Inflationary Gap\nObservation:\nIn Fig. 9.5:\n• Inflationary Gap\n= Excess demand\n* X\n=\nY (Income)/GDP\nFig. 9.5: Measurement of Inflationary Gap\n(2) Static GDP\n• Even when the level of AD is higher than its full employment level, the level of GDP does\nnot rise. It remains static.\n• Static GDP is a situation when higher demand fails to generate higher GDP in the\neconomy. This is because resources are already fully employed: there is no excess capacity\nin the economy.\n429","file":"macro 9/IMG_0586.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0587","image":"pages/IMG_0587.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\nFig. 9,6 offers diagrammatic illustration or excess demand and static GDP.\nY = AS\nADp (Planned AD)\nAD (Full\nEmployment AD)\nAggregate Demand (AD)\nObservations:\nIn Fig. 9.6:\n• Excess Demand = EF\n• GDP = OL, when AD corresponds\nto full employment\n• GDP = OL, even when AD is\nhigher than its full employment\nlevel\n430\n45°\nY (Income)/GDP\nFig. 9.6: Excess Demand and Static GDP\n• Fig. 9.6 shows that the equilibrium GDP remains constant (= OL), and at full employment\nlevel, even when AD shifts from ADF to ADP. Thus, GDP remains unimpacted by excess demand.\nHere, comes an important observation:\n• When we say that GDP remains constant, we are referring to real GDP, not the nominal\nGDP.\n• In other words, we are referring to GDP at constant prices, not GDP at current prices.\nThe fact that excess demand leads to a rise in the general price level implies that GDP at\ncurrent prices will rise.\n• Thus, in a situation of excess demand, quantum of goods and services produced in the\neconomy remains constant, though the market value of goods and services (at the current\nprices) tends to rise.\n(3) Excess Demand and Wage-Price Spiral\n• Excess demand leads to a wage-price spiral in the economy.\nIt is a situation when wages catch prices and prices catch wages.\nAccordingly, the economy may be driven to a situation of hyper\ninflation (inflation of very high magnitude.\nWage-Price Spiral → Owing to excess demand, there is pressure\nof demand on the existing resources.\nConsequently, cost of production (wages in particular) tends\nto rise → Rise in the general price level → Rise in cost of living\n→ Rise in wages again\nThus, wages catch prices, and prices catch wages. This spiral, if\nturns into hyper inflation, becomes a serious threat to economic\nstability.\nImpact of Excess Demand\n• On Output: No change\nin output (as economy is\nalready operating at full\nemployment).\n• On Employment: No\nchange in employment\n(as economy is already\noperating at full\nemployment).\n• On General Price Level:\nRise in prices due to\nincrease in demand.\n• On Trade Cycle:\nExcess demand leads to\ninflationary gap in\nthe economy.\n(4) Loss of Profit\n• Like deficient demand, excess demand also causes loss of profit. Profit is lost. because\n(owing to the lack of excess capacity), the producers are not able to raise their supplies. in\nother words, there is a loss of profit owing to unfulfilled demand in the economy.\nprefly, excess demand is not virtuous, but vicious. It generates inflationary spiral in the economy.\nProblem of Deficient Demand and Excess Demand\nDevoid of any rise in GDP, excess AD generates pressure of demand on the existing resources.\nAccordingly, cost of production (particularly wage rate) rises. Rise in cost leads to rise in prices.\nthe economy is caught in the wage-price spiral', which impairs stability of the economy.\nTable 9.1: Inflationary Gap vs. Deflationary Gap\nfusis of Difference\nInflationary Gap\n- It is the excess of AD over and above its\nDeflationary Gap\n1. Meaning\nlevel required to maintain full employment\nequilibrium in the economy.\n- It is the deficiency of AD from the level\nrequired to maintain full employment\nequilibrium in the economy.\n2 Cause\n- It occurs when AD tends to rise even when the - it occurs when AD tends to fall en when\nlevel of income is constant.\nthe level of income is constant.\n3. Impact on\n- Planned output does not rise, as it already - Planned output tends to decrease because\ncorresponds to full employment in the economy.\nof lack of demand.\n- There is no unemployment or under-employment\n4. Impact on\nEmployment\nin the economy because the output level\n- Unemployment occurs alongwith the fall in\noutput.\nremains constant.\n5. Overall Impact\n- It leads to wage-price spiral: wages catch\nprices and prices catch wages, while real GDP\nremains constant.\n- It leads to low level equilibrium trap: low AD\nleads to low income, and low income leads\nto low AD.\nAVLR 9.1\nEXCESS DEMAND vS. DEFICIENT DEMAND\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\nQ.2. How does propensity to consume affect AD-function?\nAns. Increase in the propensity to consume causes an upward shift in the AD-function (through an\nupward shift in the C-function). On the other hand, decrease in the propensity to consume\ncauses a downward shift in AD-function (through a downward shift in C-function).\nQ. 3. According to Keynes, price level rises only after full employment. Why?\nAns. Price level does not rise before full employment, because AS is assumed to be perfectly elastic.\nPrior to full employment, AS tends to rise proportionate to any rise in AD. Accordingly, price level\nremains constant. But after full employment is reached, AS stops rising in response to a rise in\nAD. Accordingly, price level tends to rise.\n94 MEASURES TO CORRECT DEFICIENT AND\nEXCESS DEMAND: FISCAL AND MONETARY POLICIES\n• Economic stability requires that the situations of excess demand and dencient demand are\n• Who does it? The government does it through its revenue-expenditure policy (Fiscal Policy),\ncorrected as fast as possible.\nand the Central Bank (RBI) does it through its monetary policy. This bring us to the discussion or\nfiscal and monetary policies.\n431","file":"macro 9/IMG_0587.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0588","image":"pages/IMG_0588.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\nLet us understand how these policies are used to combat inflationary gap (related to excess demand\nand deflationary gap (related to deficient demand).\n9.4.1 Fiscal Policy (Fiscal Measures)\n• Fiscal policy refers to revenue and expenalture policy of the government. It is also calle\nBudgetary Policy of the government.\n• It focuses on stability of the economy by correcting the situations or excess demand (inflationar\ngap) and deficient demand (deflationary gap).\nComponents of Fiscal Policy and the Way these are Used\nFollowing are the principal components of fiscal policy. Along with each component, we are\ndescribing the way it is used to correct the situations of excess and deficient demand.\n(1) Government Expenditure: It is the principal component (or principal instrument) of fiscal\npolicy. The government of a country incurs various types of expenditure, mainly:\n(i) Expenditure on public works programmes such as the construction of roads, dams,\nbridges, etc.\n(ii) Expenditure on education and public welfare programmes.\n(iii) Expenditure on the defence of the country and the maintenance of law & order.\n(iv) Expenditure on various types of subsidies to the producers with a view to encourage\nproduction.\n• It is by changing any or all types of expenditure that the government seeks to correct\nthe situations of excess demand or deficient demand in the economy. When there is\nexcess demand, government expenditure is reduced, and when there is deficient demand,\ngovernment expenditure is increased.\n• A rise in government expenditure acts as an \"injection' into the circular flow of income in\nthe economy. It is required when liquidity needs to be released to combat deflation.\n• Likewise, a cut in government expenditure acts like a 'withdrawal' from the circular flow of\nincome in the economy. It is required when liquidity needs to be soaked to combat inflation.\n(2) Taxes: Taxes are a compulsory payment made to government by the household.\n• By increasing the tax burden on the households, the government reduces their disposable\nincome. Accordingly, AD is reduced or excess demand is managed.\n• On the other hand, by lowering the tax burden, the government increases disposable\nincome of the households. Accordingly, AD is raised and deficient demand is managed.\n(3) Public Borrowing/Public Debt: By borrowing from the public, the government creates public\ndebt.\n• In a situation of deficient demand (or when AD needs to be increased), the government\nreduces its borrowing from the public. So that people are left with greater liquidity (or cash\nbalances) and aggregate expenditure remains high.\n• On the other hand, when there is a situation of excess demand (or when AD needs to be\nreduced, the government steps up public borrowing by offering attractive rate of interest.\nThis reduces liquidity with the people. Accordingly, aggregate expenditure also reduces\nand excess demand is managed.\n432\n(4) Borrowing from RBI (th cutCental Bank): Borrowing by the government from the RBI is\nProblem of Deficient Demand and Excess Demand\nanother element or fiscal policy, as increased to night deflationary gap, and reduced to fight\ncorrect deflationary gap (deficient demand).\ninflation sap. highing frowing eleases greater liquidity in the economy, as required to\n• When borrowing is reto saced, the amount of liquidity in the economy is also reduced, as\ndesired to correct intlationary gap (excess demand) in the economy.\n/CRITICAL QUESTIONS\nQ. 4. What fiscal measures would you recommend to correct deficient demand?\nAns. Following fiscal measures are recommended to correct deficient demand:\ni The govern secred step up its expenditure [on a public works programmes,\n(b) education and public welfate, (c) subsidies to the producers, and (d) defence and\nlaw & order]. Higher government expenditure acts like an injection into the circular flow of\nincome in the economy.\ni) The government should reduce tax burden on the households, so that they are left with\ngreater cash balances. Higher cash balances with the people lead to higher level of AD.\n(i) The government should raise more funds from the RBI so that there is a greater flow of\nliquidity in the economy. Higher liquidity implies higher level of AD.\n(iv) The government should plan a cut in public borrowing, so that, people are left with greater\nliquidity (or cash balances). Greater cash balances implies higher AD.\nBriefly, deficient demand is corrected when the government steps up its own expenditure and\nensures that cash balances with the people are raised.\n(Note: Students are advised to write just an opposite answer in case the question relates to excess demand.!\n9.4.2 Monetary Policy (Monetary Measures)\n• Monetary policy refer to that policy which deals with the control of liquidity or flow of credit in\nthe market with a view to managing inflationary or deflationary gaps in the economy.\n• We have already described the components of monetary policy and the way these are used to\ncorrect the situations of excess demand (inflationary gap) and deficient demand (deflationary gap)\nin the economy in chapter 6. Here, we present a brief summary of that description. Application of\nmonetary instruments to correct excess demand and deficient demand is discussed separately.\nas under:\nApplication of Monetary Instruments to Correctthe Situation of Excess Demand\n• Bank rate and repo rate are raised by the RBI. As a follow-up action, market rate of interest Is\nraised by the commercial banks. Cost of borrowing rises. It lowers the demand for credit leas\nto a cut in consumption and investment expenditure in the economy. Implying a cut in AD, as\ndesired to curb excess demand or inflationary gap.\n• Reverse repo rate is raised by the RBI. This induces the commercial banks to park their surplus\nfunds with the RBI. Accordingly, lesser funds are used as CRR-deposits for the creation of credit.\nAvailability of credit is reduced. It leads to a cut in AD. Excess demand or inflationary gap is curbed.\n• Pursuing the policy of open market operations. Securities are sold by the RBl to soak liquidity.\nAccordingly, cash reserves of the commercial banks are reduced, implying a cut in their credit","file":"macro 9/IMG_0588.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0589","image":"pages/IMG_0589.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\ncenn capac ven te avllity or cred tredues, Ao Shis, Accoraingly, excess demand is\n• CRR is raised to lower credit creaton capacity or the commercial banks. Lesser availability of credi\ncauses a fall in AD. Accordingly, excess demand is corrected.\n• SLR (Statutory iqudity kato)isralsedew ad waring signa to the commercal banks to\nmaintain healthy cash reserves with them aad Lesser funds are parked with RBl as CRR reserves.\nSupply of credit is reduced and AD is lowered. Excess demand is corrected.\n• Margın requirement (mınımum down ns •ent that d borrower is required to make as a\npercentage of his loan trom the commer quew nks) is raised to restrict the avallability of credit.\nAccordingly, AD is reduced and excess demand is corrected.\n• Moral pressure is exerted by the RBl on the commercial banks to be selective and strict in lending\nwhen AD needs to be curbed to correct the situation of excess demand or inflationary gap.\n. Credit rationing is introduced, prescribing credit limits for different sectors of the economy.\nThis restricts the availability of credit. Accordingly, AD is lowered and excess demand is corrected.\nBriefly, dear money policy is pursued to curb excess demand. This policy reduces the availability of\ncredit and increases the cost of credit. Lower demand for credit leads to a fall in AD, implying the\ncorrection of excess demand.\nApplication of Monetary Instruments to Correct the\nSituation of Deficient Demand\n• In a situation of deficient demand, AD needs to be raised. The RBl pursues cheap money policy.\nAvailability of credit is raised and cost of credit is lowered. It is achieved by using the montary\ninstruments exactly the opposite way these are used in a situation of excess demand or inflationary\ngap.\nAvoiding repetition, we may state that (for the correction of deficient demand), the availability of\ncredit is raised and the cost of credit is lowered through the following montary actions by the RBI:\n• Bank rate and repo rate are lowered. Accordingly, cost of credit is reduced and availability of\ncredit is increased.\n• Reverse repo rate is lowered, so that the commercial banks are not induced to park their funds\nwith the RBl to generate interest income. Instead, they are prompted to use their funds for the\ncreation of credit.\n• Securities are purchased to inject liquidity into the system. The banks are left with more cash\nfor the creation of credit.\n• CRR is lowered to increase the supply of credit.\n• SLR is lowered to enhance credit creation capacity of the commercial banks.\n• Margin requirement is lowered to raise the availability of credit.\n• Moral pressure is built on the commercial banks to be liberal in lending.\n• Credit rationing, if already in force, is withdrawn to enhance the availability of credit.\nThus, RBl makes every efort to increase the supply of credit and decrease the cost of credt.\nEasy availability of credit raises the demand for credit. Accordingly, AD is raised and deficiency of\ndemand (or deflationary gap) is curbed.\n434\nProblem of Deficient Demand and Excess Demand\nMeasures to Correct Excess Demand\nFiscal Measures\n(Contractionary Fiscal Policy)\n• / Government Expenditure\n• t Taxes\n• t Public Borrrowing/\nPublic Debt\n• t Borrrowing from RBI\n(Central Bank)\nMonetary Measures\n(Dear Money Policy)\nQuantitative Measures\n• 1 Bank rate and Repo rate\n• 1 Reverse repo rate\n• Sale of government\nsecurities in the open\nmarket\n• 1 CRR\n• 1 SLR\nQualitative Measures\n• f Margin requirement\n• Moral pressure for\nselective and strict\nlending\n• Credit rationing\n•I - Decrease\n•t - Increase\nMeasures to Correct Deficient Demand\nFiscal Measures\n(Expansionary Fiscal Policy)\n• 1 Government Expenditure\n• V Taxes\n• V Public Borrrowing/\nPublic Debt\n• 1 Borrrowing from RBI\n(Central Bank)\nMonetary Measures\n(Cheap Money Policy)\n• . - Decrease\n• 1 - Increase\nQuantitative Measures\n• + Bank rate and Repo rate\n• * Reverse repo rate\n• Purchase of government\nsecurities in the open\nmarket\n• * CRR\n• + SLR\nQualitative Measures\n• + Margin requirement\n• Moral pressure for\nliberal lending\n• Withdrawal of credit\nBasis of Difference\n1. Meaning\n2. Control & Direction\n3. Tools\nTable 9.2: Monetary Policy vs. Fiscal Policy\nMonetary Policy\nFiscal Policy\n- It is the policy of correcting excess or\n- It is the policy of correcting excess or deficient\ndeficient demand in the economy by\ndemand in the economy by managing revenue\ncontrolling the supply of credit.\nand expenditure of the government.\n- It is controlled and directed by the central\n- Itis controlled and directed by the goverment\nbank of country (RBl in India).\nof a country.\n- Tools of monetary policy are:\n- Tools of fiscal policy are:\n• bank rate\n• government expenditure\n• taxes\n• repo rate\n• public borrowing\n• reverse repo rate\n• open market operations\n• borrowing from central bank.\n• cash reserve ratio\n• statutory liquidity ratio\n• margin requirement\n• moral suasion and credit rationing.\nAVLR 9.2\nESCAL POLICY VS. MONETARY POLICY\n435","file":"macro 9/IMG_0589.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0590","image":"pages/IMG_0590.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\nABILITY ZONE\nHow Government Expenditure Impacts Equilibrium Income or GDP\n- A Diagrammatic Presentation\n• When the government comes into picture, we are dealing with a three sector economy. It includes:\n(i) household sector,\n(il) the producing sector, and\n(il) the government sector.\n• Accordingly, total expenditure in the economy (or components of AD) would include:\n(i) consumption expenditure (C),\n(i) private investment expenditure (I), and\n(ilI) government expenditure (G).\nEquilibrium Income/GDP in a 3-Sector Economy\n• For the sake of simplicity, we assume government expenditure to be constant (autonomous) like\nprivate investment expenditure. So that (like I-function), the government expenditure is indicated by a\nhorizontal straight line.\n• Fig. 9.7 illustrates the equilibrium income (GDP) in a three sector economy.\nC+ I + G\nObservations:\n• AD now consists of C, I and G.\n• G is assumed to be constant = LT.\nAccordingly, C + I + G is parallel\nto C + I.\n• The vertical distance between\nC+ I + G and C+ I is equal to\nLT = government expenditure.\n• Q is the point of equilibrium.\n• OS is equilibrium level of output\n(GDP).\nY (Income)/GDP\nFig. 9.7: Equilibrium Income (GDP) in a 3-Sector Economy\nGovernment expenditure is assumed to be constant and equal to LT. Accordingly, C+ I function\n(AD without government expenditure) shifts upward to become C + I + G function (AD with government\nexpenditure).\n• The two functions (AD with government expenditure and AD without government expenditure are shown\nas parallel to each other, because G (government expenditure) is assumed to be constant. Equilibrium\nis struck at point Q where C + 1 + G function intersects the 45° line. Here, desired AD = desired AS.\nEquilibrium income (GDP) = OS.\n436\nand Excess Demand?\nHow does the Government Correct the Situations of Deficient Demand\nProblem of Deficient Demand and Excess Demand\n, We consider these situations one by one.\nDeficient AD in a 3-Sector Economy and its Correction by the Government\n* We know, deficient demand is a situation of deflationary gap. The economy is driven into a state of lov\nlevel equilibrium trap. Low AD causes underemployment: tactors of production are not fully employed.\nPlanned output remains low, It leaas to low level of income, because incomes are low, once again there\n, The cycle of low AD and low income becomes repetitive. The market forces fail to break this repetitive\ncycle. The government must step in. It must raise the level of AD by increasing govemment expenditure\ni Fig. 9.8 shows the situation of deficient demand in a s-sector economy. It diso snows how this deficiem\ndemand is corrected through additional government expenditure (AG).\nY = AS\nAD= (C+ I +G+4G)\nADp (C+I+G)\nAG or additional\ngovernment\nexpenditure.\nThis is equal to\ndeflationary gap.\n450\nY (Income)/GDP\nObservations:\n• C+I + G is planned AD corresponding to\nunderemployment.\n• AG is additional government expenditure\nand is equal to deflationary gap.\n• AG is added to C+T + G to increase AD to\nthe level of full employment.\n• AG being equal to ab plugs the deflationary\n• OS is output corresponding to\nunderemployment equilibrium.\n• OT is output corresponding to full\nemployment equilibrium.\nThis is achieved by adding 4G to C+1 + G.\nFig. 9.8: 4G Clears Deflationary Gap (ab)\n• Deficient demand (deflationary gap) is indicated by ab and the government fills up this deficiency by\nmaking additional expenditure exactly equal to ab.\nThe level of AD which earlier was C + I + G (corresponding to underemployment equilibrium) is now\nraised to C + I + G + AG (corresponding to full employment equilibrium). Equilibrium level of output.\nwhich earlier was OS is now raised to OT.\n(• Now, equilibrium is achieved with full employment of the factors of production. Low level equilibrium\ntrap is broken. Thus, 4G acts as an injection in the circular flow of income in the economy.\n• 4G (additional government expenditure) acts as an injection in the circular flow of income\nthe economy, It raises the level of AD to the extent required for full employment of the factors\nof production.\n• The economy breaks the low level equilibrium trap. Actual output = Potential output (- full\nemployment level of output), and the state of deflation and unemployment is corrected\nExcess Demand in a 3-Sector Economy and its Correction by the Government\n• Excess demand is a situation of inflationary gap. The economy is over-heated: AD rises beyond full\nemployment level of AS. The general price level tends to rise, while the level of real output remains\nconstant. Such a situation often pushes the economy into a wage-price spiral, as already noted.\n437","file":"macro 9/IMG_0590.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0591","image":"pages/IMG_0591.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\nThe government intervention becomes essential to check the rising Au, It does it by way of reducin\nits own expenditure.\n• Fig. 9.9 shows the situation of excess demand/inflationary gap in a s-sector economy, and the way it is\neliminated by a cut in government expenditure.\nADp (C+1 +G)\nC,I, G (AD)\nAD: (C+ I+G+AG)\nCut in government expenditure\nto correct excess demand\nObservations:\n• ADp: Planned AD beyond full employment.\n• ADF: Full employment AD.\n• ab : Excess demand.\n• -AG: A cut in government expenditure = ab\n(excess demand).\n• OL: Full employment equilibrium output.\n• Q\nPoint of full employment equilibrium.\nNote: C+ 1 + G add up to AD which is more than its\nrequired level for full employment equilibrium GDP.\nA cut in government expenditure (= -AG) reduces\nthe level of AD to C+ I + G - 4G. It is this level that\nrestores full employment equilibrium GDP in the\neconomy. Excess demand is eliminated.\nY (Income)/GDP\nFig. 9.9: -4G Eliminates Excess Demand (ab)\nOL is full employment equilibrium output. It corresponds to AD which is full employment level of AD.\nHowever, there is excess demand in the economy, as indicated by ADp.\nExcess demand = ADp - AD = ab\n• To eliminate this excess demand, the government reduces its expenditure by ab. Reduction in\ngovernment expenditure is indicated by -AG (= ab). As a result, the level of AD is brought down from\nADp to ADp.\n• The economy is brought back to point Q which is a point of full employment equilibrium. Thus, economic\nstability is restored.\nDiscretionary and Non-discretionary Fiscal Instruments\n• Non-discretionary fiscal instruments refer to those fiscal instruments which start operating automatically\non their own. These are also called auto-stabilisers.\nOn the other hand, discretionary instruments are those instruments which are planned by the government\nto correct the situations of excess and deficient demand. We have already discussed in detail various\ndiscretionary instruments. As regards non-discretionary instruments, some illustrations may prove to be\nuseful.\n• A rise in AD automatically raises tax revenue and a fall in AD automatically decreases tax\nrevenue. Accordingly, taxation acts as an auto-stabiliser in the economy, It lowers AD when\nexcess demand is to be corrected. And, it raises AD when deficient demand is to be corrected.\n• Grants and subsidies are other examples of non-discretionary fiscal instruments or\nauto-stabilisers in the economy. During periods ofboom (when excess AD needs to be corrected),\ngrants and subsidies come down on their own, causing a cut in government expenditure and\nthereby a cut in AD. During periods of depression, on the other hand, grants and subsidies tend\nto rise. It implies a rise in government expenditure and therefore, a rise in AD, as desired to\ncorrect deficient demand during depression.\n438\nPOWER POINTS [Summary of the Chapter)\nProblem of Deficient Demand and Excess Demand\nFull Employment Equilibrium and Underemployment Equilibrium\n• Full Employment Equilibrium refers to that situation in the economy when AS = AD (or S= 1) along\nwith fuller utilisation or labour. AS = AD, corresponding to the situation of tull employment.]\n• Underemployment Equilibrium reters to that situation in the economy when AS = AD (or $ * 1)\nbut without fuller utilisation or labour. As AD, Coresponding to the Situation of less than full\nemployment.]\n, Voluntary and Involuntary Unemployment\n• Voluntary Unemployment occurs when some people are not willing to work at all, or are not willing\nto work at the existing wage rate.\n• Involuntary Unemployment refers to a situation when people are not getting work, even when they\nare willing to work at the existing wage rate.\na Full Employment and Natural Unemployment\n• Full Employment is that situation in the economy when AS = AD along with fuller utilisation of\nresources. But it does not mean a situation of zero unemployment in the economy.\n• Natural Unemployment is that rate of unemployment (minimum rate of unemployment) which\nalways exists in the economy, owing to constantly changing supply and demand parameters in\nthe economy.\n« Deficient Demand is a situation when: AD < AS (corresponding to full employment level).\n• Deflationary Gap is measured as the difference between AD-at full employment and 'AD-at\nunderemployment.\n• Causes of Deflationary Gap:\n• Reduction in private consumption expenditure,\n> Reduction in investment expenditure,\n• Reduction in government expenditure,\n› Decline in exports,\n› Rise in imports,\n• Increase in tax burden.\n• Consequences of Deficient Demand: Deficient Demand causes Deflation and Underemployment.\n* When AD fails to catch up with AS of full employment, all goods and services produced in the\neconomy cannot be sold.\n• Accordingly, profits start shrinking. This discourages investment and lowers the level of income/\nemployment in the economy.\n* The economy is caught in a low level equilibrium trap where low AD causes low output and low\noutput/income causes low AD.\n* Excess Demand is a situation when: AD > AS (corresponding to full employment level).\n• Inflationary Gap is measured as the difference between 'AD-beyond full employment and AD-at Tull\n• Causes of Inflationary Gap: Causes of inflationary gap are just opposite to the (above stated\ncauses of deflationary gap. These are related to a rise in the various components of AD, a rise that\ncontinues to occur even when resources are fully utilised\n439","file":"macro 9/IMG_0591.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0592","image":"pages/IMG_0592.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\n• Consequences of Excess Demand: Excess Demand causes Intlation: Because excess demand is\nthat level of AD which surpasses AS (at full employment level), it must cause inflation.\n> Output cannot be increased once full employment is reached. Hence, AD beyond its full\nemployment level would only generate pressure of demand on the existing supply. Implying\n* The economy is caught in a wage-price spiral: wages catch prices and prices catch wages.\n• Correcting Excess and Deficient Demand\n• Measures of Correcting\nExcess and Deficient Demand include: (i fiscal measures, and\n(i monetary measures. Fiscal measures relate to fiscal policy of the government, and monetary\nmeasures relate to monetary policy of the central bank.\n• Fiscal Policy refers to the revenue and expenditure policy (or budgetary policy) of the government\nto correct the situations of excess and deficient demand in the economy with a view to achiev\n\"growth with stability.\n• Components of Fiscal Policy and the way these are used:\n> Government Expenditure: Increased to correct deficient demand, and decreased to coreet\nexcess demand.\n* Taxes: Tax burden is lowered to correct deficient demand, and raised to correct excess\ndemand.\n› Public Borrowing: Decreased to correct deficient demand, and increased to correct excess\ndemand.\n> Borrowing from RBI: Increased to correct deficient demand, and decreased to correct excess\ndemand.\n• Monetary Policy refers to that policy which corrects excess and deficient demand by regulating\nthe cost of credit and availability of credit in the economy.\n• Components of Monetary Policy and the way these are used:\n› Bank Rate/Repo Rate: Increased to correct excess demand, and decreased to correct deficient.\ndemand.\n- Reverse Repo Rate: Increased to correct excess demand and decreased to correct deficient\ndemand.\n> Open Market Operations: The central bank sells securities to correct excess demand and buys\nsecurities to correct deficient demand.\n- CRR: Raised to correct excess demand, and lowered to correct deficient demand.\n- SLR: Raised to correct excess demand, and lowered to correct deficient demand.\n> Margin Requirement: Raised to correct excess demand, and lowered to correct deficient\n* Credit Rationing: Introduced to correct excess demand, and withdrawn (if already in force) to\ncorrect deficient demand.\n> Moral Suasion: Banks are advised to pursue cheap money policy to correct deficient demand,\nand dear money policy to correct excess demand.\n440\nWORK-B\nCompetency Based Learning:\n1. Multiple Choice Questions\nChoose the correct option:\n1. Deflationary Gap refers to t\nrequired to establish full er\n(a) ex-ante Aggregate Dem\n(c) ex-ante Aggregate Supp\n2. The monetary policy is forr\n(a) Central Government\n(c) Reserve Bank of India\n3. From the following statem\nequilibrium.\n(a) Resources are fully anc\n(b) An existence of excessi\n(c) Aggregate supply is eq\n(d) Resources are not fully\n4. Inflationary gap in an ecor\n(a) Actual Aggregate Dem\n(b) Actual Aggregate Dem\n(c) Actual Aggregate Dem\n(d) Actual Aggregate Dem\n5. In order to discourage any 1\nthe\n(a) decrease, repo rate\n(c) decrease, government\n6. A situation in which an al\nreferred to as\n(a) full employment\n(c) voluntary unemploym\n7. The main aim of monetary\n(a) to bring price stability\n(c) to increase trade surp\n8. In case of an underemploy\n(a) Aggregate demand is\n(b) There exist excess pro\n(c) Resources are not fully\n(d) Resources are fully an","file":"macro 9/IMG_0592.HEIC"}],"boxes":[{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"IMG_0584","text":"Q. 1. What is natural rate of unemployment?\nAns. It refers to the rate of unemplature is it which always exists in the economy even when labou\nmarket is in a state of equilibrium. It occurs due to:\n(i) rictional changes (changes related to shifting from one job to the other), and\n(il) structural changes (changes related to new production technology).\nFrictional changes lead to triconal unemployment, while structural changes lead to structura\nunemployment.\n(Note: Frictional unemployment and structural unemployment are the two variants\nunemployment.]\nof natural","image":"pages/IMG_0584.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"IMG_0587","text":"Q.2. How does propensity to consume affect AD-function?\nAns. Increase in the propensity to consume causes an upward shift in the AD-function (through an\nupward shift in the C-function). On the other hand, decrease in the propensity to consume\ncauses a downward shift in AD-function (through a downward shift in C-function).\nQ. 3. According to Keynes, price level rises only after full employment. Why?\nAns. Price level does not rise before full employment, because AS is assumed to be perfectly elastic.\nPrior to full employment, AS tends to rise proportionate to any rise in AD. Accordingly, price level\nremains constant. But after full employment is reached, AS stops rising in response to a rise in\nAD. Accordingly, price level tends to rise.\n94 MEASURES TO CORRECT DEFICIENT AND\nEXCESS DEMAND: FISCAL AND MONETARY POLICIES\n• Economic stability requires that the situations of excess demand and dencient demand are\n• Who does it? The government does it through its revenue-expenditure policy (Fiscal Policy),\ncorrected as fast as possible.\nand the Central Bank (RBI) does it through its monetary policy. This bring us to the discussion or\nfiscal and monetary policies.\n431","image":"pages/IMG_0587.jpg"},{"title":"/CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"IMG_0588","text":"Q. 4. What fiscal measures would you recommend to correct deficient demand?\nAns. Following fiscal measures are recommended to correct deficient demand:\ni The govern secred step up its expenditure [on a public works programmes,\n(b) education and public welfate, (c) subsidies to the producers, and (d) defence and\nlaw & order]. Higher government expenditure acts like an injection into the circular flow of\nincome in the economy.\ni) The government should reduce tax burden on the households, so that they are left with\ngreater cash balances. Higher cash balances with the people lead to higher level of AD.\n(i) The government should raise more funds from the RBI so that there is a greater flow of\nliquidity in the economy. Higher liquidity implies higher level of AD.\n(iv) The government should plan a cut in public borrowing, so that, people are left with greater\nliquidity (or cash balances). Greater cash balances implies higher AD.\nBriefly, deficient demand is corrected when the government steps up its own expenditure and\nensures that cash balances with the people are raised.\n(Note: Students are advised to write just an opposite answer in case the question relates to excess demand.!\n9.4.2 Monetary Policy (Monetary Measures)\n• Monetary policy refer to that policy which deals with the control of liquidity or flow of credit in\nthe market with a view to managing inflationary or deflationary gaps in the economy.\n• We have already described the components of monetary policy and the way these are used to\ncorrect the situations of excess demand (inflationary gap) and deficient demand (deflationary gap)\nin the economy in chapter 6. Here, we present a brief summary of that description. Application of\nmonetary instruments to correct excess demand and deficient demand is discussed separately.\nas under:\nApplication of Monetary Instruments to Correctthe Situation of Excess Demand\n• Bank rate and repo rate are raised by the RBI. As a follow-up action, market rate of interest Is\nraised by the commercial banks. Cost of borrowing rises. It lowers the demand for credit leas\nto a cut in consumption and investment expenditure in the economy. Implying a cut in AD, as\ndesired to curb excess demand or inflationary gap.\n• Reverse repo rate is raised by the RBI. This induces the commercial banks to park their surplus\nfunds with the RBI. Accordingly, lesser funds are used as CRR-deposits for the creation of credit.\nAvailability of credit is reduced. It leads to a cut in AD. Excess demand or inflationary gap is curbed.\n• Pursuing the policy of open market operations. Securities are sold by the RBl to soak liquidity.\nAccordingly, cash reserves of the commercial banks are reduced, implying a cut in their credit","image":"pages/IMG_0588.jpg"},{"title":"ABILITY ZONE","page":"IMG_0590","text":"How Government Expenditure Impacts Equilibrium Income or GDP\n- A Diagrammatic Presentation\n• When the government comes into picture, we are dealing with a three sector economy. It includes:\n(i) household sector,\n(il) the producing sector, and\n(il) the government sector.\n• Accordingly, total expenditure in the economy (or components of AD) would include:\n(i) consumption expenditure (C),\n(i) private investment expenditure (I), and\n(ilI) government expenditure (G).\nEquilibrium Income/GDP in a 3-Sector Economy\n• For the sake of simplicity, we assume government expenditure to be constant (autonomous) like\nprivate investment expenditure. So that (like I-function), the government expenditure is indicated by a\nhorizontal straight line.\n• Fig. 9.7 illustrates the equilibrium income (GDP) in a three sector economy.\nC+ I + G\nObservations:\n• AD now consists of C, I and G.\n• G is assumed to be constant = LT.\nAccordingly, C + I + G is parallel\nto C + I.\n• The vertical distance between\nC+ I + G and C+ I is equal to\nLT = government expenditure.\n• Q is the point of equilibrium.\n• OS is equilibrium level of output\n(GDP).\nY (Income)/GDP\nFig. 9.7: Equilibrium Income (GDP) in a 3-Sector Economy\nGovernment expenditure is assumed to be constant and equal to LT. Accordingly, C+ I function\n(AD without government expenditure) shifts upward to become C + I + G function (AD with government\nexpenditure).\n• The two functions (AD with government expenditure and AD without government expenditure are shown\nas parallel to each other, because G (government expenditure) is assumed to be constant. Equilibrium\nis struck at point Q where C + 1 + G function intersects the 45° line. Here, desired AD = desired AS.\nEquilibrium income (GDP) = OS.\n436\nand Excess Demand?\nHow does the Government Correct the Situations of Deficient Demand\nProblem of Deficient Demand and Excess Demand\n, We consider these situations one by one.\nDeficient AD in a 3-Sector Economy and its Correction by the Government\n* We know, deficient demand is a situation of deflationary gap. The economy is driven into a state of lov\nlevel equilibrium trap. Low AD causes underemployment: tactors of production are not fully employed.\nPlanned output remains low, It leaas to low level of income, because incomes are low, once again there\n, The cycle of low AD and low income becomes repetitive. The market forces fail to break this repetitive\ncycle. The government must step in. It must raise the level of AD by increasing govemment expenditure\ni Fig. 9.8 shows the situation of deficient demand in a s-sector economy. It diso snows how this deficiem\ndemand is corrected through additional government expenditure (AG).\nY = AS\nAD= (C+ I +G+4G)\nADp (C+I+G)\nAG or additional\ngovernment\nexpenditure.\nThis is equal to\ndeflationary gap.\n450\nY (Income)/GDP\nObservations:\n• C+I + G is planned AD corresponding to\nunderemployment.\n• AG is additional government expenditure\nand is equal to deflationary gap.\n• AG is added to C+T + G to increase AD to\nthe level of full employment.\n• AG being equal to ab plugs the deflationary\n• OS is output corresponding to\nunderemployment equilibrium.\n• OT is output corresponding to full\nemployment equilibrium.\nThis is achieved by adding 4G to C+1 + G.\nFig. 9.8: 4G Clears Deflationary Gap (ab)\n• Deficient demand (deflationary gap) is indicated by ab and the government fills up this deficiency by\nmaking additional expenditure exactly equal to ab.\nThe level of AD which earlier was C + I + G (corresponding to underemployment equilibrium) is now\nraised to C + I + G + AG (corresponding to full employment equilibrium). Equilibrium level of output.\nwhich earlier was OS is now raised to OT.\n(• Now, equilibrium is achieved with full employment of the factors of production. Low level equilibrium\ntrap is broken. Thus, 4G acts as an injection in the circular flow of income in the economy.\n• 4G (additional government expenditure) acts as an injection in the circular flow of income\nthe economy, It raises the level of AD to the extent required for full employment of the factors\nof production.\n• The economy breaks the low level equilibrium trap. Actual output = Potential output (- full\nemployment level of output), and the state of deflation and unemployment is corrected\nExcess Demand in a 3-Sector Economy and its Correction by the Government\n• Excess demand is a situation of inflationary gap. The economy is over-heated: AD rises beyond full\nemployment level of AS. The general price level tends to rise, while the level of real output remains\nconstant. Such a situation often pushes the economy into a wage-price spiral, as already noted.\n437","image":"pages/IMG_0590.jpg"},{"title":"POWER POINTS [Summary of the Chapter)","page":"IMG_0591","text":"Problem of Deficient Demand and Excess Demand\nFull Employment Equilibrium and Underemployment Equilibrium\n• Full Employment Equilibrium refers to that situation in the economy when AS = AD (or S= 1) along\nwith fuller utilisation or labour. AS = AD, corresponding to the situation of tull employment.]\n• Underemployment Equilibrium reters to that situation in the economy when AS = AD (or $ * 1)\nbut without fuller utilisation or labour. As AD, Coresponding to the Situation of less than full\nemployment.]\n, Voluntary and Involuntary Unemployment\n• Voluntary Unemployment occurs when some people are not willing to work at all, or are not willing\nto work at the existing wage rate.\n• Involuntary Unemployment refers to a situation when people are not getting work, even when they\nare willing to work at the existing wage rate.\na Full Employment and Natural Unemployment\n• Full Employment is that situation in the economy when AS = AD along with fuller utilisation of\nresources. But it does not mean a situation of zero unemployment in the economy.\n• Natural Unemployment is that rate of unemployment (minimum rate of unemployment) which\nalways exists in the economy, owing to constantly changing supply and demand parameters in\nthe economy.\n« Deficient Demand is a situation when: AD < AS (corresponding to full employment level).\n• Deflationary Gap is measured as the difference between AD-at full employment and 'AD-at\nunderemployment.\n• Causes of Deflationary Gap:\n• Reduction in private consumption expenditure,\n> Reduction in investment expenditure,\n• Reduction in government expenditure,\n› Decline in exports,\n› Rise in imports,\n• Increase in tax burden.\n• Consequences of Deficient Demand: Deficient Demand causes Deflation and Underemployment.\n* When AD fails to catch up with AS of full employment, all goods and services produced in the\neconomy cannot be sold.\n• Accordingly, profits start shrinking. This discourages investment and lowers the level of income/\nemployment in the economy.\n* The economy is caught in a low level equilibrium trap where low AD causes low output and low\noutput/income causes low AD.\n* Excess Demand is a situation when: AD > AS (corresponding to full employment level).\n• Inflationary Gap is measured as the difference between 'AD-beyond full employment and AD-at Tull\n• Causes of Inflationary Gap: Causes of inflationary gap are just opposite to the (above stated\ncauses of deflationary gap. These are related to a rise in the various components of AD, a rise that\ncontinues to occur even when resources are fully utilised\n439","image":"pages/IMG_0591.jpg"}],"subject":"macro","number":"9"},{"id":"10","title":"Government Budget and the Economy","intro":"Government budget is a statement of expected receipts and expected expenditure of the government for the coming financial year, revealing its budgetary policy.","lessons":[{"title":"Government budget and its objectives","body":"Government budget is a statement of expected receipts and expected expenditure of the government for the coming financial year, revealing its budgetary policy. Objectives include GDP growth, allocation of resources, redistribution of income and wealth, economic stability, management of public enterprises and employment generation. Public infrastructure can encourage private investment; progressive taxation and social spending can reduce inequality. Fiscal discipline means managing public finances responsibly to avoid unsustainable deficits and debt.","formula":"","source":"IMG_0598","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"10.1 CONCEPT OF GOVERNMENT BUDGET · 10.2 OBJECTIVES"},{"title":"Revenue receipts versus capital receipts","body":"Revenue receipts neither create a liability nor reduce assets. They include tax revenue and non-tax revenue such as fees, fines, interest, profits/dividends and grants. Capital receipts create a liability or reduce assets: borrowing creates liabilities, while loan recovery and disinvestment reduce assets. Do not classify a receipt by size or regularity alone; use the liability/asset test.","formula":"","source":"IMG_0601","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"10.3.1 Budget Receipts"},{"title":"Direct taxes, indirect taxes and non-tax revenue","body":"Direct taxes have impact and incidence on the same person, such as income tax and corporation tax. Indirect taxes allow the burden to be shifted, such as GST. A fee is a payment for a specific government service; a fine penalises an offence. A tax is compulsory and does not promise a direct equivalent service. Progressive taxes take a larger proportion as income rises; proportional taxes keep the same proportion; regressive burdens fall relatively more heavily on lower incomes.","formula":"","source":"IMG_0600","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"Tax Revenue · Non-tax Revenue"},{"title":"Revenue expenditure versus capital expenditure","body":"Revenue expenditure neither creates assets nor reduces liabilities. Salaries, pensions, subsidies and interest payments are examples. Capital expenditure creates assets or reduces liabilities: construction of a highway, purchase of equipment, lending by government and repayment of loan principal. Repayment of principal is capital expenditure; interest is revenue expenditure. Grants to states remain revenue expenditure for the granting government even if the recipient uses them to build assets, following the textbook convention.","formula":"","source":"IMG_0602","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"10.3.2 Budget Expenditure"},{"title":"Revenue deficit, fiscal deficit and primary deficit","body":"Revenue deficit is the excess of revenue expenditure over revenue receipts. Fiscal deficit is total expenditure minus total receipts excluding borrowings; it measures borrowing requirements. Primary deficit deducts interest payments from fiscal deficit and indicates the current borrowing requirement excluding past-debt interest. A zero revenue deficit does not guarantee zero fiscal deficit. A zero primary deficit means borrowing covers interest payments.","formula":"Revenue deficit = Revenue expenditure − Revenue receipts\nFiscal deficit = Total expenditure − Revenue receipts − Non-debt capital receipts\nPrimary deficit = Fiscal deficit − Interest payments","source":"IMG_0604","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"10.4 BUDGET DEFICIT: REVENUE DEFICIT, FISCAL DEFICIT AND PRIMARY DEFICIT"},{"title":"Implications, balanced and unbalanced budgets","body":"A revenue deficit indicates government dissaving: borrowing may finance current consumption rather than asset creation. Fiscal deficits may raise debt and interest burdens and crowd out private borrowing; financing expenditure can also support demand and productive capacity, depending on its use. A balanced budget has receipts equal to expenditure; a surplus budget has receipts above expenditure and can restrain inflation; a deficit budget can support demand in recession. Consider both the size and composition of expenditure.","formula":"","source":"IMG_0606","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"10.5 BALANCED AND UNBALANCED BUDGET"},{"title":"Ability Zone: public, private, common and club goods","body":"Public goods are non-rival and non-excludable, such as national defence. Private goods are rival and excludable. Common goods are rival but non-excludable, such as fish in an open pond. Club goods are excludable but non-rival until congestion, such as a subscription service. Rivalry means one person’s use reduces availability to others; excludability means users can be denied access. Public expenditure supports infrastructure, redistribution, stability and an investment-friendly environment.","formula":"","source":"IMG_0607","kind":"Ability Zone","textbookHeading":"ABILITY ZONE · Public Goods, Private Goods, Common Goods and Club Goods"},{"title":"Budget objectives: the headings to reproduce","textbookHeading":"10.2 OBJECTIVES OF GOVERNMENT BUDGET","body":"GDP Growth: create infrastructure and induce private investment through suitable incentives. Allocation of Resources: influence production through taxes, subsidies and public provision. Provision of Public Goods: provide collective services that markets may undersupply. Redistribution of Income and Wealth: use progressive taxation and welfare spending. Balanced Regional Growth: direct infrastructure and incentives to less-developed regions. Employment Opportunities: expand activity and labour demand. Economic Stability: raise spending during deficient demand and restrain it during excess demand. Select and explain the number of objectives requested, using these headings.","source":"IMG_0608","kind":"Exam points","formula":""},{"title":"Free-rider problem","textbookHeading":"ABILITY ZONE · Free Rider Problem","body":"A free rider enjoys the benefits of goods and services without contributing the full or partial cost of providing them. Non-excludability makes it difficult to withhold benefits from non-payers. Private providers may be unable to collect enough revenue to cover costs, so the market can fail to provide a needed public good. National defence and street lighting illustrate why public provision can be necessary.","source":"IMG_0608","kind":"Ability Zone","formula":""},{"title":"Tax rates and tax burden are different","textbookHeading":"ABILITY ZONE · Progressive and Regressive Nature of Taxation is Different from Progressive and Regressive Rates of Taxation","body":"Progressive Rate: the tax rate rises with income. Regressive Rate: the tax rate falls with income. Proportionate Rate: the tax rate stays constant as income changes. Progressive Nature: the real burden is greater on the rich. Regressive Nature: the real burden is greater on the poor. The textbook stresses that a proportionate tax can still have a regressive real burden. When answering, specify whether you mean the numerical rate or the economic burden.","source":"IMG_0608","kind":"Ability Zone","formula":""},{"title":"Does a balanced budget leave AD unchanged?","textbookHeading":"ABILITY ZONE · Does Balanced Budget Leave Aggregate Demand Unaffected in the Economy?","body":"No. Equal tax collection and government expenditure do not necessarily have equal first-round effects on AD. Government expenditure enters AD in full; a tax increase reduces disposable income, and consumption falls by MPC times the tax rise. If G and taxes each rise by ₹100 and MPC is 0.5, expenditure raises AD by ₹100 while the initial consumption loss is ₹50, giving a net initial rise of ₹50. Under the simple lump-sum-tax model, later rounds imply a balanced-budget multiplier of one. Distinguish the initial AD change from the final income change.","source":"IMG_0608","kind":"Ability Zone","formula":"Initial ΔAD = ΔG − MPC × ΔT\nIf ΔG = ΔT: initial ΔAD = (1 − MPC)ΔG"}],"pages":[{"id":"IMG_0598","image":"pages/IMG_0598.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\n10.1 CONCEPT OF GOVERNMENT BUDGET\nMeaning and Features of Government Budget\n• February-1 is a well known date in India when the Finance Minister presents annual budget of\nthe government for its approval by the parliament.\n• The budget unfolds:\n* the financial performance of the government over the past one year, and\n• the financial programmes and policies of the government for the next one year.\n• It includes an estimate of government receipts and government expenditure for the upcoming\n• Asregards financial performance of the government, it is more like a description of what happened\nduring the past one year. Focus is placed largely on the other part of the budget describing\nprogrammes and policies (and estimates of receipts and expenditure) of the government for the\nnext one year.\n• The programmes and policies of the government (as presented in the budget) are known as\n\"Budgetary Policy' of the government, or 'Fiscal Policy' of the government. It has the following\naspects:\nAspects of Budget and Budgetary Policy\nRevenue Aspect\nExpenditure Aspect\neceipts of the government.\nspenditure or the government.,\n• It is by managing the expected revenue and expected expenditure that the government reveals\nits fiscal policy (budgetary policy), focusing on growth and stability of the economy.\nThus, government budget is a statement of expected receipts and expected expenditure of the\ngovernment (for the financial year to come that reveals budgetary policy of the government to\nachieve the twin objective of growth with stability.\n10.2 OBJECTIVES OF GOVERNMENT BUDGET **\nFollowing is a brief description of some principal objectives of government budget (with special\nreference to the Indian economy):\n(1) GDP Growth: GDP growth is the central objective of government budgetary policy. It is\nachieved in two ways:\n• By expanding public expenditure particularly on the creation of economic and social\ninfrastructure.\n• By inducing private investment expenditure (through tax rebates and subsidies).\n(2) Allocation of Resources: Private enterprises will always desire to allocate resources to\nthose areas of production where profits are high. However, it is possible that such areas of\nproduction (like production of alcohol) may not promote social welfare.\n• Through its budgetary policy, the government of a country directs the allocation of resources\nGovernment Budget and the Economy\n• Production of goods which are injurious to health (ike Cigarettes and Alcohol) is discourage\nsocial welfare.\nin a manner such that there is a balance between the goals of profit maximisation and\nhadi\") is encouraged through subsidies.\nthrough heavy taxation. On the other hand, production of 'socially useful goods (i\n(i) Provision of Public Goods: Supply and demand forces in a market economy do not allow\nenough production of public goods. These are those goods which satisfy collective needs\n• Law & order and defence of the country are important examples of public goods. It is through\nbudgetary allocation of funds that these goods are sufficiently provided to the people.\nCheck Ability Zone for details on how public goods are different from other goods!\n(4) Redistribution of Income and Wealth: Taxation and subsidies are important components a\ngovernment budget. These are used to correct the distribution of income and wealth.\n• Taxes are imposed on the rich and subsidies are offered to the poor. Accordingly disposable\nincome of the rich is reduced, while the disposable income of the poor is increased.\n• Free LPG connection to the BPL population is an important example of subsidy to the poor.\nf) Balanced Regional Growth: The budgetary policy places priority on the development of\nbackward regions in the country. This is achieved through liberal tax laws for the backward\nregions.\n• Establishment of SEZ (special economic zones) in the backward regions through liberal tax\nlaws may be cited as an example.\n(6) Employment Generation: Budgetary policy focuses on the generation of employment\nopportunities through investment in public enterprises.\n• Budgetary provisions are made for schemes like MGNREGA offering employment to poorer\nsections of the society.\n(7) Economic Stability: Revenue and expenditure policies of the government are used as fiscal\ninstruments to minimise the inflationary and deflationary swings in the economy.\n• Inflation is corrected through higher taxation and lower government expenditure.\n• Deflation is managed through lower taxation and higher government expenditure.\nLily the government tries to manage its revenue and expenditure in such a way that the\n(I growth is accelerated, inflationary and deflationary pressures are eliminated, and inequality is\neduced. This imparts stability to the process of growth.\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\n4.1. What do you mean by Fiscal discipline'? What happens if fiscal discipline is not maintained in\nthe economy?\nAns. Fiscal discipline refers to the state of balance between revenues and expenditures of the\nthe government.\ngovernment. It calls for a necessary check on the expenditures in view of the limited revenues o\n465","file":"macro 10/IMG_0598.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0599","image":"pages/IMG_0599.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\nLack of fiscal discipline often causes excess expenditure. It leads to intanonary spiral. Cost o\nProduction starts rising. High cost of production hurts the process of investment. Eventually,\nGDP growth is hurt and its instability is challenged.\nQ.2. How does the government's budgetary policy strike a balance between profit maximisation by\nprivate enterprises and the promotion of social welfare?\nAns. The government's budgetary policy strikes a balance between private profit maximisation and\npromotion of social welfare through the following ways:\n(i) The government promotes the production of socially useful goods (like khadi) by offering\ntax-rebates and subsidies to the producers. Simultaneously taxes (like GST) are lowered to\npromote sales of such goods.\nExample: Under the Zero Defect Zero Effect (ZED) Scheme, the Government of India provides\nupto 80% subsidy to Mini, Small and Medium Enterprises (MSMEs).\n(i) On the other hand, socially harmful goods (like cigarettes and alcohol are highly taxed to\ndiscourage their production as well as consumption.\n(i) Profit maximisation is supported through high budgetary allocations to the construction of\nSEZ (Special Economic Zones), offering banking and transport facilities to the producers.\n(iv) Also, subsidies are offered on the purchase of 'capital goods', like tractors by the farmers so\nthat their cost of production is minimised and profits are maximised.\n0. 3. In what ways does the government budget contribute to economic stability, particularly during\nperiods of inflation and deflation?\nAns.\n(i) Inflation is managed by decreasing government expenditure, so that AD is reduced. Also,\ntaxes are raised to minimise disposable income of the people. When disposable income\nreduces, expenditure (AD) also reduces.\n(ii) Deflation is managed by raising government expenditure, so that AD is raised. Taxes are\nlowered to raise disposable income, so that AD is boosted. Business houses are offered\n'bail-out' packages so that their scale of production is not impaired.\n10.3 STRUCTURE OF THE BUDGET\nOR\nCOMPONENTS OF THE BUDGET\n• Structure of the budget refers to the components of budget. Two broad components of the\ngovernment budget are:\n• Budget Receipts (including revenue receipts and capital receipts), and\n• Budget Expenditure (including revenue expenditure and capital expenditure).\nDetails of both these components are discussed as under:\n10.3.1 Budget Receipts\n• Budget receipts refer to estimated money receipts of the government from all sources during\nthe fiscal year.\n• Broadly, the budget receipts are classified as:\n(a) Revenue Receipts, and\n(b) Capital Receipts.\n466\nGovernment Budget and the Economy\nowing are the details:\n131 (a) Revenue Receipts\nue receipts are those money receipts of the government which show the following two\ni) These receipts do not create any corresponding lability for the government.\nax receipts. Tax is a revenue receipt because it does not involve any corresponding liability for e\nthe government. Tax is a unilateral (or one-sided) compulsory payment to ine government\n(i These receipts do not cause any reduction in assets of the government.\nTax receipts. Tax is a revenue receipt because it does not lead to any reduction in assets of the\ngovernment. In contrast, if government receives money by selling its share of some company f\n(say Air India), it causes reduction in assets of the government. These are therefore, not to be\ntreated as revenue receipts.\nshort, revenue receipts of the government are those money receipts which do not create a\nSalty or the government and as well do not lead to reduction in assets of the government.\naeue receipts are broadly classified as tax receipts and non-tax receipts.\nConstituents of Revenue Receipts\n(i) Tax Receipts\n(i) Non-tax Receipts\nIncome\nCorporation\nFines\nEscheat\nEstate Duty\nSpecial\nGift Tax\nCustoms\nDuty\nExcise Duty\nGST (Goods and\nServices Tax\nIncome from\nPublic Enterprises\nGrants/\nDonations\n- Sale of Spectrum\nlike 4G and 5G\nCien below is a detailed explanation of these constituents of revenue receipts.\n() Tax Receipts\nAlaria compulsory payment to the government by the households, firms or other institutional\ni. he tapayer cannot expect any direct service or benefit from the government, in return.\nTypes of Taxes\nTares are broadly classified as:\n(a) Progressive and Regressive Taxes,\n(b) Value Added and Specific Taxes, and\n(c) Direct and Indirect Taxes.","file":"macro 10/IMG_0599.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0600","image":"pages/IMG_0600.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\n(a) Progressive and Regressive Taxes: Taxes are classited as progressive and regressive\ndepending on the real burden of taxation. Details are as under:\n• Progressive Tax: A tax is said to be progressive when the rate of tax increases with an\nincrease in income. So that, the real burden of the taxis more on the rich and less on the poc\nSuppose there is a tax system where the tax rate is 10% for annual income betwee\n* 8 to * 12 lakh. It is say 15% for annual income between 12 to ‹ 16 lakh, and so o\nThus, tax rate increases as the level of income increases.\n• Regressive Tax: A tax is said to be regressive when it causes a greater real burden on the\npoor than the rich.\nIf a person with 71,00,000 as his monthly income pays 10% income tax (or pays 1000),\nhe still has a balance of 90,000 per month.\nBut if a person with ‹ 5,000 as his monthly income has to pay 10% income tax or pays\n7 500), it might mean a cut in his essential consumption leading to poor diet and\ntherefore, poor health.\nThus, a constant rate of taxation on the rich and the poor is a regressive tax, as it causes\na greater real burden on the poor than the rich.\n(b) Value Added Tax or VAT and Specific Taxes: Depending upon tax base, taxes can be classified as:\n• Value Added Tax or VAT: Value added tax is an indirect tax which is imposed on 'Value\nAdded' at the various stages of production.\n> Value added refers to the difference between value of output and value of intermediate\nconsumption. It is imposed at each stage of production. GST is an important form of value\nadded tax.\n• Specific Tax: When a tax is levied on a commodity on the basis of its units, size or weight,\nit is called the specific tax.\nExample:\nExcise Duty on Petrol and Diesel: Levied per litre of fuel sold. Tax on Packaged Goods:\nCharged per unit or per weight (e.g., R 5 per 500 ml of soft drink).\n(c) Direct and Indirect Taxes: Taxes are classified as direct and indirect depending on their final\nburden.\n• Direct Tax: A direct tax is the one the final burden of which is borne by the person on\nwhom it is imposed.\nExample:\nIncome tax. It is imposed on the income of a person and he himself bears its burden.\nburden of this tax cannot be shifted to any other person. Corporation tax, gift tax, wea\ntax, are other examples of direct tax.\n• Indirect Tax: An indirect tax is the one whose initial burden or impact is on one person bu\nhe succeeds in shifting the burden to another persons.\nGovernment Budget and the Economy\nExample:\nCST. It is levied on the producers. They are to pay this tax to the government. But they e\ncharge this tax from the buyers by adding it to the price of the goods sold. Value added\ntax, customs duty, excise duty, octroi are other examples of indirect tax.\nDID YOU KNOW\n, Certain taxes are called 'paper taxes.\n- These refer to the taxes like gift tax in India which carry their significance only on paper.\n- These taxes are of little or no significance in terms of their revenue yield.\n, The introduction of GST (Goods and Services Tax) subsumed various indirect taxes ike VAT, excise\nduty, and service tax, creating a unified tax syster that simplifies compliance and enhances\nefficiency in India's tax framework.\nTable 10.1: Direct Tax vs. Indirect Tax\nBasis of Difference\n1. Meaning\nDirect Tax\nIndirect Tax\n- It is the tax which is finally paid by the person - It is the tax which is imposed on one person but\non whom it is legally imposed.\nis paid by another.\n2. Shifting of Burden - The burden of direct taxes cannot be shifted - The burden of indirect taxes can be shifted to\nto other person.\nothers.\n3. Nature of Tax\n- Direct taxes are generally progressive in nature. - Indi\nact taxes are generally regressive in nature.\n4. Example\n- Income tax, corporate profit tax.\n- GST, customs duty.\n(ii) Non-tax Receipts\nNon-tax receipts are those receipts which arise from sources other than taxes. Some of the non-tax\nreceipts are as follows:\n(a) Fees: A fee is a payment to the government for the services that it renders to the people.\nExamples:\nLand registration fees, birth and death registration fees, passport fees, court fees, etc.\nIt is to be noted that fee is not a payment (price) for commercial service. It is a payment for\nadministrative and judicial services provided to the people.\n(b) Fines: Fines are those payments which are made by the law breakers to the govemment.\nThese are economic punishments for breaking laws. The aim is not to earn revenue, but to\nmake people respectful to the laws.\n(c) Escheat: Escheat refers to that income of the state which arises out of the property left by\nthe people without a legal heir. There are no claimants of such property. The government\nmakes revenue out of it.\n(d) Special Assessment: Special assessment is that payment which is made by the owners of those\nproperties whose value has appreciated due to developmental activities of the government.\nExample:\nWhen as a result of construction of roads or provision of sewerage system or construction\nof drains, etc., value of the neighbouring property or its rental value appreciates, then a\npart of the developmental expenditure is recovered from the owners of such property by\nway of special assessment.","file":"macro 10/IMG_0600.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0601","image":"pages/IMG_0601.jpg","text":"470\nIntroductory Macroeconomics\n(e) Income from Public Enterprises: Several enterprises are owned by the government\nIndian Railways, Nangal Fertilizer Factory, Indian Oil, Bhilai Steel Plant, etc. Profit of\nthese enterprises are a source of revenue for the government.\n(f) Income from the Sale or spectrum like 4G and 50: income trom the sale of spectrum has\nemerged as a significant source of non-tax receipts of government.\ng) Grants/Donations: Grants are also a source of government revenue. It is very commo\nfor the people to offer donations and grants to the government when there are natural\ncalamities like earthquake, floods and famines.\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\nQ. 4. Is borrowing by the government a revenue receipt?\nAns. No, because it creates a liability (for the government) of repayment.\nQ. 5. Explain through an example, how the burden of an indirect tax is shifted.\nAns. GST is an indirect tax. A shopkeeper pays GST to the government. But, the shopkeeper recovers\nthis tax from the customers as a part of price of the commodity sold. So, impact of GST (an\nindirect tax) is ultimately shifted to the consumers.\nQ. 6. Categorise the following as direct tax or indirect tax:\n(i) Corporation tax\n(ii) GST\n(iv) Capital gains tax\n(v) Income tax\n(iii) Service tax\n(vi) CGST and SGST\nAns. Direct Tax: (i), (iv), (v);\nIndirect Tax: (i), (ili), (vi)\n10.3.1 (b) Capital Receipts\nCapital receipts are those money receipts of the government which show the following two\ncharacteristics:\n(i) These receipts create a liability for the government.\nExample:\nLoans by the government are a liability. These are to be paid back. These are, therefore,\nthe capital receipts of the government.\n(ii) These receipts cause reduction in assets of the government.\nExample:\nMoney received by the government by selling its shares (say of Air India) would cause\nreduction in assets of the government. These are, therefore, to be treated as capital receipts.\nIn short, capital receipts are those money receipts of the government which either create a liability\nfor the government or cause a reduction in its assets.\nIn india, capital receipts of the government budget are often classified as under:\nCapital Receipts\n(i) Recovery of\n(i) Borrowings and\nOther Liabilities\n(lil) Other\nReceipts\nGiven below a detailed explanation of these constituents of a capital receipts.\nGovernment Budget and the Economy\n(i) Recovery of Loans: The central government offers loans to the state governments to cope\nwith financial crises. When these loans are recovered, assets of the government are reduced.\nAccordingly, these are classified as capital receipts.\n(i) Borrowings and Other Liabilities: While lending creates assets, borrowing creates liability.\nAccordingly, borrowings are to be treated as capital receipts. It may be noted that the\ngovernment borrows money from:\n• The general public (Borrowings from the general public are called market borrowings).\n• The Reserve Bank of India, and\n• The rest of the world.\n(il) Other Receipts: These include items like 'disinvestment\". It is the opposite of investment.\nDisinvestment occurs when the government sells off its shares of public sector enterprises\nto private sector.\n• It involves transfer of ownership of public sector enterprises to the private entrepreneurs,\nleading to privatisation. Money received through disinvestment is treated as capital receipt\nbecause it causes reduction in assets of the government.\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\nQ. 7. Read the description and examples related to revenue receipts and capital receipts given\nbelow and state their impact on asset and liability status using 1 or 4 arrow in the blanks\ngiven on the right hand side of the description, and by specifying Y or N in the blanks given on\nthe left hand side of the description.\nBudget Receipts\n(A) Impact on\nAsset & Liability\nStatus\n(B) Impact on\nAsset & Liability\nstatus\nLiability\nTaxes received\nby the government\nGrants received\nby the government\nDisinvestment\nAns. (A) Liability - N\nAsset - N\n(B) Liability - T\nAsset - V\nQ. 8. Categorise the following as revenue receipts and capital receipts:\n(i) Profits of a PSU\n(ii) Recovery of loans\n(iii) Tax receipts\n(iv) Interest received on loans\n(v) Public debt\n(vi) Disinvestment\n(vii) Borrowings from public\n(vili) Gifts and grants received from abroad\n(ix) Borrowings from rest of the world\n(x) Receipts from sale of a public sector undertaking\nAns. Revenue Receipts (Neither creates liability nor reduces assets): (i), i, (v), (vii)\nCapital Receipts (Increases liability): (v), (vii), (ix)\n(Reduces assets) : (ii), (vi), (x)\n471","file":"macro 10/IMG_0601.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0602","image":"pages/IMG_0602.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\nQ. 9. Howare revenue receipts different from capital receipts in terms of their ineating and signincan\nAns. Following observations highlight the difference between revenue receipts and capital receipts\nRevenue Receipts\nBasis of Difference\nCapital Receipts\n1. Meaning\nliability status of the government.\n• Assets and liabilities are not\nincreased or decreased.\nstatus of the government.\n• Assets are lowered.\n• Liabilities are raised.\n2. Significance\n• Revenue receipts do not leave any • Capital receipts often leave burden\nburden on future generations.\non future generations.\nExample: Borrowings leave the\nburden on future generations for the\nrepayment of loans.\n• High revenue receipts (as tax receipts)\npoint to sound financial health of the\n• High capital receipts (borrowings and\ndisinvestment) point to poor financial\neconomy.\nhealth of the economy.\nQ. 10. What is disinvestment? Does it refer to revenue receipt or capital receipt of the government?\nGive an example.\nAns. Disinvestment refers to withdrawal of existing investment.\nExample: The Government of India is making disinvestment by selling its shares in the Maruti\nUdyog. It is a capital receipt of the government, as it reduces assets of the government.\n10.3.2 Budget Expenditure\n• Budget expenditure refers to estimated expenditure of the government during the fiscal year.\n• Like budget receipts, budget expenditure of the government is broadly classified as:\n(a) Revenue Expenditure and\n(b) Capital Expenditure.\nFollowing are the details:\n10.3.2 (a) Revenue Expenditure\nRevenue expenditure of the government is that expenditure which shows the following two\ncharacteristics:\n(i) It does not create any asset for the government.\nExample:\nExpenditure by the government on old-age pensions, salaries and scholarships are to be\ntreated as revenue expenditure. Because this does not lead to any type of asset formation.\n(i) It does not cause any reduction in liability of the government.\nExample:\nExpenditure by way of grants to the state government to cope with natural calamities (like\nfloods and earthquakes) does not reduce financial liability of the central government in any\nmanner. Accordingly, this is to be treated as revenue expenditure.\n472\nGovernment Budget and the Economy\nIn short, revenue expenditure refers to estimated expenditure of the government in a fiscal year\nwhich does not create assets or causes a reduction in liabilities.\n! CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS\nImportant Items of Revenue Expenditure in the indian Government Budget\n• These are:\n) Wage bill of the government\n(il) Interest payments\n(i) Expenditure on subsidies\n(iv) Defence purchases\nImportant Convention regarding Grants\nAs a matter of convention, all grants given by the centre to the state governments and the governments\nof Union territories) are treated as revenue expenditure, even when some grants may result in the creation\nof assets.\n10.3.2 (b) Capital Expenditure\nCapital expenditure of the government is that expenditure which shows the following two\ncharacteristics:\n(i) It creates assets for the government.\nExample:\nEquity (or shares) of the domestic or multinational corporations purchased by the government\ncreates assets for the government. Accordingly, this is to be treated as capital expenditure.\n(i) It causes reduction in liabilities of the government.\nExample:\nRepayment of loans certainly reduces liability of the government. Accordingly, this is to be F\ntreated as capital expenditure.\nIn short, capital expenditure refers to the estimated expenditure of the government in a fiscal year\nwhich creates assets or causes a reduction in liabilities.\nCRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS\nImportant Items of Capital Expenditure in the Indian Government Budget\n• These are:\n(i) Expenditure on land and building.\n(i Expenditure on machinery and equipment.\n(iii) Purchase of shares.\n(iv) Loans by the central government to the state governments or state corporations.\nPlan and Non-plan Expenditure\nBudget expenditure (revenue expenditure + capital expenditure) is also classified as plan and non-\nplan expenditure. Following is the difference:\n473","file":"macro 10/IMG_0602.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0603","image":"pages/IMG_0603.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\n(i) Plan Expenditure: Plan expenditure refers to that expenditure which relates to\n• specified plans and programmes of development, and\n• assistance of the central government to the state governments.\nit includes both revenue expenditure (like assistance to the states) and capital expenditur\n(like expenditure on the construction of roads, bridges and hospitals).\n(i) Non-plan Expenditure: Broadly, all expenditure other than plan expenditure is classified as\nnon-plan expenditure.\n• Specifically, non-plan expenditure relates to expenditure on routine functioning of the\ngovernment. Or, it includes expenditure on such services as or law a order, derence and\nsubsidies.\nThus, we can write that:\nBudget Expenditure = Revenue expenditure + Capital expenditure\nOr\nBudget Expenditure = Plan expenditure + Non-plan expenditure\nStructure of Government Budget at a Glance\nGovernment Budget\nBudget Expenditure\nBudget Receipts\nCapital\n(income tax,\nexcise duty, etc.)\n(fees, fines,\netc.)\nRevenue\nExpenditure\nExpenditure\n(land and building, Expenditure Expenditure\n(wage bill, interest\nmachinery &\npayments,\nequipment,\nsubsidies,\npurchase of\ndefence purchases,\nshares, loans\nto state\ngovernments, etc.)\nborrowings\nRecovery of\nReceipts\nLOans\n(disinvestment)\nRevenue expenditure - Revenue receipts= Revenue Deficit\n(Revenue expenditure + Capital expenditure) - (Revenue receipts + Capital receipts other than borrowings) = Fiscal Deficit\nFiscal deficit - Interest payment = Primary Deficit\n(These deficits are explained in detail in section 10.4.)\n[Note: Structure of the Government Budget may also be studied in terms of (i) Revenue Budet, and.\n(ii) Capital Budget.\nRevenue Budget includes revenue receipts and revenue expenditure of the government. Capital\nBudget includes capital receipts and capital expenditure of the government.]\nJAVLR 10.1\nREVENUE EXPENDITURE vs. CAPITAL EXPENDITURE\n474\n? DID YOU KNOW\nGovernment Budget and the Economy\n• In India, non-plan expenditure is a significant part of the total government expenditure. Which is\nwhy ical discipline in the country often remains a serious challenge.\n+ After the abolition of planning commission, the government is also considering to abolish the\nclassification of budgetary expenditure as plan and non-plan expenditure.\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\nq. 11. Categorise the following as revenue expenditure and capital expenditure:\n(i) Expenditure on purchasing machinery for a factory.\n(i) Repayment of amount borrowed from World Bank.\n(i) Provision of subsidies by the government.\n(iv) Interest paid on loans taken by government.\n(v) Expenditure on collection of taxes.\n(vi) Expenditure incurred on defence services.\n(vii) Purchase of cranes for building a bridge.\n(vili) Repayment of amount borrowed from USA.\n(ix) Loans granted to Haryana Government by GOl.\n(x) Repayment of a part of outstanding loan towards IME.\n(xi) Expenditure on administrative services.\n(xii) Provision of old-age pension.\nAns. Revenue Expenditure (Neither reduces liability nor creates assets): (ii, (iv), (v), (vi), (xi), (xi)\nCapital Expenditure (Reduces liability): (i), (viii), (x)\n(Increases assets): (i), (vii), (ix)\nQ. 12. Read the description and examples related to revenue expenditure and capital expenditure\ngiven below and state their impact on asset and liability status using T or v arrow in the blanks\ngiven on the right hand side of the description, and by specifying Y or N in the blanks given on\nthe left hand side of the description.\nBudget Expenditure\n(A) Impact on\nAsset & Liability\n(B) Impact on\nStatus\nLiability.\nASSeL\nScholarship\nOld-age pension\nRepayment\nof loans\nInfrastructural\nexpenditure\nAns. (A) Liability\nAsset - N\n(B) Liability - /\nAsset - T\nQ. 13. How is revenue expenditure different from capital expenditure in terms of their meaning and\nsignificance?\nAns. Following observations highlight the difference between revenue expenditure and capital expenditure:\nBasis of Difference\nRevenue Receipts\nCapital Receipts\n1. Meaning\n• Revenue expenditure does not\n• Capital expenditure impacts\nimpact asset-liability status of the\nasset-liability status of the\ngovernment.\ngovernment.\n- Assets and liabilities are not\n- Assets are raised.\nincreased or decreased.\n- Liabilities are lowered.\n475","file":"macro 10/IMG_0603.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0604","image":"pages/IMG_0604.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\n2. Significance\n• Revenue expenditure (subsidies and\nlaw & order) focuses on welfare\nof the people. It does not directly\ncontribute to GDP growth.\n• High revenue expenditure by the\ngovernment (by way of subsidies\nor old-age pensions) point\ntowards poverty of the people or\nbackwardness of the economy.\n• Capital expenditure (public\ninvestment) focuses on GDP growth.\nIt directly contributes to GDP growth.\n• High capital expenditure by the\ngovernment point towards lack\nof private investment in the\neconomy. (Crowding-out effect:\nSee page 478) Capital expenditure\nby the government is raised when\nthe economy is suffering from\ndeflationary gap.\nQ. 14. How is revenue budget different from capital budget?\nAns.\nBasis of Difference\n1. Components\n2. Impact on Asset\nand Liability Status\n4. Overall Effect\nRevenue Budget\n• Revenue budget includes revenue\nreceipts and revenue expenditure of\nthe government.\n• Revenue budget does not impact\nasset-liability status of the\ngovernment.\n• Revenue budget focuses on welfare\nof the people by way of DBT (direct\nbenefit transfers). It does not\ndirectly contribute to GDP growth.\n• High revenue receipts in the\nrevenue budget lead to low\ncapital receipts (borrowings and\ndisinvestment) in the capital budget.\nIt is a sign of a growing economy.\nCapital Budget\n• Capital budget includes capital\nreceipts and capital expenditure of\nthe government.\n• Capital budget impact asset-liability\nstatus of the government.\n• Capital budget focuses on\nGDP growth (by way of public\ninvestment).\n• High capital receipts are often\nrelated to compulsions of\nborrowings. It is a sign of a\nbackward economy.\n10.4 BUDGET DEFICIT: REVENUE DEFICIT, FISCAL DEFICIT AND\nPRIMARY DEFICIT [Meaning, Types and Measurement]\n10.4.1 What is Budget Deficit?\n• Budget defcit (also called government deficit) refers to a situation when budget expenditures\nof the government are greater than the budget receipts.\n• In other words, it is the excess of total expenditure (revenue expenditure and capital expenditue)\nover and above the total receipts (revenue receipts and capital receipts) of the government.\nBudget\nDeficit\nTotal expenditure\nTotal receipts\n(Revenue expenditure\n(Revenue receipts + Capital\n+ Capital expenditure\nreceipts)\nBD = BE - BR, when BE > BR\n(Here, BD = Budget deficit; BE = Budget expenditure; BR = Budget receipts.)\n476\n10.4.2 Types and Measurement of Budget Deficit\nGovernment Budget and the Economy\nwith reference to the budget of the Government of India, there are three important types of budget\ndeficit. These are:\n(i) Revenue Deficit,\n(ii) Fiscal Deficit, and\n(i) Primary Deficit.\n(i) Revenue Deficit\n• Revenue deficit is the excess of revenue expenditure over revenue receipts.\nRevenue Deficit = Revenue expenditure - Revenue receipts\nRD = RE- RR, when RE > RR\n(Here, RD = Revenue deficit; RE = Revenue expenditure; RR = Revenue receipts.)\nImplications\n(a) Because of revenue deficit, the government may have to cut its expenditure (subsidies in\nparticular) on several welfare programmes in the country. This leads to loss of social welfare.\n(b) The government may have to raise funds through borrowing. It involves borrowing from the\ngeneral public, RBI or rest of the world. This raises liabilities of the government and lowers\nits credit-worthiness.\n(c) The government may be compelled for disinvestment-selling its ownership of public\nenterprises. The ownership of public enterprises may be lost to foreign companies.\nConsequently, economic control of the foreigners may increase in the domestic economy.\nAVLR 10.2\nREVENUE DEFICIT AND ITS IMPLICATIONS\n(ii) Fiscal Deficit\n• Fiscal deficit is the excess of total expenditure over total receipts (other than borrowings).\nFiscal Deficit = Total expenditure (Revenue expenditure + Capital expenditure)\n- Total receipts other than borrowings (Revenue receipts\n+ Capital receipts other than borrowings)\nFD = BE - BR other than borrowings, when BE > BRother than borrowings\n(Here, FD = Fiscal deficit; BE = Budget expenditure; BR = Budget receipts.)\n• In fact, fiscal deficit is the estimation of total borrowings by the government. It is often called\n\"Gross Fiscal Deficit\". Gross fiscal deficit shows estimated borrowing by the government to cope\nwith its expenditures during the year. Often it is expressed as a percentage of GDP.\nGross Fiscal Deficit = Borrowing from RBI\n+ Borrowing from abroad\n+ Net borrowing at home","file":"macro 10/IMG_0604.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0605","image":"pages/IMG_0605.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\nImplications\nborrowings by the government. It has following implications:\nFiscal deficit is an estimate of borrowings by the government. Greater fiscal delicit #pies greate\n(a) Inflationary Spiral: Borrowing from Reis often linked to inflationary spiral in the economy.\n• Borrowing from RBl increases money supply in the economy, Increase in money supply leads\nThis is how it happens:\nto increase in the general price level considering there is no excess capacity. A persistent\nincrease in the general price level (over a period of time) leads to inflationary spiral.\nBorrowing from RBI → Increase in money supply crease in prices Intony sira\n(b) National Debt: Fiscal deficit leads to national debt. It hinders GDP growth. Because, a\nsignificant percentage of nationall income is used up to pay the past debts.\n(c) Vicious Circle of High Fiscal Deficit and Low GDP Growth: Constantly\nhigh fiscal deficit leads to a situation where:\n• GDP growth remains low because of high fiscal deficit,\n• fiscal deficit remains high because of low GDP growth.\nDID YOU KNOW\n• High fiscal deficit leads to low GDP growth because of two reasons:\n) The government lacks funds for investment, and\n(i) Owing to high fiscal deficit, taxes are raised. This reduces disposable income of the people. Low\ndisposable income leads to low AD and their low inducement to invest in the economy.\n• Also, low GDP growth leads to high fiscal deficit. Because, low GDP generates low revenue for the\n(d) Crowding-out: High fiscal deficit leads to 'Crowding-out Effect'.\n• This is a situation when high borrowings by the government (owing to high fiscal deficit)\nreduces the availability of funds (in the money market) for the private investors.\nAccordingly, overall investment in the economy is reduced.\n(e) Erosion of Government Credibility: High fiscal deficit (and consequently, the mounting\nnational debt) erodes credibility of the government in the domestic as well as international\nmoney market.\n• \"Credit rating' of the government (and the economy) is lowered. Owing to lower credit\nrating, global investors start withdrawing their investment from the domestic economy.\nConsequently, GDP growth is reduced.\nBriefly, fiscal deficit must NOT be allowed to rise beyond manageable limits (about 3 per cent of GOP\nis considered to be manageable). High fiscal deficit signals fiscal indiscipline.\nIt points to a situation when GDP growth is low and unemployment is high. The economy slips io\nstagnation and revival becomes difficult without FDI (foreign direct investment).\nJAVLR 10.3\nFISCAL DEFICIT AND ITS IMPLICATIONS\n478\nGovernment Budget and the Economy\n(il) Primary Deficit\n* Primary deficit is the difference between fiscal deficit and interest payment.\nPrimary Deficit = Fiscal deficit - Interest payment\nPD = FD - IP\nHere, PD = Primary deficit; FD = Fiscal deficit; IP = Interest payment.)\n• While fiscal deficit shows borrowing requirement of the government inclusive of interest payment\non the past loans, primary deficit shows borrowing requirement of the government exclusive of\ninterest payment.\n• In other words, primary deficit indicates government borrowings on account of current year\nexpenditures and current year receipts of the government.\nImplications\n(a) Implications of primary deficit are similar to those of fiscal deficit. The only difference is that\nprimary deficit does not carry the load of interest payments on account of the past loans.\n(b) Primary deficit just indicates borrowings when: Current year expenditure > Current year revenue.\n3 AVLR 10.4\nPRIMARY DEFICIT AND ITS IMPLICATIONS\nBasis of Difference\n1. Meaning\n2. Indication\nTable 10.2: Revenue Deficit, Fiscal Deficit and Primary Deficit\nRevenue Deficit\nFiscal Deficit\n- It is the excess of revenue\n- It is the excess of total\nexpenditure over revenue\nexpenditure over total\nreceipts.\nreceipts, other than\nRevenue Deficit\nborrowings.\n= Revenue expenditure\nFiscal Deficit\n- Revenue receipts\n= Budget expenditure\n- Budget receipts other\nthan borrowings\n- It indicates the need\nfor borrowings by the\ngovernment to manage its\nbudgetary expenditure.\n- It indicates the extent\nof borrowings by the\ngovernment when interest\npayment is accounted for.\nPrimary Deficit\n- It is the difference between\nfiscal deficit and interest\npayment.\nPrimary Deficit\n= Fiscal deficit\n- It indicates the extent\nof borrowings by the\ngovernment when interest\npayment is not accounted\n3. Cause and Impact\n- High revenue deficit\nbasically because of low tax\nreceipts and high expenditure\non subsidies.\n- It leads to borrowings.\n- High fiscal deficit (in terms\nof borrowings) points to the\nlack of fiscal discipline in the\ncountry.\n- It lowers credit-rating of the\ncountry in the international\nmoney market.\n- Primary deficit points to the\nneed tor borrowings even\nwhen interest payment on\nthe existing loans is ignored.\n- It reflects continuous lack\nof fiscal discipline in the\ncountry.\n479","file":"macro 10/IMG_0605.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0606","image":"pages/IMG_0606.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\nQ. 15. What does zero primary deficit mean?\nAns. It means the government resorts to borrowing only to clear the backlog of interest payments.\nThere are no borrowing because of the excess of current year expenditure over the current\nyear revenue. Simply because, current year expenditure happens to be equal to current year\nrevenue. It is a sign of fiscal discipline or fiscal responsibility on the part or the governmen\nQ. 16. A government budget shows a primary deficit of & 6,900 crore. The revenue expenditure o\ninterest payment is * 400 crore. How much is the fiscal deficit?\nAns. Fiscal Deficit = Primary deficit + Interest payment\n= · 6,900 crore + F 400 crore\n= = 7,300 crore.\nHow can the gulf between capital expenditure and capital receipts be reduced without\nborrowing? Suggest two ways.\nAns.\n(i) The government can resort to disinvestment, i.e., selling its stake in public sector enterprises.\n(ii) The government can sell its surplus land.\nQ. 18. Can there be a fiscal deficit without a revenue deficit?\nObviously yes. Because fiscal deficit is worked out by accounting for both the revenue and capital\nreceipts and expenditures of the government. So that, even when revenue receipts and revenue\nexpenditure are in a state of balance, there could be excess of capital expenditure over capital\nreceipts, causing fiscal deficit.\nAn Illustration on the Estimation of Various Types of Budget Deficits\nThe illustration is based on the following set of data drawn from Economic Survey, 2024-25.\nTable 10.3: Provisional Actuals on the Budgetary Status of the Government of India (2024-25)\nR in crore\n1. Revenue Receipts\n27,28,412\n2. Revenue Expenditure\n34,94,036\n17,14,131\n3. Capital Receipts\n9,48,507\n4. Capital Expenditure\n5. Total Receipts (1+3)\n44,42,543\n6. Total Expenditure (2+4)\n44,42,543\n60,461\n7. Recoveries of Loans and Other Receipts\n16,53,670\n8. Borrowings and Other Liabilities\n9. Interest Payment\n10,63,871\nSource: Economic Survey, 2024-25\nUsing the estimation procedure discussed earlier, we get the following estimates of different type\nof budget deficit:\n(i) Revenue Deficit = Revenue Expenditure - Revenue Receipts\n= 7 34,94,036 crore - & 27,28,412 crore\n= < 7,65,624 crore\nGovernment Budget and the Economy\n(il) Fiscal Deficit\n= Total Expenditure - (Revenue Receipts + Recoveries of Loans and Other Receipts)\n= 7 44,42,543 crore - (R 27,28,412 crore + 7 60,461 crore)\n=· 16,53,670 crore\nFiscal Deficit = Borrowings and Other Liabilities\n= · 16,53,670 crore\n(il) rimary Deficit = Fiscal Deficit - Interest Payment\n= F 16,53,670 crore - & 10,63,871 crore\n= 7 5,89,799 crore\n[Implying that:\nFiscal Deficit = Primary Deficit + Interest Payment\n= · 5,89,799 crore + F 10,63,871 crore\n= · 16,53,670 crore]\n10.5 BALANCED AND UNBALANCED BUDGET\n10.5.1 Concept of Balanced Budget-Its Merits and Demerits\nAbalanced budget is that budget in which government receipts are equal to government expenditure.\nBalanced Budget:\nGovernment Receipts = Government Expenditure\nMerits and Demerits of Balanced Budget\nMerits\n• The government does not indulge in wasteful expenditure.\n• A balanced budget ensures financial stability. It signals fiscal discipline in the economy.\nHowever, during the general depression of 1930's, the policy of balanced budget was severely\ncriticised. It was then that the following shortcomings of a balanced budget were highlighted.\nShortcomings or Demerits\n• Balanced budget does not offer any solution to the problem of unemployment. Particularly.\nwhen unemployment is linked with the lack of AD. It happened in most European Countries\nduring 1930's.\n• Balanced budget is not conducive to growth in less developed countries. Kick start of growth\nin these economies depends on a big-push of investment expenditure by the government.\nThis often leads to deficit budget.\n10.5.2 Concept of Unbalanced Budget-Its Types, Merits and Demerits\nAn unbalanced budget is that budget in which receipts and expenditures of the government are not\nequal. This may be a situation of:\n(i) Surplus Budget, or\nidget.","file":"macro 10/IMG_0606.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0607","image":"pages/IMG_0607.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\n(i) Surplus Budget\nThis is a budget in which government receipts are greater than government expenditures.\nSurplus Budget:\nEstimated Government Receipts > Estimated Government Expenditures\nMerits and Demerits of Surplus Budget\n• Surplus budget (when, receipts > expenditures) is desired when the economy is battling inflation\ndue to excess AD.\n• Surplus budget plugs the inflationary gap by lowering the level of AD. AD is lowered on account of\n(a) rise in revenue collection by the government, and\n(b) fall in government expenditure.\nDemerits\n• As surplus budget tends to lower the level of AD in the economy, it is not desired during periods\nof depression.\n• If surplus budget policy is constantly pursued by the government, AD may reduce to a level that\ncauses unemployment in the economy. The economy may be driven into a low level equilibrium\nLap.\n(il) Deficit Budget\nThis is a budget in which government expenditures are greater than government receipts.\nDeficit Budget:\nEstimated Government Expenditures > Estimated Government Receipts\nKeynes and other modern economists stress significance of deficit budget, highlighting its merits.\nMerits and Demerits of Deficit Budget\nMerits\n• Keynes recommends deficit budget as a key instrument to correct the state of depression.\n• According to him, depression is that phase of economic activity when the level of investment\nis low owing to the low level of AD. Consequently, planned output is much lower than the full\nemployment level of output. Unemployment becomes a national problem. Deficit budget raises\nthe level of AD in two ways:\n(a) Directly by way of high government expenditure, and\n(b) Indirectly by inducing greater (investment and consumption) expenditure by the people.\nDemerits\n• Deficit budget is not desired during periods of inflation. It is a period when the AD exceeds AS\nat full employment.\n• Deficit budget in such situations (when AS cannot increase) would further increase the guir\nbetween AD and AS. Consequently, inflationary gap would rise and wage-price spiral (when\nwages increase with prices and prices increase with wages) may set in.\n482\nGovernment Budget and the Economy\nABILITY ZONE\nSignificance or Importance of Public Expenditure\n(with reference to the Indian Economy)\nFollowing observations highlight the significance of public expenditure with reference to the Indian\neconomy:\n(1) Public expenditure accelerates the pace of GDP growth. Higher rate of GOP growth is achieved\nthrough\n> investment expenditure in public sector enterprises,\n› capital grants by the government for the purchase of capital equipment,\n› subsidies for the purchase of inputs, and\n› purchase of farm output at the minimum support price.\n(i) Public expenditure promotes equality in the distribution of income and wealth. This is achieved by\noffering old-age pensions, as well as by providing free food, education, and health services to the\nBelow Poverty Line Population.\nii) Public expenditure plays a significant role in restoring economic stability. Particularly, when the\neconomy is battling economic recession. The government expenditure (consumption expenditure\nas well as investment expenditure) raises the level of AD. Only when AD is raised that the vicious\ncircle of economic recession is broken.\n(iv) Public expenditure generates investment-friendly environment in the economy. The government\nspends money on infrastructural development. It constructs roads, dams, bridges. It introduces\nfaster and convenient means of transportation. Such facilities promote inducement to investment.\n• Briefly, public expenditure is indispensable in any welfare state like India. It not only promotes GDP\ngrowth, but also promotes social welfare.\nPublic Goods, Private Goods, Common Goods and Club Goods\nRivalry\nExcludability\nPublic Goods\nNon-rival\nNon-excludable\nPrivate Goods\nRival\nExcludable\nCommon Goods\nRival\nNon-excludable\nClub Goods\nNon-rival\nExcludable\nDefence of the country\nPrivately owned house\nFishing in a countryside pond\nSubscription of Netflix\n*Rivalry: Use by one limits the availability of the good for the other.\n*Excludability: Disallowing someone from using a good by setting a price on that good.\nPublic Goods vs. Private Goods\n• Public Goods\n• Public goods are the goods which are meant for collective use by all sections of the\nsociety, Example: Law and order, defence and public administration. These goods have two\ncharacteristics:\n(i) Non-rivalrous: These goods are non-rivalrous in the sense that the use of a public good by one\nindividual does not reduce its availability for the other.\nExample: Parks, national defence, when used by one individual are not reduced for the other.\n483","file":"macro 10/IMG_0607.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0608","image":"pages/IMG_0608.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\n(i) Non-excludability: These goods are non-exclusive in the sense that the use of a public good by\nthose individuals who pay for it does not exclude others from using it who do not pay for it.\nExample: Street light may be a paid utility service. But those who do not pay cannot be excluded\nfrom the use/benefit of it. These goods, therefore, have the problem of free-riders: even those\nwho do not pay for them continue to use them.\n• Private Goods\n• The opposite of a public good is a private good. A private good is rivalrous and excludable.\nPersonal car of an individual is an example of a private good.\n• It does not carry the characteristics of\n) non-rivalrous, and\n(ii) non-excludability.\n• Because, this particular car when purchased by me reduces the availability of cars by one unit,\nand secondly, since I have paid for it, others obviously have no right to use it.\nFree Rider Problem\nA free rider is a person who enjoys the benefits of goods and services without contributing to the full cost\nor partial cost of providing them. Consumers can take advantage of public goods without contributing\nsufficiently to their creation. This is called the free rider problem.\n• The free rider problem is usually more acute in the case of public goods. The free rider problem exists\nwhen people enjoy the benefits of government provided goods independent of whether they pay for\nthem. If too many consumers decide to 'free ride', private costs exceed private benefits and the incentive\nto provide the good or service through the market disappears. The market, thus, fails to provide a good\nor service for which there is a need.\nProgressive and Regressive Nature of Taxation is Different from\nProgressive and Regressive Rates of Taxation\n• Progressive Rate: The rate of taxation increases with increase in income.\n• Regressive Rate: The rate of taxation decreases with increase in income.\n• Proportionate Rate: The rate of taxation remains constant with increase or decrease in income.\n• Progressive Tax: A tax which (in terms of nature/effect) causes greater real burden on the rich compared\nto the poor.\n• Regressive Tax: A tax which (in terms of nature/effect) causes greater real burden on the poor compared\nto the rich. This implies that even a proportionate tax is a regressive tax.\nDoes Balanced Budget Leave Aggregate Demand Unaffected in the Economy?\n• No is the obvious reply. This is how it happens:\nBalanced Budget means:\nGovernment receipts = Government expenditure\nAssume tax as the only source of government receipts,\nTax of (say) R 100 = Expenditure of r 100\nExpenditure of * 100 increases AD by & 100.\nTax of r 100 does not decrease AD by R 100.\nTax of 7 100 decreases disposable income of the people by & 100.\n484\nuPC is assumed to be 0.5, then reduction in disposable income by & 100 would reduce consumpti\nGovernment Budget and the Economy\n(expenditure by 0.5 * 100= ≤ 50 which is MPC times decrease in income.\nthus, because of tax of & 100, AD would decrease by 7 50 only.\nMet increase in AD = 100 (increase in AD owing to government expenditure) - 50 (decrease in AD\nowing to tax\n= 750.\ntus a balanced budget is expected to increase AD. Accordingly, balanced budget is a good policy\nstrument to increase AD when the economy is close to achieving tull employment\nfower Points [Summary of the Chapter]\nAlget statement of expected receipts and expenditure of the government over the period ofa financial\near, April 1 - March 31.\n• Objectives:\n1) GDP growth\n(i) Allocation of resources\n) Provision of public goods\n(v) Redistribution of income and wealth\n(v) Balanced regional growth\n(vi) Employment opportunities\n(vi) Economic stability\n• Structure of the Budget includes\n) revenue budget showing revenue receipts and revenue expenditure of the government, and\n( capital budget showing capital receipts and capital expenditure of the government.\n• Looked at from a different angle, structure of the budget includes:\n) budget receipts (including revenue receipts and capital receipts), and\n(1) budget expenditures (including revenue expenditure and capital expenditure).\n• Budget Receipts are the estimated money receipts of the government from all sources during a fiscal year.\n• Revenue Receipts are those receipts:\n(i) which do not cause any reduction in assets [Example: Income from public sector enterprises\n(i) which do not create any liability for the government [Example: Tax receipts of the government\n• Capital Receipts are those receipts:\n) which create liability for the government [Example: Funds received by the government as loans),\n(i) which cause reduction in assets of the government [Example: Disinvestment in public sect\n* Budget Expenditure is the estimated expenditure of the government relating to its development and\nnon-development programmes during a fiscal year.\n• Revenue Expenditure is that expenditure by the governmen\n) which does not cause increase in government assets (Example: Expenditure on law & order],\nil) which does not cause any reduction in government liability (Example: Expenditure on old-age\n485","file":"macro 10/IMG_0608.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0609","image":"pages/IMG_0609.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\n• Capital Expenditure is that expenditure by the government\n(i) which causes increase in government assets [Example: Expenditure on the construction of roads],\n(i) which causes reduction in government liability [Example: Payment of loan by the government].\n• Plan Expenditure is related to specified plans and programmes of development, as well as assistance\nof the central government to the state governments. [Example: Expenditure on the construction of\ncanals for irrigation.]\n• Non-plan Expenditure is related to expenditure on routine functioning of the government.\n[Example: () Expenditure on law & order, and (i) Expenditure on defence & subsidies.]\n• Balanced and Unbalanced Budget\n• Balanced Budget: Total expenditure = Total receipts.\n• Unbalanced Budget:\n> Surplus Budget: Total expenditure ‹ Total receipts.\n> Deficit Budget: Total expenditure > Total receipts.\n- Budget Deficit is the excess of total expenditure over total receipts of the government.\n• Types:\n(i) Revenue deficit,\n(ii) Fiscal deficit,\n(iii) Primary deficit.\n• Revenue Deficit: Revenue expenditure - Revenue receipts.\n• Implication:\nSince revenue receipts and revenue expenditures are related largely to recurring expenses of the\ngovernment (as on administration and maintenance),\n• High revenue deficit gives a warning to the government either to cut its expenditure or increase its\ntax/non-tax receipts.\n• Fiscal Deficit: (Revenue expenditure + Capital expenditure) - (Revenue receipts + Capital receipts other\nthan government borrowing).\n• Implications:\n(i) Inflationary spiral\n(ii) National debt\n(i Vicious circle of high fiscal deficit and low GDP growth\n(iv) Crowding-out\n(v) Erosion of government credibility\n• Primary Deficit: Fiscal deficit - Interest payment.\n• Implication: Primary deficit indicates the extent to which the government needs to borrow to\nimplement its budgetary programmes and policies for the year ahead.\nBalanced Budget: It raises the level of AD in the economy, though moderately, It is recommended when\nthe economy is close to achieving full employment.\n• Surplus Budget: It is recommended when there is an inflationary gap and AD needs to be reduced.\n• Deficit Budget: It is recommended when there is a state of depression and AD needs to be raised.\n486\ncompetency 6\n1 Multiple Che\nChoose the corre\n1. Identity,\ncountry.\n(a) Wat\n(b) Inte\n(c) Dire\n(d) Exp\n2. The buc\nis refert\n(a) Sur\n(c) Bal\n3. Identif\n(a) FrE\n(b) De\n(c) Pu\n(d) St\n4. Accorc\ngover\nand\n225 la\n(a) re\n(c) tr\n5. Expen\nExpre\n(a) r\n(c) c\n6. Ident\nan ec\n(a)\n(\n(c) I\n7. The k\nrefer\n(a)\n(c)","file":"macro 10/IMG_0609.HEIC"}],"boxes":[{"title":"Check Ability Zone for details on how public goods are different from other goods!","page":"IMG_0598","text":"(4) Redistribution of Income and Wealth: Taxation and subsidies are important components a\ngovernment budget. These are used to correct the distribution of income and wealth.\n• Taxes are imposed on the rich and subsidies are offered to the poor. Accordingly disposable\nincome of the rich is reduced, while the disposable income of the poor is increased.\n• Free LPG connection to the BPL population is an important example of subsidy to the poor.\nf) Balanced Regional Growth: The budgetary policy places priority on the development of\nbackward regions in the country. This is achieved through liberal tax laws for the backward\nregions.\n• Establishment of SEZ (special economic zones) in the backward regions through liberal tax\nlaws may be cited as an example.\n(6) Employment Generation: Budgetary policy focuses on the generation of employment\nopportunities through investment in public enterprises.\n• Budgetary provisions are made for schemes like MGNREGA offering employment to poorer\nsections of the society.\n(7) Economic Stability: Revenue and expenditure policies of the government are used as fiscal\ninstruments to minimise the inflationary and deflationary swings in the economy.\n• Inflation is corrected through higher taxation and lower government expenditure.\n• Deflation is managed through lower taxation and higher government expenditure.\nLily the government tries to manage its revenue and expenditure in such a way that the\n(I growth is accelerated, inflationary and deflationary pressures are eliminated, and inequality is\neduced. This imparts stability to the process of growth.","image":"pages/IMG_0598.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"IMG_0598","text":"4.1. What do you mean by Fiscal discipline'? What happens if fiscal discipline is not maintained in\nthe economy?\nAns. Fiscal discipline refers to the state of balance between revenues and expenditures of the\nthe government.\ngovernment. It calls for a necessary check on the expenditures in view of the limited revenues o\n465","image":"pages/IMG_0598.jpg"},{"title":"DID YOU KNOW","page":"IMG_0600","text":", Certain taxes are called 'paper taxes.\n- These refer to the taxes like gift tax in India which carry their significance only on paper.\n- These taxes are of little or no significance in terms of their revenue yield.\n, The introduction of GST (Goods and Services Tax) subsumed various indirect taxes ike VAT, excise\nduty, and service tax, creating a unified tax syster that simplifies compliance and enhances\nefficiency in India's tax framework.\nTable 10.1: Direct Tax vs. Indirect Tax\nBasis of Difference\n1. Meaning\nDirect Tax\nIndirect Tax\n- It is the tax which is finally paid by the person - It is the tax which is imposed on one person but\non whom it is legally imposed.\nis paid by another.\n2. Shifting of Burden - The burden of direct taxes cannot be shifted - The burden of indirect taxes can be shifted to\nto other person.\nothers.\n3. Nature of Tax\n- Direct taxes are generally progressive in nature. - Indi\nact taxes are generally regressive in nature.\n4. Example\n- Income tax, corporate profit tax.\n- GST, customs duty.\n(ii) Non-tax Receipts\nNon-tax receipts are those receipts which arise from sources other than taxes. Some of the non-tax\nreceipts are as follows:\n(a) Fees: A fee is a payment to the government for the services that it renders to the people.\nExamples:\nLand registration fees, birth and death registration fees, passport fees, court fees, etc.\nIt is to be noted that fee is not a payment (price) for commercial service. It is a payment for\nadministrative and judicial services provided to the people.\n(b) Fines: Fines are those payments which are made by the law breakers to the govemment.\nThese are economic punishments for breaking laws. The aim is not to earn revenue, but to\nmake people respectful to the laws.\n(c) Escheat: Escheat refers to that income of the state which arises out of the property left by\nthe people without a legal heir. There are no claimants of such property. The government\nmakes revenue out of it.\n(d) Special Assessment: Special assessment is that payment which is made by the owners of those\nproperties whose value has appreciated due to developmental activities of the government.\nExample:\nWhen as a result of construction of roads or provision of sewerage system or construction\nof drains, etc., value of the neighbouring property or its rental value appreciates, then a\npart of the developmental expenditure is recovered from the owners of such property by\nway of special assessment.","image":"pages/IMG_0600.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"IMG_0601","text":"Q. 4. Is borrowing by the government a revenue receipt?\nAns. No, because it creates a liability (for the government) of repayment.\nQ. 5. Explain through an example, how the burden of an indirect tax is shifted.\nAns. GST is an indirect tax. A shopkeeper pays GST to the government. But, the shopkeeper recovers\nthis tax from the customers as a part of price of the commodity sold. So, impact of GST (an\nindirect tax) is ultimately shifted to the consumers.\nQ. 6. Categorise the following as direct tax or indirect tax:\n(i) Corporation tax\n(ii) GST\n(iv) Capital gains tax\n(v) Income tax\n(iii) Service tax\n(vi) CGST and SGST\nAns. Direct Tax: (i), (iv), (v);\nIndirect Tax: (i), (ili), (vi)\n10.3.1 (b) Capital Receipts\nCapital receipts are those money receipts of the government which show the following two\ncharacteristics:\n(i) These receipts create a liability for the government.\nExample:\nLoans by the government are a liability. These are to be paid back. These are, therefore,\nthe capital receipts of the government.\n(ii) These receipts cause reduction in assets of the government.\nExample:\nMoney received by the government by selling its shares (say of Air India) would cause\nreduction in assets of the government. These are, therefore, to be treated as capital receipts.\nIn short, capital receipts are those money receipts of the government which either create a liability\nfor the government or cause a reduction in its assets.\nIn india, capital receipts of the government budget are often classified as under:\nCapital Receipts\n(i) Recovery of\n(i) Borrowings and\nOther Liabilities\n(lil) Other\nReceipts\nGiven below a detailed explanation of these constituents of a capital receipts.\nGovernment Budget and the Economy\n(i) Recovery of Loans: The central government offers loans to the state governments to cope\nwith financial crises. When these loans are recovered, assets of the government are reduced.\nAccordingly, these are classified as capital receipts.\n(i) Borrowings and Other Liabilities: While lending creates assets, borrowing creates liability.\nAccordingly, borrowings are to be treated as capital receipts. It may be noted that the\ngovernment borrows money from:\n• The general public (Borrowings from the general public are called market borrowings).\n• The Reserve Bank of India, and\n• The rest of the world.\n(il) Other Receipts: These include items like 'disinvestment\". It is the opposite of investment.\nDisinvestment occurs when the government sells off its shares of public sector enterprises\nto private sector.\n• It involves transfer of ownership of public sector enterprises to the private entrepreneurs,\nleading to privatisation. Money received through disinvestment is treated as capital receipt\nbecause it causes reduction in assets of the government.","image":"pages/IMG_0601.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"IMG_0601","text":"Q. 7. Read the description and examples related to revenue receipts and capital receipts given\nbelow and state their impact on asset and liability status using 1 or 4 arrow in the blanks\ngiven on the right hand side of the description, and by specifying Y or N in the blanks given on\nthe left hand side of the description.\nBudget Receipts\n(A) Impact on\nAsset & Liability\nStatus\n(B) Impact on\nAsset & Liability\nstatus\nLiability\nTaxes received\nby the government\nGrants received\nby the government\nDisinvestment\nAns. (A) Liability - N\nAsset - N\n(B) Liability - T\nAsset - V\nQ. 8. Categorise the following as revenue receipts and capital receipts:\n(i) Profits of a PSU\n(ii) Recovery of loans\n(iii) Tax receipts\n(iv) Interest received on loans\n(v) Public debt\n(vi) Disinvestment\n(vii) Borrowings from public\n(vili) Gifts and grants received from abroad\n(ix) Borrowings from rest of the world\n(x) Receipts from sale of a public sector undertaking\nAns. Revenue Receipts (Neither creates liability nor reduces assets): (i), i, (v), (vii)\nCapital Receipts (Increases liability): (v), (vii), (ix)\n(Reduces assets) : (ii), (vi), (x)\n471","image":"pages/IMG_0601.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS","page":"IMG_0602","text":"Important Items of Revenue Expenditure in the indian Government Budget\n• These are:\n) Wage bill of the government\n(il) Interest payments\n(i) Expenditure on subsidies\n(iv) Defence purchases\nImportant Convention regarding Grants\nAs a matter of convention, all grants given by the centre to the state governments and the governments\nof Union territories) are treated as revenue expenditure, even when some grants may result in the creation\nof assets.\n10.3.2 (b) Capital Expenditure\nCapital expenditure of the government is that expenditure which shows the following two\ncharacteristics:\n(i) It creates assets for the government.\nExample:\nEquity (or shares) of the domestic or multinational corporations purchased by the government\ncreates assets for the government. Accordingly, this is to be treated as capital expenditure.\n(i) It causes reduction in liabilities of the government.\nExample:\nRepayment of loans certainly reduces liability of the government. Accordingly, this is to be F\ntreated as capital expenditure.\nIn short, capital expenditure refers to the estimated expenditure of the government in a fiscal year\nwhich creates assets or causes a reduction in liabilities.","image":"pages/IMG_0602.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS","page":"IMG_0602","text":"Important Items of Capital Expenditure in the Indian Government Budget\n• These are:\n(i) Expenditure on land and building.\n(i Expenditure on machinery and equipment.\n(iii) Purchase of shares.\n(iv) Loans by the central government to the state governments or state corporations.\nPlan and Non-plan Expenditure\nBudget expenditure (revenue expenditure + capital expenditure) is also classified as plan and non-\nplan expenditure. Following is the difference:\n473","image":"pages/IMG_0602.jpg"},{"title":"? DID YOU KNOW","page":"IMG_0603","text":"Government Budget and the Economy\n• In India, non-plan expenditure is a significant part of the total government expenditure. Which is\nwhy ical discipline in the country often remains a serious challenge.\n+ After the abolition of planning commission, the government is also considering to abolish the\nclassification of budgetary expenditure as plan and non-plan expenditure.","image":"pages/IMG_0603.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"IMG_0603","text":"q. 11. Categorise the following as revenue expenditure and capital expenditure:\n(i) Expenditure on purchasing machinery for a factory.\n(i) Repayment of amount borrowed from World Bank.\n(i) Provision of subsidies by the government.\n(iv) Interest paid on loans taken by government.\n(v) Expenditure on collection of taxes.\n(vi) Expenditure incurred on defence services.\n(vii) Purchase of cranes for building a bridge.\n(vili) Repayment of amount borrowed from USA.\n(ix) Loans granted to Haryana Government by GOl.\n(x) Repayment of a part of outstanding loan towards IME.\n(xi) Expenditure on administrative services.\n(xii) Provision of old-age pension.\nAns. Revenue Expenditure (Neither reduces liability nor creates assets): (ii, (iv), (v), (vi), (xi), (xi)\nCapital Expenditure (Reduces liability): (i), (viii), (x)\n(Increases assets): (i), (vii), (ix)\nQ. 12. Read the description and examples related to revenue expenditure and capital expenditure\ngiven below and state their impact on asset and liability status using T or v arrow in the blanks\ngiven on the right hand side of the description, and by specifying Y or N in the blanks given on\nthe left hand side of the description.\nBudget Expenditure\n(A) Impact on\nAsset & Liability\n(B) Impact on\nStatus\nLiability.\nASSeL\nScholarship\nOld-age pension\nRepayment\nof loans\nInfrastructural\nexpenditure\nAns. (A) Liability\nAsset - N\n(B) Liability - /\nAsset - T\nQ. 13. How is revenue expenditure different from capital expenditure in terms of their meaning and\nsignificance?\nAns. Following observations highlight the difference between revenue expenditure and capital expenditure:\nBasis of Difference\nRevenue Receipts\nCapital Receipts\n1. Meaning\n• Revenue expenditure does not\n• Capital expenditure impacts\nimpact asset-liability status of the\nasset-liability status of the\ngovernment.\ngovernment.\n- Assets and liabilities are not\n- Assets are raised.\nincreased or decreased.\n- Liabilities are lowered.\n475","image":"pages/IMG_0603.jpg"},{"title":"DID YOU KNOW","page":"IMG_0605","text":"• High fiscal deficit leads to low GDP growth because of two reasons:\n) The government lacks funds for investment, and\n(i) Owing to high fiscal deficit, taxes are raised. This reduces disposable income of the people. Low\ndisposable income leads to low AD and their low inducement to invest in the economy.\n• Also, low GDP growth leads to high fiscal deficit. Because, low GDP generates low revenue for the\n(d) Crowding-out: High fiscal deficit leads to 'Crowding-out Effect'.\n• This is a situation when high borrowings by the government (owing to high fiscal deficit)\nreduces the availability of funds (in the money market) for the private investors.\nAccordingly, overall investment in the economy is reduced.\n(e) Erosion of Government Credibility: High fiscal deficit (and consequently, the mounting\nnational debt) erodes credibility of the government in the domestic as well as international\nmoney market.\n• \"Credit rating' of the government (and the economy) is lowered. Owing to lower credit\nrating, global investors start withdrawing their investment from the domestic economy.\nConsequently, GDP growth is reduced.\nBriefly, fiscal deficit must NOT be allowed to rise beyond manageable limits (about 3 per cent of GOP\nis considered to be manageable). High fiscal deficit signals fiscal indiscipline.\nIt points to a situation when GDP growth is low and unemployment is high. The economy slips io\nstagnation and revival becomes difficult without FDI (foreign direct investment).\nJAVLR 10.3\nFISCAL DEFICIT AND ITS IMPLICATIONS\n478\nGovernment Budget and the Economy\n(il) Primary Deficit\n* Primary deficit is the difference between fiscal deficit and interest payment.\nPrimary Deficit = Fiscal deficit - Interest payment\nPD = FD - IP\nHere, PD = Primary deficit; FD = Fiscal deficit; IP = Interest payment.)\n• While fiscal deficit shows borrowing requirement of the government inclusive of interest payment\non the past loans, primary deficit shows borrowing requirement of the government exclusive of\ninterest payment.\n• In other words, primary deficit indicates government borrowings on account of current year\nexpenditures and current year receipts of the government.\nImplications\n(a) Implications of primary deficit are similar to those of fiscal deficit. The only difference is that\nprimary deficit does not carry the load of interest payments on account of the past loans.\n(b) Primary deficit just indicates borrowings when: Current year expenditure > Current year revenue.\n3 AVLR 10.4\nPRIMARY DEFICIT AND ITS IMPLICATIONS\nBasis of Difference\n1. Meaning\n2. Indication\nTable 10.2: Revenue Deficit, Fiscal Deficit and Primary Deficit\nRevenue Deficit\nFiscal Deficit\n- It is the excess of revenue\n- It is the excess of total\nexpenditure over revenue\nexpenditure over total\nreceipts.\nreceipts, other than\nRevenue Deficit\nborrowings.\n= Revenue expenditure\nFiscal Deficit\n- Revenue receipts\n= Budget expenditure\n- Budget receipts other\nthan borrowings\n- It indicates the need\nfor borrowings by the\ngovernment to manage its\nbudgetary expenditure.\n- It indicates the extent\nof borrowings by the\ngovernment when interest\npayment is accounted for.\nPrimary Deficit\n- It is the difference between\nfiscal deficit and interest\npayment.\nPrimary Deficit\n= Fiscal deficit\n- It indicates the extent\nof borrowings by the\ngovernment when interest\npayment is not accounted\n3. Cause and Impact\n- High revenue deficit\nbasically because of low tax\nreceipts and high expenditure\non subsidies.\n- It leads to borrowings.\n- High fiscal deficit (in terms\nof borrowings) points to the\nlack of fiscal discipline in the\ncountry.\n- It lowers credit-rating of the\ncountry in the international\nmoney market.\n- Primary deficit points to the\nneed tor borrowings even\nwhen interest payment on\nthe existing loans is ignored.\n- It reflects continuous lack\nof fiscal discipline in the\ncountry.\n479","image":"pages/IMG_0605.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"IMG_0606","text":"Q. 15. What does zero primary deficit mean?\nAns. It means the government resorts to borrowing only to clear the backlog of interest payments.\nThere are no borrowing because of the excess of current year expenditure over the current\nyear revenue. Simply because, current year expenditure happens to be equal to current year\nrevenue. It is a sign of fiscal discipline or fiscal responsibility on the part or the governmen\nQ. 16. A government budget shows a primary deficit of & 6,900 crore. The revenue expenditure o\ninterest payment is * 400 crore. How much is the fiscal deficit?\nAns. Fiscal Deficit = Primary deficit + Interest payment\n= · 6,900 crore + F 400 crore\n= = 7,300 crore.\nHow can the gulf between capital expenditure and capital receipts be reduced without\nborrowing? Suggest two ways.\nAns.\n(i) The government can resort to disinvestment, i.e., selling its stake in public sector enterprises.\n(ii) The government can sell its surplus land.\nQ. 18. Can there be a fiscal deficit without a revenue deficit?\nObviously yes. Because fiscal deficit is worked out by accounting for both the revenue and capital\nreceipts and expenditures of the government. So that, even when revenue receipts and revenue\nexpenditure are in a state of balance, there could be excess of capital expenditure over capital\nreceipts, causing fiscal deficit.\nAn Illustration on the Estimation of Various Types of Budget Deficits\nThe illustration is based on the following set of data drawn from Economic Survey, 2024-25.\nTable 10.3: Provisional Actuals on the Budgetary Status of the Government of India (2024-25)\nR in crore\n1. Revenue Receipts\n27,28,412\n2. Revenue Expenditure\n34,94,036\n17,14,131\n3. Capital Receipts\n9,48,507\n4. Capital Expenditure\n5. Total Receipts (1+3)\n44,42,543\n6. Total Expenditure (2+4)\n44,42,543\n60,461\n7. Recoveries of Loans and Other Receipts\n16,53,670\n8. Borrowings and Other Liabilities\n9. Interest Payment\n10,63,871\nSource: Economic Survey, 2024-25\nUsing the estimation procedure discussed earlier, we get the following estimates of different type\nof budget deficit:\n(i) Revenue Deficit = Revenue Expenditure - Revenue Receipts\n= 7 34,94,036 crore - & 27,28,412 crore\n= < 7,65,624 crore\nGovernment Budget and the Economy\n(il) Fiscal Deficit\n= Total Expenditure - (Revenue Receipts + Recoveries of Loans and Other Receipts)\n= 7 44,42,543 crore - (R 27,28,412 crore + 7 60,461 crore)\n=· 16,53,670 crore\nFiscal Deficit = Borrowings and Other Liabilities\n= · 16,53,670 crore\n(il) rimary Deficit = Fiscal Deficit - Interest Payment\n= F 16,53,670 crore - & 10,63,871 crore\n= 7 5,89,799 crore\n[Implying that:\nFiscal Deficit = Primary Deficit + Interest Payment\n= · 5,89,799 crore + F 10,63,871 crore\n= · 16,53,670 crore]","image":"pages/IMG_0606.jpg"},{"title":"ABILITY ZONE","page":"IMG_0607","text":"Significance or Importance of Public Expenditure\n(with reference to the Indian Economy)\nFollowing observations highlight the significance of public expenditure with reference to the Indian\neconomy:\n(1) Public expenditure accelerates the pace of GDP growth. Higher rate of GOP growth is achieved\nthrough\n> investment expenditure in public sector enterprises,\n› capital grants by the government for the purchase of capital equipment,\n› subsidies for the purchase of inputs, and\n› purchase of farm output at the minimum support price.\n(i) Public expenditure promotes equality in the distribution of income and wealth. This is achieved by\noffering old-age pensions, as well as by providing free food, education, and health services to the\nBelow Poverty Line Population.\nii) Public expenditure plays a significant role in restoring economic stability. Particularly, when the\neconomy is battling economic recession. The government expenditure (consumption expenditure\nas well as investment expenditure) raises the level of AD. Only when AD is raised that the vicious\ncircle of economic recession is broken.\n(iv) Public expenditure generates investment-friendly environment in the economy. The government\nspends money on infrastructural development. It constructs roads, dams, bridges. It introduces\nfaster and convenient means of transportation. Such facilities promote inducement to investment.\n• Briefly, public expenditure is indispensable in any welfare state like India. It not only promotes GDP\ngrowth, but also promotes social welfare.\nPublic Goods, Private Goods, Common Goods and Club Goods\nRivalry\nExcludability\nPublic Goods\nNon-rival\nNon-excludable\nPrivate Goods\nRival\nExcludable\nCommon Goods\nRival\nNon-excludable\nClub Goods\nNon-rival\nExcludable\nDefence of the country\nPrivately owned house\nFishing in a countryside pond\nSubscription of Netflix\n*Rivalry: Use by one limits the availability of the good for the other.\n*Excludability: Disallowing someone from using a good by setting a price on that good.\nPublic Goods vs. Private Goods\n• Public Goods\n• Public goods are the goods which are meant for collective use by all sections of the\nsociety, Example: Law and order, defence and public administration. These goods have two\ncharacteristics:\n(i) Non-rivalrous: These goods are non-rivalrous in the sense that the use of a public good by one\nindividual does not reduce its availability for the other.\nExample: Parks, national defence, when used by one individual are not reduced for the other.\n483","image":"pages/IMG_0607.jpg"}],"subject":"macro","number":"10"},{"id":"11","title":"Foreign Exchange Rate","intro":"Foreign exchange refers to foreign currency.","lessons":[{"title":"Foreign exchange and the quotation convention","body":"Foreign exchange refers to foreign currency. Foreign exchange rate is the price paid in domestic currency for a unit of foreign currency. Throughout these lessons, quote the rate as ₹ per US dollar. A rise from ₹80/$ to ₹84/$ means the rupee buys fewer dollars and has depreciated; a fall to ₹76/$ means rupee appreciation. Always identify which currency is in the numerator before describing a movement.","formula":"","source":"IMG_0610","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"11.1 FOREIGN EXCHANGE AND FOREIGN EXCHANGE RATE"},{"title":"Demand, supply and flexible exchange-rate determination","body":"Flexible exchange rate is determined by demand and supply in the foreign exchange market. Demand for foreign exchange comes from imports, overseas investment, foreign travel, loan repayment, income payments, grants and speculation. Supply comes from exports, foreign investment, inward loans, tourism receipts, income receipts, grants and remittances. With ₹/$ on the vertical axis, demand slopes downward and supply upward; their intersection is the equilibrium rate. A higher exchange rate makes foreign goods costlier in rupees and normally reduces import-related foreign-exchange demand.","formula":"","source":"IMG_0611","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"11.2.1 Flexible Exchange Rate"},{"title":"Movements versus shifts, appreciation versus depreciation","body":"A change in the exchange rate causes movement along a given demand or supply curve. A change in imports, investment preferences, income or foreign demand shifts a curve. Greater demand for dollars shifts demand right, raising ₹/$ and depreciating the rupee. Greater supply of dollars shifts supply right, lowering ₹/$ and appreciating the rupee. Depreciation can make exports cheaper for foreigners and imports costlier for residents; actual quantity responses depend on demand conditions.","formula":"","source":"IMG_0613","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"Appreciation and Depreciation of Domestic Currency"},{"title":"Fixed rate, devaluation and revaluation","body":"Fixed exchange rate is set and maintained by the monetary authority. An official rise in ₹/$ is devaluation of the rupee; an official fall is revaluation. Depreciation/appreciation describe market-driven changes under a flexible system. To defend a fixed rate when demand for dollars exceeds supply, the authority sells foreign currency from reserves; when dollar supply exceeds demand it buys foreign currency. An overvalued domestic currency tends to create excess demand for foreign currency at the official rate.","formula":"","source":"IMG_0614","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"11.2.2 Fixed Exchange Rate"},{"title":"Managed floating and merits/demerits","body":"Managed floating combines market determination with occasional central-bank intervention to limit undesirable movements; the textbook also calls it dirty floating. Fixed rates reduce exchange uncertainty and can support trade but require reserves and may misallocate resources. Flexible rates need less reserve defence and adjust through markets but create uncertainty and exposure to external shocks. The original comparison covers the full list of merits and demerits.","formula":"","source":"IMG_0616","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"11.3 MANAGED FLOATING · 11.4 MERITS AND DEMERITS"},{"title":"Foreign exchange market: transfer, credit and hedging","body":"The foreign exchange market is the market for national currencies. The transfer function moves purchasing power between countries. The credit function supports trade through financing arrangements. Hedging reduces exchange-rate risk. Spot transactions exchange currency for current delivery; forward transactions agree today on a rate for delivery at a future date. A forward contract fixes an exchange rate; it does not guarantee that the future spot rate will equal that rate.","formula":"","source":"IMG_0618","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"11.7 FOREIGN EXCHANGE MARKET"},{"title":"Fixed versus flexible rates: named merits and demerits","textbookHeading":"11.4 MERITS AND DEMERITS OF FIXED AND FLEXIBLE EXCHANGE RATE","body":"Fixed Rate—Merits: market stability and mobility of capital; growth of international trade; stable macroeconomic policies; devaluation as a policy tool; a check on inflation. Fixed Rate—Demerits: large reserve requirements; inefficient allocation of resources; limited forex market; discouraged venture capital; possible divergence from equilibrium. Flexible Rate—Merits: fewer reserves required for rate defence; market allocation of resources; larger forex market; international mobility of liquidity; support for venture capital. Flexible Rate—Demerits: weak-currency marginalisation; market uncertainty; difficulty maintaining stable monetary policy; difficulty with bilateral trade agreements; external shocks. These are the textbook’s headings: explain the relevant mechanism instead of only listing them.","source":"IMG_0616","kind":"Exam points","formula":""}],"pages":[{"id":"IMG_0610","image":"pages/IMG_0610.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\n11.1 FOREIGN EXCHANGE AND FOREIGN EXCHANGE RATE\nMeaning and Concept of Foreign Exchange and\nForeign Exchange Rate\n• Foreign exchange refers to foreign currency. The stock of foreign exchange with a country say\nindia) refers to the stock of all foreign currencies with the kB at a point of time, e standard\npractice is to measure the entire stock in terms or us corars, by converting the value of al\ncurrencies into US dollars.\n• The rate at which domestic currency can be exchanged for a foreign currency is known as foreign\nexchange rate. It is the price paid in domestic currency for buying a unit of the foreign currency.\nExample:\nIf 80 rupees are to be paid to buy one US dollar, then the exchange rate is:\n$1:780\n• Thus, exchange rate expresses the ratio of exchange between the currencies of two countries.\nIt is the price of a currency expressed in terms of another currency. It is also called external\nvalue of the domestic currency.\nIn the words of Crowther, \"The rate of exchange measures number of units of one currency which is\nexchanged in the foreign market for one unit of another.\"\n11.2 FLEXIBLE AND FIXED EXCHANGE RATE\nExchange rate is broadly classified as:\n11.2.1 Flexible exchange rate, and\n11.2.2 Fixed exchange rate.\nFollowing is a brief description of both these types:\n11.2.1 Flexible Exchange Rate\n• Flexible rate of exchange (also called floating rate of exchange) is that rate which is determined\nby the supply-demand forces in the foreign exchange market.\n• It is also called \"free exchange rate' as it is determined by the free play of supply and demand\nforces in the international money market.\n• The exchange rate at which demand for foreign currency is equal to its supply is called Par Rate\nof Exchange or Equilibrium Rate of Exchange.\nDetermination of Flexible Exchange Rate\ndemand for and supply of foreign exchange are the two basic determinants of flexible exchange rate\nFollowing is a brief description of both these determinants:\n(Note: For details about components of demand and supply of foreign exchange refer Section 11.5, 116\n526\nForeign Exchange Rate\nDemand for Foreign Exchange\nOther things remaining constant, demand for foreign exchange is inversely related to the price\nof foreign exchange (or the rate of foreign exchange). Thus, higher the rate of foreign exchange,\nlower the demand for foreign exchange, and vice versa.\n* Diagrammatically, demand for foreign exchange is indicated by a downward sloping curve as in\nFig. 11.1.\nRate of Foreign Exchange\n(Indian Rupees: US Dollars)\n(780: 1 US $)\n(270: 1 US $)\nDF\nObservations:\n• Fig. 11.1 shows that\nhigher the exchange rate,\nlower the demand for\nforeign exchange.\n• When exchange rate is t70\nfor one US dollar (R), the\ndemand for US dollars is\n200 million dollars (N).\n• When the exchange rate\nrises to 7 80: 1 US $ (R).\nthe demand shrinks to\n100 million dollars (M).\nDemand for Foreign Exchange (US S) (million dollars)\nFig. 11.1: Demand for Foreign Exchange\nSupply of Foreign Exchange\n• Other things remaining constant, supply of foreign exchange is positively related to the rate of\nforeign exchange. Thus, higher the rate of foreign exchange, higher the supply of foreign exchange,\nand vice versa.\n• Diagrammatically, supply of foreign exchange is indicated by an upward sloping curve, as in\nFig. 11.2.\nRate of Foreign Exchange\n(Indian Rupees: US Dollars)\n7 80: 1 US $)\n(2 70: 1 US $)\n*\nObservations:\n• Fig. 11.2 shows that higher the\nexchange rate, greater the\nsupply of foreign exchange.\n• When exchange rate is t 70\nfor one US dollar (R), supply\n= 100 million dollars (M).\n• When the exchange rate rises\nto t 80 for one US dollar (R),\nsupply expands to 200 million\ndollars (N).\nSupply of Foreign Exchange (US $) (million dollars)\nFig. 11.2: Supply of Foreign Exchange\n527","file":"macro 11/IMG_0610.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0611","image":"pages/IMG_0611.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\nEquilibrium Rate of Exchange\nPar Rate of Exchange\n•Equilibrium rate of exchange or par rate of exchange cars when: supply of foreign exchange =\npoint where supply and demand curves extine toreign exchange market intersect ear othe\nFig. 11.3 depicts this situation.\nRate of Exchange\nEquilibrium Exchange Rate = OR\nEquilibrium Quantity\nof Foreign Currency = 0Q\nPoint of Equilibrium\nObservations:\n• Sp : Supply of foreign currency.\nIt is positively related to the\nrate of exchange.\n• Dr : Demand for foreign currency.\nIt is negatively related to the\nrate of exchange.\n• E : Point of equilibrium rate of\nexchange where supply and\ndemand curves intersect\neach other.\n• OQ: Equilibrium quantity where\nsupply and demand for\nforeign exchange are equal.\nSupply/Demand (Foreign Currency)\nFig. 11.3: Determination of Equilibrium Exchange Rate\n• In Fig. 11.3, supply and demand are measured on the X-axis, and exchange rate on the Y-axis.\nD, is the demand curve and S, is the supply curve of foreign currency. Both these curves intersect\nat point E. It is an equilibrium point and OR is the equilibrium rate of exchange.\n• If the rate of exchange rises to OR, then supply of foreign currency (ON) will exceed its demand (OM)\nby an amount equivalent to MN. Supply being more than demand, rate of exchange will come\ndown to OR.\n• On the contrary, if the rate of exchange falls to OR, then demand for foreign currency (ON) will be\nmore than its supply (OM) by MN. Demand being more than supply, rate of exchange will again\nrise to OR. Rate of exchange will ultimately be determined at a point where demand for and\nsupply of foreign currency are equal.\nImpact of Change (Increase/Decrease) in Demand and Supply\n• Increase in demand for a foreign currency occurs when more of it is demanded at its existing price\n(exchange rate). Decrease in demand for a foreign currency occurs when less of it is demande\nat its existing price (exchange rate).\n• Likewise, increase in supply of a foreign currency occurs when more of it is supplied at its existing\nat its existing price (exchange rate.\nprice (exchange rate). Decrease in supply of a foreign currency occurs when less of it is supplied\nLet us examine the impact of these four situations on the equilibrium exchange rate. As a referent\nlet us consider the exchange rate between US dollar and Indian rupee.\n528\nForeign Exchange Rate\nsituation 1: Impact of Increase in Demand for a Foreign Currency (US $):\nDepreciation of Domestic Currency\n• Increase in demand is reflected by a shift in demand curve to the right, as in Fig. 11.4.\nAssumed Exchange Rate:\nObservations:\nOR : 1 US$= <70\nOR,: 1 US$= USS=780\n• D : The initial demand curve\nfor US $.\n• Dy : A forward shift in demand\ncurve for US S.\nConsequentiy:\n- Price of US $ (exchange rate)\nrises from OR to OR,\n- Domestic currency depreciates\nin relation to US S.\nRate of Exchang\nDemand and Supply of US $\nFig. 11.4: Impact of Increase in Demand for Foreign Currency\n• Demand curve shifts from D to Dy. This causes a rise in the equilibrium exchange rate from\nOR to ORy. Now one US $ is available for * 80, instead of 7 70 earlier.\n• Thus, other things remaining constant, increase in demand for foreign currency leads to a rise\nin exchange rate. This is described as a situation of currency depreciation (or depreciation of\nthe domestic currency).\n! CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS\nCurrency depreciation refers to a situation when domestic currency (rupee) depreciates (or loses its\nvalue) in relation to a foreign currency (say US dollar). So that, you need more rupees to buy a dollar.\nExample: If US $ exchanges for 7 80, instead of 2 70 earlier, the domestic cumency (Indian rupee) shows\ndepreciation.\nSituation 2: Impact of Decrease in Demand for a Foreign Currency (US S):\nAppreciation of Domestic Currency\n• Decrease in demand is reflected by a shift in demand curve to the left, as in Fig. 11.5.\nRate of excnange\nAssumed Exchange Rate:\nOR: 1 US $ = 780\nOR, : 1 US$= 770\nObservations:\n• D : Initial demand curve for\nUSS.\n• Dy: Backwardidownward shift\nin demand curve for US S.\nConsequently:\n- Price of US S (exchange rate)\nfalls from OR to OR,.\n- Domestic currency appreciates\nin relation to US S.\nDemand and Supply of US S\nFig. 11.5: Impact of Decrease in Demand for Foreign Currency\n529","file":"macro 11/IMG_0611.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0612","image":"pages/IMG_0612.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\n• Demand curve shifts from D to Dy. This causes a fall in the equilibrium exchange rate from OR\nto OR,. Now one US s is available for & 70, instead of 7 80 earlier.\n•Thus, other things remaining constant, decrease in demand for toreg currency leads to a fal\nin exchange rate. This is described as a situation of currency appreciation (or appreciation of\nthe domestic currency).\nCRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS\nCurrency appreciation refers to a situation when domestic currency (rupee) appreciates (or gains its\nvalue) in relation to a foreign currency (say US dollar). So that, you need less rupees to buy a dollar.\nExample: If US $ exchanges for & 70, instead of & 80 earlier, the domestic currency (indian rupee) shows\nappreciation.\nSituation 3: Impact of Increase in Supply of Foreign Currency (US$):\nAppreciation of Domestic Currency\n• Increase in supply is reflected by a shift in supply curve to the right, as in Fig. 11.6.\nRate of Exchange\nAssumed Exchange Rate:\nOR: 1 US $ = ≤ 80\nOR, : 1 US $ = 7 70\nObservations:\n• S: Initial supply curve for\nUS $.\n• Sy: Shift in supply curve to the\nright (increase in supply).\nConsequently:\n- Price of US $ (exchange rate)\nfalls from OR to ORy.\n- Domestic currency appreciates\nin relation to US $.\nDemand and Supply of US $\nFig. 11.6: Impact of Increase in Supply of Foreign Currency\n• Supply curve shifts from S to S,. This causes a fall in the equilibrium exchange rate from OR to\nORy. Now one US $ is available for & 70, instead of & 80 earlier.\n• This is a situation of currency appreciation (or appreciation of the domestic currency).\nSituation 4: Impact of Decrease in Supply of Foreign Currency (US $):\nDepreciation of Domestic Currency\n• Decrease in supply is reflected by a shift in supply curve to the left, as in Fig. 11.7.\n530\nForeign Exchange Rate\nRate or exchanet\nAssumed Exchange Rate:\nOR : 1 US$= ?70\nOR, : 1 US $ = 7 80\nObservations:\n• S : Initial supply curve for\nUS S.\n• Sy: Shift in supply curve to the\nleft (decrease in supply).\nConsequently:\n- Price of US S (exchange rate)\nrises from OR to ORy.\n- Domestic currency depreciates\nin relation to US S.\nDemand and Supply of US $\nFig. 11.7: Impact of Decrease in Supply of Foreign Currency\n• Supply curve shifts from S to S,. This causes a rise in the equilibrium exchange rate from OR to\nOR,. Now, one US $ is available for 7 80, instead of 70 earlier.\n• This is a situation of currency depreciation (or depreciation of the domestic currency).\n• Briefly, exchange rate rises when demand for foreign currency rises or when supply of foreign\ncurrency falls. It leads to depreciation of the domestic currency.\n• On the other hand, exchange rate falls when demand for foreign currency falls or when supply of\nforeign currency rises. It leads to appreciation of domestic currency.\nNote: The appreciation of the domestic currency implies a depreciation of the foreign currency and vice\nversa. This is because currency values are always measured relative to one another in the foreign\nexchange (forex) market.]\nBasis of Difference\n1. Meaning\nTable 11.1: Appreciation vs. Depreciation of Domestic Currency\nAppreciation of Domestic Currency\nDepreciation of Domestic Currency\n- It is a situation of a fall in exchange rate.\n- It is a situation of a rise in exchange rate.\nLess rupees are needed to buy one US$.\nMore rupees are needed to buy one US S.\n- Increase in supply of foreign exchange.\n- Increase in demand for foreign exchange.\n3. Example\n4. Impact\n- Decrease in demand for foreign exchange\n- Exchange rate falls from (1 US$: 780) to\n(1 US$: 270).\n- Exports are expected to reduce and\nimports are expected to increase.\n- Decrease in supply of foreign exchange.\n- Exchange rate rises from (1 USS: 270) to\n(1 US$: 780).\n- Exports are expected to increase and\nimports are expected to reduce.\n531","file":"macro 11/IMG_0612.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0613","image":"pages/IMG_0613.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\nImpact of Currency Depreciation and Appreciation on imports, exports, and National income\nCRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS\nNational Income\nCurrency Movement\n- Imports become - Exports become\nDepreciation\nmore expensive.\ncheaper.\n- National income may rise due to increased\nexports and reduced imports.\n- Hence, imports\n- Hence, exports\nare likely to fall.\nare likely to\n- Thus, currency depreciation typically boosts\nincrease.\nnational income in the short term by improving\nexports and reducing imports, but it can lead to\ninflation and higher import costs.\n- Imports become - Exports become\nAppreciation\nmore expensive.\n- National income may fall due to reduced exports\nand higher imports.\ncheaper.\n- Hence, imports\n- Hence, exports\n- Thus, currency appreciation generally hurts\nare likely to\nare likely to fall.\nnational income in the short term by reducing\nexports and increasing imports but can help\nin lowering inflation and attracting foreign\ninvestment.\n& AVLR 11.1,\nDEPRECIATION OF DOMESTIC CURRENCY vs. APPRECIATION OF\nDOMESTIC CURRENCY\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\nQ. 1. How does appreciation and depreciation of the domestic currency affect exports and imports\nof the domestic economy?\nAns. (i) Appreciation of the domestic currency implies (a) less rupees are required to buy a dollar.\nAccordingly, imports are likely to increase, and (b) more dollars are required to buy a rupee.\nAccordingly, exports are likely to fall.\n(il) Depreciation of the domestic currency implies (a) more rupees are required to buy a dollar.\nAccordingly, imports are likely to fall, and (b) less dollars are required to buy a rupee.\nAccordingly, exports are likely to rise.\nQ.2. Identify the appreciating and depreciating currency in the cases given below and mention in\nthe blanks given alongside.\nOld Exchange Rate\nNew Exchange Rate\nAppreciating Currency\nDepreciating Currency\n2E = $3\n2£=$4\n25 = 200 ·\n2$ = 205 ·\nR 70=$1\n< 80 = $1\n{ 150 = 4 €\n< 120 = 4 €\n1€= {60\n1€ = 7 80\nAns.\nAppreciating Currency\nDepreciating Currency\n532\n11.2.2 Fixed Exchange Rate\nForeign Exchange Rate\nExchange rate is said to be fixed when it is set and maintained by the government at a particular\nlevel. The government may set it at a level higher or lower than the equilibrium exchange rate as\ndetermined by the market forces of supply and demand.\nWhen Exchange Rate is set higher than the Equilibrium Exchange Rate:\nDevaluation of the Domestic Currency and its Implications\n• Fig. 11.8 illustrates the situation when exchange rate is set higher than the equilibrium exchange\nrate.\nExchange Rate\n(Indian Rupees: US Dollars)\n80: 1 US $)\n$ 70: 1 US $)\nEquilibrium Exchange Rate\nSupply of US S\n= Demand for US S\n(Here, PQ = ab)\n→x\nObservations:\n• In Fig. 11.8, Sp shows the supply\nof US dollars and D; shows the\ndemand for US dollars.\n• OR is the equilibrium exchange\nrate: 1 US S is exchanged for & 70.\n• OR, is the exchange rate as set by\nthe government. Here, 1 US Sis\nexchanged for 7 80.\nHere, the value of the domestic\ncurrency (indian rupee) has been\ndeliberately lowered by the\ngovemment. This is called\nSupply and Demand (US $)\nFig. 11.8: Exchange Rate higher than the Equilibrium Exchange Rate\nImplications of Devaluation\n• Devaluation leads to excess supply of foreign currency in the international money market (in a\nstate of equilibrium).\n• In Fig. 11.8, excess supply (S, > D.) = PQ. This would lead to a fall in the exchange rate. in which\nsituation the very purpose of devaluation would be lost. It, therefore, becomes essential for the\ngovernment to absorb the excess supply by way of its own purchase of the foreign currency in the\ninternational money market.\n• Accordingly, the government-reserves of forex (foreign exchange) must rise. (It may be noted that\nin India, RBI is engaged in the sale and purchase of foreign currency in the international money\nmarket.)\n• It is expected to have the following impact on a country's trade profile:\n• Increase in exports.\n• Fall in imports.","file":"macro 11/IMG_0613.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0614","image":"pages/IMG_0614.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\nCRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS\nDevaluation of the Currency is different from Depreciation of the currency\nThe difference is as under:\n- Depreciation of the (domestic) currency occurs when the value of the (domestic) currency reduces in\nthe international money market, because of the market forces of supply and demand, Ine governmen\n- Devaluation of the (domestic) currency occurs when the value of the domestic currency is deliberately\nplays no role whatsoever.\nreduced by the government by raising the exchange rate. The market forces of supply and demand\nplay no role whatsoever.\nWhen Exchange Rate is set lower than the Equilibrium Exchange Rate:\nRevaluation of the Domestic Currency and its Implications\n• Fig. 11.9 illustrates the situation when exchange rate is set lower than the equilibrium exchange\nExchange Rate\n(Indian Rupees: US Dollars)\n(T 80: 1 US $)\n(2 70: 1 US $)\nEquilibrium Exchange Rate\nSupply of US $\n= Demand for US $\n(Here, PQ = ab)\n-X\nObservations:\n• In Fig. 11.9, OR is the\nequilibrium exchange rate:\n1 US $ is exchanged for 7 80.\n• OR, is the exchange rate as\nfixed by the government.\nHere, 1 US $ is exchanged\nfor ‹ 70.\nHere, the value of the domestic\ncurrency (Indian rupee) has\nbeen deliberately raised by the\ngovernment. This is called\n'Revaluation'.\nSupply and Demand (US$)\nFig. 11.9: Exchange Rate lower than the Equilibrium Exchange Rate\nImplications of Revaluation\n• Revaluation leads to excess demand for foreign currency in the international money market.\nIn Fig. 11.9, excess demand (D, > Sp) = PQ. The RBI must fulfill this excess demand by releasing\nsupplies from its reserves of forex.\n• In case supplies are not released from the reserves of forex, there may emerge a black marke\nfor the sale and purchase of dollars. The buyers may have to pay a premium for the purchase ot\ndollars over and above the price fixed by the government.\n• It is expected to have the following impact on a country's trade profile:\n• Fall in exports.\n• Rise in imports.\n534\nForeign Exchange Rate\n| CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS\nRevaluation of the Currency is different from Appreciation of the currency\nThe difference is as under:\n• Appreciation of the (domestic) currency occurs when the value of the (domestic) curency rises in\nthe international money market, because of the market forces of supply and demand. The government\nplays no role whatsoever.\n• Revaluation of the (domestic) currency occurs when the value of the domestic currency is deliberately\nraised by the government by lowering the exchange rate. The market forces of supply and demand play\nno role whatsoever.\nLess Developed Countries often Devalue their Currency\nLess developed countries often devalue their currency: domestic currency is made cheaper in relation to\nthe foreign currency. This is because of the three principal reasons:\n) Devaluation (implying a planned fall in the value of the domestic currency) is expected to raise the\ndemand for the domestically produced goods and services. Accordingly, exports are expected\nto rise.\n(i) Rise in exports (owing to devaluation) is expected to increase the supply of foreign exchange\ninto the domestic economy. This facilitates import of essential goods from rest of the world.\n(i) A fall in the value of domestic currency induces private foreign investment. Because, for every\ndollar, the investors are going to get more rupees, after devaluation. This is expected to promote\nGDP growth.\nGold Standard System of Exchange Rate -\nAn Old Variant of Fixed Exchange Rate System\n• Gold standard system of exchange rate is an old variant of fixed exchange rate system.\n• According to this system (prevalent in most countries prior to 1920s), gold was taken as the\ncommon unit of parity between currencies of different countries. Each country was to define\nvalue of its currency in terms of gold. Accordingly, value of one currency in terms of the other\ncurrency was fixed considering gold value of each currency.\nExample:\nIf UK € (Pound) = 4 g of gold, and US $ (Dollar) = 2 g of gold, then 1 UKE = 2 USS.\nExchange rate or exchange ratio between UK E and US $ = 1: 2. Two US dollars would\nexchange for 1 UK pound.\n• This system of exchange was also known as Mint Par Value of Exchange or Mint Parity. Mint value\nof a currency implied gold value of that currency.\nBretton Woods System of Exchange Rate\nAdjustable Peg System of Exchange Rate\nBretton Woods System (even when it was a fixed system of exchange rate) allowed some adjustments.\nSo, it was called 'adjustable peg system of exchange rate'. According to this system,\nDifferent currencies were pegged (or related) to one currency, that is US dollar. US dollar was\ned gold value at a fixed price.\n535","file":"macro 11/IMG_0614.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0615","image":"pages/IMG_0615.jpg","text":"• Value of one currency in terms of US dollar ultimately implied value of that currency in terms of\ngold. Gold continued to be the ultimate unit of parity between any two currencies.\n• Adjustment in the parity value of a currency was possible but only if allowed by IMF (International\nMonetary Fund). Even this system was abandoned in 1977. It was replaced by a flexible system\nof exchange rate.\nDID YOU KNOW\nBretton Woods System is named after United Nations Monetary Financial Conterence held at brentor\nWoods (USA) in 1944 which led to the establishment of IMF (International Monetary Fund).\nTable 11.2: Fixed Exchange Rate vs. Flexible Exchange Rate\nBasis of Difference\n1. Determination\nFixed Exchange Rate\nFlexible Exchange Rate\n- Fixed exchange rate is determined by the\ngovernment.\n- Flexible exchange rate is determined by\nthe forces of supply and demand in the\ninternational money market.\n2. Role of Market Forces\n3. Impact\n- Market forces play an important role.\n- As determined by the forces of supply and\ndemand, flexible rate of exchange leads\n4. Reserves\n- Market forces do not play any role.\n- As set and maintained by the government,\nthe fixed rate of exchange leads to:\n• devaluation (when the value of the\ndomestic currency is lowered by the\ngovernment), or\n• revaluation (when the value of the domestic\ncurrency is raised by the government).\n- Fixed exchange rate requires large reserves\nof forex.\n5. Uncertainty/\nSpeculation\n- Uncertainty/Speculation is very low in the\nsystem of fixed exchange rate. It arises\nonly when people expect some change in\nthe government policy.\n• depreciation of the domestic currency\n(when the exchange rate rises), and\n• appreciation of the domestic currency\nwhen the exchange rate falls).\n- Flexible exchange rate does not require\nlarge reserves\nof forex. Reserves are\nneeded only to exercise 'managed float\".\n- Uncertainty/Speculation is very high in\nthe system of flexible exchange rate. It\nis because of the uncertainty of market\nforces of supply and demand.\nKAVLR 11.2\nFIXED EXCHANGE RATE SYSTEM vs. FLEXIBLE EXCHANGE RATE SYSTEM\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\nQ. 3. Why was gold standard system of exchange rate abandoned (discontinued)?\nAns. Gold standard system of exchange rate was abandoned because this system required lots o\nreserves of gold. This raised the demand for gold. But, the supply of gold was extremely scarce\nin relation to its demand.\n536\nForeign Exchange Rate\n11.3 MANAGED FLOATING EXCHANGE RATE SYSTEM\nMeaning and Concept of Managed Floating Exchange Rate system\n, Even when exchange rate is determined by the forces of supply and demand, at times the Central\nBank (REl) intervenes to manage the exchange rate so that it does not slip out of the desired\nlimits, It is called managed floating. It may be defined as under:\n• Managed floating is a system of floating exchange rate in which there is occasional\nintervention by the central bank to influence the float or manage the float. It is also called\nDirty Floating'.\nHow does the Central Bank exercise its influence on the 'Float' or Market Exchange Rate?\n• Itis through the sale and purchase of foreign currency in the international money market. When\nthe exchange rate (say of US $) needs to be reduced, the central bank releases the supply of\nUS $ in the foreign exchange market.\nIf 1 US dollar is worth & 75, and the RBI sells US dollars, the price may drop to 7 72, causing\nthe Indian Rupee (INR) to appreciate.\n• Other things remaining constant, higher supply of US s would lower the price of US, as desired.\nThis would lead to appreciation of domestic currency.\nExample:\nIf 1 US dollar is worth & 72, and the RBI buys more dollars, the price may rise to 275, causing fa\nthe Indian Rupee (INR) to depreciate.\n• On the other hand, when the exchange rate needs to be raised, the central bank increases\nits demand for US $ in the foreign exchange market. Other things remaining constant, higher\ndemand for US $ would raise the price of US $, as desired. This would lead to depreciation of\nthe domestic currency.\nBriefly, managed floating is an exercise of sale and purchase of foreign currency by the central bank,\nso that the exchange rate is managed within the desired limits.\nCRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS\n• Managed floating is a tool employed by the central bank to restore the value of the country's currency\n(in relation to other currencies) within the desired limits, even when exchange rate is determined by the\nmarket forces of demand and supply.\n• In fact, managed floating may be called as the mixture of both flexible and fixed exchange rate systems.\n- It comprises the element of flexible exchange rate system as the exchange rate is primarily determined\nby the forces of supply and demand.\n- Likewise, it comprises the element of fixed exchange rate system as the exchange rate is moderated\n(or managed) by way of intervention by the RBI.\n537","file":"macro 11/IMG_0615.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0616","image":"pages/IMG_0616.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\n11.4 MERITS AND DEMERITS OF FIXED AND\nFLEXIBLE EXCHANGE RATE SYSTEM\n11.4.1 (a) Fixed Exchange Rate System: Merits\nFixed exchange rate system has the following merits:\n(1) Market Stability and Mobility of Capital: It ensures stability in the international money\nmarket/exchange market by eliminating risks related to exchange rate variations\nInvestors are not exposed to uncertaintles of the market. It promotes international mobilit\n(2) Growth of International Trade: Fixed exchange rate and stability of price level offers an\nhospitable environment for the growth of international trade.\nThe traders can explore international markets for their products almost with asserted returns\ninvolving minimum possible risk.\n(3) Stable Macroeconomic Policies: Given the fixed exchange rate, the central bank can frame its\nmonetary policy and the government can frame its fiscal policy, independent of the external\nshocks relating to fluctuations in exchange rate.\n(4) Devaluation: A Key Tool to expand Foreign Market for the Domestic Producers: Fixed\nexchange rate system allows devaluation of the currency. It is a planned fall in the value of\nthe domestic currency. It helps expand foreign market for the domestic producers.\n(5) A Check on Inflation: Fixed exchange rate keeps the government under pressure to check\ninflation.\nBecause, if prices in the domestic market are high, demand for our exports will reduce. Our\nforeign exchange earnings will fall. And, our reserves of foreign exchange may fall to a low level.\n(Foreign exchange reserves are essential to pay for the import of goods from rest of the world.)\n11.4.1 (b) Fixed Exchange Rate System: Demerits\nPrincipal demerits of fixed exchange rate system are as under:\n(1) Reserves of Forex: To maintain the rate of exchange at the desired level, the government\nneed to keep a large stock of foreign exchange. This is the principal demerit of the fixed\n2) Inefficient Allocation of Resources: Exchange rate fixed by the government often deviates\nfrom the equilibrium exchange rate (in a free market economy). To that extent, allocation of\n(3) Small Size of Forex Market: When the rate of exchange is fixed, foreign exchange does not\nemerge as a trading commodity. Accordingly, size of the forex market remains small. This ac\nas a hurdle in the global economic growth.\n(4) Discourages Venture Capital: Fixed exchange rate discourages venture capital in the\ninternational money market. Foreign exchange fails to develop as a commodity of trade\nInternational money market is restricted largely to international payments of exports and\n538\nForeign Exchange Rate\n(5) Fixed Exchange Rate may not be the Equilibrium Rate: Since exchange rate is fixed by the\ngovernment, supply and demand forces are not allowed to operate. Accordingly, exchange\nrate in the international money market may not be the equilibrium exchange rate.\nIn the absence of an equilibrium exchange rate, allocation of resources (to the production of\ndifferent goods and services) in the country may not be optimum or efficient.\n! CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS\nVenture Capital in the International Money Market\nIt refers to investments in the purchase of forex (foreign exchange) in the international money market with\na view to earning profits. Fixed exchange rate system does not allow such investments.\n11.4.2 (a) Flexible Exchange Rate System: Merits\nA shift from fixed exchange rate system to flexible exchange rate system was desired in view of its\nmerits. Some of the principal merits of flexible exchange rate system (in relation to fixed exchange\nrate system) are as under:\n(1) Large Reserves of Forex not required: Flexible system of exchange rate does not require\nlarge reserves of foreign exchange with the government. Market forces of supply and\ndemand automatically drive the rate of exchange to the point of equilibrium.\n(2) Efficient Allocation of Resources: Flexible exchange rate system leads to equilibrium\nexchange rates of different currencies. Accordingly, allocation of resources (across different\ncountries of the world) is expected to become more and more efficient/optimum.\n(3) Large Size of the Forex Market: Since foreign exchange itself becomes a trading commodity.\nthe size of forex market tends to be large. This induces economic growth across all parts of\nthe world.\n(4) International Mobility of Liquidity: Since large reserves of forex are not required, flexible\nexchange rate tends to promote international mobility of liquidity. This is good for the less.\ndeveloped countries (like India), where foreign investment is a significant determinant of\nGDP growth.\nmarket. Trading in international currencies itself has emerged as an important economic\nactivity. This has led to the expansion in business activity and growth of international money\n11.4.2 (b) Flexible Exchange Rate System: Demerits\nHowever, flexible exchange rate system is not free from demerits. Some notable demerits of flexible\nexchange rate system are as under:\n(1) Marginalisation of Weak Currencies: Flexible exchange rate system leads to marginalisation\nof weak currencies in the international money market. Owing to their low demand, weak\ncurrencies (of small economies) often suffer huge depreciation in relation to strong currencies (of\nbig economies).\n(sation of weak currency - rapid loss of value of weak currency in the intermational money market.\n539","file":"macro 11/IMG_0616.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0617","image":"pages/IMG_0617.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\nAccordingly, gains of international trade accrue more to the large economies than the small\neconomies.\nIf the Sri Lankan Rupee (LKR) depreciates from & 150 to & 180 per US Dollar (USD), imports\nbecome costlier for Sri Lanka. Meanwhile, large economies like the US benefit, as their\nexports become cheaper, leading to greater gains from international trade compared to\nsmaller economies.\n(2) Uncertainty of the Market: There is a high degree of uncertainty in the market. wing to the\nfrequently changing rate of exchange, it becomes aimcult to tormulate d stable moneta.\npolicy in the domestic economy.\n(3) A Stable Monetary Policy not Possible: Owing to frequent changes in the exchange rate\n(under flexible exchange rate system), It becomes difficult tor the central Dank (kB) to\nformulate a stable monetary policy. Frequent changes in exchange rate lead to frequent\nchanges in the interest rate in the domestic economy.\n(4) Bilateral Trade Agreement becomes Difficult: Day-to-day fluctuation in exchange rate makes\nbilateral trade agreements a difficult exercise. This restricts the growth of international trade.\n(5) External Shocks: Flexible exchange rate system enhances vulnerability of the domestic\neconomy to external shocks.\nAs and when US dollar appreciates (in relation to Indian rupee), the burden of import\npayments tends to rise, even when the international price of the goods is constant.\nBecause payments are to be made in terms of dollars, and (after a rise in exchange rate)\nlarger amount of the Indian currency is needed to pay the same amount of dollars.\n11.5 COMPONENTS OF DEMAND FOR FOREIGN EXCHANGE\n(FOREIGN CURRENCY)\nOR\nWHY IS FOREIGN EXCHANGE DEMANDED?\nForeign exchange is demanded for various purposes. Basically, it is demanded for making payments\nto rest of the world. Each type of payment constitutes a component of demand for foreign exchange.\nBroadly, following are the constituents of demand for foreign exchange:\n(1) Repayment of International Loans: International loans are raised in terms of foreign currency.\nAccordingly, foreign currency is required for the repayment of these loans. In less developed\ncountries like India, this is an important component of demand for foreign exchange.\n2) Investment in Rest of the World: Investment in rest of the world is an important busines\nactivity. We need currency of the country in which investment is to be made. Thus, US dollars\n3) Imports: We import goods (like cellphones and TVs) and services (like of banking ana\nare needed/demanded if investment is to be made in USA.\ninsurance) from rest of the world. It requires foreign exchange. Because payments tor\nimports are made in foreign exchange only.\nForeign Exchange Rate\n(4) Direct Purchases Abroad: Foreign exchange is needed to make direct purchases (of goods\nand services abroad. Because, people from our country visit other countries of the world\nas tourists. They also go abroad for studies or for medical treatment. This involves direct\npurchases abroad.\n(5) Grants and Donations: Grants and donations (unilateral payments) to rest of the world also\ncontribute to the demand for foreign exchange. Grants and donations to a country are often\nmade in terms of the currency of that country (or in terms of some internationally accepted\ncurrency like US dollars).\n(6) Payment of Incomes: Demand for foreign exchange in the domestic economy also arises\nfor the payment of factor incomes (rent, interest, profit/dividend and wages) which are\nrepatriated (sent) abroad. It is like factor income to rest of the world.\n(7) Speculative Trading: Foreign exchange particularly in terms of strong currencies (like US dollar)\nis held/demanded by the people for purpose of speculative trading. Often, more foreign\nexchange is held when the exchange rate is low, and vice versa.\nTogether, these components of demand add up to aggregate demand for foreign exchange.\n11.6 COMPONENTS OF SUPPLY OF FOREIGN EXCHANGE\n(FOREIGN CURRENCY)\nSOURCES OF FOREIGN EXCHANGE\nSupply of foreign exchange depends on the following sources. Each source constitutes a component.\nof supply of foreign exchange.\nA country receives foreign exchange through the following sources of supply:\n(1) Exports: Export of goods and services is an important source of supply (inflow) of foreign\nexchange from rest of the world. Thus, export of goods and services from India to US would\nmean supply of foreign exchange to India (in terms of receipts for exports).\n(2) Investments from Rest of the World: Investments from rest of the world (including Fll and FDI)\nis another important source of supply of foreign exchange. Lots of foreign exchange flows from\ndeveloped countries to underdeveloped countries through this channel of economic activity.\n(3) Direct Purchases by Rest of the World: Direct purchases by the residents of rest of the world\nalso contribute to the flow of foreign exchange from rest of the world to the domestic economy.\n(4) Loans from Rest of the World: It refers to borrowings from rest of the world. A loan from UK\nwould mean flow of UK & from UK to India. It contributes to the supply of foreign exchange\nto India.\n(5) Grants and Donations from Rest of the World: Grants and donations from rest of the world\nare also a source of supply of foreign exchange. A significant amount of foreign exchange\nflows from the rich to the poor countries of the world by way of grants and donations.\n(6) Income Receipts: Foreign exchange also flows from rest of the world to the domestic\neconomy by way of income receipts. These receipts refer to receipts of factor incomes from\nrest of the world.\n541","file":"macro 11/IMG_0617.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0618","image":"pages/IMG_0618.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\n(7) Remittances by the Non-residents: Remittances by Nkis are an important source of supply/\nreceipt of foreign exchange from rest of the world. Such remittances are indeed a significant\ncomponent of supply of foreign exchange in developing economies like india.\nTogether, these components of supply add up to aggregate supply of foreign exchange.\no. 4. State whether the following items lead to demand for or supply of foreign exchange (from\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\nIndia's perspective):\n(i) Indian going to Japan for medical treatment.\n(ii) Export of goods to USA.\n(iii) Purchase of land in Europe.\n(iv) Remittances to India by an Indian working in Japan.\n(v) Buying 500 dollars for speculative purposes.\nAns. Demand for Foreign Exchange: (i), (ili), (v)\nSupply of Foreign Exchange: (ii), (iv)\nQ. 5. Why does the demand for foreign currency fall when its price rises?\nAns. A rise in the price of foreign currency implies that more units of the domestic currency\n(say rupees) are needed to buy a unit of foreign currency (say US $). It leads to a fall in demand\nfor foreign currency, owing to the following reasons:\n(i) With a rise in the price of foreign currency, imports tend to fall, leading to a fall in demand\nfor foreign currency.\n(it) A rise in price of foreign currency makes travelling to rest of the world more expensive.\nAccordingly, demand for foreign currency falls.\n(ili) Investment in rest of the world becomes more expensive, leading to a fall in demand for\nforeign currency.\n(iv) Opportunity cost of holding foreign currency (in terms of domestic currency) tends to rise.\nAccordingly, demand for foreign currency tends to shrink.\nQ. 6. State any two factors that explain extension of demand for a foreign currency in response to a\n(i) When foreign currency (say US $) becomes cheaper (in relation to the domestic currenc).\nwe get more dollars per unit of our currency. Accordingly, imports become lucrative.\nThis raises demand for foreign currency.\n(ii) When foreign currency becomes cheaper, domestic investors will be induced to make\ngreater investment in rest of the world. Accordingly, demand for foreign currency rises.\nQ. 7. State any two factors that explain contraction of supply of a foreign currency when its price in\nterms of the domestic currency falls.\nAns. (i) When foreign currency becomes cheaper (in relation to domestic currency), purchasin\nbower of the foreign currency in the domestic market tends to fall. This leads to a fall\nforeign currency reduces.\nforeign demand for the domestic goods. Implying fall in our exports. Accordingly, supply of\n(ii) When foreign currency becomes cheaper (in relation to domestic currency), less rupees are\nInvestment). This reduces the supply of foreign currency.\navailable for a US dollar. Accordingly, foreigners are less inclined to make FDI (Foreign Direct\n542\n11.7 FOREIGN EXCHANGE MARKET\nForeign Exchange Rate\nMeaning and Functions of Foreign Exchange Market\nForeign exchange market refers to the market for national currencies of different countres in the\nworld. It is a centre of trade for different currencies. Buyers and sellers in foreign exchange market\nwish to buy or sell foreign exchange.\nFunctions\nForeign exchange market performs the following functions:\n(1) Transfer Function: It implies transfer of purchasing power in terms of foreign exchange\nacross different countries of the world.\n(2) Credit Function: It implies provision of credit in terms of foreign exchange for the export and\nimport of goods and services across different countries of world.\n(3) Hedging Function: It implies protection against risk related to variations in foreign exchange\nrate. Exchange rate is locked for future supplies of foreign exchange.\nOperation of Foreign Exchange Market\nForeign exchange market operates either as\n• Spot Market (Current Market), or as\n• Forward Market.\nFollowing is a brief description of both these types:\nSpot Market (Current Market)\nSpot market for foreign exchange is that market which handles only spot transactions or current\ntransactions.\nIts principal characteristics are that:\n(i) In terms of 'period of transaction', spot market is of 'daily nature\". It does not trade in future\ndeliveries.\n(ii) The rate of exchange which is determined in the spot market is known as spot rate of\nexchange. The spot rate of exchange (or current rate of exchange) is that rate which prevails\nat the time when transactions are made.\nForward Market\nForward market for foreign exchange is that market which handles such transactions of foreign\nexchange that are meant for future delivery. Such transactions are signed today but are to materialise\n(or are to be honoured) on some future date.\nPrincipal characteristics of forward market are that:\n(i) It only caters to forward transactions; it does not deal with spot transactions in foreign\nexchange.\n(ii) It defines (or determines) forward exchange rate-the exchange rate at which forward\ntransactions are to be honoured.\n543","file":"macro 11/IMG_0618.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0619","image":"pages/IMG_0619.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\nWhy are Forward Transactions Contracted?\nForward transactions are contracted for two reasons:\n(i) To avoid the risk of any adverse change in exchange rate, and\n(i) To make speculative gains.\nTable 11.3: Spot Market vs. Forward Market\nSpot Market\nBasis of Difference\n1. Nature of Transactions\n- Spot market handles current transactions.\n2. Nature of Exchange\nRate\n- Exchange rate determined in this market\nis called spot rate of exchange.\nForward Market\n- Forward\nmeant for future delivery.\nmarket handles transactions\n- Exchange rate determined in this market\nis called forward rate of exchange or\ncontracted rate of exchange.\n- It does not allow 'hedging'.\n3. Hedging\n- It allows 'hedging'.\n\"Hedging' means avoiding the risk of an adverse change in exchange rate in future by signing exchange rate now for\nfuture delivery.\nCRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS\n• Spot exchange rate (also known as current rate of exchange) is that rate of exchange which prevails\nin the market at the time when transactions are made. It relates only to spot transactions in the\ninternational money market.\n• Forward exchange rate is that exchange rate at which forward transactions are to be honoured. It has\nnothing to do with spot transactions in the international money market. In fact, forward exchange rate\nis a sort of 'contracted' exchange rate to be applicable for the transactions which are signed today but\nare to be honoured sometimes in the future.\nPower Points [Summary of the Chapter]\n• Exchange Rate refers to the price of one currency in relation to other currencies in the international\nmoney market (or international exchange market). [Example: If ? 80 are to be paid to buy one US\ndollar, exchange rate between the two currencies = 80: 1.]\n• Systems:\n(i) Flexible exchange rate system, and\n(ii) Fixed exchange rate system.\n• Flexible Exchange Rate is a floating rate of exchange, determined by the supply of and demand for\ndifferent currencies.\n• When owing to the free play of the forces of supply and demand, exchange rate happens to ris\n• On the other hand, when exchange rate happens to fall, it is called 'appreciation of the (domestic)\nit is called 'depreciation' of the (domestic) currency.\n• Equilibrium Exchange Rate occurs when:\nSupply of toreign currency/foreign exchange = Demand for foreign currency/foreign exchang\n544\n/a Fixed Exchange Rate System\nForeign Exchange Rate\n• Exchange rate is set and maintained by the government at a particular level. Market forces of\nsupply and demand have no role to play.\n- When the exchange rate is raised by the government, it is called devaluation of the (domestic)\ncurrency.\n- When it is lowered, it is called 'revaluation' of the (domestic) currency.\nDetermination: Fixed exchange rate is determined by the government of the country.\n• Managed Floating, also called Dirty Floating, is a system of floating exchange rate (where exchange\nrate is determined by the forces of demand and supply) but occasionally, the float is managed by\nthe central bank of the country by way of sale and purchase of foreign exchange in the international\nmoney market. Managed floating is an attempt to keep the exchange rate within the desired limits.\n- Merits and Demerits of Fixed Exchange Rate\n• Merits: (i) Market stability and mobility of capital, (ii) Growth of international trade, (i) Stable\nmacroeconomic policies, (iv) Devaluation: A key tool to expand foreign market for the domestic\nproducers, and (v) A check on inflation.\n• Demerits: (i) Required large reserves of forex, (ii) Inefficient allocation of resources, (i) Small\nsize of forex market, (iv) Discourages venture capital, and (v) Fixed exchange rate may not be the\nequilibrium rate.\n• Merits and Demerits of Flexible Exchange Rate\n• Merits: (i) Large reserves of forex not required, (i) Efficient allocation of resources, (il) Large\nsize of the forex market, (iv) Promote international mobility of liquidity, and (V) Promotes venture\ncapital.\n• Demerits: (i) Marginalisation of weak currencies, (ii) Uncertainty of the market, (il) A stable\nmonetary policy not possible, (iv) Bilateral trade agreement becomes difficult, and (v) External\nshocks.\n• Demand for Foreign Currency/Foreign Exchange depends upon:\n(i) Repayment of international loans, (i) Investment in rest of the world, (ill) Imports, (iv) Direct\npurchases abroad, (v) Grants and donations, (vi) Payment of incomes, and (vil) Speculative\ntrading.\n• Supply of Foreign Currency/Foreign Exchange depends upon:\n(il) Investments from rest of the world\n(i) Exports\n(ili) Direct purchases by rest of the world\n(iv) Loans from rest of the world\n(v) Grants and donations from rest of the world (vi) Income receipts\n(vii) Remittances by the non-residents\n• Foreign Exchange Market refers to the market for national currencies of different countries of the\nworld.\n• Functions: (i) Transfer function, (i) Credit function, and (il) Hedging function.\n• Spot Market deals with current sale and purchase of foreign exchange. It determines spot rate of\nexchange.\nForward Market deals with such sale and purchase of foreign exchange which are contracted\ntoday but are implemented sometimes in the future. It determines forward rate of exchange.\n545","file":"macro 11/IMG_0619.HEIC"}],"boxes":[{"title":"CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS","page":"IMG_0611","text":"Currency depreciation refers to a situation when domestic currency (rupee) depreciates (or loses its\nvalue) in relation to a foreign currency (say US dollar). So that, you need more rupees to buy a dollar.\nExample: If US $ exchanges for 7 80, instead of 2 70 earlier, the domestic cumency (Indian rupee) shows\ndepreciation.\nSituation 2: Impact of Decrease in Demand for a Foreign Currency (US S):\nAppreciation of Domestic Currency\n• Decrease in demand is reflected by a shift in demand curve to the left, as in Fig. 11.5.\nRate of excnange\nAssumed Exchange Rate:\nOR: 1 US $ = 780\nOR, : 1 US$= 770\nObservations:\n• D : Initial demand curve for\nUSS.\n• Dy: Backwardidownward shift\nin demand curve for US S.\nConsequently:\n- Price of US S (exchange rate)\nfalls from OR to OR,.\n- Domestic currency appreciates\nin relation to US S.\nDemand and Supply of US S\nFig. 11.5: Impact of Decrease in Demand for Foreign Currency\n529","image":"pages/IMG_0611.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS","page":"IMG_0612","text":"Currency appreciation refers to a situation when domestic currency (rupee) appreciates (or gains its\nvalue) in relation to a foreign currency (say US dollar). So that, you need less rupees to buy a dollar.\nExample: If US $ exchanges for & 70, instead of & 80 earlier, the domestic currency (indian rupee) shows\nappreciation.\nSituation 3: Impact of Increase in Supply of Foreign Currency (US$):\nAppreciation of Domestic Currency\n• Increase in supply is reflected by a shift in supply curve to the right, as in Fig. 11.6.\nRate of Exchange\nAssumed Exchange Rate:\nOR: 1 US $ = ≤ 80\nOR, : 1 US $ = 7 70\nObservations:\n• S: Initial supply curve for\nUS $.\n• Sy: Shift in supply curve to the\nright (increase in supply).\nConsequently:\n- Price of US $ (exchange rate)\nfalls from OR to ORy.\n- Domestic currency appreciates\nin relation to US $.\nDemand and Supply of US $\nFig. 11.6: Impact of Increase in Supply of Foreign Currency\n• Supply curve shifts from S to S,. This causes a fall in the equilibrium exchange rate from OR to\nORy. Now one US $ is available for & 70, instead of & 80 earlier.\n• This is a situation of currency appreciation (or appreciation of the domestic currency).\nSituation 4: Impact of Decrease in Supply of Foreign Currency (US $):\nDepreciation of Domestic Currency\n• Decrease in supply is reflected by a shift in supply curve to the left, as in Fig. 11.7.\n530\nForeign Exchange Rate\nRate or exchanet\nAssumed Exchange Rate:\nOR : 1 US$= ?70\nOR, : 1 US $ = 7 80\nObservations:\n• S : Initial supply curve for\nUS S.\n• Sy: Shift in supply curve to the\nleft (decrease in supply).\nConsequently:\n- Price of US S (exchange rate)\nrises from OR to ORy.\n- Domestic currency depreciates\nin relation to US S.\nDemand and Supply of US $\nFig. 11.7: Impact of Decrease in Supply of Foreign Currency\n• Supply curve shifts from S to S,. This causes a rise in the equilibrium exchange rate from OR to\nOR,. Now, one US $ is available for 7 80, instead of 70 earlier.\n• This is a situation of currency depreciation (or depreciation of the domestic currency).\n• Briefly, exchange rate rises when demand for foreign currency rises or when supply of foreign\ncurrency falls. It leads to depreciation of the domestic currency.\n• On the other hand, exchange rate falls when demand for foreign currency falls or when supply of\nforeign currency rises. It leads to appreciation of domestic currency.\nNote: The appreciation of the domestic currency implies a depreciation of the foreign currency and vice\nversa. This is because currency values are always measured relative to one another in the foreign\nexchange (forex) market.]\nBasis of Difference\n1. Meaning\nTable 11.1: Appreciation vs. Depreciation of Domestic Currency\nAppreciation of Domestic Currency\nDepreciation of Domestic Currency\n- It is a situation of a fall in exchange rate.\n- It is a situation of a rise in exchange rate.\nLess rupees are needed to buy one US$.\nMore rupees are needed to buy one US S.\n- Increase in supply of foreign exchange.\n- Increase in demand for foreign exchange.\n3. Example\n4. Impact\n- Decrease in demand for foreign exchange\n- Exchange rate falls from (1 US$: 780) to\n(1 US$: 270).\n- Exports are expected to reduce and\nimports are expected to increase.\n- Decrease in supply of foreign exchange.\n- Exchange rate rises from (1 USS: 270) to\n(1 US$: 780).\n- Exports are expected to increase and\nimports are expected to reduce.\n531","image":"pages/IMG_0612.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS","page":"IMG_0613","text":"National Income\nCurrency Movement\n- Imports become - Exports become\nDepreciation\nmore expensive.\ncheaper.\n- National income may rise due to increased\nexports and reduced imports.\n- Hence, imports\n- Hence, exports\nare likely to fall.\nare likely to\n- Thus, currency depreciation typically boosts\nincrease.\nnational income in the short term by improving\nexports and reducing imports, but it can lead to\ninflation and higher import costs.\n- Imports become - Exports become\nAppreciation\nmore expensive.\n- National income may fall due to reduced exports\nand higher imports.\ncheaper.\n- Hence, imports\n- Hence, exports\n- Thus, currency appreciation generally hurts\nare likely to\nare likely to fall.\nnational income in the short term by reducing\nexports and increasing imports but can help\nin lowering inflation and attracting foreign\ninvestment.\n& AVLR 11.1,\nDEPRECIATION OF DOMESTIC CURRENCY vs. APPRECIATION OF\nDOMESTIC CURRENCY","image":"pages/IMG_0613.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"IMG_0613","text":"Q. 1. How does appreciation and depreciation of the domestic currency affect exports and imports\nof the domestic economy?\nAns. (i) Appreciation of the domestic currency implies (a) less rupees are required to buy a dollar.\nAccordingly, imports are likely to increase, and (b) more dollars are required to buy a rupee.\nAccordingly, exports are likely to fall.\n(il) Depreciation of the domestic currency implies (a) more rupees are required to buy a dollar.\nAccordingly, imports are likely to fall, and (b) less dollars are required to buy a rupee.\nAccordingly, exports are likely to rise.\nQ.2. Identify the appreciating and depreciating currency in the cases given below and mention in\nthe blanks given alongside.\nOld Exchange Rate\nNew Exchange Rate\nAppreciating Currency\nDepreciating Currency\n2E = $3\n2£=$4\n25 = 200 ·\n2$ = 205 ·\nR 70=$1\n< 80 = $1\n{ 150 = 4 €\n< 120 = 4 €\n1€= {60\n1€ = 7 80\nAns.\nAppreciating Currency\nDepreciating Currency\n532\n11.2.2 Fixed Exchange Rate\nForeign Exchange Rate\nExchange rate is said to be fixed when it is set and maintained by the government at a particular\nlevel. The government may set it at a level higher or lower than the equilibrium exchange rate as\ndetermined by the market forces of supply and demand.\nWhen Exchange Rate is set higher than the Equilibrium Exchange Rate:\nDevaluation of the Domestic Currency and its Implications\n• Fig. 11.8 illustrates the situation when exchange rate is set higher than the equilibrium exchange\nrate.\nExchange Rate\n(Indian Rupees: US Dollars)\n80: 1 US $)\n$ 70: 1 US $)\nEquilibrium Exchange Rate\nSupply of US S\n= Demand for US S\n(Here, PQ = ab)\n→x\nObservations:\n• In Fig. 11.8, Sp shows the supply\nof US dollars and D; shows the\ndemand for US dollars.\n• OR is the equilibrium exchange\nrate: 1 US S is exchanged for & 70.\n• OR, is the exchange rate as set by\nthe government. Here, 1 US Sis\nexchanged for 7 80.\nHere, the value of the domestic\ncurrency (indian rupee) has been\ndeliberately lowered by the\ngovemment. This is called\nSupply and Demand (US $)\nFig. 11.8: Exchange Rate higher than the Equilibrium Exchange Rate\nImplications of Devaluation\n• Devaluation leads to excess supply of foreign currency in the international money market (in a\nstate of equilibrium).\n• In Fig. 11.8, excess supply (S, > D.) = PQ. This would lead to a fall in the exchange rate. in which\nsituation the very purpose of devaluation would be lost. It, therefore, becomes essential for the\ngovernment to absorb the excess supply by way of its own purchase of the foreign currency in the\ninternational money market.\n• Accordingly, the government-reserves of forex (foreign exchange) must rise. (It may be noted that\nin India, RBI is engaged in the sale and purchase of foreign currency in the international money\nmarket.)\n• It is expected to have the following impact on a country's trade profile:\n• Increase in exports.\n• Fall in imports.","image":"pages/IMG_0613.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS","page":"IMG_0614","text":"Devaluation of the Currency is different from Depreciation of the currency\nThe difference is as under:\n- Depreciation of the (domestic) currency occurs when the value of the (domestic) currency reduces in\nthe international money market, because of the market forces of supply and demand, Ine governmen\n- Devaluation of the (domestic) currency occurs when the value of the domestic currency is deliberately\nplays no role whatsoever.\nreduced by the government by raising the exchange rate. The market forces of supply and demand\nplay no role whatsoever.\nWhen Exchange Rate is set lower than the Equilibrium Exchange Rate:\nRevaluation of the Domestic Currency and its Implications\n• Fig. 11.9 illustrates the situation when exchange rate is set lower than the equilibrium exchange\nExchange Rate\n(Indian Rupees: US Dollars)\n(T 80: 1 US $)\n(2 70: 1 US $)\nEquilibrium Exchange Rate\nSupply of US $\n= Demand for US $\n(Here, PQ = ab)\n-X\nObservations:\n• In Fig. 11.9, OR is the\nequilibrium exchange rate:\n1 US $ is exchanged for 7 80.\n• OR, is the exchange rate as\nfixed by the government.\nHere, 1 US $ is exchanged\nfor ‹ 70.\nHere, the value of the domestic\ncurrency (Indian rupee) has\nbeen deliberately raised by the\ngovernment. This is called\n'Revaluation'.\nSupply and Demand (US$)\nFig. 11.9: Exchange Rate lower than the Equilibrium Exchange Rate\nImplications of Revaluation\n• Revaluation leads to excess demand for foreign currency in the international money market.\nIn Fig. 11.9, excess demand (D, > Sp) = PQ. The RBI must fulfill this excess demand by releasing\nsupplies from its reserves of forex.\n• In case supplies are not released from the reserves of forex, there may emerge a black marke\nfor the sale and purchase of dollars. The buyers may have to pay a premium for the purchase ot\ndollars over and above the price fixed by the government.\n• It is expected to have the following impact on a country's trade profile:\n• Fall in exports.\n• Rise in imports.\n534\nForeign Exchange Rate","image":"pages/IMG_0614.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS","page":"IMG_0614","text":"Revaluation of the Currency is different from Appreciation of the currency\nThe difference is as under:\n• Appreciation of the (domestic) currency occurs when the value of the (domestic) curency rises in\nthe international money market, because of the market forces of supply and demand. The government\nplays no role whatsoever.\n• Revaluation of the (domestic) currency occurs when the value of the domestic currency is deliberately\nraised by the government by lowering the exchange rate. The market forces of supply and demand play\nno role whatsoever.\nLess Developed Countries often Devalue their Currency\nLess developed countries often devalue their currency: domestic currency is made cheaper in relation to\nthe foreign currency. This is because of the three principal reasons:\n) Devaluation (implying a planned fall in the value of the domestic currency) is expected to raise the\ndemand for the domestically produced goods and services. Accordingly, exports are expected\nto rise.\n(i) Rise in exports (owing to devaluation) is expected to increase the supply of foreign exchange\ninto the domestic economy. This facilitates import of essential goods from rest of the world.\n(i) A fall in the value of domestic currency induces private foreign investment. Because, for every\ndollar, the investors are going to get more rupees, after devaluation. This is expected to promote\nGDP growth.\nGold Standard System of Exchange Rate -\nAn Old Variant of Fixed Exchange Rate System\n• Gold standard system of exchange rate is an old variant of fixed exchange rate system.\n• According to this system (prevalent in most countries prior to 1920s), gold was taken as the\ncommon unit of parity between currencies of different countries. Each country was to define\nvalue of its currency in terms of gold. Accordingly, value of one currency in terms of the other\ncurrency was fixed considering gold value of each currency.\nExample:\nIf UK € (Pound) = 4 g of gold, and US $ (Dollar) = 2 g of gold, then 1 UKE = 2 USS.\nExchange rate or exchange ratio between UK E and US $ = 1: 2. Two US dollars would\nexchange for 1 UK pound.\n• This system of exchange was also known as Mint Par Value of Exchange or Mint Parity. Mint value\nof a currency implied gold value of that currency.\nBretton Woods System of Exchange Rate\nAdjustable Peg System of Exchange Rate\nBretton Woods System (even when it was a fixed system of exchange rate) allowed some adjustments.\nSo, it was called 'adjustable peg system of exchange rate'. According to this system,\nDifferent currencies were pegged (or related) to one currency, that is US dollar. US dollar was\ned gold value at a fixed price.\n535","image":"pages/IMG_0614.jpg"},{"title":"DID YOU KNOW","page":"IMG_0615","text":"Bretton Woods System is named after United Nations Monetary Financial Conterence held at brentor\nWoods (USA) in 1944 which led to the establishment of IMF (International Monetary Fund).\nTable 11.2: Fixed Exchange Rate vs. Flexible Exchange Rate\nBasis of Difference\n1. Determination\nFixed Exchange Rate\nFlexible Exchange Rate\n- Fixed exchange rate is determined by the\ngovernment.\n- Flexible exchange rate is determined by\nthe forces of supply and demand in the\ninternational money market.\n2. Role of Market Forces\n3. Impact\n- Market forces play an important role.\n- As determined by the forces of supply and\ndemand, flexible rate of exchange leads\n4. Reserves\n- Market forces do not play any role.\n- As set and maintained by the government,\nthe fixed rate of exchange leads to:\n• devaluation (when the value of the\ndomestic currency is lowered by the\ngovernment), or\n• revaluation (when the value of the domestic\ncurrency is raised by the government).\n- Fixed exchange rate requires large reserves\nof forex.\n5. Uncertainty/\nSpeculation\n- Uncertainty/Speculation is very low in the\nsystem of fixed exchange rate. It arises\nonly when people expect some change in\nthe government policy.\n• depreciation of the domestic currency\n(when the exchange rate rises), and\n• appreciation of the domestic currency\nwhen the exchange rate falls).\n- Flexible exchange rate does not require\nlarge reserves\nof forex. Reserves are\nneeded only to exercise 'managed float\".\n- Uncertainty/Speculation is very high in\nthe system of flexible exchange rate. It\nis because of the uncertainty of market\nforces of supply and demand.\nKAVLR 11.2\nFIXED EXCHANGE RATE SYSTEM vs. FLEXIBLE EXCHANGE RATE SYSTEM","image":"pages/IMG_0615.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"IMG_0615","text":"Q. 3. Why was gold standard system of exchange rate abandoned (discontinued)?\nAns. Gold standard system of exchange rate was abandoned because this system required lots o\nreserves of gold. This raised the demand for gold. But, the supply of gold was extremely scarce\nin relation to its demand.\n536\nForeign Exchange Rate","image":"pages/IMG_0615.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS","page":"IMG_0615","text":"• Managed floating is a tool employed by the central bank to restore the value of the country's currency\n(in relation to other currencies) within the desired limits, even when exchange rate is determined by the\nmarket forces of demand and supply.\n• In fact, managed floating may be called as the mixture of both flexible and fixed exchange rate systems.\n- It comprises the element of flexible exchange rate system as the exchange rate is primarily determined\nby the forces of supply and demand.\n- Likewise, it comprises the element of fixed exchange rate system as the exchange rate is moderated\n(or managed) by way of intervention by the RBI.\n537","image":"pages/IMG_0615.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS","page":"IMG_0616","text":"Venture Capital in the International Money Market\nIt refers to investments in the purchase of forex (foreign exchange) in the international money market with\na view to earning profits. Fixed exchange rate system does not allow such investments.\n11.4.2 (a) Flexible Exchange Rate System: Merits\nA shift from fixed exchange rate system to flexible exchange rate system was desired in view of its\nmerits. Some of the principal merits of flexible exchange rate system (in relation to fixed exchange\nrate system) are as under:\n(1) Large Reserves of Forex not required: Flexible system of exchange rate does not require\nlarge reserves of foreign exchange with the government. Market forces of supply and\ndemand automatically drive the rate of exchange to the point of equilibrium.\n(2) Efficient Allocation of Resources: Flexible exchange rate system leads to equilibrium\nexchange rates of different currencies. Accordingly, allocation of resources (across different\ncountries of the world) is expected to become more and more efficient/optimum.\n(3) Large Size of the Forex Market: Since foreign exchange itself becomes a trading commodity.\nthe size of forex market tends to be large. This induces economic growth across all parts of\nthe world.\n(4) International Mobility of Liquidity: Since large reserves of forex are not required, flexible\nexchange rate tends to promote international mobility of liquidity. This is good for the less.\ndeveloped countries (like India), where foreign investment is a significant determinant of\nGDP growth.\nmarket. Trading in international currencies itself has emerged as an important economic\nactivity. This has led to the expansion in business activity and growth of international money\n11.4.2 (b) Flexible Exchange Rate System: Demerits\nHowever, flexible exchange rate system is not free from demerits. Some notable demerits of flexible\nexchange rate system are as under:\n(1) Marginalisation of Weak Currencies: Flexible exchange rate system leads to marginalisation\nof weak currencies in the international money market. Owing to their low demand, weak\ncurrencies (of small economies) often suffer huge depreciation in relation to strong currencies (of\nbig economies).\n(sation of weak currency - rapid loss of value of weak currency in the intermational money market.\n539","image":"pages/IMG_0616.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"IMG_0618","text":"India's perspective):\n(i) Indian going to Japan for medical treatment.\n(ii) Export of goods to USA.\n(iii) Purchase of land in Europe.\n(iv) Remittances to India by an Indian working in Japan.\n(v) Buying 500 dollars for speculative purposes.\nAns. Demand for Foreign Exchange: (i), (ili), (v)\nSupply of Foreign Exchange: (ii), (iv)\nQ. 5. Why does the demand for foreign currency fall when its price rises?\nAns. A rise in the price of foreign currency implies that more units of the domestic currency\n(say rupees) are needed to buy a unit of foreign currency (say US $). It leads to a fall in demand\nfor foreign currency, owing to the following reasons:\n(i) With a rise in the price of foreign currency, imports tend to fall, leading to a fall in demand\nfor foreign currency.\n(it) A rise in price of foreign currency makes travelling to rest of the world more expensive.\nAccordingly, demand for foreign currency falls.\n(ili) Investment in rest of the world becomes more expensive, leading to a fall in demand for\nforeign currency.\n(iv) Opportunity cost of holding foreign currency (in terms of domestic currency) tends to rise.\nAccordingly, demand for foreign currency tends to shrink.\nQ. 6. State any two factors that explain extension of demand for a foreign currency in response to a\n(i) When foreign currency (say US $) becomes cheaper (in relation to the domestic currenc).\nwe get more dollars per unit of our currency. Accordingly, imports become lucrative.\nThis raises demand for foreign currency.\n(ii) When foreign currency becomes cheaper, domestic investors will be induced to make\ngreater investment in rest of the world. Accordingly, demand for foreign currency rises.\nQ. 7. State any two factors that explain contraction of supply of a foreign currency when its price in\nterms of the domestic currency falls.\nAns. (i) When foreign currency becomes cheaper (in relation to domestic currency), purchasin\nbower of the foreign currency in the domestic market tends to fall. This leads to a fall\nforeign currency reduces.\nforeign demand for the domestic goods. Implying fall in our exports. Accordingly, supply of\n(ii) When foreign currency becomes cheaper (in relation to domestic currency), less rupees are\nInvestment). This reduces the supply of foreign currency.\navailable for a US dollar. Accordingly, foreigners are less inclined to make FDI (Foreign Direct\n542","image":"pages/IMG_0618.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS","page":"IMG_0619","text":"• Spot exchange rate (also known as current rate of exchange) is that rate of exchange which prevails\nin the market at the time when transactions are made. It relates only to spot transactions in the\ninternational money market.\n• Forward exchange rate is that exchange rate at which forward transactions are to be honoured. It has\nnothing to do with spot transactions in the international money market. In fact, forward exchange rate\nis a sort of 'contracted' exchange rate to be applicable for the transactions which are signed today but\nare to be honoured sometimes in the future.","image":"pages/IMG_0619.jpg"},{"title":"Power Points [Summary of the Chapter]","page":"IMG_0619","text":"• Exchange Rate refers to the price of one currency in relation to other currencies in the international\nmoney market (or international exchange market). [Example: If ? 80 are to be paid to buy one US\ndollar, exchange rate between the two currencies = 80: 1.]\n• Systems:\n(i) Flexible exchange rate system, and\n(ii) Fixed exchange rate system.\n• Flexible Exchange Rate is a floating rate of exchange, determined by the supply of and demand for\ndifferent currencies.\n• When owing to the free play of the forces of supply and demand, exchange rate happens to ris\n• On the other hand, when exchange rate happens to fall, it is called 'appreciation of the (domestic)\nit is called 'depreciation' of the (domestic) currency.\n• Equilibrium Exchange Rate occurs when:\nSupply of toreign currency/foreign exchange = Demand for foreign currency/foreign exchang\n544\n/a Fixed Exchange Rate System\nForeign Exchange Rate\n• Exchange rate is set and maintained by the government at a particular level. Market forces of\nsupply and demand have no role to play.\n- When the exchange rate is raised by the government, it is called devaluation of the (domestic)\ncurrency.\n- When it is lowered, it is called 'revaluation' of the (domestic) currency.\nDetermination: Fixed exchange rate is determined by the government of the country.\n• Managed Floating, also called Dirty Floating, is a system of floating exchange rate (where exchange\nrate is determined by the forces of demand and supply) but occasionally, the float is managed by\nthe central bank of the country by way of sale and purchase of foreign exchange in the international\nmoney market. Managed floating is an attempt to keep the exchange rate within the desired limits.\n- Merits and Demerits of Fixed Exchange Rate\n• Merits: (i) Market stability and mobility of capital, (ii) Growth of international trade, (i) Stable\nmacroeconomic policies, (iv) Devaluation: A key tool to expand foreign market for the domestic\nproducers, and (v) A check on inflation.\n• Demerits: (i) Required large reserves of forex, (ii) Inefficient allocation of resources, (i) Small\nsize of forex market, (iv) Discourages venture capital, and (v) Fixed exchange rate may not be the\nequilibrium rate.\n• Merits and Demerits of Flexible Exchange Rate\n• Merits: (i) Large reserves of forex not required, (i) Efficient allocation of resources, (il) Large\nsize of the forex market, (iv) Promote international mobility of liquidity, and (V) Promotes venture\ncapital.\n• Demerits: (i) Marginalisation of weak currencies, (ii) Uncertainty of the market, (il) A stable\nmonetary policy not possible, (iv) Bilateral trade agreement becomes difficult, and (v) External\nshocks.\n• Demand for Foreign Currency/Foreign Exchange depends upon:\n(i) Repayment of international loans, (i) Investment in rest of the world, (ill) Imports, (iv) Direct\npurchases abroad, (v) Grants and donations, (vi) Payment of incomes, and (vil) Speculative\ntrading.\n• Supply of Foreign Currency/Foreign Exchange depends upon:\n(il) Investments from rest of the world\n(i) Exports\n(ili) Direct purchases by rest of the world\n(iv) Loans from rest of the world\n(v) Grants and donations from rest of the world (vi) Income receipts\n(vii) Remittances by the non-residents\n• Foreign Exchange Market refers to the market for national currencies of different countries of the\nworld.\n• Functions: (i) Transfer function, (i) Credit function, and (il) Hedging function.\n• Spot Market deals with current sale and purchase of foreign exchange. It determines spot rate of\nexchange.\nForward Market deals with such sale and purchase of foreign exchange which are contracted\ntoday but are implemented sometimes in the future. It determines forward rate of exchange.\n545","image":"pages/IMG_0619.jpg"}],"subject":"macro","number":"11"},{"id":"12","title":"Balance of Payments","intro":"Balance of payments is a statement of accounts showing all monetary transactions of a country with the rest of the world during a period of time, generally one year.","lessons":[{"title":"Balance of payments: account and recording rule","body":"Balance of payments is a statement of accounts showing all monetary transactions of a country with the rest of the world during a period of time, generally one year. It records goods, services, income, transfers and asset-related transactions between residents and non-residents. Foreign-exchange receipts are credit items; payments are debit items. The textbook uses current, capital and official-reserves accounts. Keep its school-level account terminology when answering.","formula":"","source":"IMG_0620","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"12.1 BoP (or BoP ACCOUNTS)"},{"title":"Current account, trade balance and invisibles","body":"Current account records exports and imports of goods and services, factor income and current transfers. Goods are visible trade or merchandise; services, factor incomes and transfers are invisibles. Balance of trade covers goods only. A gift from abroad is a current transfer; wages for a service are factor income. The supplied purple box treats direct tourist purchases as invisible trade: preserve that convention for this textbook.","formula":"Trade balance = Goods exports − Goods imports\nCurrent-account balance = Trade balance + Net services + Net factor income + Net current transfers","source":"IMG_0621","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"12.2.1 Current Account"},{"title":"Capital account and asset/liability changes","body":"Capital account records transactions that change a country’s assets or liabilities in relation to the rest of the world. The textbook includes borrowing, foreign investment, NRI deposits, banking capital and short-term debt. Inward FDI or an external loan is a credit; an outward investment or repayment of foreign loan principal is a debit. Interest on a foreign loan belongs in the current account; the principal belongs in the capital account. FDI involves a lasting productive interest/control; portfolio investment purchases financial securities.","formula":"","source":"IMG_0622","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"12.2.2 Capital Account"},{"title":"Official reserves, deficit and surplus","body":"If autonomous foreign-exchange payments exceed autonomous receipts, there is a BoP deficit, financed by accommodating transactions such as a drawdown of reserves or official borrowing. A surplus can increase official reserves. A decrease in reserve assets finances a shortfall; an increase uses foreign exchange. Follow the sign convention explicitly stated in a numerical instead of confusing a positive stock increase with a credit entry.","formula":"","source":"IMG_0624","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"12.2.3 Official Reserves Account"},{"title":"Autonomous versus accommodating transactions","body":"Autonomous transactions are undertaken for independent economic motives such as profit, rather than to correct a BoP imbalance; they are above-the-line items. Accommodating transactions are undertaken to finance or correct the imbalance and are below the line. A foreign loan may be autonomous when raised commercially or accommodating when arranged specifically to finance a deficit. Classification depends on purpose, not merely the type of transaction.","formula":"","source":"IMG_0625","kind":"Purple-box concept","textbookHeading":"12.3.1 Autonomous and Accommodating Items of BoP Account"},{"title":"Accounting balance versus operational balance","body":"BoP balances in the accounting sense because every transaction has a counterpart and financing entries close the accounts. This does not mean autonomous receipts equal autonomous payments or that the economy has no external problem. A current-account deficit financed by foreign borrowing is consistent with accounting balance and increasing indebtedness. Read the Ability Zone worked table to see a current-account deficit offset by capital inflows.","formula":"","source":"IMG_0627","kind":"Ability Zone","textbookHeading":"ABILITY ZONE · Balance of Payments: Accounting Sense and Operational Sense"},{"title":"Causes and significance of disequilibrium","body":"Economic causes include large development imports, business cycles, domestic inflation, import substitution and changes in trading partners’ costs. Political instability can discourage foreign investment; policy changes can increase imports. Social causes include changes in tastes and cross-border preferences. BoP data show external strengths, weaknesses, debt and reserve pressures and help formulate policy. A trade deficit alone does not tell you the whole BoP position.","formula":"","source":"IMG_0627","kind":"Concept","textbookHeading":"12.3.2 Significance of BoP Accounts · Causes of Disequilibrium"}],"pages":[{"id":"IMG_0620","image":"pages/IMG_0620.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\n12.1 BoP (or BoP ACCOUNTS)\nMeaning and Concept of BoP\nBalance of payments (BoP) is a statement of accounts showing all monetary transactions of a\ncountry with the rest of the world during a period of time, generally one year. Being monetary\ntransactions, these are also called economic transactions. These transactions may be made by the\nindividuals, firms and the government of a country. Broadly, the monetary transactions relate to:\ni) export (X) and import (M) of goods. In the BoP language, it is called 'merchandise' or\n'visible trade' (simply because goods are visible when they cross the borders or goods can\nbe seen),\n(ii) export and import of services. It is called 'invisible trade' (simply because services are not\nvisible when they cross the borders or services cannot be seen),\n(iii) international sale and purchase of financial assets. Financial assets include stocks and\nbonds, and\n(iv) international sale and purchase of real assets. Real assets are like plant and machinery.\n•There is an inflow of foreign exchange into the country when we export goods and services or\nwhen the foreigners invest in our financial or real assets. Likewise, there is an outflow of foreign\nexchange from our country to rest of the world when we import goods and services or when our\nresidents invest in the financial or real assets of other countries.\nBoP accounts record all receipts and payments of foreign exchange. Receipts are recorded as\ncredit items, while payments are recorded as debit items. The BoP accounts, thus prepared, reflect\nperformance of our economy in relation to rest of the world.\nCRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS\nNon-inclusion of Tourist Spending on Goods as 'Export' or 'Import'\n• Only such goods are treated as 'export or 'import which are tangible and can be seen crossing the\nborders as merchandise.\n• Goods bought by the foreign tourists in the domestic markets are not treated as 'export.\nLikewise goods purchased by the domestic tourists in rest of the world are not treated as import.\n• All direct purchases by non-resident tourists in the domestic economy and by resident tourists abroad\nare treated as \"invisible trade.\n12.2 COMPONENTS OF BoP ACCOUNT\nBoP accounts include the following components:\nComponents of BoP Accounts\nCurrent Account\nCapital Account\nOfficial Reserves Account\n566\nBalance of Payments\nFollowing is their brief description:\n12.2.1 Current Account\nMeaning and Concept of Current Account\nCurrent account records receipt and payment of foreign exchange on account of such transactions\nwhich do not impact asset-liability status of a country in relation to rest of the world. Liabilities or\nassets of a country (in relation to rest of the world) are neither raised nor reduced. In other words,\ncurrent account transactions do not give rise to 'future claims.\nComponents of Current Account\nPrincipal components (items) of current account BoP are as under:\n(1) Export and Import of Goods: Export and import of goods is treated as 'merchandise or\n'visible trade'. This is a visible trade because goods are tangible (material) and therefore, can\nbe seen while crossing the borders.\nExample:\nExport or import of smart-phones can be seen while crossing the borders.\n(2) Export and Import of Services: Export and import of services is treated as invisible trade!. This is\nbecause services are not tangible (material) and therefore, cannot be seen while crossing the\nborders.\nExample:\nInsurance services being rendered across the borders cannot be seen as crossing the\nborders.\nServices are further classified as: factor services and non-factor services.\n(i) Factor Services: Factor services are those which lead to factor payments or factor\nincome.\n> In the BoP accounts, monetary transactions related to factor incomes are split as:\n(a) investment income, and (b) compensation of employees.\n> Investment income includes income on account of rent, interest and profit.\nIn BoP Accounts:\nFactor Incomes = Compensation of employees\n+ Investment income (= rent + interest + profit)\n(ii) Non-factor Services: Non-factor services include all services, other than factor services.\nInsurance and banking services may be cited as examples.\n> Monetary transactions related to non-factor services are recorded as receipts when\nthese services are exported, and as payments when these services are imported.\n(3) Current Transfers: Current transfers refer to transfers for free. These are unilateral transfers\nmade by way of gifts, grants and remittances (by the residents settled abroad), In the Bop\naccounts, current transfers are treated as an element of 'invisibles\".\n567","file":"macro 12/IMG_0620.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0621","image":"pages/IMG_0621.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\nQ. 1. State whether the following items are visible or invisible items of trade:\n(i) Export of laptops to Nepal\n(il) Insurance services to Bhutan\n(il) Export of coffee from Coorg\n(iv) Import of COVID vaccines from USA\n(v) Consultancy services of KPMG India used by a foreign firm\nAns. Visible Items: (i), (iii, (iv)\nInvisible Items: (ii), (v)\nQ. 2. In what way do current account transactions not lead to future claims?\nAns. Current account transactions (such as trade in goods and services and current transfers) do not\nlead to future claims in the sense that these transactions (unlike capital account transactions like\nborrowings) do not lead to any contractual obligation to be settled in future.\nQ. 3. How is the import of services recorded in the BoP, and what does it signify?\nAns. The import of services is recorded as a debit item in the current account of BoP, and is recorded\nas invisible trade. It signifies outflow of foreign exchange to rest of the world impacting\nCAD (current account balance)\nCRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS\nImportant Observations\n• In the BoP accounts, current transfers are treated as an element of invisibles. So that, invisibles in\nthe BoP accounts include:\n(i) monetary transactions related to export and import of factor services,\n(i) monetary transactions related to export and import of non-factor services, and\n(i) monetary transactions related to current transfers.\nInvisibles in BoP Accounts\n(1) Monetary transactions related to export and\nimport of services\n(2) Monetary transactions related\nto current transfers\n(1) Monetary transactions related to export and\nimport of factor services (briefly\ncalled 'income)\n(i) Monetary transactions related to export and\nimport of non-factor services\n568\nFollowing is a flow chart presentation of the components of current account Bop:\nComponents of Current Account BoP\nStructure of Current Account BoP\nBallance of Payments\nExport of Goods\n8\nImport of Goods\n(M)\nInvisibles\nExport and Import\nof Merchandise\nExport and Import\nof Visibles\nIncome\ncomponents of incom\nfrom factor services\nNon-facto\nServices\nMonetary\ntransactions on\ntransactions on\naccount of export\naccount of export\nand import of\nand import of\nfactor services\nnon-factor services\n[Note: Current account records all payments to rest of the world as debit (indicated by the '- sign) and\nall receipts from rest of the world as credit (indicated by the ' sign). Net receipts refer to the\ndifference between receipts and payments.]\nEstimation of Balance Related to Current Account\nBalance' relating to current account BoP is estimated in terms of four parameters as under:\n(1) Trade Balance/Merchandise Balance = X - M.\nTrade balance is reflected as:\n(a) Trade Deficit when M > X, and\n(b) Trade Surplus when X > M.\n(2) Goods and Services Balance\nExport of Goods\nImport of Goods\nExport of non-factor services\nImport of non-factor services\n= Trade balance + Balance on account of non-factor services\n(3) Invisibles Balance\n= Balance on non-factor services\n+ Balance on income (balance on factor services)\n+ Balance on current transfers\n(4) Current Account Balance\n= Trade Balance\n+ Invisibles Balance\nIt must be clearly understood that:\n• While estimating trade balance, we consider goods only.\n• While estimating goods and services balance, we consider (i) goods, and i) non-factor services\n569","file":"macro 12/IMG_0621.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0622","image":"pages/IMG_0622.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\n• While estimating invisibles balance, we consider (i) balance relating to non-factor services, (i) balance\nrelating to factor services (called balance on income), and (iii) balance on current transfers\nTable 12.1 shows actual estimation of Balance relating to Current Account BoP.\nTable 12.1: Estimation of Balance related to Current Account Bop\n(based on data for the period 2018-19, Economic Survey, 2019-20)\nUS million $\n3,37,237\n5,17,519\n1,23,026\n81,941\n-28,861\n69,946\nBalance of Payments\nExports\nImports\nInvisibles (net)\n(a) Non-factor Services\n(b) Income\n(c) Transfers\n(1) Trade Balance\n= X-M\n= 3,37,237 - 5,17,519\n= (-) 1,80,282 Trade Deficit\n(2) Goods and Services Balance\n= Trade balance + Balance on account of non-factor services\n= (-) 1,80,282 + 81,941\n= (-) 98,341\n(3) Invisibles Balance\n= Balance on non-factor services + Balance on income (factor services) + Balance on transfers\n= 81,941 - 28,861 + 69,946\n= 1,23,026\n(4) Current Account Balance\n= Trade balance + Invisibles balance (= Net of invisibles)\n= (-) 1,80,282 + 1,23,026\n= (-) 57,256\nNote: • Exports of goods and services are recorded as positive (+) items as these cause flow of foreign exchange\ninto the domestic economy.\n• omports of goods and services are recorded as negative (-)items as these cause flow of foreign echange\nout of the domestic economy.\n• All receipts on account of transfers are recorded as positive (+) items, while payments are recorded a\nnegative (-) items.]\nAVLR 12,\nBALANCE OF TRADE AND CURRENT ACCOUNT BALANCE\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\nQ. 4. Name three such items which are not included in balance of trade.\nAns. Three items which are not included in balance of trade are:\n(i) Export and import of services such as of shipping, insurance and banking.\n(ii) Interest and dividend payments between the countries.\n(iii) Expenditure by the tourists.\nQ. 5. What is the difference between balance of trade and current account balance?\nAns. Balance of trade refers to the balance occurring on account of export and import of visible items\n(goods only).\nCurrent account balance includes the balance of trade as well as balance on invisibles.\n12.2.2 Capital Account\nMeaning and Concept of Capital Account\n• Capital account records receipts and payments of such transactions which cause an impact on\nasset-liability status of a country in relation to rest of the world. Liabilities or assets of a country\n(in relation to rest of the world) are either raised or reduced. In other words, capital account\ntransactions lead to future claims.\nConfusion is sometimes raised with regard to the export and import of capital goods, like plant\nand machinery. Should such transactions be included in the capital account?\n• Answer is 'No'. Let it be absolutely clear that the export and import of all types of goods (consumer\ngoods or capital goods) is recorded as 'merchandise' or 'visible trade' in the current account of\nBoP. Thus, export and import of capital goods has nothing to do with capital account of BoP.\nDID YOU KNOW\n• Export and import of capital goods (plant and machinery) is NOT included in the capital account.\n• Export and import of all types of goods (consumer goods or capital goods) is recorded as\n'merchandise' or 'visible trade' in the current account of BoP.\nComponents of Capital Account\nTwo principal components of capital account are:\n(1) Borrowing: Borrowing is split as:\n(i) External Commercial Borrowing: It is available at the market rate of interest (in the\ninternational money market).\n(ii) External Assistance: It is available at the concessional rate of interest.\n> Borrowing from rest of the world raises our liability to rest of the world. But, note it\ncarefully, that it is recorded as a 'credit item\" in the capital account of BoP. The reason\nis this: all receipts of foreign exchange are recorded as credit items in the BoP accounts.\n> Thus, borrowing of rest of the world (or lending to rest of the world) would be\nrecorded as 'debit item' in the capital account, as it causes flow of foreign exchange\nfrom our country to rest of the world.\n(2) Foreign Investment: Foreign investment is split as:\n(i) Portfolio Investment: It basically refers to foreign institutional investment (FIl). It is\ninvestment by rest of the world in shares and bonds of the domestic companies.\n571\n570","file":"macro 12/IMG_0622.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0623","image":"pages/IMG_0623.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\n(ii) Foreign Direct investment: It relates to ownership of enterprises in the domestic\neconomy by rest of the world.\nExample:\nWalmart stores in India.\nOther Components of Capital Account\nBesides, borrowing and investment (which are the principal components or capital account) there are\nother components, as under:\n(3) NRI Deposits: In the context of the Indian economy, deposits is also a significant\nconstituent of capital account. However, here a note of caution is essential:\n• Only such NRI deposits are to be considered (as a component of capital account) which are\nmade in the domestic economy. Thus, non resident Indians should make deposits in India,\n• It also needs to be noted that money sent by the NRIs to their families in India is to be\ntreated as current transfers\", and are to be recorded in current account of Bop. Only\ndeposits held by NRIs in the domestic economy are to be considered as a component of\ncapital account.\n(4) Banking Capital (Other than NRI Deposits): Banking capital is yet another component of\ncapital account. Banking Capital refers to 'foreign assets' held by the commercial banks.\n• Owing to drawdown of foreign assets of the commercial banks (the commercial banks\nconverting their foreign assets into liquidity, inflow of foreign exchange into the domestic\neconomy tends to rise.\n(5) Short-term Trade Credit: In the context of the Indian BoP, short-term trade credit is another\nimportant component of capital account. It arises on account of purchases in the international\nmarket without making immediate payment.\n• Repayment of short-term debt to rest of the world leads to outflow of foreign exchange to\nrest of the world. Accordingly, it is recorded in the capital account with a negative sign.\nInward flow of foreign exchange (from rest of the world), on the other hand, is recorded\nwith a positive sign.\nFollowing is a flow chart presentation of the components of capital account BoP:\nComponents of Capital Account BoP\n9\nStructure of Capital Account BoP\n(indicating Net Capital Flows)\nBorrowing\nForeign\nNRI\nDeposits\nBanking Capital\n(excluding\nNRI Deposits)\nShort-term\nTrade Credit\nCommercial\nBorrowing\nExternal\nAssistance\nInstitutional\nnvestment also\nInvestment)\n(Foreign\nInvestment)\n572\nBalance of Payments\n(Note: • Similar to cureal receipt, famal account records all payments to rest of the word as debit indicated by\nthe '-' sign and all receipts from rest of the world as credit, indicated by the + sign.\n• Often, flows in the capital account are shown as 'Net Capital Flows. Net flows reflect the balance on\naccount of capital account transactions, which may be positive or negative. Positive balance indicates that\njust the opposite.]\nthe inward flow of foreign exchange is greater than the outward flow, while the negative balance indicates\nEstimation of Balance Related to Capital Account\nBalance of the capital account is estimated as the net of positive and negative values. Table 12.2\nshows the actual estimation of Capital Account Balance in India.\nTable 12.2: Estimation of Capital Account Balance (based on data\nfor the period 2018-19, Economic Survey, 2019-20)\nExternal Assistance (net)\nExternal Commercial Borrowing (net)\nUS million $\n3,413\n10,416\nShort-term Credit\nBanking Capital (net) (inclusive of NRI Deposits)\nForeign Investment (net)\nOther Flows (net)\nCapital Account Balance\n1,026\n54,403\nIt shows that during the period (2018-19), there has been a net inflow of foreign exchange of\nUS $ 54,403 on account of capital account BoP transactions.\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\nQ. 6. State whether the following transactions will be recorded on debit or credit side of India's BoP:\n(i) Import of spices by US from India.\n(i) Loan raised from World Bank to finance deficit in BoP.\n(ili) Repayment of loan by Indian government to IMF.\n(iv) Purchase of a firm by Reliance Ltd. in Japan.\n(v) Sale of Indian jewellery in Europe.\n(vi) Purchase of shares of Nykaa by an American resident.\n(vii) Export of machinery to India.\n(vili) Takeover of a foreign company by Reliance Ltd.\n(ix) Purchase of guns from Russia.\n(x) Receipt of unilateral transfers from ROW for pandemic affected areas.\n(xi) Export of computer software.\n(xil) Transfer of funds to relatives abroad.\nAns. Debit Side: (ii, (iv), (vii), (vii, (ix), (xii)\nCredit Side: (i), (ii), (v), (vi), (x), (xi)\nQ. 7. State whether the following transactions will be recorded in the current account or the capital\naccount of India's BoP:\n(i) Purchase of shares of Reliance Jio by Facebook.\n(i) Import of capital goods from USA.\n(iii) Lending by India to Bhutan.\n573","file":"macro 12/IMG_0623.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0624","image":"pages/IMG_0624.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\n(iv) Repayment of loan by Government of India.\n(v) Grant received from abroad amidst COVID-19.\n(vi) Remittances from abroad.\n(vil) Infrastructural investment abroad.\n(vili) Export of soft toys from China to India.\nAns. Current Account: (ii), (v), (vi), (viii)\nCapital Account: (i), (iii), (iv), (vii)\nConcept of Overall Balance\n• In the Indian BoP accounting system, overall balance is estimated as the sum total of\n(i) current account balance,\n(ii) capital account balance, and\n(i) errors and omissions (accounting for statistical discrepancies).\nOverall Balance = Current account balance\n+ Capital account balance\n+ Errors and omissions\nHere is an illustration of the Estimation of Overall Balance (based on data for the period 2018-19,\nEconomic Survey, 2019-20).\nOverall Balance\n= Current account balance + Capital account balance + Errors and omissions\n= (-) 57,256 + 54,403 - 486 = -3,339 (US million $)\nCapital Estimated\nbalance errors and\n12.2.3 Concept of Official Reserves Account\n(indicating Reserves of Forex with the RBI)\n• The overall balance is finally reflected in the Official Reserves Account of the RBI. Because, RBlis\nthe custodian of forex reserves of the country, and all forex transactions in the country are routed\nthrough the RBI.\n• If overall balance is positive, it causes increase in official reserves; if overall balance is negative\n(as in the above illustration), it causes decrease in official reserves.\n• In the above illustration, Official Reserves Account of the RBI would show a decrease of\n3,339 US million $.\nHere, comes an important observation which needs to be carefully noted:\n• Sometimes, Official Reserves Account is shown as a part of the capital account BoP, rather than a\nseparate account. If official reserves are shown as a part of the capital account BoP, then BoP alway\n• Because (for purpose of balancing BoP accounts), increase in the official reserves is indicated bya\nnegative sign, while decrease is indicated by a positive sign.\n574\n• Table 12.3 Is an example or Indias Bop accounts, wherein increase/decrease in official reserves\nBalance of Payments\nis shown as a part of the capital account BoP. Accordingy. Bop shows a perfect balance, or the\nsome major items of India's BoP.\nbalance of BoP accounts is equal to O (zero). Table 12.3 is based on the actual data relating to\nTable 12.3: Major Items of India's BoP, showing how Balance of Payments always\nBalances (2018-19, Economic Survey, 2019-20)\nUS million $\n1. Exports\n3,37,237\n3. Trade Balance\n5,17,519\n- 1,80,282\n4. Invisibles (net)\n5. Current Account Balance\n(-) 57,256\n6. Capital Account Balance\n54,403\n7. Errors and Omissions\n(- 486\n8. Overall Balance\n(- 3,339\n9. *Official Reserves [Increase (-)/Decrease (+)]\n*Item 9 in BoP accounts always shows change in official reserves (increase or decrease). It never shows total official\nreserves. In the present table, there is a decrease in official reserves as indicated by a't sign.\nTo repeat, increase in official reserves is indicated by a '- sign, while the decrease is indicated by a * sign. This is only\nthen that the BoP account would reflect a perfect balance (= 0).\n• In case, Official Reserves are not shown as a part of the capital account, the Official Reserves\nAccount is separately presented and classified as \"Below the Line\". In such situations, the current\nand capital accounts together may show a surplus or deficit BoP.\nThus:\nBoP Surplus = Current Account Surplus + Capital Account Surplus\n(When Official Reserves Account is not a part of capital account)\nBoP Deficit = Current Account Deficit + Capital Account Deficit (When Official\nReserves Account is not a part of capital account)\n• BoP always balances when Official Reserves Account is a part of capital account, and therefore:\nCurrent account balance + Capital account balance = Zero.\nCRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS\nChange in Official Reserves in BoP Accounting\nIn the Balance of Payments (BoP) system, the total inflows (credits) and outflows (debits) must balance\nto zero. The change in official reserves is crucial in maintaining this balance.\n(i) '+' sign = Decrease in official reserves (inflow of foreign currency).\n(ii) - sign = Increase in official reserves (outflow of domestic currency).\nThese signs reflect whether a country is increasing or decreasing its stock of reserves.\n(i) Increase in Official Reserves (-sign): An increase in reserves, such as purchasing foreign currency,\nrepresents an outflow of domestic currency. This represents a debit in the BoP, as it reflects a use of\ndomestic financial resources. In the context of the BoP, an outflow of resources (like buying foreign\nreserves) is a debit, and debits are traditionally represented with a minus sign.\n575","file":"macro 12/IMG_0624.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0625","image":"pages/IMG_0625.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\n(il) Decrease in Official Reserves (+ sign): A decrease in reserves, such as selling foreign assets,\nreflects an inflow of foreign currency, Selling foreign reserves or reducing them increases the supply\nof domestic currency in the economy (as the central bank might buy back domestic currency i\nexchange for foreign currency, and this is considered a credit in the Bor accounting system\n12.3 EQUILIBRIUM AND DISEQUILIBRIUM IN BOP\nEquilibrium in BoP\n• BoP equilibrium is struck when: Current account balance + Capital account balance + errors and\nomissions = Zero, and there is no movement (increase or decrease) of official reserves of the\ncentral bank. In case we ignore the element of errors and omissions, we can st\nequilibrium is stuck when:\nCurrent account balance + Capital account balance = Zero\nand there is no movement (increase/decrease) of the official reserves.\n• We can say that BoP is in a state of equilibrium when any negative balance in the current account\nis equally counterbalanced by a positive balance in the capital account, and there is no changein\nofficial reserves of the central bank.\n• Thus, in a state of BoP equilibrium inward flow of foreign exchange (on account of current account\nand capital account transactions) is exactly equal to the outward flow of foreign exchange, and\nthere is no change in official reserves (of foreign exchange and gold) with the central bank of the\ncountry.\nDisequilibrium in BoP\n• Disequilibrium in BoP occurs when the sum total of current account balance and capital account\nbalance is not zero; instead it is either some positive number or some negative number.\n• In case the sum total of current account balance and capital account balance is some posive\nnumber, it indicates BoP Surplus. On the other hand, if the sum total of current account balance\nand capital account balance is some negative number, it indicates BoP Deficit.\nThus, we have:\n(1) BoP Disequilibrium when: Current account balance + Capital account balance is NOT equal\nto zero, and it causes the movement of official reserves.\n(ii) BoP Surplus when: Current account balance + Capital account balance is some posi\nofficial reserves.\nnumber, pointing to net inward flow of foreign exchange, and leading to an increase in\n(ili) BoP Deficit when: Current account balance + Capital account balance is some negative\nofficial reserves.\nnumber, pointing to net outward flow of foreign exchange, and leading to a decrease in\n576\nBalance of Payments\n12.3.1 Autonomous and Accommodating Items of BoP Account\n• BoP transactions or items in BoP account are often classified as autonomous items and\naccommodating items. The difference is as under:\n• Autonomous items refer to such BoP transactions which are undertaken with a view to making\nprofits. It is due to these transactions that there is a BoP deficit/surplus.\n• Accommodating items, on the other hand, refer to such transactions which are undertaken by the\ncentral bank of a country with a view to correcting BoP imbalance and restoring BoP equilibrium.\n• It is important to note that accommodating items do not cause any movement of goods and\nservices across the borders. These relate only to the movement of official reserves with a view to\ncorrecting BoP imbalances.\n• Often the accommodating items are not reflected as an element of BoP accounts. These are\ntherefore called 'below the line' items, while autonomous items are called 'above the line' items.\nThus, when BoP deficit/surplus is estimated, it is with reference to only the autonomous items or\nautonomous transactions of BoP.\nBasis of Difference\n1. Meaning\n2. Classification\n3. Movement of\nGoods\n4. Impact\nTable 12.4: Autonomous Items vs. Accommodating Items\nAutonomous Items\n- Autonomous items refer to such BoP\ntransactions which are undertaken for\nconsiderations of profit.\n- Autonomous items are classified as 'above\nthe line' items of BoP.\n- Autonomous\ninvolve\nmovement of goods across the borders\n(like export and import of consumer goods\nor capital goods).\n- Autonomous items lead to BoP imbalance\n(BoP surplus or BoP deficit).\nAccommodating items\n- Accommodating items are free from the\nconsiderations of profit.\n- Accommodating items are classified as\n\"below the line items of BoP.\n- Accommodating items do not involve the\nmovement of goods across the borders.\nThese items only involve the movement of\nofficial reserves with the RBI.\n- Accommodating items lead to correction of\nBoP imbalance.\nJAVLR 12.2\nAUTONOMOUS ITEMS vs. ACCOMMODATING ITEMS\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\nQ. 8. State whether the following transactions are autonomous items or accommodating items:\n(i) Purchase of a computer from USA by an Indian resident.\n(ii) Sale of financial assets by RBI to cover BoP deficit.\n(iii) TCS raising loans from a bank in London.\n(iv) Lending activities performed by India.\n(v) Surplus in BoP account used to achieve BoP equilibrium.\n(vi) Liquidation of Forex Reserves by the RBI to finance BoP deficit.\nns. Autonomous Items: (i), (iii, (iv)\nAccommodating Items: (ii), (v), (vi)\n577","file":"macro 12/IMG_0625.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0626","image":"pages/IMG_0626.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\nQ. 9. Compare and contrast the implications of BoP surplus and deficit on a country'S foreign policy\nAns. A BoP surplus may enable a country to engage in more assertive foreign policy, as it has the\nfinancial resources to invest in internatonal intanives. in contrast, a Bordencit may force a\ncountry to adopt a more cautious stance, prioritising negotiations for trade agreements or\nfinancial aid to stabilise its economy.\nQ. 10. If a country's current account deficit is primarily due to high consumer imports, what strategies\ncould be employed to achieve BoP equilibrium?\nAns. Strategies may include promoting domestic production to reduce reliance on imports\nimplementing tariffs to discourage non-essential imports, enhancing export competitiveness\nthrough subsidies or support for key industries, and encouraging foreign direct investment to\nimprove the capital account balance.\n12.3.2 Significance of BoP Accounts (or BoP Data)\nHaving understood the concept and composition of BoP accounts, we can now write the significance\nof these accounts, in terms of the following observations:\n(1) Financial Status of the Domestic Economy: BoP accountsreveal financial status of the domestic\neconomy in relation to rest of the world. Borrowing reveals dependence on rest of the world.\nIt points to backwardness of the domestic economy.\n(2) Net Factor Income from Abroad: BoP data offers information on net factor income from\nabroad. It is an important component of national income.\n(3) X - M (A Component of AD): BoP accounts show exports and imports of a country. We know,\nnet of exports (X - M) is an important component of AD (aggregate demand in the domestic\neconomy).\nRise in 'X - M' leads to a rise in AD. It directly impacts the level of output and employment in\nthe domestic economy (particularly when there is unemployment due to deficiency of AD).\n(4) Market Potential: BoP accounts reflect market potential in the domestic economy, It is\nreflected by the size of foreign investment. Larger size of foreign investment points to high\nmarket potential in the economy.\n(5) Monetary and Fiscal Policies: BoP performance of a country impacts its monetary and fiscal\npolicies. In the event of greater flow of foreign exchange from rest of the world, there is a\npressure of demand for the domestic currency.\n• The RBI has to account for it in the formulation of its monetary policy. Likewise, poor-flow\nof foreign investment in the domestic economy, may point to hard tax laws in the domestic\neconomy. The government must account for it in the formulation of its fiscal policy.\nBriefly, Bor accounts (or BoP data) reveals financial strength of a country in relation to rest of the\nworld as well as the need for foreign investment.\nvorld. For less developed countries like India, it highlights the need for borrowing from rest of the\n578\nBalance of Payments\nBasis of Difference\n1. Concept\n2. Composition\n3. Significance\nBasis of Difference\n1. Concept\n2. Composition\n3. Nature of\nTransactions\nSOME VITAL DIFFERENCES\nTable 12.5: Current Account BoP vs. Capital Account BoP\nCurrent Account BoP\nCapital Account BoP\n- Current account records receipts\nand\npayments of foreign exchange on account\n- Capital account records receipts and\nof such transactions which do not impact\npayments of foreign exchange on account\nasset-liability status of a country in relation\nof such transactions which impact asset-\nto rest of the world.\nliability status of a country in relation to rest\nof the world.\n- Thus, current account transactions do not\ngive rise to future claims.\n- Thus, capital account transactions give rise\nto future claims.\n- Current account transactions include:\n- Capital account transactions include:\n• Export and import of goods (called visible\n• Borrowing,\ntrade),\nPrincipal\n• Export and import of services (called\ninvisible trade),\n• NRI Deposits,\n• Current transfers.\n• Banking Capital,\ncomponents\n• Short-term Debt.\n- Current account\ntransactions\nreveal\n- Capital account transactions reveal borrowings\nX-M, an important component of AD in the\nfrom rest of the world, It is a reffection of\ndomestic economy.\nbackwardness of the domestic economy.\n- Current account transactions reveal net\n- Capital account transactions show foreign\nfactor income from abroad, an important\ninvestment in the domestic economy. While\ncomponent of national income.\nit reveals market potential of the domestic\neconomy, it also reveals dependence on rest\nof the world for our GDP growth.\n- Because of their direct impact on AD, current\n- Capital account transactions do not cause\naccount transactions impact the level of\nany direct impact on the level of output\noutput and employment in the domestic\nand employment in the economy. These\neconomy.\ntransactions just reveal asset-liability status\nof the economy in relation to rest of the world.\nTable 12.6: Balance of Trade vs. Current Account Balance of Payments\nBalance of Trade\nCurrent Account Balance of Payments\n- Balance of trade is that account which\nCurrent account balance of payments is that\nrecords imports and exports of goods only.\naccount which records\n) import and export of goods,\n(il) import and export of services, and\n(ill) current transfers.\n- It is the difference between visible exports\nand visible imports.\n- It is the sum total of trade balance and\ninvisibles balance. Invisibles balance includes\n) balance on non-factor services,\n(il) balance on income arising out of factor\nservices, and\n(iil) balance on transfers.\n- It involves transactions relating to physical\n- It involves transactions relating to physical\ngoods which can be seen crossing the\ngoods (which can be seen crossing the\nborders) as well as transactions relating to\nservices which cannot be seen crossing the\nborders.\n579","file":"macro 12/IMG_0626.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0627","image":"pages/IMG_0627.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\nBasis of Difference\nTable 12.7: Balance of Payments vs. Balance of Trade\nBalance of Payments\n1. Concept\n2. Nature of\nTransactions\n3. Scope of\nTransactions\n4. Balance\n- Balance of payments\na summary\nstatement of all economic transactions of a\ncountry with rest of world.\nBalance of Trade\n- Balance of trade is the difference between\nvisible exports (X) and visible imports (M).\n- It records transactions related to goods as\nwell as services.\n- It records transactions related to goods\nonly.\n- It records both current account as well as\ndoes\nnot\ncapital account transactions.\nrecord\ntransactions.\ncapital\naccount\n- BoP always balances, when movement of\nRBI reserves is reflected in it.\n- It is either positive (X > M) or negative\n(X < M). It balances only when X = M.\nABILITY ZONE\nWhat Causes Disequilibrium in BoP?\nDisequilibrium in BoP is caused by a number of factors, broadly categorised as:\n(i) Economic Factors\n(a) Huge development expenditure by the government owing to which there are large scale imports.\nIt may cause a 'deficit BoP disequilibrium.\n(b) Business cycles in terms of recession, depression, recovery and boom. A period of boom may\nwitness large scale exports of a country. Accordingly, a 'surplus BoP disequilibrium' may occur.\n(c) High rate of inflation in the domestic market, compelling large scale imports of essential goods.\nThis causes 'deficit BoP disequilibrium.\n(d) Development of import substitutes, because of which imports are reduced and surplus BoP may\nemerge in place of deficit BoP.\n(e) Change in the cost structure of trading partners, because of technological and managerial\ninnovations. Favourable change in cost structure would encourage exports and lead to 'surplus\nBoP disequilibrium'.\n(i) Political Factors\n(a) Political instability owing to which inflow of direct foreign investment and portfolio investment.\nfrom abroad may shrink. It causes deficit on capital account BoP.\n(b) Populism policies of the government like huge cuts in import duty. This encourages imports\nbecause of which current account deficit may swell.\n(iil) Social Factors\n(a) Change in tastes and preferences, owing to which pattern of demand (in the international market)\nmay change. A favourable change may encourage exports, while an unfavourable shift may cause\na rise in imports. A rise in exports leads to current account surplus. A rise in imports leads to a\ncurrent account deficit.\n(b) Cross-border prejudices, which sometimes force the countries (like, India and Pakistan) to shit.\neconomy towards the state of deficit BoP disequilibrium.\no expensive sources of imports and less profitable areas of exports. Such situations often drive at\n580\nBalance of Payments\nBalance of Payments (BoP): Accounting Sense and Operational Sense\nLet us understand the concept of balance of payments in 'accounting sense and operational sense\nthrough the following illustration. Carefully examine the data in the given table:\nTable 12.8: Balance of Payments\nAssets\n7 in crore\n(Credits)\nLiabilities\nE in crore\n(Debits)\n1. Export of goods\n550\n1. Import of goods\n800\n2. Export of services (banking, shipping,\ninsurance, tourism, etc.)\n2. Import of services (banking, shipping,\ninsurance, tourism, etc.)\n3. Transfer payments from rest of the\n3. Transfer payments to rest of the world\nworld (gifts, aid, etc.)\n(gifts, aid, etc.)\n4. Capital receipts (loans, sale of assets\n200\n4. Capital payments (loans to foreigners,\nto foreigners, receipt of capital\nbuying of assets from foreigners, payment\nof capital to foreigners)\nTotal Receipts\n1,000\nTotal Payments\n1,000\nThe above table shows that in the accounting sense balance of payments balances, because total\nreceipts (credits) are equal to total payments (debits), i.e., * 1,000 crore. But in the operational sense, the\nanalysis of the table reveals the following facts:\n• Balance of Trade (the difference between the value of total exports and imports) is negative.\nBalance of Trade\n= Export of goods - Import of goods\n= 7 550 crore - 7 800 crore\n= (-) ‹ 250 crore\n• Balance of Payments on Current Account shows a deficit of ? 130 crore.\nBalance of Payments on Current Account\n= Trade Balance (Export of goods - Import of goods) + Invisibles Balance [Balance on\nnon-factor services (banking, shipping, etc.) + Balance on current transfers]\n= RF crore - 7 800 crore) + [R [(R crore - 750 crore) + ® R crore - 7 80 crore)]\n= (-)? 250 crore + ‹ 100 crore + 7 20 crore\n= (-) = 130 crore\n• Balance of Payments on Capital Account shows a surplus of ? 130 crore.\nBalance of Payments on Capital Account\n= Capital Receipts - Capital Payments\n= F 200 crore - ‹ 70 crore\n= · 130 crore\n• Overall Balance of Payments Balances (*1,000 crore of receipts = * 1,000 crore of payments)\nbecause the surplus balance of * 130 crore on capital account compensates deficit balance of\n* 130 crore on current account.\n• From where has the 7 130 crore surplus on capital account emerged?\nIn fact & 130 crore may have been received through foreign loans. In that case, the surplus of\ncapital account actually reflects our indebtedness, not a sound financial status.\n581","file":"macro 12/IMG_0627.HEIC"},{"id":"IMG_0628","image":"pages/IMG_0628.jpg","text":"Introductory Macroeconomics\nPower Points [Summary of the Chapter]\n• Balance of Payments is a summary statement of all monetary (or economic) transactions between a\ncountry and rest of the world.\n• Components:\n• Current account\n• Capital account\n• Official reserves account.\n• Economic Transactions in BoP are broadly classified as:\n• merchandise\n• invisibles\n• transactions leading to change in the ownership of assets vis-a-vis rest of the world.\n• Components:\n- Export and import of goods\n• Export and import of services [(a) factor services + (b) non-factor services]\n• Current transfers.\n• Export and import of goods is called 'merchandise' or 'visible trade'.\n• Invisibles include: Non-factor services, Factor income, Current transfers\n• Balance of Trade (or Trade Balance) = Export of merchandise - Import of merchandise\n• Current Account Balance = Trade balance + Invisibles balance.\n• Capital Account records receipts and payments of foreign exchange of a country on account of such\ntransactions which impact asset-liability status of a country in relation to rest of the world.\n• Components:\n› Borrowing\n> Foreign investment\n> NRI deposits\n> Banking capital\n> Short-term debt.\n• Borrowing includes: external commercial borrowing, and external assistance.\n• Investment includes: FIl (Foreign Institutional Investment), and FDI (Foreign Direct Investment).\n• Capital Account Balance shows net capital flows.\n• Positive Balance shows that inward flow of capital is greater than the outward flow.\n• Negative Balance shows that inward flow of capital is less than the outward flow.\n• BoP Equilibrium is a situation when current account balance and capital account balance add up to\nzero, and there is no movement (increase or decrease) in official reserves.\nOr, it is a situation when receipts and payments of a country on account of economic transactions\nwith rest of the world are exactly equal to each other, and there is no movement of official reserves.\n• BoP Deficit occurs when payments of a country on account of economic transactions with rest of\nthe world exceed its receipts and consequently, there is a decrease in official reserves.\n• BoP Surplus occurs when receipts of a country on account of economic transactions with rest of\nthe world exceed its payments and consequently, there is increase in official reserves.\n• Autonomous Items refer to such BoP transactions which are determined by considerations of profit.\nIt is due to these transactions that the BoP deficit/surplus arises. These are called 'above the line\nitems.\n• Accommodating Items refer to such BoP transactions which are not determined by considerations of\nprofit. It is due to these transactions that the BoP deficit/surplus is corrected. These are called below\nthe line items.\n582\nCompeteng\n1. Multiple\n(\nChoose the i\n1. In th\nstate\n(a)\n(b)\n(c)\n2. If 3\nval\n3.\nF\n4.","file":"macro 12/IMG_0628.HEIC"}],"boxes":[{"title":"CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS","page":"IMG_0620","text":"Non-inclusion of Tourist Spending on Goods as 'Export' or 'Import'\n• Only such goods are treated as 'export or 'import which are tangible and can be seen crossing the\nborders as merchandise.\n• Goods bought by the foreign tourists in the domestic markets are not treated as 'export.\nLikewise goods purchased by the domestic tourists in rest of the world are not treated as import.\n• All direct purchases by non-resident tourists in the domestic economy and by resident tourists abroad\nare treated as \"invisible trade.","image":"pages/IMG_0620.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"IMG_0621","text":"Q. 1. State whether the following items are visible or invisible items of trade:\n(i) Export of laptops to Nepal\n(il) Insurance services to Bhutan\n(il) Export of coffee from Coorg\n(iv) Import of COVID vaccines from USA\n(v) Consultancy services of KPMG India used by a foreign firm\nAns. Visible Items: (i), (iii, (iv)\nInvisible Items: (ii), (v)\nQ. 2. In what way do current account transactions not lead to future claims?\nAns. Current account transactions (such as trade in goods and services and current transfers) do not\nlead to future claims in the sense that these transactions (unlike capital account transactions like\nborrowings) do not lead to any contractual obligation to be settled in future.\nQ. 3. How is the import of services recorded in the BoP, and what does it signify?\nAns. The import of services is recorded as a debit item in the current account of BoP, and is recorded\nas invisible trade. It signifies outflow of foreign exchange to rest of the world impacting\nCAD (current account balance)","image":"pages/IMG_0621.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS","page":"IMG_0621","text":"Important Observations\n• In the BoP accounts, current transfers are treated as an element of invisibles. So that, invisibles in\nthe BoP accounts include:\n(i) monetary transactions related to export and import of factor services,\n(i) monetary transactions related to export and import of non-factor services, and\n(i) monetary transactions related to current transfers.\nInvisibles in BoP Accounts\n(1) Monetary transactions related to export and\nimport of services\n(2) Monetary transactions related\nto current transfers\n(1) Monetary transactions related to export and\nimport of factor services (briefly\ncalled 'income)\n(i) Monetary transactions related to export and\nimport of non-factor services\n568\nFollowing is a flow chart presentation of the components of current account Bop:\nComponents of Current Account BoP\nStructure of Current Account BoP\nBallance of Payments\nExport of Goods\n8\nImport of Goods\n(M)\nInvisibles\nExport and Import\nof Merchandise\nExport and Import\nof Visibles\nIncome\ncomponents of incom\nfrom factor services\nNon-facto\nServices\nMonetary\ntransactions on\ntransactions on\naccount of export\naccount of export\nand import of\nand import of\nfactor services\nnon-factor services\n[Note: Current account records all payments to rest of the world as debit (indicated by the '- sign) and\nall receipts from rest of the world as credit (indicated by the ' sign). Net receipts refer to the\ndifference between receipts and payments.]\nEstimation of Balance Related to Current Account\nBalance' relating to current account BoP is estimated in terms of four parameters as under:\n(1) Trade Balance/Merchandise Balance = X - M.\nTrade balance is reflected as:\n(a) Trade Deficit when M > X, and\n(b) Trade Surplus when X > M.\n(2) Goods and Services Balance\nExport of Goods\nImport of Goods\nExport of non-factor services\nImport of non-factor services\n= Trade balance + Balance on account of non-factor services\n(3) Invisibles Balance\n= Balance on non-factor services\n+ Balance on income (balance on factor services)\n+ Balance on current transfers\n(4) Current Account Balance\n= Trade Balance\n+ Invisibles Balance\nIt must be clearly understood that:\n• While estimating trade balance, we consider goods only.\n• While estimating goods and services balance, we consider (i) goods, and i) non-factor services\n569","image":"pages/IMG_0621.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"IMG_0622","text":"Q. 4. Name three such items which are not included in balance of trade.\nAns. Three items which are not included in balance of trade are:\n(i) Export and import of services such as of shipping, insurance and banking.\n(ii) Interest and dividend payments between the countries.\n(iii) Expenditure by the tourists.\nQ. 5. What is the difference between balance of trade and current account balance?\nAns. Balance of trade refers to the balance occurring on account of export and import of visible items\n(goods only).\nCurrent account balance includes the balance of trade as well as balance on invisibles.\n12.2.2 Capital Account\nMeaning and Concept of Capital Account\n• Capital account records receipts and payments of such transactions which cause an impact on\nasset-liability status of a country in relation to rest of the world. Liabilities or assets of a country\n(in relation to rest of the world) are either raised or reduced. In other words, capital account\ntransactions lead to future claims.\nConfusion is sometimes raised with regard to the export and import of capital goods, like plant\nand machinery. Should such transactions be included in the capital account?\n• Answer is 'No'. Let it be absolutely clear that the export and import of all types of goods (consumer\ngoods or capital goods) is recorded as 'merchandise' or 'visible trade' in the current account of\nBoP. Thus, export and import of capital goods has nothing to do with capital account of BoP.\nDID YOU KNOW\n• Export and import of capital goods (plant and machinery) is NOT included in the capital account.\n• Export and import of all types of goods (consumer goods or capital goods) is recorded as\n'merchandise' or 'visible trade' in the current account of BoP.\nComponents of Capital Account\nTwo principal components of capital account are:\n(1) Borrowing: Borrowing is split as:\n(i) External Commercial Borrowing: It is available at the market rate of interest (in the\ninternational money market).\n(ii) External Assistance: It is available at the concessional rate of interest.\n> Borrowing from rest of the world raises our liability to rest of the world. But, note it\ncarefully, that it is recorded as a 'credit item\" in the capital account of BoP. The reason\nis this: all receipts of foreign exchange are recorded as credit items in the BoP accounts.\n> Thus, borrowing of rest of the world (or lending to rest of the world) would be\nrecorded as 'debit item' in the capital account, as it causes flow of foreign exchange\nfrom our country to rest of the world.\n(2) Foreign Investment: Foreign investment is split as:\n(i) Portfolio Investment: It basically refers to foreign institutional investment (FIl). It is\ninvestment by rest of the world in shares and bonds of the domestic companies.\n571\n570","image":"pages/IMG_0622.jpg"},{"title":"DID YOU KNOW","page":"IMG_0622","text":"• Export and import of capital goods (plant and machinery) is NOT included in the capital account.\n• Export and import of all types of goods (consumer goods or capital goods) is recorded as\n'merchandise' or 'visible trade' in the current account of BoP.\nComponents of Capital Account\nTwo principal components of capital account are:\n(1) Borrowing: Borrowing is split as:\n(i) External Commercial Borrowing: It is available at the market rate of interest (in the\ninternational money market).\n(ii) External Assistance: It is available at the concessional rate of interest.\n> Borrowing from rest of the world raises our liability to rest of the world. But, note it\ncarefully, that it is recorded as a 'credit item\" in the capital account of BoP. The reason\nis this: all receipts of foreign exchange are recorded as credit items in the BoP accounts.\n> Thus, borrowing of rest of the world (or lending to rest of the world) would be\nrecorded as 'debit item' in the capital account, as it causes flow of foreign exchange\nfrom our country to rest of the world.\n(2) Foreign Investment: Foreign investment is split as:\n(i) Portfolio Investment: It basically refers to foreign institutional investment (FIl). It is\ninvestment by rest of the world in shares and bonds of the domestic companies.\n571\n570","image":"pages/IMG_0622.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"IMG_0623","text":"Q. 6. State whether the following transactions will be recorded on debit or credit side of India's BoP:\n(i) Import of spices by US from India.\n(i) Loan raised from World Bank to finance deficit in BoP.\n(ili) Repayment of loan by Indian government to IMF.\n(iv) Purchase of a firm by Reliance Ltd. in Japan.\n(v) Sale of Indian jewellery in Europe.\n(vi) Purchase of shares of Nykaa by an American resident.\n(vii) Export of machinery to India.\n(vili) Takeover of a foreign company by Reliance Ltd.\n(ix) Purchase of guns from Russia.\n(x) Receipt of unilateral transfers from ROW for pandemic affected areas.\n(xi) Export of computer software.\n(xil) Transfer of funds to relatives abroad.\nAns. Debit Side: (ii, (iv), (vii), (vii, (ix), (xii)\nCredit Side: (i), (ii), (v), (vi), (x), (xi)\nQ. 7. State whether the following transactions will be recorded in the current account or the capital\naccount of India's BoP:\n(i) Purchase of shares of Reliance Jio by Facebook.\n(i) Import of capital goods from USA.\n(iii) Lending by India to Bhutan.\n573","image":"pages/IMG_0623.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL TERMS & CONCEPTS","page":"IMG_0624","text":"Change in Official Reserves in BoP Accounting\nIn the Balance of Payments (BoP) system, the total inflows (credits) and outflows (debits) must balance\nto zero. The change in official reserves is crucial in maintaining this balance.\n(i) '+' sign = Decrease in official reserves (inflow of foreign currency).\n(ii) - sign = Increase in official reserves (outflow of domestic currency).\nThese signs reflect whether a country is increasing or decreasing its stock of reserves.\n(i) Increase in Official Reserves (-sign): An increase in reserves, such as purchasing foreign currency,\nrepresents an outflow of domestic currency. This represents a debit in the BoP, as it reflects a use of\ndomestic financial resources. In the context of the BoP, an outflow of resources (like buying foreign\nreserves) is a debit, and debits are traditionally represented with a minus sign.\n575","image":"pages/IMG_0624.jpg"},{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"IMG_0625","text":"Q. 8. State whether the following transactions are autonomous items or accommodating items:\n(i) Purchase of a computer from USA by an Indian resident.\n(ii) Sale of financial assets by RBI to cover BoP deficit.\n(iii) TCS raising loans from a bank in London.\n(iv) Lending activities performed by India.\n(v) Surplus in BoP account used to achieve BoP equilibrium.\n(vi) Liquidation of Forex Reserves by the RBI to finance BoP deficit.\nns. Autonomous Items: (i), (iii, (iv)\nAccommodating Items: (ii), (v), (vi)\n577","image":"pages/IMG_0625.jpg"},{"title":"ABILITY ZONE","page":"IMG_0627","text":"What Causes Disequilibrium in BoP?\nDisequilibrium in BoP is caused by a number of factors, broadly categorised as:\n(i) Economic Factors\n(a) Huge development expenditure by the government owing to which there are large scale imports.\nIt may cause a 'deficit BoP disequilibrium.\n(b) Business cycles in terms of recession, depression, recovery and boom. A period of boom may\nwitness large scale exports of a country. Accordingly, a 'surplus BoP disequilibrium' may occur.\n(c) High rate of inflation in the domestic market, compelling large scale imports of essential goods.\nThis causes 'deficit BoP disequilibrium.\n(d) Development of import substitutes, because of which imports are reduced and surplus BoP may\nemerge in place of deficit BoP.\n(e) Change in the cost structure of trading partners, because of technological and managerial\ninnovations. Favourable change in cost structure would encourage exports and lead to 'surplus\nBoP disequilibrium'.\n(i) Political Factors\n(a) Political instability owing to which inflow of direct foreign investment and portfolio investment.\nfrom abroad may shrink. It causes deficit on capital account BoP.\n(b) Populism policies of the government like huge cuts in import duty. This encourages imports\nbecause of which current account deficit may swell.\n(iil) Social Factors\n(a) Change in tastes and preferences, owing to which pattern of demand (in the international market)\nmay change. A favourable change may encourage exports, while an unfavourable shift may cause\na rise in imports. A rise in exports leads to current account surplus. A rise in imports leads to a\ncurrent account deficit.\n(b) Cross-border prejudices, which sometimes force the countries (like, India and Pakistan) to shit.\neconomy towards the state of deficit BoP disequilibrium.\no expensive sources of imports and less profitable areas of exports. Such situations often drive at\n580\nBalance of Payments\nBalance of Payments (BoP): Accounting Sense and Operational Sense\nLet us understand the concept of balance of payments in 'accounting sense and operational sense\nthrough the following illustration. Carefully examine the data in the given table:\nTable 12.8: Balance of Payments\nAssets\n7 in crore\n(Credits)\nLiabilities\nE in crore\n(Debits)\n1. Export of goods\n550\n1. Import of goods\n800\n2. Export of services (banking, shipping,\ninsurance, tourism, etc.)\n2. Import of services (banking, shipping,\ninsurance, tourism, etc.)\n3. Transfer payments from rest of the\n3. Transfer payments to rest of the world\nworld (gifts, aid, etc.)\n(gifts, aid, etc.)\n4. Capital receipts (loans, sale of assets\n200\n4. Capital payments (loans to foreigners,\nto foreigners, receipt of capital\nbuying of assets from foreigners, payment\nof capital to foreigners)\nTotal Receipts\n1,000\nTotal Payments\n1,000\nThe above table shows that in the accounting sense balance of payments balances, because total\nreceipts (credits) are equal to total payments (debits), i.e., * 1,000 crore. But in the operational sense, the\nanalysis of the table reveals the following facts:\n• Balance of Trade (the difference between the value of total exports and imports) is negative.\nBalance of Trade\n= Export of goods - Import of goods\n= 7 550 crore - 7 800 crore\n= (-) ‹ 250 crore\n• Balance of Payments on Current Account shows a deficit of ? 130 crore.\nBalance of Payments on Current Account\n= Trade Balance (Export of goods - Import of goods) + Invisibles Balance [Balance on\nnon-factor services (banking, shipping, etc.) + Balance on current transfers]\n= RF crore - 7 800 crore) + [R [(R crore - 750 crore) + ® R crore - 7 80 crore)]\n= (-)? 250 crore + ‹ 100 crore + 7 20 crore\n= (-) = 130 crore\n• Balance of Payments on Capital Account shows a surplus of ? 130 crore.\nBalance of Payments on Capital Account\n= Capital Receipts - Capital Payments\n= F 200 crore - ‹ 70 crore\n= · 130 crore\n• Overall Balance of Payments Balances (*1,000 crore of receipts = * 1,000 crore of payments)\nbecause the surplus balance of * 130 crore on capital account compensates deficit balance of\n* 130 crore on current account.\n• From where has the 7 130 crore surplus on capital account emerged?\nIn fact & 130 crore may have been received through foreign loans. In that case, the surplus of\ncapital account actually reflects our indebtedness, not a sound financial status.\n581","image":"pages/IMG_0627.jpg"},{"title":"Power Points [Summary of the Chapter]","page":"IMG_0628","text":"• Balance of Payments is a summary statement of all monetary (or economic) transactions between a\ncountry and rest of the world.\n• Components:\n• Current account\n• Capital account\n• Official reserves account.\n• Economic Transactions in BoP are broadly classified as:\n• merchandise\n• invisibles\n• transactions leading to change in the ownership of assets vis-a-vis rest of the world.\n• Components:\n- Export and import of goods\n• Export and import of services [(a) factor services + (b) non-factor services]\n• Current transfers.\n• Export and import of goods is called 'merchandise' or 'visible trade'.\n• Invisibles include: Non-factor services, Factor income, Current transfers\n• Balance of Trade (or Trade Balance) = Export of merchandise - Import of merchandise\n• Current Account Balance = Trade balance + Invisibles balance.\n• Capital Account records receipts and payments of foreign exchange of a country on account of such\ntransactions which impact asset-liability status of a country in relation to rest of the world.\n• Components:\n› Borrowing\n> Foreign investment\n> NRI deposits\n> Banking capital\n> Short-term debt.\n• Borrowing includes: external commercial borrowing, and external assistance.\n• Investment includes: FIl (Foreign Institutional Investment), and FDI (Foreign Direct Investment).\n• Capital Account Balance shows net capital flows.\n• Positive Balance shows that inward flow of capital is greater than the outward flow.\n• Negative Balance shows that inward flow of capital is less than the outward flow.\n• BoP Equilibrium is a situation when current account balance and capital account balance add up to\nzero, and there is no movement (increase or decrease) in official reserves.\nOr, it is a situation when receipts and payments of a country on account of economic transactions\nwith rest of the world are exactly equal to each other, and there is no movement of official reserves.\n• BoP Deficit occurs when payments of a country on account of economic transactions with rest of\nthe world exceed its receipts and consequently, there is a decrease in official reserves.\n• BoP Surplus occurs when receipts of a country on account of economic transactions with rest of\nthe world exceed its payments and consequently, there is increase in official reserves.\n• Autonomous Items refer to such BoP transactions which are determined by considerations of profit.\nIt is due to these transactions that the BoP deficit/surplus arises. These are called 'above the line\nitems.\n• Accommodating Items refer to such BoP transactions which are not determined by considerations of\nprofit. It is due to these transactions that the BoP deficit/surplus is corrected. These are called below\nthe line items.\n582\nCompeteng\n1. Multiple\n(\nChoose the i\n1. In th\nstate\n(a)\n(b)\n(c)\n2. If 3\nval\n3.\nF\n4.","image":"pages/IMG_0628.jpg"}],"subject":"macro","number":"12"},{"id":"i1","number":"1","subject":"ied","title":"Indian Economy on the Eve of Independence","intro":"","lessons":[{"title":"Before British rule and the colonial transformation","body":"Before British rule: strong handicrafts, especially textiles, alongside a mainly agricultural economy.\n\nColonial policy: turn India into a raw-material supplier and a market for British manufactured goods.\n\nResult: slow growth, low productivity, weak industry and widespread poverty.\n\nThe economy was organised around British needs, which explains the weaknesses in every sector.","formula":"","source":"ied-1-11","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"1.1 Overall Features of Indian Economy","points":["Before British rule: strong handicrafts, especially textiles, alongside a mainly agricultural economy.","Colonial policy: turn India into a raw-material supplier and a market for British manufactured goods.","Result: slow growth, low productivity, weak industry and widespread poverty."],"takeaway":"The economy was organised around British needs, which explains the weaknesses in every sector."},{"title":"Why agriculture stagnated","body":"Exploitative land settlement: high rent and insecure tenure left cultivators with little money or reason to improve land.\n\nLow investment: poor irrigation, old techniques and limited inputs kept yields low.\n\nSmall, fragmented holdings: scattered plots made efficient cultivation difficult.\n\nMost people depended on farming, but dependence is not the same as productivity.","formula":"","source":"ied-1-10","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"1.2 Agricultural Sector on the Eve of Independence","points":["Exploitative land settlement: high rent and insecure tenure left cultivators with little money or reason to improve land.","Low investment: poor irrigation, old techniques and limited inputs kept yields low.","Small, fragmented holdings: scattered plots made efficient cultivation difficult."],"takeaway":"Most people depended on farming, but dependence is not the same as productivity."},{"title":"Commercialisation, subsistence and vulnerability","body":"Subsistence farming: grow mainly for the family’s own use.\n\nCommercial farming: grow mainly for sale, including colonial cash crops such as cotton and jute.\n\nThe problem: pressure to grow cash crops could reduce food cultivation without improving farmers’ incomes.\n\nCommercialisation describes where the crop goes; it does not guarantee that the farmer benefits.","formula":"","source":"ied-1-09","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"1.2 Agricultural Sector · Commercialisation of Agriculture","points":["Subsistence farming: grow mainly for the family’s own use.","Commercial farming: grow mainly for sale, including colonial cash crops such as cotton and jute.","The problem: pressure to grow cash crops could reduce food cultivation without improving farmers’ incomes."],"takeaway":"Commercialisation describes where the crop goes; it does not guarantee that the farmer benefits."},{"title":"Decline of handicrafts: causes and consequences","body":"British machine-made imports competed with Indian handmade goods.\n\nDiscriminatory duties weakened Indian goods in foreign markets while favouring British goods in India.\n\nLoss of royal patronage reduced demand for specialised crafts.\n\nDisplaced artisans moved towards agriculture because modern industry could not absorb them.\n\nDe-industrialisation = handicrafts decline without enough modern industry to replace them.","formula":"","source":"ied-1-08","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"1.3.1 Systematic De-industrialisation","points":["British machine-made imports competed with Indian handmade goods.","Discriminatory duties weakened Indian goods in foreign markets while favouring British goods in India.","Loss of royal patronage reduced demand for specialised crafts.","Displaced artisans moved towards agriculture because modern industry could not absorb them."],"takeaway":"De-industrialisation = handicrafts decline without enough modern industry to replace them."},{"title":"A limited modern industrial base","body":"Cotton mills developed mainly in western India; jute mills were concentrated in Bengal.\n\nIron and steel emerged later, including TISCO in 1907.\n\nCapital-goods industries remained weak, limiting the ability to make machinery.\n\nModern industry grew too slowly to employ displaced artisans.\n\nA few successful industries existed, but the overall industrial base remained narrow.","formula":"","source":"ied-1-07","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"1.3.2 Growth of Modern Industry during 1850–1947","points":["Cotton mills developed mainly in western India; jute mills were concentrated in Bengal.","Iron and steel emerged later, including TISCO in 1907.","Capital-goods industries remained weak, limiting the ability to make machinery.","Modern industry grew too slowly to employ displaced artisans."],"takeaway":"A few successful industries existed, but the overall industrial base remained narrow."},{"title":"Foreign trade and the drain of wealth","body":"Trade pattern: export raw materials; import manufactured goods.\n\nBritish control: trade served Britain’s factories and markets; the Suez Canal strengthened these links.\n\nDrain of wealth: export earnings financed British administration, wars and payments abroad.\n\nExport surplus means exports exceed imports. It helped India only if the resulting resources stayed and supported development.","formula":"","source":"ied-1-07","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"1.4 Foreign Trade on the Eve of Independence","points":["Trade pattern: export raw materials; import manufactured goods.","British control: trade served Britain’s factories and markets; the Suez Canal strengthened these links.","Drain of wealth: export earnings financed British administration, wars and payments abroad."],"takeaway":"Export surplus means exports exceed imports. It helped India only if the resulting resources stayed and supported development."},{"title":"Population conditions and the year 1921","body":"Poor health: high death and infant-mortality rates, low life expectancy, disease and inadequate sanitation.\n\nLow literacy: education reached few people, especially women.\n\n1921: the Year of the Great Divide—population growth became more consistently positive afterwards.\n\nHigh birth and death rates initially kept population growth modest; falling death rates later widened the gap.","formula":"Historical indicators in your textbook: birth rate ≈48 per 1,000; death rate ≈40 per 1,000; infant mortality ≈218 per 1,000 live births; life expectancy ≈32 years; literacy below 16%; female literacy ≈7%.\nInfant mortality concerns deaths below age one per 1,000 live births—not per 1,000 total people.","source":"ied-1-05","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"1.5 Demographic Profile","points":["Poor health: high death and infant-mortality rates, low life expectancy, disease and inadequate sanitation.","Low literacy: education reached few people, especially women.","1921: the Year of the Great Divide—population growth became more consistently positive afterwards."],"takeaway":"High birth and death rates initially kept population growth modest; falling death rates later widened the gap."},{"title":"Occupational imbalance","body":"Occupational structure = how workers are distributed across sectors.\n\nAbout 70–75% worked in the primary sector; industry employed only about 10% in the textbook’s colonial summary.\n\nWeak non-farm opportunities pushed extra workers onto land, lowering average output per worker.\n\nEmployment share tells you where people work; GDP share tells you where output is produced.","formula":"","source":"ied-1-04","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"1.6 Occupational Structure","points":["Occupational structure = how workers are distributed across sectors.","About 70–75% worked in the primary sector; industry employed only about 10% in the textbook’s colonial summary.","Weak non-farm opportunities pushed extra workers onto land, lowering average output per worker."],"takeaway":"Employment share tells you where people work; GDP share tells you where output is produced."},{"title":"Railways and other infrastructure: purpose and effects","body":"Colonial purpose: move raw materials, sell British goods, transport troops and administer India.\n\nInfrastructure built: railways, ports, post and telegraph; rural roads and social facilities remained inadequate.\n\nSide effects: better transport, communication and links between regions.\n\nA balanced answer recognises the benefits while explaining that colonial interests determined the priorities.","formula":"","source":"ied-1-03","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"1.7 Infrastructure · 1.7.1 Positive Impact","points":["Colonial purpose: move raw materials, sell British goods, transport troops and administer India.","Infrastructure built: railways, ports, post and telegraph; rural roads and social facilities remained inadequate.","Side effects: better transport, communication and links between regions."],"takeaway":"A balanced answer recognises the benefits while explaining that colonial interests determined the priorities."}],"pages":[{"id":"ied-1-11","image":"ied-pages/ied-1-11.jpg","text":"indian Economic Development\n1.1 OVERALL FEATURES OF INDIAN ECONOMY\nON THE EVE OF INDEPENDENCE\nWe start with a brief description of (i) Indian economy prior to the British rule, and (it) exploitation\nand transformation of the Indian economy during the British rule. This is to enable the reader to have\na meaningful understanding of the features of the Indian economy on the eve of independence.\n1.1.1 Indian Economy prior to British Rule\n• Prior to the British rule, Indian economy was a vibrant and prosperous economy.\n• On the agricultural front, it was a self-contained rural economy where cuitivators themselves were\nthe owners of land.\n• Industrial sector was famous throughout the world for its\nhandicraft products.\nBefore British Rule\nExport of\nHandicrafts\n• Our exports enjoyed global reputation. The silk and\ncotton textiles were famous for their fine quality and the\nInflux of Gold\nmetal & precious stonework were famous for excellent\nRest of\nthe World\ncraftsmanship.\n• India excelled in the export of handicrafts and the influx of gold in return.\n1.1.2 Exploitation and Transformation of Indian Economy\nduring British Rule\n•The British exploited Indian economy to maximise their\neconomic\ngains. This is popularly known as colonial\nexploitation.\nDuring British Rule\nExport of\nRaw Material\n• Colonial exploitation has two aspects:\nIndia\n(i) Supply aspect, and (ii) Demand aspect.\nImport of\nFinished Goods\nRest of\nthe World\n- On the supply side, India was used as a supplier of raw material to the British industry.\n- On the demand side, India was used as a market for the British goods.\n- Accordingly, Indian handicraft industry faced systematic destruction.\neat a ta destract globat reputation or our exports\nof handicrafts.\n• faricultural sector was yemaily eagle trout it demand of land renie from the\n• Ownership rights of the farmers were withdrawn in case they talled to comply with the\nrising demand of land revenue. The owners of soil were ofte reduced to the status of 'tillers o\n• Thus, Indian economy was completely transformed (or toppled) during the British rule. A vibrant\nand prosperous economy was converted into a backward and stagnant economy.\n1. Vibrant and prosperous economy \" an economy win ng tile or growt and widespread prosper\nIndian Economy on the Eve of independence\n1.1.3 Features of Indian Economy on the Eve of Independence\nConsequent upon its exploitation during the British rule, Indian economy exhibited the following\nfeatures on the eve of independence:\nFeatures of Indian Economy on the Eve of Independence\nStagnant\nEconomy\nBackward\nDominance\nconomy (\nHeavy\nBleak\nindustrialisation\nDependence\non imports\nLimited\nUrbanisation\nSemi-leudal\nEconomy\n(a) Stagnant Economy: On the eve of independence, Indian economy was a completely stagnant\neconomy. A stagnant economy is the one which shows little or no growth in income.\n* The British government never attempted to estimate India's national income? and\nper capita income*. However, Dadabhai Naoroji, William Digby, Findlay Shirras, V.K.R.V. Rao\nand R.C. Desai were some notable individuals who attempted to estimate India's national\nand per capita income.\n• Of course, these estimates yielded conflicting and inconsistent results. But, among them,\nit was V.K.R.V. Rao whose estimates during the colonial period were considered very significant.\n› Most of the estimates reveal that the country's growth of aggregate real output during the\nfirst half of the twentieth century was less than 2 percent and growth in per capita output\nper year was a meagre 0.5 per cent.\n> Standard of living of people was miserably low. Epidemics and famines*\nwere a recurring\n(b) Backward Economy: Indian economy was a backward economy on the eve of independence.\nAn economy is called a backward economy when it has a very low per capita income.\nIn 1947-48, the per capita income in India was just R 230 per annum.\n• The bulk of the population was very poor, without sufficient food, clothing and shelter.\n> Widespread unemployment (caused by the destruction of handicraft industries) was an\nimportant factor contributing to poverty.\n(c) Dominance of Agriculture: Agricultural sector dominated the indian economy as the principal\nsource of livelihood.\n> Nearly 85 per cent of India's population was (directly or indirectly) dependent on agriculture\nfor its livelihood.\n- Nearly 70-75 percent of the working population was engaged in agriculture.\nHowever, despite the dominance of agriculture, famines were a recurring phenomenon in\nrural India. Food shortage and starvation-deaths continued to exist as serious challenges.\nLow productivity (output per acre kept agriculture in a state of backwardness.\n2. National income - money value of all the final goods and services produced in an economy during a given period of\ntime.\nNational income\n3. Per capita income - average income, estimated as:\nTotal population\n4. Famines - severe shortage of food (owing to crop failure) resulting in acute hunger and starvation.","file":"chap1.pdf"},{"id":"ied-1-10","image":"ied-pages/ied-1-10.jpg","text":"inclan Economic Development\n(d) Bleak Industrialisation: Prior to the British rule, industrial sector in India was well-known for\nhandicrafts. But it was systematically destroyed by the discriminatory British policies,\n+ By the time of independence, small-scale and cottage industries were almost ruined.\n- Heavy industry showed a bleak growth, and for the bulk of capital goods requirements, we\nwere dependent upon imports from Britain.\n(e) Heavy Dependence on Imports: The country was heavily dependent upon imports, particularly\nfor machinery and related equipment of production.\n- Armed forces of the country also depended heavily on foreign imports for most of the\ndefence equipment.\n• Besides, several consumer goods like sewing machines, medicines, kerosene oil, bicycles,\netc., used to be imported from abroad.\n(f) Limited Urbanisation: At the time of independence, bulk of the population of India lived in\nvillages. In 1948, only 15 per cent of population lived in urban areas while 85 per cent lived in\n• Rural population lacked opportunities outside agriculture. This compounded their poverty.\n(g) Semi-feudal Economy: On the eve of independence, Indian economy was neither wholly\nfeudal\" nor a capitalist economy®. It was a mixed economy or a semi-feudal economy.\n• Feudalistic mode of production leads to low productivity. Low productivity leads to\nBriefly, we can state that on the eve of independence, Indian economy was characterised with low\nlevel of economic development. It was developed only to the extent it could serve the colonial interest\nof the British government.\nif railways, ports, post and telegraph were developed, this was to promote the process of colonial\nexploitation of the Indian economy. It was directed towards the protection and promotion of the\neconomic interest of the British economy rather than the Indian economy.\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\nQ. 1. State the sole purpose of the British colonial rule in India.\nAns. It was:\ni) to exploit India as a source of raw material for the industrial growth in Britain, and\n(i) to exploit indian market for the industrial goods produced in Britain.\n0.2. At the time of independence, what was the main source of livelihood in India?\nAns. Agriculture.\nQ. 3. State exampies or well-known handicraft industries in India prior to the British period.\nAns. Cotton and silk textiles, metal and precious stonework.\nQ. 4. Enlist the names of individuals who attempted to estimate India's national income prior to\nindependence.\nAns. Dadabhai Naoroji, William Digby, Findlay Shirras, VK.R.V. Rao and R.C. Desal.\n5. Feudal economy - an economy nich means or production are Piney sidel lords (powerful land-owning\n6. Capitalist economy nee if which meater production are owned by the individuals and business house\nand prices are set by the market forces of supply and demand.\n7. Semi-feudal economy - a mixture of feudalistic and capitalistic models of production.\nIndian Economy on the Eve of independence\n1.2 AGRICULTURAL SECTOR ON THE EVE OF INDEPENDENCE\n• On the eve of independence, Indian economy was primarily an agrarian economy.\n• Around 85 percent of the India's population (directly or indirectly) derived its livelihood from the\nagriculture sector which reflects the importance of this sector in the Indian economy. However,\nthis sector suffered from deep stagnation and chronic backwardness.\nThe principal features of the indian agriculture on the eve of independence are described as under.\nThese features highlight the state of agricultural sector on the eve of independence:\nPrincipal Features of Indian Agriculture on the Eve of Independence\n(a)\nLOW\nProduction\nand\nProductivity\n• Total output\nper hectare\nof land was\nloW\n(b)\nExploitative\nLand\nSettlement\nSystem\n* Lamindari\nsystem-\nto extract\nmaximum\nfrom the\nfarmers\n(g)\nForced\nCommercia-\nlisation of\nAgriculture\n• Shift from\nsubsistence\nto commercial\nfarming\nLack of\nInvestment\nin Agriculture\nInfrastructure\n• Old and\noutdated\ntechniques\nof production\nused\nGulf between\nowners and\nTillers of the\nSoil\n* Lack of\ninducement\nto invest\nSmall and\nFragmented\nHoldings\nSubsistence\nOutlook\n• Economically • Conventional\nunviable\nOutlook:\nProduction tor\nself-consumption\n(a) Low Production and Productivity: Production refers to total output, while productivity\nrefers to output per hectare of land.\n* Both (production as well as productivity) were found to be extremely low on the eve of\nindependence. This was because the farmers lacked resources as well as the incentive to\nimprove their farming.\n* Table 1.1 shows production and productivity levels in 1947 for wheat and rice, compared\nwith their levels in 2023-24.\nTable 1.1: Production and Productivity of Wheat and Rice-\nA Comparison between the Levels in 1947 and 2023-24\nProductivity (kg per hectare)\nProduction (in lakh tonnes)\nCrop\n1947\n2023-241\n1947\n2023-24\n1. Wheat\n660\n3,559\n1,133\n2. Rice\n665\n2,882\n220\n1,378\nSource: D. Bhattacharya: Economic History of India: Economic Survey, 2024-25\n• Table 1.1 shows that productivity of wheat was nearly 5.4 times lower in 1947 compared\nwith its level in 2023-24. Productivity of rice was nearly 4.3 times lower in 1947 compared\nwith its level in 2023-24.\n- Likewise, level of output of wheat was nearly 17.7 times lower, and that of rice was nearly\n6.3 times lower in 1947 compared with their levels in 2023-24.\nHowever, it must be mentioned that there was some expansion of the area under cultivation,\nowing to the mounting pressure of population.","file":"chap1.pdf"},{"id":"ied-1-09","image":"ied-pages/ied-1-09.jpg","text":"Indian Economic Development\n(b) Exploitative Land Settlement System: The British government in india introduced a unique\nsystem of land settlement. It set-up a triangular relationship among the government, the\nowner of the soil and the tiller of the soil. This was popularly known as zamindari system\"\nLand Settlement and was particularly implemented in the then Bengal Presidency\n(comprising parts of India's present-day eastern states). The distinct features of this system\nwere as these:\n(i) The zamindars were recognised as permanent owners of the soil,\nwhich implied that the profits generated from the cultivation were\nto accrue to zamindars instead of the tillers.\n(ii) The zamindars were to pay a fixed sum to the government as\nBritish\ngovernment\nland revenue.\n(iii) The zamindars were free to extract as much from the\nLamingars:\n(Owners of the soil!\ntillers of the soil as they could. In fact, the only interest\nof the zamindars was to maximise revenue collection,\nignoring the plight of the tillers living in poverty.\nPeasants and Labourers\nfullers of the soil)\nThe zamindars did nothing to improve the condition\nof agriculture.\nThe implications of the land settlement system were alarming for the farmers and the farming.\nMost importantly:\n• It led to unlimited exploitation of the tillers of the soil by the zamindars.\n* Rates of land revenue were frequently raised by the zamindars which led to frequent\neviction of the tillers of the soil.\n- Tillers were reduced to the status of landless labourers, while a loyal class of zamindars (as\nowners of the soil) was created.\n* Deprived of ownership rights as well as any surplus on land, the farmers of the soil were\ndriven to extreme poverty (and indebtedness), while farming faced stagnation.\n(c) Forced Commercialisation of Agriculture: Commercialisation of agriculture refers to a shift\nfrom subsistence cultivation to cultivation of cash crops for the market. Farmers were forced\nto grow commercial crops to cope with the needs of the British industry.\nFollowing are some notable points in this context:\n* Farmers were forced to shift to produce indigo, as it was require! My the textile industry\nin Britain Tor ayeing beaching of the textile. The farmerry ere either lured or forced to\naccept advance payments for the cultivation of indigo.\n- Production of commercial crops exposea ine subsistence farmers to uncertainties of the\nmarket for their own subsistence.\n*Switching Tom suaster Coops raised creat needs of the farmers which led to\ntheir perpetual indebtedness.\n9. Subsistence cultivation - cultivation for self-consumption.\nLamindan system a systemat waka unique demen called zamindars an intermediar\nbetween the colonial government and the peasants.\nindian Economy on the Eve of independence\nHIGHLIGHTS\nFamines in India During the British Rule\n• From 1760 till 1943, India was hit by terrible famines on a regular basis.\n• The most significant amongst those was the great famine of Bengal of 1769-70, which claimed a large\nToll of lives.\n• More than 85 million people died owing to repeated famines.\n• In contrast, there have been no famine related deaths since independence.\nPartition of the Country and its Impact\n• The partition of the country had a negative impact on Indian agriculture.\n• India got 82 percent of population and 65 per cent of food grain area.\n• Rich food producing areas of West Punjab and Sindh went to Pakistan.\n• This aggravated the food crisis in the country.\n(d) Lack of Investment in Agriculture Infrastructure: The colonial government or zamindars took\nno interest in promoting investment in agricultural infrastructure?.\n• Permanent means of irrigation were a far cry.\n* Agriculture was heavily dependant on rainfall and therefore continued to be uncertain.\n> Investment in terracing, flood-control, drainage and desalinisation of the soil which could\nhave led to increased productivity of the sector, were totally neglected.\n(e) Gulf between Owners of the Soil and Tillers of the Soil: Agriculture, during the British Raj\nwas characterised by a huge gulf between 'owners of the soil' on the one hand and 'tillers of\n> While the owners shared the output, they seldom (hardly) shared the cost of production.\nThey were merely interested in maximising their rental income (in terms of share of output).\n› The tillers of the soil were merely given enough for subsistence.\n• Thus, the tillers of the soil did not have resources to invest while the owners had no\ninterest.\n› 'Stagnation and backwardness' of agriculture was the obvious consequence.\n! HIGHLIGHTS\nIndebtedness of Tillers of the Soil and Conspicuous Consumption of Owners of the Soil\n• During the British rule, while tillers of the soil confronted absolute poverty and accepted indebtedness\nas their way of life, the owners of the soil indulged in conspicuous consumption.\n• The tillers of the soil viewed agriculture merely as a source of subsistence. The owners of the soil\nviewed it as a hereditary source of income without investment.\n(f) Small and Fragmented Holdings: Landholdings were both small as well as fragmented\".\nAccordingly, most landholdings were uneconomic: yielding low output at high cost.\n10. Agricultural infrastructure - diverse systems and networks of resources that are vital to produce, procure, preserve,\nprocess and trade agricultural products.\n11. Fragmented holdings - small and scattered pieces of land holdings.","file":"chap1.pdf"},{"id":"ied-1-08","image":"ied-pages/ied-1-08.jpg","text":"Indian Economic Development\n(g) Subsistence Outlook: Farming was taken mostly as a means of subsistence.\n• Subsistence farming is a form of farming in which the crops are produced to provide for the\nbasic needs of the family.\nAccordingly, backwardness prevailed and poverty dominated.\nBriefly, on the eve of independence, Indian agriculture was both backward as well as stagnant\nCharacteristics of Agriculture on the Eve of Independence\nStagnant\n• Exploitative Land Settlement System\nunder the British Raj\n• Forced commercialisation of agriculture\n- Gulf between owners of the soil and\ntillers of the soil\nBackward\n• Low production and low productivity\n• Lack of investment in agriculture\nintrastructure\n•Small and tragmented holdings\n• Subsistence outlook\n: HIGHLIGHTS\nPre-British Period Agriculture\n• Backward, stagnant and non-vibrant agriculture during\nthe British Raj sharply contrasted with the pre-British\nperiod agriculture.\n• Prior to the British Raj in India, rural India was described\nas a system of self-contained village communities. These\nvillage communities included farmers and the functionaries.\n• The farmers were engaged in crop farming or cattle\nfarming while the functionaries would render essential\nservices like that of blacksmiths, goldsmiths, washermen\nand shoe-makers.\nProsperous Agriculture even without\nMechanisation (Pre-British Period)\n• There were no intermediaries (like zamindars) between the state and the farmers; the farmers would\npay land revenue directly to the king.\n• Prospery are were be key aractersues one acon is, the French traveller,\nBernier, described co as richerthan Egypt producing amply for self-consumption\nand exporting in abundance.\nAVLR 1.1\nSTATE OF AGRICULTURAL SECTOR ON THE EVE OF INDEPENDENCE\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\nQ. 5. Enlist any two causes of low agricultural productivity under the British rule.\nAns.\n(1) Exploitative land settlement system.\nwas appropriated by the zamindars and wasted on conspicuous consumpaon).\nIndian economy on the eve of independence\nQ. 6. State key areas where there was a lack of investment in indian agriculture during the British\nAnS.\n(i) Irrigation, which left agriculture dependent on rainfall.\n(ii) Farm-technology, which left farmers dependent on primitive techniques of farming.\n(iii) Land improvement, which neglected terracing (cutting steep areas into flatter sections).\ndrainage (avoiding run-off) and soil conservation (ensuring soil health).\nQ. 7. State the principal difference between owners of the soil and tillers of the soil during the\nBritish regime in India.\nOwners of the soil were the zamindars who were granted ownership rights by the British, and\ntillers of the soil were landless peasants and labourers.\n1.3 INDUSTRIAL SECTOR ON THE EVE OF INDEPENDENCE\n1.3.1 Systematic De-industrialisation during early years of British\nRule: Decay of Indian Handicrafts\n• India suffered systematic de-industrialisation during the early years of British rule. There was a\ncomplete decay of Indian handicrafts which were the backbone of the Indian economy prior to\nthe British rule.\n• The decay happened not due to some accidental historical events or circumstances. It happened\nbecause of the colonial policy of the British government focusing on exploitation of the Indian\n• During the colonial era in India, there was industrial revolution in Great Britain. Expansion of\nindustry in Britain needed expansion of the Indian colonial market for the British goods. Accordingly,\nde-industrialisation in India was systematically planned.\n• In fact, de-industrialisation (by way of decay of Indian handicrafts) was the principal motive of\nthe British government in India. It was expected to cause a shift in domestic demand from indian\ngoods to the goods produced in Britain.\n• The motive behind the systematic de-industrialisation during the British rule was two-fold.\nTwo-fold Motive behind Systematic De-industrialisation in India\n1. To reduce India to the status or a mere\nexporter of raw material for the\nupcoming modern industries in Britain\n2. To turn india into an emerging\nmarket for the industralisation\nal Butain:\nIndian Handicrafts of Worldwide Reputation prior to British Rule\n12. Systematic de-industrialisation -term that describes the decline of India's of industrial sector during the British rule.\n11","file":"chap1.pdf"},{"id":"ied-1-07","image":"ied-pages/ied-1-07.jpg","text":"Indian Economic Development\nFactors Contributing to the Decay of Indian Handicrafts\nFollowing are some important factors which explain the decay of handicrafts in India:\n(a) Discriminatory Tariff Policy: The British government pursued a discriminatory tarm policy.\nIt allowed:\n(i) tariff-free import of British goods in India. So that the British goods gained\nprice-competitiveness in the indian markets.\n(i) heavy export-duty on the export of Indian handicraft- goods to rest of the world. So that,\nthe Indian goods lost price-competitiveness in the international market.\n(il) duty-free export of industrial raw material from India to Britain. So that, the British\ngoods gained cost competitiveness in the indian market.\n(b) Expansion of Railways: Railway network was expanded in india so that the British goods\ncould reach Indian markets across all parts of the country.\n(c) Low Cost of British Goods: Because of large-scale production, British goods were produced at\nlow cost. They were cheaper compared to handmade Indian goods. Accordingly, Indian goods\nlost market to the British goods.\n(d) Disintegration/Disappearance of Indian Kingdoms: During the British rule, Indian kingdoms\nstarted disappearing. Kings and Nawabs, who used to provide patronage (protection) to the\nIndian handicrafts, gradually lost their foothold. Hence, the decline of Indian handicrafts.\n(e) Loss of Skilled Artisans and Lack of Capital for Investment: Due to its low competitiveness,\nhandicraft industry became economically unviable. Availability of skilled artisans was reduced,\nand surplus (capital) for investment started shrinking. This accelerated the pace of decay of\nIndian handicrafts.\n! HIGHLIGHTS\nPrincipal Factors Contributing to the Decay of Indian Handicrafts\n• Discriminatory tariff policy of the British goods.\n• Lack of cost competitiveness of the Indian goods compared to industrial goods of Britain.\n• Expansion of railways which led to expansion of domestic market for the low-cost British goods.\nTextile Industry in Bengal\n• Prior to British rule, the textile industry of india wan anous worldwide. Deccai Muslin, found in\nfinest type of cotton textile.\nBengal, in places near Dhaka (present capital of Bangladesh) was tamous all over the world as the\n• Malmar was the nest variety or musin and was prim. by royaity giving it the name of malma\nshahi or malmal khas.\n1.3.2 policy. significant) Growth of Modern Industry during 1850-1947\nUnder the British Raj, modern industry saw only a policy, th. It was only in second half of the\n19th century that the modern industry showed its emergence.\nFive observations need to be noted in this regard:\n(i) Initially, cotton and Jute textile mills were ser-up. COltye textile mills were located in westert\n12\nIndia, mainly in Manarasntra and Gujarat and were fry controlled by the Indians. Jut\ntextile industries were concentrated in bengal and of thany controled by the foreigners\nIndian Economy on the Eve of Independence\n(ii) In the beginning of twentieth century, iron and steel industry came up and Tata Iron and Steel\nCompany (TISCO) was incorporated in 1907. After World War II, cement, paper and sugar\nindustries also started coming up.\n(iii) 'Capital goods industry', producing machines and industrial plants (to further propel the\nprocess of industrialisation) showed hardly any emergence. Accordingly, industrialisation\nfailed to gain momentum during the British rule.\n(iv) The contribution of the new industrial sector in the Gross Value Added (GVA) or Gross\nDomestic Product of the economy remained extremely low.\n(v) The state participation in the process of modern industrialisation was limited. It was confined\nonly to the strategic areas (like railways, power generation, means of communication, ports\nand some other departmental undertakings) which helped expansion of the Indian market for\nthe British products.\nJAVLR 1.2\nSTATE OF INDUSTRIAL SECTOR ON THE EVE OF INDEPENDENCE\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\nQ. 8. State the motive behind the systematic de-industrialisation during the British Rule in India.\nAns. The motive behind the systematic de-industrialisation during the British rule in India was two-fold:\n(i) To exploit India's wealth of raw material and primary products (like cotton and jute).\nIt was required to fulfil the emerging needs of industrial inputs in the wake of the industrial\nrevolution in Britain.\n(ii) To exploit India as a potential market for the industrial products of Britain.\nQ. 9. What was the impact of systemic de-industrialisation in pre-independent India?\nAns. The systemic de-industrialisation led to:\n(i) Decline of the handicraft industries and the consequent massive unemployment.\n(ii) The penetration of British goods in the Indian markets.\nQ. 10. Name some key industries that came up in India after the Second World War.\nAns. Sugar, cement and paper.\nQ. 11. State a significant drawback of the new industrial set-up in India under the British rule.\nDormant (slow) growth of capital-good industry.\n1.4 FOREIGN TRADE ON THE EVE OF INDEPENDENCE\n• India had acquired eminence in the area of foreign trade*\n, since ancient times.\n• The Romans used to call India, \"the sink of world's bullion\". But the British rule in India brought an\nend to it.\n• Prior to the British rule, India was a well-known exporter of finished goods (such as fine cotton,\nsilk, textiles, iron goods, wooden goods, ivory work and precious stones).\n13. Gross Value Added (GVA) - total value of goods and services produced by all sectors of an economy.\n14. Foreign trade the export and import of goods and services between two or more nations in the international market.\n13","file":"chap1.pdf"},{"id":"ied-1-06","image":"ied-pages/ied-1-06.jpg","text":"Indian Economic Development\n• But the Britsh converted India into a net exporter of raw material and importer of Tinished goods.\nIt was all due to discriminatory policy of trade and tariff pursued by the British government.\nState of India's foreign trade at the time of independence can be described terms Of the Tong\nobservations:\nState of Foreign Trade\nNet Exporter of Raw Material\nMonopoly Control of India's\nSurplus Trade but only to\nBenetit the British\nand importer of Finished Goods\nForeign Trade\n(a) Net Exporter of Raw Material and Importer of Finished Goods: Due to exploitative colonial\npolicies of trade and tariff, India became net exporter of raw materials and primary products\n(like raw silk, cotton, wool, jute, indigo, sugar, etc.).\n> At the same time, India became net importer of finished goods produced by the British\nindustry. Our imports of finished consumer goods included cotton, silk and woollen clothes,\nbesides several types of capital goods like machinery produced in England.\n* Composition of exports and imports reflected utter backwardness of the Indian economy.\n(b) Monopoly Control of India's Foreign Trade: During the British rule, exports and imports of\nthe country came under monopoly control of the British government. In this context, following\nobservations are of critical significance:\n- More than 50 percent of India's foreign trade was directed towards Great Britain. The rest\nof the trade was directed towards China, Ceylon (Sri Lanka) and Persia (Iran).\n* The control of Britain over India's foreign trade intensified (increased) after the opening of\nSuez Canal in 1869.\n* While exports of primary products (raw material) from India supplied inputs to the British\nindustry, imports of finished goods from Britain provided a huge market to the British industry.\nHIGHLIGHTS\nSuez Canal and access to Indian Markets\n• Suez Canal is an artificial waterway running from north to south across Isthmus of Suez in north-eastern\nEgypt.\n• The canal provides a direct trade route between curopean or As au and ports located in South\nAsia, East Africa and Oceania by removing the need to sail around Africa.\n• Opening orsuez Canal in 1009 Signicanty reduced the cost of transportation of goods between Britain\n•A significant reds automoted monopoly control of indias foreign trade by the\nBritish government.\n(c) Surplus Trade but only to Benefit the British: Surprisingly, during the British regime, our\nexports exceeded our imports. It implied a surplus of balance of trade.\n*But, this surplus was owing lagery to the export entary goods not the industria\ngoods) which is a sign of economic backwardness.\n15. Finished goods- goods that have crossed the production boundary and are available fe\n14\nIndian Economy on the Eve of Independence\n* Despite the surplus trade, supplies of several essential commodities like food grains, and\nclothes remained deficient in the domestic market.\n- Unlike during the pre-British period, trade surplus did not lead to the influx of bullion into\nthe domestic economy. Instead, it was splurged (wastefully spent) to meet:\n(i) administrative expenses of the British government in India,\n(i) expenses of wars fought by the British government, and\n(iii) the import of invisible items.\n> Administrative and war expenses, along with the import of \"invisibles' led to the drain of\nwealth from India. It compounded the problem of backwardness and stagnation of the\nIndian economy-\nHIGHLIGHTS\nDrain of India's Wealth\n• Huge administrative expenses were incurred by the British government to manage their colonial rule in India.\n• Also, huge expenses were incurred by the British government to fight wars in pursuit of their policy of\nimperialism and for import of invisible items.\n• All these expenses were borne by the Indian Exchequer (government's treasury, or the place where\ngovernment money goes.\n• This implied a drain of India's wealth.\nRAVLR 1.3\nSTATE OF INDIA'S FOREIGN TRADE ON THE EVE OF INDEPENDENCE\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\nQ. 12. What was the most important characteristic of India's foreign trade throughout the colonial\nperiod?\nAns.\nGeneration of a large export surplus. But, unlike during the pre-British period, this surplus did\nnot lead to the influx of bullion into the domestic economy. Instead, it was wastefully used by\nthe British Government (to meet administrative and war expenses) which led to drain of wealth\nfrom India.\nQ. 13. State some trade related characteristics of Indian economy at the time of independence.\nAns. Some of the trade related characteristics of Indian economy at the time of independence are as\nfollow:\n(i) Colonial policy of the British government resulted in a monopoly control of India's foreign trade.\n(i) Exports and imports were largely restricted to be between India and Britain.\n(iii) India's exports provided raw material to the British industry, while India's imports provided\na huge market for the British industry.\n(iv) Surplus generated as balance of trade was only spent to meet administrative and war\nexpenses by the British government in India. These expenses led to a huge drain of wealth\nas it was not used for investment. Consequently, poverty and backwardness were elevated.\nAll these are typical characteristics of a backward economy.\n15","file":"chap1.pdf"},{"id":"ied-1-05","image":"ied-pages/ied-1-05.jpg","text":"Indian Economic Development\n1.5 DEMOGRAPHIC PROFILE™\n• Demographic conditions during the British rule exibited areatares of a stagnant and backward\neconomy.\nHere, we focus on the following parameters:\n(a) Birth Rate and eath hate: Both birth rate (k) and death fate tOk were very high-nearly\n48 and 40 per thousand respectively. High BR and high DR suggest a state of massive povert\nin the country.\ninfant mortality rate was very high. It was about 218 per thousand, while at present, it is\n28 per thousand. High infant mortality rate is a sign of poor healthcare connected with\nextreme poverty.\nHIGHLIGHTS\nPopulation Census\n• Population census in India is a detailed estimation of population size, along with a complete demographic\nprofile of the country.\n• It was first conducted under the British rule in 1881. Since then it is conducted after every ten years.\n(c) Life Expectancy: Life expectancy\nwas as low as 32 years, while presently it is 69 years.\nLow life expectancy reflects lack of healthcare facilities, lack of awareness as well as lack of\nmeans to avail them.\nHIGHLIGHTS\n• High mortality rate and low life expectancy are important social indicators of backwardness and poverty\nof the masses.\n• During the British rule, frequent famines and recurring epidemics were the prime causes behind a high\nmortality rate.\n• While famines occurred largely due to droughts, epidemics occurred due to the neglect of public health\nservices.\n(d) Literacy Rate: Literacy rate was less than 16 per cent, pointing to social backwardness as\na reflection of economic backwardness. Out of this, the female literacy rate was just about\n7 per cent. This indicated gender-bias in the society.\n16. Demographic profile - statistical description of population size and structure as driven by birth rate, death rate and\nmigration.\n17. Birth rate - number of live births per 1,000 people in a year.\n18. Death rate - number of people dying per 1,000 people in a year.\n19. Infant mortality rate - number of deaths of children below the age of one year per 1,000 live births.\n20. Life expectancy - a statistical measure that estimates the number of years an individual is expected to live.\n21. Literacy rate - the proportion of individuals of a particular age group who have the ability to rear and write.\n16\nIndian Economy on the Eve of Independence\n! HIGHLIGHTS\nDemographic Transition\nFollowing are some notable points relating to demographic transition in India:\n(i) In the history of demographic transition, 1921 is regarded as the 'Year of Great Divide'.\n(ii) Till 1921, population growth in india was never consistent. Size of population kept fluctuating.\nincreasing in one census and decreasing in the other. It is described as the first stage of demographic\ntransition.\n(iii) The census 1901 showed a decline of 0.04 crore in total population (from 23.87 crore in 1891 to\n23.83 crore in 1901).\n(iv) The census 1911 showed a rise of 1.38 crore (from 23.83 crore in 1901 to 25.21 crore in 1911).\n(v) The census 1921 showed a decline of 0.07 crore (from 25.21 crore in 1911 to 25.14 crore in 1921).\n(vi) After 1921, India entered the second stage of demographic transition when population recorded a\nconsistent rise.\n(vii) It is after this year that the total population in India never declined; it recorded a consistent rise: the\ncensus 1931 recorded a rise of 2.76 crore; the census 1941 recorded a rise of 3.96 crore; the census\n1951 recorded a rise of 4.24 crore, and so on.\n(vili) A consistently rising population (on the eve of independence) led to excessive burden of maintenance\ninvestment. It is an expenditure which a country has to incur on the maintenance of the existing\npopulation.\n(ix) When maintenance investment is high, investment for GDP growth remains low.\n(x) High maintenance and low investment for GDP growth is another parameter of the backwardness\nand stagnation of the Indian economy.\n(xi) However, the underlying fact is that till 1951, the rise in India's population was never alarming;\nit ranged between mild to modest. It was because both birth rate and death rate were high and the\ngulf between two was small.\n(xii) It was only from 1951 onwards that the rise in population became explosive in nature, and the\ncountry started tacing a serious challenge in terms of population explosion. Because, whereas birth\nrate continued to be high, death rate recorded a significant decline.\nSTAVLR 1.4\nINDIA'S DEMOGRAPHIC PROFILE ON THE EVE OF INDEPENDENCE\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\nQ. 14. In the history of demographic transition in India which year is considered as the 'Year of Great\nDivide'?\nAns. 1921.\nQ. 15. What does high infant mortality rate and death rate signify about a country?\nAns. (i) Poor healthcare, (ii) Extreme poverty.\n17","file":"chap1.pdf"},{"id":"ied-1-04","image":"ied-pages/ied-1-04.jpg","text":"Indian economic Development\n1.6 OCCUPATIONAL STRUCTURE\ncation structre ret stripton of works population across Primary, sectuary\nand ternary sectors of the economy.\n• Table 1.2 shows occupational structure of Indian economy at the time of independence. The data\nrelates to 1951, because reliable statistics for the year 1947 are not available.\nTable 1.2: Occupational Structure of India at the Time of Independence\n1951 (in %)\nOccupation\n70-75\nAgniculture\n10\nIndustry\n15-20\nServices\ni Table 1.2 offers the following observations:\n(a) Agriculture-The Principal Source of Occupation: On the eve of independence, about\n70-75 percent of working population was engaged in primary sector with agriculture as its\nprincipal component.\n* As per recent estimates, percentage of population dependent on agriculture is much less\nin advanced countries of the world. For instance, in England around 2 per cent and in USA\nless than 2 percent, in Japan around 3 percent and in Germany around 1 percent of the\npopulation depends on agriculture.\n• This establishes backwardness of the Indian economy at the time of independence.\nHIGHLIGHTS\nAgriculture as a Means of Subsistence\n• Greater dependence on agriculture (as suggested by occupational structure on the eve of independence)\nimplied lesser availability of land per head of the farming population.\n• Accordingly, agriculture was taken largely as a means of subsistence, and less as an occupation for profit.\n(b) Industry-An Insignificant Source of Occupation: On the eve of independence, barely\n10.0 per cent of the working population in India was engaged in industry with manufacturing\nas its principal component.\n* As against it, 32 per cent in the USA, 42 percent in England and 39 per cent in Japan was\nengaged in the industrial sector.\n* It further proves how backward the indian economy was at the time of independence.\n(c) Insignificant Transition from Agriculture to Industry: High percentage of working population\nwas engaged in agriculture and very low in the industrial sector in 1951 (as reflected in\nTable 12). It points to the fact that during the Britissau transition from agriculture\nto industry has been quite grincant it may be noted in this context that an economy\nesperences take ol ony wen there is as shinicant transition of worktorce rom agricuiture\nto inct arget e conclusion that during the British period, indian economy was far\naway from the point of take-off; instead it continas fo be backward and stagnant.\n* There were some green snoots in the parts of Madras Presidency (comprising areas of\npresent-day Tamil Nadu, Andra Prades, Kerala and Karnataka), Bombay and Bengal\ndecline in dependence on agricultural sector.\n18\nIndian Economy on the Eve of Independence\n* But, at the same time, Odisha, Rajasthan and Punjab recorded an increase in workforce\nengaged in agricultural sector.\n* On the whole, occupational structure of India (on the eve of independence) pointed to\nbackwardness and lopsidedness of the Indian economy.\nAVLR 1.5\nOCCUPATIONAL STRUCTURE DURING THE BRITISH RULE IN INDIA\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\nQ. 16. Industrial sector being an insignificant source of occupation in india on the eve of independence\nclassified India as what type of an economy?\nAns. Backward economy.\nQ. 17. Transition of workforce from agriculture to industry which was seen in parts of Madras\nPresidency (during the British rule) hinted towards which economic situation?\nAns. Economic 'take-off'.\nQ. 18. Highlight some points on occupational structure of India at the time of independence.\nAns. Some important points on occupational structure of India are as under:\n(i) The bulk of working population was engaged in agricultural sector.\n(ii) The transition from agriculture to industry was insignificant or almost negligible.\n(iii) Heavy dependence on agriculture along with the fact that farming was largely driven by\nthe subsistence motive (rather than any commercial motive points to backwardness of\nthe Indian economy at the time of independence.\n(iv) Rampant poverty and occupational structure were closely related to each other.\n1.7 INFRASTRUCTURE\n• Infrastructure? refers to the elements of\n(i) economic change (like roads, railways, ports, means of transport, communication, banking,\npower/energy), and\n(ii) social change (like growth of educational, health and housing facilities), which serve as a\nfoundation for growth and development of a country.\nElements of infrastructural change during the British rule\nmay be described as under:\n• Railways were introduced in India in 1850 and were\ndeveloped to transport finished goods from Britain to the\ninteriors of the colonial India (with a view to widening the\nsize of the market).\n-It aimed at widening the size of the market for the\nFirst Train run from Bombay (Mumbai)\nto Thane (1853)\nBritish products in India.\n22. Infrastructure - support system required for the growth and development of the economy.\n19","file":"chap1.pdf"},{"id":"ied-1-03","image":"ied-pages/ied-1-03.jpg","text":"Indian Economic Development\n- However, introduction of railways is considered one of the most impon i tributions of the\nBritish in India.\n• Ports were developed to handle export of raw material to Britain and import of finished goods from\nBritain.\n- The development of inland waterways proved uneconomical.\n- For example, coast canal on the Odisha (erstwhile Orissa) Coast was built at a huge cost, but\nfailed to compete with railways and was ultimately abandoned.\n• Post and telegraphs were developed to enhance administrative efficiency. Doubtless, electric\ntelegraphs served the purpose of maintaining law and order. The postal services, on the other\nhand, despite serving a useful public purpose remained consistently inadequate.\n• Roads were developed to facilitate the mobilisation of the army and transportation of raw material\nfrom different parts of the country to the ports.\n- There were no efforts made to develop all-weather roads to reach out to rural areas during the\nrainy season. As a result, people living in rural areas suffered at the time of natural calamities\nand famines.\nBriefly, some modest infrastructural change in the economy during the British Raj is not denied. But,\nthe motive behind this change was not the growth and development of the Indian economy; rather\nit was to facilitate colonial exploitation of the Indian economy for the growth and development of\nthe British economy. Consequently, Indian economy remained backward, even when it experienced\nsome infrastructural change.\n1.7.1 Was there any Positive Impact of the British Rule in India?\nCertainly not, if the impact of the British rule is studied with reference to 'motive' of the British\ngovernment in India. The motive was clear and focused: it was colonial exploitation of the Indian\neconomy. However, the means to achieve the end yielded some positive side-effects. These are as\ngiven below:\nPositive Impact of British Rule\n(e)\nCommercial\nNew\nControl of\nMonetary\nOutlook of\nOpportunities\nFamines\nSystem of\nEfficient\nthe Farmers\nof Employment\nExchange\nSystem of\nAdministration\n(a) Commercial Outlook of the rarmers: Forced commercialisation of agriculture under the\nBritish rule exposed the subsistence farmers to uncertawties of the market. True, but it also\nled to a grew ca te tres. ne farmers started considering marke\nprice of the produce as an important determinant of their production decision\n(b) New Opportunities of Employment spren (g) \"ways and roadways opened up new\n(c) Control of Famines: Rapid means of transport facilitated rapid movement of food grain to the\nopportunities of economic and social growth.\nfamine-affected areas. Accordingly, famines were controlled.\n20\nIndian Economy on the Eve of Independence\n(d) Monetary System of Exchange: There was a transition from barter system of exchange to\nmonetary system of exchange. Growth of monetary system of exchange facilitated division of\nlabour, specialisation, and large-scale production.\n(e) Efficient System of Administration: The British government in India left a legacy of an efficient\nsystem of administration. This served as a ready-reference for our politicians and planners.\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\nQ. 19. What was the main motive behind the development of infrastructure in India during the\nBritish rule?\n(i) Exploiting the Indian economy via widening of the size of market for British goods in India\nand procuring raw material from all routes.\n(ii) Facilitating administration.\nQ. 20. How railways reduced the impact of famines during the colonial period?\nAns. Railways facilitated the movement of food grains from the surplus zones or from the government\nwarehouses (godowns) to the areas afflicted with famines. Accordingly, the impact of famines\nwas reduced.\n• HIGHLIGHTS\nImpact of Railways in India\n• Positive Impact\n(i) Railways facilitated expansion of the domestic market.\nAccordingly, exports and imports of the country showed\na significant rise.\n(ii) Railways facilitated commercialisation of agriculture,\nas goods could then be moved to distant places.\nThis implied a modest change in the outlook of the\nfarmers. They started viewing farming as a business,\nrather than merely as a source of subsistence.\n(III) Railways enabled people to break the barriers of\nRailways in the British Rule\ndistance and undertake journeys to far off places.\nThis promoted cultural affinity among the countrymen.\n(iv) Faster movement of food grain across different parts of the country (owing to Railways) helped\ncontrol the spread of famines. Food supplies could reach the people before they were driven to\n• Negative Impact\n(i) Railways contributed to colonial exploitation of the Indian economy. Because, primary goods (raw\nmaterial) could then be easily transported from the fields and farms to the ports for the purpose of\nexports to the British economy.\n(ii) Finished goods coming as imports to the Indian economy could be easily transported to the interiors\nof the country for purpose of sale.\nThus, the spread of railways led to the spread of the domestic market for the British products.\n23. Barter system of exchange -exchange of goods or services between the two parties without exchanging money.\n21","file":"chap1.pdf"},{"id":"ied-1-02","image":"ied-pages/ied-1-02.jpg","text":"indian Economic Development\nABILITY ZONE\nColonial Exploitation of the indian Economy Under the British Rule\n• Indian economy under the British rule was subjected to colonial exploitation. It implied a targeted\nexploitation of all sectors of the economy by the British government. This is how it happened:\n(1) Colonial Exploitation of Agricultural Sector: Agriculture was exploited through zamindari system\nof land revenue.\nFollowing observations highlight how this system led to exploitation of the indian agriculture:\n- Zamindars were declared as owners of the soil. They were to pay a fixed sum to the government by\nway of land revenue, and were free to extract as much as they wished (or as much as they could\nfrom the tillers of the soil (farmers).\n- The tillers of the soil (the actual cultivators) got bare minimum for survival. They were left with no\nsurplus for investment in agricuiture.\n- The zamindars, on the other hand, spent their revenue income on the luxuries of life. Little or no\ninvestment was made for the development of agriculture.\n(il) Colonial Exploitation of Industrial Sector: Prior to the British rule, industrial sector in India was\nwell known for its handicrafts.\n- Indian handicrafts (work produced by hand labour) enjoyed a world-wide reputation for their\nvariety and quality. But these were destroyed by the British government in two ways:\n- Foreign demand for the Indian handicrafts was destroyed by way of heavy duty on their exports.\n- Domestic demand for the Indian handicrafts was destroyed by way of duty-free import of the\nBritish goods in the indian markets.\n(iii) Colonial Exploitation of International Trade: India's international trade was exploited through\ndiscriminatory tariff (a tax on imports) policy. It implied:\n- Duty-free export of Indian raw material to fulfil the industrial needs in Britain.\n- Duty-free import of British goods to expand demand for the British goods in the Indian markets.\nIt was owing to discriminatory tariff policy that India became importer of finished goods from Britain,\nand exporter of raw material to Britain.\n• Briefly, owing to its colonial exploitation, the Indian economy became both stagnant as well as backward.\nReversal of Growth Process Under the British Raj\n• Prior to the Colonial Rule in india, Indian economy was a vibrant economy. Doubtless, the agricultural sector\nwas the main source of livelihood. But there was an all round prosperity across all sectors of the economy.\n• Agriculture was subsistence based, but the cultivators enjoyed the ownership rights and were to pay\na reasonable amount of land revenue directly to the king. There were no middlemen, and therefore, no\nexploitation.\n• Industry was dominated by handicrafts, but it enjoyed worldwide reputation of producing quality products\nlike \"Deccai Muslin'.\n= Exports consisted largely of finished products. Imports were largely of bullion (gold and silver).\n• Colonial policies of the British governd imp armost reversed the composition, or structure of output, as\nwell as the composition of exports and imports. Also, the pace of growth was severely hit.\n• No Systematic official attempts were ever made of estimating per capita income (indicating per-hear\navailability of goods and services in the economy).\n•Howeve sore naividuals made So nodes attempts to measure per capita income during the British\nperiod. These individuals included V.K.R.V. Rao, Dadabhai Naorojs/indlay Shirras, William Digby ant\n- Rao's study was most comprenensive and systematic. He found that during the 1st half of the\n20th century, while national income showed a modest growth of about 2 per cent, per capita income\nshowed a dismal growth of about 0.5 per cent.\nIndian Economy on the Eve of Independence\nPower Points [Summary of the Chapter]\nOverall Features of indian Economy on the Eve of Independence:\n(1) Stagnant economy\n(fi) Backward economy\n(iii) Dominance of agriculture\n(IV) Bleak industrialisation\n(V) Heavy dependence on imports\n(vi) Limited urbanisation\n(Vil) Semi-teudal economy\nState of Agriculture: Indian agriculture under the British rule showed these characteristics:\n(i) Low production and productivity\n(il) Exploitative land settlement system\n(iii) Forced commercialisation of agriculture\n(iv) Lack of investment in the agriculture infrastructure\n(v) Gulf between owners of the soil (zamindars) and tillers of the soil\n(vi) Small and fragmented holdings\n(Vil) Subsistence outlook\nIndustry: Indian industry (as dominated by handicrafts) which at one time enjoyed global reputation,\nsuffered a systematic destruction during the British rule.\n• It was largely owing to discriminatory tariff policy of the British government. While cheaper industrial\ngoods were allowed tariff-free access to the Indian markets, handicraft products from India were\nsubjected to heavy export-duty.\n• Handicrafts in India lost both domestic as well as international market.\n• Growth of the modern industry was tardy. It lacked state-initiative. Capital goods industry was almost\nnon-existent. Process of industrialisation remained lop-sided.\nForeign Trade: Foreign trade of India took a hit in two ways:\n• Composition of trade showed a shift from the export of finished goods to the export of raw material and\nfrom the import of bullion (gold and silver) to the import of finished industrial products, largely from\n• It was monopolised by the British government, ending multinational exports from India.\nDemographic Profile: India's demographic profile showed high birth rate, high death rate, high infant\nmortality rate, low expectancy of life and low rate of literacy. These characteristics point to economic and\nsocial backwardness of the country.\nOccupational Structure: Nearly 70-75 percent of working population was engaged in primary sector.\nIndustry offered employment merely to about 10 per cent of the working population. This is yet another\npointer to economic and social backwardness of a country.\nInfrastructure: Infrastructure - economic as well as social —continued to be highly deficient. There was\na modest change, but only to facilitate colonial exploitation of the Indian economy.\nSome Positive Side-effects of the British Rule in India: With a view to enlarging size of the market\nfor British goods in India, the British government needed to provide some infrastructural facilities in\nIndia. These included: (i) Transport facilities, largely in terms of railways, ii) Development of ports, and\n(iii) Provision of post and telegraph services.\nBesides, the British government left a legacy of a strong and efficient administrative set-up.\n23","file":"chap1.pdf"},{"id":"ied-1-01","image":"ied-pages/ied-1-01.jpg","text":"WORK-BOOK [Question-answer Format]\ncompetency Based Learning: Objective Questions\n1. Multiple Choice Questions\nChoose the correct option:\nmaintained a monopoly control over India's exports and imports, while the\nrest was allowed with a few other countries.\nCBSE Sample Paper 2026]\n(a) China\n(c) Britain\n(b) Ceylon (Sri Lanka)\n(d) Persia (Iran)\nof agriculture refers to production of crops for sale in the open market rather\nthan for self-consumption purpose.\n[CBSE 2025(58/2/1)]\n(a) Commercialisation\n(b) Diversification\n(c) Digitisation\n(d) Modernisation\n3. During the British rule, India's foreign trade had various features except\n[CBSE 2024(58/2/1)]\n(a) restrictive policies of commodity production, trade and tariff\n(b) British monopoly over India's export and imports\n(c) free trade from India to the rest of the world\n(d) large export surplus\n4. Under the British Rule, commercialisation of agriculture resulted in shifting of agricultural\nproduction from\n[CBSE 2024(58/4/1), 2023(58/4/1)]\n(a) Cash, Food\n(b) Cotton, Jute\n(c) Food, Cash\n(d) Jute, Food\n5. Britain maintained a monopoly control over India's exports and imports while the rest was\nallowed with a few other countries like\n[CBSE (C) 2024]\n(i) China\n(ii) Ceylon (Sri Lanka)\n(iii) Persia (Iran)\nAlternatives:\n(a) Only (i)\n(b) (i) and (ii).\n(c) (il) and (ill)\n(d) (i). (ii) and (iii)\n6. Which of the following is not true about foreign trade during the colonial rule?\n(a) India suffered from large trade deficit\n[CBSE 2023 (58/2/1)]\n(b) Restrictive policies of commodity production were followed\n(C) Britain maintained a monopoly control ovug (sy xports and imports\n(d) India became an exporter of primary products and an importer of finished consumer goods\n7. In 1951, agricultural contribution to India's national income was\n(a) highest among the three sectors\n(c) lower than the industrial sector\n(b) lowest among the three sectors\n[CBSE 2023 (58/4/1)]\n(d) lower than the service sector\nIndian Economy on the Eve of Independence\n8. During the British rule in India, Indian agricultural output witnessed stagnation due to\n(a) decline in handicrafts\n[CBSE 2023 (58/5/1))\n(c) land settlement\n(b) drain of Indian wealth\n(d) introduction of railways\n9. Identify, which of the following indicates the adverse impact of British rule in India.\nCBSE Sample Paper 2023)\n(a) Introduction of communication networks in India\n(b) Change in composition of India's foreign trade\n(c) Introduction of modern administrative system in India\n(d) Introduction of railways in India\n10. The multifaceted process of demographic transition within the context of India pertains to:\n[CBSE (APQ) 2023-24]\n(a) a sudden decline in the population growth rate due to migration\n(b) a change in the population density across different states in India\n13.\n(d) a shift from high birth and death rates to low birth and death rates over time\nIdentify the result of the large export surplus during the colonial period. [CBSE (APQ) 2022-23]\n(a) Domestic markets flooded with raw\n(b) An increase in gold and silver reserves materials\n(c) Scarcity of essential commodities\n(d) Cheap imported consumer goods\nWhich of the following statements is not true about the demographic condition of India during\nthe colonial period?\nCBSE 2021 (58/1/4), Term-1]\n(a) Infant mortality rate was very high\n(b) After 1921, India entered the second stage of demographic transition\n(c) Life expectancy was too high\n(d) Female literacy rate was just 7%\nsector played a predominant role in occupational structure during the colonial\n[CBSE 2021 (58/1/4), Term-1]\nperiod.\n(a) Agriculture\n(c) Service\n(b) Manufacturing\n(d) Infrastructure\n14. Identify which of the following statement is correct.\n[CBSE 2021 (58/1/4), Term-1)\n(a) British colonial rule made India net importer of raw material\n(b) The real motive behind infrastructure development by Britishers was to serve the people of\nIndia\n(c) India generated large export surplus during the British rule\n(d) Indian handicraft industry enjoyed world-wide reputation under the British rule\nstage of demographic transition after the year 1921.\n15. India entered the\n[CBSE Sample Paper 2021]\n(a) fourth\n(c) third\n(b) second\n(d) first\n25","file":"chap1.pdf"}],"boxes":[{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"ied-1-10","image":"ied-pages/ied-1-10.jpg","text":"Q. 1. State the sole purpose of the British colonial rule in India.\nAns. It was:\ni) to exploit India as a source of raw material for the industrial growth in Britain, and\n(i) to exploit indian market for the industrial goods produced in Britain.\n0.2. At the time of independence, what was the main source of livelihood in India?\nAns. Agriculture.\nQ. 3. State exampies or well-known handicraft industries in India prior to the British period.\nAns. Cotton and silk textiles, metal and precious stonework.\nQ. 4. Enlist the names of individuals who attempted to estimate India's national income prior to\nindependence.\nAns. Dadabhai Naoroji, William Digby, Findlay Shirras, VK.R.V. Rao and R.C. Desal.\n5. Feudal economy - an economy nich means or production are Piney sidel lords (powerful land-owning\n6. Capitalist economy nee if which meater production are owned by the individuals and business house\nand prices are set by the market forces of supply and demand.\n7. Semi-feudal economy - a mixture of feudalistic and capitalistic models of production.\nIndian Economy on the Eve of independence"},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-1-09","image":"ied-pages/ied-1-09.jpg","text":"Famines in India During the British Rule\n• From 1760 till 1943, India was hit by terrible famines on a regular basis.\n• The most significant amongst those was the great famine of Bengal of 1769-70, which claimed a large\nToll of lives.\n• More than 85 million people died owing to repeated famines.\n• In contrast, there have been no famine related deaths since independence.\nPartition of the Country and its Impact\n• The partition of the country had a negative impact on Indian agriculture.\n• India got 82 percent of population and 65 per cent of food grain area.\n• Rich food producing areas of West Punjab and Sindh went to Pakistan.\n• This aggravated the food crisis in the country.\n(d) Lack of Investment in Agriculture Infrastructure: The colonial government or zamindars took\nno interest in promoting investment in agricultural infrastructure?.\n• Permanent means of irrigation were a far cry.\n* Agriculture was heavily dependant on rainfall and therefore continued to be uncertain.\n> Investment in terracing, flood-control, drainage and desalinisation of the soil which could\nhave led to increased productivity of the sector, were totally neglected.\n(e) Gulf between Owners of the Soil and Tillers of the Soil: Agriculture, during the British Raj\nwas characterised by a huge gulf between 'owners of the soil' on the one hand and 'tillers of\n> While the owners shared the output, they seldom (hardly) shared the cost of production.\nThey were merely interested in maximising their rental income (in terms of share of output).\n› The tillers of the soil were merely given enough for subsistence.\n• Thus, the tillers of the soil did not have resources to invest while the owners had no\ninterest.\n› 'Stagnation and backwardness' of agriculture was the obvious consequence."},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-1-09","image":"ied-pages/ied-1-09.jpg","text":"Indebtedness of Tillers of the Soil and Conspicuous Consumption of Owners of the Soil\n• During the British rule, while tillers of the soil confronted absolute poverty and accepted indebtedness\nas their way of life, the owners of the soil indulged in conspicuous consumption.\n• The tillers of the soil viewed agriculture merely as a source of subsistence. The owners of the soil\nviewed it as a hereditary source of income without investment.\n(f) Small and Fragmented Holdings: Landholdings were both small as well as fragmented\".\nAccordingly, most landholdings were uneconomic: yielding low output at high cost.\n10. Agricultural infrastructure - diverse systems and networks of resources that are vital to produce, procure, preserve,\nprocess and trade agricultural products.\n11. Fragmented holdings - small and scattered pieces of land holdings."},{"title":": HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-1-08","image":"ied-pages/ied-1-08.jpg","text":"Pre-British Period Agriculture\n• Backward, stagnant and non-vibrant agriculture during\nthe British Raj sharply contrasted with the pre-British\nperiod agriculture.\n• Prior to the British Raj in India, rural India was described\nas a system of self-contained village communities. These\nvillage communities included farmers and the functionaries.\n• The farmers were engaged in crop farming or cattle\nfarming while the functionaries would render essential\nservices like that of blacksmiths, goldsmiths, washermen\nand shoe-makers.\nProsperous Agriculture even without\nMechanisation (Pre-British Period)\n• There were no intermediaries (like zamindars) between the state and the farmers; the farmers would\npay land revenue directly to the king.\n• Prospery are were be key aractersues one acon is, the French traveller,\nBernier, described co as richerthan Egypt producing amply for self-consumption\nand exporting in abundance.\nAVLR 1.1\nSTATE OF AGRICULTURAL SECTOR ON THE EVE OF INDEPENDENCE"},{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"ied-1-08","image":"ied-pages/ied-1-08.jpg","text":"Q. 5. Enlist any two causes of low agricultural productivity under the British rule.\nAns.\n(1) Exploitative land settlement system.\nwas appropriated by the zamindars and wasted on conspicuous consumpaon).\nIndian economy on the eve of independence\nQ. 6. State key areas where there was a lack of investment in indian agriculture during the British\nAnS.\n(i) Irrigation, which left agriculture dependent on rainfall.\n(ii) Farm-technology, which left farmers dependent on primitive techniques of farming.\n(iii) Land improvement, which neglected terracing (cutting steep areas into flatter sections).\ndrainage (avoiding run-off) and soil conservation (ensuring soil health).\nQ. 7. State the principal difference between owners of the soil and tillers of the soil during the\nBritish regime in India.\nOwners of the soil were the zamindars who were granted ownership rights by the British, and\ntillers of the soil were landless peasants and labourers."},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-1-07","image":"ied-pages/ied-1-07.jpg","text":"Principal Factors Contributing to the Decay of Indian Handicrafts\n• Discriminatory tariff policy of the British goods.\n• Lack of cost competitiveness of the Indian goods compared to industrial goods of Britain.\n• Expansion of railways which led to expansion of domestic market for the low-cost British goods.\nTextile Industry in Bengal\n• Prior to British rule, the textile industry of india wan anous worldwide. Deccai Muslin, found in\nfinest type of cotton textile.\nBengal, in places near Dhaka (present capital of Bangladesh) was tamous all over the world as the\n• Malmar was the nest variety or musin and was prim. by royaity giving it the name of malma\nshahi or malmal khas.\n1.3.2 policy. significant) Growth of Modern Industry during 1850-1947\nUnder the British Raj, modern industry saw only a policy, th. It was only in second half of the\n19th century that the modern industry showed its emergence.\nFive observations need to be noted in this regard:\n(i) Initially, cotton and Jute textile mills were ser-up. COltye textile mills were located in westert\n12\nIndia, mainly in Manarasntra and Gujarat and were fry controlled by the Indians. Jut\ntextile industries were concentrated in bengal and of thany controled by the foreigners\nIndian Economy on the Eve of Independence\n(ii) In the beginning of twentieth century, iron and steel industry came up and Tata Iron and Steel\nCompany (TISCO) was incorporated in 1907. After World War II, cement, paper and sugar\nindustries also started coming up.\n(iii) 'Capital goods industry', producing machines and industrial plants (to further propel the\nprocess of industrialisation) showed hardly any emergence. Accordingly, industrialisation\nfailed to gain momentum during the British rule.\n(iv) The contribution of the new industrial sector in the Gross Value Added (GVA) or Gross\nDomestic Product of the economy remained extremely low.\n(v) The state participation in the process of modern industrialisation was limited. It was confined\nonly to the strategic areas (like railways, power generation, means of communication, ports\nand some other departmental undertakings) which helped expansion of the Indian market for\nthe British products.\nJAVLR 1.2\nSTATE OF INDUSTRIAL SECTOR ON THE EVE OF INDEPENDENCE"},{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"ied-1-07","image":"ied-pages/ied-1-07.jpg","text":"Q. 8. State the motive behind the systematic de-industrialisation during the British Rule in India.\nAns. The motive behind the systematic de-industrialisation during the British rule in India was two-fold:\n(i) To exploit India's wealth of raw material and primary products (like cotton and jute).\nIt was required to fulfil the emerging needs of industrial inputs in the wake of the industrial\nrevolution in Britain.\n(ii) To exploit India as a potential market for the industrial products of Britain.\nQ. 9. What was the impact of systemic de-industrialisation in pre-independent India?\nAns. The systemic de-industrialisation led to:\n(i) Decline of the handicraft industries and the consequent massive unemployment.\n(ii) The penetration of British goods in the Indian markets.\nQ. 10. Name some key industries that came up in India after the Second World War.\nAns. Sugar, cement and paper.\nQ. 11. State a significant drawback of the new industrial set-up in India under the British rule.\nDormant (slow) growth of capital-good industry."},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-1-06","image":"ied-pages/ied-1-06.jpg","text":"Suez Canal and access to Indian Markets\n• Suez Canal is an artificial waterway running from north to south across Isthmus of Suez in north-eastern\nEgypt.\n• The canal provides a direct trade route between curopean or As au and ports located in South\nAsia, East Africa and Oceania by removing the need to sail around Africa.\n• Opening orsuez Canal in 1009 Signicanty reduced the cost of transportation of goods between Britain\n•A significant reds automoted monopoly control of indias foreign trade by the\nBritish government.\n(c) Surplus Trade but only to Benefit the British: Surprisingly, during the British regime, our\nexports exceeded our imports. It implied a surplus of balance of trade.\n*But, this surplus was owing lagery to the export entary goods not the industria\ngoods) which is a sign of economic backwardness.\n15. Finished goods- goods that have crossed the production boundary and are available fe\n14\nIndian Economy on the Eve of Independence\n* Despite the surplus trade, supplies of several essential commodities like food grains, and\nclothes remained deficient in the domestic market.\n- Unlike during the pre-British period, trade surplus did not lead to the influx of bullion into\nthe domestic economy. Instead, it was splurged (wastefully spent) to meet:\n(i) administrative expenses of the British government in India,\n(i) expenses of wars fought by the British government, and\n(iii) the import of invisible items.\n> Administrative and war expenses, along with the import of \"invisibles' led to the drain of\nwealth from India. It compounded the problem of backwardness and stagnation of the\nIndian economy-"},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-1-06","image":"ied-pages/ied-1-06.jpg","text":"Drain of India's Wealth\n• Huge administrative expenses were incurred by the British government to manage their colonial rule in India.\n• Also, huge expenses were incurred by the British government to fight wars in pursuit of their policy of\nimperialism and for import of invisible items.\n• All these expenses were borne by the Indian Exchequer (government's treasury, or the place where\ngovernment money goes.\n• This implied a drain of India's wealth.\nRAVLR 1.3\nSTATE OF INDIA'S FOREIGN TRADE ON THE EVE OF INDEPENDENCE"},{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"ied-1-06","image":"ied-pages/ied-1-06.jpg","text":"Q. 12. What was the most important characteristic of India's foreign trade throughout the colonial\nperiod?\nAns.\nGeneration of a large export surplus. But, unlike during the pre-British period, this surplus did\nnot lead to the influx of bullion into the domestic economy. Instead, it was wastefully used by\nthe British Government (to meet administrative and war expenses) which led to drain of wealth\nfrom India.\nQ. 13. State some trade related characteristics of Indian economy at the time of independence.\nAns. Some of the trade related characteristics of Indian economy at the time of independence are as\nfollow:\n(i) Colonial policy of the British government resulted in a monopoly control of India's foreign trade.\n(i) Exports and imports were largely restricted to be between India and Britain.\n(iii) India's exports provided raw material to the British industry, while India's imports provided\na huge market for the British industry.\n(iv) Surplus generated as balance of trade was only spent to meet administrative and war\nexpenses by the British government in India. These expenses led to a huge drain of wealth\nas it was not used for investment. Consequently, poverty and backwardness were elevated.\nAll these are typical characteristics of a backward economy.\n15"},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-1-05","image":"ied-pages/ied-1-05.jpg","text":"Population Census\n• Population census in India is a detailed estimation of population size, along with a complete demographic\nprofile of the country.\n• It was first conducted under the British rule in 1881. Since then it is conducted after every ten years.\n(c) Life Expectancy: Life expectancy\nwas as low as 32 years, while presently it is 69 years.\nLow life expectancy reflects lack of healthcare facilities, lack of awareness as well as lack of\nmeans to avail them."},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-1-05","image":"ied-pages/ied-1-05.jpg","text":"• High mortality rate and low life expectancy are important social indicators of backwardness and poverty\nof the masses.\n• During the British rule, frequent famines and recurring epidemics were the prime causes behind a high\nmortality rate.\n• While famines occurred largely due to droughts, epidemics occurred due to the neglect of public health\nservices.\n(d) Literacy Rate: Literacy rate was less than 16 per cent, pointing to social backwardness as\na reflection of economic backwardness. Out of this, the female literacy rate was just about\n7 per cent. This indicated gender-bias in the society.\n16. Demographic profile - statistical description of population size and structure as driven by birth rate, death rate and\nmigration.\n17. Birth rate - number of live births per 1,000 people in a year.\n18. Death rate - number of people dying per 1,000 people in a year.\n19. Infant mortality rate - number of deaths of children below the age of one year per 1,000 live births.\n20. Life expectancy - a statistical measure that estimates the number of years an individual is expected to live.\n21. Literacy rate - the proportion of individuals of a particular age group who have the ability to rear and write.\n16\nIndian Economy on the Eve of Independence"},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-1-05","image":"ied-pages/ied-1-05.jpg","text":"Demographic Transition\nFollowing are some notable points relating to demographic transition in India:\n(i) In the history of demographic transition, 1921 is regarded as the 'Year of Great Divide'.\n(ii) Till 1921, population growth in india was never consistent. Size of population kept fluctuating.\nincreasing in one census and decreasing in the other. It is described as the first stage of demographic\ntransition.\n(iii) The census 1901 showed a decline of 0.04 crore in total population (from 23.87 crore in 1891 to\n23.83 crore in 1901).\n(iv) The census 1911 showed a rise of 1.38 crore (from 23.83 crore in 1901 to 25.21 crore in 1911).\n(v) The census 1921 showed a decline of 0.07 crore (from 25.21 crore in 1911 to 25.14 crore in 1921).\n(vi) After 1921, India entered the second stage of demographic transition when population recorded a\nconsistent rise.\n(vii) It is after this year that the total population in India never declined; it recorded a consistent rise: the\ncensus 1931 recorded a rise of 2.76 crore; the census 1941 recorded a rise of 3.96 crore; the census\n1951 recorded a rise of 4.24 crore, and so on.\n(vili) A consistently rising population (on the eve of independence) led to excessive burden of maintenance\ninvestment. It is an expenditure which a country has to incur on the maintenance of the existing\npopulation.\n(ix) When maintenance investment is high, investment for GDP growth remains low.\n(x) High maintenance and low investment for GDP growth is another parameter of the backwardness\nand stagnation of the Indian economy.\n(xi) However, the underlying fact is that till 1951, the rise in India's population was never alarming;\nit ranged between mild to modest. It was because both birth rate and death rate were high and the\ngulf between two was small.\n(xii) It was only from 1951 onwards that the rise in population became explosive in nature, and the\ncountry started tacing a serious challenge in terms of population explosion. Because, whereas birth\nrate continued to be high, death rate recorded a significant decline.\nSTAVLR 1.4\nINDIA'S DEMOGRAPHIC PROFILE ON THE EVE OF INDEPENDENCE"},{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"ied-1-05","image":"ied-pages/ied-1-05.jpg","text":"Q. 14. In the history of demographic transition in India which year is considered as the 'Year of Great\nDivide'?\nAns. 1921.\nQ. 15. What does high infant mortality rate and death rate signify about a country?\nAns. (i) Poor healthcare, (ii) Extreme poverty.\n17"},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-1-04","image":"ied-pages/ied-1-04.jpg","text":"Agriculture as a Means of Subsistence\n• Greater dependence on agriculture (as suggested by occupational structure on the eve of independence)\nimplied lesser availability of land per head of the farming population.\n• Accordingly, agriculture was taken largely as a means of subsistence, and less as an occupation for profit.\n(b) Industry-An Insignificant Source of Occupation: On the eve of independence, barely\n10.0 per cent of the working population in India was engaged in industry with manufacturing\nas its principal component.\n* As against it, 32 per cent in the USA, 42 percent in England and 39 per cent in Japan was\nengaged in the industrial sector.\n* It further proves how backward the indian economy was at the time of independence.\n(c) Insignificant Transition from Agriculture to Industry: High percentage of working population\nwas engaged in agriculture and very low in the industrial sector in 1951 (as reflected in\nTable 12). It points to the fact that during the Britissau transition from agriculture\nto industry has been quite grincant it may be noted in this context that an economy\nesperences take ol ony wen there is as shinicant transition of worktorce rom agricuiture\nto inct arget e conclusion that during the British period, indian economy was far\naway from the point of take-off; instead it continas fo be backward and stagnant.\n* There were some green snoots in the parts of Madras Presidency (comprising areas of\npresent-day Tamil Nadu, Andra Prades, Kerala and Karnataka), Bombay and Bengal\ndecline in dependence on agricultural sector.\n18\nIndian Economy on the Eve of Independence\n* But, at the same time, Odisha, Rajasthan and Punjab recorded an increase in workforce\nengaged in agricultural sector.\n* On the whole, occupational structure of India (on the eve of independence) pointed to\nbackwardness and lopsidedness of the Indian economy.\nAVLR 1.5\nOCCUPATIONAL STRUCTURE DURING THE BRITISH RULE IN INDIA"},{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"ied-1-04","image":"ied-pages/ied-1-04.jpg","text":"Q. 16. Industrial sector being an insignificant source of occupation in india on the eve of independence\nclassified India as what type of an economy?\nAns. Backward economy.\nQ. 17. Transition of workforce from agriculture to industry which was seen in parts of Madras\nPresidency (during the British rule) hinted towards which economic situation?\nAns. Economic 'take-off'.\nQ. 18. Highlight some points on occupational structure of India at the time of independence.\nAns. Some important points on occupational structure of India are as under:\n(i) The bulk of working population was engaged in agricultural sector.\n(ii) The transition from agriculture to industry was insignificant or almost negligible.\n(iii) Heavy dependence on agriculture along with the fact that farming was largely driven by\nthe subsistence motive (rather than any commercial motive points to backwardness of\nthe Indian economy at the time of independence.\n(iv) Rampant poverty and occupational structure were closely related to each other."},{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"ied-1-03","image":"ied-pages/ied-1-03.jpg","text":"Q. 19. What was the main motive behind the development of infrastructure in India during the\nBritish rule?\n(i) Exploiting the Indian economy via widening of the size of market for British goods in India\nand procuring raw material from all routes.\n(ii) Facilitating administration.\nQ. 20. How railways reduced the impact of famines during the colonial period?\nAns. Railways facilitated the movement of food grains from the surplus zones or from the government\nwarehouses (godowns) to the areas afflicted with famines. Accordingly, the impact of famines\nwas reduced."},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-1-03","image":"ied-pages/ied-1-03.jpg","text":"Impact of Railways in India\n• Positive Impact\n(i) Railways facilitated expansion of the domestic market.\nAccordingly, exports and imports of the country showed\na significant rise.\n(ii) Railways facilitated commercialisation of agriculture,\nas goods could then be moved to distant places.\nThis implied a modest change in the outlook of the\nfarmers. They started viewing farming as a business,\nrather than merely as a source of subsistence.\n(III) Railways enabled people to break the barriers of\nRailways in the British Rule\ndistance and undertake journeys to far off places.\nThis promoted cultural affinity among the countrymen.\n(iv) Faster movement of food grain across different parts of the country (owing to Railways) helped\ncontrol the spread of famines. Food supplies could reach the people before they were driven to\n• Negative Impact\n(i) Railways contributed to colonial exploitation of the Indian economy. Because, primary goods (raw\nmaterial) could then be easily transported from the fields and farms to the ports for the purpose of\nexports to the British economy.\n(ii) Finished goods coming as imports to the Indian economy could be easily transported to the interiors\nof the country for purpose of sale.\nThus, the spread of railways led to the spread of the domestic market for the British products.\n23. Barter system of exchange -exchange of goods or services between the two parties without exchanging money.\n21"},{"title":"ABILITY ZONE","page":"ied-1-02","image":"ied-pages/ied-1-02.jpg","text":"Colonial Exploitation of the indian Economy Under the British Rule\n• Indian economy under the British rule was subjected to colonial exploitation. It implied a targeted\nexploitation of all sectors of the economy by the British government. This is how it happened:\n(1) Colonial Exploitation of Agricultural Sector: Agriculture was exploited through zamindari system\nof land revenue.\nFollowing observations highlight how this system led to exploitation of the indian agriculture:\n- Zamindars were declared as owners of the soil. They were to pay a fixed sum to the government by\nway of land revenue, and were free to extract as much as they wished (or as much as they could\nfrom the tillers of the soil (farmers).\n- The tillers of the soil (the actual cultivators) got bare minimum for survival. They were left with no\nsurplus for investment in agricuiture.\n- The zamindars, on the other hand, spent their revenue income on the luxuries of life. Little or no\ninvestment was made for the development of agriculture.\n(il) Colonial Exploitation of Industrial Sector: Prior to the British rule, industrial sector in India was\nwell known for its handicrafts.\n- Indian handicrafts (work produced by hand labour) enjoyed a world-wide reputation for their\nvariety and quality. But these were destroyed by the British government in two ways:\n- Foreign demand for the Indian handicrafts was destroyed by way of heavy duty on their exports.\n- Domestic demand for the Indian handicrafts was destroyed by way of duty-free import of the\nBritish goods in the indian markets.\n(iii) Colonial Exploitation of International Trade: India's international trade was exploited through\ndiscriminatory tariff (a tax on imports) policy. It implied:\n- Duty-free export of Indian raw material to fulfil the industrial needs in Britain.\n- Duty-free import of British goods to expand demand for the British goods in the Indian markets.\nIt was owing to discriminatory tariff policy that India became importer of finished goods from Britain,\nand exporter of raw material to Britain.\n• Briefly, owing to its colonial exploitation, the Indian economy became both stagnant as well as backward.\nReversal of Growth Process Under the British Raj\n• Prior to the Colonial Rule in india, Indian economy was a vibrant economy. Doubtless, the agricultural sector\nwas the main source of livelihood. But there was an all round prosperity across all sectors of the economy.\n• Agriculture was subsistence based, but the cultivators enjoyed the ownership rights and were to pay\na reasonable amount of land revenue directly to the king. There were no middlemen, and therefore, no\nexploitation.\n• Industry was dominated by handicrafts, but it enjoyed worldwide reputation of producing quality products\nlike \"Deccai Muslin'.\n= Exports consisted largely of finished products. Imports were largely of bullion (gold and silver).\n• Colonial policies of the British governd imp armost reversed the composition, or structure of output, as\nwell as the composition of exports and imports. Also, the pace of growth was severely hit.\n• No Systematic official attempts were ever made of estimating per capita income (indicating per-hear\navailability of goods and services in the economy).\n•Howeve sore naividuals made So nodes attempts to measure per capita income during the British\nperiod. These individuals included V.K.R.V. Rao, Dadabhai Naorojs/indlay Shirras, William Digby ant\n- Rao's study was most comprenensive and systematic. He found that during the 1st half of the\n20th century, while national income showed a modest growth of about 2 per cent, per capita income\nshowed a dismal growth of about 0.5 per cent.\nIndian Economy on the Eve of Independence"},{"title":"Power Points [Summary of the Chapter]","page":"ied-1-02","image":"ied-pages/ied-1-02.jpg","text":"Overall Features of indian Economy on the Eve of Independence:\n(1) Stagnant economy\n(fi) Backward economy\n(iii) Dominance of agriculture\n(IV) Bleak industrialisation\n(V) Heavy dependence on imports\n(vi) Limited urbanisation\n(Vil) Semi-teudal economy\nState of Agriculture: Indian agriculture under the British rule showed these characteristics:\n(i) Low production and productivity\n(il) Exploitative land settlement system\n(iii) Forced commercialisation of agriculture\n(iv) Lack of investment in the agriculture infrastructure\n(v) Gulf between owners of the soil (zamindars) and tillers of the soil\n(vi) Small and fragmented holdings\n(Vil) Subsistence outlook\nIndustry: Indian industry (as dominated by handicrafts) which at one time enjoyed global reputation,\nsuffered a systematic destruction during the British rule.\n• It was largely owing to discriminatory tariff policy of the British government. While cheaper industrial\ngoods were allowed tariff-free access to the Indian markets, handicraft products from India were\nsubjected to heavy export-duty.\n• Handicrafts in India lost both domestic as well as international market.\n• Growth of the modern industry was tardy. It lacked state-initiative. Capital goods industry was almost\nnon-existent. Process of industrialisation remained lop-sided.\nForeign Trade: Foreign trade of India took a hit in two ways:\n• Composition of trade showed a shift from the export of finished goods to the export of raw material and\nfrom the import of bullion (gold and silver) to the import of finished industrial products, largely from\n• It was monopolised by the British government, ending multinational exports from India.\nDemographic Profile: India's demographic profile showed high birth rate, high death rate, high infant\nmortality rate, low expectancy of life and low rate of literacy. These characteristics point to economic and\nsocial backwardness of the country.\nOccupational Structure: Nearly 70-75 percent of working population was engaged in primary sector.\nIndustry offered employment merely to about 10 per cent of the working population. This is yet another\npointer to economic and social backwardness of a country.\nInfrastructure: Infrastructure - economic as well as social —continued to be highly deficient. There was\na modest change, but only to facilitate colonial exploitation of the Indian economy.\nSome Positive Side-effects of the British Rule in India: With a view to enlarging size of the market\nfor British goods in India, the British government needed to provide some infrastructural facilities in\nIndia. These included: (i) Transport facilities, largely in terms of railways, ii) Development of ports, and\n(iii) Provision of post and telegraph services.\nBesides, the British government left a legacy of a strong and efficient administrative set-up.\n23"}]},{"id":"i2","number":"2","subject":"ied","title":"Indian Economy 1950–1990","intro":"","lessons":[{"title":"Capitalist, socialist and mixed economies","body":"Capitalist economy: private ownership; markets and profit guide production.\n\nSocialist economy: government ownership and planning guide resource use.\n\nMixed economy: public and private sectors coexist; India used public leadership with regulated private enterprise.\n\nIndia wanted industrial growth while also pursuing social welfare and equity.","formula":"","source":"ied-2-15","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"2.1 Economic Systems","points":["Capitalist economy: private ownership; markets and profit guide production.","Socialist economy: government ownership and planning guide resource use.","Mixed economy: public and private sectors coexist; India used public leadership with regulated private enterprise."],"takeaway":"India wanted industrial growth while also pursuing social welfare and equity."},{"title":"Economic planning and Five Year Plans","body":"Planning = deliberately allocating resources towards stated goals over a fixed period.\n\nIndia’s First Five Year Plan began in 1951.\n\nPlans coordinated investment in agriculture, industry and infrastructure when resources were scarce.\n\nCommon goals stayed broadly similar, but the priority given to each sector could change between plans.","formula":"","source":"ied-2-14","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"2.2.1 Concept of Economic Planning","points":["Planning = deliberately allocating resources towards stated goals over a fixed period.","India’s First Five Year Plan began in 1951.","Plans coordinated investment in agriculture, industry and infrastructure when resources were scarce."],"takeaway":"Common goals stayed broadly similar, but the priority given to each sector could change between plans."},{"title":"Growth, modernisation, self-reliance and equity","body":"Growth: increase productive capacity and output.\n\nModernisation: adopt better technology and a progressive social outlook, including women’s participation.\n\nSelf-reliance: reduce dependence on foreign resources for essential needs.\n\nEquity: ensure benefits reach all sections and basic needs are met.\n\nMore GDP does not automatically mean fairer distribution; growth and equity are separate goals.","formula":"","source":"ied-2-14","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"2.2.2 Common Goals of Five Year Plans","points":["Growth: increase productive capacity and output.","Modernisation: adopt better technology and a progressive social outlook, including women’s participation.","Self-reliance: reduce dependence on foreign resources for essential needs.","Equity: ensure benefits reach all sections and basic needs are met."],"takeaway":"More GDP does not automatically mean fairer distribution; growth and equity are separate goals."},{"title":"Land reforms: changing agrarian relations","body":"Abolition of intermediaries: remove rent-collecting middlemen and connect cultivators directly with the state.\n\nLand ceiling: limit holdings and redistribute surplus land.\n\nConsolidation: combine scattered plots; cooperative farming: pool resources.\n\nLimitation: loopholes, litigation and weak implementation reduced the gains.\n\nInstitutional reforms change ownership and tenure; technical reforms change production methods.","formula":"","source":"ied-2-11","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"2.3.2 Land Reforms [Institutional Reforms]","points":["Abolition of intermediaries: remove rent-collecting middlemen and connect cultivators directly with the state.","Land ceiling: limit holdings and redistribute surplus land.","Consolidation: combine scattered plots; cooperative farming: pool resources.","Limitation: loopholes, litigation and weak implementation reduced the gains."],"takeaway":"Institutional reforms change ownership and tenure; technical reforms change production methods."},{"title":"The Green Revolution package","body":"Green Revolution = a sharp rise in agricultural output through HYV technology.\n\nThe package: HYV seeds + irrigation + fertilisers + pesticides + credit.\n\nEarly gains concentrated in wheat and well-irrigated regions; later gains spread to more crops and areas.\n\nSeeds alone cannot deliver the promised yield when irrigation and supporting inputs are missing.","formula":"","source":"ied-2-11","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"2.3.3 Technical Reforms: HYV Technology and Green Revolution","points":["Green Revolution = a sharp rise in agricultural output through HYV technology.","The package: HYV seeds + irrigation + fertilisers + pesticides + credit.","Early gains concentrated in wheat and well-irrigated regions; later gains spread to more crops and areas."],"takeaway":"Seeds alone cannot deliver the promised yield when irrigation and supporting inputs are missing."},{"title":"Green Revolution: gains","body":"Higher yields raised foodgrain production and reduced dependence on imports.\n\nMore marketable surplus supplied people outside agriculture.\n\nBetter food supplies supported buffer stocks and price stability.\n\nHigher farm incomes increased demand for industrial goods.\n\nMarketable surplus = production left for sale after the farmer’s own requirements.","formula":"","source":"ied-2-10","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"2.3.5 Gains of Green Revolution","points":["Higher yields raised foodgrain production and reduced dependence on imports.","More marketable surplus supplied people outside agriculture.","Better food supplies supported buffer stocks and price stability.","Higher farm incomes increased demand for industrial goods."],"takeaway":"Marketable surplus = production left for sale after the farmer’s own requirements."},{"title":"Green Revolution: limitations","body":"Crop imbalance: early gains mainly benefited wheat and rice.\n\nRegional imbalance: well-irrigated regions advanced faster.\n\nIncome inequality: larger farmers could afford the input package more easily.\n\nEnvironmental damage: excessive chemicals and groundwater use harmed soil and water.\n\nEvaluate both the increase in food output and how its gains and costs were distributed.","formula":"","source":"ied-2-09","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"2.3.6 Limitations of Green Revolution","points":["Crop imbalance: early gains mainly benefited wheat and rice.","Regional imbalance: well-irrigated regions advanced faster.","Income inequality: larger farmers could afford the input package more easily.","Environmental damage: excessive chemicals and groundwater use harmed soil and water."],"takeaway":"Evaluate both the increase in food output and how its gains and costs were distributed."},{"title":"The subsidy debate","body":"In favour: subsidies make inputs affordable, encourage new technology and support small farmers.\n\nAgainst: benefits may favour large farmers; government spending rises; cheap inputs can encourage waste.\n\nBetter design: target farmers who need support and discourage excessive input use.\n\nThe issue is who receives the subsidy and what behaviour it encourages.","formula":"","source":"ied-2-09","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"2.3.7 Agricultural Subsidies: Vice or Virtue?","points":["In favour: subsidies make inputs affordable, encourage new technology and support small farmers.","Against: benefits may favour large farmers; government spending rises; cheap inputs can encourage waste.","Better design: target farmers who need support and discourage excessive input use."],"takeaway":"The issue is who receives the subsidy and what behaviour it encourages."},{"title":"Why the public sector led industrialisation","body":"Basic and heavy industries needed large investments and long waiting periods before returns.\n\nPrivate firms often lacked the capital or incentive to undertake them.\n\nThe public sector supplied infrastructure, strategic industries and investment in backward regions.\n\nPublic-sector leadership supported a mixed economy; it did not mean private firms disappeared.","formula":"","source":"ied-2-08","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"2.4.1 Public and Private Sectors in Indian Industrial Development","points":["Basic and heavy industries needed large investments and long waiting periods before returns.","Private firms often lacked the capital or incentive to undertake them.","The public sector supplied infrastructure, strategic industries and investment in backward regions."],"takeaway":"Public-sector leadership supported a mixed economy; it did not mean private firms disappeared."},{"title":"IPR 1956 and industrial licensing","body":"IPR 1956: one group reserved for the state; a second with state leadership and private participation; the remainder open to regulated private enterprise.\n\nIndustrial licensing controlled new factories and expansion.\n\nPurpose: direct scarce resources and promote regional balance; drawback: too many controls weakened competition.\n\nRemember the three groups by the degree of state involvement, rather than as an unexplained list.","formula":"","source":"ied-2-07","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"2.4.2 Industrial Policy Resolution, 1956","points":["IPR 1956: one group reserved for the state; a second with state leadership and private participation; the remainder open to regulated private enterprise.","Industrial licensing controlled new factories and expansion.","Purpose: direct scarce resources and promote regional balance; drawback: too many controls weakened competition."],"takeaway":"Remember the three groups by the degree of state involvement, rather than as an unexplained list."},{"title":"Small-scale industries and the Karve Committee","body":"Karve Committee, 1955: emphasised small industries’ role in rural development.\n\nWhy protect them? They create jobs with relatively little capital and spread production across regions.\n\nSupport: reserved products, concessions and credit.\n\nWeaknesses: limited finance, outdated technology and marketing difficulties.\n\nTheir main advantage was labour-intensive, decentralised growth.","formula":"","source":"ied-2-07","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"2.4.3 Small-Scale Industry","points":["Karve Committee, 1955: emphasised small industries’ role in rural development.","Why protect them? They create jobs with relatively little capital and spread production across regions.","Support: reserved products, concessions and credit.","Weaknesses: limited finance, outdated technology and marketing difficulties."],"takeaway":"Their main advantage was labour-intensive, decentralised growth."},{"title":"Import substitution, tariffs and quotas","body":"Import substitution = make domestically what was previously imported.\n\nTariff: tax imports, making them costlier. Quota: limit their quantity.\n\nBenefit: protect young domestic industries and conserve foreign exchange.\n\nCost: prolonged protection weakens pressure to improve quality and efficiency.\n\nProtection can help an industry get started; permanent shelter can make it complacent.","formula":"","source":"ied-2-06","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"2.5 Trade Policy: Import Substitution","points":["Import substitution = make domestically what was previously imported.","Tariff: tax imports, making them costlier. Quota: limit their quantity.","Benefit: protect young domestic industries and conserve foreign exchange.","Cost: prolonged protection weakens pressure to improve quality and efficiency."],"takeaway":"Protection can help an industry get started; permanent shelter can make it complacent."},{"title":"Appraising the pre-reform strategy","body":"Strategy: public-sector leadership, licensing, small-industry protection and import substitution.\n\nAchievements: a wider industrial base, infrastructure and greater food security.\n\nLimitations: inefficient enterprises, restrictive controls, weak job creation and persistent poverty.\n\nThe pre-reform strategy built capacity, but its limitations created pressure for change.","formula":"","source":"ied-2-05","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"2.6 India’s Growth Strategy during 1950–1990","points":["Strategy: public-sector leadership, licensing, small-industry protection and import substitution.","Achievements: a wider industrial base, infrastructure and greater food security.","Limitations: inefficient enterprises, restrictive controls, weak job creation and persistent poverty."],"takeaway":"The pre-reform strategy built capacity, but its limitations created pressure for change."}],"pages":[{"id":"ied-2-15","image":"ied-pages/ied-2-15.jpg","text":"Indian Economic Development\n(b) Socialist Economy\nI. Meaning\n• A socialist economy is the one in which there is a\nsocial (collective/public) ownership of the means\nof production, and economic decisions are taken\nby some central authority of the government with\na view to maximise social welfare.\n• Allocation of resources is done on the basis of 'needs\nof the people', as decided by the central authority.\nECONOM\nSocialist Economy\nIl. Features\nThe three salient features of a socialist economy are as these:\n(i) Means of production are collectively owned by the society as a whole. Or, there is a public\nownership of the means of production. There is no private property.\n(ii) Means of production are used in a manner such that social welfare is maximised.\n(iii) The government controls:\n(a) production of goods and services,\n(b) distribution of goods and services, and\n(c) prices of goods and services.\nHIGHLIGHTS\n• 'From each according to his capacity, and to each according to his need' is the directive principle of a\nsocialist economy.\nIll. Merit\n• Socialist economy achieves 'equitable distribution of income, because allocation of resources is\nbased on the principle of social justice?.\nIV. Demerit\n• The principal demerit is that unlike a capitalist economy, a socialist economy does not allow\n'consumers' sovereignty'.\n• Consumers don't have choices and their needs are decided by the government Also berauce\nproduction is not profit-oriented, GDP growth remains a slow process.\n• It is owing to a high degree of public discontent (related to the lack of consumers' sovereigntv)\nand slow GDP growth, that the erstwhile socialist economice like Soviet Union and china\nhad to switch over to more 'market based' economic order, often termed as Sprise Market\nEconomy.\n2. Social lustice - is achieved when the process of economic growth leads to be able distribution of income and\n52\nIndian Economy (1950-1990)\n(c) Mixed Economy\nI. Meaning\n• A mixed economy is the one in which there is private as well\nas public ownership of the means of production.\n• Production decisions are governed largely by the principle of\nprofit maximisation, but not without checks and balances of\nsocial justice.\nIl. Features\nMixed Economy\nThe three salient features of a mixed economy are as under:\n(i) Means of production are owned by the private entrepreneurs as well as the government.\n(ii) In the private sector, production decisions are governed by the principle of profit maximisation,\nwhile in the public sector, social welfare predominates.\n(iii) Both private and public sectors play a significant role in the process of production.\nIll. Merit\n• The principal merit of a mixed economy is that it combines the merits of capitalist as well as a\nsocialist economy.\n• On the one hand, GDP growth is encouraged because private entrepreneurs are free to focus on\n'profit maximisation'.\n• On the other hand, 'social justice' or equality is promoted because the government sector places\nhigh priority on the maximisation of social welfare.\nIV. Demerit\n• The principal demerit is that the government sector is often inflicted with corruption, leading to low\nlevel of efficiency/productivity.\n• It is owing to this demerit that the mixed economies (like India) are gradually opting for privatisation\nof the public enterprises.\n• It may be noted that most economies of the world are now mixed economies where production\ndecisions are governed largely by the market forces of demand and supply, but are regulated by\nway of direct or indirect intervention by the government.\nMixed Economic System - An Ideal System of Growth\n• Leaders and planners of independent India sought mixed economic system as an ideal strategy of\ngrowth, avoiding the extreme versions of capitalism and socialism.\n• The Industrial Policy Resolution of 1948 and the Directive Principles of the Indian Constitution\nreflected the outlook of the planners that India would adopt an economic order where there was\na strong public sector but with full respect to private property and democracy.\n• The public sector would take a leading role and private sector would be encouraged\n(and incentivised) to be an active participant in the process of growth and development.","file":"chap 2.pdf"},{"id":"ied-2-14","image":"ied-pages/ied-2-14.jpg","text":"Indian Economic Development\n3 AVLR 2.1\nTYPES OF ECONOMIC SYSTEM\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\nQ. 1. What dominates the process of decision-making under free play of market forces?\nAns. Maximisation of profit.\nQ. 2. \"Capitalist economy ignores the collective interest of the society.\" Comment.\nAns. True. Because resource allocation, production and prices are determined by the market forces\nof supply and demand, oblivious (unaware) of collective interest of the society or basic needs of\nthe poor (food, clothing and education).\n'The government of the United Kingdom controls the entire healthcare system through the\nNHS. Their health system is fully socialized, where the government pays the salaries of doctors.\nHowever, the UK also has a thriving private sector. In 2016, the UK had over 3 million private-sector\nbusinesses. Its capital city, London, is a financial hub of the world and hosts one of the largest\nglobal stock exchanges, the London Stock Exchange (LSE).\"\nSource: https://helpfulprofessor.com/mixed-economy-examples/\nWhat can be said about the choice of economic system in UK from the case presented above?\nAns. It follows a mixed economic model.\n2.2 FIVE YEAR PLANS\n2.2.1 Concept of Economic Planning, and its Adoption in 1951\n• \"Economic planning means utilisation of country's resources in different development activities in\naccordance with national priorities.\" —Planning Commission.\n• India adopted the model of 'Five Year Plans' as pioneered by the Soviet Union.\n• Mixed economic system was integrated with economic planning in the form of Five Year Plans.\n• The First Five Year Plan was introduced in 1951 and the last in 2012. In between these years, some\nannual plans were also introduced as these: three annual plans from April 1, 1966 to March 31,\n1969; one annual plan from April 1, 1979 to March 31, 1980; two annual plans from April 1, 1990\nto March 31, 1992.\n• Different Five Year Plans had different goals. It is important to note that the plan documents not\nonly specify the objectives to be achieved in the five years of a plan, but also what is to be achieved\nin the long run. Long-term plan is called 'perspective plan?.\nOrigin of Planning in India\n• In India, the idea of economic planning became a reality with the setting up of Planning Commiccion in\n1950. under the chairmanshio of the then Prime Minister Pandit loucharlal Nehru It ic important to\nnote that the Planning Commission has now been dissolved. In January 2015 it has been replaced by\n'NIT Aayog'. The role of NIT Aayog is to suggest policies to accelerate the pace of GDP growth.]\n[Note: Description related to 'NITI Aayog' is provided in the Ability Zone.]\n3. Perspective plan - long-term planning where targets are fixed for a long period say 15 to 25 years.\nIndian Economy (1950-1990)\nP.C. Mahalanobis\n• Prashant Chandra Mahalanobis is known as the Architect of Indian Planning.\n• An eminent statistician, he made a landmark contribution to Indian Planning during the\nSecond Five Year Plan when he developed a model of industrial investment towards\nbuilding up a domestic consumption goods sector.\n• Mahalanobis made significant contribution to the field of Statistics in India. He\nestablished Indian Statistical Institute (ISI) in Calcutta, and started a journal, Sankhya,\nwhich is a respectable forum for statisticians to discuss their ideas.\nNeed for Planning in India\n• India inherited from the British a backward and stagnant economy. It was backward as the level of\noutput and productivity were low. And, it was stagnant as the GDP growth was dismal (extremely low).\n• Such an economy could not be left to the market forces of supply and demand to trace its path of growth\nand development. It needed a big push of investment, supported by the government. Hence, the recourse\nto economic planning.\n2.2.2 Common Goals of Five Year Plans\nGoals of planning are common to all the Five Year Plans and are therefore generally studied as\nCommon Goals of Five Year Plans or Objectives of Planning.\n• It may be noted that depending on ground realities, each plan is designed as a distinct plan with\na distinct set of objectives. However, according to syllabus specifications by the CBSE we are\nfocussing only on Common Goals of Five Year Plans.\nHIGHLIGHTS\nWhy should Plans have Goals?\n• Without goals, there is no planning. In fact, planning is defined as a strategy that defines how to allocate\nthe country's scarce resources to different uses with a view to achieving a given set of goals.\n• These goals often relate to growth and social justice.\nCommon Goals of Five Year Plans are as under:\nCommon Goals of Five Year Plans\n(a)\nGrowth\nModernisation\nSelf-reliance and\nEquity or Equitable\nSelf-sufficiency\n(a) Growth: It refers to increase in the country's capacity to produce goods and services within\nthe country. It implies either a larger stock of productive capital, or a larger size of supporting\nservices (like transport and banking) or an increase in the efficiency of productive capital.\n• Increase in GDP (Gross Domestic Product) is a good indicator of economic growth. Increase\nin GDP implies increase in the level of output in the economy. It implies an increase in the\nflow of goods and services in the economy.\n• When the increase in the flow of goods and services is consistent over a long period of\ntime, it is called 'economic growth'. Thus, GDP growth leads to economic growth.","file":"chap 2.pdf"},{"id":"ied-2-13","image":"ied-pages/ied-2-13.jpg","text":"Indian Economic Development\n› Increase in GDP depends on two factors:\n(1) Increase in resource-base of the country, and\nil)Increase in productivity (output per unit of input) through innovative technology.\n• Planning in India was to ensure that natural resources were fully explored and production\ntechnology was continuously improved. So that, GDP growth achieved a momentum.\nHarvester\nThresher\nInnovative Technology raises Efficiency/Productivity\n! HIGHLIGHTS\nStructural Composition and Structural Change\n• Concepts of structural composition and structural change assume significance in the context of GDP\n• The contribution of each economic sector to the GDP of a country makes up the structural composition\nof the economy. In India, the agriculture and allied sector contributes 17.94 per cent to GDP, the\nindustrial sector 27.13 per cent, and the service sector 54.93 per cent to the GDP of India.\n• As a country develops, the contribution of each economic sector to GDP changes, which is referred\nto as 'structural change'. Usually with development, the share of agriculture declines while share of\nindustry increases. At higher level of development, the share of industry declines paving way for higher\ncontribution by the service sector. However, in case of India, the shift has been from agriculture sector\nto service sector directly. At the time of independence, agriculture had highest share in GDP, around\n50 percent. By 1990, the share of service sector rose to 40.59 percent, more than that of agriculture\nand industrial sector.\n(b) Modernisation: It refers to updating and adoption of modern (or new) technology in the\nprocess of growth. Output can be increased either by increasing the pool of resources or by\nusing innovative technology.\n> Modern age is the age of science and innovations. Science has offered us new ways of\ndoing things, such that productivity in farms and factories has shown an exponential rise\nover time.\n› Green Revolution in Indian agriculture is a well-known example of how technology can\nbring about revolutionary changes in output. Recently, IT revolution has redefined the\nconcept of domestic production through BPO (Business Process Outsourcing).\n» However, modernisation in the context of goals of plans in India, was assigned a social\nangle as well. Here, it refers to changes in social outlook, focusing on 'Gender Equality'.\nIndian Economy (1950-1990)\n› The planners were of the strong opinion that conventional wisdom (wisdom without a valid\nreason) must give way to modern outlook. It included issues like empowerment of women\nso that (like men) they also could participate in the process of production and contribute\nto the process of economic and social prosperity.\n! HIGHLIGHTS\nModernisation and Employment Generation: Complementary or Contradictory?\n• Modernisation as a planning objective was not expected to contradict with employment generation.\n• Both (modernisation and employment generation) as goals of planning were taken as complementary to\neach other. The complementarity between the two was expected to work as under:\n- Modernisation leads to increase in productivity.\n- Increase in productivity leads to higher level of production activity and higher level of income in the\neconomy. Implying, a rising demand for goods and services in the economy.\n- Expansion of demand leads to expansion of employment opportunities.\n• However, of late the nature of growth process has taken a dramatic turn. Technology has emerged\nas the principal driver of growth process. And, the nature of technology is such that it is substituting\nlabour rather than supplementing it. Consequently, 'jobless growth is emerging to be challenge for the\npoliticians and the planners.\n• Thus, even when 'modernisation' and 'employment generation' were taken as complementary objectives\n(under Five Year Plans), the dichotomy between the two is an emerging challenge.\n(c) Self-reliance and Self-sufficiency: Self-reliance means the state of non-dependence on rest of\nthe world for the financial resources. Self-sufficiency refers to the state of non-dependence\nupon rest of the world for the essential supplies in the domestic economy.\n> This twin goal (of self-reliance and self-sufficiency) was accorded a high priority during the\nfirst seven plans to promote economic growth and modernisation.\n› The basic idea was not to expose Indian economy to political pressures from rest of the\nworld when we are dependent upon them for the supply of essential goods.\n> Indeed, it happened in 1965, when USA threatened to stop exports of food grains to India\nif it did not stop the then war with Pakistan. India wanted to be self-sufficient in food grain\nproduction to avoid the uncertainties of supplies from rest of the world.\nHIGHLIGHTS\n• It was necessary for India to achieve self-sufficiency in food grain production. Otherwise, we had to yield\nto USA for the imports of food grains on the specified terms and conditions (which could undermine our\npolitical sovereignty).\n• Example: India was pestered to end war with Pakistan (1965) for favour of food grain supplies from USA.\n(d) Equity or Equitable Distribution: Economic growth would become a meaningless exercise if\nthe benefits of growth accrue to only a handful of people in the society. Note the following\nthree observations carefully:\n4. Jobless growth - a situation when the level of output in the economy tends to rise owing to innovative technology\nwithout any perceptible rise in the level of employment.\n57","file":"chap 2.pdf"},{"id":"ied-2-12","image":"ied-pages/ied-2-12.jpg","text":"Indian Economic Development\n(i) Growth serves virtually no purpose in a society where rich tend to become richer and\nthe poor continue to struggle even for the essential of life.\n(i) Benefits of growth must spread across all sections of the society, so that the distribution\nof income becomes equitable.\n(il) Equitable distribution of income implies social equality and this was one of the principal\nobjectives or goals of planning in India.\nThe planners in India stressed the point that every citizen of the country must be able to fulfil\nhis basic needs of food, clothing and shelter, besides education and healthcare.\nHIGHLIGHTS\nEqual Distribution and Equitable Distribution\n• Equal distribution would mean every individual in the society gets the same share in the country's\nnational income. It would imply a situation when a doctor gets the same salary as a clerk in the hospital.\nNo society would ever seek a situation like this.\n• Equitable distribution, on the other hand, refers to a situation when differences in income are allowed\nbut only within certain limits.\n- These differences are socially unwarranted and are to be proportionate to the differences in\nqualifications and skills of different individuals in the society.\n- When a doctor gets ‹ 50,000 to perform a surgery (lasting for 1 hour while, on the other hand, a\nfactory worker gets ‹ 50,000 for the entire year (working 8 hours a day), the difference in income is\ntoo large to be socially justified. Such differences (which are socially unwarranted) must be corrected.\n- 'Equity' (in terms of equitable distribution of income) implies that economic growth is related to social\njustice.\n- It is only when economic growth is related to social justice that 'growth' is converted into 'development.\nPlanning in India focuses on 'growth with social justice.\nDepending upon our needs and means, our planners have been redefining the relative significance of\ndifferent objectives from plan to plan.\nOn the whole, the focus has been on achieving better quality of life across all sections of the society,\nnow popularly known as 'Inclusive Growth.\nConclusion\n• Mixed economy was our model of growth after independence.\n• Public sector was to play the flagship role in the growth and development of the country.\nThe growth process was to be pursued according to the specified targets, plan after plan.\n) AVLR 2.2\nCOMMON GOALS OF FIVE YEAR PLANS IN INDIA\n5. Inclusive growth - economic growth that is distributed fairly across society and creates opportunities for all.\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\nQ. 4. Why is equitable distribution of income an essential component of long-term objectives?\nAns. Growth serves no purpose until benefits of growth are spread across all sections of society.\nQ. 5. How is growth different from the term 'development'?\nAns. Growth combined with social justice becomes development.\nQ. 6. State two ways of increasing the GDP.\nAns. Two ways of increasing the GDP are as under:\n(i) Discovery of more and more resources in the country that increases resource-base of the\ncountry.\n(ii) Innovative technology, that enhances productivity or output per unit of input.\n[Note: The following section highlights the condition of various sectors of Indian economy, viz., agriculture, industry\nand trade. It reflects the impact of policy formulations and reforms initiated to redefine, restructure and uplift\nthe Indian economy during the period 1950-1990.]\n2.3 AGRICULTURE\n2.3.1 Recap of the State of Agriculture under the British Rule\n• Agricultural sector was both backward and stagnant (non-vibrant) at the time of independence.\nIt was backward as it exhibited the following four features:\n(i) Low level of output and productivity.\n(il) Low level of investment as well as low inducement to invest.\n(iii) Small and scattered holdings, forcing reliance on primitive technology and consequently high\ncost of production.\n(iv) Subsistence outlook of the farmers, taking agriculture largely as a means of subsistence rather\nthan a source of earning profit.\n• Further, agriculture was stagnant, as it exhibited the following three features:\n(i) It was subjected to an exploitative land revenue system (like zamindari system of land\nrevenue) which deprived the farmers of their ownership rights and left them with no surplus\n(of output) beyond subsistence.\n(il) Forced commercialisation of agriculture (by way of cultivation of indigo) to meet industrial\nneeds of the British textile industry.\n(ili) Widening gulf between the tillers of the soil and owners of the soil. The tillers had no surplus\nto invest and the owners wasted their surplus in conspicuous consumption.\n• In view of these issues of backwardness and stagnation of Indian agriculture, the planners in India\nintroduced a series of reforms which are broadly classified as (i) Land (or Institutional Reforms,\nand (ii) Technical Reforms.\n59","file":"chap 2.pdf"},{"id":"ied-2-11","image":"ied-pages/ied-2-11.jpg","text":"Indian Economic Development\nFollowing is a briet description of these reforms:\n2.3.2 Land Reforms [institutional Reforms]\nLand reforms or institutional reforms included the following actions by the government:\ntwo objectives:\n(i) Stop exploitation of the cultivators by the zamindars.\n(i) Give incentives to the cultivators to make improvements on land as its owners.\n» The abolition of intermediaries led to restoration of ownership rights of nearly 200 lakh\ntenants who, thus, came into direct contact with the government for the payment of land\nrevenue. This ended exploitation of the actual tillers of the soil.\n[Note: In the context of Indian agriculture, the terms \"land reforms' and institutional reforms' are used\ninterchangeably.]\n• HIGHLIGHTS\nBasic Objective of Land Reforms\n• The government pursued land reforms with the basic objective of promoting equity (equality) in the rural\neconomy via promoting the land to tiller' policy.\n• It was to be achieved by restoring ownership rights of the tillers of the soil and by eliminating the class\nof intermediaries (zamindars) so as to stop exploitation of the actual cultivators of the soil.\n(b) Ceiling on Land Holdings: With a view to promoting equity (equality) in the distribution of\nland, ceiling were imposed on the holding-size.\n> It implies fixation of the maximum size of cultivable land that an individual or a family could\n> Land ceiling aims at reducing the concentration of land ownership in a few hands.\n> The surplus land (over and above the ceiling limit) was resumed by the government and\nredistributed among small holders or landless labourers.\n(c) Consolidation of Holdings: With a view to reducing fragmentation, consolidation of holdings\nwas accorded a high priority.\n* Consolidation is a practice to allot land to the farmer at one place for his scattered holdings\nhere and there. It saves the cost of cultivation.\n> By 2004, more than 1,633 lakh hectares of land was brought under consolidated holdings.\n(d) Regulation of Rent: To put an end to excessive and illegal extortions from the cultivators,\nrents were regulated. Generally, these were not to exceed 1/3rd of the value of crop.\n(e) Cooperative farming: Cooperative farming was promoted to enhance bargaining power of\nthe small holders. Collectively, the farmers could buy inputs at a lower price and sell their\nproduce at a higher price.\n60\nIndian Economy (1950-1990)\nHurdles in the Implementation of Land Reforms\nWhile land reforms were successfully pursued in the states of West Bengal and Kerala, some serious\nhurdles were experienced in other parts of the country, owing to the following reasons:\n(i) The zamindars managed to evict the tenants and project themselves as owners of the soil.\n(i) A sizeable section of the landless labourers were kept out of the ambit of ownership rights.\n(iii) The big landlords challenged the land ceiling legislation in courts which delayed/stalled the\nimplementation of the reforms.\nThis delay was used by the landlords to register their surplus land in the name of their close\nrelatives, escaping the Ceiling Act'\n2.3.3 Technical Reforms: HYV Technology and Green Revolution\n• The problem of low productivity\nwas tackled by way of technical\nreforms.\n• Technical reforms evolved around\nHYV (High Yielding Variety) seeds.\nUse of HY seeds along with\nchemical fertilizers, insecticides\nand pesticides led to substantial\nrise in the level of agricultural\nGreen Revolution: A Revolutionary Rise in Productivity\noutput. The rise in output was so significant that it was called 'Green Revolution'.\n• The term 'Green' refers to 'crops' and 'Revolution' refers to 'spurt' (significant rise). Together,\nthese terms imply a spurt in crop production (especially for wheat and rice). It started happening\nin India in the year 1967-68.\n• In the year 1967-68 itself, food grain production increased by nearly 25 per cent.\n• India became self-sufficient in the production of food grains and successfully broke the vicious\ncycle of agricultural stagnation. rucion of roo grains and succestly broke the vicious\nElements of HYV Technology\nHYV technology includes four principal elements, as under:\n(a) Use of HYV Seeds: Since 1965, High\nYielding Variety seeds started replacing\nthe conventional varieties.\n> HYV seeds gained popularity\nwheat, bajra, rice, maize, jowar, and\ncotton, in particular.\n> It is these seeds\nwhich led to a\nsubstantial rise in agricultural output.\n(b) Use of Chemical Fertilizers: Use of HYV\nHigh Yielding Variety Seeds and High Crop Productivity\nseeds requires the use of fertilizers.\n61","file":"chap 2.pdf"},{"id":"ied-2-10","image":"ied-pages/ied-2-10.jpg","text":"Indian Economic Development\nconsumed in India till December 2024.\n(c) Use of Insecticides and Pesticides for\nCrop Protection: Along with fertilizers,\nthe use of HYV seeds also requires the\nuse of pesticides to protect the crops\nagainst pests and diseases.\n• Fourteen Central Plant Protection\nCentres were set-up in this respect.\n> For crop protection, Integrated\nPest Management Programme was\nPesticides for Crop Protection\nadopted along with the adoption of\nHYV technology.\n(d) Expansion of Irrigation Facilities: HYV seeds need to be deeply irrigated.\n> To cope with the need, several major and minor irrigation projects were launched across\ndifferent parts of the country.\n› It is significant that while in 1951, barely 17 percent of land was under permanent means\nof irrigation, it increased to nearly 45 per cent during the plan period.\n2.3.4 Two Distinct Phases of Green Revolution\nPhase 1: Mid-60s — Mid-70s\n• During this phase, scope of Green Revolution was restricted largely to wheat and rice growing\nregions of the country. These included the states of Punjab, Andhra Pradesh and Tamil Nadu\nThis is because the HYV technology requires abundance of water supply which was available on\nin these states.\n• Initially, only the financially rich farmers who could afford to buy expensive inputs (chemica\nfertilizers in particular) could reap the benefit of new technology. This further restricted the scope\nof Green Revolution during its first phase.\nPhase 2: Mid-70s -Mid-80s\n• During this phase, the impact of Green Revolution became widespread, covering most areas of th\ncountry and larger number of crops.\n• During second phase, the government provided loans to the small farmers at a very low interest\nrate and subsused tertizers & pestices, making the modern inputs accessible to the smal\nfarmers. As a result, Green Revolution covered the small landholders as well\n• Thus, it was owing to leet by the government that the benets of the Green Revolution\naccrued to the small farmers as well.\n62\nIndian Economy (1950-1990)\n2.3.5 Gains of Green Revolution\nFollowing observations highlight the benefits of Green Revolution:\n(a) Spurt in Crop Productivity: There has been a substantial jump in crop productivity.\n> To illustrate: Productivity of wheat was estimated at 3,559 kg per hectare in 2023-24\ncompared to 660 kg per hectare in 1951; of rice, it was 2,882 kg per hectare in 2023-24\ncompared to 665 kg per hectare in 1951; and of maize, it was 3,351 kg per hectare in 2023-24\ncompared to 704 kg per hectare in 1951.\n> Spurt in productivity has led to a structural shift in Indian agriculture. This has marked the\nend of long period stagnation in Indian agriculture.\n(b) Substantial Rise in Acreage (Area under Cultivation): HYV technology has significantly\nreduced the time lag between sowing and harvesting of crops.\n> Use of chemical fertilizers has eliminated the need for fallowing®. Accordingly, double cropping'\n> In 1950-51, gross area under cultivation was 13 crore hectare which now has shot up to\n22 crore hectare.\n(c) Shift from Subsistence Farming to Commercial Farming: A substantial rise in output (owing to\nincrease in productivity and increase in acreage) has started generating marketable surplus.\n> This has prompted the farmers to gradually shift from subsistence farming to commercial\nfarming. This is a sign of growth and development.\nHIGHLIGHTS\nMarketable Surplus\n• It refers to surplus of farmer's output over and above his 'on-farm consumption'. Thus:\nMarketable Surplus of Wheat = Output of wheat - On-farm consumption of wheat (or expected\nconsumption of wheat by the farmer's family during the year).\nThis surplus is available to the farmer for sale in the market. Hence, called marketable surplus.\n• By selling the marketable surplus he gets cash. He can use this cash to buy producer goods for further\nproduction and also to buy consumer goods for his current consumption.\n• Marketable surplus is a sign of commercialisation of agriculture.\n(d) Change in Farmers' Outlook: Commercialisation of agriculture® has caused a change in outlook\nof the farmers. Farming is no longer viewed as a source of subsistence; it is considered as a\ncommercial venture as well.\n(e) Self-sufficiency in Food Grain Production and Buffer Stocks: Increase in crop production\n(on account of increase in acreage and increase in productivity) led to self-sufficiency in food\ngrain production.\n6. Fallowing - a practice of leaving land as uncultivated for some time so that, it regains its fertility. It was a common\npractice among the Indian farmers prior to the launch of HYV technology.\n7. Double cropping - planting several crops in the same area and in the same crop year.\n8. Commercialisation of agriculture - production of crops for sale in the market rather than for self-consumption.","file":"chap 2.pdf"},{"id":"ied-2-09","image":"ied-pages/ied-2-09.jpg","text":"Indian Economic Development\nso i die to market price or to grains. Act, een po\nsections of the society could comfortably fulfil their subsistence needs.\n* Food grain production increased so substantially that it enabled the government to kee\nbutter stock olose stocks are used to meet demand during lean seasons of farm supple\n• HIGHLIGHTS\nBuffer Stock in India\n• The concept of buffer stock in India was introduced in Fourth Five Year Plan.\n• It refers to reserve of a commodity to be used at the time of emergencies. Government of India maintai\nbuffer stock of food grains to:\n) release food grains through Public Distribution System.\n(il) meet emergency situations like crop failure, or a natural disaster.\n2.3.6 Limitations of Green Revolution\nFollowing observations highlight the limitations of Green Revolution in India:\n(a) Limited Crops: Revolutionary rise in output (due to Green Revolution) was confined mainlyt.\nthe production of food grains (wheat and rice).\n› There has been no similar rise in the production of pulses and commercial crops like jute\ncotton, tea, etc.\n(b) Un-even Spread: Spread of Green Revolution was not uniform across all regions.\n> In states like Punjab, Haryana, Maharashtra and Tamil Nadu, it made a remarkable impac.\n» But in Eastern UP, Bihar, Madhya Pradesh and Odisha, its impact was relatively insignificant.\n(c) Limited Farming Population: The bulk of the farming population in India consists of small and.\n» The gains of Green Revolution eluded these farmers. Because, HY technology required\nexpensive inputs which were beyond the reach of marginal farmers.\nimpressive in terms of overall rise in output.\n(d) Economic Divide: Despite aid by the governent in terms of subsidised inputs, HYV technolog\nremained beyond the reach of thost marginal holders. Bi equently, the gulf between rich\nand poor tended to swell over time.\nfailed to bridge the gap between the rich and the poor in the rural India.\nBriefly, gains of teen keraton were round to be scan, duncant, Green Revotutio\nof the small and margial folders for whom adauz onew technology remained a far cry owing\nto their poverty. Thus, even the revolutionary phase of agricultural expansion failed to achiev\n'Equity'.\n64\nIndian Economy 1950-1990)\n2.3.7 Agricultural Subsidies: Vice or Virtue?\nSpread of HYV technology widely relied upon the supply of subsidised inputs by the government.\nBut, grant of subsidy to the farmers has turned out to be a highly debatable topic. Agricultural subsidies\nare considered both as a vice and a virtue. A brief description of contrary arguments is as under:\nAgricultural Subsidies: A Vice\nArguments against agricultural subsidies are as under:\n(a) Huge Burden on Government Finances: It is argued that once the subsidies have served their\npurpose, implying the technology is proven useful and is adopted widely, subsidies should be\nphased out as they put a huge burden on government finances.\n(b) Misappropriated Benefits: The subsidies not only benefit the farmers, but also the input\nindustry, like fertilizer industry.\n> Among farmers, the benefits of subsidies are mostly reaped by those in prosperous regions.\n› Since, the target beneficiaries are left behind, there is no rationale in continuing with the\nsubsidies.\n(c) Wasteful Consumption: Subsidies do not allow market price to dictate the supply of goods.\nAvailability of supplies at low (subsidised) price leads to wasteful consumption.\nExample:\nIf water is supplied free to the farmers, they would tend to use it unmindful of the fact\nthat water is a scarce resource and needs to be used rationally.\nSimilarly, subsidies on fertilizers and pesticides may lead to their excessive use causing\nsoil and water pollution, and erosion of soil health.\nAgricultural Subsidies: A Virtue\nArguments in support of agricultural subsidies are as under:\n• Agriculture is a risky business in India, therefore, subsidies are needed. It is argued that a large\nnumber of farmers are poor and do not have sufficient means to purchase the required inputs.\nHence, the need for subsidies.\n• If subsidies are eliminated, the inequality between rich and poor farmers will expand, violating\nthe goal of equity (equality). Because, while the rich can reap the benefits of new technology, the\npoor cannot.\nBriefly, we can say the need of the hour is not to lower subsidies, but to ensure that the benefits of\nsubsidies is directed towards the intended beneficiaries.\nAVLR 2.3\nGREEN REVOLUTION -GAINS vs. LIMITATIONS\n9. Subsidy - monetary assistance given by the government to sustain and promote or boost the production activity.\n65","file":"chap 2.pdf"},{"id":"ied-2-08","image":"ied-pages/ied-2-08.jpg","text":"Indian Economic Development\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\nQ.7. What was the basic objective of land reforms initiated by the government?\nAns. Promoting equity (equality) in the ownership of land in the rural economy.\nQ. 8. Enlist the principal elements of HYV technology.\nAns. Use or HYV seeds, chemical termlizers, insecucides and pestcides along with expansion of\nirrigation facilities.\nQ. 9. State the meaning of the term fallowing and enlist its major advantage.\nAns. It refers to the practice of leaving land uncultivated for some time as it helps the soil to regain its\nfertility.\nQ. 10. What is farmer's output over and above his 'on-farm consumption' called?\nAns. Marketable surplus.\nQ. 11. State an argument given by those who denounce agricultural subsidy.\nAns. Agricultural subsidies lead to availability of goods at a lower price which promotes wasteful\nconsumption.\nQ. 12. Features of Indian agriculture reveal the story of its backwardness. Do you agree?\nAns. Yes, it is true that the features of Indian agriculture reveal the story of its backwardness.\nThe notable features are:\n(i) low productivity,\n(ii) small size of holdings,\n(iii) subsistence-oriented farming,\n(iv) existence of disguised unemployment, and\n(v) use of backward technology.\nEach of these features points to the backwardness of Indian agriculture.\nQ. 13. Marginal farmers are subsistence farmers. Do you agree?\nAns. Yes, it is true that marginal farmers are subsistence farmers. Because, the marginal farmers\nare so small farmers that they avoid exposing themselves to market-risks. Accordingly,\nthey focus on subsistence farming-the farming which is not market-oriented, instead it is\nsubsistence-oriented. Only those crops are grown which are needed for self-consumption by the\nfarming families (even when commercial crops could yield good profit).\n2.4 INDUSTRY\n• Sustained GDP growth is possible only when its epicentre lies in the industrial sector.\n• Industries provide employment opportunities that are stable in comparison to those in agricultural\n• Since the colonial rule had left behind a decayed handicraft industry, besides a bleak existence of\nthe large-scale industry (confined mainly to cotton and jute textiles), the planners accorded high\npriority to industrial development through the Five Year Plans.\n• It was also recognised by the planners that industrial development needed a strong infrastructural\nbase requiring a massive investment which could be realised only through a direct and significant\nparticipation of the public sector.\n66\nIndian Economy 1950-1990\n2.4.1 Public and Private Sectors in Indian Industrial Development\n• Along with the need to develop industry as the epicentre of growth, the planners and the politicians\nalso realised that the process of industrialisation could not be left to the market forces of supply\nand demand. Also, it could not be left to the wisdom of private entrepreneurs alone.\nHIGHLIGHTS\nPublic and Private Sectors\n• Public Sector reters to those business enterprises which are controlled and run by the government.\nThese are largely driven by considerations of social welfare.\n• Private Sector refers to those business enterprises which are controlled and run by private individuals.\nThese are largely driven by considerations of profit-maximisation.\n• Direct participation of the state was considered essential in view of the factors discussed below:\nFactors Necessitating Direct Participation of the State\n(Public Sector) in Industrial Development\nLack of Capital\nLow Inducement to Invest\nGrowth with Social Justice\n(a) Lack of Capital: Industrial development in India needed a big push*. Implying a large amount\nof capital expenditure.\n> At the time of independence, Tata and Birla were the only established industrial houses in\nIndia.\n> The requirement of capital for the country's industrial growth was far beyond the capacity\nof these industrial houses.\n> Accordingly, it became essential for the state (or the government) to achieve industrial\ngrowth through public sector undertakings.\n(b) Low Inducement to Invest: The private investors lacked inducement to invest. It was owing to\nlimited size of the market. Limited size of the market was owing to low level of demand. Level\nof demand was low because of low level of income. Thus, there was a sort of vicious circle\noperating like this:\nLow\nLow\ninvestment\nLow\ninducement\nto invest\nLow\ndemand\nLimited\nsize of the\nmarket\n10. Big push - relatively high level of investment which stresses the conditions of take-off.\n67","file":"chap 2.pdf"},{"id":"ied-2-07","image":"ied-pages/ied-2-07.jpg","text":"Indian Economic Development\n› Only a big push of public investment could break this vicious circle. Accordingly, pub\nenterprises were the obvious choice to initiate the process of industrialisation.\njustice was stated as one of the critical objective of Five Year Plans.\n> The government realised that this objective could be achieved only through dire\nparacipation or the state it ine process or inaustrialisaton, becduse il requires investe\nthat generates employment rather than investment that maximises profit.\n> Concentration of wealth was to be discouraged and public investment (rather than private.\ninvestment) was considered as the best means to achieve it.\nHIGHLIGHTS\nControl of Commanding Heights of the Economy\n• Commanding heights of the economy refers to industries of strategic significance. These industries\n(including those like transport and communication) were of strategic significance because they provided\nan infrastructural base for the overall spread of industrialisation in the economy. The government\ndecided to develop these industries as public sector undertakings.\n• The 'commanding heights' also included industries of defence production which, owing to strategic\nneed of country's defence, could not be left in the hands of private entrepreneurs.\n2.4.2 IPR, 1956 (Industrial Policy Resolution, 1956) -\nA Declaration on Leading Role of the State\n• Industrial Policy Resolution, 1956 was a clear declaration of the government on the leading role of\nthe government in the process of industrialisation.\n• This resolution laid a roadmap of the Second Five Year Plan (the plan meant to promote the socialist\npattern of society). Also, it was a milestone of achieving growth with social justice.\nFollowing were the principal elements of IPR, 1956:\n(a) Three-fold Classification of Industries: Industries were classified into three categories:\nThree-fold Classification of Industries\nThose which would be\nestablished and developed\nexclusively as public sector\nenterprises.\nThese include atomic energy,\ndefence and railways.\nThose which could be\nestablished both as the private\nand public sector enterprises.\nHowever, the private sector\nwas to play only a secondary\n(tii)\nOther industries other than\ncategories (i) and (ii).\nAll these were left to the private\nsector.\nrole. New enterprises were to\nbe established primarily in the\npublic sector.\nThis category includes fertilizer\nindustry, mining industry, etc.\n> Thus, IPK, 1756 clearly implied that the state was to spearhead (sponsor) the process o\nindustrialisation in the economy.\n68\nIndian Economy (1950-1990)\n(b) Industrial Licensing: Industries in the private sector could be established only through a\nlicence\" from the government.\n* The basic idea of licensing policy was to encourage industry in backward regions of the\ncountry. It was easier to obtain a license if the industrial unit was located in an economically\nbackward area. This was to promote regional equality.\n› A licence was needed not only for establishing a new enterprise, but also for expanding\nproduction capacity of the existing ones. This was to regulate the allocation of resources to\ndifferent uses. The focus was to promote social welfare rather than private profits.\n(c) Industrial Concessions: The private entrepreneurs were offered many types of industrial\nconcessions for establishing industry in the backward regions of the country. These\nconcessions included:\n> tax holiday (freedom from the payment of tax for sometime), and\n> subsidised power supply.\nLike the policy of industrial licensing, the industrial concessions were expected to promote\nregional equality.\n2.4.3 Small-Scale Industry\nThe process of industrialisation had two broad dimensions, as shown in the following flow chart:\nTwo Major Dimensions of the Process of Industrialisation\nGrowth of large-scale industry (like iron\nand steel, transport and communication)\nto generate infrastructural facilities\nGrowth of small-scale industry to generate\nopportunities of employment, and promote\nequity (social justice)\nThus, small-scale industry was considered as a core element of the process of industrialisation with a\nview to generating the opportunities of employment.\nI. Definition of Small-Scale Industry\n• A small-scale industry is defined with reference to the maximum investment allowed on the\nassets of a unit. This limit has changed over a period of time.\n• According to the Ministry of MSME, a small-scale industry is presently defined as a unit having\nfixed investment of less than & 25 crore with annual turnover of less than & 100 crore.\n• Earlier, it was defined as one whose investment did not exceed & 1 crore.\n• In 1950, a small-scale industrial unit was defined as one whose investment did not exceed\n75 lakh.\n11. Licence - permission granted by a competent authority to engage in a business activity.\n69","file":"chap 2.pdf"},{"id":"ied-2-06","image":"ied-pages/ied-2-06.jpg","text":"Indian Economic Development\nI. Role and Significance\nby Karve Committee (also called Village and Small-scale Industries Committee in 1955.\n• It underlined the significance of small-scale innistries in terms of their three princ\ncharacteristics as under:\nconsidered to be labour-intensive\",\n, while large-scale industry is capital-intensive?\n• To produce a given output, small-scale\nindustry is expected to use more of\nlabour than capital. A large-scale\nindustry, on the other hand, uses\nmore of capital than labour.\n> We inherited an economy from the\nBritish which was a capital-deficient\neconomy 14\nand a labour-surplus\neconomy '15.\n. Accordingly, our planners\nrightly thought of achieving full\nSmall-scale Industry: Labour-intensive\nemployment by focusing on small-scale industry.\n(b) SSI shows Locational Flexibility and is therefore Equality-Oriented (referring to inter-regiona\nequality: Large-scale industry (like Bhilai Steels or Rourkela Steels) is often established close\nto the source of raw material.\nrequirement of the large-scale industry\nis very huge. Its transportation from\na distant place leads to high cost of\ntransportation.\n> SSl, on the other hand, shows\nlocational flexibility. It is therefore\nequality-oriented, contributing\nbalanced regional growth.\nLarge-scale Industry: Capital-intensive\n(c)Ss needs small Investment and is therefore Equity-Oriented (referring to equality acros\ndifferent sections of the society); SSI needs much smaller investment, compared t\narge-scale Industry. Accords not at centration of economic power. Rathe\nit promotes equality across different sections of the society.\n* Equity is turther promoted because of greater employment per unit of investment in S\ncompared to large-scale industry.\n12. Labour-intensive - a process of production requiring more labour (than capital.\n14. Capital-deficient economy - an economy with less of ranital.\n15. Labour-surplus economy - an economy with exce\n70\nIll. Initiative by the Government to Promote SSI\nIndian Economy (1950-1990)\n• Development of small-scale industry requires government support as these industries cannot\ncompete with the big industrial houses. As a result, production of a number of goods was reserved\nfor the small-scale industries based on the capability of these small-scale industries to manufacture\nthose goods.\n• They were also given benefits of lower excise duty and lower interest rates on the bank loans.\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\nQ. 14. Give two examples of industries which are categorised as commanding heights of the\neconomy.\nAns. (i) Transportation, and (ii) Communication.\nQ. 15. What was the main idea behind industrial licensing?\nAns. Provide encouragement to industries in backward regions of the country.\nQ. 16. What is the present turnover limit for any industry to be categorised as a small-scale industry?\nAns. Less than F 50 crore.\nQ. 17. \"Credit Guarantee Scheme (CGS) can only be availed by upcoming and existing micro and small\nenterprises that are engaged in services or manufacturing activities. Agriculture businesses,\ntraining, and educational institutions can also apply for this scheme. Under this scheme, you can\navail a loan of up to & 10 lakh.\"\nSource: https://www.adityabirlacapital.com/abc-of-money/top-6-government-loan-schemes-\nfor-small-businesses\nState one reason why initiatives like the one stated above are taken by the government?\nAns. To promote small-scale industries and reap the benefits associated with the same.\n2.5 TRADE POLICY: IMPORT SUBSTITUTION\n• To complement the efforts of industrial development, India adopted the 'Inward Looking Trade\nStrategy' in the first seven plans (during the period 1950-1990). It is also called 'Strategy of Import\nSubstitution'.\n• Inward looking trade strategy refers to the policy of reliance on 'import substitution' and\nprotection to the domestic industry through import restrictions and import duties in the area of\ninternational trade.\n• This policy aimed at replacing or substituting imports with domestic production.\nNote these points carefully:\n(i) Import substitution implies domestic production of those goods which the economy has been\nimporting from rest of the world. This is a strategy to save foreign exchange by restricting the\nvolume of imports.\n(ii) Foreign exchange (being extremely scarce) was to be utilised for developmental imports\n(referring to import of such plant and machinery which is essential for growth and\ndevelopment but cannot be produced domestically, owing to the lack of technology or the\nlack of investment funds).\n71","file":"chap 2.pdf"},{"id":"ied-2-05","image":"ied-pages/ied-2-05.jpg","text":"Indian Economic Development\n(il) By adopting inward looking trade strategy, the governen preferred to economise the u\nof foreign exchange (through import substitution, rather than maximise the generation o\nforeign exchange (through export promotion).\ncontinue till cost-competitiveness' was achieved.\nHIGHLIGHTS\nImport Substitution and Export Promotion Strategies\n• Import Substitution is a strategy to save foreign exchange by encouraging domestic production of\nsuch goods which the country has been importing from rest of the world. Domestic industry is offered\nprotection from foreign competition through import restrictions and import duties.\n• Export Promotion is a strategy to earn foreign exchange by promoting domestic exports and making\ndomestic industry competitive in the international market.\nForms of Protection\nProtection from imports took two forms:\n• Tariffs: A tariff is a tax on exports and imports, but largely focuses on imports. It makes the\nimported goods more expensive and thereby discourage their use. Thus, it directly increases the\nprice of imported commodities thereby offering protection to the domestic producers from foreign\ncompetition.\n• Quotas: Under it, the government specifies (fixes) the 'quantity' of goods that can be imported from\ndifferent countries. This is yet another way to restrict supplies from rest of the world so that domestic\nsupplies (domestic production) is not adversely impacted.\nAVLR 2.4\nIMPORT SUBSTITUTION AND EXPORT PROMOTION\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\nQ. 18. What are the benefits of imposing tariffs and quotas on imports?\nAns. Imposition of tariffs and quotas restrict imports and in turn protect domestic producers from\nforeign competition.\n9. 1%. Should India rely more on import subsatution rather than 'export promotion' to improve it\nbalance of trade (export - import of goods)?\nAns. In the wake of globalisation when market economies are increasingly shifting to the policy o\nfree trade, and tarins are being moderated) the policy of im tort substitution (supported wit\nprotection to the domestic industry) is no longer a viable opticas india has to rely on the polic\nexport protect. can certainly focus on greate\ndomestic output so that our import bills are reduced. But it would be more through competitio\nrather than the policy of protection (through tariffs).\n16. Foreign exchange - refers to foreign currency.\n72\nIndian Economy (1950-1990)\n2.6 INDIA'S GROWTH STRATEGY DURING THE PERIOD 1950-1990\n2.6.1 Characteristic Features\nA combined view of the above sections of this chapter (sections 2.1 through 2.5) offers certain\ncharacteristic features of India's growth strategy' during the period 1950-1990. These are as under:\n(i) India adopted Mixed Economy and Five Year Plans' as the overall growth model or the overall\nstrategy of growth.\n(ii) Public enterprises were to play a central role in the process of industrialisation.\n(iii) Private enterprises were to play only a secondary role in the process of industrialisation and\nthat too under 'Permit-Licence Raj'\n(iv) Process of industrialisation focused on \"import substitution'. Implying that the production\nof such goods was to be accorded a high priority which were imported from rest of the\n> The idea was to achieve self-reliance as well as to economise the use of foreign exchange.\n(v) As far as possible, domestic industry was to be protected from foreign competition. Protection\nwas to be offered through:\n(a) heavy duty on imports, and\n(b) fixation of import quotas. It was realised that protection would foster the growth of the\ndomestic industry.\n(vi) Large-scale industry was to be developed with a view to building an infrastructural base in the\ncountry.\n(Vil) Small-scale industry was to be developed with a view to achieving the objectives of\nemployment and equity.\n2.6.2 Pros & Cons\nGood and Bad Effect\nGood Effect\nThe strategy of growth during the period 1950-1990 yielded the following good effects:\n(i) Economic growth got a big push with a blend of Green Revolution.\n> Despite a massive increase in agricultural output, the proportion of industrial sector in GDP\nincreased from 13 per cent in 1950-51 to 24.6 per cent in 1990-91 which is a sign of growth\nwith structural change.\n• Industrial output recorded a significant rise. There was about 6 per cent annual increase in\noutput during the period 1950-1990.\n(ii) Indian industry was no longer confined merely to cotton textiles and jute; in fact, the industrial\nsector became well diversified by 1990, largely due to the public sector.\n17. Permit-Licence\nRaj - a system under which private entrepreneurs were to obtain a license for their industrial\nestablishments, and in most cases were to produce goods within the legally prescribed limits of production capacity.\n73","file":"chap 2.pdf"},{"id":"ied-2-04","image":"ied-pages/ied-2-04.jpg","text":"Indian Economic Development\n(ili) Tithee was a marked diversication in the industrial sector the then sunrise industry\n(electronics in particular) marked its emergence in the domestic economy.\n(iN) Growth or large-scale industry like kourkela and Bhilal Steel Plants) projected an infrastructura.\nlarge-scale industrialisation.\n(v) Growth of small-scale industry (5s) made a stostanta contribution in achieving t\nobjectives of growth with socla justice. he devest pment of small-scale industries reme\nentrepreneurial opportunities for the small investors, lacking funds for large enterprises.\nBrietly, the growth-process during the period 1950-1990 showed a noticeable structural shift in the\nSignificance of agricultural sector (in terms of its relative contribution to GDP) started declining in\nfavour of the industrial sector. Goals of Five Year Plans (growth, modernisation, equity and self.\nreliance) started showing green shoots (signs of improvement).\nBad Effect\nBad effects were as significant as good effects. These are as under:\n(i) In due course of time, the planners realised excessive dominance of public sector.\n> Covering the production of certain consumer goods® (which the private sector was well\nequipped to do) was realised as the key element of excessive dominance.\n> It was indeed surprising that even bread and shoes were produced by the public sector\nenterprises. This undermined the significance of private sector as an agent of growth.\n(ii) Public sector monopolies gradually turned out to be a 'dead social weight'. Inefficiency,\ncorruption, leakage and pilferage emerged as their principal characteristics.\n> By incurring huge losses, public sector enterprises led to inefficient use of scarce national\n(iii) The need to obtain license for opening of new firms or expanding the existing firms was\nmisused by big industrial houses.\n> These houses would obtain more and more licenses simply to prevent new competitors\nentering the market. Thus, 'Permit-Licence Raj emerged in the economy that undermined\nthe very spirit of market competition.\n(iv) Protection of domestic industry stimulated its growth. But, it failed to achieve international\nstandards of product-quality.\n* Lack of competition prompted the domestic ert in preneurs to focus upon monopoly-contro\nof the market, Growthrough competton and diversincation was conveniently avoided\n(v) Saving foreign exchange through import substitution (rather than generating it througt\nexport promotion) proved to be an inefficient policy instrument.\n* Our foreign exchange reserves started shrinking, and by the end of 1990, these stocks sank\nto the bottom. So much so that we had to pledge our gold reserves with the World Bank to\nsalvage our borrowings.\n18. Consumer goods - goods directly used for the satisfaction of human wants.\n74\nIndian Economy (1950-1990)\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\nQ. 20. State a bad effect associated with the growth strategy followed by India prior to 1990.\nExcessive dominance of public sector prior to 1990 promoted public sector monopolies. In the\nabsence of market competition, public sector monopolies avoided innovative technology and\n(owing to the lack of accountability) became the breeding centres of corruption, leakage and\nQ. 21. Substantiate the statement that during the period 1950-1990, good effects of the strategy of\nindustrialisation were overshadowed by its bad effects.\nAns. Good effect emerged in terms of the spread of large-scale industry in India. The contribution\nof industrial sector to GDP started rising. But the bad effect was more predominant: the public\nenterprises emerged as the breeding centres of corruption and inefficiency. The private sector\nfailed to diversify. Foreign exchange reserves were reduced to a point of panic. GDP growth\nbecame uncertain. The economy suffered a huge 'trust loss' in the global markets.\nEvaluation of the Strategy of Growth\nFollowing observations are presented as an evaluation of the strategy of growth:\n• As a result of the strategy of growth under Five Year Plans, Indian economy was able to break the\nbarrier of economic stagnation. India became self-sufficient in food grain production.\n• Land reforms and HYV technology turned Indian agriculture from a stagnant and backward sector\ninto a vibrant and growing sector of the economy.\n• The industrial sector was diversified. Infrastructural development became perceptible reality.\nA structural shift in terms of the relative contribution of agricultural and industrial sectors to GDP\nalso became perceptible.\n• But paradoxically, even as the industrial sector grew and the contribution of agricultural sector\nto GDP declined, almost 65 per cent of the population continued to be employed in agricultural\nsector till 1990. Implying that the growth in industrial and service sector was not sufficient to\nabsorb the surplus labour force of the agricultural sector.\n• Spurt in productivity owing to Green Revolution led to a huge backlog of surplus labour force\nin agriculture. But, industrial growth was not enough to absorb that surplus. Consequently, the\nplanners were confronted with a situation of massive rural unemployment in the economy, besides\ngrowing unemployment among the educated youth in the urban areas. This was recognised as one\nof the major limitations of the industrial policy pursued under Five Year Plan.\n• In addition, the excessive regulation and protection from foreign\ncompetition led to\nincompetitiveness and inefficiency of the domestic producers. The protected private sector seldom\nrealised the need for modernisation and diversification.\nWith the passage of time, bad effects of the strategy of industrialisation started overshadowing\nthe good effects. So much so, that the government was compelled to revisit the entire strategy of\neconomic growth. This led to the emergence of NEP (New Economic Policy), the subject matter of","file":"chap 2.pdf"},{"id":"ied-2-03","image":"ied-pages/ied-2-03.jpg","text":"Indian Economic Development\nABILITY ZONE.\nShort Period Objectives during Planning Period\n• 'Short period objectives of planning reter to Plan-wise key Objectives. nese are presented in the\nflow chart as under.\nPlan & Period\nKey Objectives\n1st Plan: April 1, 1951-March 31, 1956\n(i) Increase in agricultural production.\n(i) Equitable distribution of production, income and wealth.\n2nd Plan: April 1, 1956—March 31, 1961\n(i) Increase in industrial production.\n(i) Development of heavy industry.\n3rd Plan: April 1, 1961-March 31, 1966\n(i) Self-sufficiency in food grain production.\n(ii) Generation of employment opportunities.\n(iii) Reduction in inequality\nThree Annual Plans/April 1, 1966-March 31, 1969\n4th Plan: April 1, 1969-March 31, 19/4\n(i) Accelerating the process of growth.\n(ii) Price stability.\n5th Plan: April 1, 1974-March 31, 1979\nRaising the living standards with a focus on weaker sections of the\nsociety.\nAnnual Plan/April 1, 1979-March 31, 1980\n6th Plan: April 1, 1980-March 31, 1985\n(i) Removal of poverty.\n(ii) Reduction of inequality.\n(li) Development of infrastructure.\n7th Plan: April 1, 1985 —March 31, 1990\n(i) Generation of employment opportunities.\n(i) Increase in agricultural productivity.\nTwo Annual Plans/April 1, 1990-March 31, 1992\n8th Plan: April 1, 1992- March 31, 1997\n(i) Fuller utilisation of manpower by the turn of the century.\n(ii) Universalisation of elementary education.\n9th Plan: April 1, 1997-March 31, 2002\n(ili) Strengthening of infrastructure.\n(i) Agricultural and rural development.\n(ii) Growth with price stability.\n10th Plan: April 1, 2002—March 31,\n(iii) Checking the growth of population.\n(i) Improving the quality of life through better health and\n11th Plan: April 1, 2007-March 31,\neducational facilities and improved levels of consumption.\n(i) Reduction in inequality through inclusive growth.\n(i) Multple targets covering not only growth but also poverty reduction.\n(i) Improving quality of education and public health services.\n(ii) Strategy of second green revolution.\n(iv) Generating high quality of job.\n12th Plan: April 1, 2012— March 31, 2017\n(v) Protection of environment.\nFaster, sustainable and more inclusive growth.\nNITI Aayog\n• The National Institution for Transformingesda, also called NIT Aayog was established o\n76\nJanuary 1, 2015 to replace the Planning Commission. It is a policy think tank' of the Government of Indi\nIndian Economy (1950-1990)\nand is engaged in designing strategic and long-term policies and programmes' related to the growth\nand development of the Indian economy. The Prime Minister of India serves as the Chairperson of the\ninstitution.\nFive Principal Objectives/Functions\n• Principal objectives/functions of NITI Aayog are as under:\n(i) Policy Formulation: Formulation of long period strategies of growth is the principal function of\nNITI Aayog. It is also called a 'think tank' function of NITI Aayog.\n(ii) Monitoring and Evaluation: Implementation of the policies and programmes is continuously monitored\nand evaluated, and expert advice is periodically offered to the central and state governments.\nNITI Aayog also monitors and evaluates implementation of key projects of the government like\n'Make in India', Digital India', Swachh Bharat Programme', etc.\n(iii) Cooperative and Competitive Federalism: NITI Aayog designs a 'national agenda' for the Prime\nMinister representing central government, and the chief ministers representing state governments.\nIt thus focuses on cooperative federalism' by offering a common platform for the central and\nstate governments to work together. Further, it promotes competitive federalism by ranking the\nperformance of different states on key indices of growth and development.\n(iv) Technology Upgradation and Capacity Building: NITI Aayog is actively engaged in technology\nupgradation and capacity building at the national and regional levels.\n(v) Promoting Equity: It focuses on achieving inclusive growth, ensuring that the benefits of growth\ntrickle across all sections of the society.\n'Growth' and 'Development' though related are different Concepts\n• Growth refers to a situation of sustained rise in GDP over a long period of time. It leads to a rise in\naverage standard of living of the people.\n• Development refers to growth with equity as well as structural change in the economy.\n• Equity implies equitable distribution of income, so that the benefits of growth are shared by all sections\nof the society.\n• Structural change implies a shift in the sectoral share in GDP: Share of secondary and tertiary sectors\nshould gradually rise and that of primary sector should gradually fall.\n•It implies a situation when secondary and tertiary sectors emerge as the key contributors to the\nGDP growth. It does not mean that primary sector (or the agricultural sector) is neglected. It only means\nthat the epicentre of GDP growth shifts from the primary sector to the secondary and tertiary sectors.\nParadox of Saving and Investment\n• Although during the planning period there has been appreciable increase in saving and investment, yet\nthe growth rate of economy has been very slow. Several factors account for this paradox:\n(i) Capital-output ratio is very high in India. It is around 4: 1 and has been rising over time. Because\nof this high ratio, increase in investment is followed by relatively less increase in production.\n(il) Considerable part of investment is in the form of buffer-stocks of food grains and not in the form\nof fixed capital formation. No wonder, despite the increase in the rate of investment there is no\ncorresponding increase in production.\n(ili) Large portion of investment is made in traditional sector with backward technology, leading to low\nproductivity.\nThe Concept of Interventionist State\n• 'Interventionist State' refers to the system in which the state intervenes to regulate and control the forces\nof the market (the forces of supply and demand and the consequent relative price structure) in a manner\nsuch that the production of essentials of life and their delivery to the relatively poorer sections of the\nsociety is not hurt.","file":"chap 2.pdf"},{"id":"ied-2-02","image":"ied-pages/ied-2-02.jpg","text":"Indian Economic Development\n• In case the state does not intervene, the producers will tend to allocate resources only to the products,\nmaximising their profits. Accordingly, growth may occur, but not the development.\n• Development occurs only when the benefits of growth reach the poorer sections of the society and whe\nthe gulf between the rich and the poor is reduced.\nYet there are Starvation Deaths\n• Green Revolution in indian economy has brought about a tremendous breakthrough in food gi\nDomestic production is estimated to be enough tor domestic consumption. here has been\nproduction. India no longer needs to import food grains.\nexplosive rise in the countrys pot, is a t a pred ete\nare starvation deaths in the country.\n• How do we explain this phenomenon? This is explained in terms of two factors:\n(1) Gross inequalities in the distribution of income. So much so that the country's 21.9 per cer\npopulation is below poverty line, not having enough means to buy even the essentials of life.\n(i) Ineffective PDS (Public Distribution System). No logic of the world can explain why food is no\nreaching the poor even when the state is incurring huge cost on the storage of millions of tone.\nof food grains for PDS.\nOur Record Relating to Distribution has been Grossly Dismal (Disappointing)\n(i) Distribution of Income: Politicians can make a tall claim that over time, the distribution of income\nhas improved or it has become more equitable. This claim is founded on two statistical facts:\n(a) over time the percentage of taxpayers in total population has increased, and\n(b) over time, the percentage of population below the poverty line has reduced.\n- But there is another statistical fact of equal significance. It is that the total number of those below\npoverty line and the number of non-tax payers has tended to increase over time.\n- Thus, the number of poor people in India is surging over time which is a poor reflection on ou\nachievements with regard to distribution of income.\n(i) Distribution of Consumption Expenditure: Of the total private consumption expenditure, the\nin the urban areas has tended to decline.\nshare of bottom 40 per cent in the rural areas has tended to rise, but the share of bottom 80 per cer\n-Thus, While roll inequality of consumption expenditure has improved, urban inequality o\nconsumption expenditure has worsened over time.\ntotal ta cat improved the distribution o\n(a) Abolite else the cars ustave on extortions from the farmers\n0) imposition or celling on te hole and redistributon the surplus land to the small an\nmarginal farmers.\n- But simultaneously we must not lose sight or the fact that over time tassize of holding has tende\nto shrink which has impeded the process ores snogical improvements in agriculture.\nDistribution dust Weal Dist wat has tended to be gros\nunequal as is evident from the following observations:\nand Godrei) who are controlling the bulk of industrial wealth in the country\n- in 2003-04, it was found that the top most six industrial houses controlled nearly 60 per cent of\nthe assets of the top 20 industrial houses.\n78\nIndian Economy (1950-1990)\nPower Points (Summary of the Chapter]\nEconomic System: Economic system refers to economic order in the country.\nTypes of Economies:\n• Capitalist Economy is the one in which there is a private ownership of the means of production.\nMaximisation of profit is the principal objective.\n• Socialist Economy is the one in which there is a social (collective/public) ownership of the means of\nproduction. Maximisation of social welfare is the principal objective.\n• Mixed Economy is the one in which means of production are owned by the private entrepreneurs as\nwell as the government.\n• Economic Planning: Economic planning refers to a system under which a central authority sets a set\nof targets and specifies a set of programmes and policies to achieve those targets with in the specified\nperiod of time.\nPlan: A plan lays down how the limited resources of an economy should be put to use to achieve precise\nobjectives within a specified period of time.\nGoals of Planning: (i) GDP growth, (ii) Modernisation, (iii) Self-reliance and self-sufficiency, (iv) Equitable\ndistribution or equity.\nAgriculture: Agriculture included a series of land reforms: (i) Abolition of intermediaries, (ii) Ceiling on\nland holdings, (iii) Consolidation of holdings, (iv) Regulation of rent, (v) Cooperative farming.\nTechnical Reforms- Green Revolution: It refers to a spurt in farm output during mid 60's. Measures\nintroduced as part of Green Revolution are:\n(i) Use of HYV seeds\n(ii) Use of chemical fertilizers\n(iii) Use of insecticides and pesticides for crop protection\n(iv) Expansion of irrigation facilities\nGains of Green Revolution:\n(i) Spurt in crop productivity\n(il) Substantial rise in acreage (area under cultivation)\n(iii) Shift from subsistence farming to commercial tarming\n(iv) Change in farmers' outlook\n(V) Self-sufficiency in food grain production and buffer stocks\nLimitations of Green Revolution:\n(1) Limited crops\n(ii) Un-even spread\n(Iii) Limited farming population\n(iv) Economic divide\nAgricultural Subsidies: Instrumental in encouraging adoption of new technology that is deemed risky by\nthe farmers. Limitations of agricultural subsidies are:\n(1) Huge burden on government finances\n(ii) Misappropriated benefits\n(ili) Wasteful consumption\n79","file":"chap 2.pdf"},{"id":"ied-2-01","image":"ied-pages/ied-2-01.jpg","text":"Indian Economic Development\ntacts:\n(i) There was lack of capital with private entrepreneurs.\n(i) Private entrepreneurs lacked inducement to invest, owing to limited size of the market.\n(i) There was a growing realisation that the principal objective of planning (growth with social justice)\ncould be achieved only through direct intervention by the state.\nIndustrial Policy Resolution, 1956 was an official declaration on the leading role of the government\nin the process of industrialisation. Private sector was assigned only a secondary role in the process of\nindustrialisation. Industries in the private sector could be established only through a licence from the\ngovernment.\nSmall-Scale Industries (SSI): Small-scale industries were accorded high priority with a view to\npromoting the goals of employment and equity.\nThree Important Characteristics of SSI:\n(i) These industries are more labour-intensive than the large-scale industries. Accordingly, the\nplanners relied on SSI for generating employment opportunities in the economy.\n(ii) SSI shows locational flexibility enabling them to achieve inter-regional equality.\n(iii) SSI needs small investment and therefore, do not cause concentration of wealth; instead, they\npromote equity.\nInward Looking Trade Strategy: It refers to the policy of import substitution' and protection to the\ndomestic industry through import restrictions and import duties.\nSalient Features of Growth Strategy during 1950-1990:\n(i) Public sector was to play a central role in the process of industrialisation.\n(i) Private sector was to play a secondary role, and that too under Permit-License Raj.\n(il) Process of industrialisation was to proceed in tandem with the key policy instrument of import\nsubstitution.\n(iv) Domestic industry was to be protected from foreign competition.\n(v) Large-scale industry was to be developed with a view to generating infrastructural base in the\n(vi) Small-scale industry was to be developed to promote the objectives of employment and equity.\nGood Effect of our Growth Strategy' is highlighted in terms of the following achievements:\nsubstantial spread across different regions of the country.\nstress to sewere successtully launched, (i Ssi recorded a\nBad Effect of our Growth Strategy Is highlighted ecords of the following observations\n(I) Protection shielded the domestic inaustry, but rendered it leefficient to cope with international\n(i) Public sector monopolies started causing dead social weight.\ncompetition.\n(iii) Relying upon import substitution, the forex (foreign exchange) res\ndwindling.\n80\nIndian Economy (1950-1990)\nWORK-BOOK [Question-answer Format]\nCompetency Based Learning: Objective Questions\nI. Multiple Choice Questions\nChoose the correct option:\n1. In the post-independence era, the policy makers of India pushed for 'self-reliance' for the first\nFive Year Plans.\n[CBSE 2025 (58/4/1), (58/5/1)]\n(b) 7\n(c) 6\nd) 5\n2. \"The Indian Government adopted a policy of fixing maximum land holding limit (ownership) for\nan individual.\"\nIt is commonly known as\n(a) Green Revolution\n[CBSE 2025 (58/6/1)]\n(b) Land Ceiling\n(c) Marketable Surplus\n(d) Golden Revolution\n3. Modernisation is an important economic planning objective that focuses on\n(i) Adoption of innovative technology\nCBSE 2025 (58/7/1)]\n(ii) Bringing positive change in social outlook\n(iii) Equal distribution of income and wealth (iv) Abolition of intermediaries\nOptions:\n(a) (i) and (iv)\n(b) (i) and (ill)\n(c) (i), (iii) and (iv)\n(d) (i) and (ii)\n4. In the post-independence era, the policymakers of India adopted an economic system that\nfundamentally implies the coexistence of.\nand\n[CBSE Sample Paper 2025]\n(a) small and large-scale industries\n(b) small and medium-scale industries\n(c) public and private sector\n(d) private and foreign sector\n5. Modernisation is an important economic planning objective that focuses on\n[CBSE 2024 (58/2/1)]\nI. Adoption of innovative technology\nIl. Equal distribution of income and wealth\nII. Bringing positive changes in the social outlook\nAlternatives:\n(b) land I!\n(a) Only I\n(d) I, Il and I!!\n(c) I and Ill\n6. After independence, the rationale behind choosing modernisation planning objectives for the\n[CBSE 2024(58/5/1)]\nIndian economy were\nI. To bring positive changes in the social outlook\nII. Equitable distribution of income\nIII. Technological upgradation\nIV. Increase in economic divide\nAlternatives:\n(a) | and ||\n(b)ll and !!\nd) I and IV\n(c) 1 and III\n81","file":"chap 2.pdf"}],"boxes":[{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-2-15","image":"ied-pages/ied-2-15.jpg","text":"• 'From each according to his capacity, and to each according to his need' is the directive principle of a\nsocialist economy.\nIll. Merit\n• Socialist economy achieves 'equitable distribution of income, because allocation of resources is\nbased on the principle of social justice?.\nIV. Demerit\n• The principal demerit is that unlike a capitalist economy, a socialist economy does not allow\n'consumers' sovereignty'.\n• Consumers don't have choices and their needs are decided by the government Also berauce\nproduction is not profit-oriented, GDP growth remains a slow process.\n• It is owing to a high degree of public discontent (related to the lack of consumers' sovereigntv)\nand slow GDP growth, that the erstwhile socialist economice like Soviet Union and china\nhad to switch over to more 'market based' economic order, often termed as Sprise Market\nEconomy.\n2. Social lustice - is achieved when the process of economic growth leads to be able distribution of income and\n52\nIndian Economy (1950-1990)\n(c) Mixed Economy\nI. Meaning\n• A mixed economy is the one in which there is private as well\nas public ownership of the means of production.\n• Production decisions are governed largely by the principle of\nprofit maximisation, but not without checks and balances of\nsocial justice.\nIl. Features\nMixed Economy\nThe three salient features of a mixed economy are as under:\n(i) Means of production are owned by the private entrepreneurs as well as the government.\n(ii) In the private sector, production decisions are governed by the principle of profit maximisation,\nwhile in the public sector, social welfare predominates.\n(iii) Both private and public sectors play a significant role in the process of production.\nIll. Merit\n• The principal merit of a mixed economy is that it combines the merits of capitalist as well as a\nsocialist economy.\n• On the one hand, GDP growth is encouraged because private entrepreneurs are free to focus on\n'profit maximisation'.\n• On the other hand, 'social justice' or equality is promoted because the government sector places\nhigh priority on the maximisation of social welfare.\nIV. Demerit\n• The principal demerit is that the government sector is often inflicted with corruption, leading to low\nlevel of efficiency/productivity.\n• It is owing to this demerit that the mixed economies (like India) are gradually opting for privatisation\nof the public enterprises.\n• It may be noted that most economies of the world are now mixed economies where production\ndecisions are governed largely by the market forces of demand and supply, but are regulated by\nway of direct or indirect intervention by the government.\nMixed Economic System - An Ideal System of Growth\n• Leaders and planners of independent India sought mixed economic system as an ideal strategy of\ngrowth, avoiding the extreme versions of capitalism and socialism.\n• The Industrial Policy Resolution of 1948 and the Directive Principles of the Indian Constitution\nreflected the outlook of the planners that India would adopt an economic order where there was\na strong public sector but with full respect to private property and democracy.\n• The public sector would take a leading role and private sector would be encouraged\n(and incentivised) to be an active participant in the process of growth and development."},{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"ied-2-14","image":"ied-pages/ied-2-14.jpg","text":"Q. 1. What dominates the process of decision-making under free play of market forces?\nAns. Maximisation of profit.\nQ. 2. \"Capitalist economy ignores the collective interest of the society.\" Comment.\nAns. True. Because resource allocation, production and prices are determined by the market forces\nof supply and demand, oblivious (unaware) of collective interest of the society or basic needs of\nthe poor (food, clothing and education).\n'The government of the United Kingdom controls the entire healthcare system through the\nNHS. Their health system is fully socialized, where the government pays the salaries of doctors.\nHowever, the UK also has a thriving private sector. In 2016, the UK had over 3 million private-sector\nbusinesses. Its capital city, London, is a financial hub of the world and hosts one of the largest\nglobal stock exchanges, the London Stock Exchange (LSE).\"\nSource: https://helpfulprofessor.com/mixed-economy-examples/\nWhat can be said about the choice of economic system in UK from the case presented above?\nAns. It follows a mixed economic model."},{"title":"[Note: Description related to 'NITI Aayog' is provided in the Ability Zone.]","page":"ied-2-14","image":"ied-pages/ied-2-14.jpg","text":"3. Perspective plan - long-term planning where targets are fixed for a long period say 15 to 25 years.\nIndian Economy (1950-1990)\nP.C. Mahalanobis\n• Prashant Chandra Mahalanobis is known as the Architect of Indian Planning.\n• An eminent statistician, he made a landmark contribution to Indian Planning during the\nSecond Five Year Plan when he developed a model of industrial investment towards\nbuilding up a domestic consumption goods sector.\n• Mahalanobis made significant contribution to the field of Statistics in India. He\nestablished Indian Statistical Institute (ISI) in Calcutta, and started a journal, Sankhya,\nwhich is a respectable forum for statisticians to discuss their ideas.\nNeed for Planning in India\n• India inherited from the British a backward and stagnant economy. It was backward as the level of\noutput and productivity were low. And, it was stagnant as the GDP growth was dismal (extremely low).\n• Such an economy could not be left to the market forces of supply and demand to trace its path of growth\nand development. It needed a big push of investment, supported by the government. Hence, the recourse\nto economic planning.\n2.2.2 Common Goals of Five Year Plans\nGoals of planning are common to all the Five Year Plans and are therefore generally studied as\nCommon Goals of Five Year Plans or Objectives of Planning.\n• It may be noted that depending on ground realities, each plan is designed as a distinct plan with\na distinct set of objectives. However, according to syllabus specifications by the CBSE we are\nfocussing only on Common Goals of Five Year Plans."},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-2-14","image":"ied-pages/ied-2-14.jpg","text":"Why should Plans have Goals?\n• Without goals, there is no planning. In fact, planning is defined as a strategy that defines how to allocate\nthe country's scarce resources to different uses with a view to achieving a given set of goals.\n• These goals often relate to growth and social justice.\nCommon Goals of Five Year Plans are as under:\nCommon Goals of Five Year Plans\n(a)\nGrowth\nModernisation\nSelf-reliance and\nEquity or Equitable\nSelf-sufficiency\n(a) Growth: It refers to increase in the country's capacity to produce goods and services within\nthe country. It implies either a larger stock of productive capital, or a larger size of supporting\nservices (like transport and banking) or an increase in the efficiency of productive capital.\n• Increase in GDP (Gross Domestic Product) is a good indicator of economic growth. Increase\nin GDP implies increase in the level of output in the economy. It implies an increase in the\nflow of goods and services in the economy.\n• When the increase in the flow of goods and services is consistent over a long period of\ntime, it is called 'economic growth'. Thus, GDP growth leads to economic growth."},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-2-13","image":"ied-pages/ied-2-13.jpg","text":"Structural Composition and Structural Change\n• Concepts of structural composition and structural change assume significance in the context of GDP\n• The contribution of each economic sector to the GDP of a country makes up the structural composition\nof the economy. In India, the agriculture and allied sector contributes 17.94 per cent to GDP, the\nindustrial sector 27.13 per cent, and the service sector 54.93 per cent to the GDP of India.\n• As a country develops, the contribution of each economic sector to GDP changes, which is referred\nto as 'structural change'. Usually with development, the share of agriculture declines while share of\nindustry increases. At higher level of development, the share of industry declines paving way for higher\ncontribution by the service sector. However, in case of India, the shift has been from agriculture sector\nto service sector directly. At the time of independence, agriculture had highest share in GDP, around\n50 percent. By 1990, the share of service sector rose to 40.59 percent, more than that of agriculture\nand industrial sector.\n(b) Modernisation: It refers to updating and adoption of modern (or new) technology in the\nprocess of growth. Output can be increased either by increasing the pool of resources or by\nusing innovative technology.\n> Modern age is the age of science and innovations. Science has offered us new ways of\ndoing things, such that productivity in farms and factories has shown an exponential rise\nover time.\n› Green Revolution in Indian agriculture is a well-known example of how technology can\nbring about revolutionary changes in output. Recently, IT revolution has redefined the\nconcept of domestic production through BPO (Business Process Outsourcing).\n» However, modernisation in the context of goals of plans in India, was assigned a social\nangle as well. Here, it refers to changes in social outlook, focusing on 'Gender Equality'.\nIndian Economy (1950-1990)\n› The planners were of the strong opinion that conventional wisdom (wisdom without a valid\nreason) must give way to modern outlook. It included issues like empowerment of women\nso that (like men) they also could participate in the process of production and contribute\nto the process of economic and social prosperity."},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-2-13","image":"ied-pages/ied-2-13.jpg","text":"Modernisation and Employment Generation: Complementary or Contradictory?\n• Modernisation as a planning objective was not expected to contradict with employment generation.\n• Both (modernisation and employment generation) as goals of planning were taken as complementary to\neach other. The complementarity between the two was expected to work as under:\n- Modernisation leads to increase in productivity.\n- Increase in productivity leads to higher level of production activity and higher level of income in the\neconomy. Implying, a rising demand for goods and services in the economy.\n- Expansion of demand leads to expansion of employment opportunities.\n• However, of late the nature of growth process has taken a dramatic turn. Technology has emerged\nas the principal driver of growth process. And, the nature of technology is such that it is substituting\nlabour rather than supplementing it. Consequently, 'jobless growth is emerging to be challenge for the\npoliticians and the planners.\n• Thus, even when 'modernisation' and 'employment generation' were taken as complementary objectives\n(under Five Year Plans), the dichotomy between the two is an emerging challenge.\n(c) Self-reliance and Self-sufficiency: Self-reliance means the state of non-dependence on rest of\nthe world for the financial resources. Self-sufficiency refers to the state of non-dependence\nupon rest of the world for the essential supplies in the domestic economy.\n> This twin goal (of self-reliance and self-sufficiency) was accorded a high priority during the\nfirst seven plans to promote economic growth and modernisation.\n› The basic idea was not to expose Indian economy to political pressures from rest of the\nworld when we are dependent upon them for the supply of essential goods.\n> Indeed, it happened in 1965, when USA threatened to stop exports of food grains to India\nif it did not stop the then war with Pakistan. India wanted to be self-sufficient in food grain\nproduction to avoid the uncertainties of supplies from rest of the world."},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-2-13","image":"ied-pages/ied-2-13.jpg","text":"• It was necessary for India to achieve self-sufficiency in food grain production. Otherwise, we had to yield\nto USA for the imports of food grains on the specified terms and conditions (which could undermine our\npolitical sovereignty).\n• Example: India was pestered to end war with Pakistan (1965) for favour of food grain supplies from USA.\n(d) Equity or Equitable Distribution: Economic growth would become a meaningless exercise if\nthe benefits of growth accrue to only a handful of people in the society. Note the following\nthree observations carefully:\n4. Jobless growth - a situation when the level of output in the economy tends to rise owing to innovative technology\nwithout any perceptible rise in the level of employment.\n57"},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-2-12","image":"ied-pages/ied-2-12.jpg","text":"Equal Distribution and Equitable Distribution\n• Equal distribution would mean every individual in the society gets the same share in the country's\nnational income. It would imply a situation when a doctor gets the same salary as a clerk in the hospital.\nNo society would ever seek a situation like this.\n• Equitable distribution, on the other hand, refers to a situation when differences in income are allowed\nbut only within certain limits.\n- These differences are socially unwarranted and are to be proportionate to the differences in\nqualifications and skills of different individuals in the society.\n- When a doctor gets ‹ 50,000 to perform a surgery (lasting for 1 hour while, on the other hand, a\nfactory worker gets ‹ 50,000 for the entire year (working 8 hours a day), the difference in income is\ntoo large to be socially justified. Such differences (which are socially unwarranted) must be corrected.\n- 'Equity' (in terms of equitable distribution of income) implies that economic growth is related to social\njustice.\n- It is only when economic growth is related to social justice that 'growth' is converted into 'development.\nPlanning in India focuses on 'growth with social justice.\nDepending upon our needs and means, our planners have been redefining the relative significance of\ndifferent objectives from plan to plan.\nOn the whole, the focus has been on achieving better quality of life across all sections of the society,\nnow popularly known as 'Inclusive Growth.\nConclusion\n• Mixed economy was our model of growth after independence.\n• Public sector was to play the flagship role in the growth and development of the country.\nThe growth process was to be pursued according to the specified targets, plan after plan.\n) AVLR 2.2\nCOMMON GOALS OF FIVE YEAR PLANS IN INDIA\n5. Inclusive growth - economic growth that is distributed fairly across society and creates opportunities for all."},{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"ied-2-12","image":"ied-pages/ied-2-12.jpg","text":"Q. 4. Why is equitable distribution of income an essential component of long-term objectives?\nAns. Growth serves no purpose until benefits of growth are spread across all sections of society.\nQ. 5. How is growth different from the term 'development'?\nAns. Growth combined with social justice becomes development.\nQ. 6. State two ways of increasing the GDP.\nAns. Two ways of increasing the GDP are as under:\n(i) Discovery of more and more resources in the country that increases resource-base of the\ncountry.\n(ii) Innovative technology, that enhances productivity or output per unit of input."},{"title":"[Note: The following section highlights the condition of various sectors of Indian economy, viz., agriculture, industry","page":"ied-2-12","image":"ied-pages/ied-2-12.jpg","text":"and trade. It reflects the impact of policy formulations and reforms initiated to redefine, restructure and uplift\nthe Indian economy during the period 1950-1990.]"},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-2-11","image":"ied-pages/ied-2-11.jpg","text":"Basic Objective of Land Reforms\n• The government pursued land reforms with the basic objective of promoting equity (equality) in the rural\neconomy via promoting the land to tiller' policy.\n• It was to be achieved by restoring ownership rights of the tillers of the soil and by eliminating the class\nof intermediaries (zamindars) so as to stop exploitation of the actual cultivators of the soil.\n(b) Ceiling on Land Holdings: With a view to promoting equity (equality) in the distribution of\nland, ceiling were imposed on the holding-size.\n> It implies fixation of the maximum size of cultivable land that an individual or a family could\n> Land ceiling aims at reducing the concentration of land ownership in a few hands.\n> The surplus land (over and above the ceiling limit) was resumed by the government and\nredistributed among small holders or landless labourers.\n(c) Consolidation of Holdings: With a view to reducing fragmentation, consolidation of holdings\nwas accorded a high priority.\n* Consolidation is a practice to allot land to the farmer at one place for his scattered holdings\nhere and there. It saves the cost of cultivation.\n> By 2004, more than 1,633 lakh hectares of land was brought under consolidated holdings.\n(d) Regulation of Rent: To put an end to excessive and illegal extortions from the cultivators,\nrents were regulated. Generally, these were not to exceed 1/3rd of the value of crop.\n(e) Cooperative farming: Cooperative farming was promoted to enhance bargaining power of\nthe small holders. Collectively, the farmers could buy inputs at a lower price and sell their\nproduce at a higher price.\n60\nIndian Economy (1950-1990)\nHurdles in the Implementation of Land Reforms\nWhile land reforms were successfully pursued in the states of West Bengal and Kerala, some serious\nhurdles were experienced in other parts of the country, owing to the following reasons:\n(i) The zamindars managed to evict the tenants and project themselves as owners of the soil.\n(i) A sizeable section of the landless labourers were kept out of the ambit of ownership rights.\n(iii) The big landlords challenged the land ceiling legislation in courts which delayed/stalled the\nimplementation of the reforms.\nThis delay was used by the landlords to register their surplus land in the name of their close\nrelatives, escaping the Ceiling Act'\n2.3.3 Technical Reforms: HYV Technology and Green Revolution\n• The problem of low productivity\nwas tackled by way of technical\nreforms.\n• Technical reforms evolved around\nHYV (High Yielding Variety) seeds.\nUse of HY seeds along with\nchemical fertilizers, insecticides\nand pesticides led to substantial\nrise in the level of agricultural\nGreen Revolution: A Revolutionary Rise in Productivity\noutput. The rise in output was so significant that it was called 'Green Revolution'.\n• The term 'Green' refers to 'crops' and 'Revolution' refers to 'spurt' (significant rise). Together,\nthese terms imply a spurt in crop production (especially for wheat and rice). It started happening\nin India in the year 1967-68.\n• In the year 1967-68 itself, food grain production increased by nearly 25 per cent.\n• India became self-sufficient in the production of food grains and successfully broke the vicious\ncycle of agricultural stagnation. rucion of roo grains and succestly broke the vicious\nElements of HYV Technology\nHYV technology includes four principal elements, as under:\n(a) Use of HYV Seeds: Since 1965, High\nYielding Variety seeds started replacing\nthe conventional varieties.\n> HYV seeds gained popularity\nwheat, bajra, rice, maize, jowar, and\ncotton, in particular.\n> It is these seeds\nwhich led to a\nsubstantial rise in agricultural output.\n(b) Use of Chemical Fertilizers: Use of HYV\nHigh Yielding Variety Seeds and High Crop Productivity\nseeds requires the use of fertilizers.\n61"},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-2-10","image":"ied-pages/ied-2-10.jpg","text":"Marketable Surplus\n• It refers to surplus of farmer's output over and above his 'on-farm consumption'. Thus:\nMarketable Surplus of Wheat = Output of wheat - On-farm consumption of wheat (or expected\nconsumption of wheat by the farmer's family during the year).\nThis surplus is available to the farmer for sale in the market. Hence, called marketable surplus.\n• By selling the marketable surplus he gets cash. He can use this cash to buy producer goods for further\nproduction and also to buy consumer goods for his current consumption.\n• Marketable surplus is a sign of commercialisation of agriculture.\n(d) Change in Farmers' Outlook: Commercialisation of agriculture® has caused a change in outlook\nof the farmers. Farming is no longer viewed as a source of subsistence; it is considered as a\ncommercial venture as well.\n(e) Self-sufficiency in Food Grain Production and Buffer Stocks: Increase in crop production\n(on account of increase in acreage and increase in productivity) led to self-sufficiency in food\ngrain production.\n6. Fallowing - a practice of leaving land as uncultivated for some time so that, it regains its fertility. It was a common\npractice among the Indian farmers prior to the launch of HYV technology.\n7. Double cropping - planting several crops in the same area and in the same crop year.\n8. Commercialisation of agriculture - production of crops for sale in the market rather than for self-consumption."},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-2-09","image":"ied-pages/ied-2-09.jpg","text":"Buffer Stock in India\n• The concept of buffer stock in India was introduced in Fourth Five Year Plan.\n• It refers to reserve of a commodity to be used at the time of emergencies. Government of India maintai\nbuffer stock of food grains to:\n) release food grains through Public Distribution System.\n(il) meet emergency situations like crop failure, or a natural disaster.\n2.3.6 Limitations of Green Revolution\nFollowing observations highlight the limitations of Green Revolution in India:\n(a) Limited Crops: Revolutionary rise in output (due to Green Revolution) was confined mainlyt.\nthe production of food grains (wheat and rice).\n› There has been no similar rise in the production of pulses and commercial crops like jute\ncotton, tea, etc.\n(b) Un-even Spread: Spread of Green Revolution was not uniform across all regions.\n> In states like Punjab, Haryana, Maharashtra and Tamil Nadu, it made a remarkable impac.\n» But in Eastern UP, Bihar, Madhya Pradesh and Odisha, its impact was relatively insignificant.\n(c) Limited Farming Population: The bulk of the farming population in India consists of small and.\n» The gains of Green Revolution eluded these farmers. Because, HY technology required\nexpensive inputs which were beyond the reach of marginal farmers.\nimpressive in terms of overall rise in output.\n(d) Economic Divide: Despite aid by the governent in terms of subsidised inputs, HYV technolog\nremained beyond the reach of thost marginal holders. Bi equently, the gulf between rich\nand poor tended to swell over time.\nfailed to bridge the gap between the rich and the poor in the rural India.\nBriefly, gains of teen keraton were round to be scan, duncant, Green Revotutio\nof the small and margial folders for whom adauz onew technology remained a far cry owing\nto their poverty. Thus, even the revolutionary phase of agricultural expansion failed to achiev\n'Equity'.\n64\nIndian Economy 1950-1990)\n2.3.7 Agricultural Subsidies: Vice or Virtue?\nSpread of HYV technology widely relied upon the supply of subsidised inputs by the government.\nBut, grant of subsidy to the farmers has turned out to be a highly debatable topic. Agricultural subsidies\nare considered both as a vice and a virtue. A brief description of contrary arguments is as under:\nAgricultural Subsidies: A Vice\nArguments against agricultural subsidies are as under:\n(a) Huge Burden on Government Finances: It is argued that once the subsidies have served their\npurpose, implying the technology is proven useful and is adopted widely, subsidies should be\nphased out as they put a huge burden on government finances.\n(b) Misappropriated Benefits: The subsidies not only benefit the farmers, but also the input\nindustry, like fertilizer industry.\n> Among farmers, the benefits of subsidies are mostly reaped by those in prosperous regions.\n› Since, the target beneficiaries are left behind, there is no rationale in continuing with the\nsubsidies.\n(c) Wasteful Consumption: Subsidies do not allow market price to dictate the supply of goods.\nAvailability of supplies at low (subsidised) price leads to wasteful consumption.\nExample:\nIf water is supplied free to the farmers, they would tend to use it unmindful of the fact\nthat water is a scarce resource and needs to be used rationally.\nSimilarly, subsidies on fertilizers and pesticides may lead to their excessive use causing\nsoil and water pollution, and erosion of soil health.\nAgricultural Subsidies: A Virtue\nArguments in support of agricultural subsidies are as under:\n• Agriculture is a risky business in India, therefore, subsidies are needed. It is argued that a large\nnumber of farmers are poor and do not have sufficient means to purchase the required inputs.\nHence, the need for subsidies.\n• If subsidies are eliminated, the inequality between rich and poor farmers will expand, violating\nthe goal of equity (equality). Because, while the rich can reap the benefits of new technology, the\npoor cannot.\nBriefly, we can say the need of the hour is not to lower subsidies, but to ensure that the benefits of\nsubsidies is directed towards the intended beneficiaries.\nAVLR 2.3\nGREEN REVOLUTION -GAINS vs. LIMITATIONS\n9. Subsidy - monetary assistance given by the government to sustain and promote or boost the production activity.\n65"},{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"ied-2-08","image":"ied-pages/ied-2-08.jpg","text":"Q.7. What was the basic objective of land reforms initiated by the government?\nAns. Promoting equity (equality) in the ownership of land in the rural economy.\nQ. 8. Enlist the principal elements of HYV technology.\nAns. Use or HYV seeds, chemical termlizers, insecucides and pestcides along with expansion of\nirrigation facilities.\nQ. 9. State the meaning of the term fallowing and enlist its major advantage.\nAns. It refers to the practice of leaving land uncultivated for some time as it helps the soil to regain its\nfertility.\nQ. 10. What is farmer's output over and above his 'on-farm consumption' called?\nAns. Marketable surplus.\nQ. 11. State an argument given by those who denounce agricultural subsidy.\nAns. Agricultural subsidies lead to availability of goods at a lower price which promotes wasteful\nconsumption.\nQ. 12. Features of Indian agriculture reveal the story of its backwardness. Do you agree?\nAns. Yes, it is true that the features of Indian agriculture reveal the story of its backwardness.\nThe notable features are:\n(i) low productivity,\n(ii) small size of holdings,\n(iii) subsistence-oriented farming,\n(iv) existence of disguised unemployment, and\n(v) use of backward technology.\nEach of these features points to the backwardness of Indian agriculture.\nQ. 13. Marginal farmers are subsistence farmers. Do you agree?\nAns. Yes, it is true that marginal farmers are subsistence farmers. Because, the marginal farmers\nare so small farmers that they avoid exposing themselves to market-risks. Accordingly,\nthey focus on subsistence farming-the farming which is not market-oriented, instead it is\nsubsistence-oriented. Only those crops are grown which are needed for self-consumption by the\nfarming families (even when commercial crops could yield good profit)."},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-2-08","image":"ied-pages/ied-2-08.jpg","text":"Public and Private Sectors\n• Public Sector reters to those business enterprises which are controlled and run by the government.\nThese are largely driven by considerations of social welfare.\n• Private Sector refers to those business enterprises which are controlled and run by private individuals.\nThese are largely driven by considerations of profit-maximisation.\n• Direct participation of the state was considered essential in view of the factors discussed below:\nFactors Necessitating Direct Participation of the State\n(Public Sector) in Industrial Development\nLack of Capital\nLow Inducement to Invest\nGrowth with Social Justice\n(a) Lack of Capital: Industrial development in India needed a big push*. Implying a large amount\nof capital expenditure.\n> At the time of independence, Tata and Birla were the only established industrial houses in\nIndia.\n> The requirement of capital for the country's industrial growth was far beyond the capacity\nof these industrial houses.\n> Accordingly, it became essential for the state (or the government) to achieve industrial\ngrowth through public sector undertakings.\n(b) Low Inducement to Invest: The private investors lacked inducement to invest. It was owing to\nlimited size of the market. Limited size of the market was owing to low level of demand. Level\nof demand was low because of low level of income. Thus, there was a sort of vicious circle\noperating like this:\nLow\nLow\ninvestment\nLow\ninducement\nto invest\nLow\ndemand\nLimited\nsize of the\nmarket\n10. Big push - relatively high level of investment which stresses the conditions of take-off.\n67"},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-2-07","image":"ied-pages/ied-2-07.jpg","text":"Control of Commanding Heights of the Economy\n• Commanding heights of the economy refers to industries of strategic significance. These industries\n(including those like transport and communication) were of strategic significance because they provided\nan infrastructural base for the overall spread of industrialisation in the economy. The government\ndecided to develop these industries as public sector undertakings.\n• The 'commanding heights' also included industries of defence production which, owing to strategic\nneed of country's defence, could not be left in the hands of private entrepreneurs.\n2.4.2 IPR, 1956 (Industrial Policy Resolution, 1956) -\nA Declaration on Leading Role of the State\n• Industrial Policy Resolution, 1956 was a clear declaration of the government on the leading role of\nthe government in the process of industrialisation.\n• This resolution laid a roadmap of the Second Five Year Plan (the plan meant to promote the socialist\npattern of society). Also, it was a milestone of achieving growth with social justice.\nFollowing were the principal elements of IPR, 1956:\n(a) Three-fold Classification of Industries: Industries were classified into three categories:\nThree-fold Classification of Industries\nThose which would be\nestablished and developed\nexclusively as public sector\nenterprises.\nThese include atomic energy,\ndefence and railways.\nThose which could be\nestablished both as the private\nand public sector enterprises.\nHowever, the private sector\nwas to play only a secondary\n(tii)\nOther industries other than\ncategories (i) and (ii).\nAll these were left to the private\nsector.\nrole. New enterprises were to\nbe established primarily in the\npublic sector.\nThis category includes fertilizer\nindustry, mining industry, etc.\n> Thus, IPK, 1756 clearly implied that the state was to spearhead (sponsor) the process o\nindustrialisation in the economy.\n68\nIndian Economy (1950-1990)\n(b) Industrial Licensing: Industries in the private sector could be established only through a\nlicence\" from the government.\n* The basic idea of licensing policy was to encourage industry in backward regions of the\ncountry. It was easier to obtain a license if the industrial unit was located in an economically\nbackward area. This was to promote regional equality.\n› A licence was needed not only for establishing a new enterprise, but also for expanding\nproduction capacity of the existing ones. This was to regulate the allocation of resources to\ndifferent uses. The focus was to promote social welfare rather than private profits.\n(c) Industrial Concessions: The private entrepreneurs were offered many types of industrial\nconcessions for establishing industry in the backward regions of the country. These\nconcessions included:\n> tax holiday (freedom from the payment of tax for sometime), and\n> subsidised power supply.\nLike the policy of industrial licensing, the industrial concessions were expected to promote\nregional equality.\n2.4.3 Small-Scale Industry\nThe process of industrialisation had two broad dimensions, as shown in the following flow chart:\nTwo Major Dimensions of the Process of Industrialisation\nGrowth of large-scale industry (like iron\nand steel, transport and communication)\nto generate infrastructural facilities\nGrowth of small-scale industry to generate\nopportunities of employment, and promote\nequity (social justice)\nThus, small-scale industry was considered as a core element of the process of industrialisation with a\nview to generating the opportunities of employment.\nI. Definition of Small-Scale Industry\n• A small-scale industry is defined with reference to the maximum investment allowed on the\nassets of a unit. This limit has changed over a period of time.\n• According to the Ministry of MSME, a small-scale industry is presently defined as a unit having\nfixed investment of less than & 25 crore with annual turnover of less than & 100 crore.\n• Earlier, it was defined as one whose investment did not exceed & 1 crore.\n• In 1950, a small-scale industrial unit was defined as one whose investment did not exceed\n75 lakh.\n11. Licence - permission granted by a competent authority to engage in a business activity.\n69"},{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"ied-2-06","image":"ied-pages/ied-2-06.jpg","text":"Q. 14. Give two examples of industries which are categorised as commanding heights of the\neconomy.\nAns. (i) Transportation, and (ii) Communication.\nQ. 15. What was the main idea behind industrial licensing?\nAns. Provide encouragement to industries in backward regions of the country.\nQ. 16. What is the present turnover limit for any industry to be categorised as a small-scale industry?\nAns. Less than F 50 crore.\nQ. 17. \"Credit Guarantee Scheme (CGS) can only be availed by upcoming and existing micro and small\nenterprises that are engaged in services or manufacturing activities. Agriculture businesses,\ntraining, and educational institutions can also apply for this scheme. Under this scheme, you can\navail a loan of up to & 10 lakh.\"\nSource: https://www.adityabirlacapital.com/abc-of-money/top-6-government-loan-schemes-\nfor-small-businesses\nState one reason why initiatives like the one stated above are taken by the government?\nAns. To promote small-scale industries and reap the benefits associated with the same."},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-2-05","image":"ied-pages/ied-2-05.jpg","text":"Import Substitution and Export Promotion Strategies\n• Import Substitution is a strategy to save foreign exchange by encouraging domestic production of\nsuch goods which the country has been importing from rest of the world. Domestic industry is offered\nprotection from foreign competition through import restrictions and import duties.\n• Export Promotion is a strategy to earn foreign exchange by promoting domestic exports and making\ndomestic industry competitive in the international market.\nForms of Protection\nProtection from imports took two forms:\n• Tariffs: A tariff is a tax on exports and imports, but largely focuses on imports. It makes the\nimported goods more expensive and thereby discourage their use. Thus, it directly increases the\nprice of imported commodities thereby offering protection to the domestic producers from foreign\ncompetition.\n• Quotas: Under it, the government specifies (fixes) the 'quantity' of goods that can be imported from\ndifferent countries. This is yet another way to restrict supplies from rest of the world so that domestic\nsupplies (domestic production) is not adversely impacted.\nAVLR 2.4\nIMPORT SUBSTITUTION AND EXPORT PROMOTION"},{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"ied-2-05","image":"ied-pages/ied-2-05.jpg","text":"Q. 18. What are the benefits of imposing tariffs and quotas on imports?\nAns. Imposition of tariffs and quotas restrict imports and in turn protect domestic producers from\nforeign competition.\n9. 1%. Should India rely more on import subsatution rather than 'export promotion' to improve it\nbalance of trade (export - import of goods)?\nAns. In the wake of globalisation when market economies are increasingly shifting to the policy o\nfree trade, and tarins are being moderated) the policy of im tort substitution (supported wit\nprotection to the domestic industry) is no longer a viable opticas india has to rely on the polic\nexport protect. can certainly focus on greate\ndomestic output so that our import bills are reduced. But it would be more through competitio\nrather than the policy of protection (through tariffs).\n16. Foreign exchange - refers to foreign currency.\n72\nIndian Economy (1950-1990)"},{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"ied-2-04","image":"ied-pages/ied-2-04.jpg","text":"Q. 20. State a bad effect associated with the growth strategy followed by India prior to 1990.\nExcessive dominance of public sector prior to 1990 promoted public sector monopolies. In the\nabsence of market competition, public sector monopolies avoided innovative technology and\n(owing to the lack of accountability) became the breeding centres of corruption, leakage and\nQ. 21. Substantiate the statement that during the period 1950-1990, good effects of the strategy of\nindustrialisation were overshadowed by its bad effects.\nAns. Good effect emerged in terms of the spread of large-scale industry in India. The contribution\nof industrial sector to GDP started rising. But the bad effect was more predominant: the public\nenterprises emerged as the breeding centres of corruption and inefficiency. The private sector\nfailed to diversify. Foreign exchange reserves were reduced to a point of panic. GDP growth\nbecame uncertain. The economy suffered a huge 'trust loss' in the global markets.\nEvaluation of the Strategy of Growth\nFollowing observations are presented as an evaluation of the strategy of growth:\n• As a result of the strategy of growth under Five Year Plans, Indian economy was able to break the\nbarrier of economic stagnation. India became self-sufficient in food grain production.\n• Land reforms and HYV technology turned Indian agriculture from a stagnant and backward sector\ninto a vibrant and growing sector of the economy.\n• The industrial sector was diversified. Infrastructural development became perceptible reality.\nA structural shift in terms of the relative contribution of agricultural and industrial sectors to GDP\nalso became perceptible.\n• But paradoxically, even as the industrial sector grew and the contribution of agricultural sector\nto GDP declined, almost 65 per cent of the population continued to be employed in agricultural\nsector till 1990. Implying that the growth in industrial and service sector was not sufficient to\nabsorb the surplus labour force of the agricultural sector.\n• Spurt in productivity owing to Green Revolution led to a huge backlog of surplus labour force\nin agriculture. But, industrial growth was not enough to absorb that surplus. Consequently, the\nplanners were confronted with a situation of massive rural unemployment in the economy, besides\ngrowing unemployment among the educated youth in the urban areas. This was recognised as one\nof the major limitations of the industrial policy pursued under Five Year Plan.\n• In addition, the excessive regulation and protection from foreign\ncompetition led to\nincompetitiveness and inefficiency of the domestic producers. The protected private sector seldom\nrealised the need for modernisation and diversification.\nWith the passage of time, bad effects of the strategy of industrialisation started overshadowing\nthe good effects. So much so, that the government was compelled to revisit the entire strategy of\neconomic growth. This led to the emergence of NEP (New Economic Policy), the subject matter of"},{"title":"ABILITY ZONE.","page":"ied-2-03","image":"ied-pages/ied-2-03.jpg","text":"Short Period Objectives during Planning Period\n• 'Short period objectives of planning reter to Plan-wise key Objectives. nese are presented in the\nflow chart as under.\nPlan & Period\nKey Objectives\n1st Plan: April 1, 1951-March 31, 1956\n(i) Increase in agricultural production.\n(i) Equitable distribution of production, income and wealth.\n2nd Plan: April 1, 1956—March 31, 1961\n(i) Increase in industrial production.\n(i) Development of heavy industry.\n3rd Plan: April 1, 1961-March 31, 1966\n(i) Self-sufficiency in food grain production.\n(ii) Generation of employment opportunities.\n(iii) Reduction in inequality\nThree Annual Plans/April 1, 1966-March 31, 1969\n4th Plan: April 1, 1969-March 31, 19/4\n(i) Accelerating the process of growth.\n(ii) Price stability.\n5th Plan: April 1, 1974-March 31, 1979\nRaising the living standards with a focus on weaker sections of the\nsociety.\nAnnual Plan/April 1, 1979-March 31, 1980\n6th Plan: April 1, 1980-March 31, 1985\n(i) Removal of poverty.\n(ii) Reduction of inequality.\n(li) Development of infrastructure.\n7th Plan: April 1, 1985 —March 31, 1990\n(i) Generation of employment opportunities.\n(i) Increase in agricultural productivity.\nTwo Annual Plans/April 1, 1990-March 31, 1992\n8th Plan: April 1, 1992- March 31, 1997\n(i) Fuller utilisation of manpower by the turn of the century.\n(ii) Universalisation of elementary education.\n9th Plan: April 1, 1997-March 31, 2002\n(ili) Strengthening of infrastructure.\n(i) Agricultural and rural development.\n(ii) Growth with price stability.\n10th Plan: April 1, 2002—March 31,\n(iii) Checking the growth of population.\n(i) Improving the quality of life through better health and\n11th Plan: April 1, 2007-March 31,\neducational facilities and improved levels of consumption.\n(i) Reduction in inequality through inclusive growth.\n(i) Multple targets covering not only growth but also poverty reduction.\n(i) Improving quality of education and public health services.\n(ii) Strategy of second green revolution.\n(iv) Generating high quality of job.\n12th Plan: April 1, 2012— March 31, 2017\n(v) Protection of environment.\nFaster, sustainable and more inclusive growth.\nNITI Aayog\n• The National Institution for Transformingesda, also called NIT Aayog was established o\n76\nJanuary 1, 2015 to replace the Planning Commission. It is a policy think tank' of the Government of Indi\nIndian Economy (1950-1990)\nand is engaged in designing strategic and long-term policies and programmes' related to the growth\nand development of the Indian economy. The Prime Minister of India serves as the Chairperson of the\ninstitution.\nFive Principal Objectives/Functions\n• Principal objectives/functions of NITI Aayog are as under:\n(i) Policy Formulation: Formulation of long period strategies of growth is the principal function of\nNITI Aayog. It is also called a 'think tank' function of NITI Aayog.\n(ii) Monitoring and Evaluation: Implementation of the policies and programmes is continuously monitored\nand evaluated, and expert advice is periodically offered to the central and state governments.\nNITI Aayog also monitors and evaluates implementation of key projects of the government like\n'Make in India', Digital India', Swachh Bharat Programme', etc.\n(iii) Cooperative and Competitive Federalism: NITI Aayog designs a 'national agenda' for the Prime\nMinister representing central government, and the chief ministers representing state governments.\nIt thus focuses on cooperative federalism' by offering a common platform for the central and\nstate governments to work together. Further, it promotes competitive federalism by ranking the\nperformance of different states on key indices of growth and development.\n(iv) Technology Upgradation and Capacity Building: NITI Aayog is actively engaged in technology\nupgradation and capacity building at the national and regional levels.\n(v) Promoting Equity: It focuses on achieving inclusive growth, ensuring that the benefits of growth\ntrickle across all sections of the society.\n'Growth' and 'Development' though related are different Concepts\n• Growth refers to a situation of sustained rise in GDP over a long period of time. It leads to a rise in\naverage standard of living of the people.\n• Development refers to growth with equity as well as structural change in the economy.\n• Equity implies equitable distribution of income, so that the benefits of growth are shared by all sections\nof the society.\n• Structural change implies a shift in the sectoral share in GDP: Share of secondary and tertiary sectors\nshould gradually rise and that of primary sector should gradually fall.\n•It implies a situation when secondary and tertiary sectors emerge as the key contributors to the\nGDP growth. It does not mean that primary sector (or the agricultural sector) is neglected. It only means\nthat the epicentre of GDP growth shifts from the primary sector to the secondary and tertiary sectors.\nParadox of Saving and Investment\n• Although during the planning period there has been appreciable increase in saving and investment, yet\nthe growth rate of economy has been very slow. Several factors account for this paradox:\n(i) Capital-output ratio is very high in India. It is around 4: 1 and has been rising over time. Because\nof this high ratio, increase in investment is followed by relatively less increase in production.\n(il) Considerable part of investment is in the form of buffer-stocks of food grains and not in the form\nof fixed capital formation. No wonder, despite the increase in the rate of investment there is no\ncorresponding increase in production.\n(ili) Large portion of investment is made in traditional sector with backward technology, leading to low\nproductivity.\nThe Concept of Interventionist State\n• 'Interventionist State' refers to the system in which the state intervenes to regulate and control the forces\nof the market (the forces of supply and demand and the consequent relative price structure) in a manner\nsuch that the production of essentials of life and their delivery to the relatively poorer sections of the\nsociety is not hurt."},{"title":"Power Points (Summary of the Chapter]","page":"ied-2-02","image":"ied-pages/ied-2-02.jpg","text":"Economic System: Economic system refers to economic order in the country.\nTypes of Economies:\n• Capitalist Economy is the one in which there is a private ownership of the means of production.\nMaximisation of profit is the principal objective.\n• Socialist Economy is the one in which there is a social (collective/public) ownership of the means of\nproduction. Maximisation of social welfare is the principal objective.\n• Mixed Economy is the one in which means of production are owned by the private entrepreneurs as\nwell as the government.\n• Economic Planning: Economic planning refers to a system under which a central authority sets a set\nof targets and specifies a set of programmes and policies to achieve those targets with in the specified\nperiod of time.\nPlan: A plan lays down how the limited resources of an economy should be put to use to achieve precise\nobjectives within a specified period of time.\nGoals of Planning: (i) GDP growth, (ii) Modernisation, (iii) Self-reliance and self-sufficiency, (iv) Equitable\ndistribution or equity.\nAgriculture: Agriculture included a series of land reforms: (i) Abolition of intermediaries, (ii) Ceiling on\nland holdings, (iii) Consolidation of holdings, (iv) Regulation of rent, (v) Cooperative farming.\nTechnical Reforms- Green Revolution: It refers to a spurt in farm output during mid 60's. Measures\nintroduced as part of Green Revolution are:\n(i) Use of HYV seeds\n(ii) Use of chemical fertilizers\n(iii) Use of insecticides and pesticides for crop protection\n(iv) Expansion of irrigation facilities\nGains of Green Revolution:\n(i) Spurt in crop productivity\n(il) Substantial rise in acreage (area under cultivation)\n(iii) Shift from subsistence farming to commercial tarming\n(iv) Change in farmers' outlook\n(V) Self-sufficiency in food grain production and buffer stocks\nLimitations of Green Revolution:\n(1) Limited crops\n(ii) Un-even spread\n(Iii) Limited farming population\n(iv) Economic divide\nAgricultural Subsidies: Instrumental in encouraging adoption of new technology that is deemed risky by\nthe farmers. Limitations of agricultural subsidies are:\n(1) Huge burden on government finances\n(ii) Misappropriated benefits\n(ili) Wasteful consumption\n79"}]},{"id":"i3","number":"3","subject":"ied","title":"Economic Reforms Since 1991","intro":"","lessons":[{"title":"Why reforms became necessary in 1991","body":"Fiscal pressure: spending exceeded revenue, increasing borrowing and debt.\n\nExternal crisis: foreign-exchange reserves fell to roughly two weeks of imports.\n\nOther problems: inflation and weak public-enterprise returns.\n\nResponse: assistance from IMF/World Bank and a shift towards a more competitive economy.\n\n1991 combined an immediate payments crisis with deeper weaknesses in the old growth strategy.","formula":"","source":"ied-3-12","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"3.1 Need for New Economic Policy","points":["Fiscal pressure: spending exceeded revenue, increasing borrowing and debt.","External crisis: foreign-exchange reserves fell to roughly two weeks of imports.","Other problems: inflation and weak public-enterprise returns.","Response: assistance from IMF/World Bank and a shift towards a more competitive economy."],"takeaway":"1991 combined an immediate payments crisis with deeper weaknesses in the old growth strategy."},{"title":"Liberalisation: meaning and industrial reforms","body":"Liberalisation = reduce direct government controls over producing units.\n\nIndustrial changes: reduce licensing, open more activities to private firms, ease expansion and imports of capital goods.\n\nMechanism: easier entry → more competition → pressure to improve efficiency.\n\nFewer controls does not mean no regulation; safety, competition and other safeguards still matter.","formula":"","source":"ied-3-12","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"3.2 Elements of NEP · 3.2.1 Liberalisation","points":["Liberalisation = reduce direct government controls over producing units.","Industrial changes: reduce licensing, open more activities to private firms, ease expansion and imports of capital goods.","Mechanism: easier entry → more competition → pressure to improve efficiency."],"takeaway":"Fewer controls does not mean no regulation; safety, competition and other safeguards still matter."},{"title":"Financial-sector reforms","body":"Increase competition by allowing more private and foreign participation in finance.\n\nGive banks more operational freedom within safeguards.\n\nShift RBI’s emphasis from detailed control towards facilitation and regulation.\n\nRBI becoming a facilitator does not mean it stopped supervising banks.","formula":"","source":"ied-3-11","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"3.2.1 Liberalisation · Financial Sector Reforms","points":["Increase competition by allowing more private and foreign participation in finance.","Give banks more operational freedom within safeguards.","Shift RBI’s emphasis from detailed control towards facilitation and regulation."],"takeaway":"RBI becoming a facilitator does not mean it stopped supervising banks."},{"title":"Tax reforms, GST and demonetisation","body":"Tax reforms: simplify taxes, rationalise rates, widen the tax base and improve compliance.\n\nGST, 1 July 2017: brought many indirect taxes into a common framework.\n\nDemonetisation, November 2016: withdrew legal-tender status of the then ₹500/₹1,000 notes, aiming to address unaccounted money and encourage formal/digital activity.\n\nKeep a policy’s objective separate from whether it fully achieved that objective.","formula":"","source":"ied-3-10","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"3.2.1 Liberalisation · Fiscal and Tax Reforms","points":["Tax reforms: simplify taxes, rationalise rates, widen the tax base and improve compliance.","GST, 1 July 2017: brought many indirect taxes into a common framework.","Demonetisation, November 2016: withdrew legal-tender status of the then ₹500/₹1,000 notes, aiming to address unaccounted money and encourage formal/digital activity."],"takeaway":"Keep a policy’s objective separate from whether it fully achieved that objective."},{"title":"Devaluation and trade reforms","body":"1991 devaluation: officially lower the rupee’s value to improve export competitiveness and tackle the external crisis.\n\nTrade reforms: reduce tariffs, quantitative restrictions and licensing barriers.\n\nEffect: cheaper exports in foreign-currency terms, costlier imports in rupees and greater competition for domestic firms.\n\nDevaluation is an official change; depreciation is a market-driven fall in currency value.","formula":"","source":"ied-3-09","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"3.2.1 Liberalisation · Foreign Exchange and Trade Reforms","points":["1991 devaluation: officially lower the rupee’s value to improve export competitiveness and tackle the external crisis.","Trade reforms: reduce tariffs, quantitative restrictions and licensing barriers.","Effect: cheaper exports in foreign-currency terms, costlier imports in rupees and greater competition for domestic firms."],"takeaway":"Devaluation is an official change; depreciation is a market-driven fall in currency value."},{"title":"Privatisation and disinvestment","body":"Privatisation: greater private ownership or management of public enterprises.\n\nDisinvestment: sale of government equity; a minority sale need not transfer control.\n\nPossible gains: efficiency, commercial discipline and revenue.\n\nConcerns: undervaluation, job losses and loss of future public income.\n\nAsk whether ownership or control actually changed before calling a sale full privatisation.","formula":"","source":"ied-3-09","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"3.2.2 Privatisation","points":["Privatisation: greater private ownership or management of public enterprises.","Disinvestment: sale of government equity; a minority sale need not transfer control.","Possible gains: efficiency, commercial discipline and revenue.","Concerns: undervaluation, job losses and loss of future public income."],"takeaway":"Ask whether ownership or control actually changed before calling a sale full privatisation."},{"title":"Autonomy and public-enterprise status","body":"Selected public enterprises received greater financial and managerial freedom.\n\nNavratna/Maharatna arrangements aim to support quicker commercial decisions.\n\nThe enterprise can remain government-owned while gaining autonomy.\n\nAutonomy changes decision-making freedom; privatisation changes the private sector’s role in ownership or management.","formula":"","source":"ied-3-07","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"3.2.2 Privatisation · Public Enterprise Autonomy","points":["Selected public enterprises received greater financial and managerial freedom.","Navratna/Maharatna arrangements aim to support quicker commercial decisions.","The enterprise can remain government-owned while gaining autonomy."],"takeaway":"Autonomy changes decision-making freedom; privatisation changes the private sector’s role in ownership or management."},{"title":"Globalisation and outsourcing","body":"Globalisation = deeper integration through trade, investment, technology and services.\n\nOutsourcing = hiring an external provider for particular work.\n\nIndia’s advantages: skilled English-speaking workers, relatively low costs, IT links and time-zone differences.\n\nAn overseas company using an Indian accounting service is outsourcing; the wider cross-border integration is globalisation.","formula":"","source":"ied-3-06","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"3.2.3 Globalisation","points":["Globalisation = deeper integration through trade, investment, technology and services.","Outsourcing = hiring an external provider for particular work.","India’s advantages: skilled English-speaking workers, relatively low costs, IT links and time-zone differences."],"takeaway":"An overseas company using an Indian accounting service is outsourcing; the wider cross-border integration is globalisation."},{"title":"WTO: purpose and debate","body":"WTO began in 1995, succeeding GATT.\n\nRole: multilateral trade rules, negotiations and dispute settlement.\n\nDebate: developing countries face unequal bargaining power and competition from subsidised producers in richer countries.\n\nExplain its intended rules-based role, then assess whether the gains are distributed fairly.","formula":"","source":"ied-3-06","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"3.2.3 Globalisation · World Trade Organisation","points":["WTO began in 1995, succeeding GATT.","Role: multilateral trade rules, negotiations and dispute settlement.","Debate: developing countries face unequal bargaining power and competition from subsidised producers in richer countries."],"takeaway":"Explain its intended rules-based role, then assess whether the gains are distributed fairly."},{"title":"Reforms: favourable effects","body":"Competition and choice: firms face pressure to improve quality; consumers get more options.\n\nInvestment and technology: foreign links can bring finance, knowledge and market access.\n\nGrowth and external strength: the textbook highlights faster growth and stronger forex reserves over particular periods.\n\nUse a cause-and-effect chain, not just “reforms were good”: competition → efficiency → better products.","formula":"","source":"ied-3-05","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"3.3.1 Good Effect of LPG Policies","points":["Competition and choice: firms face pressure to improve quality; consumers get more options.","Investment and technology: foreign links can bring finance, knowledge and market access.","Growth and external strength: the textbook highlights faster growth and stronger forex reserves over particular periods."],"takeaway":"Use a cause-and-effect chain, not just “reforms were good”: competition → efficiency → better products."},{"title":"Reforms: criticisms and uneven gains","body":"Agriculture: reduced support and foreign competition hurt some producers.\n\nIndustry and jobs: import competition and capital-intensive growth limited gains for some firms/workers.\n\nUnequal development: benefits concentrated in certain sectors, cities and income groups.\n\nOther concerns: disinvestment, fiscal priorities, economic dependence and consumerism.\n\nReforms can raise total output while leaving particular people or sectors behind.","formula":"","source":"ied-3-04","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"3.3.2 Bad Effect of LPG Policies","points":["Agriculture: reduced support and foreign competition hurt some producers.","Industry and jobs: import competition and capital-intensive growth limited gains for some firms/workers.","Unequal development: benefits concentrated in certain sectors, cities and income groups.","Other concerns: disinvestment, fiscal priorities, economic dependence and consumerism."],"takeaway":"Reforms can raise total output while leaving particular people or sectors behind."}],"pages":[{"id":"ied-3-12","image":"ied-pages/ied-3-12.jpg","text":"Indian Economic Development\n3,1 NEED FOR NEW ECONOMIC POLICY (A BACKGROUND STO»\n1 After independence, our strategy of growth was based on the model of 'Mixed Economy' When\neading role was assigned to public sector. Private sector was regulated through multiple cont\n1 \"Inward-looking' policy was promoted which focused on self-reliane? and self-sufficien\nIt implied minimum possible exposer to rest of the world.\n• Overtime we realised that our GDP growth was not accelerated to the extent desired. In ord\nto pursue development policies, the Government of India had to spend more than the reven.\nit earned. However, the government failed to generate sufficient revenue through sources l.\ntaxation. The disappointed performance of public sector enterprises meant that the governmer\ndid not generate much revenue from these enterprises.\n• In addition to it, public sector enterprises (which were more like public sector monopolies\nemerged, by and large, as centres of leakage, pilferage and large-scale corruption.\n• The foreign exchange reserves which were mainly used for importing petroleum products ant,\nmeeting other consumption needs, dropped to levels that were barely enough to pay for twr\nweeks' imports.\n• Further, prices of many essential goods rose sharply.\n• By 1990 we found that the economy was facing a helpless failure (collapse), and we were left with\nno options but to approach World Bank [also known as International Bank for Reconstruction and\nDevelopment (IBRD)) and IMF (International Monetary Fund) for financial help.\n• We were granted a loan of US $ 7 billion to manage the crises, but subject to the condition that we\nwould revise our strategy of growth. We were asked to shift from an 'inward-looking strategy to\nan 'outward-looking strategy' of growth.\n• Controls and Quotas were to be abolished, paving way for a competitive business environment.\n• Private foreign investment was to be allowed, and priority was to be placed on export-promotion.\nrather than import-substitution.\n• It was in the backdrop of these developments that the planners and politicians in India decided to\nshift to NEP (New Economic Policy).\n• The main aim of the New Economic Policy was to foster a more competitive economic environment\nand to remove the barriers to the entry and growth of firms.\n& AVLR 3.1\nNEED FOR NEW ECONOMIC POLICY (ECONOMIC REFORMS) -1991\na need ted restrict t in t mr r mear val o god that country is\nallowed to import or export during a particular period.\n2. Self-reliance - reliance on own resources rather than foreign investment.\n3. Self-sufficiency - catering to domestic needs through domestic produc\n118\nEconomic Reforms Since 1991: New Economic Policy\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\nQ. 1. Prior to the launching of economic reforms, whom did India approach for availing a loan?\nAns. World Bank and IMF.\nQ.2.\nThe economic reforms were based around which assumption?\nAns.\nMarket forces would drive the economy towards the path of competitive growth and development.\nQ. 3.\n\"India had asked for a $ 1.8 billion bailout loan from the International Monetary Fund (IMF),\nwhich demanded de-regulation in return.\"\nSource: financialexpress.com\nState any two factors that compelled India to take a loan from the IMF.\nAns. Two principal factors that compelled India to take a loan from IMF are as these:\n(i) Mounting fiscal deficit which led us to a debt trap.\n(ii) Unprecedented BoP crises and severely depleted forex reserves.\n3.2 ELEMENTS OF NEP (NEW ECONOMIC POLICY)\nAND ECONOMIC REFORMS\n• Liberalisation, privatisation and globalisation are the three main elements of NEP.\nThree Main Elements of NEP\n(a)\nLiberalisation\nPrivatisation\nGlobalisation\n- Liberalisation (L) was in place of licensing (L) for the industries and trade.\n- Privatisation (P) was in place of quotas (Q) for the industrialists.\n- Globalisation (G) was in place of permits (P) for exports and imports.\n- Thus, LPG was set to replace LQP in 1991.\n3.2.1 Liberalisation\n• Liberalisation of the economy means freedom of the producing units from direct or physical\ncontrols of the government.\n• Liberalisation was introduced to remove the restrictions and open up various sectors of the\neconomy.\n• Though a few liberalisation measures were introduced in 1980s in areas of industrial licensing,\nexport-import policy, technology upgradation, fiscal policy and foreign investment, reform policies\ninitiated in 1991 were more comprehensive.\nWhat prompted a push towards Liberalisation?\nFour observations are notable in this regard:\n(i) Prior to 1991, government had imposed several types of controls on private enterprises in the\ndomestic economy.\n4. Fiscal policy - revenue and expenditure policy of the government.\n119","file":"chap3.pdf"},{"id":"ied-3-11","image":"ied-pages/ied-3-11.jpg","text":"Indian Economic Development\nHIGHLIGHTS\nDemonetisation\nlemonetisation, introduced in 2016 is closelv related to financial sector reforms. Let us understand its concent...\nconsequences in the context of the Indian economy.\n• Demonetisation is a policy action of the government that withdraws the status of 'legal tender\" from the existing.\n- Once the status of 'legal tender' is withdrawn, the existing currency (or the currency notes) are reduced meet\nto pieces of paper.\n- These notes lose their acceptance as a medium of exchange, or they lose their power to buy goods and service\n- It was on November 8, 2016 that the Government of India announced demonetisation of the currency notes of\n· 500 and 1,000.\n- The people were required to deposit the demonetised currency notes with the banks within a period of 2 months.\nThe demonetised notes were replaced by new currency notes of 500 and 7 2,000.\n• Basic Purpose\n- Basically, demonetisation aims at curbing illegal transactions and anti-social activities (funded through illegal\ntransactions).\n• Principal Merits\n(i) It helps unearth (find out) black money.\n(ii) Reduction of black money leads to shrinkage of shadow economy (an economy with unrecorded production\nactivity and tax evasion).\n(ii) When shadow economy shrinks and tax evasion is reduced, government revenue tends to rise.\n(iv) Demonetisation compels people to deposit their demonetised notes with the banks. Accordingly, financial-base\nof the banking sector tends to expand.\n(v) A check on funding to terrorists helps combat anti-social activities in the economy.\n(vi) With cash almost disappearing from the market, people were driven to digital mode of transactions. This was\na big move towards cashless (or low cash) economy. It also promoted banking habits of the people, a big leap\ntowards financial inclusion.\n• Principal Demerits\n(i) In a developing economy like India, people are used to cash-transactions. With demonetisation, people are\nstripped of (deprived of cash in hand.\n- It impairs (disturbs) their routine transactions and therefore, their normal living. Thus, demonetisation in 2016\nled to a serious social chaos (unrest) in India.\n(i) Cash-crunch (owing to demonetisation) jolts production activity in the shadow economy. Accordingly, jobs are\nlost and poor people are further marginalised.\n(il) A jolt to production activity in the shadow economy invariably hurts the overal level of economic activity\nAncluding production, consumption and investment) in the economy. The economy starts facing slowdown:\nIndia is yet to recover from the slowdown triggered by demonetisation in 2016.\n(iv Nearly 90 percent of the workforce in India is engaged in informal economy for unordaniced copter of the\neconomy for their livelihood. And this seament of the economy is highly cash dependent and desh dancitiva\n- A huge cut in liquidity (following note ban) implied that cash purchase of inputs and such payment of manes\nlarge-scale lav-off, Daily wagers loet their aroleument from day-tof demanatice an\nbecame impossible. These led to an instantaneous cut in production activity. Consequently, there was a\n(v) Exchange of demonetised currency with the valid currency leads to long queues at the banks. People feel hurt\nwhen they find it difficultto use their own money. Accordingly, consumption is lowered and inducemento invest\npeople in India have successfully managed.\n122\nEconomic Reforms Since 1991: New Economic Policy\n(c) Tax Reforms\nand public expenditure policy of the government.\nTax' reforms are an important component of fiscal policy of the government or taxation (revenue)\nHIGHLIGHTS\nFiscal Policy\n.. It refers to taxation (revenue) and public expenditure policy of the government.\n• It is a policy that seeks to achieve stability in the economy by managing the revenue and expenditure\nof the government.\nTaxes are broadly classified as under:\nClassification of Taxes\nDirect Tax\n• Direct taxes are those taxes, the burden of\nwhich cannot be shifted onto others.\n• Examples: Income tax, corporation tax,\nwealth tax.\n• One who pays such a tax himself bears\nthe burden of it.\n• Indirect taxes (levied on goods and\nservices) are those taxes, the burden of\nwhich can be shifted onto others.\n• Examples: GST (Goods and Services Tax),\ncustom duty.\n• One who pays such a tax to the\ngovernment can shift the burden of this\ntax onto the final buyers of the goods by\nadding the tax amount to the basic price\nof the goods sold.\n• Prior to liberalisation, tax structure was quite complex and tax rates were quite high. High tax\nrates induced tax evasion®, causing loss of revenue to the government.\n• Following liberalisation, income tax and corporate tax have gradually\nbeen reduced.\nIndirect tax structure has also been reformed to establish a common national market for goods\nand services.\n• In 2016, the Indian Parliament enacted Goods and Services Tax (GST) Act 2016, to simplify and\nintroduce a unified indirect tax system in India.\n- This law came into effect from July 2017. This has raised tax compliance and therefore, tax\nrevenue of the government.\n- GST is expected to generate additional tax revenue for the government, reduce tax evasion and\ncreate 'one nation, one tax and one market'.\n7. Tax - a compulsory payment imposed on an individual, household or corporation by a government entity.\n8. Tax evasion - an illegal practice where a person or an entity intentionally does not pay due taxes.\n9. Tax compliance - refers to the extent to which individuals and organisations obey tax laws.\n123","file":"chap3.pdf"},{"id":"ied-3-10","image":"ied-pages/ied-3-10.jpg","text":"Indian Economic Development\n- These included industrial licensing svstem, price control or financial control on good.\nimport licence, foreign exchange control, restrictions on investment by big busin.,\nil) It was experienced by the government that several shortcomings had emerged in the econor.\non account of these controls.\n(ii) These controls had given rise to corruption, undue delays and inefficiency.\niv) Growth rate of GDP had fallen sharply and high-cost economic system (rather than a low-cos\ncompetitive economic system) came into being.\n• In view of these facts, liberalisation of the economy was considered as a key component of NEP-\nGreater reliance was to be placed on market forces (of supply and demand) rather than checks and\ncontrols.\n• Examples of developing countries (Korea, Thailand, Singapore) that had achieved rapid growth due\nto liberalisation were considered as worthy of emulation.\nHIGHLIGHTS\nEconomic Reforms (with liberalisation as its key component) were based on the assumption that market\nforces would drive the economy towards the path of competitive growth and development.\nEconomic Reforms under Liberalisation\nLiberalisation included the following reforms:\n(a) Industrial Sector Reforms\n• Liberalisation virtually implied de-regulation of industrial sector of the economy.\nFollowing flow chart highlights how de-regulation of industrial sector happened:\nLiberalisation Measures\nAbolition of\nIndustrial Licensing\n• In July 1991, a new\nindustrial policy was\nannounced.\n• It abolished the\nrequirement of\nlicensing except for\nthe following five\nindustries:\n(i) liquor,\n(ii) cigarette,\n(iii) defence\nequipments,\n(iv) industrial\nexplosives, and\nContraction of\nPublic Sector\n• Under the new\nindustrial policy,\nnumber of industries\nreserved for public\nsector was reduced\nfrom 17 to 8.\n• In 2010-11, the\nnumber of these\nindustries was\nreduced merely to\nthree including:\n(i) Atomic energy,\n(ii) Railways, and\n(iii) Defence\nequipments.\n(v) dangerous\nchemicals.\n*SS/ (Small-scale industries) - industries that manufacture, produce and render\nDe-reservation of\nProduction Areas\nExpansion of\nProduction Capacity\nFreedom to Import\n- Many production\nareas which earlier\n• Earlier production\nCapital Goods\nwere reserved for\ncapacity was linked\nSSI (small-scale\nwith licensing.\n• Liberalisation also\nindustries)* were\nNow, freedom from\nimplied freedom for\nde-reserved.\nlicensing implied\nthe industrialists\nfreedom from\nto import capital\n• Forces of the market\nwere allowed to\ncapacity constraints.\ngoods with a view\ndetermine allocation\n• 'What to produce\nto upgrading their\nof resources (rather\nand how much to\ntechnology.\nthan the directive\n• Permission\nproduce was now a\npolicy of the\nmatter of producer's\ngovernment).\nchoice depending on\nwas no longer\nmarket conditions.\nrequired from the\ngovernment to enter\ninto international\nagreements for\nthe import of\ntechnology.\nhall or micro level.\n120\n(b) Financial Sector® Reforms\nEconomic Reforms Since 1991: New Economic Policy\na Financial sector reforms related primarily to the regulation and control of financial institutions,\nincluding commercial banks and investment banks. In India, these institutions are regulated and\ncontrolled by the RBI (Reserve Bank of India).\n• Liberalisation\nimplied\na substantial shift\nin the role of the RBI from 'a regulator' to\n'a facilitator' of the financial sector.\n- As a regulator (prior to liberalisation), the\nRBI would itself fix interest rate structure for\nthe commercial banks.\n- But as a facilitator (after liberalisation),\nBANK OF\nthe RBI would only facilitate the free play\nof the market forces and leave it to the\ncommercial banks to decide their interest\nRBI - The Apex Bank of the Country\nrate structure.\n- Now, competition (rather than control) rules the decision-making process.\nHIGHLIGHTS\n• Consequent upon the policy of liberalisation, there has been a substantial shift in the role of the RBI\nfrom 'a regulator' to 'a facilitator' of the financial sector.\n• Now, market forces (rather than controls) decide the interest rate structure, volume and the pattern of\ninvestment.\nSignificant Changes in the Wake of Liberalisation\n• Free play of the market forces has led to the emergence of private sector banks-both domestic as\nwell as international -in the Indian banking industry.\n• FIl (Foreign Institutional Investors) such as merchant bankers, mutual funds and pension funds are\nnow allowed to invest in Indian financial markets. This has led to a significant expansion of the\nfinancial sector in India.\n• Banks fulfilling certain conditions are allowed to set-up new branches without the approval\nof RBI.\n• Banks have been given permission to generate resources from India and abroad. In this context, it\nis important to note that foreign investment limit in banks is as high as 74 per cent.\nOverall Impact\nConsequent upon these changes, financial sector in India has shown a multi-dimensional growth and\nis playing a significant role in the growth and development of the economy.\n5. Financial sector - The learners may note that the financial sector includes: (i) financial institutions such as commercial\nbanks and investment banks, (ii) stock exchange market, and (ili) foreign exchange market.\n6. Financial markets - a system that provides buyers and sellers the means to trade financial instruments, including\nbonds, equities, international currencies, etc.\n121","file":"chap3.pdf"},{"id":"ied-3-09","image":"ied-pages/ied-3-09.jpg","text":"il) Removal of Import Licensing: The policy of import licensing13 was abolished except in.\nif hazardous and environmentally sensitive industries. Quantitative restrictions on imp,\nf manufactured consumer goods and agricultural products were also fully removed\n(iv) Removal of Export Duties: Export duties were removed to increase the competitive posi\nof Indian goods in the international market.\nHIGHLIGHTS\nDon't Confuse Liberalisation with a Laissez-Faire System\n• Laissez-faire refers to a system in which there is no intervention by the state in the functioning of\n- All decisions relating to allocation of resources and the goods & services to be produced are takens\nproducers on the basis of market forces of supply and demand.\n- Role of the government is restricted just to the maintenance of law & order and defence of the coum,\nfrom external aggressions: it is nothing beyond being a night watchman of the country.\n• Liberalisation should not be confused with a laissez-faire system. Liberalisation only implies a situatio\nwherein the government allows greater degree of freedom and flexibility to the private entrepreneurs\nmatters relating to allocation of resources.\n- To illustrate, pursuing the policy of liberalisation, the government may abolish licensing/registration d\nthe enterprises as an essential requirement.\n- Likewise, the government may liberalise or abolish limits on the production capacity of the firms.\nBut all this is in consonance with direct participation of the government in production activity.\n- Thus, liberalisation does not exclude government's intervention in the economy; it does not rule out.\nthe existence of checks and controls by the government.\n- It only implies greater degree of freedom to the private entrepreneurs in deciding their areas of\neconomic activity and expanding their scale of production.\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\nQ. 4. Who decides the interest rate in situations where RBl acts as a facilitator rather than a\nregulator of the financial sector?\nAns. Commercial banks via free play of the market forces.\nQ. 5. When did demonetisation occur in India?\nAns. On November 8, 2016.\nQ. 6. Wealth tax is an example of which type of tax?\nAns. Direct tax.\nQ.7. Value added tax has been replaced by which tax?\nAns. GST (Goods and Services tax).\n2. 8. Lowering the value of domestic currency in relation to foreign currencies is called?\nAns. Devaluation.\nQ. 9. A system in which there is no intervention by the state in the functioning of an economy is\ncalled\nAns. Laissez-faire.\n12. Import licensing - an administrative procedure requiring documentation prior to the import of goode\n126\nEconomic Reforms Since 1991: New Economic Policy\nQ. 10. Why were controls imposed post independence in India?\nIn the context of the Indian experience, controls were imposed by the government with a view\n(i) checking the growth of private monopolies, and\n(ii) minimising the hold of large industrial houses on the financial resources of the country.\nIllustration: Some banks were nationalised in sixties because these were owned by the big\nindustrial houses who would use banking funds primarily to meet their own financial needs.\n3.2.2 Privatisation\n• Privatisation is the process of involving the private sector in the ownership or operation of a\ngovernment owned enterprise.\nIt implies gradual withdrawal of government ownership/management from the public sector\nenterprises. It may happen in two ways:\n(i) By withdrawal of the government ownership and management from the public sector\ncompanies, or\n(ii) By outright sale of the public sector companies to the private entrepreneurs.\nJourney towards Privatisation\nThe journey from PSEs (public sector enterprises as the fulcrum of growth) to privatisation\n(private sector enterprises as the fulcrum of growth) is explained through the flow chart as given\nbelow:\nJOURNEY TOWARDS PRIVATISATION\n1956 IPR\n(Industrial Policy\nResolution)\nPrincipal Gains\n(1956-1990)\nPrincipal Losses\n(causing the\nneed for\nprivatisation)\n• PSEs begin to play as 'fulcrum of growth.\nNavratnas - the industries of eminance.\nDiversification of industrial base.\nStructural transformation* of the economy (increase in percentage contribution\nof secondary sector to GDP).\nIncreasing inefficiencies in production.\nPSEs as breeding centres of leakage, pilferage and corruption.\nPSEs as 'deadweight loss' (social liability).\nProcess of privatisation begins.\nPSEs start phasing out: Equity of PSEs being sold off to private enterprises.\n* Structural transformation - the reallocation of economic activity across the three sectors, viz., primary, secondary and\ntertiary sectors of the economy.\n127","file":"chap3.pdf"},{"id":"ied-3-08","image":"ied-pages/ied-3-08.jpg","text":"Indian Economic Development\nHIGHLIGHTS\n• GST (Goods and Services Tax has been introduced in India with a view to providing a uniform tax structure across\nall parts of the country.\n• It is a one tax in place of all taxes on goods and services, and it is a uniform tax across all states of the count\nThus, GST carries the slogan of 'one tax, one nation, one market.\n• GST is expected to generate additional tax revenue for the government, increase\ntax compliance and reduce tax evasion.\n• The Basic Structure of GST\n- Goods and services have been classified into different categories.\n- Each category of goods carries a specific rate of taxation. And, there is a\nGST\nGOODS AND SERVICES TAX\ncategory of goods which is tax-exempt, considering the needs and means of\npoorer section of the society.\nTax Reform\nFollowing flow chart shows the structure of GST:\nStructure of GST\n124\nCategory of Goods\nUltra-High Temperature (UHT) milk, paratha, pre-packaged paneer, packaged snacks,\npencils, sharpeners, crayons, exercise books, graph books, essential life-saving drugs\nTax Rate\n0%\nCondensed milk, butter, cheese, packaged food items, preserved vegetables, fruits, toilet\nsoap, bicycles, leather, footwear, textiles, handicrafts, toys\n5%\nPetrol and Petrol Hybrid, LPG, CNG cars (more than 1200cc and more than 4000mm), three\nwheelers, motorcycles (more than 350cc), air conditioners, television, dish washing machines\n18%\nmotorcycles exceeding 350cc\nLuxury and sin goods like tobacco products, pan masala, aerated drinks, high-end cars,\n40%\n• Typical Characteristics\nThree typical characteristics of GST must be noted:\n® GST is levied at each stage of value addition.\n(r) ST focuses on the supply of goods rather than their production. Tax is levied as and when goods are supplied\n(or when goods leave their destination for their buyers).\n(il) GST paid by the producers on the purchase of inputs is allowed to be adjusted by the producers in their payment\nof GST proceeds to the government.\n• Principal Merits\nFour principal merits of GST are as under:\nItis simplified tay structure, as it is one tax instead of all the indirect taxes, and it is a uniform tay acred all narte\nSuppliers. it ensures hicher dearee of transparency in business: Black money tranean one are reducer\n(i) Being a uniform tax across all parts of the country. GST has enhanced size of the made for the domestic\n• Some Teething Problems and Demerits\n(iv) GST has raised government revenue as there is a higher degree of transparency in business.\nAny new concept always suffers from some teething problems when it comes to its implementation coe CST.\nThe teething problems of GST are often highlighted as its demerits. Two observations are notable in this context:\n)GST is vet to cover all goods produced in the country. Electricity generation, alcohol, petrol and diesel are some\nnotable products out of the ambit of GST.\n[Note: Forfurther details on GST, students are advised to refer to Project Work at\nfiy GST rates across different goods and services are still not finally settled, and to that extent uncertainty looms in\nthe economy. This uncertainty hampers decision-making and therefore,\nproduction activity.\nctory Macroeconomics.)\nEconomic Reforms Since 1991: New Economic Policy\n* AVLR 3.2\nGOODS AND SERVICES TAX (GST)\n(d) Foreign Exchange Reforms\n• Foreign exchange market relates to external sector of the economy, and is regulated by the RBI in\nIndia.\n• BoP crises\" on the eve of NEP prompted the government to initiate foreign exchange reforms,\nstarting with devaluation of the Indian currency against foreign currencies.\n• Devaluation means deliberately (or officially) lowering the value of domestic currency in relation\nto foreign currencies. It is a policy instrument pursued under 'Fixed Exchange Rate' system.\nConsequent upon devaluation, exports are expected to rise, while imports are expected to fall.\nAccordingly, BoP crises are expected to be addressed.\n• In the wake of NEP, India devalued its currency in 1991. Devaluation was attempted in two steps:\nfirst by 9% followed by another 11% within a span of three days (July 1-July 3) in the year 1991.\n• Followed by devaluation in 1991, India opted for 'Float Exchange Rate.\n• Float Exchange Rate means value of the domestic currency in relation to foreign currencies\nis determined by the market forces of supply and demand (of different currencies) in the\ninternational money market.\n• Float Exchange Rate is expected to consolidate integration of the domestic economy with rest of\nthe world.\n(e) Trade and Investment Policy Reforms\n• Trade and Investment Policy underwent a substantial change in the wake of liberalisation.\nThe reforms in this area focussed on:\n(i) increasing international competitiveness of the domestic industry,\n(ii) inducing foreign investment in the domestic economy, and\n(iii) bringing efficient foreign technology into the economy.\n• The aim of these reforms was also to promote the efficiency of local industries and promote\nmodern technology. Trade and investment policy reforms were carried out in the form of:\n(i) Removal of Quantitative Restrictions on Imports and Exports: Quantitative restrictions11\non imports of manufactured consumer goods and agricultural goods were removed\n(or dismantled).\n(i) Reduction in Tariff Rates: Tariff 2 restrictions were considerably moderated, rather withdrawn\nfrom many items of export and import.\n10. BoP (Balance of Payments) crises - crises related to reserves of foreign exchange, and inability to pay for essential\nimports, or service the external debt.\n11. Quantitative restrictions - specific numerical limits on the quantity or value of goods that can be imported\n(or exported) during a specific period.\n12. Tariff - a tax on goor\nservices imported into a country.\n125","file":"chap3.pdf"},{"id":"ied-3-07","image":"ied-pages/ied-3-07.jpg","text":"Indian Economic Development\nHIGHLIGHTS\nIt is a term derived from'nine jewels' in the court of king Vikramaditya who were men of eminence and\n• To promote efficiency and competitiveness in the industrial sector, the government started identifying\nndustries with a potential edge in the global market and bestowed upon them the titles of Maharatnas,\nNavratnas and Miniratnas.\n• These industries were granted greater managerial and operational autonomy to make decisions, so\nthat they were able to achieve seamless growth in efficiency and profitability.\n• Greater operational, financial and managerial autonomy has also been granted to profit-making\nenterprises known as Miniratnas.\nSome examples of Maharatnas, Navratnas and Miniratnas are as under:\nMaharatnas: (i) Indian Oil Corporation (IOC) Limited, and (i) Steel Authority of India Limited (SAIL).\nNavratnas: (i) Hindustan Aeronautics Limited, and (i) Mahanagar Telephone Nigam Limited (MTNL).\nMiniratnas: (i) Bharat Sanchar Nigam Limited (BSNL), (ii) Airports Authority of India Limited, and\n(iii) Indian Railway Catering and Tourism Corporation Limited (IRCTC).\nIndianOil\nBSNL\nConnecting India\nHM SAIL\nAIRPORTS AUTHORITY OF INDIA\nMTNL\nIRCTC\nNavratnas\n• These enterprises have often been quoted by the government as the epicentre of growth in the Indian\neconomy. And, it is not denying the fact that these enterprises brought about an exemplary shift in the\nconcept of industrialisation in the economy.\n• These enterprises served not only as a significant source of employment, but also as an infrastructural\nbase that induced private investment in diverse areas of industrial growth.\n• In the wake of privatisation, the government had initially thought of disinvestment of Navratnas as well.\nBut, owing to a stiff political resistance, it is now decided to develop Navratnas as global players in\ntheir respective areas of industrialisation.\nObvious Gains and Imperative Losses of Privatisation\nObvious Gains\n(i) Privatisation implies supremacy of \"celf-interest over social interest When self inton\nprevails, the entrepreneurs work with 100 per cent commitment, and 'efficiency\" becomes\nthe condition of survival for the workers. High productivity is the obvious result.\n(i) privatication expects private enterprises to work in a competitive environment_both\ndomestic as well as international.\nCompetition induces upgradation and modernisation. These are the or\n-tial conditions of\ngrowth and development.\n(iii) Privatisation promotes\nEconomic Reforms Since 1991: New Economic Policy\ndiversification of production. Unlike PSUs, private enterprises\nnvariably generate high profits. These are used for expansion and diversification of production.\nare capable of redefining the benchmark of growth.\nMNCs (Multinational Corporations) are a testimony to the fact that private sector enterprises\n(iv) Privatisation promotes consumers' sovereignty 14.\nimplies wider choice and better quality of life.\nHigher degree of consumers' sovereignty\nImperative Losses\n(i) Socialistic pattern of the society (in which 'social interest' is accorded top priority) is left to\nsurvive only as theoretical possibility. It loses its practical relevance once PSUs are sold off to\nthe private entrepreneurs.\n(i) Privatisation encourages the free play of market forces. But in the process, goods are\nproduced only for those who have the means to buy them. When prices rise (which is an\nobvious tendency in a system driven by the free play of market forces), weaker sections of the\nsociety suffer deprivation. Sircilla Tragedy is a notable evidence to this point.\nHIGHLIGHTS\nDisinvestment\n• Disinvestment is a policy instrument to promote privatisation.\n• It occurs when the government sells off its share capital of PSUs (public sector undertakings) to the\nprivate investors.\n• Argument in favour of disinvestment is the same as in case of privatisation.\n• It is taken as a remedial measure to improve production and managerial efficiency, as well as to\nfacilitate modernisation.\n• It is also envisaged that private capital and managerial capabilities could be effectively utilised to\nimprove the performance of the PSUs.\nSircilla Tragedy\n• Sircilla, in Andhra Pradesh, is a small town, known for its powerloom industry. This industry is the main\nsource of livelihood for most people in the town.\n• Privatisation of power supply to this industry led to a substantial hike in power-tariff. Implying an\nunsustainable burden on the industry.\n• Cut in wages has been a serious consequence, as wages are linked to the production of cloth which\nhas suffered a huge decline.\n• When wages are cut beyond the point of subsistence, what do the workers do? Committing suicide is\nconsidered as a desperate option. This is what the workers did in Sircilla. About 50 workers committed\nsuicide which is known as Sicilla Tragedy.\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\nQ.11. State the major reasons for phasing out of public sector enterprises.\n(i) Mounting losses of PSUs.\n(i) Public sector enterprises were emerging as the breeding centres of corruption, leakage and\npilferage (all leading to bloating inefficiency).\nEnlist the pillars of growth and development on which privatisation rests.\nAns. (i) Upgradation and modernisation, and (i) Research and innovations.\n14. Consumers' sovereignty - a situation where production of goods and services is determined by the consumers choice\nas reflected through the p\n129","file":"chap3.pdf"},{"id":"ied-3-06","image":"ied-pages/ied-3-06.jpg","text":"0.13. Bharat Sanchar Nigam Limited has been bestowed with which title by the government?\nAns. Miniratna.\nQ. 14. State the expected gains and imperative losses from privatisation.\nAns. Expected gains of privatisation include: (i) Rise in production efficiency, driven by self-interes\nil) Uberadation and modernisation to enhance competitiveness. (ili) Diversification of producti\nto minimise market risks, (iv) Consumer's sovereignty.\nmoerative losses are: (f Disregard of social (collective) interest. (ii) Neglect of weaker certi\nof the society in the wake of profit maximisation by the private entrepreneurs.\n3.2.3 Globalisation\n• Globalisation means integrating the economy\nof a country with the economies of other\ncountries under conditions of free flow of trade\nand capital across borders.\n• Globalisation may be defined as a process\nassociated with increasing openness, growing\neconomic interdependence and deepening\neconomic integration in the world economy.\n• Economic reforms aim at integrating the Indian\nGlobalisation: Economic Integration of World Economies\neconomy with the global economy. As a result, there will be unrestricted flow of goods and\nservices, technology and expertise between India and rest of the world.\n• Particularly, it is expected that capital and technology will flow from the developed countries of\nthe world towards India.\nHIGHLIGHTS\nGlobalisation, in fact, is the outcome of the policies of Liberalisation and Privatisation\n• At the international level, it is a process of bringing the world economies, economically and socially,\nGlobal Growth.\ncloser to each other in a manner such that each country becomes instrumental to the process of\nOutsourcing\n• This is an important outcome of the process of globalisation.\n• It refers to a system of hiring business services from the\noutside world. These services include: call centres, record\nkeeping, accountancy, banking services, music recording.\nfilm editing, book transcription, clinical advice, teaching/\ncoaching and the like.\nOutsourcing as a business activity has globally expanded due\nto the expansion of Information Technology (IT).\nOutsourcing: Sourcing far/can from Outside\nprocesses also called call centres). This is because of two important reason.\nadia has emerged as an important destination of outsourcing particularly, BP tee bared bennet\nAvailability of cheap labour in India, or relatively low wage rate for\nA revolutionary growth of IT industry in India.\nyorkers, and\nEconomic Reforms Since 1991: New Economic Policy\nPolicy Strategies Promoting Globalisation of the Indian Economy\nFollowing are some important policy strategies that have influenced the process of globalisation of\nthe Indian economy:\n(a) Increase in Equity Limit of Foreign Investment: Equity limit of foreign capital investment has\nbeen raised from the initial 40 per cent. It now ranges between 51 to 100 percent.\n> In 47 high priority industries, foreign direct investment to the extent of 100 per cent has\nbeen allowed without any restriction and red-tapism.\n> Export trading houses have also been allowed foreign capital investment up to 100 per cent.\n> However, Foreign Exchange Management Act (FEMA) has been enforced. Compliance to\nFEMA has been accorded high priority.\n(b) Partial Convertibility: To achieve the objective of globalisation, partial convertibility15 of\nIndian rupee has been allowed for the following transactions:\n(i) Import and export of goods and services,\n(ii) Payment of interest or dividend on investment, and\n(iii) Remittances to meet family expenses. It is called partial convertibility because it does\nnot cover capital transactions.\n(c) Long-term Trade Policy: In conformity with economic reforms, foreign trade policy is enforced\nfor a longer duration (nearly five years). Implying that it is a liberal policy.\n> Under this policy, all restrictions and controls on foreign trade have been removed. Open\ncompetition is encouraged.\n> Barring some specific goods, most goods are traded free of restrictions.\n(d) Reduction in Tariffs: In order to encourage competitiveness, tariff barriers have been\nwithdrawn on most goods traded between India and rest of the world.\n(e) Withdrawal of Quantitative Restrictions: Since 2001, the quantitative restrictions on all import\nitems have been totally withdrawn. This is in conformity with India's commitment to the WTO.\nHIGHLIGHTS\nTariff and Non-Tariff Barriers\n• Tariff barriers mainly refer to barriers on imports through high import duty.\n• Non-tariff barriers generally refer to quota-barriers, implying quantitative restrictions on imports (or\nrestrictions on the quantum of imports).\nFirst Generation Reforms and Second Generation Reforms\n• First Generation Reforms are those which do not require any legislative action. These reforms can be\ncarried out simply through the executive and administrative machinery of the government.\n• Second Generation Reforms, on the other hand, are those which require legislative action.\nThese reforms cannot be carried out through the existing administrative and executive set-up of the\ngovernment. Accordingly, Second Generation Reforms are often delayed.\nAVLR 3.3\nGLOBALISATION - AN ESSENTIAL OUTCOME OF LIBERALISATION\n15. Partial convertibility - the sale and purchase of foreign currency (for foreign transactions) at the market price.\n131","file":"chap3.pdf"},{"id":"ied-3-05","image":"ied-pages/ied-3-05.jpg","text":"Indian Economic Development\nHIGHLIGHTS\nWorld Trade Organisation (WTO)\n• WTO commenced its operations on January 1, 1995 under Marrakesh Agreement,\nsigned by 123 countries.\n- It replaced the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade (GATT).\n- As of August 30, 2024 the WTO has 166 members.\n< RADE\nORG,\nWORLD\n• GATT was established in 1948 with 23 countries as the global trade organisation to\nadminister all multilateral trade agreements by providing equal opportunities to all\ncountries in the international market for trading purposes.\n• World Trade Organisation is expected to play an important role in the context of globalisation of the wit\neconomies. What is it supposed to do?\nFollowing five observations may be noted in this regard:\n() WTO is expected to promote fre trade by eliminating tariff and non-tariff barriers/restrictions by the mentar\n(i) It is expected to establish a rural-based trade regime in which nations cannot enforce arbitrary restrictions on trade\n(ii) It intends to promote competition and free access to the global markets by the member countries.\n(iv) It is expected to facilitate bilateral as well as multilateral trade agreements.\n(v) It is to ensure optimum utilisation of the world resources by enlarging size of the global market through\ntariff-free movement of the goods and services across different parts of the world.\nImpact (Benefits) of WTO on the Indian Economy\nFollowing are the expected benefits of WTO:\n(i) It is expected that WTO will offer greater export opportunities\nto the Indian economy. Accordingly, our share in international\ntrade is expected to increase in future.\n(ii) Under Multi-fibre Arrangements (MA), our textile and\nreadymade garment trade was subject to quota restrictions.\n- As per provisions of the WTO, all these restrictions have\nbeen removed. It has helped India to increase its exports\nof garments and textile.\nWTO Head Office in Geneva\n- Particularly, our textile exports to America and European countries have shown a substantial rise. It has\npromoted textile industry and generated employment opportunities.\n(iii) Due to 'agreed' reduction in trade barriers and reduction in subsidies to the domestic producers of agricultural\nAccordingly, India's exports of agricultural products are expected to rise.\ngoods in the developed countries, prices of these goods are expected to rise in the international market\nNote: - WTO agreement is offering only an asymmetric treatment (something which is not uniformly distributed for\nall nations of the world to capital market and labour market.\n- WTO advocates free movement of capital across different nations, not the free movement of labour, while\nboth labour and capital are commodities of the factor market.\nexploit markets in less developed countries.\n132\nis not encouraged by WTO.\n- Accordingly, Indian labour does not find free access to the lucrative factor market in develoned countries,\n(Lucrative factor market refers to the market offering high wage rate.)]\nBilateral and Multilateral Trade Agreements\n• Multilateral trade agreements refer to trade agreements of on\nBilateral trade agreements refer to trade agreements of one country with the other. Or, these are trade agreements\nbetween any two countries of the world.\nOr, these are trade agreements among many countries of the work\nmany countries of the world.\nEconomic Reforms Since 1991: New Economic Policy\n& AVLR 3.4\nWORLD TRADE ORGANISATION\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\nQ. 15. State two reasons why India is emerging as an important destination of outsourcing.\nAns.\n(i) Availability of cheap labour.\n(ii) Revolutionary growth of the IT sector.\nQ. 16. Name any two non-tariff barriers.\nAns.\n(i) Quotas, and (ii) Quantitative restrictions.\nQ. 17. Globalisation focuses more on the free movement of labour than capital in the international\nNo. It is false. Globalisation has promoted the free movement of capital, rather than labour, in\nthe international market. Both FIl (Foreign Institutional Investment) as well as FDI (Foreign Direct\nInvestment) have exponentially risen across different parts of the world. Movement of labour,\non the other hand, has remained low on account of strict immigration rules and regulations.\n3.3 AN APPRAISAL OF LPG POLICIES: PERFORMANCE OF THE\nINDIAN ECONOMY DURING REFORMS\n• LPG (Liberalisation, Privatisation and Globalisation) policies were a blend of both short-term\nmeasures and long-term measures.\n• Short-term measures were termed as 'Stabilisation Measures'\n- These measures aimed at\n(i) correcting the deficit in balance of payments, and\n(ii) moderating the pace of inflation.\n• Long-term measures focused on structural reforms in the economy.\n- These reforms aimed at\n(i) raising the level of efficiency, and\n(ii) improving the competitive environment in the economy.\n• It is largely in the light of these objectives of LPG that we shall now be attempting an appraisal of\nLPG policies. To make the matter simpler, we are attempting an appraisal of LPG policies in terms\nof Good Effect and Bad Effect of these policies.\n3.3.1 Good Effect\nFollowing observations highlight the good effect of LPG policies on the Indian economy:\n(a) Vibrant Economy: Indian economy has definitely become a more vibrant economy. Overall\nlevel of economic activity has trended up as indicated by GDP growth.\n- Post LPG policies, the growth of GDP increased from 5.6 percent during 1980-91 to\n8.2 per cent during 2007-12. GDP growth further increased to 9.4 per cent during 2021-22.\n133","file":"chap3.pdf"},{"id":"ied-3-04","image":"ied-pages/ied-3-04.jpg","text":"Indian Economic Development\n• However, during the reform period, agricultural sector has suffered a decline in its grove\nrate, while the growth rate in the industrial sector has not been consistent: it suffers\nluctuations over-time. Only the service sector has shown a robust (healthy) growt\neading to a significant conclusion that service sector has emerged as the principal drive\nof GDP growth in India.\n(b) Stimulant to Industrial Production: LPG policies have worked as a great stimulant to industria\nproduction in the Indian economy.\n» It is owing to these policies that IT industry in India has achieved global recognition.\n(c) Check on Fiscal Deficit: Mounting fiscal deficit has been a serious threat to the process of\ninvestment in the Indian economy.\n• From as high as 8.5 per cent of GDP has been brought down to around 4.4 per cent of GDP.\n(d) Increase in Foreign Exchange Reserves: Depletion of forex reserves was one of the compelling\nreasons for the government to shift to LPG policies.\n› Thanks to these policies, forex reserves of the country have now reached a comfortable level.\n> While in 1990-91, the foreign exchange reserves were US $ 6 billion, in 2018-19, these reserves\nincreased to US $ 413 billion while at the end of September 2024, these rose to US $ 704.89\nbillion. It may be noted that India is now one of the biggest forex-holders in the world.\n> Good amount of forex reserves enhances economic confidence of the global investors in\nthe Indian markets.\n(e) Check on Inflation: Owing to a greater flow of goods and services in the economy, rate of\ninflation has been lowered.\n> In the years 2011-12 to 2013-14, inflation was in the range of 6-9 per cent per annum.\n> As per World Bank estimates, inflation rate was 5.4 per cent during 2024-25.\n> It may be noted that high inflation leads to a rise in interest rate, implying a rise in the cost\nof investment. Accordingly, growth rate is hurdled.\n(f) Consumer's Sovereignty: Consumers' sovereignty has expanded over time. This is evident\nfrom the fact that a large variety of goods and services from the diverse global markets are\nnow within the easy reach of the buyers.\n> Producers are widely responding to the consumers choice and preference.\n> Consequently, overall level of expenditure of the households has tended to rise. Implying\nan overall rise in the welfare status of the people.\n(g) Flow of Private Foreign Investment: Private foreien investment has taken a quantum jump\nafter the adoption of LPG policies.\nin 2017-18.\n> This has been a matter of great relief to the government in view of the facts that:\n(i) domestic economy was not generating enough of surplus for reinvestment, and\n(ii) indigenous technology was getting obsolete.\n16. Fiscal deficit- the difference between the total expenditure and total receipts mi\n134\nEconomic Reforms Since 1991: New Economic Policy\n- It is significant to note that private foreign investment is often accompanied with\ninnovative techniques of production.\nHIGHLIGHTS\nFDI and FIl\noreign investment includes FDI (Foreign Direct Investment) and FIl (Foreign Institutional Investment)\n• FDI (Foreign Direct Investment) refers to investment by the foreigners by way of their business\nestablishments in India. It implies ownership and control of business. Examples: Coke, Pepsi,\nDomino's, McDonald's.\n• FI (Foreign Institutional Investment) refers to investment in Indian companies (by way of purchasing\ntheir equity or shares) by the foreign banking and non-banking institutions. It does not involve any kind\nof direct control on the management of the Indian companies where investment is made. Fil, unlike\nFDI, is simply an investment in the stock market in India by the foreign banking and non-banking\ninstitutions.\n(h) Recognition of India as an Emerging Economic Power: It is owing to LPG policies and the\nconsequent rise in the overall level of economic activity, that India is now being recognised\nas an emerging economic power in the world.\n> This recognition (particularly by developed nations of the world) not only raises India's\neconomic ranking in the world, but also boosts confidence of the global investors in the\nIndian economy as their preferred destination of investment.\n> India has also become a leading exporter of auto parts, engineering goods, IT software and\ntextiles.\n(i) A Shift from Monopoly Market to Competitive Market: Launch of LPG policies has caused a\nsignificant shift in the structure of the Indian markets.\n> Indian markets are now increasingly shedding its monopolistic character, and becoming\nmore and more competitive in nature.\n> For instance, a couple of decades back, products like cars, refrigerators, ACs and PCs were\nthe monopoly markets of select brands only. Now a variety of these products are available\nat competitive prices.\nBriefv owing to LpG nolicies, the Indian economy has definitely gained a 'growth momentum\nThe process of growth has not only accelic + ed. but has also become more diversified.\nThere is a definite change in welfare level of iump ople. Recognition of the Indian economy as an\nemerging economic power in the world is of crucial significance.\n3.3.2 Bad Effect\nAll that glitters is not gold There is a negative side of the story as well. Following observations\nhighlight the negative effect of LPG policies in India:\n(a) Neglect of Agriculture: Post New Economic Billion (P) of 1991, growth of GDP has largely\nbeen driven bv the secondarv and tertiary sectors of the economy\n* Agricultural sector has suffered a cerious neglect anoke, rowth rate has slided/slipped to\na miserably low level (2-3 per cent per annum).","file":"chap3.pdf"},{"id":"ied-3-03","image":"ied-pages/ied-3-03.jpg","text":"Some important observations in the context of NEP and agricultural sector of the ing\neconomy are presented below:\nFactors indicating Neglect of Agriculture\nFall in Investment\nWithdrawal of Subsidies\nCut in Import Duty\n• About: Since 1991,\npublic investment in\nthe agriculture sector\ndeclined, especially\nin agricultural\ninfrastructure sector:\nirrigation, power, roads,\nresearch, extension\nservices and market\nlinkages.\n• Impact: Under\nNEP, infrastructural\ninvestment almost dried\nup to leave agriculture\nin a vulnerable state.\n• About: Partial\nwithdrawn of fertilizer\nsubsidy has led to an\nincrease in input costs.\n• Impact: It has adversely\nhit the economic\ninterest of small and\nmarginal farmers.\n• About: In the wake of\ntrade-related reforms,\nimport duties on\nagricultural products\nhave been reduced,\nminimum support price\nhas been lowered and\nquantitative restrictions\non the import of\nagricultural products\nhave been lifted.\n• Impact: The\nIndian farmer has\nbeen exposed to\nunsustainable global\ncompetition.\nShift in Cropping Pat\n• About: The farmers ar\nincreasingly shifting to\ncash crop in place of\nsubsistence crops\n(food crops).\n• Impact:\nCommercialisation is\ngood as it promotes\ncompetitiveness, but\nis bad as it exposes\nthe subsistence farmer\nto uncertainties of the\nmarket.\nOverall Impact\n(i) There has been an upward pressure on the prices of the food grains. This has tended to\ncompound the problem of poverty in India.\n(i) Slow growth of agricultural sector must ultimately hinder the process of growth of the\nindustrial sector as well. This is because:\n> Agricultural sector is an important source of raw material for the industrial sector.\n• Agricultural sector is the principal source of labour supply to the industrial sector, and\n> Agricultural sector is a significant source of demand for the industrial products like\ntractors and threshers.\n(b) slowdown in Industrial Growth: AcIndia has removed its quantitative and qualitative barriers\non imports, there have been cheaper imports of industrial products, which have replaced the\ndemand for domestic goods.\n* Globalisation often creates conditions for the free movement of goods and corines\nthat adversely affect the local industries and employment opportunities in develoning\n* The developing countries have to remove their barriers to free trade: however, the\ndeveloped countries impose high tariffs barriers on their imports to restrict the arrace of\ndeveloping countries in their markets.\n-Forexample, although all auota restrictions on exports of textiles and cothin housheer\nremoved in India, USA has not removed their quota restriction on import of textiles from\nIndia and China.\n(c) Unemployment: Globalisation has increased the influx of foreign investment and foreien\ntechnology.\nEconomic Reforms Since 1991: New Economic Policy\n- New technology has stimulated GDP growth. But, at the same time it has ctifled Ichoked\nthe opportunities of employment.\n> It is a labour saving technology, owing to which absorption of labour has reduced in the process\nof GDP growth. Indeed, 'jobless growth is an emerging challenge of the Indian economy.\nd) Disinvestment: NEP has encouraged disinvestment by the government. It is good in so far\nas it is used as a tool to load off the loss-making assets. But when used as a tool to manage\nfiscal deficit, disinvestment highlights inefficiency of the government as a manager of the\ngovernment.\n» It is observed that as an immediate solution to the problem of fiscal deficit, the assets of\nPSUs are often undervalued causing a substantial loss to the exchequer.\n> To the extent the proceeds from disinvestment have been used for offsetting the shortage\nin revenue of the government (rather than investment in increasing the efficiency\nand profitability of the other PSUs), disinvestment (in the wake of NEP) is definitely a\nquestionable policy instrument of the government.\n(e) Fiscal Policy: Owing to NEP, fiscal policy of the government has come under a severe stress.\n> During the reform period, the Government of India had reduced tax rates to induce more\npeople to pay tax and prevent tax evasion. But this has not yielded an anticipated result:\ntax-net continues to be small and tax revenue continues to be low. This has suppressed\npublic expenditure on social welfare.\n> The reform period also marked a reduction in tariff rates and custom duties, curtailing the\noption of the government to earn additional revenues from these sources.\n> To increase foreign investment, tax incentives have been given to foreign investors which\nfurther compounded the problem of low revenue income of the government.\n> The cumulative impact of the curtailed sources of revenue has led to a constraint on\nwelfare expenditure of the government.\n(f) Lopsided Growth Process: LPG has accelerated the growth process of the Indian economy,\nbut it is lopsided.\n> It is not an inclusive growth process. It does not include all the sectors of the economy.\nInstead, it is increasingly relying on 'service sector' of the economy.\n> In fact, it is just an 'IT-focused' growth process which is gradually over-shadowing the\nprocess of industrialisation, besides neglecting the farming sector.\n> It is alarming to note that owing to liberalisation and globalisation, the Indian farmer is\nshifting to the production of cash crops for the foreign markets, causing a shortfall of\ndomestic supplies of food grains.\n- We are often forced to import food grains despite Green Revolution. Rising prices of food\nproducts towards the end of 2009 should serve as a wake up call to the fact that LPG\npolicies are almost neglecting the farming sector of the economy.","file":"chap3.pdf"},{"id":"ied-3-02","image":"ied-pages/ied-3-02.jpg","text":"Indian Economic Development\nareas of the country.\nwipro).\nTech\nMahindra\nTATA\nHCL\nInfosys\nMindtree\nWelcome to possible\nCYIENT\nORACLE:\nMphasis\nInleash the Next\nConcentration of MNC IT Companies in Urban Areas deepens the 'Rural-Urban Gulf'\n> All MNCs are focusing only on urban areas, where they find conducive infrastructural\nfacilities. This has further widened the 'rural-urban gulf'.\n• Rural-urban gulf implies economic dualism which deepens social dualism as well.\n› Economic and social dualism are always a big threat to the process of growth and development.\nHIGHLIGHTS\nEconomic and Social Dualism\n• Economic Dualism: It refers to disintegration of the economy into traditional and modern sectors of\nproduction. While the traditional sector relies upon traditional technology, the modern sector is driven\nby modern technology.\n• Social Dualism: It refers to disintegration of the society into 'haves' (enjoying high social status) and\n\"have-nots' (living with low social status).\n(h) Economic Colonialism: India suffered nearly 200 years of political colonialism during the\nBritish rule.\n> Now, while MNCs are expanding their economic control, we might suffer a sort of\neconomic colonialism.\n> Implying a situation where MNCs are exploiting the Indian markets to sell their products\nand in the process, domestic producers are marginalised owing to their poor competitive\nstrength.\n(i) Spread of Consumerism: Spread of MNCs in the country as a consequence of LPG policies\nhas resulted in a large-scale spread of consumerism??.\n> Availability of global brands has tempted the young Indians to splurge money (spend\nmoney beyond their means).\n> The Indian society is adapting itself to the western culture of spend\n17. Consumerism - the protection or promotion of the interests of consumers.\n138\nEconomic Reforms Since 1991: New Economic Policy\n» This may expand size of the market for the traders and the manufacturers, but certainly\nenhances vulnerability of the households as consumers.\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\nQ.18. What are trade agreements involving many countries called?\nAns. Multilateral trade agreements.\nQ. 19. State the meaning of FDI.\nAns. Investment by foreigners by way of their business establishments in India. It implies ownership\nand control of the business.\nQ. 20.\nWhy was private foreign investment promoted by the government?\nAns.\n(i) Domestic economy was not generating enough surplus for reinvestment.\n(ii) Indigenous technology was getting obsolete.\nQ. 21. What are short-term measures aimed at correcting the deficit in balance of payments and\ncontrolling inflation called?\nAns. Stabilisation measures.\n0. 22. State an instance which reflects how agriculture was neglected in the post-reform phase.\nAns. Fall in investment in the agriculture sector, especially in infrastructure like irrigation, power,\nroads, etc.\nQ. 23. How has industrial growth been affected by the removal of barriers on imports?\nAns. Industrial growth has slowed down due to cheaper imports of industrial products, which have\nreplaced the demand for domestic goods.\nQ. 24. Write a critical review of NEP.\nAns. A critical review of NEP includes description of positive as well as negative impact of the\neconomic reforms initiated in 1991.\nThe positive impact is evident in terms of a substantial rise in the level of economic activity in\nthe economy, as indicated by a sustained rise in GDP.\nThe negative impact is evident in terms of sectoral imbalances and concentration of economic\npower. Sectoral imbalances have occurred on account of the fact that:\n(i) agricultural sector has suffered a setback while the industrial and tertiary sectors have\ngained a momentum, and\n(ii) growth process has largely been confined to urban sector of the economy.\nConcentration of economic power has occurred owing to rapid control of the domestic market\nby the multinational corporations.\nWhich Way to Go?\n• Should we or should we not subscribe to the LPG policies? It is a debatable issue. But avoiding\nthe pros & cons of LPG, the +2 graders can definitely make one concrete observation: that LPG\npolicies are the only way out to accelerate the pace of growth and development.\n• Indeed perusal of LPG policies was to a great extent a matter of economic compulsion rather than\na matter of choice for the politicians of the country.\nHowever, a compulsion should never mean an abject surrender. It is strongly recommended that\nLPG policies are nursued with guarded precautions.\n139","file":"chap3.pdf"},{"id":"ied-3-01","image":"ied-pages/ied-3-01.jpg","text":"Indian Economic Development\n• Notably:\n¡i) We must see to it that we do not surrender to big players in the international markete\n(ii) We must see to it that we do not compromise with economic interest of our dome\nproducers, while allowing a free access to the foreign investors in the Indian markets.\n(ili) We must see to it that we do not become economically subservient to multinational corporati\n(iv) We must be in a position to channelise FDI (foreien direct investment) more into areas:\ninfrastructure rather than retail trading or fast-food junctions like KFC and Domino's.\nPower Points [Summary of the Chapter]\n• Economic Reforms: Economic reforms refer to all those measures which aim at rendering the econo-\nmore efficient, competitive and developed.\n• Liberalisation, privatisation and globalisation are the principal elements of economic reforms in India.\n• Liberalisation: Liberalisation implies freedom of the producing units from controls imposed by the government.\n• Liberalisation includes industrial sector reforms, financial sector reforms, tax reforms, foreign exchange\nreforms, trade and investment policy reforms.\nIndustrial Sector Reforms: (i) Abolition of industrial licensing, (ii) Contraction of public sector,\n(i) De-reservation of production areas, (iv) Expansion of production capacity, (v) Freedom to import\ncapital goods.\nFinancial Sector Reforms: These refer to the reforms in country's monetary and banking policies.\nTax Reforms: These refer to increasing the revenue of the government and lowering the expenditure in a\nway that it causes no adverse effect on production and economic welfare.\n• Foreign Exchange Reforms: These reforms were focused on: (i) Increasing international competitiveness\nof industrial sector, i) Promotion of foreign investment in the economy, (i) Promotion of adoption of\nmodern technology.\nTrade and Investment Policy Reforms: ()Dismantling of quantitative restrictions on imports and exports,\n(i) Reduction in tariff rates, (i Removal of import licensing, (i) Removal of export duties.\n• Privatisation: It implies partial or full ownership and management of public sector enterprise hy th\nprivate sector.\nsale of the government enterprises to the private entrepreneurs.\nMeasures of Privatisation: (i) Withdrawal of the government ownership and management, (ii) Outright\nand deepening economic integration in the global economy.\nGlobalisation: It is a process associated with increasing openness, growing economic interdenan dance\nPolicy Strategies to Promote Globalisation: (i) Increase in equity limit of foreign investment. (il) Partial\n•Good Effect of the LPG Policies: (i) Virbrant economy, (ii) Stimulant to industrial production, (ill) Check on\nfiscal deficit, (iv) Substantial increase in foreign exchange reserves, (V) Check on inflation. (vi) Consumer's\npower, (ix) A shift from monopoly market to competitive market.\nsovereignty, (vil) Flow of private foreign investment, (vili) Recognition of India as an emerging economic\n(iii) Unemployment, (iv) Disinvestment, (v) Fiscal policy, (vi) Lopsi\n•Bad Effect of the LPG Policies: (i) Neglect of agriculture, (ii) Slowdown in industrial arowth\nconcentration of growth process, (vili) Economic colonialism * Sp\n140\nith process, (vil) Urban\namerism.\nEconomic Reforms Since 1991: New Economic Policy\nWORK-BOOK (Question-answer Format)\nCompetency Based Learning: Objective Questions\n1. Multiple Choice Questions\nChoose the correct option:\n1. Read the given text carefully:\n\"The Government has now decided to further promote the development of mutual funds by\nthrowing the field open to the private sector and joint sector mutual funds. In order to safeguard\nthe interests of the investing public, and to encourage a healthy growth of the capital markets,\na comprehensive set of guidelines is being evolved for the operation of all mutual funds.\"\"\nSource: https://www.indiabudget.gov.in/doc/bspeech/bs199192.pdf (Budget 1991-92 Speech of\nShri Manmohan Singh Minister of Finance)\nIdentify the sector under which the aforesaid reform was introduced and choose the correct\noption.\n[CBSE Sample Paper 2026]\n(a) Industrial\n(b) Financial\n(c) Tax\n(d) Foreign trade\n2. Identify, which of the following correctly defines liberalisation.\n[CBSE 2025 (58/6/1)]\n(a) Outright sale of part of shares of Public Sector Undertakings (PSUs)\n(b) Increased integration with the rest of the world\n(c) Removal of restrictions imposed by government on different sectors of the economy\n(d) Focus on import substitution\n3. Outsourcing from India has become more intensified in recent times due to the expansion of the\n[CBSE 2025 (58/7/1)]\nsector.\n4.\n(a) Agriculture\n(b) Manufacturing\n(c) Information Technology\n(d) Construction\nimplies shedding of the ownership or management of a government owned\n[CBSE (C) 2025]\nenterprise.\n(a) Liberalisation\n(b) Privatisation\n(d) Nationalisation\n(c) Globalisation\n5. Identify, which of the following statement is incorrect about the financial sector reform\n[CBSE Sample Paper 2025]\nintroduced in 1991?\n(a) Enabled the establishment of private sector banks, Indian as well as foreign\n(b) Foreign investment limit in banks was raised to around 74%\n(c) Foreign Institutional Investors were allowed to invest in Indian financial markets\n(d) Change in the role of the Reserve Bank of India from facilitator to regulator\n6. India has become a preferred outsourcing destination because of\n[CBSE 2021 (58/1/4), Term-1]\n(i) Availability of unskilled labour\n(ii) Quality of IT services\n(iii) Availability of skilled labour\nAlternatives:\n(a) o\n(b) (i) and (ii)\n(d) (ii) and (iii)\n141","file":"chap3.pdf"}],"boxes":[{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"ied-3-12","image":"ied-pages/ied-3-12.jpg","text":"Q. 1. Prior to the launching of economic reforms, whom did India approach for availing a loan?\nAns. World Bank and IMF.\nQ.2.\nThe economic reforms were based around which assumption?\nAns.\nMarket forces would drive the economy towards the path of competitive growth and development.\nQ. 3.\n\"India had asked for a $ 1.8 billion bailout loan from the International Monetary Fund (IMF),\nwhich demanded de-regulation in return.\"\nSource: financialexpress.com\nState any two factors that compelled India to take a loan from the IMF.\nAns. Two principal factors that compelled India to take a loan from IMF are as these:\n(i) Mounting fiscal deficit which led us to a debt trap.\n(ii) Unprecedented BoP crises and severely depleted forex reserves."},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-3-11","image":"ied-pages/ied-3-11.jpg","text":"Demonetisation\nlemonetisation, introduced in 2016 is closelv related to financial sector reforms. Let us understand its concent...\nconsequences in the context of the Indian economy.\n• Demonetisation is a policy action of the government that withdraws the status of 'legal tender\" from the existing.\n- Once the status of 'legal tender' is withdrawn, the existing currency (or the currency notes) are reduced meet\nto pieces of paper.\n- These notes lose their acceptance as a medium of exchange, or they lose their power to buy goods and service\n- It was on November 8, 2016 that the Government of India announced demonetisation of the currency notes of\n· 500 and 1,000.\n- The people were required to deposit the demonetised currency notes with the banks within a period of 2 months.\nThe demonetised notes were replaced by new currency notes of 500 and 7 2,000.\n• Basic Purpose\n- Basically, demonetisation aims at curbing illegal transactions and anti-social activities (funded through illegal\ntransactions).\n• Principal Merits\n(i) It helps unearth (find out) black money.\n(ii) Reduction of black money leads to shrinkage of shadow economy (an economy with unrecorded production\nactivity and tax evasion).\n(ii) When shadow economy shrinks and tax evasion is reduced, government revenue tends to rise.\n(iv) Demonetisation compels people to deposit their demonetised notes with the banks. Accordingly, financial-base\nof the banking sector tends to expand.\n(v) A check on funding to terrorists helps combat anti-social activities in the economy.\n(vi) With cash almost disappearing from the market, people were driven to digital mode of transactions. This was\na big move towards cashless (or low cash) economy. It also promoted banking habits of the people, a big leap\ntowards financial inclusion.\n• Principal Demerits\n(i) In a developing economy like India, people are used to cash-transactions. With demonetisation, people are\nstripped of (deprived of cash in hand.\n- It impairs (disturbs) their routine transactions and therefore, their normal living. Thus, demonetisation in 2016\nled to a serious social chaos (unrest) in India.\n(i) Cash-crunch (owing to demonetisation) jolts production activity in the shadow economy. Accordingly, jobs are\nlost and poor people are further marginalised.\n(il) A jolt to production activity in the shadow economy invariably hurts the overal level of economic activity\nAncluding production, consumption and investment) in the economy. The economy starts facing slowdown:\nIndia is yet to recover from the slowdown triggered by demonetisation in 2016.\n(iv Nearly 90 percent of the workforce in India is engaged in informal economy for unordaniced copter of the\neconomy for their livelihood. And this seament of the economy is highly cash dependent and desh dancitiva\n- A huge cut in liquidity (following note ban) implied that cash purchase of inputs and such payment of manes\nlarge-scale lav-off, Daily wagers loet their aroleument from day-tof demanatice an\nbecame impossible. These led to an instantaneous cut in production activity. Consequently, there was a\n(v) Exchange of demonetised currency with the valid currency leads to long queues at the banks. People feel hurt\nwhen they find it difficultto use their own money. Accordingly, consumption is lowered and inducemento invest\npeople in India have successfully managed.\n122\nEconomic Reforms Since 1991: New Economic Policy\n(c) Tax Reforms\nand public expenditure policy of the government.\nTax' reforms are an important component of fiscal policy of the government or taxation (revenue)"},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-3-11","image":"ied-pages/ied-3-11.jpg","text":"Fiscal Policy\n.. It refers to taxation (revenue) and public expenditure policy of the government.\n• It is a policy that seeks to achieve stability in the economy by managing the revenue and expenditure\nof the government.\nTaxes are broadly classified as under:\nClassification of Taxes\nDirect Tax\n• Direct taxes are those taxes, the burden of\nwhich cannot be shifted onto others.\n• Examples: Income tax, corporation tax,\nwealth tax.\n• One who pays such a tax himself bears\nthe burden of it.\n• Indirect taxes (levied on goods and\nservices) are those taxes, the burden of\nwhich can be shifted onto others.\n• Examples: GST (Goods and Services Tax),\ncustom duty.\n• One who pays such a tax to the\ngovernment can shift the burden of this\ntax onto the final buyers of the goods by\nadding the tax amount to the basic price\nof the goods sold.\n• Prior to liberalisation, tax structure was quite complex and tax rates were quite high. High tax\nrates induced tax evasion®, causing loss of revenue to the government.\n• Following liberalisation, income tax and corporate tax have gradually\nbeen reduced.\nIndirect tax structure has also been reformed to establish a common national market for goods\nand services.\n• In 2016, the Indian Parliament enacted Goods and Services Tax (GST) Act 2016, to simplify and\nintroduce a unified indirect tax system in India.\n- This law came into effect from July 2017. This has raised tax compliance and therefore, tax\nrevenue of the government.\n- GST is expected to generate additional tax revenue for the government, reduce tax evasion and\ncreate 'one nation, one tax and one market'.\n7. Tax - a compulsory payment imposed on an individual, household or corporation by a government entity.\n8. Tax evasion - an illegal practice where a person or an entity intentionally does not pay due taxes.\n9. Tax compliance - refers to the extent to which individuals and organisations obey tax laws.\n123"},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-3-10","image":"ied-pages/ied-3-10.jpg","text":"Economic Reforms (with liberalisation as its key component) were based on the assumption that market\nforces would drive the economy towards the path of competitive growth and development.\nEconomic Reforms under Liberalisation\nLiberalisation included the following reforms:\n(a) Industrial Sector Reforms\n• Liberalisation virtually implied de-regulation of industrial sector of the economy."},{"title":"Following flow chart highlights how de-regulation of industrial sector happened:","page":"ied-3-10","image":"ied-pages/ied-3-10.jpg","text":"Liberalisation Measures\nAbolition of\nIndustrial Licensing\n• In July 1991, a new\nindustrial policy was\nannounced.\n• It abolished the\nrequirement of\nlicensing except for\nthe following five\nindustries:\n(i) liquor,\n(ii) cigarette,\n(iii) defence\nequipments,\n(iv) industrial\nexplosives, and\nContraction of\nPublic Sector\n• Under the new\nindustrial policy,\nnumber of industries\nreserved for public\nsector was reduced\nfrom 17 to 8.\n• In 2010-11, the\nnumber of these\nindustries was\nreduced merely to\nthree including:\n(i) Atomic energy,\n(ii) Railways, and\n(iii) Defence\nequipments.\n(v) dangerous\nchemicals.\n*SS/ (Small-scale industries) - industries that manufacture, produce and render\nDe-reservation of\nProduction Areas\nExpansion of\nProduction Capacity\nFreedom to Import\n- Many production\nareas which earlier\n• Earlier production\nCapital Goods\nwere reserved for\ncapacity was linked\nSSI (small-scale\nwith licensing.\n• Liberalisation also\nindustries)* were\nNow, freedom from\nimplied freedom for\nde-reserved.\nlicensing implied\nthe industrialists\nfreedom from\nto import capital\n• Forces of the market\nwere allowed to\ncapacity constraints.\ngoods with a view\ndetermine allocation\n• 'What to produce\nto upgrading their\nof resources (rather\nand how much to\ntechnology.\nthan the directive\n• Permission\nproduce was now a\npolicy of the\nmatter of producer's\ngovernment).\nchoice depending on\nwas no longer\nmarket conditions.\nrequired from the\ngovernment to enter\ninto international\nagreements for\nthe import of\ntechnology.\nhall or micro level.\n120\n(b) Financial Sector® Reforms\nEconomic Reforms Since 1991: New Economic Policy\na Financial sector reforms related primarily to the regulation and control of financial institutions,\nincluding commercial banks and investment banks. In India, these institutions are regulated and\ncontrolled by the RBI (Reserve Bank of India).\n• Liberalisation\nimplied\na substantial shift\nin the role of the RBI from 'a regulator' to\n'a facilitator' of the financial sector.\n- As a regulator (prior to liberalisation), the\nRBI would itself fix interest rate structure for\nthe commercial banks.\n- But as a facilitator (after liberalisation),\nBANK OF\nthe RBI would only facilitate the free play\nof the market forces and leave it to the\ncommercial banks to decide their interest\nRBI - The Apex Bank of the Country\nrate structure.\n- Now, competition (rather than control) rules the decision-making process."},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-3-10","image":"ied-pages/ied-3-10.jpg","text":"• Consequent upon the policy of liberalisation, there has been a substantial shift in the role of the RBI\nfrom 'a regulator' to 'a facilitator' of the financial sector.\n• Now, market forces (rather than controls) decide the interest rate structure, volume and the pattern of\ninvestment.\nSignificant Changes in the Wake of Liberalisation\n• Free play of the market forces has led to the emergence of private sector banks-both domestic as\nwell as international -in the Indian banking industry.\n• FIl (Foreign Institutional Investors) such as merchant bankers, mutual funds and pension funds are\nnow allowed to invest in Indian financial markets. This has led to a significant expansion of the\nfinancial sector in India.\n• Banks fulfilling certain conditions are allowed to set-up new branches without the approval\nof RBI.\n• Banks have been given permission to generate resources from India and abroad. In this context, it\nis important to note that foreign investment limit in banks is as high as 74 per cent.\nOverall Impact\nConsequent upon these changes, financial sector in India has shown a multi-dimensional growth and\nis playing a significant role in the growth and development of the economy.\n5. Financial sector - The learners may note that the financial sector includes: (i) financial institutions such as commercial\nbanks and investment banks, (ii) stock exchange market, and (ili) foreign exchange market.\n6. Financial markets - a system that provides buyers and sellers the means to trade financial instruments, including\nbonds, equities, international currencies, etc.\n121"},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-3-09","image":"ied-pages/ied-3-09.jpg","text":"Don't Confuse Liberalisation with a Laissez-Faire System\n• Laissez-faire refers to a system in which there is no intervention by the state in the functioning of\n- All decisions relating to allocation of resources and the goods & services to be produced are takens\nproducers on the basis of market forces of supply and demand.\n- Role of the government is restricted just to the maintenance of law & order and defence of the coum,\nfrom external aggressions: it is nothing beyond being a night watchman of the country.\n• Liberalisation should not be confused with a laissez-faire system. Liberalisation only implies a situatio\nwherein the government allows greater degree of freedom and flexibility to the private entrepreneurs\nmatters relating to allocation of resources.\n- To illustrate, pursuing the policy of liberalisation, the government may abolish licensing/registration d\nthe enterprises as an essential requirement.\n- Likewise, the government may liberalise or abolish limits on the production capacity of the firms.\nBut all this is in consonance with direct participation of the government in production activity.\n- Thus, liberalisation does not exclude government's intervention in the economy; it does not rule out.\nthe existence of checks and controls by the government.\n- It only implies greater degree of freedom to the private entrepreneurs in deciding their areas of\neconomic activity and expanding their scale of production."},{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"ied-3-09","image":"ied-pages/ied-3-09.jpg","text":"Q. 4. Who decides the interest rate in situations where RBl acts as a facilitator rather than a\nregulator of the financial sector?\nAns. Commercial banks via free play of the market forces.\nQ. 5. When did demonetisation occur in India?\nAns. On November 8, 2016.\nQ. 6. Wealth tax is an example of which type of tax?\nAns. Direct tax.\nQ.7. Value added tax has been replaced by which tax?\nAns. GST (Goods and Services tax).\n2. 8. Lowering the value of domestic currency in relation to foreign currencies is called?\nAns. Devaluation.\nQ. 9. A system in which there is no intervention by the state in the functioning of an economy is\ncalled\nAns. Laissez-faire.\n12. Import licensing - an administrative procedure requiring documentation prior to the import of goode\n126\nEconomic Reforms Since 1991: New Economic Policy\nQ. 10. Why were controls imposed post independence in India?\nIn the context of the Indian experience, controls were imposed by the government with a view\n(i) checking the growth of private monopolies, and\n(ii) minimising the hold of large industrial houses on the financial resources of the country.\nIllustration: Some banks were nationalised in sixties because these were owned by the big\nindustrial houses who would use banking funds primarily to meet their own financial needs.\n3.2.2 Privatisation\n• Privatisation is the process of involving the private sector in the ownership or operation of a\ngovernment owned enterprise.\nIt implies gradual withdrawal of government ownership/management from the public sector\nenterprises. It may happen in two ways:\n(i) By withdrawal of the government ownership and management from the public sector\ncompanies, or\n(ii) By outright sale of the public sector companies to the private entrepreneurs.\nJourney towards Privatisation\nThe journey from PSEs (public sector enterprises as the fulcrum of growth) to privatisation\n(private sector enterprises as the fulcrum of growth) is explained through the flow chart as given\nbelow:\nJOURNEY TOWARDS PRIVATISATION\n1956 IPR\n(Industrial Policy\nResolution)\nPrincipal Gains\n(1956-1990)\nPrincipal Losses\n(causing the\nneed for\nprivatisation)\n• PSEs begin to play as 'fulcrum of growth.\nNavratnas - the industries of eminance.\nDiversification of industrial base.\nStructural transformation* of the economy (increase in percentage contribution\nof secondary sector to GDP).\nIncreasing inefficiencies in production.\nPSEs as breeding centres of leakage, pilferage and corruption.\nPSEs as 'deadweight loss' (social liability).\nProcess of privatisation begins.\nPSEs start phasing out: Equity of PSEs being sold off to private enterprises.\n* Structural transformation - the reallocation of economic activity across the three sectors, viz., primary, secondary and\ntertiary sectors of the economy.\n127"},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-3-08","image":"ied-pages/ied-3-08.jpg","text":"• GST (Goods and Services Tax has been introduced in India with a view to providing a uniform tax structure across\nall parts of the country.\n• It is a one tax in place of all taxes on goods and services, and it is a uniform tax across all states of the count\nThus, GST carries the slogan of 'one tax, one nation, one market.\n• GST is expected to generate additional tax revenue for the government, increase\ntax compliance and reduce tax evasion.\n• The Basic Structure of GST\n- Goods and services have been classified into different categories.\n- Each category of goods carries a specific rate of taxation. And, there is a\nGST\nGOODS AND SERVICES TAX\ncategory of goods which is tax-exempt, considering the needs and means of\npoorer section of the society.\nTax Reform\nFollowing flow chart shows the structure of GST:\nStructure of GST\n124\nCategory of Goods\nUltra-High Temperature (UHT) milk, paratha, pre-packaged paneer, packaged snacks,\npencils, sharpeners, crayons, exercise books, graph books, essential life-saving drugs\nTax Rate\n0%\nCondensed milk, butter, cheese, packaged food items, preserved vegetables, fruits, toilet\nsoap, bicycles, leather, footwear, textiles, handicrafts, toys\n5%\nPetrol and Petrol Hybrid, LPG, CNG cars (more than 1200cc and more than 4000mm), three\nwheelers, motorcycles (more than 350cc), air conditioners, television, dish washing machines\n18%\nmotorcycles exceeding 350cc\nLuxury and sin goods like tobacco products, pan masala, aerated drinks, high-end cars,\n40%\n• Typical Characteristics\nThree typical characteristics of GST must be noted:\n® GST is levied at each stage of value addition.\n(r) ST focuses on the supply of goods rather than their production. Tax is levied as and when goods are supplied\n(or when goods leave their destination for their buyers).\n(il) GST paid by the producers on the purchase of inputs is allowed to be adjusted by the producers in their payment\nof GST proceeds to the government.\n• Principal Merits\nFour principal merits of GST are as under:\nItis simplified tay structure, as it is one tax instead of all the indirect taxes, and it is a uniform tay acred all narte\nSuppliers. it ensures hicher dearee of transparency in business: Black money tranean one are reducer\n(i) Being a uniform tax across all parts of the country. GST has enhanced size of the made for the domestic\n• Some Teething Problems and Demerits\n(iv) GST has raised government revenue as there is a higher degree of transparency in business.\nAny new concept always suffers from some teething problems when it comes to its implementation coe CST.\nThe teething problems of GST are often highlighted as its demerits. Two observations are notable in this context:\n)GST is vet to cover all goods produced in the country. Electricity generation, alcohol, petrol and diesel are some\nnotable products out of the ambit of GST.\n[Note: Forfurther details on GST, students are advised to refer to Project Work at\nfiy GST rates across different goods and services are still not finally settled, and to that extent uncertainty looms in\nthe economy. This uncertainty hampers decision-making and therefore,\nproduction activity.\nctory Macroeconomics.)\nEconomic Reforms Since 1991: New Economic Policy\n* AVLR 3.2\nGOODS AND SERVICES TAX (GST)\n(d) Foreign Exchange Reforms\n• Foreign exchange market relates to external sector of the economy, and is regulated by the RBI in\nIndia.\n• BoP crises\" on the eve of NEP prompted the government to initiate foreign exchange reforms,\nstarting with devaluation of the Indian currency against foreign currencies.\n• Devaluation means deliberately (or officially) lowering the value of domestic currency in relation\nto foreign currencies. It is a policy instrument pursued under 'Fixed Exchange Rate' system.\nConsequent upon devaluation, exports are expected to rise, while imports are expected to fall.\nAccordingly, BoP crises are expected to be addressed.\n• In the wake of NEP, India devalued its currency in 1991. Devaluation was attempted in two steps:\nfirst by 9% followed by another 11% within a span of three days (July 1-July 3) in the year 1991.\n• Followed by devaluation in 1991, India opted for 'Float Exchange Rate.\n• Float Exchange Rate means value of the domestic currency in relation to foreign currencies\nis determined by the market forces of supply and demand (of different currencies) in the\ninternational money market.\n• Float Exchange Rate is expected to consolidate integration of the domestic economy with rest of\nthe world.\n(e) Trade and Investment Policy Reforms\n• Trade and Investment Policy underwent a substantial change in the wake of liberalisation.\nThe reforms in this area focussed on:\n(i) increasing international competitiveness of the domestic industry,\n(ii) inducing foreign investment in the domestic economy, and\n(iii) bringing efficient foreign technology into the economy.\n• The aim of these reforms was also to promote the efficiency of local industries and promote\nmodern technology. Trade and investment policy reforms were carried out in the form of:\n(i) Removal of Quantitative Restrictions on Imports and Exports: Quantitative restrictions11\non imports of manufactured consumer goods and agricultural goods were removed\n(or dismantled).\n(i) Reduction in Tariff Rates: Tariff 2 restrictions were considerably moderated, rather withdrawn\nfrom many items of export and import.\n10. BoP (Balance of Payments) crises - crises related to reserves of foreign exchange, and inability to pay for essential\nimports, or service the external debt.\n11. Quantitative restrictions - specific numerical limits on the quantity or value of goods that can be imported\n(or exported) during a specific period.\n12. Tariff - a tax on goor\nservices imported into a country.\n125"},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-3-07","image":"ied-pages/ied-3-07.jpg","text":"It is a term derived from'nine jewels' in the court of king Vikramaditya who were men of eminence and\n• To promote efficiency and competitiveness in the industrial sector, the government started identifying\nndustries with a potential edge in the global market and bestowed upon them the titles of Maharatnas,\nNavratnas and Miniratnas.\n• These industries were granted greater managerial and operational autonomy to make decisions, so\nthat they were able to achieve seamless growth in efficiency and profitability.\n• Greater operational, financial and managerial autonomy has also been granted to profit-making\nenterprises known as Miniratnas.\nSome examples of Maharatnas, Navratnas and Miniratnas are as under:\nMaharatnas: (i) Indian Oil Corporation (IOC) Limited, and (i) Steel Authority of India Limited (SAIL).\nNavratnas: (i) Hindustan Aeronautics Limited, and (i) Mahanagar Telephone Nigam Limited (MTNL).\nMiniratnas: (i) Bharat Sanchar Nigam Limited (BSNL), (ii) Airports Authority of India Limited, and\n(iii) Indian Railway Catering and Tourism Corporation Limited (IRCTC).\nIndianOil\nBSNL\nConnecting India\nHM SAIL\nAIRPORTS AUTHORITY OF INDIA\nMTNL\nIRCTC\nNavratnas\n• These enterprises have often been quoted by the government as the epicentre of growth in the Indian\neconomy. And, it is not denying the fact that these enterprises brought about an exemplary shift in the\nconcept of industrialisation in the economy.\n• These enterprises served not only as a significant source of employment, but also as an infrastructural\nbase that induced private investment in diverse areas of industrial growth.\n• In the wake of privatisation, the government had initially thought of disinvestment of Navratnas as well.\nBut, owing to a stiff political resistance, it is now decided to develop Navratnas as global players in\ntheir respective areas of industrialisation.\nObvious Gains and Imperative Losses of Privatisation\nObvious Gains\n(i) Privatisation implies supremacy of \"celf-interest over social interest When self inton\nprevails, the entrepreneurs work with 100 per cent commitment, and 'efficiency\" becomes\nthe condition of survival for the workers. High productivity is the obvious result.\n(i) privatication expects private enterprises to work in a competitive environment_both\ndomestic as well as international.\nCompetition induces upgradation and modernisation. These are the or\n-tial conditions of\ngrowth and development.\n(iii) Privatisation promotes\nEconomic Reforms Since 1991: New Economic Policy\ndiversification of production. Unlike PSUs, private enterprises\nnvariably generate high profits. These are used for expansion and diversification of production.\nare capable of redefining the benchmark of growth.\nMNCs (Multinational Corporations) are a testimony to the fact that private sector enterprises\n(iv) Privatisation promotes consumers' sovereignty 14.\nimplies wider choice and better quality of life.\nHigher degree of consumers' sovereignty\nImperative Losses\n(i) Socialistic pattern of the society (in which 'social interest' is accorded top priority) is left to\nsurvive only as theoretical possibility. It loses its practical relevance once PSUs are sold off to\nthe private entrepreneurs.\n(i) Privatisation encourages the free play of market forces. But in the process, goods are\nproduced only for those who have the means to buy them. When prices rise (which is an\nobvious tendency in a system driven by the free play of market forces), weaker sections of the\nsociety suffer deprivation. Sircilla Tragedy is a notable evidence to this point."},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-3-07","image":"ied-pages/ied-3-07.jpg","text":"Disinvestment\n• Disinvestment is a policy instrument to promote privatisation.\n• It occurs when the government sells off its share capital of PSUs (public sector undertakings) to the\nprivate investors.\n• Argument in favour of disinvestment is the same as in case of privatisation.\n• It is taken as a remedial measure to improve production and managerial efficiency, as well as to\nfacilitate modernisation.\n• It is also envisaged that private capital and managerial capabilities could be effectively utilised to\nimprove the performance of the PSUs.\nSircilla Tragedy\n• Sircilla, in Andhra Pradesh, is a small town, known for its powerloom industry. This industry is the main\nsource of livelihood for most people in the town.\n• Privatisation of power supply to this industry led to a substantial hike in power-tariff. Implying an\nunsustainable burden on the industry.\n• Cut in wages has been a serious consequence, as wages are linked to the production of cloth which\nhas suffered a huge decline.\n• When wages are cut beyond the point of subsistence, what do the workers do? Committing suicide is\nconsidered as a desperate option. This is what the workers did in Sircilla. About 50 workers committed\nsuicide which is known as Sicilla Tragedy."},{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"ied-3-07","image":"ied-pages/ied-3-07.jpg","text":"Q.11. State the major reasons for phasing out of public sector enterprises.\n(i) Mounting losses of PSUs.\n(i) Public sector enterprises were emerging as the breeding centres of corruption, leakage and\npilferage (all leading to bloating inefficiency).\nEnlist the pillars of growth and development on which privatisation rests.\nAns. (i) Upgradation and modernisation, and (i) Research and innovations.\n14. Consumers' sovereignty - a situation where production of goods and services is determined by the consumers choice\nas reflected through the p\n129"},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-3-06","image":"ied-pages/ied-3-06.jpg","text":"Globalisation, in fact, is the outcome of the policies of Liberalisation and Privatisation\n• At the international level, it is a process of bringing the world economies, economically and socially,\nGlobal Growth.\ncloser to each other in a manner such that each country becomes instrumental to the process of\nOutsourcing\n• This is an important outcome of the process of globalisation.\n• It refers to a system of hiring business services from the\noutside world. These services include: call centres, record\nkeeping, accountancy, banking services, music recording.\nfilm editing, book transcription, clinical advice, teaching/\ncoaching and the like.\nOutsourcing as a business activity has globally expanded due\nto the expansion of Information Technology (IT).\nOutsourcing: Sourcing far/can from Outside\nprocesses also called call centres). This is because of two important reason.\nadia has emerged as an important destination of outsourcing particularly, BP tee bared bennet\nAvailability of cheap labour in India, or relatively low wage rate for\nA revolutionary growth of IT industry in India.\nyorkers, and\nEconomic Reforms Since 1991: New Economic Policy\nPolicy Strategies Promoting Globalisation of the Indian Economy\nFollowing are some important policy strategies that have influenced the process of globalisation of\nthe Indian economy:\n(a) Increase in Equity Limit of Foreign Investment: Equity limit of foreign capital investment has\nbeen raised from the initial 40 per cent. It now ranges between 51 to 100 percent.\n> In 47 high priority industries, foreign direct investment to the extent of 100 per cent has\nbeen allowed without any restriction and red-tapism.\n> Export trading houses have also been allowed foreign capital investment up to 100 per cent.\n> However, Foreign Exchange Management Act (FEMA) has been enforced. Compliance to\nFEMA has been accorded high priority.\n(b) Partial Convertibility: To achieve the objective of globalisation, partial convertibility15 of\nIndian rupee has been allowed for the following transactions:\n(i) Import and export of goods and services,\n(ii) Payment of interest or dividend on investment, and\n(iii) Remittances to meet family expenses. It is called partial convertibility because it does\nnot cover capital transactions.\n(c) Long-term Trade Policy: In conformity with economic reforms, foreign trade policy is enforced\nfor a longer duration (nearly five years). Implying that it is a liberal policy.\n> Under this policy, all restrictions and controls on foreign trade have been removed. Open\ncompetition is encouraged.\n> Barring some specific goods, most goods are traded free of restrictions.\n(d) Reduction in Tariffs: In order to encourage competitiveness, tariff barriers have been\nwithdrawn on most goods traded between India and rest of the world.\n(e) Withdrawal of Quantitative Restrictions: Since 2001, the quantitative restrictions on all import\nitems have been totally withdrawn. This is in conformity with India's commitment to the WTO."},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-3-06","image":"ied-pages/ied-3-06.jpg","text":"Tariff and Non-Tariff Barriers\n• Tariff barriers mainly refer to barriers on imports through high import duty.\n• Non-tariff barriers generally refer to quota-barriers, implying quantitative restrictions on imports (or\nrestrictions on the quantum of imports).\nFirst Generation Reforms and Second Generation Reforms\n• First Generation Reforms are those which do not require any legislative action. These reforms can be\ncarried out simply through the executive and administrative machinery of the government.\n• Second Generation Reforms, on the other hand, are those which require legislative action.\nThese reforms cannot be carried out through the existing administrative and executive set-up of the\ngovernment. Accordingly, Second Generation Reforms are often delayed.\nAVLR 3.3\nGLOBALISATION - AN ESSENTIAL OUTCOME OF LIBERALISATION\n15. Partial convertibility - the sale and purchase of foreign currency (for foreign transactions) at the market price.\n131"},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-3-05","image":"ied-pages/ied-3-05.jpg","text":"World Trade Organisation (WTO)\n• WTO commenced its operations on January 1, 1995 under Marrakesh Agreement,\nsigned by 123 countries.\n- It replaced the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade (GATT).\n- As of August 30, 2024 the WTO has 166 members.\n< RADE\nORG,\nWORLD\n• GATT was established in 1948 with 23 countries as the global trade organisation to\nadminister all multilateral trade agreements by providing equal opportunities to all\ncountries in the international market for trading purposes.\n• World Trade Organisation is expected to play an important role in the context of globalisation of the wit\neconomies. What is it supposed to do?\nFollowing five observations may be noted in this regard:\n() WTO is expected to promote fre trade by eliminating tariff and non-tariff barriers/restrictions by the mentar\n(i) It is expected to establish a rural-based trade regime in which nations cannot enforce arbitrary restrictions on trade\n(ii) It intends to promote competition and free access to the global markets by the member countries.\n(iv) It is expected to facilitate bilateral as well as multilateral trade agreements.\n(v) It is to ensure optimum utilisation of the world resources by enlarging size of the global market through\ntariff-free movement of the goods and services across different parts of the world.\nImpact (Benefits) of WTO on the Indian Economy\nFollowing are the expected benefits of WTO:\n(i) It is expected that WTO will offer greater export opportunities\nto the Indian economy. Accordingly, our share in international\ntrade is expected to increase in future.\n(ii) Under Multi-fibre Arrangements (MA), our textile and\nreadymade garment trade was subject to quota restrictions.\n- As per provisions of the WTO, all these restrictions have\nbeen removed. It has helped India to increase its exports\nof garments and textile.\nWTO Head Office in Geneva\n- Particularly, our textile exports to America and European countries have shown a substantial rise. It has\npromoted textile industry and generated employment opportunities.\n(iii) Due to 'agreed' reduction in trade barriers and reduction in subsidies to the domestic producers of agricultural\nAccordingly, India's exports of agricultural products are expected to rise.\ngoods in the developed countries, prices of these goods are expected to rise in the international market\nNote: - WTO agreement is offering only an asymmetric treatment (something which is not uniformly distributed for\nall nations of the world to capital market and labour market.\n- WTO advocates free movement of capital across different nations, not the free movement of labour, while\nboth labour and capital are commodities of the factor market.\nexploit markets in less developed countries.\n132\nis not encouraged by WTO.\n- Accordingly, Indian labour does not find free access to the lucrative factor market in develoned countries,\n(Lucrative factor market refers to the market offering high wage rate.)]\nBilateral and Multilateral Trade Agreements\n• Multilateral trade agreements refer to trade agreements of on\nBilateral trade agreements refer to trade agreements of one country with the other. Or, these are trade agreements\nbetween any two countries of the world.\nOr, these are trade agreements among many countries of the work\nmany countries of the world.\nEconomic Reforms Since 1991: New Economic Policy\n& AVLR 3.4\nWORLD TRADE ORGANISATION"},{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"ied-3-05","image":"ied-pages/ied-3-05.jpg","text":"Q. 15. State two reasons why India is emerging as an important destination of outsourcing.\nAns.\n(i) Availability of cheap labour.\n(ii) Revolutionary growth of the IT sector.\nQ. 16. Name any two non-tariff barriers.\nAns.\n(i) Quotas, and (ii) Quantitative restrictions.\nQ. 17. Globalisation focuses more on the free movement of labour than capital in the international\nNo. It is false. Globalisation has promoted the free movement of capital, rather than labour, in\nthe international market. Both FIl (Foreign Institutional Investment) as well as FDI (Foreign Direct\nInvestment) have exponentially risen across different parts of the world. Movement of labour,\non the other hand, has remained low on account of strict immigration rules and regulations."},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-3-04","image":"ied-pages/ied-3-04.jpg","text":"FDI and FIl\noreign investment includes FDI (Foreign Direct Investment) and FIl (Foreign Institutional Investment)\n• FDI (Foreign Direct Investment) refers to investment by the foreigners by way of their business\nestablishments in India. It implies ownership and control of business. Examples: Coke, Pepsi,\nDomino's, McDonald's.\n• FI (Foreign Institutional Investment) refers to investment in Indian companies (by way of purchasing\ntheir equity or shares) by the foreign banking and non-banking institutions. It does not involve any kind\nof direct control on the management of the Indian companies where investment is made. Fil, unlike\nFDI, is simply an investment in the stock market in India by the foreign banking and non-banking\ninstitutions.\n(h) Recognition of India as an Emerging Economic Power: It is owing to LPG policies and the\nconsequent rise in the overall level of economic activity, that India is now being recognised\nas an emerging economic power in the world.\n> This recognition (particularly by developed nations of the world) not only raises India's\neconomic ranking in the world, but also boosts confidence of the global investors in the\nIndian economy as their preferred destination of investment.\n> India has also become a leading exporter of auto parts, engineering goods, IT software and\ntextiles.\n(i) A Shift from Monopoly Market to Competitive Market: Launch of LPG policies has caused a\nsignificant shift in the structure of the Indian markets.\n> Indian markets are now increasingly shedding its monopolistic character, and becoming\nmore and more competitive in nature.\n> For instance, a couple of decades back, products like cars, refrigerators, ACs and PCs were\nthe monopoly markets of select brands only. Now a variety of these products are available\nat competitive prices.\nBriefv owing to LpG nolicies, the Indian economy has definitely gained a 'growth momentum\nThe process of growth has not only accelic + ed. but has also become more diversified.\nThere is a definite change in welfare level of iump ople. Recognition of the Indian economy as an\nemerging economic power in the world is of crucial significance.\n3.3.2 Bad Effect\nAll that glitters is not gold There is a negative side of the story as well. Following observations\nhighlight the negative effect of LPG policies in India:\n(a) Neglect of Agriculture: Post New Economic Billion (P) of 1991, growth of GDP has largely\nbeen driven bv the secondarv and tertiary sectors of the economy\n* Agricultural sector has suffered a cerious neglect anoke, rowth rate has slided/slipped to\na miserably low level (2-3 per cent per annum)."},{"title":"fiscal deficit, disinvestment highlights inefficiency of the government as a manager of the","page":"ied-3-03","image":"ied-pages/ied-3-03.jpg","text":"government.\n» It is observed that as an immediate solution to the problem of fiscal deficit, the assets of\nPSUs are often undervalued causing a substantial loss to the exchequer.\n> To the extent the proceeds from disinvestment have been used for offsetting the shortage\nin revenue of the government (rather than investment in increasing the efficiency\nand profitability of the other PSUs), disinvestment (in the wake of NEP) is definitely a\nquestionable policy instrument of the government.\n(e) Fiscal Policy: Owing to NEP, fiscal policy of the government has come under a severe stress.\n> During the reform period, the Government of India had reduced tax rates to induce more\npeople to pay tax and prevent tax evasion. But this has not yielded an anticipated result:\ntax-net continues to be small and tax revenue continues to be low. This has suppressed\npublic expenditure on social welfare.\n> The reform period also marked a reduction in tariff rates and custom duties, curtailing the\noption of the government to earn additional revenues from these sources.\n> To increase foreign investment, tax incentives have been given to foreign investors which\nfurther compounded the problem of low revenue income of the government.\n> The cumulative impact of the curtailed sources of revenue has led to a constraint on\nwelfare expenditure of the government.\n(f) Lopsided Growth Process: LPG has accelerated the growth process of the Indian economy,\nbut it is lopsided.\n> It is not an inclusive growth process. It does not include all the sectors of the economy.\nInstead, it is increasingly relying on 'service sector' of the economy.\n> In fact, it is just an 'IT-focused' growth process which is gradually over-shadowing the\nprocess of industrialisation, besides neglecting the farming sector.\n> It is alarming to note that owing to liberalisation and globalisation, the Indian farmer is\nshifting to the production of cash crops for the foreign markets, causing a shortfall of\ndomestic supplies of food grains.\n- We are often forced to import food grains despite Green Revolution. Rising prices of food\nproducts towards the end of 2009 should serve as a wake up call to the fact that LPG\npolicies are almost neglecting the farming sector of the economy."},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-3-02","image":"ied-pages/ied-3-02.jpg","text":"Economic and Social Dualism\n• Economic Dualism: It refers to disintegration of the economy into traditional and modern sectors of\nproduction. While the traditional sector relies upon traditional technology, the modern sector is driven\nby modern technology.\n• Social Dualism: It refers to disintegration of the society into 'haves' (enjoying high social status) and\n\"have-nots' (living with low social status).\n(h) Economic Colonialism: India suffered nearly 200 years of political colonialism during the\nBritish rule.\n> Now, while MNCs are expanding their economic control, we might suffer a sort of\neconomic colonialism.\n> Implying a situation where MNCs are exploiting the Indian markets to sell their products\nand in the process, domestic producers are marginalised owing to their poor competitive\nstrength.\n(i) Spread of Consumerism: Spread of MNCs in the country as a consequence of LPG policies\nhas resulted in a large-scale spread of consumerism??.\n> Availability of global brands has tempted the young Indians to splurge money (spend\nmoney beyond their means).\n> The Indian society is adapting itself to the western culture of spend\n17. Consumerism - the protection or promotion of the interests of consumers.\n138\nEconomic Reforms Since 1991: New Economic Policy\n» This may expand size of the market for the traders and the manufacturers, but certainly\nenhances vulnerability of the households as consumers."},{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"ied-3-02","image":"ied-pages/ied-3-02.jpg","text":"Q.18. What are trade agreements involving many countries called?\nAns. Multilateral trade agreements.\nQ. 19. State the meaning of FDI.\nAns. Investment by foreigners by way of their business establishments in India. It implies ownership\nand control of the business.\nQ. 20.\nWhy was private foreign investment promoted by the government?\nAns.\n(i) Domestic economy was not generating enough surplus for reinvestment.\n(ii) Indigenous technology was getting obsolete.\nQ. 21. What are short-term measures aimed at correcting the deficit in balance of payments and\ncontrolling inflation called?\nAns. Stabilisation measures.\n0. 22. State an instance which reflects how agriculture was neglected in the post-reform phase.\nAns. Fall in investment in the agriculture sector, especially in infrastructure like irrigation, power,\nroads, etc.\nQ. 23. How has industrial growth been affected by the removal of barriers on imports?\nAns. Industrial growth has slowed down due to cheaper imports of industrial products, which have\nreplaced the demand for domestic goods.\nQ. 24. Write a critical review of NEP.\nAns. A critical review of NEP includes description of positive as well as negative impact of the\neconomic reforms initiated in 1991.\nThe positive impact is evident in terms of a substantial rise in the level of economic activity in\nthe economy, as indicated by a sustained rise in GDP.\nThe negative impact is evident in terms of sectoral imbalances and concentration of economic\npower. Sectoral imbalances have occurred on account of the fact that:\n(i) agricultural sector has suffered a setback while the industrial and tertiary sectors have\ngained a momentum, and\n(ii) growth process has largely been confined to urban sector of the economy.\nConcentration of economic power has occurred owing to rapid control of the domestic market\nby the multinational corporations.\nWhich Way to Go?\n• Should we or should we not subscribe to the LPG policies? It is a debatable issue. But avoiding\nthe pros & cons of LPG, the +2 graders can definitely make one concrete observation: that LPG\npolicies are the only way out to accelerate the pace of growth and development.\n• Indeed perusal of LPG policies was to a great extent a matter of economic compulsion rather than\na matter of choice for the politicians of the country.\nHowever, a compulsion should never mean an abject surrender. It is strongly recommended that\nLPG policies are nursued with guarded precautions.\n139"},{"title":"Power Points [Summary of the Chapter]","page":"ied-3-01","image":"ied-pages/ied-3-01.jpg","text":"• Economic Reforms: Economic reforms refer to all those measures which aim at rendering the econo-\nmore efficient, competitive and developed.\n• Liberalisation, privatisation and globalisation are the principal elements of economic reforms in India.\n• Liberalisation: Liberalisation implies freedom of the producing units from controls imposed by the government.\n• Liberalisation includes industrial sector reforms, financial sector reforms, tax reforms, foreign exchange\nreforms, trade and investment policy reforms.\nIndustrial Sector Reforms: (i) Abolition of industrial licensing, (ii) Contraction of public sector,\n(i) De-reservation of production areas, (iv) Expansion of production capacity, (v) Freedom to import\ncapital goods.\nFinancial Sector Reforms: These refer to the reforms in country's monetary and banking policies.\nTax Reforms: These refer to increasing the revenue of the government and lowering the expenditure in a\nway that it causes no adverse effect on production and economic welfare.\n• Foreign Exchange Reforms: These reforms were focused on: (i) Increasing international competitiveness\nof industrial sector, i) Promotion of foreign investment in the economy, (i) Promotion of adoption of\nmodern technology.\nTrade and Investment Policy Reforms: ()Dismantling of quantitative restrictions on imports and exports,\n(i) Reduction in tariff rates, (i Removal of import licensing, (i) Removal of export duties.\n• Privatisation: It implies partial or full ownership and management of public sector enterprise hy th\nprivate sector.\nsale of the government enterprises to the private entrepreneurs.\nMeasures of Privatisation: (i) Withdrawal of the government ownership and management, (ii) Outright\nand deepening economic integration in the global economy.\nGlobalisation: It is a process associated with increasing openness, growing economic interdenan dance\nPolicy Strategies to Promote Globalisation: (i) Increase in equity limit of foreign investment. (il) Partial\n•Good Effect of the LPG Policies: (i) Virbrant economy, (ii) Stimulant to industrial production, (ill) Check on\nfiscal deficit, (iv) Substantial increase in foreign exchange reserves, (V) Check on inflation. (vi) Consumer's\npower, (ix) A shift from monopoly market to competitive market.\nsovereignty, (vil) Flow of private foreign investment, (vili) Recognition of India as an emerging economic\n(iii) Unemployment, (iv) Disinvestment, (v) Fiscal policy, (vi) Lopsi\n•Bad Effect of the LPG Policies: (i) Neglect of agriculture, (ii) Slowdown in industrial arowth\nconcentration of growth process, (vili) Economic colonialism * Sp\n140\nith process, (vil) Urban\namerism.\nEconomic Reforms Since 1991: New Economic Policy\nWORK-BOOK (Question-answer Format)\nCompetency Based Learning: Objective Questions\n1. Multiple Choice Questions\nChoose the correct option:\n1. Read the given text carefully:\n\"The Government has now decided to further promote the development of mutual funds by\nthrowing the field open to the private sector and joint sector mutual funds. In order to safeguard\nthe interests of the investing public, and to encourage a healthy growth of the capital markets,\na comprehensive set of guidelines is being evolved for the operation of all mutual funds.\"\"\nSource: https://www.indiabudget.gov.in/doc/bspeech/bs199192.pdf (Budget 1991-92 Speech of\nShri Manmohan Singh Minister of Finance)\nIdentify the sector under which the aforesaid reform was introduced and choose the correct\noption.\n[CBSE Sample Paper 2026]\n(a) Industrial\n(b) Financial\n(c) Tax\n(d) Foreign trade\n2. Identify, which of the following correctly defines liberalisation.\n[CBSE 2025 (58/6/1)]\n(a) Outright sale of part of shares of Public Sector Undertakings (PSUs)\n(b) Increased integration with the rest of the world\n(c) Removal of restrictions imposed by government on different sectors of the economy\n(d) Focus on import substitution\n3. Outsourcing from India has become more intensified in recent times due to the expansion of the\n[CBSE 2025 (58/7/1)]\nsector.\n4.\n(a) Agriculture\n(b) Manufacturing\n(c) Information Technology\n(d) Construction\nimplies shedding of the ownership or management of a government owned\n[CBSE (C) 2025]\nenterprise.\n(a) Liberalisation\n(b) Privatisation\n(d) Nationalisation\n(c) Globalisation\n5. Identify, which of the following statement is incorrect about the financial sector reform\n[CBSE Sample Paper 2025]\nintroduced in 1991?\n(a) Enabled the establishment of private sector banks, Indian as well as foreign\n(b) Foreign investment limit in banks was raised to around 74%\n(c) Foreign Institutional Investors were allowed to invest in Indian financial markets\n(d) Change in the role of the Reserve Bank of India from facilitator to regulator\n6. India has become a preferred outsourcing destination because of\n[CBSE 2021 (58/1/4), Term-1]\n(i) Availability of unskilled labour\n(ii) Quality of IT services\n(iii) Availability of skilled labour\nAlternatives:\n(a) o\n(b) (i) and (ii)\n(d) (ii) and (iii)\n141"}]},{"id":"i4","number":"4","subject":"ied","title":"Human Capital Formation in India","intro":"","lessons":[{"title":"Human resource, human capital and formation","body":"Human resource: people’s abilities to contribute to production.\n\nHuman capital: the stock of their skills, knowledge and health.\n\nHuman capital formation: adding to that stock through education, training and health investment.\n\nPopulation counts people; human capital asks what they are able to do.","formula":"","source":"ied-4-01","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"4.1.1 Human Resource · 4.1.2 Human Capital Formation","points":["Human resource: people’s abilities to contribute to production.","Human capital: the stock of their skills, knowledge and health.","Human capital formation: adding to that stock through education, training and health investment."],"takeaway":"Population counts people; human capital asks what they are able to do."},{"title":"Human capital versus human development","body":"Human capital view: education and health are means to higher productivity and income.\n\nHuman development view: education, health, freedom and well-being are valuable ends in themselves.\n\nImplication: people deserve health and education even without an immediate measured productivity gain.\n\nSame services, different ultimate purpose: productive returns versus a better human life.","formula":"","source":"ied-4-01","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"4.1.3 Human Capital vs. Human Development","points":["Human capital view: education and health are means to higher productivity and income.","Human development view: education, health, freedom and well-being are valuable ends in themselves.","Implication: people deserve health and education even without an immediate measured productivity gain."],"takeaway":"Same services, different ultimate purpose: productive returns versus a better human life."},{"title":"Physical capital versus human capital","body":"Physical capital: tangible, separable from its owner and saleable as an asset.\n\nHuman capital: embodied in a person; only its services can be sold.\n\nBoth depreciate, but learning and health investment can counter the loss of human capability.\n\nHuman capital also creates social benefits, such as informed citizenship.\n\nA machine can be sold separately; a driver’s skill cannot be separated from the driver.","formula":"","source":"ied-4-02","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"4.1 Physical Capital vs. Human Capital","points":["Physical capital: tangible, separable from its owner and saleable as an asset.","Human capital: embodied in a person; only its services can be sold.","Both depreciate, but learning and health investment can counter the loss of human capability.","Human capital also creates social benefits, such as informed citizenship."],"takeaway":"A machine can be sold separately; a driver’s skill cannot be separated from the driver."},{"title":"Education and health expenditure","body":"Education builds knowledge, skills and earning capacity.\n\nHealth increases efficiency and reduces working time lost to illness.\n\nHealth spending includes preventive care, treatment, awareness, hygiene and sanitation.\n\nThey complement each other: a sick worker cannot fully use even excellent skills.","formula":"","source":"ied-4-02","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"4.2 Sources of Human Capital","points":["Education builds knowledge, skills and earning capacity.","Health increases efficiency and reduces working time lost to illness.","Health spending includes preventive care, treatment, awareness, hygiene and sanitation."],"takeaway":"They complement each other: a sick worker cannot fully use even excellent skills."},{"title":"Training, migration and information","body":"On-the-job training: learn skills needed in actual work.\n\nAdult study: update knowledge and capabilities after initial schooling.\n\nMigration: bear travel/settlement costs to reach better opportunities.\n\nInformation: spend to find suitable education and jobs.\n\nEach is an investment when its cost is expected to produce future benefits.","formula":"","source":"ied-4-05","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"4.2 Sources of Human Capital","points":["On-the-job training: learn skills needed in actual work.","Adult study: update knowledge and capabilities after initial schooling.","Migration: bear travel/settlement costs to reach better opportunities.","Information: spend to find suitable education and jobs."],"takeaway":"Each is an investment when its cost is expected to produce future benefits."},{"title":"How human capital contributes to growth","body":"Change in emotional/material environment: education encourages new ideas and techniques.\n\nHigher productivity of physical capital: skilled workers use machines better.\n\nHigher participation and equality: more people can join and benefit from growth.\n\nThe relationship runs both ways: capabilities support growth, and growth finances better education and health.","formula":"","source":"ied-4-05","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"4.3 Human Capital Formation and Economic Growth","points":["Change in emotional/material environment: education encourages new ideas and techniques.","Higher productivity of physical capital: skilled workers use machines better.","Higher participation and equality: more people can join and benefit from growth."],"takeaway":"The relationship runs both ways: capabilities support growth, and growth finances better education and health."},{"title":"Problems of human capital formation","body":"Rising population stretches education and health resources.\n\nBrain drain reduces domestic access to skilled people.\n\nDeficient manpower planning creates a skills–jobs mismatch.\n\nInsufficient primary-sector training and low academic standards weaken practical capability.\n\nQualifications improve human capital only when they bring usable knowledge and skills.","formula":"","source":"ied-4-04","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"4.4 Problems Facing Human Capital Formation in India","points":["Rising population stretches education and health resources.","Brain drain reduces domestic access to skilled people.","Deficient manpower planning creates a skills–jobs mismatch.","Insufficient primary-sector training and low academic standards weaken practical capability."],"takeaway":"Qualifications improve human capital only when they bring usable knowledge and skills."},{"title":"Education: wider objectives","body":"Good citizenship and broader horizons: people make more informed choices.\n\nScience, technology and resource use: better knowledge improves production.\n\nCulture and personality: education develops people beyond their jobs.\n\nParticipation: more people can contribute to development.\n\nEducation generates both private returns and benefits for society.","formula":"","source":"ied-4-03","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"4.5.1 Importance and Objectives of Education","points":["Good citizenship and broader horizons: people make more informed choices.","Science, technology and resource use: better knowledge improves production.","Culture and personality: education develops people beyond their jobs.","Participation: more people can contribute to development."],"takeaway":"Education generates both private returns and benefits for society."},{"title":"Why government intervenes in education and health","body":"Public intervention widens access when private prices exclude poorer households.\n\nGovernment also finances costly facilities and sets quality standards.\n\nKothari Commission: education spending of 6% of GDP.\n\nRTE Act, 2009: free and compulsory education for ages 6–14.\n\nSpending as a share of GDP and as a share of the government budget have different denominators.","formula":"","source":"ied-4-03","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"4.5.2 Need for Government Intervention","points":["Public intervention widens access when private prices exclude poorer households.","Government also finances costly facilities and sets quality standards.","Kothari Commission: education spending of 6% of GDP.","RTE Act, 2009: free and compulsory education for ages 6–14."],"takeaway":"Spending as a share of GDP and as a share of the government budget have different denominators."},{"title":"Achievements, knowledge economy and remaining gaps","body":"Achievements: more schools, higher education, literacy and technical skills support a knowledge economy.\n\nAccess gaps: illiteracy, gender bias, low rural access and expensive private provision.\n\nQuality gap: weak vocationalisation and insufficient public spending.\n\nSteep education pyramid: fewer people reach each higher level.\n\nAccess gets people into education; quality and relevant skills determine what they gain.","formula":"","source":"ied-4-07","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"4.5 Education Sector: Achievements and Challenges","points":["Achievements: more schools, higher education, literacy and technical skills support a knowledge economy.","Access gaps: illiteracy, gender bias, low rural access and expensive private provision.","Quality gap: weak vocationalisation and insufficient public spending.","Steep education pyramid: fewer people reach each higher level."],"takeaway":"Access gets people into education; quality and relevant skills determine what they gain."}],"pages":[{"id":"ied-4-01","image":"ied-pages/ied-4-01.jpg","text":"4.1 CONCEPTS OF HUMAN REDEVELOP MENAN CAPITAL\nFORMATION AND HUMAN DEVELOPMENT\n4.1.1 Human Resource\n• Human resource refers to abilities\n(skill and expertise) of human beings\nto contribute to the process of\nvalue-addition' in the economy.\nExample:\nFarmer has the ability to grow\nfood and an agricultural scientist\nhas the ability to give us HW\n(high yielding variety seeds).\nLikewise, a doctor has the\nability to cure patients while\na pharmacist has the ability\nto provide\nmedicines\nprescribed by the doctor.\nWith equal size of\nland, how is it that\nhis yield is more than\nmine?\nSkill and Expertise-The Core Determinants of Growth\n4.1.2 Human Capital Formation\n• Human capital refers to the stock of 'skill and expertise' of a nation at a point of time.\n- It is the sum total of skill and expertise of engineers, doctors, professors and workers of all\ntypes who are engaged (or have the capacity and expertise to be engaged) in the process of\nvalue-addition.\n• Human capital formation is simply the process of adding to the stock of human capital.\n- While human capital is a stock concept, human capital formation is a flow concept. Thus, human\ncapital formation occurs when more doctors are produced and when more & more engineers\nare added to their existing stock in the economy.\nExample:\nIf human capital stock of a nation consists of 10,000 skilled workers at the beginning of\n2025, and if at the beginning of 2026 it increases to 12,000 skilled workers (implying an\nincrease of human capital stock by 2,000 workers in a year), there is human capital formation\nto the extent of 2,000 skilled workers in a year.\n• \"Human Capital Formation is the process of acquiring and increasing the number of persons\nwho have the skill, education and experience which are essential for the economic and political\ndevelopment of a country.\" -G.M. Meier\n1. Value-addition - the difference between the value of output and the cost of materials or supplies that are used in\nproducing them.\n2. Stock- value of an economic variable measured at a point of time.\n3. Flow-value of an economic variable measured over a period of time.\n168\nHuman Capital Formation in India\n• we need good human capital to create more human capital. For example, skilled teachers, doctors,\nand engineers are essential to train and develop others. Hence, investment in human capital is\nvital to transform human resources into productive human capital.\n1.1.3 Human Capital vs. Human Development\n1 Both human capital and 'human development™ are related to abilities (skill and expertise) of\nhuman beings. Yet, there is a clear distinction between the two concepts.\n- Human capital treats human beings as a means to an end: the end being the increase in\nvalue-addition (productivity) in the economy. Any investment in education and health is seen as\nproductive only if it raises output of goods and services.\n- Human development, on the other hand, treats human beings as an end in themselves, i.e., as\nan end-result: 'skill and expertise' of human beings in itself is an achievement, irrespective of its\ncontribution in the process of value-addition. It focuses on expanding human capabilities- the\nability to read and write, live a long and healthy life, and make choices which they value.\n• Thus, an educated man (with skill and expertise) leads a more meaningful and more satisfying life\nthan the one who is not. An educated man enjoys varied and wider choices in life (which tend to\nenhance his welfare) than the one who is not educated.\n• Briefly, as a means to an end, human capital is linked with value-addition (productivity). And, as an\nend-result itself, human development is an index of human welfare, irrespective of its contribution\nin the process of value-addition. From this perspective, every individual has a right to be literate\nand lead a healthy life, even if it does not contribute anything to labour productivity.\nAVIR 4.1 1\nHUMAN CAPITAL vs. HUMAN DEVELOPMENT\n! HIGHLIGHTS\nHow is Human Capital different from Physical Capital?\nThough both contribute to economic growth by raising the level of efficiency, there is vital difference\nbetween physical capital and human capital, as under:\nBasis of Difference\nPhysical Capital\n1. Meaning\n• Physical capital refers to the produced\nmeans of production.\n2. Process of\nFormation\nHuman Capital\n• Human capital refers to skill and expertise\nacquired by man over time through\neducation, training and experience.\n• The decision to invest in physical capital\n• The decision to invest in human capital is\nis taken by the owner after considering\nnot taken after calculating the returns on\nthe expected rate of returns from the\ninvestment.\ninvestment.\n• It is taken due to influences of peers.\n• Thus, it is an economic and technical\neducators and society. Thus, it is largely a\nprocess.\nsocial process.\nrefers to 'skill and expertise' of human beings as their achievement, enabling them to enhance\n169\n4. Human devel\ntheir qualits","file":"chap4.pdf"},{"id":"ied-4-02","image":"ied-pages/ied-4-02.jpg","text":"3. Tangibility\n4. Separability\nfrom Owner\n5. Mobility\n• Physical capital is tangible, and can be • Human capital is intangible, it cannot be\nsold in the market like any commodity.\nsold in the market like a commodity Ori\nthe services are sold.\n• Physical capital is separable from owner, • Human capital is inseparable fromi\nmeaning presence\nowner is not\nowner (e.g. a bus driver possesses the\nessential in the place of production.\nknowledge and the ability to drive the\nbus); therefore, it is essential that the\nowner be present at the place where the\nservices are being sold.\n• Movement of human capital is restricted by\nnationality and culture; it is not perfectly\nmobile.\n6. Depreciation\n7. Benefit\n• Mobility of physical capital across\nborders is restricted mainly due to\ntrade barriers; otherwise, it is completely\nmobile, i.e., it can even take place\nthrough imports.\n• Depreciation cannot be avoided.\n• Continuous use of physical capital leads\nto depreciation; innovation may also\nlead to obsolescence of physical capital.\n• Depreciation occurs with ageing, but can\nbe avoided with continuous investment in\neducation, training and health.\n• Investment in regular training to keep up\nwith innovations helps human capital avoid\ndepreciation.\n• Physical capital generates only economic\n• Human capital generates both economic\nbenefit to its owner.\nand social benefits: economic benefit to\nthe owner, and social benefit to others.\nFor example, educated citizens participate\neffectively in democracy, and healthy\nindividuals help prevent the spread of\ndiseases by maintaining personal hygiene\nand sanitation.\n3 AVLR 42\nPHYSICAL CAPITAL vS. HUMAN CAPITAL\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\nQ. 1. Human development is a means to an end. Is it true?\nAns. No, it is false. Human development is not a means to an end. Instead, it is the end-result itself.\nIt is the achievement of human being that enhances their quality of life. Human capital is a\nmeans to an end.\nQ. 2. How can we avoid depreciation of human capital?\nAns. Investment in education, training and health can help avoid depreciation of human car.\n5. Depreciation - a reduction in the value of an asset over time, due to wear and tear.\n170\n1,2 SOURCES OF HUMAN CAPITAL\nHuman Capital Formation in India\n• Sources of human capital (also called determinants of human capital) refer to the ways of adding\nto the stock of human capital in the economy.\nFollowing description identifies the possible ways cof dding to the stock of human capital.\nWays of Adding to the Stock of Human Capital of a Country\n(a) Expenditure on Education: Expenditure\non education is the most effective way\nof raising a productive workforce® in\nthe country. It is, therefore, a very\nimportant determinant of human capital\nformation.\n> Most families decide to incur huge\nexpenditure on education, even\nwhen they have to raise loans.\nEducation: Raises Productive Workforce in the Country\nReason: Investment in education leads\nto increase in their future income. Returns on such expenditures are substantially large.\n› Education enables an individual to make a good living throughout his life. His total earnings\nduring his life span would far exceed his initial expenditure on education.\n! HIGHLIGHTS\n• Monetary benefits of education (in terms of earnings of the educated person during his lifetime) far\nexceed the cost of education.\n• The difference between benefit and cost is an approximate market value of human capital formation.\n(b) Expenditure on Health: \"A sound mind in a sound body\" is an old saying. Expenditure on\nhealth makes a man more efficient and, therefore, more productive.\n* His contribution in the value-addition process tends to rise. He adds more to GDP of the\nnation than a sick person.\n* Expenditure on health in the form of certain critical components (as in the flow chart given\nbelow) is an important determinant of human capital formation.\nCritical Components (or Forms) of Expenditure on Health\nExbenditure on\nExpenditure on\nExpenditure on\nCurative Medicines\nSocial Medicines\nPreventive Medicines\n(medical intervontions\n(spread of\n(vaccination)\nat the time of illness\nheath literacy)\nExpenditure on\nProvisions of Hyaiene\nand Sanitation\n- Health expenditure directly increases the supply of healthy labour force and is, thus, a\nsource of human capital formation.\n6. Workforce - totalm\n(people in a country or region who are physically able to do a job and are available for work.\n171","file":"chap4.pdf"},{"id":"ied-4-05","image":"ied-pages/ied-4-05.jpg","text":"Indian Economic Development\n(c) On-the-Job Training: On-the-iob-training helps workers to hone/sharpen their specia\nskills. It enables them to raise the level of their efficiency/productivity.\n• Firms are always inclined to undertake\n'on-the-job training programmes?. Because,\nreturns (on account of enhanced productivity)\nfar exceed the cost of such programmes.\n> On-the-job training can take two different\nforms:\n(i) Workers may be trained in the firms\nitself under the supervision of a skilled\nOn-the-Job Training raises Efficiency\nworker.\n(il) Workers may be sent for some off-campus training. The firms prefer to retain the\ntrained workers with a view to enjoying the benefits of higher productivity.\n> Expenditure on such training programmes, therefore, is yet another important determinant\n(and a source) of human capital formation.\n(d) Study Programmes for Adults: Other than formal education at the primary, secondary and\nuniversity levels, the Government and NGOs organise study programmes for adults to make\nthem proficient in their work areas. This enhances their productivity, serving as a source of\nhuman capital formation.\n(e) Migration: Migration®\ncontributes to human\ncapital formation as it facilitates utilisation of\n(otherwise) inactive skills of the people, or it\nfacilitates fuller/better utilisation of the skills.\nDifferent Types of Migration\n1. Internal Migration\nVillagers migrating\nto cities in search\n* Migration involves a cost (such as, cost of\ntransport, higher cost of living in the migrated\nplaces and psychic cost of living in a strange\nsocio-cultural set up) yet people migrate in\nsearch of better job opportunities. Because:\nGains of migration (in terms of higher salaries)\nare greater than the cost of migration.\n2. External Migration\nIndians migrating\nto other countries\nsearch of a better\nstandard of living\nLive Your Dreams\n› Implying that migration leads to human capital\nformation, through fuller/better utilisation of skills.\nf) Expenditure on Information: People incur expenditure to obtain information about\neducational institutions, includine skills offered by different incitions and the relator cocts.\nExpenditure is also incurred to know about job markets and the salary structure of different\njobs. This information helps them decide where to invest in education or training and how to\nuse their acquired skills efficientlv. Thus, expenditure on acquiring such information heromes\nan important source and determinant of human capital formation as it enables odividhals 10\nactualise their productive potential.\nOn-the-lob training programmes - a form of training provided at the workplace under the supervise arenad\nworker, or by sending workers for off-campus training programmes.\nMigration - the movement of people from one country to another in order to gain benne\nopportunities in the receiving country.\ngreater economic\non-the-job training' programmes.\nBriefly, human capital formation is promoted by investment on education and health, besides\nHuman Capital Formation in India\nalso contributes to human capital formation.\nInvestment on migration and information relating to availability of specialised skills and job markets\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\na. 3. Why do families incur huge expenditure on education?\nAns. Monetary benefits of education (in terms4 (e)ings of the educated person durine his lifetime)\nfar exceed the cost of education.\n9. 4. Why are firms inclined to undertake 'on-the-job-training' programmes?\nAns. These programmes help in enhancing the productivity of labour and pave way for higher levels\nof output.\nQ.5. What is the main reason for migration of labour?\nAns. Search of better job opportunities.\n0.6. What is the cost of migration?\nThere are two costs of migration:\n(i) Cost of transportation from one place to another.\n(ii) Cost of living in a different social environment.\nQ.7. Migration leads to human capital formation. How?\nAns. It is true that migration leads to human capital formation. Unemployed and underemployed\npeople often migrate from rural to urban areas. This is because:\n(i) those who are unemployed get opportunities of actualising their skill, and\n(i) those who are underemployed get opportunities of fuller utilisation of their skills. in a market\neconomy, migration offers the best opportunities of higher supplies of skill corresponding to\nhigher wages.\n4.3 HUMAN CAPITAL FORMATION AND ECONOMIC GROWTH\nOR\nSIGNIFICANCE OF HUMAN CAPITAL FORMATION\nThe sienificance of human capital lies in the fact that it has emerged as a key determinant of economic\ngrowth?. This is amplitied through the following observations:\n(a) Change in Emotional and Material Environment of Growth: Human capital formation\ngenerates a change in emotional and material environment of growth.\n- Emotional environment becomes conducive to growth as people tend to acquire\ngrowth-oriented attitude and aspirations.\n- Material environment becomes helpful to growth as the society now possesses higher\nnumber of skilled and trained workers to implement the plans and programmes of\neconomic growth.\n9. Economic g\nincrease in the size doeconomy over a period of time.\n173","file":"chap4.pdf"},{"id":"ied-4-04","image":"ied-pages/ied-4-04.jpg","text":"Indian Economic Development\n(b) Higher Productivity of Physical Capital: Human capital formation increases productivity ?\nphysical capital (referring to output per unit of capital).\n- Specialised engineers and skilled workers can certainly handle machines better than the\nothers. It enhances productivity and accelerates the pace of growth.\n- Human capital formation gives birth to innovative skill which leads to innovations\ninventions and technical improvements, the undercurrent of growth and development.\n• Larger the number of skilled and trained personnel greater the possibilities of innovation!\nniventions and technical improvements in the area of production and related activitie\nThese are the life-line of growth.\n(c) Higher Rate of Participation and Equality: By enhancing productive capacities of the labour\nforce, human capital formation induces greater employment.\nprocess of production or simply the percentage of employment of the existing labour force.\n- Higher the rate of participation, greater is the degree of economic and social equality in\nthe society.\nThe Seventh Five Year Plan of India clearly recognised the importance of human capital in the process\nof economic growth.\nIt underlined the significance of large population in India as a source of human resource and as an\nasset in accelerating the process of economic and social change in the country.\n'Cause and Effect' relationship between\nHuman Capital Formation and Economic Growth\nChain Effect of Human Capital Formation\n• Human capital formation (based on higher education and hisher chill levell drives the process of\neconomic growth by boosting productivity and diversifying the growth process through innovative\ntechnology.\n• Conversely, economic growth generates opportunities to acquire higher skill and knowledge\n(besides opportunities of healthier population) which contributes to the stock of human capital.\n• Thus, human capital formation drives the process of growth, and economic growth drives the\nprocess of human capital formation.\nHIGHLIGHTS\nhain Effect of Human Capital Formation\ntuman Capital Formation [increase in the stock of human capital) → Better/Eficient utilisation of\nong with higher rate of participation/employment → Higher level of income of the individuals and\nuseholds → Higher expenditure on education and health → Increase in the stor\nplying human capital formation).\nHuman Capital Formation in India\nHard to assess the Impacudysluman Capital on Economic Growth\nin the context of a deep relationship between human capital and economic growth, it must also be\nare some notable observations in this regard:\nunderstood that it is very hard to assess the impact of human capital on economic growth. Following\n• It is very difficult to measure growth in human capital. Education, for example, is measured in\nterms of schooling year, teacher-pupil ratio and enrolment rates 10\nquality of education.\n, but it is difficult to assess the\n\" Likewise, while it is possible to measure health services in monetary terms, it is extremely difficult\nto assess the improvement in quality of health services.\nBecause of the poor quality of human capital formation, we find that in developing countries like\nIndia, human capital is often found to be growing faster than the rate of economic growth.\nAccordingly, assessment of human capital as a determinant of economic growth becomes\nquestionable.\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\nQ. 8. How does human capital formation facilitate growth and development?\nAns. It promotes innovations, inventions and technical improvement which is the undercurrent of\ngrowth and development.\nQ. 9. \"Deen Dayal Upadhyaya Grameen Kaushalya Yojana (DDU-GKY), as a part of the Skill India\ncampaign plays an instrumental role in supporting the social and economic programs of the\ngovernment like the Make in India, Digital India, Smart Cities and Start-Up India, Stand-Up India\ncampaigns.\"\nSource: ddugky.gov.in\nInvestment in programmes like DDU-GKY, leads to which outcome?\nAns. Human capital formation (as it enhances the skill, expertise and talent of the people).\n4.4 PROBLEMS FACING HUMAN CAPITAL FORMATION IN INDIA\n• Human capital formation in India is not a smorewnH is. There are several factors that\nretard (hinder) this process. These factors are discussed as under:\nMajor Problems facing Human Capital Formation in India\nInsufficient\nDeficient\nOn-the-Job\nRising\nBrain\nPopulation\nManpower\nTraining in\nLow\nAcademic\nStandards\nDrain\nPlanning\nPrimary Sector\n(a) Rising Population: Rapidly rising population adversely affects the quality of human capital.\nThis is because it reduces per head availability of the existing facilities relating to housing,\nsanitation, drainage, water-system, hospitals, education, power supply, etc.\n10. Earned the number of students of a particular age-group enrolled in education divided by the total population\n175","file":"chap4.pdf"},{"id":"ied-4-03","image":"ied-pages/ied-4-03.jpg","text":"Indian Economic Development\n• Table 4.1 shows how education is different from literacy.\nTable 4.1: Education vs. Literacy\nEducation\nBasis of Difference\n1. Definition\n• Education refers to the process of imparting\nknowledge to others, or the process of\nreceiving knowledge from others,\n2. Scope\n• Scope of education includes: primary\neducation, secondary education and tertiary\n(higher) education. It spreads across all areas\nof educational activities.\n3. Notion\n• All educated people are literate.\nLiteracy\n• Literacy refers to the ability to read\nwrite and communicate effectively.\n• It refers to diverse elements of reading\nand writing, besides the abilities to\ncommunicate.\n• All literate people are not necessarily:\neducated.\n4.5.1 Importance and Objectives of Education\nFollowing points highlight the importance and objectives of education:\n(i) Education produces responsible citizens, and enables them to make rational choices in life.\n(i) It promotes science and technology, the essential ingredients of inventions and innovations.\n(ii) It facilitates use of natural and human resources across all regions of the country.\n(iv) As a source of knowiedge, education expands mental horizon of the people (by way of\nexpanding their understanding and experiences).\n(V) It helps economic development through greater participation of the people in the process of\ngrowth and development.\n(vi) It promotes cultural standard of the citizens.\n(vil) Education is critical for human empowerment (particularly, women empowerment) which\nleads to societal growth.\n4.5.2 Need\nvention in Education (and Health)\nVeed for government intervention in education and health arises primarily on account of these facts.\n(a) Hupe Investment: Education and health sectors require hupe investment involving hish fixed.\ncosts) which is penerally found to be above and bevond the means of collid actors.\n(b) Hich Market Price: Owing to their high fixed costs, education and health sectors herome\nviable enterprises only when market price of health and educational services is hish. But at\nhigh price, the essential services of health and education may not reach the poorer certions\nof the society. Hence, the government intervention.\n(c) Standardisation of Services: Individual consumers of these services are unaware about\nthe quality and costs of these services, which leads to the exploitative behaviour of private\nservice provide vernet tens that these service pride sto\nstipulated standards.\n(d) Constitutional Right: When basic education and healthcare is considered as rights of citizens,\nthe government needs to make provision for free of cost edu\nat least for the backward sections of the society.\nnd health services:\n178\nimbrief, it may be stated that these services would remain beyond the reach of most people unles:\nHuman Capital Formation in India\nthese are subsidised or directly provided by the government\n45.9 Government Expenditure on Education\n• Expedit sed on education can be measured in two ways: as percentage of total expenditure\nof the government and as percentage of GDP.\n•The Education Commission (1964-66) had recommended that to make noticeable growth i\neducational achievements, the Government of rot ispend around 6 per cent of GDP on\n• In 1 s•overnment of India appointed the Tapas Majumdar Committee, which estimated\nthat the government would need to incur an expenditure of 7 1.37 lakh crore over a period of\n10 years (1998-99 to 2006-07) to bring all the children in the age group of 6-14 years under the\neducation cover.\n! HIGHLIGHTS\nA Legislative Measure\n• In 2009, the Government of India made education a fundamental right for all children in the age group\nof6-14 years by enacting the Right of Children to Free and Compulsory Education Act (popularly known\nas Right to Education Act).\n• This Act is based on the Directive Principles of Indian Constitution which state that the government\nshould provide free and compulsory education for all children up to the age of 14 years within 10 years.\nfrom the commencement of Constitution.\n• To cope with the consequent expenditure, the government started levying a 2 per cent education cess\non all union taxes. It was specified that the revenue generated from the cess would be utilised for\nincurring expenses on elementary education.\nA Note of Caution\n• Just because the absolute expenditure is higher in elementary expenditure and expenditure per\nstudent is higher in tertiary education, it does not mean that financial resources should be transferred\nfrom tertiary to elementary or from elementary to tertiary education.\n• As the school education is expanded in India, there would be need for teachers at higher level of\neducation, so expenditure should be increased at all levels of education.\n• Impact: Compared to the recommended level v • enditure, in recent years, the Indian\nGovernment has been spending a little over 4 pe bedtiP on education, which has proven to\nbe quite inadequate to achitu the educational aspirations of the country.\n• Da: Table 4 2 shows the overall increase in government expenditure during the period 1952-2020.\nIt looks to be substantial but is certainly not sufficient.\nTable 4.2: Increase in Expenditure on Education during 1952-2020 (in percentage)\nFrom\nBasis\n792\nEducation expenditure as percentage of total expenditure of the government\n0,64\n16.54\n4,47\nEducation expenditure as percentage of GDP\n179","file":"chap4.pdf"},{"id":"ied-4-08","image":"ied-pages/ied-4-08.jpg","text":"1Ulis Ut lilis Data: It is important to note that this data pertains only to the expendit\nundertaken by the public sector, and not the private sector. If the expenditure incurred by\nprivate sector is considered, the total expenditure on education in Indian economy would i\nmuch higher.\n• Most of the expenditure undertaken on education goes to elementary education?\n- In elementary elementary education accounted for per percent of the total expenditu\neducation while the share of tertiary education (Colleges and Universities) was 11.86 pre\nent. However, expenditure per student in tertiary education is higher as compared to that in ?\nelementary education.\n- Based on the current trends on expenditure and the recommendations. it is clear that the indi\nGovernment needs to increase expenditure on all levels of education.\n• The states of India show variation in the expenditure incurred on elementary education.\n- In 2020-21, Sikkim incurred highest expenditure on elementary education (amounting to\nr 96,968 per capita) while the lowest expenditure was incurred by Bihar (amounting to\n7 10,710 per capita). As a result, great variations are observed in educational opportunities and\nattainments across the states.\nHIGHLIGHTS\nNeed of the Hour\n• The National Education Policy 2020 underlines the change in the knowledge landscape of the world\n• With dramatic scientific and technological advancement, such as the rise of big data, machine\nlearning and artificial intelligence, many unskilled sector jobs have been consumed by machines in\n• There is a dire need for skilled workforce, i.e., one involving multidisciplinary abilities across various\ndomains like sciences, social sciences, and humanities.\nMultidisciplinary Learning\ndisciplines of learning to address the issue at hand and illustrate a theme or concept.\npandemics worldwide is the need of the hour.\n• Way Ahead: There exists a large pool of scientific and technical manpower in our country. All we need\nto do is hone/sharpen their skills in a multidisciplinary manner.\nEducational Finance: Who spends on Education and how much?\n(i) The State Governments: A state government is spending between 10-30 per cent of its total annual\nbudget for India.\n*. State government budgets amount to approximately 74 per cent of the total education\n(il) The Central Government: <45,000 crore annually (nearly 3 per cent of its total annual budget).\nElementary education - primary and middle school education together is called elementary education\nTertiary education - educational level following the completion of secondary education.\nBudget ~ a statement of estimated receipts and expenditure of the government during a financial year.\nHuman Capital Formation in India\n(iv) The Parents (by way of tees) for their children: 4-2 per cent.\niD Total Percentage Expenditure by the Governmeo (0 tes as well as the centre): 80-90 per cent.\n(v) The Private Trusts: 7 per cent.\n(vi) Endowments: 3 per cent.\nA broad conclusion thus emerges that in India, di peog significant trend towards privatisation of\neducation, the bulk of spendinhly done by the central and the state governments\nAVLR 4.3\nEDUCATION - IMPORTANCE AND NEED FOR GOVERNMENT INTERVENTION\n4,5,4 Educational Achievements in india\nThere has been an expansion of general education during the period of Five Year Plans, as indicated\nby Table 4.3.\nTable 4.3: Expansion of General Education\nItem\n1950-51\nI. Institutions\n) Primary and Middle Schools\n2.23 lakh\n(ii) Secondary and Senior (Higher) Secondary Schools\n(i) Colleges (General)\n7.4 thousand\n578\nIl. Number of Students\n19.15 lakh\n(i) Primary and Middle\n2015-16\n12.70 lakh\n2.52 lakh\n39,071\n799\n17 crore\n(ii) High and Higher Secondary Schools\n15.0 lakh\n3.6 lakh\n3.2 crore\n346 lakh\n(i) Higher Education\nSource: Statistical Outline of India 2012-13, Economic Survey 2018-19 and www.ncert nic.in\nObservat\n• Substantial progress has been made in the field of education.\n• During the plans, the number of educational institutions providing elementary education has\nincreased roughly by five times and the number of students has increased ten-fold.\n• In 1951, hardly 18.33 per cent of the total population was literate. In 2011, the percentage of\nliterates increased to 74.04 per cent.\n181","file":"chap4.pdf"},{"id":"ied-4-07","image":"ied-pages/ied-4-07.jpg","text":"Indian Economic Development\nHIGHLIGHTS\nIndia an emerging Knowledge Economy\nKnowledge economy is an economy where growth is driven by the 'quantity, quality and accessiti\nof information, more than the means of production.\n• Expanding IT (information technology) in India is a critical sign of india emerging as a knowedne\neconomy.\n• Use of internet and digitisation of exchange is expanding even in rural areas of the country.\nable 4.4 shows the details of literacy rates (adult and youth) as well as completion rate for primar\neducation.\nTable 4.4: Educational Attainment in India\nItem\n1990\n2000\n2011\n2017-18\nAdult Literacy Rate (per cent of people aged 15+)\n(i) Male\n(ii) Female\nPrimary Completion Rate\" (per cent of relevant age group)\n(i) Male\n(il) Female\n61.9\n37.9\n68.4\n45.4\n79\n59\n78\n61\n85\n69\n94\nYouth Literacy Rate (per cent of people aged 15+ to 24)\n(i) Male\n(ii) Female\n76.6\n54,2\n79.7\n64.8\n82\n8\nSource: NCERT, 2025-26\nHIGHLIGHTS\n• Considering the importance of education in human capital formation, the Government of India tries to\nregulate education sector through various organisations. Important among these are:\n(i)NCERT (National Council of Educational Research and Training): It is basically engaged in\ndesigning text material up to the senior secondary level.\n(il) UGC (University Grants Commission): it is primarily the funding authority for university education.\nIt also enforces the rules and regulations regarding higher education in the country.\n(i) ICTE (All India Council of Technical Education): It enforces rules and regulations regarding\ntechnical/engineering education in the country.\n(iv) ICMR (Indian Council for Medical Research): It enforces rules and regulations relating to\neducation and research in the health sector.\n15, Primary completion rate - number of new entrants in the last grade of primary education, red\ndivided by the population at the entrance age for the last grade of primary education.\ntheir age.\n182\n1,5.5 Education Still a Lingering Challenge\nHuman Capital Formation in India\n• Focus on education has tremendously increased over time. It is encouraging to note that nearb\n96.5 per cent children are now the school goers.\n- But in the backdrop of this statistics theknowled tter truth: despite the constitutional provision\nfor free and compulsory education for the for primary between the age group of 6-14 years, quality\nof education and infrastructure continues to be disappointing.\n• There are numerous administrative hurdles, leading to low\nadmissions in the school. A survey by the\n\"Child Rights and\nYou\" (CRY) finds that children continue to be refused admission\nto schools because of the lack of transfer documentation, birth\nregistration and proof of residence.\n- In some cases, parents of school-going children are\nCHILO RIGHTS AND YOU\nwww.cry.org\nEnsuring lasting change\ncompelled to pay fees. Such instances point to a serious\nbottleneck in the education system of the country.\nChild Rights and You (CRY)\n• This along with the following facts and figures makes education still a lingering challenge in India:\n(a) Large Number of Illiterates: India harbours the largest number of illiterates in the world.\n> Presently, nearly 36 crore people are estimated to be illiterate. The number exceeds even\nthe total population of most countries in the world.\n(b) Inadequate Vocationalisation: Education continues to be largely degree-oriented throwing\nmillions of educated youths down to the corridors of employment exchanges.\n• Vocationalisation of education* is still a far cry. As a result, the rate of unemployment\namong the educated youth is trending up.\n* In 2011-12, according to NSSO data, the rate of unemployment among graduate youth\nin rural areas was 19 per cent while that in urban areas was 16 per cent. The situation is\nmore grim in case of female graduates. Nearly, 30 per cent of the female graduates are\nunemployed.\n* Contrary to it, only 3-6 per cent of primary level youth were unemployed in both rural and\nurban areas. As per the Periodic Labour Force Survey 2022-23, this situation has improved\n(c) Gender Bias: There is still a significant gender bias* in offering opportunities of education\nto male and female children.\n* The enrolment ratio is relatively low for the female candidates and their drop-out ratio\nis considerably high. Education system in India thus needs a substantial change in the\nattitudes of the people.\n- It is important to promote education among women to improve their economic\nindependence and social status.\n16. Lingering challenge - a challenge (or a problem) that persists over time.\n17. Vocationalisation of educarion - practical knowledaton LIvork to enable skill-building in humans.\n18. Gender bias\ntency to give preferential treatment to one gender over another.\n183","file":"chap4.pdf"},{"id":"ied-4-06","image":"ied-pages/ied-4-06.jpg","text":"(d) Low Rural Access Level: There is a high degree of disparity in access to educ\nThe 'access level? is considerably low for the rural population compared to the the\n(e) Privatisation: There is a growing trend towards privatisation of education. Being wel\nexpensive, private education has tended to widen the gulf between access level for the m\nand the poor. There is almost a drought of educational opportunities for poorer section.\nf) Low Government Expenditure on Education: The government has failed to fulfi\nommitment of spending nearly 6 per cent of GDP on education: actual expenditure has ber\naround 4-5 per cent only. This points to the gap between what is intended or desired and\nwhat is actually achieved.\nGiven these facts, there follows an obvious inference that education in India has failed to reach the\nmasses, and has also failed to break the vicious circle? of illiteracy21\n- illiteracy breeding poverte\nand poverty breading illiteracy.\nO AVIR 4.4\nEDUCATION SECTOR-ACHIEVEMENTS AND CHALLENGES\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\nQ. 12. \"Excessive involvement of the private sector in education causes more harm than good!\nComment.\nAns. It is true, as it makes education unaffordable for the people.\nQ. 13. Which body is responsible for making rules and regulations regarding technical/engineering\neducation in India?\nAns. AICTE (All India Council of Technical Education).\nQ. 14. Why do we say that the education pyramid of India is steep?\nAns. Because lesser and lesser number of people reach higher education level.\n0. 15. What does 'education expenditure of total government expenditure indicate?\nAns. It Indicates the importance of education in comparison to other areas of government\n0. 16. What flaws do you find in your education system that contributes to the problem\nof unemployment in the country?\nAns. Education system in India lacks vocationalisation. It is degree-oriented without imparting skill\nto the degree-holders to look for avenues of self-employment. It causes excess supply of labour\nseeking white collar jobs. Thus, it contributes to unemployment in the country.\nAccess level (in terms of availability of goods and services) - the opportunity or right to own it, use it or see it.\nliteracy - the condition of being unable to read and write.\noverty - the inability to fulfil the basic requirements of life.\nHuman Capital Formation in India\nPower Points (Summary of the Chapter]\nof value-addition in the economy.\nHuman Resource: It refers to abilities (skill and expert man beings to contribute to the process\nHuman Capital: It refers to the stock of skill and experly of a nation at a point of time\nHuman Capital Formation: It refers to the procesewnhing to the stock of human capital.\nSources of Human Capital:\n(i) Expenditure on education\n(il) On-the-job training\n(v) Migration\n(ii) Expenditure on health\n(iv) Study programmes for adults\n(vi) Expenditure on information\nSignificance of Human Capital Formation:\n(i) Change in emotional and material environment of growth\n(i) Higher productivity of physical capital\n(i) Higher rate of participation and equality\nProblems facing Human Capital Formation in India:\n) Rising population\n(i) Brain drain\n(il) Deficient manpower planning\n(iv) Insufficient on-the-job training in primary sector\n(v) Low academic standards\nImportance and Objectives of Education:\n(i) Education produces good citizens.\n(ii) It develops science and technology.\n(iii) It facilitates use of resources in the country.\n(iv) It expands mental horizon of the people.\n(v) It helps in economic development through greater participation of the people in the process of\ngrowth and development.\n(vi) It promotes cultural standard of the citizens.\n(vii): It develops human personality.\nGrowth of Education Sector in India: It includes:\nexpenditure incurred on education, and\n(i) educational achievements in India.\nProblems Relating to Development of Education in India:\n(i) Large number of illiterates\n(il) Inadequate vocationalisation\n(ill) Gender bias\n(iv) Low rural access level\n(v) Privatisation\n(vi) Low government expenditure on education\n185","file":"chap4.pdf"}],"boxes":[{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-4-01","image":"ied-pages/ied-4-01.jpg","text":"How is Human Capital different from Physical Capital?\nThough both contribute to economic growth by raising the level of efficiency, there is vital difference\nbetween physical capital and human capital, as under:\nBasis of Difference\nPhysical Capital\n1. Meaning\n• Physical capital refers to the produced\nmeans of production.\n2. Process of\nFormation\nHuman Capital\n• Human capital refers to skill and expertise\nacquired by man over time through\neducation, training and experience.\n• The decision to invest in physical capital\n• The decision to invest in human capital is\nis taken by the owner after considering\nnot taken after calculating the returns on\nthe expected rate of returns from the\ninvestment.\ninvestment.\n• It is taken due to influences of peers.\n• Thus, it is an economic and technical\neducators and society. Thus, it is largely a\nprocess.\nsocial process.\nrefers to 'skill and expertise' of human beings as their achievement, enabling them to enhance\n169\n4. Human devel\ntheir qualits"},{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"ied-4-02","image":"ied-pages/ied-4-02.jpg","text":"Q. 1. Human development is a means to an end. Is it true?\nAns. No, it is false. Human development is not a means to an end. Instead, it is the end-result itself.\nIt is the achievement of human being that enhances their quality of life. Human capital is a\nmeans to an end.\nQ. 2. How can we avoid depreciation of human capital?\nAns. Investment in education, training and health can help avoid depreciation of human car.\n5. Depreciation - a reduction in the value of an asset over time, due to wear and tear.\n170\n1,2 SOURCES OF HUMAN CAPITAL\nHuman Capital Formation in India\n• Sources of human capital (also called determinants of human capital) refer to the ways of adding\nto the stock of human capital in the economy.\nFollowing description identifies the possible ways cof dding to the stock of human capital.\nWays of Adding to the Stock of Human Capital of a Country\n(a) Expenditure on Education: Expenditure\non education is the most effective way\nof raising a productive workforce® in\nthe country. It is, therefore, a very\nimportant determinant of human capital\nformation.\n> Most families decide to incur huge\nexpenditure on education, even\nwhen they have to raise loans.\nEducation: Raises Productive Workforce in the Country\nReason: Investment in education leads\nto increase in their future income. Returns on such expenditures are substantially large.\n› Education enables an individual to make a good living throughout his life. His total earnings\nduring his life span would far exceed his initial expenditure on education."},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-4-02","image":"ied-pages/ied-4-02.jpg","text":"• Monetary benefits of education (in terms of earnings of the educated person during his lifetime) far\nexceed the cost of education.\n• The difference between benefit and cost is an approximate market value of human capital formation.\n(b) Expenditure on Health: \"A sound mind in a sound body\" is an old saying. Expenditure on\nhealth makes a man more efficient and, therefore, more productive.\n* His contribution in the value-addition process tends to rise. He adds more to GDP of the\nnation than a sick person.\n* Expenditure on health in the form of certain critical components (as in the flow chart given\nbelow) is an important determinant of human capital formation.\nCritical Components (or Forms) of Expenditure on Health\nExbenditure on\nExpenditure on\nExpenditure on\nCurative Medicines\nSocial Medicines\nPreventive Medicines\n(medical intervontions\n(spread of\n(vaccination)\nat the time of illness\nheath literacy)\nExpenditure on\nProvisions of Hyaiene\nand Sanitation\n- Health expenditure directly increases the supply of healthy labour force and is, thus, a\nsource of human capital formation.\n6. Workforce - totalm\n(people in a country or region who are physically able to do a job and are available for work.\n171"},{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"ied-4-05","image":"ied-pages/ied-4-05.jpg","text":"a. 3. Why do families incur huge expenditure on education?\nAns. Monetary benefits of education (in terms4 (e)ings of the educated person durine his lifetime)\nfar exceed the cost of education.\n9. 4. Why are firms inclined to undertake 'on-the-job-training' programmes?\nAns. These programmes help in enhancing the productivity of labour and pave way for higher levels\nof output.\nQ.5. What is the main reason for migration of labour?\nAns. Search of better job opportunities.\n0.6. What is the cost of migration?\nThere are two costs of migration:\n(i) Cost of transportation from one place to another.\n(ii) Cost of living in a different social environment.\nQ.7. Migration leads to human capital formation. How?\nAns. It is true that migration leads to human capital formation. Unemployed and underemployed\npeople often migrate from rural to urban areas. This is because:\n(i) those who are unemployed get opportunities of actualising their skill, and\n(i) those who are underemployed get opportunities of fuller utilisation of their skills. in a market\neconomy, migration offers the best opportunities of higher supplies of skill corresponding to\nhigher wages."},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-4-04","image":"ied-pages/ied-4-04.jpg","text":"hain Effect of Human Capital Formation\ntuman Capital Formation [increase in the stock of human capital) → Better/Eficient utilisation of\nong with higher rate of participation/employment → Higher level of income of the individuals and\nuseholds → Higher expenditure on education and health → Increase in the stor\nplying human capital formation).\nHuman Capital Formation in India\nHard to assess the Impacudysluman Capital on Economic Growth\nin the context of a deep relationship between human capital and economic growth, it must also be\nare some notable observations in this regard:\nunderstood that it is very hard to assess the impact of human capital on economic growth. Following\n• It is very difficult to measure growth in human capital. Education, for example, is measured in\nterms of schooling year, teacher-pupil ratio and enrolment rates 10\nquality of education.\n, but it is difficult to assess the\n\" Likewise, while it is possible to measure health services in monetary terms, it is extremely difficult\nto assess the improvement in quality of health services.\nBecause of the poor quality of human capital formation, we find that in developing countries like\nIndia, human capital is often found to be growing faster than the rate of economic growth.\nAccordingly, assessment of human capital as a determinant of economic growth becomes\nquestionable."},{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"ied-4-04","image":"ied-pages/ied-4-04.jpg","text":"Q. 8. How does human capital formation facilitate growth and development?\nAns. It promotes innovations, inventions and technical improvement which is the undercurrent of\ngrowth and development.\nQ. 9. \"Deen Dayal Upadhyaya Grameen Kaushalya Yojana (DDU-GKY), as a part of the Skill India\ncampaign plays an instrumental role in supporting the social and economic programs of the\ngovernment like the Make in India, Digital India, Smart Cities and Start-Up India, Stand-Up India\ncampaigns.\"\nSource: ddugky.gov.in\nInvestment in programmes like DDU-GKY, leads to which outcome?\nAns. Human capital formation (as it enhances the skill, expertise and talent of the people)."},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-4-03","image":"ied-pages/ied-4-03.jpg","text":"A Legislative Measure\n• In 2009, the Government of India made education a fundamental right for all children in the age group\nof6-14 years by enacting the Right of Children to Free and Compulsory Education Act (popularly known\nas Right to Education Act).\n• This Act is based on the Directive Principles of Indian Constitution which state that the government\nshould provide free and compulsory education for all children up to the age of 14 years within 10 years.\nfrom the commencement of Constitution.\n• To cope with the consequent expenditure, the government started levying a 2 per cent education cess\non all union taxes. It was specified that the revenue generated from the cess would be utilised for\nincurring expenses on elementary education.\nA Note of Caution\n• Just because the absolute expenditure is higher in elementary expenditure and expenditure per\nstudent is higher in tertiary education, it does not mean that financial resources should be transferred\nfrom tertiary to elementary or from elementary to tertiary education.\n• As the school education is expanded in India, there would be need for teachers at higher level of\neducation, so expenditure should be increased at all levels of education.\n• Impact: Compared to the recommended level v • enditure, in recent years, the Indian\nGovernment has been spending a little over 4 pe bedtiP on education, which has proven to\nbe quite inadequate to achitu the educational aspirations of the country.\n• Da: Table 4 2 shows the overall increase in government expenditure during the period 1952-2020.\nIt looks to be substantial but is certainly not sufficient.\nTable 4.2: Increase in Expenditure on Education during 1952-2020 (in percentage)\nFrom\nBasis\n792\nEducation expenditure as percentage of total expenditure of the government\n0,64\n16.54\n4,47\nEducation expenditure as percentage of GDP\n179"},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-4-08","image":"ied-pages/ied-4-08.jpg","text":"Need of the Hour\n• The National Education Policy 2020 underlines the change in the knowledge landscape of the world\n• With dramatic scientific and technological advancement, such as the rise of big data, machine\nlearning and artificial intelligence, many unskilled sector jobs have been consumed by machines in\n• There is a dire need for skilled workforce, i.e., one involving multidisciplinary abilities across various\ndomains like sciences, social sciences, and humanities.\nMultidisciplinary Learning\ndisciplines of learning to address the issue at hand and illustrate a theme or concept.\npandemics worldwide is the need of the hour.\n• Way Ahead: There exists a large pool of scientific and technical manpower in our country. All we need\nto do is hone/sharpen their skills in a multidisciplinary manner.\nEducational Finance: Who spends on Education and how much?\n(i) The State Governments: A state government is spending between 10-30 per cent of its total annual\nbudget for India.\n*. State government budgets amount to approximately 74 per cent of the total education\n(il) The Central Government: <45,000 crore annually (nearly 3 per cent of its total annual budget).\nElementary education - primary and middle school education together is called elementary education\nTertiary education - educational level following the completion of secondary education.\nBudget ~ a statement of estimated receipts and expenditure of the government during a financial year.\nHuman Capital Formation in India\n(iv) The Parents (by way of tees) for their children: 4-2 per cent.\niD Total Percentage Expenditure by the Governmeo (0 tes as well as the centre): 80-90 per cent.\n(v) The Private Trusts: 7 per cent.\n(vi) Endowments: 3 per cent.\nA broad conclusion thus emerges that in India, di peog significant trend towards privatisation of\neducation, the bulk of spendinhly done by the central and the state governments\nAVLR 4.3\nEDUCATION - IMPORTANCE AND NEED FOR GOVERNMENT INTERVENTION\n4,5,4 Educational Achievements in india\nThere has been an expansion of general education during the period of Five Year Plans, as indicated\nby Table 4.3.\nTable 4.3: Expansion of General Education\nItem\n1950-51\nI. Institutions\n) Primary and Middle Schools\n2.23 lakh\n(ii) Secondary and Senior (Higher) Secondary Schools\n(i) Colleges (General)"},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-4-07","image":"ied-pages/ied-4-07.jpg","text":"India an emerging Knowledge Economy\nKnowledge economy is an economy where growth is driven by the 'quantity, quality and accessiti\nof information, more than the means of production.\n• Expanding IT (information technology) in India is a critical sign of india emerging as a knowedne\neconomy.\n• Use of internet and digitisation of exchange is expanding even in rural areas of the country.\nable 4.4 shows the details of literacy rates (adult and youth) as well as completion rate for primar\neducation.\nTable 4.4: Educational Attainment in India\nItem\n1990\n2000\n2011\n2017-18\nAdult Literacy Rate (per cent of people aged 15+)\n(i) Male\n(ii) Female\nPrimary Completion Rate\" (per cent of relevant age group)\n(i) Male\n(il) Female\n61.9\n37.9\n68.4\n45.4\n79\n59\n78\n61\n85\n69\n94\nYouth Literacy Rate (per cent of people aged 15+ to 24)\n(i) Male\n(ii) Female\n76.6\n54,2\n79.7\n64.8\n82\n8\nSource: NCERT, 2025-26"},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-4-07","image":"ied-pages/ied-4-07.jpg","text":"• Considering the importance of education in human capital formation, the Government of India tries to\nregulate education sector through various organisations. Important among these are:\n(i)NCERT (National Council of Educational Research and Training): It is basically engaged in\ndesigning text material up to the senior secondary level.\n(il) UGC (University Grants Commission): it is primarily the funding authority for university education.\nIt also enforces the rules and regulations regarding higher education in the country.\n(i) ICTE (All India Council of Technical Education): It enforces rules and regulations regarding\ntechnical/engineering education in the country.\n(iv) ICMR (Indian Council for Medical Research): It enforces rules and regulations relating to\neducation and research in the health sector.\n15, Primary completion rate - number of new entrants in the last grade of primary education, red\ndivided by the population at the entrance age for the last grade of primary education.\ntheir age.\n182\n1,5.5 Education Still a Lingering Challenge\nHuman Capital Formation in India\n• Focus on education has tremendously increased over time. It is encouraging to note that nearb\n96.5 per cent children are now the school goers.\n- But in the backdrop of this statistics theknowled tter truth: despite the constitutional provision\nfor free and compulsory education for the for primary between the age group of 6-14 years, quality\nof education and infrastructure continues to be disappointing.\n• There are numerous administrative hurdles, leading to low\nadmissions in the school. A survey by the\n\"Child Rights and\nYou\" (CRY) finds that children continue to be refused admission\nto schools because of the lack of transfer documentation, birth\nregistration and proof of residence.\n- In some cases, parents of school-going children are\nCHILO RIGHTS AND YOU\nwww.cry.org\nEnsuring lasting change\ncompelled to pay fees. Such instances point to a serious\nbottleneck in the education system of the country.\nChild Rights and You (CRY)\n• This along with the following facts and figures makes education still a lingering challenge in India:\n(a) Large Number of Illiterates: India harbours the largest number of illiterates in the world.\n> Presently, nearly 36 crore people are estimated to be illiterate. The number exceeds even\nthe total population of most countries in the world.\n(b) Inadequate Vocationalisation: Education continues to be largely degree-oriented throwing\nmillions of educated youths down to the corridors of employment exchanges.\n• Vocationalisation of education* is still a far cry. As a result, the rate of unemployment\namong the educated youth is trending up.\n* In 2011-12, according to NSSO data, the rate of unemployment among graduate youth\nin rural areas was 19 per cent while that in urban areas was 16 per cent. The situation is\nmore grim in case of female graduates. Nearly, 30 per cent of the female graduates are\nunemployed.\n* Contrary to it, only 3-6 per cent of primary level youth were unemployed in both rural and\nurban areas. As per the Periodic Labour Force Survey 2022-23, this situation has improved\n(c) Gender Bias: There is still a significant gender bias* in offering opportunities of education\nto male and female children.\n* The enrolment ratio is relatively low for the female candidates and their drop-out ratio\nis considerably high. Education system in India thus needs a substantial change in the\nattitudes of the people.\n- It is important to promote education among women to improve their economic\nindependence and social status.\n16. Lingering challenge - a challenge (or a problem) that persists over time.\n17. Vocationalisation of educarion - practical knowledaton LIvork to enable skill-building in humans.\n18. Gender bias\ntency to give preferential treatment to one gender over another.\n183"},{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"ied-4-06","image":"ied-pages/ied-4-06.jpg","text":"Q. 12. \"Excessive involvement of the private sector in education causes more harm than good!\nComment.\nAns. It is true, as it makes education unaffordable for the people.\nQ. 13. Which body is responsible for making rules and regulations regarding technical/engineering\neducation in India?\nAns. AICTE (All India Council of Technical Education).\nQ. 14. Why do we say that the education pyramid of India is steep?\nAns. Because lesser and lesser number of people reach higher education level.\n0. 15. What does 'education expenditure of total government expenditure indicate?\nAns. It Indicates the importance of education in comparison to other areas of government\n0. 16. What flaws do you find in your education system that contributes to the problem\nof unemployment in the country?\nAns. Education system in India lacks vocationalisation. It is degree-oriented without imparting skill\nto the degree-holders to look for avenues of self-employment. It causes excess supply of labour\nseeking white collar jobs. Thus, it contributes to unemployment in the country.\nAccess level (in terms of availability of goods and services) - the opportunity or right to own it, use it or see it.\nliteracy - the condition of being unable to read and write.\noverty - the inability to fulfil the basic requirements of life.\nHuman Capital Formation in India"},{"title":"Power Points (Summary of the Chapter]","page":"ied-4-06","image":"ied-pages/ied-4-06.jpg","text":"of value-addition in the economy.\nHuman Resource: It refers to abilities (skill and expert man beings to contribute to the process\nHuman Capital: It refers to the stock of skill and experly of a nation at a point of time\nHuman Capital Formation: It refers to the procesewnhing to the stock of human capital.\nSources of Human Capital:\n(i) Expenditure on education\n(il) On-the-job training\n(v) Migration\n(ii) Expenditure on health\n(iv) Study programmes for adults\n(vi) Expenditure on information\nSignificance of Human Capital Formation:\n(i) Change in emotional and material environment of growth\n(i) Higher productivity of physical capital\n(i) Higher rate of participation and equality\nProblems facing Human Capital Formation in India:\n) Rising population\n(i) Brain drain\n(il) Deficient manpower planning\n(iv) Insufficient on-the-job training in primary sector\n(v) Low academic standards\nImportance and Objectives of Education:\n(i) Education produces good citizens.\n(ii) It develops science and technology.\n(iii) It facilitates use of resources in the country.\n(iv) It expands mental horizon of the people.\n(v) It helps in economic development through greater participation of the people in the process of\ngrowth and development.\n(vi) It promotes cultural standard of the citizens.\n(vii): It develops human personality.\nGrowth of Education Sector in India: It includes:\nexpenditure incurred on education, and\n(i) educational achievements in India.\nProblems Relating to Development of Education in India:\n(i) Large number of illiterates\n(il) Inadequate vocationalisation\n(ill) Gender bias\n(iv) Low rural access level\n(v) Privatisation\n(vi) Low government expenditure on education\n185"}]},{"id":"i5","number":"5","subject":"ied","title":"Rural Development","intro":"","lessons":[{"title":"Rural development and the action plan","body":"Rural development addresses economic, social and environmental problems together.\n\nPriorities: education/health, land and productive resources, infrastructure, credit and marketing.\n\nDiversification and poverty reduction create livelihoods beyond seasonal crop work.\n\nImproving rural life is broader than simply increasing farm output.","formula":"","source":"ied-5-12","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"5.1 Rural Development: Meaning and Significance","points":["Rural development addresses economic, social and environmental problems together.","Priorities: education/health, land and productive resources, infrastructure, credit and marketing.","Diversification and poverty reduction create livelihoods beyond seasonal crop work."],"takeaway":"Improving rural life is broader than simply increasing farm output."},{"title":"Why rural credit is needed","body":"Short-term credit pays for seeds, fertilisers, wages and needs before harvest income arrives.\n\nLong-term credit pays for machinery, irrigation and land improvement.\n\nInstitutional sources: cooperatives, banks, RRBs and land-development banks.\n\nNon-institutional sources: moneylenders, landlords and traders.\n\nThe timing gap between spending on a crop and earning from it explains the need for credit.","formula":"","source":"ied-5-11","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"5.2.1 Rural Credit","points":["Short-term credit pays for seeds, fertilisers, wages and needs before harvest income arrives.","Long-term credit pays for machinery, irrigation and land improvement.","Institutional sources: cooperatives, banks, RRBs and land-development banks.","Non-institutional sources: moneylenders, landlords and traders."],"takeaway":"The timing gap between spending on a crop and earning from it explains the need for credit."},{"title":"NABARD, self-help groups and micro-credit","body":"NABARD, 1982: apex institution supporting and coordinating rural credit/development.\n\nSHGs pool members’ small savings and lend within the group, often linking to banks.\n\nMicro-credit can reach borrowers without conventional collateral and support women’s participation.\n\nSmall loans help inclusion; viable work, skills and market access determine whether incomes improve.","formula":"","source":"ied-5-10","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"5.2.1 Rural Credit · NABARD and Micro-credit","points":["NABARD, 1982: apex institution supporting and coordinating rural credit/development.","SHGs pool members’ small savings and lend within the group, often linking to banks.","Micro-credit can reach borrowers without conventional collateral and support women’s participation."],"takeaway":"Small loans help inclusion; viable work, skills and market access determine whether incomes improve."},{"title":"Weaknesses of rural credit","body":"Unequal access: small farmers may still depend on informal lenders.\n\nDelays, paperwork and collateral make institutional credit hard to obtain.\n\nCrop failure and unproductive borrowing make repayment difficult.\n\nOverdues weaken lenders and restrict future credit.\n\nGood credit needs the right timing, terms and productive use—not just a larger loan.","formula":"","source":"ied-5-09","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"5.2.1 Rural Credit · Problems","points":["Unequal access: small farmers may still depend on informal lenders.","Delays, paperwork and collateral make institutional credit hard to obtain.","Crop failure and unproductive borrowing make repayment difficult.","Overdues weaken lenders and restrict future credit."],"takeaway":"Good credit needs the right timing, terms and productive use—not just a larger loan."},{"title":"Agricultural marketing and its defects","body":"Marketing includes gathering, processing, grading, packing, storage, transport and sale.\n\nDefects: faulty weights, poor information, weak transport/storage and large intermediary margins.\n\nDistress sale: urgent cash needs force farmers to sell cheaply after harvest.\n\nCredit and marketing are linked: debt pressure plus no storage weakens the farmer’s bargaining power.","formula":"","source":"ied-5-08","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"5.2.2 Agricultural Marketing","points":["Marketing includes gathering, processing, grading, packing, storage, transport and sale.","Defects: faulty weights, poor information, weak transport/storage and large intermediary margins.","Distress sale: urgent cash needs force farmers to sell cheaply after harvest."],"takeaway":"Credit and marketing are linked: debt pressure plus no storage weakens the farmer’s bargaining power."},{"title":"Improving agricultural marketing","body":"Regulated markets improve fairness; roads, storage and information reduce losses.\n\nCooperatives pool produce and strengthen bargaining.\n\nMSP, procurement, buffer stocks and PDS support the food-marketing system.\n\nDirect markets reduce intermediaries; contracts offer markets but can create bargaining risks.\n\nDifferent measures fix different problems: storage addresses timing; market information addresses bargaining.","formula":"","source":"ied-5-07","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"5.2.2 Agricultural Marketing · Remedial Measures","points":["Regulated markets improve fairness; roads, storage and information reduce losses.","Cooperatives pool produce and strengthen bargaining.","MSP, procurement, buffer stocks and PDS support the food-marketing system.","Direct markets reduce intermediaries; contracts offer markets but can create bargaining risks."],"takeaway":"Different measures fix different problems: storage addresses timing; market information addresses bargaining."},{"title":"Why rural diversification matters","body":"Crop diversification changes the mix of crops; broader diversification adds allied/non-farm work.\n\nWhy? Spread risk, earn outside the crop season and absorb surplus farm labour.\n\nOptions: animal husbandry, fisheries, horticulture, processing and rural services.\n\nDiversification turns a single seasonal income source into several livelihood opportunities.","formula":"","source":"ied-5-06","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"5.3.1 Diversification of Productive Activities","points":["Crop diversification changes the mix of crops; broader diversification adds allied/non-farm work.","Why? Spread risk, earn outside the crop season and absorb surplus farm labour.","Options: animal husbandry, fisheries, horticulture, processing and rural services."],"takeaway":"Diversification turns a single seasonal income source into several livelihood opportunities."},{"title":"Animal husbandry, fisheries and Operation Flood","body":"Animal husbandry supplies milk, eggs and meat, adding income alongside crops.\n\nOperation Flood: milk cooperatives linked producers and consumers, supporting the White Revolution.\n\nFisheries create jobs but need finance, equipment, storage and protection from pollution.\n\nVeterinary care, feed and better breeds raise livestock productivity.\n\nAllied activities reduce dependence on crops, but still require reliable support services and markets.","formula":"","source":"ied-5-05","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"5.3.1 Diversification · Animal Husbandry and Fisheries","points":["Animal husbandry supplies milk, eggs and meat, adding income alongside crops.","Operation Flood: milk cooperatives linked producers and consumers, supporting the White Revolution.","Fisheries create jobs but need finance, equipment, storage and protection from pollution.","Veterinary care, feed and better breeds raise livestock productivity."],"takeaway":"Allied activities reduce dependence on crops, but still require reliable support services and markets."},{"title":"Horticulture and information technology","body":"Horticulture: fruits, vegetables, flowers and plantation crops can raise incomes and improve nutrition.\n\nPerishability makes cold chains, transport and processing important.\n\nIT provides weather, prices, advice and links to work/markets.\n\nElectricity, connectivity and skills determine who benefits from IT.\n\nProducing a valuable crop is only half the task; getting it to buyers without spoilage is the other half.","formula":"","source":"ied-5-04","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"5.3.1 Diversification · Horticulture and IT","points":["Horticulture: fruits, vegetables, flowers and plantation crops can raise incomes and improve nutrition.","Perishability makes cold chains, transport and processing important.","IT provides weather, prices, advice and links to work/markets.","Electricity, connectivity and skills determine who benefits from IT."],"takeaway":"Producing a valuable crop is only half the task; getting it to buyers without spoilage is the other half."},{"title":"Organic farming: meaning and advantages","body":"Organic farming uses organic inputs and ecological methods rather than synthetic chemicals.\n\nBenefits: healthier soil, less pollution and lower dependence on non-renewable chemical inputs.\n\nTextbook headings also emphasise healthier food, inexpensive technology and potential export premiums.\n\nIndia’s abundant labour can support labour-intensive organic methods.\n\nKeep the environmental benefits clear; premium prices and lower costs are possibilities, not guarantees.","formula":"","source":"ied-5-03","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"5.3.2 Organic Farming","points":["Organic farming uses organic inputs and ecological methods rather than synthetic chemicals.","Benefits: healthier soil, less pollution and lower dependence on non-renewable chemical inputs.","Textbook headings also emphasise healthier food, inexpensive technology and potential export premiums.","India’s abundant labour can support labour-intensive organic methods."],"takeaway":"Keep the environmental benefits clear; premium prices and lower costs are possibilities, not guarantees."},{"title":"Organic farming: constraints and the PRANAM box","body":"Constraints: lower transition yields, high cost per unit and shorter shelf life.\n\nFarmers need training, suitable inputs, certification and marketing.\n\nPRANAM box: encourage alternative nutrients and balanced fertiliser use to protect soil health.\n\nOrganic farming becomes viable when production support is matched by a reliable market.","formula":"","source":"ied-5-02","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"5.3.2 Organic Farming · Limitations and Support","points":["Constraints: lower transition yields, high cost per unit and shorter shelf life.","Farmers need training, suitable inputs, certification and marketing.","PRANAM box: encourage alternative nutrients and balanced fertiliser use to protect soil health."],"takeaway":"Organic farming becomes viable when production support is matched by a reliable market."}],"pages":[{"id":"ied-5-12","image":"ied-pages/ied-5-12.jpg","text":"Indian Economic Development\n5.1 RURAL DEVELOPMENT: MEANING AND SIGNIFICANCE\n5.1.1 Meaning\nkne geropment meansal Achon-plan for adaressing the economic, social and environmental\na ta be a can t angers an emerging challenges di\nthe rural econoy, al ram tse allenges in a manner such that\npa de gre with the overall development of the economy as a wide,\n• te context or the dian rural economy, there aleans of lingering and emerging challenge\nwhich tend to restrict the growth and development of our rural sector.\n• Two principal lingering challenges' are:\n(i) challenge of rural credit, and\n(ii) challenge of rural marketing.\n• Two principal emerging challenges are:\n(i) the need for diversification of productive activities, and\n(ii) the need for organic farming.\n• Besides these core elements of the action-plan, there are certain related issues such as:\n(i) development of the means of transport, linking rural areas with the urban areas,\n(ii) provision of power/energy for the rural areas,\n(iii) permanent means of irrigation,\n(iv) facilities for agricultural research, and\n(V) development of human resources (via focus on education, skill development, health and\nHowever, this chapter of our text focuses only on the four core elements (in accordance with the\nsyllabus specifications), viz., 'rural credit'\nand 'rural marketing'\n'diversification of productive activities' and organic farming' as the emerging challenges.\n5.1.2 Significance\nFollowing observations highlight the significance of rural development in India:\nThe bulk of our population lives in rural areas, loadedle gabject poverty. Overall development of\nte e wo bur - unless ge with the growth and development\nof the rural areas.\n• The reforms of 1991 tocused largely on industruch elopment and external trade,\ngricultural sector Was ignored. investment in Concultural infrastructuantwas nowhere in the\npriority list of the reform programmes.\n- Consequency, the Suous If between the overall GDP growth on the one hand and\ngrowth of the agricultural sector on the other.\n1. Lingering challenges - ssues or challenges that have lasted a long um bawa - of rural development, these\nrelate to issues of rural credit and rural marketing.\nthese relate to issues of need for diversification in productive activities and organin s\n212\nRural Development\n• Historically, the growth process of developed nations has been driven by the growth process of\nindustrial sector, of course, but not without the concurrent growth of the agricultural sector.\n- But, the Indian growth story retlects agricultural sector as the laggard sector intlicted with\nmassive unemployment and poverty. This sector considerably lacks alternative means of\nsustainable living, alternative to crop farming.\n* After the initiation of economic reforms, the growth rate of the agriculture sector hovered around\n3 per cent per annum during 1991-2012, which was lower than the earlier decades. The growth\nrate further decelerated to around 2 per cent in 2023-24, compared to the overall GDP growth of\n7-8 percent during the same period.\n• The persistent gap between the growth rate of agricultural sector and overall GDP growth has led\nto a structural imbalance in the economy.\n• Deceleration of growth of the agricultural sector has happened primarily due to the deceleration\nof government investment in this sector since 1991, besides a host of other factors such as the\nlack of rural infrastructure, limited alternative employment opportunities, and the increasing\ncasualisation of rural labour.\n• The widening rural-urban gap in terms of their growth rate has led to a widening 'rural-urban\neconomic divide, and a widespread distress among the farming families.\nIt is in the light of these observations that the significance of rural development cannot be undermined.\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\n9.1. \"The COVID-19 pandemic and consequent lockdowns have adversely impacted the livelihoods\nof street vendors. Accordingly, the Ministry of Housing & Urban Affairs launched a scheme\nPM Street Vendor's AatmaNirbhar Nidhi (PM SVANidhi) to empower Street Vendors by not only\nextending loans to them, but also for their holistic development and economic upliftment.\"\nSource: india.gov.in\nThe above scheme was initiated to satisfy the credit requirements of vendors. Defend or\nrefute the given statement.\nAns. The above statement is defended as PM SVANidhi strives to provide credit to help in economic\nupliftment of pandemic affected vendors.\n9.2. As a minister for rural development, what will be your action-plan for the development of\nAns. As a minister for rural development, my action-plan would focus on three basic objectives:\n(i) Crop productivity must be increased to increase income from farming. Agriculture should no\nlonger remain merely as a means of subsistence. It must be transformed into a commercial\nactivity that generates profit.\n(ii) Avenues of employment outside agriculture must be explored and developed. This is\nto ensure that rural development no longer remains farm-centric. It becomes more\ncomprehensive and meaningful.\n(i) Rural development must be integrated with human development. This can be achieved only\nwhen a significant focus is placed on education and health in the rural areas.\nBriefly, action-plan for rural development must be holistic/comprehensive. It must focus on all\naspects of rural development. It should not be restricted merely to the development of agriculture.\n3. Concurrent growth - relates to simultaneous development.\n4. Structural imbalance - when different sectors of the economy do not grow in tandem (or side by side) with each other.\n5. Casualisation (in the context of employment) - refers to a situation when the percentage of casually-hired workers (like\ndaily wagers, not\nanent rolls of the employers) in the total workforce tends to rise over time.\n213","file":"chap5.pdf"},{"id":"ied-5-11","image":"ied-pages/ied-5-11.jpg","text":"Indian Economic Development\n5.2 LINGERING CHALLENGES OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT:\nRURAL CREDIT AND AGRICULTURAL MARKETING\n5.2.1 Rural Credit\n• Rural credit or agricultural creal means credit for fart systemart is the lifeline of taming\nactivity in rural areas.\nNeed for Rural Credit\n• The need for rural credit arises due to two reasons:\n(i) Most farming families in India are small and marginal holders, producing just enough for\nsubsistence. They seldom generate surplus for further investment. The need for credit,\ntherefore, is unavoidable.\n(i) The gestation lag between sowing and harvesting of the crops is quite long. This compounds\nthe need for credit to meet the initial investment on seeds, fertilizers, implements and some\nfamily expenses like marriage, death, birth, or other religious ceremonies.\nCredit needs of the typical Indian farmer are broadly classified into three categories as under:\nCredit Needs of Indian Farmer\nShort-term Credit\n• It is basically required for the\npurchase of inputs like seeds,\nfertilizers, pesticides and\ninsecticides, besides making\npayments of electricity bills.\n• These loans are raised generally\n• It is required for the purchase\nof machinery, construction of\ntences, and digging the wells.\n• These loans are raised generally\nfor a period ranging between\n12 months to 5 years.\nfor a period ranging between\nLong-term Credit\n• It is required for the purchase of\nadditional land or for carrying\nout permanent improvements\non the existing land.\n• The period of such loans ranges\nbetween 5 to 20 years.\n6 to 12 months.\n• The credit requirement of the farmers may also be classified as productive and unproductive.\n- The productive requirement of credit relates to production activity like purchase of inputs).\n- The unproductive requirement of credit relates to consumption activity (like expenses on\nceremonies, or personal expenses).\nHIGHLIGHTS\nEvolution of Rural Credit\n• At the time of independence, institutional credit showed only a notional existence.\n• Non-institutional credit ruled the roost. It was dominated by moneylenders, traders and commission\nilliterate farmers.\nagents who charged exorbitantly high interest rates, and manipulated the accounts to exploit the\n• In fact, non-institutional credit often led to a debt trap for the farmers.\n• A significant change occurred in 1969 with nationalisation of commercial banks.\nwas ten that the concept suble banking was patinto practice and credit needs of the farmers wer\naddressed as a priority issue in the context of rural development.\npayment in the form of interest.\n7. Debt trap - a situation where an individual or a company borrows money to repay old debte\nnever ending cycle of debt.\nand in turn lands in a\n214\nRural Development\nGreen Revolution: The Harbinger of Changes in Rural Credit System\n• Green Revolution led to the change in outlook of the farmers from supsistence tarring to commercia\n• Consequently, with the increasing demand or rural credit, there was a major change in the credit system\nasiled to the diversication te port or tra creat, tocusing on production loans.\nSources of Rural Credit\nsources of rural or agricuitural credit are broadly classified as under:\nSources of Rural Credit\nInstitutional Sources\n(modern/emerging sources)\nNon-institutional Sources\n(conventional/traditional sources)\n(a) Non-institutional Sources\n• Sources: (i) Landlords, (ii) Village traders, and (ili) Moneylenders\n•Evolution: Traditionally, most credit needs of the farmers were met through these sources.\n- Non-institutional sources accounted for 93 per cent of the total borrowing of the farmers in the\nbeginning of First Five Year Plan.\n- But by 1981, the institutional sources emerged as the principal channel of rural credit.\n(b) Institutional Sources\n• Sources: (i) Government, (i) Cooperatives, (i) Commercial banks, and (iv) Regional Rural Banks.\n• Evolution: Together, they accounted for only 7 per cent of the credit needs of the farmers in the\nbeginning of First Five Year Plan, but presently their share has increased to over 66 per cent.\n- Out of total institutional credit, cooperative credit contributes nearly 15 per cent and commercial\nbanks about 75 per cent.\n- Apart from substantial expansion in the flow of credit by commercial banks and Regional Rural\nBanks (RRBs), the cooperative credit institutions have emerged as important institutional\nagencies offering credit support and related services.\nFollowing is a brief description of some important institutional agencies offering rural credit in\nImportant Institutional Agencies offering Rural Credit in India\nCooperative\nCredit Societies\nState Bank\nand other\nCommercial Banks\nRegional Rurar\nBanks (RRBs) and\nLand Development\nNational Bank for\nAgricultural and\nRural Development\n(NABARD)\n215","file":"chap5.pdf"},{"id":"ied-5-10","image":"ied-pages/ied-5-10.jpg","text":"Indian Economic Development\n(a) Cooperative Credit Societies: he cooperative credit some ofprovide adequate credit to th\nfarmers at reasonable rate of interest.\n• These societes also provide sudance it alved to offer untural operations with a view to\nraising crop productivity.\n› Currently, cooperatives account for 16-17 per cent of rural credit flow.\nCooperative credit societies are to ensure:\n(i) Timely and rapid flow of credit to the farmers.\n(ii) Elimination of the moneylenders as credit agencies.\n(iii) Spread credit facilities across all regions of the country.\n(iv) Provision of adequate credit in areas covered by special programmes of development.\n(b) State Bank of India and Other Commercial Banks: The State Bank of India was set-up in 195\nwith a focus on rural credit.\n> The government realised that rural credit needs could not be met by the cooperative\ncredit societies alone, and that commercial banks should play an important role.\n> This prompted nationalisation® of certain banks in 1969. The nationalised commercial\nbanks (through their branch expansion programmes) were directed to offer credit directy\nto the farmers as well as indirectly through cooperative societies.\nDSBI\nBanks-Institutional Sources of Rural Credit\n(c) Regional Kural Banks (RRBS) and Land Development Banks: RRBs and Land Development\nBanks were set-up to promote credit suppies, particularl rations mote rural areas and\nbackward districts.\n* These banks Licut/ons district level and are under obligation to focus on credit need\nof weaker sections of the rural population.\n(d) National Bank for Agricultural and Rural Development (NABARD):\nNABARD is an apex institution handling policy, planning and operations\nin the field of rural credit and related economic activities, It was set-up in\n1982.\nNABARD\n8. Nationalisation - the transfer of a major branch of industry or commerce from private to state owners\n216\nRural Development\n! HIGHLIGHTS\nMulti-agency Institutions\n• Institutional structure of rural banking consists of:\n(i) Commercial banks,\n(i) Cooperative Credit Societies, and\n(il) Regional Rural Banks (RRBs),\n(iv) Land Development Banks.\n• This institutional structure of rural banking is called multi-agency institutions. ney are expected to offer\nadequate credit at cheaper rates.\nKudumbashree —The Poor Women's Bank\n• Kudumbashree was started in 1995 in Kerala as a small savings bank tor poor women to encourage\nsavings.\nThese societies have been acclaimed as the largest informall banks in Asia in terms of participation and\nsavings mobilised. The thrift and credit society mobilised? 1 crore as thrift savings.\nThe four main functions of NABARD are as these:\n(i) To serve as an apex funding agency for the institutions providing credit in rural areas.\n(ii) To take appropriate measures to improve the credit delivery system. The bank was to\nfocus on restructuring of credit institutions and training of personnel.\n(i) To coordinate the rural financing activities of all credit institutions and maintain liaison\n(connection) with Government of India, State Government, Reserve Bank, and other\nnational level institutions concerned with policy formulation.\n(iv) To undertake monitoring and evaluation of projects refinanced by it.\n• HIGHLIGHTS\nRBI (Reserve Bank of India) and Rural Credit\n• If the rural credit system in India has acquired its present size and significance, it is essentially due to\nthe key role played by RBI.\n• Since its beginning in 1935, the Reserve Bank of India has been playing an important role in providing\ncredit to agriculturists and strengthening credit institution, primary cooperative credit societles ana\nLand Development Banks in particular.\n• It focuses on:\n() short-term refinance facilities for agriculture and allied activities.\n(i) development of cooperative credit institutions,\n(ill) expansion of the sources of tunds for short-term and long-term wings of the cooperative creait systen,\n(iv) provision of long-term funds to bring about state partnership in cooperative credit agencies,\n(v) training and professionalisation of cooperatives,\n(vi) conducting rural credit surveys to determine the coverage of rural housenolas by creditstions\n(vii) helping bank-branch expansion programme to provide cheaper remittance facilities for banks\noperating in rural areas, and\n(vii) guidance to all concerned on matters relating to rural credit.\n• Thus, the role of RBI in the field of agricultural credit has been multi-dimensional, though largely\nregulatory in nature.\n9. Allied activities - activities that depend upon agricultural activity in various ways and often atfect the outcome o\nThana include antivities like animal husbandry and fishery.\n217","file":"chap5.pdf"},{"id":"ied-5-09","image":"ied-pages/ied-5-09.jpg","text":"malan Economic Development\nMicro Credit Programmes and Selt-help Groups\nc me in Small dan to the individuals, enabling them to become self-employs\nthrough small enterprises.\nMicro Credit Frogames have been starten in less developed countries to render financial he\ni to the tact that these people are table heir bank ly collateral for the grant of ans\nmic cret programs have become populat as Intermar rean Denvery Mechaniem', becaus\nloans are outered without collateral, at a very moderate rause of interest, involving minimum lea\nindia, one sun micro real programme is selt-help Groups (SHGS. Shos promote thrift saving\nhabit) among rural households. Small savings are mobilised by the SiGs and offered as credit to is\ndifferent members, depending on their needs.\n• By May 2019, nearly 54 lakh women SHGs with almost 6 crore women in India as members are\noperating across different rural areas. About & 10-15,000 per SHG and another? 2.5 lakh per\nSHG as a Community Investment Support Fund (CISF) are provided as part of renovating fund\nto take up self-employment for income generation. SHGs have significantly contributed to the\nempowerment of women.\n! HIGHLIGHTS\nKisan Credit Card (KCC) Scheme\n• The Kisan Credit Card Scheme aims at adequate and timely support to the farmers for their short-term\ncredit needs.\n• Under the scheme, banks may issue Kisan Credit Cards to the farmers who are otherwise eligible for\nshort-term credit for crop production, allied activities and other non-farm activities.\n• The farmers under the scheme are issued a credit card cum passbook incorporating the name, address,\nparticulars of land holding, borrowing limits, validity period, etc.\n8 AVLR 5.1\nMICRO CREDIT PROGRAMMES AND SELF-HELP GROUPS\nRural Banking - A Critical Evaluation\nAdvantages\nL Da ts Say expanded over ame. te expansion has been signincan\nand came banks in 1ant. Natonalisation of commercia\nbanks marked the beginning of social banking\"\nand personalised credit needs of farmers.\n10. Self-employed - working for oneself as a freelance or the owner of a business rather than for an employer.\n11. Collateral - refers to the asset to be pledged as security for the grant of loans.\n12. Social banking - banking with the purpose of creating a positive impact on the society.\n218\n• Establishment of NABARD (as an apex body coordinating activities of all financial institutions\nRural Development\nengaged in rural creal pros ahesh me deas these: dillerence in the institution of rural\ncredit. Rural nancing has become much more organised than ever before\nInstitutional credit has gone a long way in iberauing the farmers from the debt trap of mahajans\nand moneylenders.\na Institutional credit has promotea commercialisation of agriculture.\nDeficiencies\nDeficiencies or rural banking system are equally glaring. Notable observations in this regard are as these:\nbanking credit or institutional credit has invariably been tea to collateral because of which a large\nsection of small and marginal holders are often left out.\n• Apart from commercial banks, most financial institutions have failed to develop a culture of thrift\n(saving for the future) among the farming families. Mobilisation of deposits has remained rather\nsubdued.\nSocial banking in rural areas has taken a hit in the wake of economic reforms of 1991. Impact of debt\non the farmers is generally becoming so huge that suicidal deaths are rising from scores to centuries,\nparticularly in the states of Andhra Pradesh and Maharashtra.\nThus, the expansion and promotion of the rural banking sector has taken a backseat after reforms.\nHIGHLIGHTS\nAn Alarming Fact\n• Default rate among the borrowers is assuming alarming proportions.\n\" While many may be genuine defaulters, investigations suggest that nearly 50 per cent are wiltul\n• This certainly is an alarming development and may shake the very foundation of rural banking system\nWay Ahead\n• To improve the situation, in recent years, all the adults are encouraged to open bank accounts a\na part of a scheme known as Jan-Dhan Yojana\".\n• The account-holders are entitled to * 1-2 lakh accidental insurance coverage and overdraft\nfacility for & 10.000. Also, they can receive their wages (related to any government job unde\nschemes like MGNREGA), old-age pension and other social security payments through their bank\naccounts. (For description of MGNREGA, see next chapter.)\n13. Default rate - hiaher interest rate imposed on a borrower who has missed regular payments on a loan.\n14. Jan-Dhan Yojana (also known as Pradhan Mantri Jan-Dhan Yojana) - is a financial inclusion scheme launched by the\n15. Overdraft - occurs when one withdraws or spends more money than what is available in his/her bank account,\ninsurance, and financial services.\nGovernment of India in 2014 to ensure universal access to basic banking services, including zero-balance accounts,\nwith fees or interest.\nresulting in a negative balance. The bank temporarily covers the extra amount and he/she is required to repay it - often\n219","file":"chap5.pdf"},{"id":"ied-5-08","image":"ied-pages/ied-5-08.jpg","text":"Indian Economic Development\nwith the banks. This has led to more than so crore new account-holders in the country; indirect,\npromoting thie and emcient allocaton of resources, particularly among the rural masse\n• Jan-Dhan Yojana nas enabled banks to mobilise funds of more than $ 2,00,000 crore, even when\nany minimum balance is not required to be maintained.\n• it is high time that creal prog aties in the turdi areas are promoted as a critical catalyst of rura.\ndevelopment, rather than as an instrument of political popularity of the government.\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\nQ. 3. Why is credit considered as the lifeline of farming activity in rural areas?\n(i) Farmers lack surplus for investment.\n(ii) Gestation period between sowing and harvesting is long.\nQ. 4. Name two non-institutional sources of rural credit.\nAns. (i) Landlords, and (ii) Moneylenders.\nQ. 5. Why are cooperative credit societies welcomed?\nAns. Because they facilitate timely and rapid flow of credit to farmers at reasonable rate of interest.\nQ. 6. Which agency serves as the apex funding agency for the institutions providing credit in rural\nAns. National Bank for Agriculture and Rural Development (NABARD).\nQ. 7. Enlist a micro credit programme that has promoted thrift among rural households.\nAns. Self-help Groups (SHGs).\nQ. 8. What is the main aim of the Kisan Credit Card Scheme?\nAns. It aims to provide adequate and timely support to the farmers for their short-term credit\nneeds.\nQ. 9. Why does a farmer require credit? Write your observations.\nAns. Credit need of the farmer may be split into three parts:\n(i) Short-term Credit\n: Needed for the purchase of inputs.\n(i) Medium-term Credit: Needed for the purchase of equipments/appliances.\n(ili) Long-term Credit\n: Needed for the purchase of land or improvement of the existing\n5.2.2 Agricultural Marketing [Marketing in Rural Areas]\nAfter harvestine, the fathers need to assemble their produce on the farm, process It, grade it\npassed nan pat market for sale. Agricurtural marketing\nincludes all these processes between harvesting and final sale of the produce by the farmers.\n• Meaning: Agricultural marketing may be defined as the process that involves:\n(i) gathering the produce after harvesting,\n(ii) processing the produce (like separating the straw from the grains),\ngrading the produce according to its quality,\n(iv) packaging the produce according to buyers' preferences,\n220\n(v) storing the produce for future sale, and\n(vi) selling and distributing the produce when the price is lucrative.\none full simply terer ters to bring terme for sale\nrather it includes all those activities/ processes which help ist getting best price for his produce\n! HIGHLIGHTS\nAgricultural Marketing\n• Need for State intervention. At the time the full ence, agricultural marketing was almost\nunder the compress contrarer the money gis pre, daders and commission agents\nTheir holding capacity was almost zero.\n- Having taken loans from the moneylenders (who happened to be traders and commission\nagents as well), the farmers were obliged to sell their crop to them only.\n- Often, it implied a low price, besides a substantial loss due to faulty weighing, manipulation of\naccounts, lack of storage facilities and customary commissions.\n- Farmers also lacked the required information about prices prevailing in the market which\nfurther forced them to sell their produce at a low price.\nAll these factors make it important that state intervenes in the agricultural markets to regulate the\nactivities of private traders.\n! HIGHLIGHTS\nWhy Distress Sale?\n• The compulsion to sell arises because:\n() the farmers need immediate cash to pay off their debts, and\n(it hey lack storage facilities or the cost of storage is very high.\nMeasures initiated by the Government to improve Marketing System\nin order to improve the system of agricultural marketing, the government has initiated a series of\nmeasures, as given below:\n(a) Regulated Markets: Regulated markets have been established where sale and purchase of the\nproduce is monitored by the Market Committee comprised of representatives of government\nfarmers and the traders.\n* Market system is made transparent with a strict vigil on the use of proper scales and weights\nThis has gone a long way in protecting the interest of the farmers as well as the consumers\n• The market committees ensure that the farmers get appropriate price for their produce.\n* There is a need to develop about 27,000 rural periodic market places to realise the full\npotential of rural markets.\n10. Distress sale - a situation when the farmers are compelled to sell their produce immediately after the harvest, no\nmatter how low the market price is.","file":"chap5.pdf"},{"id":"ied-5-07","image":"ied-pages/ied-5-07.jpg","text":"Indian Economic Development\nHIGHLIGHTS\nRegulated Market Yards\n•The farmers can bone to these fatas tor ail farmers i their produce. these yards also offer storag\n(b) Provision of Physical infrastructure: Physical infrastructure includes transport ate\nstorage facilities, and processing units.\n• Better transport tacilities like roadways, ralways enary e the farmers to sell their produc\nat locations offering lucrative price.\n* Storage helps the farmers to sell their produce at a time when the market price is lucrative.\nThere is a need to increase storage facilities (warehouses, godowns and cold storage units)\nas even today, more than 10 per cent of the goods produced in farms are wasted due to\n> The current physical infrastructure facilities are quite inadequate, and need to be\nsignificantly improved to meet the growing demand\n(c) Cooperative Agricultural Marketing Societies: The government is encouraging the formation\nof Cooperative Agricultural Marketing Societies.\n> As members of these societies, farmers find themselves better bargainers in the market,\ngetting better price of their produce through collective sale?.\n! HIGHLIGHTS\nCooperative Marketing\n• Cooperative marketing is a significant progressive step in the context of agricultural market system.\n• Milk cooperatives in Gujarat are a brilliant example of cooperative marketing in rural India.\nThese cooperatives have not only changed the social and economic fabric of the economy of Gujarat,\nbut also have played a key role in bringing about White Revolution in the country.\n• However, of late, some kinks have emerged in the system of cooperative marketing. These are:\n(i) poor coordination between the marketing cooperatives and processing cooperatives, and\n(i) snags (shortcomings) in financial management of the cooperative.\n(d) MSP (Minimum Support Price) and Related Policy Instruments: MSP is a policy instrument\nthat assures the tarmers that their produce, if remains unsold, would be purchased by the\ngovernment at the specified minimum price.\n> This is an important policy instrument in the context of the agricultural marketing system,\nas it addresses volatility of the market and assures minimum stable income to the farmers.\nAlso, MSP works as a critical minimum price in the market; often the market price happens\nto be higher than the MSP.\n> However, success of MSP depends upon the two related policy instruments which the\ngovernment is simultaneously pursuing, viz.,\n17. Collective sale - relates to pooled sale by owners of separate units within a development and is often assoriated with\nhigher prices due to increased bargaining power.\n222\n(i) Maintenance of Ms purchases as a butter stock, the task assigned to Food Corporation\n(i). Distribution of Mr purchases among te Bro (below poverty line) residents with\ndistribution system.\n[Here, Imay be noted traur asshotsurely conned totne distributon or Mor purchases\nit also supplies ner essent ter poetaton such as san ersene\n• HIGHLIGHTS\nBuffer Stocks and PDS\n• MSP purchases from the farmers are kept as buffer stocks by\nthe government. These Stocks (of grains) are used, primarily\nfor Public Distribution System (PDS), besides meeting urgent\nneeds during periods of low output and scarcity.\n• PDS implies distribution of food grains (and other essentials\nlike sugar and kerosene) through \"fair price shops' at\nsubsidised rates, so that the poorer sections of the society\nhave an easy access to these things.\nBuffer Stock to Support MSP\nWay Ahead\n• On account of these measures by the government, agricultural marketing system has made\nsome notable improvements. But, it has yet to go a long way before the millions of small and\nmarginal farmers are able to defend their self-interest at all stages of the marketing process.\n• The fact is that small and marginal farmers still continue to be the victims of distress sale to the\nrural moneylenders and commission agents.\n• Surprisingly enough, nearly 90 per cent of agricultural marketing is still handled by the private\ntraders, while the government agencies and cooperative societies are handling merely 10 per cent.\n• HIGHLIGHTS\nShortcomings of Agricultural Marketing\n• Lack of transportation facilities\n• Forced sales\n• Numerous market changes\n• Lack of credit facilities\n• Lack of storage facilities\n• Presence of middlemen\n• Lack of standard weights and measures\n• Inadequate market information.\nAlternative (Emerging) Marketing Channels-A Ray of Hope\n• Emerging alternatives of agricultural marketing are a ray of hope to the small and marginal farmers\n• Direct sale by the farmers to the consumers is one such channel. This channel increases their\nwho have been silent sufferers at tne nands or middlemen\nincome directly.","file":"chap5.pdf"},{"id":"ied-5-06","image":"ied-pages/ied-5-06.jpg","text":"are launching it through kyunu Bakdals unel ale and vegetables markets), and the state of\nTamil Nadu is launching it through 'Uzhavar Sandies' (farmers markets).\n• Direct sales tract amance with the laters by the natonal and multinational companies\n(like Reliance Retails) is another alternative channel of agricultural marketing.\n- Through this channel, the farmers are often induderging cultivate lucrative products of hig\na te a de payerging the farmers to supply farm products\nat the pre-determined rates.\n- It is hoped that, these systems of marketing grow in tandem with the growth of national and\nmultinational retails. This would not only help in expansion of market for the farm products,\nbut also reduce 'price-risk' for the small and marginal holders.\n! HIGHLIGHTS\nReduction of Price-Risk (Market-Risk) through Crop Diversification\n• Market risk of the farmers can be lowered if crop\ndiversification is adopted. It occurs when more than\none crop is grown on a given land.\n• Prices of all crops may not drop at the same time. If\none crop fetches low revenue (because of low price), the\nother may fetch high revenue (because of high price).\n• However, small and marginal farmers are reluctant\nto adopt crop diversification. Instead, they focus on a\nsingle crop which provides them food for subsistence.\nDiversification lowers Market Risk\n• Small and marginal farmers continue to grow subsistence crops), no matter what the market price of\nthese crops is. Because, this does not expose them to uncertainties of the market as regards their need\n• Thus, security of food for the family deters the subsistence farmers to opt for crop diversification.\nshops to the poorer sections of society.\ninvolves distribution of food grains at subsidised rates through fair price\nAns. Public Distribution System (PDS).\n5.3 EMERGING CHALLENGES OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT:\nDIVERSIFICATION OF PRODUCTIVE ACTIVITIES\nAND ORGANIC FARMING\n5.3.1 Diversification of Productive Activities\n• rinding optons or sustainable livelinood (other than crop tarming/s a important emerging\nchallenge of rural development in India.\nSome possible options of sustainable livelihood are as under:\nAlternative Productive Activities\nHorticulture\nFisheries\nAnimal Husbandry\n(a) Animal Husbandry\n• Animal Husbandry in India: Animal husbandry is an important area of employment in India\ndifferent from crop farming. It is also called livestock farming.\n- Poultry, cattle, and goats/sheep are important components of livestock in India. Poultry accounts\nfor 61 per cent, cattle & buffaloes 22 per cent and goats & sheep nearly 16 per cent share in\ntotal livestock in India.\n- In 2019, India had about 303 million cattle including 110 million buffaloes.\nAGRICULTURAL MARKET SYSTEM\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\nQ. 10. What are the main advantages of regulated markets to farmers?\nAns.\n(i) It ensures transparency and a check on the use of proper scales and weights.\n(ii) It ensures that farmers get appropriate price for their produce,\nQ. 11. How does grading of output raise revenue of the farmer?\nAns. Grading of output refers to division of output in different categories on\nThe farmer can charge higher price for the high-graded output. This incre\nfrom the sale of his output.\n'quality.\nI revenue\n224\n18. Animal husban\nAnimal Husbandry: Employment Outside Agriculture\nrience of breeding and caring for farm animals.\n225","file":"chap5.pdf"},{"id":"ied-5-05","image":"ied-pages/ied-5-05.jpg","text":"• Crop-livestock: in most areas, vestock arming is combined With crop farming by the tur\nfamilies with a view to supplerelig their income. This is known as mixed crop-livestock\nfarming system.\nard areas, the sighilale livestock lating ends to increase while that of crop farming\ntends to decrease. Obviously because, lesser the irrigation (or more ard the area lesser 1s i\nscope for crop farming.\nL importance, Livestock targ in india is an important source of emproyment for the women.\nand marginal farmers (including landless labourers) in the countryside.\n- Over the last three decades, the pertormance of dairy secttyas peen quite impressive. buring\nthe period 1951-2021, milk production in the country has increased by about twelve times. This\nmainly a result of successful implementation of 'Operation Flood'.\n- Meat, eggs, wool and other by-products are also emerging as important productive sectors for\ndiversification.\n• Problems: However, livestock sector is not free from problems. In terms of its size, our livestock\npopulation is perhaps the largest in the world. But in terms of its quality we are far behind from\nmost developed countries in the world. Two principal problems related to livestock sector are:\n(i) Low productivity owing to backward know-how, and\n(ii) Deficient veterinary care.\n• Way Ahead: There is an urgent need to bring in new technology for promotion of good breeds of\nanimals to enhance productivity.\n- Provisions need to be made for improved veterinary services and credit facilities to small &\nmarginal farmers and landless labourers so that they can effectively take up livestock production\nand enhance their livelihood.\nHIGHLIGHTS\nOperation Flood\n• It is a system of Milk Cooperatives, started in 1970 also known as White Revolution'.\n• The system requires the member farmers to pool their produce of milk according to different grading\n(based on quality) for collective sale in the urban market.\n• Pooling increases quantum of sale, and thereby increases bargaining power of the farmers.\n• Pooling also makes grading and processing of the product as economically viable propositions.\nThis raises market value of the product and assured farmers of a fair price and income from supply of\nmilk to urban markets.\n• Milk cooperatives in India have their epicentre in the state of Gujarat. The other major milk producing\nstates are Madhya Pradesh, Uttar Pradesh, Andhra Pradesh, Maharashtra, Punjab and Rajasthan.\n• Over the last su years or so, inese cooperatives have recorded a wonderful progress, significantly\ncontributing to the production of milk. It has proved to be artlynportant non-tarm area of incom\ngeneration in the rural areas.\n19. Crop-livestock- a system of agricultural production which combines one or more cror\nne type of livestock.\n226\nRural Development\n(b) Fisheries\n* Fisheries in India: vest bengal, Anahra\nPradesh, Kerala, Manarashtra, Gujarat and\nTamil Nadu are the principal states in India\nwhere fisheries is an important source of\nlivelihood in the rural areas.\n- The progressive\nallocation by the Government of India and\ninnovations in fisheries and aquaculture\nhas led to the development of this sector.\nFisheries: A Source of Livelihood Outside Agriculture\n- At present, total fish production accounts for 0.9 per cent of the total GDP.\n• Interesting Facts: Women are not directly involved in fish production though their involvement in\nfish marketing is quite substantial.\n- About 60 per cent of the workforce in export marketing and 40 per cent in internal marketing\n• Problems and Solutions: But fishing community continues to be as one\nof the most backward communities in the country.\n- Some of the major problems faced by the fishing community in India\nSources of Fishing\nused by Fishing\nCommunity in India\ntoday are rampant unemployment, low per capita earnings, absence\nof mobility of labour to other sectors and a high rate of illiteracy.\n- The sector also faces the issues from over-fishing and pollution of\nwater-bodies.\nMarine\nSources\n• It includes\nseas and\n- Widespread indebtedness of the fishing communities makes fishing a\nvulnerable occupation. It is suggested that:\n› Credit facilities be provided to the fishing communities that breaks\nstreams.\n• It contributes\nit contributes\nto 35%\nto 65% of the\nof the total\ntotal value of\nvalue of fish\nfish prodchon.\nproduction.\ntheir vicious circle of indebtedness.\n> Micro-financing through SHGs (Self-help Groups) may go a long\nway in redressing credit needs of the marginal fishing families in\nrural areas.\n> Fishery technology be upgraded and made available to the fishing\nWater-bodies\nTogether these sources\n(inland and marine sources) are\nregarded as 'mother/provider'\n(of subsistence) by the fishing\ncommunities.\ncommunity.\nHIGHLIGHTS\nTANWA\n• Tamil Nadu Women in Agriculture (TANWA) was a project launched in the late 1980s\n• It focuses on women empowerment by equipping them with improved skills of farming.\n• Working with improved skills, small and marginal women-farmers are able to raise their\nincome through higher productivity.\n• Higher incomes have led to higher savings. For the productive use of their savings, the\nwomen-farmers have formed their cooperative group, called \"Farm Women's Group'.\n• These groups function like SHGs or Mini Banks to promote micro-credit among women-farmers.\n• Micro Credit System serves as a critical support system for small-scale productive activities like\nmushroom cultiv\nap manufacture, doll-making or other income-generating activities.","file":"chap5.pdf"},{"id":"ied-5-04","image":"ied-pages/ied-5-04.jpg","text":"(c) Horticulture\n• About\nHorticulture:\nHorticulture\nanother alternative source of employment in\nthe rural areas. It is more like diversification\nof crop production rather than a source of\nemployment different from crop farming.\n• Horticulture in India: Horticultural crops\ninclude fruits, vegetables and flowers besides\nseveral others.\n- Over time, there has been a substantial\nHorticulture: Alternative Source of Employment\nincrease in area under horticulture.\n- Horticulture contributes one-third of value of agricultural output and 6 per cent of GDP of\nIndia.\n- Presently, India is second largest producer of fruits and vegetables in the world. We are emerging\nas a leading producer of mangoes, bananas, coconuts, cashew nuts and a variety of spices.\n- High crop productivity led to Golden Revolution in horticultural farming, during the years\n1991-2012.\nHIGHLIGHTS\nGolden Revolution vs. Green Revolution\n• Golden Revolution refers to a series of research, development and technology transfer initiatives that\nincreased production of horticultural crops (vegetables and fruits) and honey.\n• Green Revolution, on the other hand, refers to a revolutionary rise in crop production owing to\nHYV technology and chemical inputs of farming (fertilizers, pesticides and insecticides).\n• Importance: Doubtless, horticulture is emerging as an important means of sustainable living in\nthe rural areas. There has been a significant rise in income levels of the farming families engaged\nin horticultural production.\n- Shifting to horticultural farming has reduced economic vulnerability of the small and marginal\nfarmers. This has opened up new avenues of employment for the woren folk in the rural\n- Significant it is to note that flower harvesting, nursery maintenance, hybrid seeds production\nand tissue culture are quite lucrative employment options for women in rural areas.\n• Triggering Issues: However, an alarming fact is that the bulk of acreage under horticulture has\nente can pass. se sheas of supplies of pulses an\na cut in their consumption owing to high prices which are an important source of protein for the\nbulk of households in India relying on vegetarian diet.\n• Way Ahead: Enhancing the role of horticulture requires investment in infrastructure like\nelectricity, cold storage systems, marketing linkages, small-scale processing un?\nimprovement and dissemination.\ntechnology\n20. Horticulture - the study or practice of growing flowers, fruits and vegetables/\n228\nRural Development\n! HIGHLIGHTS\nInformation Technology (IT) as an option or Livelnood in Rural Areas\n• We know, IT revolution is promoting Indian economy\nas a 'knowledge economy 21.\n. It highlights the\nsignificance of human capital as\nan important\ndeterminant of growth, beyond the significance of\nphysical and financial capital?\n• Consensus is emerging among the planners and\npoliticians that in the 21st century, knowledge\neconomy of India will spread its wings across rural\nareas of the country.\n• IT is expected to play an important role in achieving\noption of livelihood in rural areas and food security\nin the twenty tirst century.\n• Information Technology can be used to predict prices, weather and soil conditions for different crops.\n• It can also be used (by the government) to predict areas of food insecurity and vulnerability, so that\ntimely actions can be taken to tackle emergencies.\n• In fact, action-programme has already been initiated: an academy, called Jamshedji Tata National\nVirtual Academy for Rural Prosperity has been established. This academy is to impart operational\ntraining to nearly 10 lakh rural people to run 'info-kiosks'.\n• These kiosks are to be equipped with computers, scanners, printers and photocopiers. These are to\nprovide services like e-mailing, video-conferencing and transfer of documents to different places.\nSelling these services, the operators/owners of the kiosks are likely to make a reasonable living.\n• Thus, every village will ultimately be a knowledge center where IT provides a sustainable option of\nemployment and livelihood.\nAdoption of Village by Parliamentarians\n• In October, 2014, the Government of India launched a new scheme called Saansad Adarsh Gram\nYojana (SAGY). Under this scheme, each MP (member of Parliament) was required to adopt at least one\nvillage (in his constituency) and develop it as a model village.\n\" This scheme was to cover more than 2,500 villages across all parts of the country.\n• The selected village must have a population of 3,000-5,000 in the plains and 1,000-3,000 in the\nhilly areas. Also, It was required that the selected village was not the native place (own village) or the MP.\n• The village development programme was to focus on building the rural intrastructure in the dreas o\nhealth, education and nutrition. Participation of the villagers was to be encouraged in the development\nprogramme.\nAVLR 5.3\nNEED FOR DIVERSIFICATION IN INDIAN AGRICULTURE\n21. Knowledge economy - an economy in which growth depends upon the quality, quantity and accessibility of the\ninformation available, rather than the means of production.\n22. Financial capital-\nney, credit, and other forms of funding that build wealth for people and businesses.\n229","file":"chap5.pdf"},{"id":"ied-5-03","image":"ied-pages/ied-5-03.jpg","text":"Indian coonomic Development\nHIGHLIGHTS\nAgricultural Diversification\nused in two different ways as under:\nAgricultural Diversification\nDiversification of\nCrop Production\nDiversification of Production\nActivity/Employment\n• Diversification of crop production implies production of a diverse variety of crops rather than one\nspecialised crop. It implies a shift from single-cropping system to multi-cropping system?3.\n- In India, where agriculture is still dominated by subsistence farming, diversification would mean a\nshift from subsistence farming to commercial farming, choosing cropping pattern in accordance with\nthe price structure in the market.\n• Diversification of production (productive) activity refers to a shift of workforce from agriculture to allied\nactivities (such as livestock, poultry, fisheries, etc.) and non-agriculture sectors.\n- The need for diversification is crucial to lower the risk of dependence (for livelihood) exclusively on\n- The diversification is essential, also because agriculture is highly weather-dependent, and therefore\na risk-prone activity for livelihood. Alternative livelihood opportunities need to be explored which are\nmore productive and less risk-prone (or more sustainable).\n- Yet another factor favouring diversification is the fact that agriculture is a seasonal occupation,\nCominated by Rabi and \"Kharif seasons of farming. Farmers (particularly the small and marginal\nfarmers) need to find alternative sources of employment to supplement their low farm-incomes.\n- Further, agriculture in India is over-crowded and sustaining\nlot of disguised unemployment.\nit is essential that non-farm areas of employment are explored, for the sustainable livelihood of the\never-rising surplus population in agriculture.\n- Some critical non-tarm employment oppontunities are: agro-proter ang, food processing, handicrafts\n(pottery & weaving), small workshops, besides the government schemes like MGNREGA.\n• Merits of Crop Diversification\n- It lowers the market risk due to price fluctuations.\n- It lowers the risk of crop failure due to climatic changes.\n- It brings a shift in outlook of the farmers: subsistence outlook is replaced by commercial outlook.\n[Note: in a situation were farmer is relying only on subsistencebility for (where production is only fo\ncommercial farming. This exposes the farmer to uncertainties of the market, because now: () he has to\nsell his commercial produce in the market, and (i) buy from the market what he needs for self-subsistence.\nFood for the family no longer remains a secured option.]\n3. Multi-cropping system - the practice of growing two or more crops on the same plot c\nthan simply one, throughout the same growing season.\name time, rather\n• Merits of Diversitication of Production Actvity\nRural Development\n- It raises income as well as stabilises it.\n- It reduces burden on crop farming.\n- It reduces possibities or disguised unemployment in agriculture.\n- It leads to balanced rural growth/development.\nSee to me man tom tr dr Argy, Somental ssues related\nto urbanisation are addressed.\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\n0. 14. What is the other name for animal husbandry?\nAns. Livestock farming.\nQ. 15. What is the major drawback of the development of horticulture in India?\nAns. The bulk of acreage under horticulture has expanded at the cost of acreage under pulses.\nThis causes shortfall of pulses for consumption.\nQ. 16. What is White revolution associated with?\nAns. White revolution is associated with an increase in milk production. It involves member farmers\npooling their produce of milk for collective sale in the market.\nQ. 17. State the meaning of the term 'knowledge economy'.\nAns. It refers to an economy in which growth depends upon the quality, quantity and accessibility of\nthe information available, rather than the means of production.\nQ.18. What is the major outcome of diversification of crop production?\nAns. It implies a shift from single-cropping system to multi-cropping system.\nQ.19. What are the benefits of development of allied sector in agriculture?\nAns. It strives (aims) to provide supplementary gainful employment along with ensuring higher levels\nof income for rural people.\n5.3.2 Organic Farming\nOrganic farming is another emerging challenge\nin the context of rural development of the\nIndian economy. In fact, organic farming is\nemerging to be a global challenge in view of the\nfact that the excessive use of chemical fertilizers\nhas become a serious issue of environmental\ndegradation24\nEnvironment-friendly Organic Farming leading to\nSustainable Development\nConcept of Organic Farming\n• Organic farming is that system of farming which relies upon the use of organic inputs, so that wo\nare able to restore, maintain and enhance 'ecological balance\".\n- Organic farming is deeply linked with sustainable development25\n. It minimises environmental\ndegradation. In fact, organic farming and sustainable development go hand-in-hand.\n24. Environmental degradation - deterioration of the environment through depletion of resources such as air, water and soil.\n25. Sustainable development - a process of development which uses existing resources so optimally that their availability for\nuture generations is not reduced and the process of development is sustained over a long period of time\n231","file":"chap5.pdf"},{"id":"ied-5-02","image":"ied-pages/ied-5-02.jpg","text":"Rural Development\nIndian Economic Development\n• Organic Inputs: Animal manures and composts are the basic organic inputs.\norgan tarming scares the use of Chelat fe cem eters, insecticides and\npesticides which have been the mainstay of conventional farming.\n- It is important to hote that chemical inputent) ter the tood supply, penetrate the water sources.\nharm the livestock, deplete the soil and devastate natural ecosystems.\n• However, it neeas to ve notea that simply aiscarding lesser of chemical fertilizers is not organic\nfarminge assass arming at tocuses on maintaining soil health (rather than plan\nhealth) so that farming becomes a long period sustaiprice is ocess along with an eco-friend\n• Owing to an expanding sit in preterence of the peoprice is inorganic to organic food, farmers\nare induced to shift to organic farming though (in developing countries like India) challenges of\nhigh cost and consumer-trust continue to linger.\n• To encourage the sale of organic food, the government gives green-status'\nretail-chains and super-markets for promoting green choices.\nHIGHLIGHTS\nOrganically Grown Cotton of Maharashtra\n• It was Kisan Mehta of Prakruti (an NGO) who in 1995 suggested that the biggest user of chemical\npesticides - COTTON'\ncould be grown\norganically. Today around 130 farmers have pledged\n1,200 hectares of their land to grow cotton organically (on the International Federation of Organic\nAgriculture Movement's Standards).\n• Organic agriculture is more profitable in terms of money and soil conservation in the long run & this acts\nas an added advantage for the small and marginal farmers of the nation.\nAdvantages of Organic Farming\nFollowing are some notable advantages of organic farming, compared with conventional farming:\n(a) Discards the Use of Non-renewable Resources: Unlike conventional farming, organic farming\ndoes not use synthetic chemicals which are petroleum-based. We all know that petroleum is\na non-renewable resource.\n(ke ironment-friendly: Organic farming is environment-friendly. Chemical fertilizers, on the\nother hand, pollute the groundwater by raising its nitrate content.\n* Nitrates are a nealtn-nazard and polute the environment. Organic farming discards the\nuse of chemical fertilizers.\n(c) Sustains Soil Fertility: Use\nof animal\nmanures and composts helps sustain\nsoil fertility.\n› Chemical fertilizers, on the other hand,\nerode soil fertility. Accordingly, organic\nfarming is conducive to sustainable\ndevelopment of agriculture, while\nconventional farming is not.\nHIGHLIGHTS\nFarming and Soil Fertility\n• Conventional farming (using chemical fertilizers) erodes fertilty of Soll, whereas organic farming (using\nanimal manures and composts) sustains fertility of soil.\n• Organic farming nurtures soil as a resource for tuture generations. Accordingly, It is not only\nenvironment-friendly but also contributes to sustainable development.\n(d) Healthier and Taster Food: Organic farming offers healthier and taster food compared with\nconventional farming.\n, Recent studies have found that organically grown food is more nutritious than the food\ngrown by using the chemical inputs.\n> Existing trends indicate that demand for organic food is trending up, even when high price\nis a constraining factor.\n! HIGHLIGHTS\nIndia has a Comparative Advantage\n• India has comparative advantage in organic farming. Because, organic farming involves a labour-intensive\nprocess.\n• Abundance of labour places India in a better position to specialise in organic farming.\n(e) Inexpensive Technology for the Small and Marginal Farmers: Organic farming offers an\ninexpensive farming-technology to small and marginal farmers who constitute the bulk of\nfarming population in India.\n> Conventional technology is to be adopted as a package including HYV seeds, fertilizers,\npesticides, insecticides, besides good irrigation facilities.\n> Small and marginal farmers in India, in their desperate bid to use this package, often slip\ninto indebtedness. No such package of expensive inputs is required for organic farming.\nLimitations of Organic Farming\n• It cannot be denied that organic farming (as compared to conventonal farming) offers lesser\nyield and organic foods (as compared to conventional foods) have shorter shelf-life. Implying that\norganic farming is not cost efficient.\n• However, considering the fact that organic foods are in high demand, higher product price is\nexpected to compensate for lower yield.\n• Overall advantages of organic farming (as it is deeply linked with sustainable development) should\ncertainly make it a more acceptable proposition than the conventonal farming.\nAVLR 5.4\nORGANIC FARMING\n233\nAnimal M=\n232\nustain","file":"chap5.pdf"},{"id":"ied-5-01","image":"ied-pages/ied-5-01.jpg","text":"Indian Economic Development\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\nQ. 20. Fertilizers restore crop health, not the soil health. Is it ture?\nAns. Yes, it is true, rertilizers are meant to raise crop producti oty, in ract, continuous use of fertili\nerodes fertility of the soil.\nQ. 21. Enlist two demerits of organic farming.\nAns. (i) Lower crop-yield, and (ii) Shorter shelf-life of the product.\n9.22. The Prime bisters program tor nestoranon, Awareness, Nurturing and Improvement e\nPert cats de launche test promote balancer use\nof alternative and chemical fertilizers, said Mansukh Mandaviya, Minister for Chemicals and\nFertilizers, on Friday. Addressing a virtual meeting of agriculture ministers of states and union\nterritories, Mandaviya appealed to them to jointly contribute towards the promotion of organic\nfarming for preservation of soil health.\"\nSource: https://indianexpress.com/article/india/promote-organic-farming-to-boost-soil-health-\nsays-mandaviya-8695072/\nWhat are the principal reasons for the promotion of organic farming by the government?\n(i) It is environmental-friendly.\n(i It offers inexpensive farming-technology to the small and marginal farmers.\n(ili) Organic food is more nutritious compared to food from chemical inputs.\nPower Points [Summary of the Chapter]\nRural Development: It means an action-plan for addressing the economic, social and environmental\nissues of the rural economy.\nChallenges of Rural Development:\n• Lingering Challenges of Rural Development:\n(i) Challenge of rural credit\n(i) Challenge of rural marketing\n• Emerging Challenges of Rural Development:\n(i) Diversification of productive activities\n(ii) Organic farming\nSources of Rural Credit:\n• Non-institutional Credit Sources: Landlords, village traders and moneylenders.\n• Institutional Credit Sources: Cooperative Credit Socisu• Bank of India and Other Commercia\nBanks, Regional Rural Banks and Land Development Banks, NABARD.\n• Agricultural Marketing: It includes:\n(i) gathering the produce after harvesting\n(ii) processing the produce\n(ii) grading the produce according to its quality\n(iv) packaging the produce according to buyers' preferences\n(v) storing the produce for future sale and selling the produce when price is lucrative\n234\nRural Development\n(i) Regulated markets\n(i) Provision of physical infrastructure\n(i) Cooperative agricultural marketing societies\n(iv) Policy instruments like Minimum Support Price (MSP), maintenance of buffer stock of wheat\nand rice by Food Corporation of India, and distribution of food grains through Public Distribution\nAlternative Marketing Channels:\n(i) Direct sale by the farmers to the consumers\n(i) Contract sale to the MINCs and corporate retails\nDiversification of Productive Activities: It implies a shift from crop farming to non-farming areas of\nemployment. Non-farm areas of employment include:\n(i) Animal husbandry\n(ii) Fisheries\n(iii) Horticulture\n(iv) Information technology\nOrganic Farming and Sustainable Development: Organic farming is basically a system of farming that\nreles upon the use of organic inputs for cultivation. It discards the use of chemical inputs.\n• Merits of Organic Farming:\n(i) Discards the use of non-renewable resources\n(il) Environment-friendly\n(iii) Sustains soil fertility\n(iv) Healthier and tastier food\n(v) Inexpensive technology for the small and marginal farmers\n• India has Comparative Advantage in Organic Farming: Because it involves labour-intensive process of\nproduction, and India has abundance of labour.\nLow Yield and High Cost per Unit of Output\" is the principal constraint in switching over from\nconventional farming to organic farming.\n235","file":"chap5.pdf"}],"boxes":[{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"ied-5-12","image":"ied-pages/ied-5-12.jpg","text":"9.1. \"The COVID-19 pandemic and consequent lockdowns have adversely impacted the livelihoods\nof street vendors. Accordingly, the Ministry of Housing & Urban Affairs launched a scheme\nPM Street Vendor's AatmaNirbhar Nidhi (PM SVANidhi) to empower Street Vendors by not only\nextending loans to them, but also for their holistic development and economic upliftment.\"\nSource: india.gov.in\nThe above scheme was initiated to satisfy the credit requirements of vendors. Defend or\nrefute the given statement.\nAns. The above statement is defended as PM SVANidhi strives to provide credit to help in economic\nupliftment of pandemic affected vendors.\n9.2. As a minister for rural development, what will be your action-plan for the development of\nAns. As a minister for rural development, my action-plan would focus on three basic objectives:\n(i) Crop productivity must be increased to increase income from farming. Agriculture should no\nlonger remain merely as a means of subsistence. It must be transformed into a commercial\nactivity that generates profit.\n(ii) Avenues of employment outside agriculture must be explored and developed. This is\nto ensure that rural development no longer remains farm-centric. It becomes more\ncomprehensive and meaningful.\n(i) Rural development must be integrated with human development. This can be achieved only\nwhen a significant focus is placed on education and health in the rural areas.\nBriefly, action-plan for rural development must be holistic/comprehensive. It must focus on all\naspects of rural development. It should not be restricted merely to the development of agriculture.\n3. Concurrent growth - relates to simultaneous development.\n4. Structural imbalance - when different sectors of the economy do not grow in tandem (or side by side) with each other.\n5. Casualisation (in the context of employment) - refers to a situation when the percentage of casually-hired workers (like\ndaily wagers, not\nanent rolls of the employers) in the total workforce tends to rise over time.\n213"},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-5-11","image":"ied-pages/ied-5-11.jpg","text":"Evolution of Rural Credit\n• At the time of independence, institutional credit showed only a notional existence.\n• Non-institutional credit ruled the roost. It was dominated by moneylenders, traders and commission\nilliterate farmers.\nagents who charged exorbitantly high interest rates, and manipulated the accounts to exploit the\n• In fact, non-institutional credit often led to a debt trap for the farmers.\n• A significant change occurred in 1969 with nationalisation of commercial banks.\nwas ten that the concept suble banking was patinto practice and credit needs of the farmers wer\naddressed as a priority issue in the context of rural development.\npayment in the form of interest.\n7. Debt trap - a situation where an individual or a company borrows money to repay old debte\nnever ending cycle of debt.\nand in turn lands in a\n214\nRural Development\nGreen Revolution: The Harbinger of Changes in Rural Credit System\n• Green Revolution led to the change in outlook of the farmers from supsistence tarring to commercia\n• Consequently, with the increasing demand or rural credit, there was a major change in the credit system\nasiled to the diversication te port or tra creat, tocusing on production loans.\nSources of Rural Credit\nsources of rural or agricuitural credit are broadly classified as under:\nSources of Rural Credit\nInstitutional Sources\n(modern/emerging sources)\nNon-institutional Sources\n(conventional/traditional sources)\n(a) Non-institutional Sources\n• Sources: (i) Landlords, (ii) Village traders, and (ili) Moneylenders\n•Evolution: Traditionally, most credit needs of the farmers were met through these sources.\n- Non-institutional sources accounted for 93 per cent of the total borrowing of the farmers in the\nbeginning of First Five Year Plan.\n- But by 1981, the institutional sources emerged as the principal channel of rural credit.\n(b) Institutional Sources\n• Sources: (i) Government, (i) Cooperatives, (i) Commercial banks, and (iv) Regional Rural Banks.\n• Evolution: Together, they accounted for only 7 per cent of the credit needs of the farmers in the\nbeginning of First Five Year Plan, but presently their share has increased to over 66 per cent.\n- Out of total institutional credit, cooperative credit contributes nearly 15 per cent and commercial\nbanks about 75 per cent.\n- Apart from substantial expansion in the flow of credit by commercial banks and Regional Rural\nBanks (RRBs), the cooperative credit institutions have emerged as important institutional\nagencies offering credit support and related services.\nFollowing is a brief description of some important institutional agencies offering rural credit in\nImportant Institutional Agencies offering Rural Credit in India\nCooperative\nCredit Societies\nState Bank\nand other\nCommercial Banks\nRegional Rurar\nBanks (RRBs) and\nLand Development\nNational Bank for\nAgricultural and\nRural Development\n(NABARD)\n215"},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-5-10","image":"ied-pages/ied-5-10.jpg","text":"Multi-agency Institutions\n• Institutional structure of rural banking consists of:\n(i) Commercial banks,\n(i) Cooperative Credit Societies, and\n(il) Regional Rural Banks (RRBs),\n(iv) Land Development Banks.\n• This institutional structure of rural banking is called multi-agency institutions. ney are expected to offer\nadequate credit at cheaper rates.\nKudumbashree —The Poor Women's Bank\n• Kudumbashree was started in 1995 in Kerala as a small savings bank tor poor women to encourage\nsavings.\nThese societies have been acclaimed as the largest informall banks in Asia in terms of participation and\nsavings mobilised. The thrift and credit society mobilised? 1 crore as thrift savings.\nThe four main functions of NABARD are as these:\n(i) To serve as an apex funding agency for the institutions providing credit in rural areas.\n(ii) To take appropriate measures to improve the credit delivery system. The bank was to\nfocus on restructuring of credit institutions and training of personnel.\n(i) To coordinate the rural financing activities of all credit institutions and maintain liaison\n(connection) with Government of India, State Government, Reserve Bank, and other\nnational level institutions concerned with policy formulation.\n(iv) To undertake monitoring and evaluation of projects refinanced by it."},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-5-10","image":"ied-pages/ied-5-10.jpg","text":"RBI (Reserve Bank of India) and Rural Credit\n• If the rural credit system in India has acquired its present size and significance, it is essentially due to\nthe key role played by RBI.\n• Since its beginning in 1935, the Reserve Bank of India has been playing an important role in providing\ncredit to agriculturists and strengthening credit institution, primary cooperative credit societles ana\nLand Development Banks in particular.\n• It focuses on:\n() short-term refinance facilities for agriculture and allied activities.\n(i) development of cooperative credit institutions,\n(ill) expansion of the sources of tunds for short-term and long-term wings of the cooperative creait systen,\n(iv) provision of long-term funds to bring about state partnership in cooperative credit agencies,\n(v) training and professionalisation of cooperatives,\n(vi) conducting rural credit surveys to determine the coverage of rural housenolas by creditstions\n(vii) helping bank-branch expansion programme to provide cheaper remittance facilities for banks\noperating in rural areas, and\n(vii) guidance to all concerned on matters relating to rural credit.\n• Thus, the role of RBI in the field of agricultural credit has been multi-dimensional, though largely\nregulatory in nature.\n9. Allied activities - activities that depend upon agricultural activity in various ways and often atfect the outcome o\nThana include antivities like animal husbandry and fishery.\n217"},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-5-09","image":"ied-pages/ied-5-09.jpg","text":"Kisan Credit Card (KCC) Scheme\n• The Kisan Credit Card Scheme aims at adequate and timely support to the farmers for their short-term\ncredit needs.\n• Under the scheme, banks may issue Kisan Credit Cards to the farmers who are otherwise eligible for\nshort-term credit for crop production, allied activities and other non-farm activities.\n• The farmers under the scheme are issued a credit card cum passbook incorporating the name, address,\nparticulars of land holding, borrowing limits, validity period, etc.\n8 AVLR 5.1\nMICRO CREDIT PROGRAMMES AND SELF-HELP GROUPS\nRural Banking - A Critical Evaluation\nAdvantages\nL Da ts Say expanded over ame. te expansion has been signincan\nand came banks in 1ant. Natonalisation of commercia\nbanks marked the beginning of social banking\"\nand personalised credit needs of farmers.\n10. Self-employed - working for oneself as a freelance or the owner of a business rather than for an employer.\n11. Collateral - refers to the asset to be pledged as security for the grant of loans.\n12. Social banking - banking with the purpose of creating a positive impact on the society.\n218\n• Establishment of NABARD (as an apex body coordinating activities of all financial institutions\nRural Development\nengaged in rural creal pros ahesh me deas these: dillerence in the institution of rural\ncredit. Rural nancing has become much more organised than ever before\nInstitutional credit has gone a long way in iberauing the farmers from the debt trap of mahajans\nand moneylenders.\na Institutional credit has promotea commercialisation of agriculture.\nDeficiencies\nDeficiencies or rural banking system are equally glaring. Notable observations in this regard are as these:\nbanking credit or institutional credit has invariably been tea to collateral because of which a large\nsection of small and marginal holders are often left out.\n• Apart from commercial banks, most financial institutions have failed to develop a culture of thrift\n(saving for the future) among the farming families. Mobilisation of deposits has remained rather\nsubdued.\nSocial banking in rural areas has taken a hit in the wake of economic reforms of 1991. Impact of debt\non the farmers is generally becoming so huge that suicidal deaths are rising from scores to centuries,\nparticularly in the states of Andhra Pradesh and Maharashtra.\nThus, the expansion and promotion of the rural banking sector has taken a backseat after reforms."},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-5-09","image":"ied-pages/ied-5-09.jpg","text":"An Alarming Fact\n• Default rate among the borrowers is assuming alarming proportions.\n\" While many may be genuine defaulters, investigations suggest that nearly 50 per cent are wiltul\n• This certainly is an alarming development and may shake the very foundation of rural banking system\nWay Ahead\n• To improve the situation, in recent years, all the adults are encouraged to open bank accounts a\na part of a scheme known as Jan-Dhan Yojana\".\n• The account-holders are entitled to * 1-2 lakh accidental insurance coverage and overdraft\nfacility for & 10.000. Also, they can receive their wages (related to any government job unde\nschemes like MGNREGA), old-age pension and other social security payments through their bank\naccounts. (For description of MGNREGA, see next chapter.)\n13. Default rate - hiaher interest rate imposed on a borrower who has missed regular payments on a loan.\n14. Jan-Dhan Yojana (also known as Pradhan Mantri Jan-Dhan Yojana) - is a financial inclusion scheme launched by the\n15. Overdraft - occurs when one withdraws or spends more money than what is available in his/her bank account,\ninsurance, and financial services.\nGovernment of India in 2014 to ensure universal access to basic banking services, including zero-balance accounts,\nwith fees or interest.\nresulting in a negative balance. The bank temporarily covers the extra amount and he/she is required to repay it - often\n219"},{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"ied-5-08","image":"ied-pages/ied-5-08.jpg","text":"Q. 3. Why is credit considered as the lifeline of farming activity in rural areas?\n(i) Farmers lack surplus for investment.\n(ii) Gestation period between sowing and harvesting is long.\nQ. 4. Name two non-institutional sources of rural credit.\nAns. (i) Landlords, and (ii) Moneylenders.\nQ. 5. Why are cooperative credit societies welcomed?\nAns. Because they facilitate timely and rapid flow of credit to farmers at reasonable rate of interest.\nQ. 6. Which agency serves as the apex funding agency for the institutions providing credit in rural\nAns. National Bank for Agriculture and Rural Development (NABARD).\nQ. 7. Enlist a micro credit programme that has promoted thrift among rural households.\nAns. Self-help Groups (SHGs).\nQ. 8. What is the main aim of the Kisan Credit Card Scheme?\nAns. It aims to provide adequate and timely support to the farmers for their short-term credit\nneeds.\nQ. 9. Why does a farmer require credit? Write your observations.\nAns. Credit need of the farmer may be split into three parts:\n(i) Short-term Credit\n: Needed for the purchase of inputs.\n(i) Medium-term Credit: Needed for the purchase of equipments/appliances.\n(ili) Long-term Credit\n: Needed for the purchase of land or improvement of the existing\n5.2.2 Agricultural Marketing [Marketing in Rural Areas]\nAfter harvestine, the fathers need to assemble their produce on the farm, process It, grade it\npassed nan pat market for sale. Agricurtural marketing\nincludes all these processes between harvesting and final sale of the produce by the farmers.\n• Meaning: Agricultural marketing may be defined as the process that involves:\n(i) gathering the produce after harvesting,\n(ii) processing the produce (like separating the straw from the grains),\ngrading the produce according to its quality,\n(iv) packaging the produce according to buyers' preferences,\n220\n(v) storing the produce for future sale, and\n(vi) selling and distributing the produce when the price is lucrative.\none full simply terer ters to bring terme for sale\nrather it includes all those activities/ processes which help ist getting best price for his produce"},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-5-08","image":"ied-pages/ied-5-08.jpg","text":"Agricultural Marketing\n• Need for State intervention. At the time the full ence, agricultural marketing was almost\nunder the compress contrarer the money gis pre, daders and commission agents\nTheir holding capacity was almost zero.\n- Having taken loans from the moneylenders (who happened to be traders and commission\nagents as well), the farmers were obliged to sell their crop to them only.\n- Often, it implied a low price, besides a substantial loss due to faulty weighing, manipulation of\naccounts, lack of storage facilities and customary commissions.\n- Farmers also lacked the required information about prices prevailing in the market which\nfurther forced them to sell their produce at a low price.\nAll these factors make it important that state intervenes in the agricultural markets to regulate the\nactivities of private traders."},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-5-08","image":"ied-pages/ied-5-08.jpg","text":"Why Distress Sale?\n• The compulsion to sell arises because:\n() the farmers need immediate cash to pay off their debts, and\n(it hey lack storage facilities or the cost of storage is very high.\nMeasures initiated by the Government to improve Marketing System\nin order to improve the system of agricultural marketing, the government has initiated a series of\nmeasures, as given below:\n(a) Regulated Markets: Regulated markets have been established where sale and purchase of the\nproduce is monitored by the Market Committee comprised of representatives of government\nfarmers and the traders.\n* Market system is made transparent with a strict vigil on the use of proper scales and weights\nThis has gone a long way in protecting the interest of the farmers as well as the consumers\n• The market committees ensure that the farmers get appropriate price for their produce.\n* There is a need to develop about 27,000 rural periodic market places to realise the full\npotential of rural markets.\n10. Distress sale - a situation when the farmers are compelled to sell their produce immediately after the harvest, no\nmatter how low the market price is."},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-5-07","image":"ied-pages/ied-5-07.jpg","text":"Regulated Market Yards\n•The farmers can bone to these fatas tor ail farmers i their produce. these yards also offer storag\n(b) Provision of Physical infrastructure: Physical infrastructure includes transport ate\nstorage facilities, and processing units.\n• Better transport tacilities like roadways, ralways enary e the farmers to sell their produc\nat locations offering lucrative price.\n* Storage helps the farmers to sell their produce at a time when the market price is lucrative.\nThere is a need to increase storage facilities (warehouses, godowns and cold storage units)\nas even today, more than 10 per cent of the goods produced in farms are wasted due to\n> The current physical infrastructure facilities are quite inadequate, and need to be\nsignificantly improved to meet the growing demand\n(c) Cooperative Agricultural Marketing Societies: The government is encouraging the formation\nof Cooperative Agricultural Marketing Societies.\n> As members of these societies, farmers find themselves better bargainers in the market,\ngetting better price of their produce through collective sale?."},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-5-07","image":"ied-pages/ied-5-07.jpg","text":"Cooperative Marketing\n• Cooperative marketing is a significant progressive step in the context of agricultural market system.\n• Milk cooperatives in Gujarat are a brilliant example of cooperative marketing in rural India.\nThese cooperatives have not only changed the social and economic fabric of the economy of Gujarat,\nbut also have played a key role in bringing about White Revolution in the country.\n• However, of late, some kinks have emerged in the system of cooperative marketing. These are:\n(i) poor coordination between the marketing cooperatives and processing cooperatives, and\n(i) snags (shortcomings) in financial management of the cooperative.\n(d) MSP (Minimum Support Price) and Related Policy Instruments: MSP is a policy instrument\nthat assures the tarmers that their produce, if remains unsold, would be purchased by the\ngovernment at the specified minimum price.\n> This is an important policy instrument in the context of the agricultural marketing system,\nas it addresses volatility of the market and assures minimum stable income to the farmers.\nAlso, MSP works as a critical minimum price in the market; often the market price happens\nto be higher than the MSP.\n> However, success of MSP depends upon the two related policy instruments which the\ngovernment is simultaneously pursuing, viz.,\n17. Collective sale - relates to pooled sale by owners of separate units within a development and is often assoriated with\nhigher prices due to increased bargaining power.\n222\n(i) Maintenance of Ms purchases as a butter stock, the task assigned to Food Corporation\n(i). Distribution of Mr purchases among te Bro (below poverty line) residents with\ndistribution system.\n[Here, Imay be noted traur asshotsurely conned totne distributon or Mor purchases\nit also supplies ner essent ter poetaton such as san ersene"},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-5-07","image":"ied-pages/ied-5-07.jpg","text":"Buffer Stocks and PDS\n• MSP purchases from the farmers are kept as buffer stocks by\nthe government. These Stocks (of grains) are used, primarily\nfor Public Distribution System (PDS), besides meeting urgent\nneeds during periods of low output and scarcity.\n• PDS implies distribution of food grains (and other essentials\nlike sugar and kerosene) through \"fair price shops' at\nsubsidised rates, so that the poorer sections of the society\nhave an easy access to these things.\nBuffer Stock to Support MSP\nWay Ahead\n• On account of these measures by the government, agricultural marketing system has made\nsome notable improvements. But, it has yet to go a long way before the millions of small and\nmarginal farmers are able to defend their self-interest at all stages of the marketing process.\n• The fact is that small and marginal farmers still continue to be the victims of distress sale to the\nrural moneylenders and commission agents.\n• Surprisingly enough, nearly 90 per cent of agricultural marketing is still handled by the private\ntraders, while the government agencies and cooperative societies are handling merely 10 per cent."},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-5-07","image":"ied-pages/ied-5-07.jpg","text":"Shortcomings of Agricultural Marketing\n• Lack of transportation facilities\n• Forced sales\n• Numerous market changes\n• Lack of credit facilities\n• Lack of storage facilities\n• Presence of middlemen\n• Lack of standard weights and measures\n• Inadequate market information.\nAlternative (Emerging) Marketing Channels-A Ray of Hope\n• Emerging alternatives of agricultural marketing are a ray of hope to the small and marginal farmers\n• Direct sale by the farmers to the consumers is one such channel. This channel increases their\nwho have been silent sufferers at tne nands or middlemen\nincome directly."},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-5-06","image":"ied-pages/ied-5-06.jpg","text":"Reduction of Price-Risk (Market-Risk) through Crop Diversification\n• Market risk of the farmers can be lowered if crop\ndiversification is adopted. It occurs when more than\none crop is grown on a given land.\n• Prices of all crops may not drop at the same time. If\none crop fetches low revenue (because of low price), the\nother may fetch high revenue (because of high price).\n• However, small and marginal farmers are reluctant\nto adopt crop diversification. Instead, they focus on a\nsingle crop which provides them food for subsistence.\nDiversification lowers Market Risk\n• Small and marginal farmers continue to grow subsistence crops), no matter what the market price of\nthese crops is. Because, this does not expose them to uncertainties of the market as regards their need\n• Thus, security of food for the family deters the subsistence farmers to opt for crop diversification.\nshops to the poorer sections of society.\ninvolves distribution of food grains at subsidised rates through fair price\nAns. Public Distribution System (PDS)."},{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"ied-5-06","image":"ied-pages/ied-5-06.jpg","text":"Q. 10. What are the main advantages of regulated markets to farmers?\nAns.\n(i) It ensures transparency and a check on the use of proper scales and weights.\n(ii) It ensures that farmers get appropriate price for their produce,\nQ. 11. How does grading of output raise revenue of the farmer?\nAns. Grading of output refers to division of output in different categories on\nThe farmer can charge higher price for the high-graded output. This incre\nfrom the sale of his output.\n'quality.\nI revenue\n224\n18. Animal husban\nAnimal Husbandry: Employment Outside Agriculture\nrience of breeding and caring for farm animals.\n225"},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-5-05","image":"ied-pages/ied-5-05.jpg","text":"Operation Flood\n• It is a system of Milk Cooperatives, started in 1970 also known as White Revolution'.\n• The system requires the member farmers to pool their produce of milk according to different grading\n(based on quality) for collective sale in the urban market.\n• Pooling increases quantum of sale, and thereby increases bargaining power of the farmers.\n• Pooling also makes grading and processing of the product as economically viable propositions.\nThis raises market value of the product and assured farmers of a fair price and income from supply of\nmilk to urban markets.\n• Milk cooperatives in India have their epicentre in the state of Gujarat. The other major milk producing\nstates are Madhya Pradesh, Uttar Pradesh, Andhra Pradesh, Maharashtra, Punjab and Rajasthan.\n• Over the last su years or so, inese cooperatives have recorded a wonderful progress, significantly\ncontributing to the production of milk. It has proved to be artlynportant non-tarm area of incom\ngeneration in the rural areas.\n19. Crop-livestock- a system of agricultural production which combines one or more cror\nne type of livestock.\n226\nRural Development\n(b) Fisheries\n* Fisheries in India: vest bengal, Anahra\nPradesh, Kerala, Manarashtra, Gujarat and\nTamil Nadu are the principal states in India\nwhere fisheries is an important source of\nlivelihood in the rural areas.\n- The progressive\nallocation by the Government of India and\ninnovations in fisheries and aquaculture\nhas led to the development of this sector.\nFisheries: A Source of Livelihood Outside Agriculture\n- At present, total fish production accounts for 0.9 per cent of the total GDP.\n• Interesting Facts: Women are not directly involved in fish production though their involvement in\nfish marketing is quite substantial.\n- About 60 per cent of the workforce in export marketing and 40 per cent in internal marketing\n• Problems and Solutions: But fishing community continues to be as one\nof the most backward communities in the country.\n- Some of the major problems faced by the fishing community in India\nSources of Fishing\nused by Fishing\nCommunity in India\ntoday are rampant unemployment, low per capita earnings, absence\nof mobility of labour to other sectors and a high rate of illiteracy.\n- The sector also faces the issues from over-fishing and pollution of\nwater-bodies.\nMarine\nSources\n• It includes\nseas and\n- Widespread indebtedness of the fishing communities makes fishing a\nvulnerable occupation. It is suggested that:\n› Credit facilities be provided to the fishing communities that breaks\nstreams.\n• It contributes\nit contributes\nto 35%\nto 65% of the\nof the total\ntotal value of\nvalue of fish\nfish prodchon.\nproduction.\ntheir vicious circle of indebtedness.\n> Micro-financing through SHGs (Self-help Groups) may go a long\nway in redressing credit needs of the marginal fishing families in\nrural areas.\n> Fishery technology be upgraded and made available to the fishing\nWater-bodies\nTogether these sources\n(inland and marine sources) are\nregarded as 'mother/provider'\n(of subsistence) by the fishing\ncommunities.\ncommunity."},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-5-05","image":"ied-pages/ied-5-05.jpg","text":"TANWA\n• Tamil Nadu Women in Agriculture (TANWA) was a project launched in the late 1980s\n• It focuses on women empowerment by equipping them with improved skills of farming.\n• Working with improved skills, small and marginal women-farmers are able to raise their\nincome through higher productivity.\n• Higher incomes have led to higher savings. For the productive use of their savings, the\nwomen-farmers have formed their cooperative group, called \"Farm Women's Group'.\n• These groups function like SHGs or Mini Banks to promote micro-credit among women-farmers.\n• Micro Credit System serves as a critical support system for small-scale productive activities like\nmushroom cultiv\nap manufacture, doll-making or other income-generating activities."},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-5-04","image":"ied-pages/ied-5-04.jpg","text":"Golden Revolution vs. Green Revolution\n• Golden Revolution refers to a series of research, development and technology transfer initiatives that\nincreased production of horticultural crops (vegetables and fruits) and honey.\n• Green Revolution, on the other hand, refers to a revolutionary rise in crop production owing to\nHYV technology and chemical inputs of farming (fertilizers, pesticides and insecticides).\n• Importance: Doubtless, horticulture is emerging as an important means of sustainable living in\nthe rural areas. There has been a significant rise in income levels of the farming families engaged\nin horticultural production.\n- Shifting to horticultural farming has reduced economic vulnerability of the small and marginal\nfarmers. This has opened up new avenues of employment for the woren folk in the rural\n- Significant it is to note that flower harvesting, nursery maintenance, hybrid seeds production\nand tissue culture are quite lucrative employment options for women in rural areas.\n• Triggering Issues: However, an alarming fact is that the bulk of acreage under horticulture has\nente can pass. se sheas of supplies of pulses an\na cut in their consumption owing to high prices which are an important source of protein for the\nbulk of households in India relying on vegetarian diet.\n• Way Ahead: Enhancing the role of horticulture requires investment in infrastructure like\nelectricity, cold storage systems, marketing linkages, small-scale processing un?\nimprovement and dissemination.\ntechnology\n20. Horticulture - the study or practice of growing flowers, fruits and vegetables/\n228\nRural Development"},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-5-04","image":"ied-pages/ied-5-04.jpg","text":"Information Technology (IT) as an option or Livelnood in Rural Areas\n• We know, IT revolution is promoting Indian economy\nas a 'knowledge economy 21."},{"title":". It highlights the","page":"ied-5-04","image":"ied-pages/ied-5-04.jpg","text":"significance of human capital as\nan important\ndeterminant of growth, beyond the significance of\nphysical and financial capital?\n• Consensus is emerging among the planners and\npoliticians that in the 21st century, knowledge\neconomy of India will spread its wings across rural\nareas of the country.\n• IT is expected to play an important role in achieving\noption of livelihood in rural areas and food security\nin the twenty tirst century.\n• Information Technology can be used to predict prices, weather and soil conditions for different crops.\n• It can also be used (by the government) to predict areas of food insecurity and vulnerability, so that\ntimely actions can be taken to tackle emergencies.\n• In fact, action-programme has already been initiated: an academy, called Jamshedji Tata National\nVirtual Academy for Rural Prosperity has been established. This academy is to impart operational\ntraining to nearly 10 lakh rural people to run 'info-kiosks'.\n• These kiosks are to be equipped with computers, scanners, printers and photocopiers. These are to\nprovide services like e-mailing, video-conferencing and transfer of documents to different places.\nSelling these services, the operators/owners of the kiosks are likely to make a reasonable living.\n• Thus, every village will ultimately be a knowledge center where IT provides a sustainable option of\nemployment and livelihood.\nAdoption of Village by Parliamentarians\n• In October, 2014, the Government of India launched a new scheme called Saansad Adarsh Gram\nYojana (SAGY). Under this scheme, each MP (member of Parliament) was required to adopt at least one\nvillage (in his constituency) and develop it as a model village.\n\" This scheme was to cover more than 2,500 villages across all parts of the country.\n• The selected village must have a population of 3,000-5,000 in the plains and 1,000-3,000 in the\nhilly areas. Also, It was required that the selected village was not the native place (own village) or the MP.\n• The village development programme was to focus on building the rural intrastructure in the dreas o\nhealth, education and nutrition. Participation of the villagers was to be encouraged in the development\nprogramme.\nAVLR 5.3\nNEED FOR DIVERSIFICATION IN INDIAN AGRICULTURE\n21. Knowledge economy - an economy in which growth depends upon the quality, quantity and accessibility of the\ninformation available, rather than the means of production.\n22. Financial capital-\nney, credit, and other forms of funding that build wealth for people and businesses.\n229"},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-5-03","image":"ied-pages/ied-5-03.jpg","text":"Agricultural Diversification\nused in two different ways as under:\nAgricultural Diversification\nDiversification of\nCrop Production\nDiversification of Production\nActivity/Employment\n• Diversification of crop production implies production of a diverse variety of crops rather than one\nspecialised crop. It implies a shift from single-cropping system to multi-cropping system?3.\n- In India, where agriculture is still dominated by subsistence farming, diversification would mean a\nshift from subsistence farming to commercial farming, choosing cropping pattern in accordance with\nthe price structure in the market.\n• Diversification of production (productive) activity refers to a shift of workforce from agriculture to allied\nactivities (such as livestock, poultry, fisheries, etc.) and non-agriculture sectors.\n- The need for diversification is crucial to lower the risk of dependence (for livelihood) exclusively on\n- The diversification is essential, also because agriculture is highly weather-dependent, and therefore\na risk-prone activity for livelihood. Alternative livelihood opportunities need to be explored which are\nmore productive and less risk-prone (or more sustainable).\n- Yet another factor favouring diversification is the fact that agriculture is a seasonal occupation,\nCominated by Rabi and \"Kharif seasons of farming. Farmers (particularly the small and marginal\nfarmers) need to find alternative sources of employment to supplement their low farm-incomes.\n- Further, agriculture in India is over-crowded and sustaining\nlot of disguised unemployment.\nit is essential that non-farm areas of employment are explored, for the sustainable livelihood of the\never-rising surplus population in agriculture.\n- Some critical non-tarm employment oppontunities are: agro-proter ang, food processing, handicrafts\n(pottery & weaving), small workshops, besides the government schemes like MGNREGA.\n• Merits of Crop Diversification\n- It lowers the market risk due to price fluctuations.\n- It lowers the risk of crop failure due to climatic changes.\n- It brings a shift in outlook of the farmers: subsistence outlook is replaced by commercial outlook.\n[Note: in a situation were farmer is relying only on subsistencebility for (where production is only fo\ncommercial farming. This exposes the farmer to uncertainties of the market, because now: () he has to\nsell his commercial produce in the market, and (i) buy from the market what he needs for self-subsistence.\nFood for the family no longer remains a secured option.]\n3. Multi-cropping system - the practice of growing two or more crops on the same plot c\nthan simply one, throughout the same growing season.\name time, rather\n• Merits of Diversitication of Production Actvity\nRural Development\n- It raises income as well as stabilises it.\n- It reduces burden on crop farming.\n- It reduces possibities or disguised unemployment in agriculture.\n- It leads to balanced rural growth/development.\nSee to me man tom tr dr Argy, Somental ssues related\nto urbanisation are addressed."},{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"ied-5-03","image":"ied-pages/ied-5-03.jpg","text":"0. 14. What is the other name for animal husbandry?\nAns. Livestock farming.\nQ. 15. What is the major drawback of the development of horticulture in India?\nAns. The bulk of acreage under horticulture has expanded at the cost of acreage under pulses.\nThis causes shortfall of pulses for consumption.\nQ. 16. What is White revolution associated with?\nAns. White revolution is associated with an increase in milk production. It involves member farmers\npooling their produce of milk for collective sale in the market.\nQ. 17. State the meaning of the term 'knowledge economy'.\nAns. It refers to an economy in which growth depends upon the quality, quantity and accessibility of\nthe information available, rather than the means of production.\nQ.18. What is the major outcome of diversification of crop production?\nAns. It implies a shift from single-cropping system to multi-cropping system.\nQ.19. What are the benefits of development of allied sector in agriculture?\nAns. It strives (aims) to provide supplementary gainful employment along with ensuring higher levels\nof income for rural people.\n5.3.2 Organic Farming\nOrganic farming is another emerging challenge\nin the context of rural development of the\nIndian economy. In fact, organic farming is\nemerging to be a global challenge in view of the\nfact that the excessive use of chemical fertilizers\nhas become a serious issue of environmental\ndegradation24\nEnvironment-friendly Organic Farming leading to\nSustainable Development\nConcept of Organic Farming\n• Organic farming is that system of farming which relies upon the use of organic inputs, so that wo\nare able to restore, maintain and enhance 'ecological balance\".\n- Organic farming is deeply linked with sustainable development25\n. It minimises environmental\ndegradation. In fact, organic farming and sustainable development go hand-in-hand.\n24. Environmental degradation - deterioration of the environment through depletion of resources such as air, water and soil.\n25. Sustainable development - a process of development which uses existing resources so optimally that their availability for\nuture generations is not reduced and the process of development is sustained over a long period of time\n231"},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-5-02","image":"ied-pages/ied-5-02.jpg","text":"Organically Grown Cotton of Maharashtra\n• It was Kisan Mehta of Prakruti (an NGO) who in 1995 suggested that the biggest user of chemical\npesticides - COTTON'\ncould be grown\norganically. Today around 130 farmers have pledged\n1,200 hectares of their land to grow cotton organically (on the International Federation of Organic\nAgriculture Movement's Standards).\n• Organic agriculture is more profitable in terms of money and soil conservation in the long run & this acts\nas an added advantage for the small and marginal farmers of the nation.\nAdvantages of Organic Farming\nFollowing are some notable advantages of organic farming, compared with conventional farming:\n(a) Discards the Use of Non-renewable Resources: Unlike conventional farming, organic farming\ndoes not use synthetic chemicals which are petroleum-based. We all know that petroleum is\na non-renewable resource.\n(ke ironment-friendly: Organic farming is environment-friendly. Chemical fertilizers, on the\nother hand, pollute the groundwater by raising its nitrate content.\n* Nitrates are a nealtn-nazard and polute the environment. Organic farming discards the\nuse of chemical fertilizers.\n(c) Sustains Soil Fertility: Use\nof animal\nmanures and composts helps sustain\nsoil fertility.\n› Chemical fertilizers, on the other hand,\nerode soil fertility. Accordingly, organic\nfarming is conducive to sustainable\ndevelopment of agriculture, while\nconventional farming is not."},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-5-02","image":"ied-pages/ied-5-02.jpg","text":"Farming and Soil Fertility\n• Conventional farming (using chemical fertilizers) erodes fertilty of Soll, whereas organic farming (using\nanimal manures and composts) sustains fertility of soil.\n• Organic farming nurtures soil as a resource for tuture generations. Accordingly, It is not only\nenvironment-friendly but also contributes to sustainable development.\n(d) Healthier and Taster Food: Organic farming offers healthier and taster food compared with\nconventional farming.\n, Recent studies have found that organically grown food is more nutritious than the food\ngrown by using the chemical inputs.\n> Existing trends indicate that demand for organic food is trending up, even when high price\nis a constraining factor."},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-5-02","image":"ied-pages/ied-5-02.jpg","text":"India has a Comparative Advantage\n• India has comparative advantage in organic farming. Because, organic farming involves a labour-intensive\nprocess.\n• Abundance of labour places India in a better position to specialise in organic farming.\n(e) Inexpensive Technology for the Small and Marginal Farmers: Organic farming offers an\ninexpensive farming-technology to small and marginal farmers who constitute the bulk of\nfarming population in India.\n> Conventional technology is to be adopted as a package including HYV seeds, fertilizers,\npesticides, insecticides, besides good irrigation facilities.\n> Small and marginal farmers in India, in their desperate bid to use this package, often slip\ninto indebtedness. No such package of expensive inputs is required for organic farming.\nLimitations of Organic Farming\n• It cannot be denied that organic farming (as compared to conventonal farming) offers lesser\nyield and organic foods (as compared to conventional foods) have shorter shelf-life. Implying that\norganic farming is not cost efficient.\n• However, considering the fact that organic foods are in high demand, higher product price is\nexpected to compensate for lower yield.\n• Overall advantages of organic farming (as it is deeply linked with sustainable development) should\ncertainly make it a more acceptable proposition than the conventonal farming.\nAVLR 5.4\nORGANIC FARMING\n233\nAnimal M=\n232\nustain"},{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"ied-5-01","image":"ied-pages/ied-5-01.jpg","text":"Q. 20. Fertilizers restore crop health, not the soil health. Is it ture?\nAns. Yes, it is true, rertilizers are meant to raise crop producti oty, in ract, continuous use of fertili\nerodes fertility of the soil.\nQ. 21. Enlist two demerits of organic farming.\nAns. (i) Lower crop-yield, and (ii) Shorter shelf-life of the product.\n9.22. The Prime bisters program tor nestoranon, Awareness, Nurturing and Improvement e\nPert cats de launche test promote balancer use\nof alternative and chemical fertilizers, said Mansukh Mandaviya, Minister for Chemicals and\nFertilizers, on Friday. Addressing a virtual meeting of agriculture ministers of states and union\nterritories, Mandaviya appealed to them to jointly contribute towards the promotion of organic\nfarming for preservation of soil health.\"\nSource: https://indianexpress.com/article/india/promote-organic-farming-to-boost-soil-health-\nsays-mandaviya-8695072/\nWhat are the principal reasons for the promotion of organic farming by the government?\n(i) It is environmental-friendly.\n(i It offers inexpensive farming-technology to the small and marginal farmers.\n(ili) Organic food is more nutritious compared to food from chemical inputs."},{"title":"Power Points [Summary of the Chapter]","page":"ied-5-01","image":"ied-pages/ied-5-01.jpg","text":"Rural Development: It means an action-plan for addressing the economic, social and environmental\nissues of the rural economy.\nChallenges of Rural Development:\n• Lingering Challenges of Rural Development:\n(i) Challenge of rural credit\n(i) Challenge of rural marketing\n• Emerging Challenges of Rural Development:\n(i) Diversification of productive activities\n(ii) Organic farming\nSources of Rural Credit:\n• Non-institutional Credit Sources: Landlords, village traders and moneylenders.\n• Institutional Credit Sources: Cooperative Credit Socisu• Bank of India and Other Commercia\nBanks, Regional Rural Banks and Land Development Banks, NABARD.\n• Agricultural Marketing: It includes:\n(i) gathering the produce after harvesting\n(ii) processing the produce\n(ii) grading the produce according to its quality\n(iv) packaging the produce according to buyers' preferences\n(v) storing the produce for future sale and selling the produce when price is lucrative\n234\nRural Development\n(i) Regulated markets\n(i) Provision of physical infrastructure\n(i) Cooperative agricultural marketing societies\n(iv) Policy instruments like Minimum Support Price (MSP), maintenance of buffer stock of wheat\nand rice by Food Corporation of India, and distribution of food grains through Public Distribution\nAlternative Marketing Channels:\n(i) Direct sale by the farmers to the consumers\n(i) Contract sale to the MINCs and corporate retails\nDiversification of Productive Activities: It implies a shift from crop farming to non-farming areas of\nemployment. Non-farm areas of employment include:\n(i) Animal husbandry\n(ii) Fisheries\n(iii) Horticulture\n(iv) Information technology\nOrganic Farming and Sustainable Development: Organic farming is basically a system of farming that\nreles upon the use of organic inputs for cultivation. It discards the use of chemical inputs.\n• Merits of Organic Farming:\n(i) Discards the use of non-renewable resources\n(il) Environment-friendly\n(iii) Sustains soil fertility\n(iv) Healthier and tastier food\n(v) Inexpensive technology for the small and marginal farmers\n• India has Comparative Advantage in Organic Farming: Because it involves labour-intensive process of\nproduction, and India has abundance of labour.\nLow Yield and High Cost per Unit of Output\" is the principal constraint in switching over from\nconventional farming to organic farming.\n235"}]},{"id":"i6","number":"6","subject":"ied","title":"Employment","intro":"","lessons":[{"title":"Workers and economic activity","body":"A worker participates in an economic activity producing goods or services.\n\nSelf-employed and hired workers both count.\n\nUnpaid family helpers in a farm/business can count even without a separate wage.\n\nOwn-family domestic services are generally outside measured employment.\n\nDo not confuse “no wage” with “no economic production”.","formula":"","source":"ied-6-13","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"6.1.1 Who is a Worker? · 6.1.2 Workers and Employment","points":["A worker participates in an economic activity producing goods or services.","Self-employed and hired workers both count.","Unpaid family helpers in a farm/business can count even without a separate wage.","Own-family domestic services are generally outside measured employment."],"takeaway":"Do not confuse “no wage” with “no economic production”."},{"title":"Self-employed, regular and casual workers","body":"Self-employed: operate their own enterprise, such as a shop owner.\n\nRegular salaried: continuing employment with regular pay.\n\nCasual wage labourer: hired for short periods/tasks, such as a daily-wage construction worker.\n\nEmployment status tells you the work relationship; construction or agriculture tells you the sector.","formula":"","source":"ied-6-13","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"6.1.3 Self-employed and Hired Workers","points":["Self-employed: operate their own enterprise, such as a shop owner.","Regular salaried: continuing employment with regular pay.","Casual wage labourer: hired for short periods/tasks, such as a daily-wage construction worker."],"takeaway":"Employment status tells you the work relationship; construction or agriculture tells you the sector."},{"title":"Labour force, workforce and participation","body":"Workforce: people actually employed.\n\nLabour force: employed people plus unemployed people willing and available for work.\n\nLabour supply: the hours/services people offer at a wage.\n\nSomeone not seeking or available for work is outside the labour force, rather than automatically unemployed.\n\nWorker-population ratio uses population; unemployment rate uses labour force. Always check the denominator.","formula":"Labour force = Workforce + Unemployed\nWorker-population ratio = Workers ÷ Population × 100\nUnemployment rate = Unemployed ÷ Labour force × 100","source":"ied-6-11","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"6.1.4 Labour Supply, Labour Force, Workforce","points":["Workforce: people actually employed.","Labour force: employed people plus unemployed people willing and available for work.","Labour supply: the hours/services people offer at a wage.","Someone not seeking or available for work is outside the labour force, rather than automatically unemployed."],"takeaway":"Worker-population ratio uses population; unemployment rate uses labour force. Always check the denominator."},{"title":"Rural–urban and male–female participation","body":"Rural work participation reflects farming, family labour and the need to earn even in low-productivity work.\n\nUrban patterns differ because of education and the available jobs.\n\nWomen face care responsibilities, social barriers and under-recording of economic activity.\n\nA lower measured participation rate does not mean women make no productive contribution.","formula":"","source":"ied-6-12","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"6.2 Size of Workforce · 6.3 Rate of Participation","points":["Rural work participation reflects farming, family labour and the need to earn even in low-productivity work.","Urban patterns differ because of education and the available jobs.","Women face care responsibilities, social barriers and under-recording of economic activity."],"takeaway":"A lower measured participation rate does not mean women make no productive contribution."},{"title":"Workforce across sectors","body":"Primary: agriculture and allied activities.\n\nSecondary: manufacturing and construction. Tertiary: services.\n\nRural workers are more concentrated in farming; urban work is more concentrated outside it.\n\nA high farm employment share with a low farm GDP share signals low average output per worker.\n\nDevelopment needs productive non-farm jobs, not just movement out of agriculture.","formula":"","source":"ied-6-10","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"6.5 Occupational Structure or Distribution of Workforce","points":["Primary: agriculture and allied activities.","Secondary: manufacturing and construction. Tertiary: services.","Rural workers are more concentrated in farming; urban work is more concentrated outside it.","A high farm employment share with a low farm GDP share signals low average output per worker."],"takeaway":"Development needs productive non-farm jobs, not just movement out of agriculture."},{"title":"Jobless growth and technology","body":"Jobless growth: GDP rises faster than employment opportunities.\n\nCapital-intensive technology and productivity gains can raise output without many new jobs.\n\nResponses: labour-intensive activities, small enterprises, relevant skills and rural diversification.\n\nIt means inadequate job creation relative to growth—not necessarily zero new jobs in every year.","formula":"","source":"ied-6-07","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"6.6 Jobless Growth","points":["Jobless growth: GDP rises faster than employment opportunities.","Capital-intensive technology and productivity gains can raise output without many new jobs.","Responses: labour-intensive activities, small enterprises, relevant skills and rural diversification."],"takeaway":"It means inadequate job creation relative to growth—not necessarily zero new jobs in every year."},{"title":"Casualisation of the workforce","body":"Casualisation: a rising share of casual wage work rather than secure regular jobs/self-employment.\n\nSurplus rural labour migrates; limited jobs and weak bargaining power encourage daily-wage hiring.\n\nConsequences: insecurity and fewer benefits.\n\nThe concept describes a process; check actual table years before claiming the casual share always rose.","formula":"","source":"ied-6-06","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"6.7.1 Casualisation","points":["Casualisation: a rising share of casual wage work rather than secure regular jobs/self-employment.","Surplus rural labour migrates; limited jobs and weak bargaining power encourage daily-wage hiring.","Consequences: insecurity and fewer benefits."],"takeaway":"The concept describes a process; check actual table years before claiming the casual share always rose."},{"title":"Formal and informal employment","body":"Formal sector: government/public enterprises and private establishments with ten or more workers in the textbook definition.\n\nInformal sector: smaller private enterprises, farming and self-employment.\n\nInformalisation: more employment without formal protection and social-security benefits.\n\nCasualisation concerns job status; informalisation concerns organisation and protection. They can overlap.","formula":"","source":"ied-6-06","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"6.7.2 Informalisation","points":["Formal sector: government/public enterprises and private establishments with ten or more workers in the textbook definition.","Informal sector: smaller private enterprises, farming and self-employment.","Informalisation: more employment without formal protection and social-security benefits."],"takeaway":"Casualisation concerns job status; informalisation concerns organisation and protection. They can overlap."},{"title":"Open, disguised and seasonal unemployment","body":"Open: willing and able to work, but unable to get a job.\n\nDisguised: extra workers can leave without reducing output; their marginal productivity is zero.\n\nSeasonal: work disappears outside the working season.\n\nEducated/structural: qualifications or skills do not match available opportunities.\n\nIf two people leave a family farm and output stays the same, those two were disguisedly unemployed.","formula":"","source":"ied-6-04","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"6.8 Unemployment · 6.8.1 Types","points":["Open: willing and able to work, but unable to get a job.","Disguised: extra workers can leave without reducing output; their marginal productivity is zero.","Seasonal: work disappears outside the working season.","Educated/structural: qualifications or skills do not match available opportunities."],"takeaway":"If two people leave a family farm and output stays the same, those two were disguisedly unemployed."},{"title":"Why unemployment persists","body":"Slow job growth and rapid population growth widen the employment gap.\n\nSeasonal farming and inadequate irrigation leave people without year-round work.\n\nJoint-family farming can conceal surplus labour; declining cottage industries reduce alternatives.\n\nLow investment, skills mismatch and limited mobility restrict opportunities.\n\nExplain the mechanism after the heading: inadequate irrigation → fewer crop seasons → seasonal unemployment.","formula":"","source":"ied-6-03","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"6.8.2 Causes of Unemployment in India","points":["Slow job growth and rapid population growth widen the employment gap.","Seasonal farming and inadequate irrigation leave people without year-round work.","Joint-family farming can conceal surplus labour; declining cottage industries reduce alternatives.","Low investment, skills mismatch and limited mobility restrict opportunities."],"takeaway":"Explain the mechanism after the heading: inadequate irrigation → fewer crop seasons → seasonal unemployment."},{"title":"Government measures and MGNREGA","body":"Direct employment: government hires workers itself. Indirect: public activity raises demand and jobs in other firms.\n\nMGNREGA: at least 100 days of unskilled manual wage work per financial year for a rural household whose adults volunteer.\n\nWork is to be provided within 15 days of demand; eligible failure attracts unemployment allowance.\n\nSkills, rural livelihoods, roads and small-business support create other opportunities.\n\nThe MGNREGA entitlement is per household, not 100 days separately for every adult.","formula":"","source":"ied-6-02","kind":"Understand & remember","textbookHeading":"6.9 Government and Employment Generation","points":["Direct employment: government hires workers itself. Indirect: public activity raises demand and jobs in other firms.","MGNREGA: at least 100 days of unskilled manual wage work per financial year for a rural household whose adults volunteer.","Work is to be provided within 15 days of demand; eligible failure attracts unemployment allowance.","Skills, rural livelihoods, roads and small-business support create other opportunities."],"takeaway":"The MGNREGA entitlement is per household, not 100 days separately for every adult."}],"pages":[{"id":"ied-6-13","image":"ied-pages/ied-6-13.jpg","text":"Indian Economic Development\n6.1 SOME BASIC CONCEPTS\n6.1.1 Who is a Worker?\nnemyolan:\n• A worker is an individual who is engaged in some production activity. Since production means\n'value-addition, we can also define worker as an individual who is engaged in the process of\n• We know, sum total of value-addition by the residents of a country amounts to national income.\nAccordingly, we can state that a worker, engaged in the process of value-addition, contributes to\nnational income of the country in one way or the other.\n! HIGHLIGHTS\nA Note of Caution\n\"All those who are engaged in economic activities, in whatever capacity-high or low, are workers.\n- NCERT Text (Page 94)\n• Read this statement with a note of caution.\n- The authors (VK Publications) are of the opinion that economic activities include all those activities\nwhich are related to the use of scarce resources. Like production (value-addition), consumption is\nalso an economic activity, as it is related to the use of scarce resources. The fact is that while all\nproduction activities are economic activities, all economic activities are not necessarily production\nactivities.\n- Accordingly, it would be appropriate to define a worker as the one who is engaged in production\nactivity the activity that leads to value-addition.\n- Consumption is certainly an economic activity, but a person engaged in consumption (called\nconsumer) is certainly not a worker.\n6.1.2 Workers and Employment\n• The nature of employment in India is multifaceted. Some workers get emnloyment\nthroughout the year while some others get employment only for a few months in a year.\nIrrespective of their duration of employment, those engaged in value-addition procees are trested\nas workers.\n• Workers need not necessarily be emploved by others and paid wages as a reward. Even those who\nare self-employed (and not getting wages) are also workers. Simply because they are engaged in\nthe 'value-adding process'. This brings us to the distinction between 'self-employed' and hired\nworkers', as in the following section.\n6.1.3 Self-employed and Hired Workers\nThe distinction between self-employed hired worker chanted\nnext page:\n1. Value-addition - economic value added to a product before it is sold in the\n268\nEmployment\nTypes of Workers\nSelf employed Workers\n• These are those\nworkers who are\nengaged in their\nown business or\nown profession.\n• Examples: A farmer\nworking on his\nown farm, or a\nshopkeeper working\nin his own cement\nshop.\nHired Workers\n• These are those workers who work for\nothers; they render their services to\nothers and as a reward, get ages/salaries.\nOr, may be they are paid in kind.\n• Examples: A proof-reader working in a\npublication house, or a teacher working\nin a school.\nCasual Workers\n• These are the daily wagers. They are not\nhired by their employers on regular basis.\n• They are not given social security\nbenefits, like provident fund, gratuity\nor pension.\n• Causal workers are usually unskilled\nworkers, like a worker working at the\nconstruction site.\nRegular Workers\n• These are on permanent pay-roll of\ntheir employers.\n• They are entitled to all social security\nbenefits including pension, gratuity\nand provident fund.\n• Regular workers are usually skilled\nworkers like an engineer working in a\nfactory.\n6.1.4 Labour Supply, Labour Force, Workforce,\nUnemployment and Participation Rate\n• Labour supply refers to the amount of labour that the workers are willing to offer corresponding\nto a particular wage rate. You may be able to work 10 hours a day, but willing to work only for\n6 hours a day at a particular wage.\n- Labour supply, thus, is measured in terms of man-hours of work (or man-days, considering\n8 man hours equal to one man-day. It is always estimated in relation to wage rate.\n• Labour force, on the other hand, refers to the number of workers actually working or willing to\nwork. It is not related to wage rate.\nTable 6.1: Labour Supply vs. Labour Force\nBasis of Difference\n1. Meaning\nLabour Force\nLabour Supply\n• It refers to supply of labour corresponding\n• It refers to the number of persons actually\nto different wage rates.\nworking or willing to work.\n• Supply of labour is measured in terms of\n• It is not related to wage rate.\nman-days of work and is always related to\nwage rate.\n2. Impact of\nChange in\nNumber of\nWorkers\n• Supply of labour can increase or decrease\n• Size of labour force increases or decreases\neven when the number of workers remains\nonly when the number of persons actually\nworking or willing to work increases or\nconstant.\n• Because the supply of labour is measured\ndecreases.\nin terms of man-days or person-days: one\n• Because it is measured in terms of the number\nperson-day referring to 8 hours of work.\nof persons (not in terms of person-days).\n269","file":"chap6.pdf"},{"id":"ied-6-12","image":"ied-pages/ied-6-12.jpg","text":"272\nIndian Economic Development\n6.2.1 Why is the bulk of our Workforce Rural-based?\n• This is simply because bulk of the jobs are in rural areas. Does it mean, bulk of our GDP is derived\nfrom the rural economy? Paradoxically not. Implying that more people are engaged in farming\nand allied activities, but contributing less to GDP.\n• For instance, the rural economy in India employs nearly 50 per cent of workforce but contribute\nless than 20 percent to the GDP This means low productivity of the workers in rural areas. Low\nproductivity implies low earnings, which is why there is widespread poverty in rural areas.\n6.2.2 Why is the Percentage of Female Workers Low,\nand Lower Still in Urban Areas?\nThis is because of the following three reasons:\n(i) Female education in India is still a far cry, implying low opportunities for jobs.\n(i) Among most families in urban areas, job work for women is still governed by family decisions\nrather than the individual's own decision. Implying that even the available opportunities are\n(iii) Higher employment among women in rural areas is owing to widespread rural poverty.\nFemale workers in rural areas are largely engaged in low paid and less productive jobs just\nto add to their family income. Many of them are given wages in terms of grains during the\nharvesting season, the season when they find most jobs.\n! HIGHLIGHTS\n• Low employment among women is a sign of social and economic backwardness of a nation.\n• Economic backwardness, because the economy fails to generate enough jobs.\n• Social backwardness, because job work for the women (outside their homes) is considered as a social\ntaboo.\nAVLR 6.2\nREASONS FOR LOW FEMALE WORKFORCE PARTICIPATION IN INDIA-\nRURAL vs. URBAN\n6.3 RATE OF PARTICIPATION ACROSS RURAL AND URBAN AREAS\n• We have already noted that participation rate refers to the proportion of the population that\nis actively engaged in the process of production or the process of value-addition in the country.\nIt is the worker-population ratio and for estimating it, divide the total number of workers by the\ntotal population of the country and multiply it by 100.\n• As an indicator, the worker-population ratio is used to analyse the employment situation in a\ncountry. If the ratio is higher, it implies that the participation of people is greater. if the ratio is\nmedium, or low, it implies that a very high proportion of its population is no\nthe production of goods and services of a country.\n*Here are some statistical facts relating to rural-urban rate of participation, and the rate of\nparticipation for male and female population in the country.\nTable 6.2: Worker-Population Ratio across Rural and Urban Areas (2022-23)\nSex\n54.0\n55.6\n54.4\nMen\n30.0\n18.7\nWomen\n27.0\n42.3\nTotal\n37.7\n41.1\nSource: NCERT, 2025-26\nObservations\n• Table 6.2 reveals that rate of participation for the urban areas is about 37.7 per cent in 2022-23.\nRate of participation for the rural areas is about 42.3 per cent in 2022-23.\n• In urban areas, rate of participation is about 55.6 per cent for men and 18.7 per cent for women.\n• In rural areas, rate of participation is about 54.0 per cent for men and 30.0 per cent for women.\n• Overall rate of participation in the country is about 41.1 per cent.\nBased on these facts, one can draw the following three conclusions:\n(a) High Dependency Ratio: Overall rate of participation in the country is not very high, implying\nnot many people are engaged in production activity. It points to a high dependency ratio in\nthe country.\n(b) High Rate of Participation, but Low\nLevel of Productivity in Rural Areas:\nRate of participation is higher in rural\nareas compared with urban areas.\nImplying that larger percentage of\npopulation is engaged in production\nactivity in rural areas.\n> But, given the fact that contribution\nHigh Rate of Participation for Women in Rural Areas\nof rural economy to GDP is lower than\nthat of urban economy, higher rate of participation in rural areas only points to lower\nproductivity, lower level of income and greater poverty in the rural India. We have already\nsubstantiated this point in the previous section.\n(c) High Rate of Participation for Women in Rural Areas: Rate of participation for women is\nhigher in rural areas compared with urban areas.\n> In rural areas, out of every 100 women, 30 women participate in employment, while in\nurban areas, only 19 women are engaged in employment, out of every 100 urban women.\nFollowing three observations support this fact:\n(i) In rural areas, poverty compels women to avoid education and seek employment.\nThus, employment (even without education) is a priority.\n(il) When employment (and not education) is a priority, women tend to seek employment\nat an early age. This also raises rate of participation in the rural areas.\na ratio of number of dependants (aged 0 to 14 & over 65 years) to the total population aged 15 to 65.","file":"chap6.pdf"},{"id":"ied-6-11","image":"ied-pages/ied-6-11.jpg","text":"Indian Economic Development\n• Workforce refers to the number of persons actually working, and does not account for those whe\nare willing to work (but not working). Thus,\nWorkforce = Labour force - Number of persons not working but are willing to work\n• Unemployment: The above concepts help the estimation of unemployment? (number of persons\nunemployed), as under:\nNumber of Persons Unemployed = Labour force - Workforce\n- Rate of unemployment is estimated as under:\nRate of Unemployment\nRate of Unemployment\n• Aa\n- Number of persons unemployed\nWorking or actively looking\n- × 100\nfor work\n= Labour force\nSize of labour force\n= 100\n• Participation rate refers to participation of people\nDon't have a job but\nin production activity, and is measured as a ratio of\nactively looking for it\n= 20\nworkforce to total population of the country. Thus,\nRate of Unemployment\nTotal workforce\nParticipation Rate =\n-× 100\n_ Number of persons unemployed x 100\nSize of labour force\nTotal population\n= 20\n100 × 100 = 20%\n! HIGHLIGHTS\nWhy do People Work and Why Should We Study about Working People?\n• Three factors explain why do people work:\n- People work to earn a living. It is essential for existence in a system of mutual interdependences.\n- Employment infuses in us a sense of self-esteem, and brings to the fore our sense of self-worth.\n- Employment contributes to GDP of the nation, and contributing to GDP gives us a sense of global pride\nbecause growth of GDP implies growth of the nation.\n• Four factors explain why studying about working people (or studving about employment is\n- Studying about working people offers insights into the quality and quantity of our human resource or\nhuman capital, which is an important determinant of economic growth.\n- Emplovment study helps analyse the significance of different sectore Primary Congra, and Tortia,\ncontributes how much towards GDP.\n- It helps analyse many social issues such as social security of the workers, work environment.\nexploitation of marginalised sections of the society, child labour, etc.\n- It offers inputs for manpower planning. Understandino the nature and autont of evicting amaloument.\nwe can plan for our future requirement of manpower across different sectors of the economy.\n& AVLR 6.1\nBASIC CONCEPTS RELATING TO UNEMPLOYMENT\n2. Unemployment - number of persons who are able to work and willing to work, but not getting work.\n270\nEmployment\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\nQ. 1. Clacsify the following as self-employed, regular salaried emplovee and casual wage labourer\n(i) Owner of a boutique.\n(i) Labourer working at a jute mill who is a daily wager but hired on regular basis.\n(ii) Clerk at Bank of Maharashtra.\n(iv) Labourer hired from the labour contract site to clean the road.\n(v) Teacher of a private school who gets monthly salary and has been working for the\npast 2 years on permanent roll.\n(vi) An ice cream seller running his own ice cream van near a school.\n(vil) A receptionist working as a full-time employee in an office.\n(vili) Workers hired during Diwali season to fulfil the excess demand of peak season.\n(ix) A lecturer hired as a permanent faculty member of a university.\n(x) Producer of a film working in his own movie.\nAns. Self-employed: (i), (vi), (x)\nRegular Salaried Employee: (ii), (iii), (v), (vii), (ix)\nCasual Wage Labourer: (iv), (viii)\nQ. 2. Self-employed are different from hired workers. Is it true?\nAns. Yes, it is true. Self-employed workers are those workers who are engaged in their own business\nor profession while hired workers are those workers who work for others and get wages and\nQ. 3. Can labour supply change even when the number of workers remain constant? If yes, state why?\nAns. Yes, as labour supply is measured in terms of man-days of work.\n0. 4. State two reasons why people engage themselves in work.\n(i) To earn a living, and\n6.2 SIZE OF WORKFORCE IN INDIA\n• Here are some important facts:\n- India has a workforce of nearly 471 million of persons.\n- About 77 per cent of the workforce comprises of male workers; only 23 per cent are female\nworkers (male and female workers also involve child labourers of respective sexes).\n- Nearly 67 per cent or two-third of the workforce is found in rural areas; urban workforce is only\n33 per cent or one-third of the total.\n- Female workforce (out of the total) in rural areas is nearly 25 per cent or one-fourth of the total\nrural workforce, while it is only 20 per cent or one-fifth of the total workforce in urban areas.\nThese facts raise two important questions which need to be addressed.\nTwo Major Questions related to Workforce\nWhy is the bulk of\nour workforce rural-based?\nWhy is the percentage\nof female workers low, and\nlower still in urban areas?\n271","file":"chap6.pdf"},{"id":"ied-6-10","image":"ied-pages/ied-6-10.jpg","text":"Indian Economic Development\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\nQ. 5. What does low level of employment among women indicate?\nAns. It indicates economic and social backwardness.\nQ. 6. Why is the rate of participation low in the case of urban women?\nAns. High priority for education in comparison to jobs at an early age results in low participation rate\namong urban women.\nQ. 7. State the principal reason behind high incidence of female workers of rural areas in primary sector.\nAns. Female workers in rural areas are unwilling to migrate for jobs outside their villages.\n6.5 OCCUPATIONAL STRUCTURE OR\nDISTRIBUTION OF WORKFORCE BY INDUSTRY\nWe now study distribution of our workforce across primary\", secondary\" and tertiary sectors of\nthe economy, briefly called occupational structure of the economy.\n• Generally, the economy has 8 industrial divisions which can be clubbed into 3 major sectors of the\neconomy as under:\n(i) Agriculture\nPrimary Sector\n(ii) Mining and Quarrying\n(iii) Manufacturing\n(iv) Electricity, Gas and Water Supply\nSecondary Sector\n(v) Construction\n(vi) Trade\n(vii) Transport and Storage\nTertiary Sector\n(viii) Services\nEmployment\nduring the year 2022-23.\nFig. 6.3 shows percentage distribution of workforce across primary, secondary and tertiary sectors\n29.0%\nSecondary Sector\nTertiary Sector\n46.1%\n24.9%\n• Heavy dependence on primary sector\n(for making a living) is a sign of\nbackwardness, as productivity level is\nvery low in this sector, and accordingly,\nstructure of wage rate is not much\nbeyond the subsistence levels of life.\nFig. 6.3: Percentage Distribution of Workforce by Industry (2022-23)\n• Fig. 6.3 offers two principal observations:\n(i) Primary sector is the main source of employment for majority of workers in India. As high as\n46.1 per cent of our workforce is engaged in primary sector, dominated by farming.\n(i) Tertiary sector accounts for 29.0 per cent of employment, while the secondary sector offers\nemployment to only 24.9 per cent of our workforce.\nThese observations lead to the following implications:\n• A large segment of workforce continues to • Greater employment\nin Secondary sector lagging behind the\ndepend on primary activities to make a living.\nprimary sector (despite its\ntertiary sector points to the fact that\n• This is despite the fact that the level of\nlow productivity) reveals\nindustrialisation has failed to take-off to\nproductivity and therefore the wage rate\nthat secondary and tertiary\nbecome the leading sector of growth.\nis significantly lower in primary sector\nsectors have failed to • We\nall\nknow\nthat\ndeveloped\ncompared to secondary and tertiary sectors.\ngenerate\nample\njob\neconomies are industrial economies®.\nopportunities.\nThese implications point to the backwardness of the Indian economy.\n6.5.1 Distribution of Rural-Urban Workforce across Different Sectors\nFig. 6.4 shows the distribution of rural-urban workforce across different sectors.\nUrban Workforce\n9.0%\nRural Workforce\n18.0%\n60.4%\nPrimary Sector\nSecondary Sector\nTertiary Sector\n32.7%\n57.8%\n22.5%\nPrimary Sector: Farming\nSecondary Sector: Industry\nTertiary\n4. Primary sector - a sector concerned with or relating to the exploitation of natural resources.\n5. Secondary sector - a sector concerned with manufacturing.\n6. Tertiary sector - a sector concerned with services.\n76\nFig. 6.4: Distribution of Rural and Urban Workforce across Different Sectors of the Economy (2022-23)\n7. Developed economies - economies with sustained economic growth, security, high per capita income and advanced\ntechnological infrastructure.\n8. Industrial economies - economies that are highly dependent upon the industrial sector for their growth and development.\n277","file":"chap6.pdf"},{"id":"ied-6-09","image":"ied-pages/ied-6-09.jpg","text":"Indian Economic Development\nEntry into jobs without education → Less productive jobs → Low wage rate\n→ Higher rate of participation to make a sustainable living\n• In urban areas, on the other hand, high priority for education implies low priority for jobs\nat an early age. This keeps the rate of participation relatively low.\n» Also, people in urban areas wait for the lucrative opportunities to suit their aualifications\nand skills. They are not so desperate (unlike their counterparts in rural areas) to get into\nemployment even if these are low-productive or less remunerative.\n!\nHIGHLIGHTS\nUnder-estimation of Women Workers in the Country\n• There is a significant under-estimation of women workers in the country. This is because:\n- Women rendering household services as housewives are not deemed as workers. Because (a) they\nare not paid for it, and (b) it is difficult to ascertain market value of the services rendered by the\nhousewives.\n- Women rendering services on the family farms in rural areas and in the family business in the urban\nareas are often not treated as workers, largely because of the convention of not considering unpaid\nwomen as workers, even when unpaid men working on family farms or in family business are\nconsidered as employed and therefore as workers.\n6.4 DISTRIBUTION OF WORKERS BY REGION AND GENDER\nFig. 6.1 shows percentage distribution of workers as self-employed, 'regular salaried employees\nand 'casual wage labourers\", separately for the rural and urban areas of the country.\nUrban Workers\nRural Workers\n13%\nSelf-employed\nRegular salaried\nemployees\nCasual wage\nlabourers\n274\nFig. 6.1: Percentage Distribution of Workers (Self-employed, Regular salaried employees\nand Casual wage labourers) according to Region\n• The figure shows that self-employment is higher in rural areas compared to urban areas. Following\nprincipal factors support this fact:\n(i) In urban areas, people look for skilled jobs in offices and factories while in rural areas, family\nfarms are the most attractive means of employment.\n(it) In rural areas, non-farm job opportunities are limited. Accordingly, people generally do not\nfamily farms and fields as self-employed.\nshow preference to acquire skill and training for non-farm occupations. They prefer to stay on\n• All the above factors imply that self-employment is an easy option in rural areas. These factors\nalso imply that the proportion of salaried workers is quite high in urban areas (47 per cent) as\ncompared to those in rural areas (13 per cent).\n« The proportion of casual wage labourers is higher in rural areas (26 per cent) as compared to\nthose in urban areas (13 per cent).\n_ This is because agriculture is seasonal in nature and generates employment primarily during\nsowing and harvesting seasons.\nFig. 6.2 shows percentage distribution of workers as 'self-employed\", 'regular salaried employees' and\n'casual wage labourers', separately among men and women.\nFemale Workers\nMale Workers\n21%\nSelf-employed\nRegular salaried\nemployees\nCasual ware\nabourers\n54%\nFig. 6.2: Percentage Distribution of Workers (Self-employed, Regular salaried employees\nand Casual wage labourers) according to Gender\nThe figure (Fig. 6.2) shows that:\n• Both across male and female workers, self-employment is a predominant source of livelihood,\naccounting for more than 50 per cent of the workers.\n• Percentage of 'regular salaried employees' is slightly higher for men (23 per cent) than\nwomen (17 per cent).\n- This is because: owing to their low level of education, but high compulsion for jobs (to make a\nsustainable living for the family), women are ready to work even at low wage.\n• Self-employment among women is as significant as wage-employment (regular + casual).\n- This is because women in rural areas find ample opportunities of self-employment, engaging\nthemselves in traditional jobs like spinning, weaving, dyeing and bleaching.\n\" Among women, 'casual wage employment' is the second major source of employment.\n! HIGHLIGHTS\n• In India, self-employment is a significant source of livelihood. Because:\n- Literacy rate is low, implying low acquisition of skill for wage jobs.\n- Owing to diverse\nvironment across different regions, mobility of workers is low.\nny, generating less jobs owing to low level of investment.\n- India is a dev\n275","file":"chap6.pdf"},{"id":"ied-6-08","image":"ied-pages/ied-6-08.jpg","text":"278\nIndian Economic Development\n• Fig. 6.4 offers two principal observations:\n(i) While those living in urban areas are engaged largely in secondary and tertiary sectors, those\nin the rural areas find primary sector as their preferred source of employment.\n(it) Tertiary sector is taking a lead over secondary sector as a source of employment in urban areas.\nThe tabular presentation, given below, highlights these observations along with the valid reason\nURBAN WORKFORCE\n(bent towards tertiary sector)\nRURAL WORKFORCE\n(bent towards primary sector)\n1. Observation • In India, trade, commerce, banking, insurance, • Rural workforce shows a sinificant bot\nhealth, education, tourism and related services\nare developing faster than manufacturing and\ntowards 'primary sector of employment.\nallied production activities.\n• These activities represent 'Tertiary sector\nof employment. Urban workforce shows a\nsignificant bent towards the tertiary sector of\nemployment.\n2. Reason\n• This may largely be explained in terms of\n(i) Lack of non-farm job opportunities in the\nglobalisation of the economy, besides the\nrural areas.\nfact that people in urban areas are becoming\nincreasingly conscious of their quality of life,\n(ii) Lack of mobility (or limited mobility) owing\nspending the bulk of their income on a variety\nto family/social constraints (particularly for\nwomen workers).\nof services (like health, education and tourism).\n6.5.2 Distribution of Male-Female Workforce across Different Sectors\nFig. 6.5 shows male-female workforce across different sectors of the economy.\nMale Workforce\nFemale Workforce\n32.2%%\n24.7%\nPrimary Sector\nSecondary Sector\nTertiary Sector\n37.5%\n64.4%\n29.39\n19.3%\nFig. 6.5 offers the following two observations:\nFig. 6.5: Distribution of Male-Female Workforce across Different Sectors of the Economy (2022-23)\n(a) Primary activities of production are confined largely to rural areas, and\nMobility of wore in rural areas extremely wOwing the and corl\nconstraints, female workers would prefer to do jobs dose to their homes, even when\nwage rate is low.\nEmployment\n(i) Percentage of men finding employment in primary sector (37.5 per cent) is only next to that of\nwomen. It is also fairly low. It is explained in terms of the lack of jobs outside agriculture in the\nrural areas. Men get more employment opportunities in both secondary and tertiary sectors\n6,5.3 Change in Occupational Structure\nTable 6.3 shows change in occupational structure in India over the period 1972-73 to 2022-23.\nTable 6.3: Change in Occupational Structure (1972-73 to 2022-23)\nSector\nPercentage of Workforce\nPrimary\nSecondary\nTertiary\nTotal\n100\n100\n2022-23\n46.1\n24.9\n29.0\n100\nSource: NCERT, 2025-26\nObservations\n• Table 6.3 shows that between the period 1972-73 to 2022-23:\n- percentage of workforce engaged in primary sector has declined from 74.3 per cent to 46.1 per cent.\n- percentage of workforce engaged in secondary sector has risen from 10.9 per cent to 24.9 per cent.\n- percentage of workforce engaged in tertiary sector has risen from 14.8 per cent to 29.0 per cent.\nDoes it mean that we have become an industrial economy (an essential feature of a developed\neconomy)?\nAnswer is: Not at all.\n- Changes in occupational structure no way indicates the shift in epicentre (hub of economic activity)\nof the economy-from primary to secondary sector. So long as the epicentre does not shift (or so\nlong as primary sector continues to dominate as principal source of employment), Indian economy\nwould continue to marked as agricultural economy?, rather than an industrial economy.\n• Historical experience of the developed countries suggests a predominant shift of workforce from\nprimary sector to secondary and tertiary sectors of employment. The shift has to be so prominent\nthat industrial sector emerges as the principal source of employment, or a principal source of\nlivelihood. But the Indian experience presents only a dismal picture.\n- The shift is so modest that we can at best call ourselves a developing economy rather than a\ndeveloped economy.\n! HIGHLIGHTS\nWork at Home\n• Earlier work at home was associated with traditional work like weaving, lace making, etc.\n• It was only after the technological revolution that work at home' became a diversified term associated\nwith tech-enabled IT sector as well.\n• This has in fact become a common household name ever since the nationwide lockdown was imposed\nin our country amidst the outbreak of COVID-19 pandemic in 2020-21.\n- an economy where agriculture is the principal source of occupation.\n9. Agricultural e\n279","file":"chap6.pdf"},{"id":"ied-6-07","image":"ied-pages/ied-6-07.jpg","text":"Indian Economic Development\n6.6 JOBLESS GROWTH\n• Jobless growth is a situation when GDP of a country increases without a corresponding increase\nin employment. There could even be a fall in the level of employment while GDP is rising.\n• There are two ways of raising the level of output, as under:\nTwo Ways of Raising the Level of Output\nthrough greater employment\nthrough better technology\n• In countries like India where there is staggering unemployment, economic growth becomes\nmeaningful only when it is associated with greater opportunities of employment, so that povety\n• Unfortunately, Indian economy is generating GDP more through technology than the employment\nof labour. It is a situation of jobless growth. It leads to chronic unemployment?°\n• HIGHLIGHTS\nJobless growth is a situation when the level of output in the economy tends to rise owing to innovative\ntechnology without any perceptible (noticeable) rise in the level of employment.\n6.6.1 The Gulf between GDP Growth and Employment Growth\n• The trend path of GDP and employment during the period 1950-51 to 2022-23 is reflected in\nFig. 6.6 as under:\nGDP\n1o Employment\n0.39\n0.85\n203199184 173189 13 0.98\n3.2\n0.28\n1.12\nFig 6.6 offers the following observations:\nFig. 6.6: Percentage Growth Rate of GDP and Employment (1951-2023)\nGDP growth rate recorded a rise from 3.6 per cent in 1951 to 87 percent in 1000-000 # chowed\n10. Chronic unemployment - persistent unemployment over an extended period of time.\na modest slump during the period 2005-2023, and in 2022-23 it was recorded to be 7 per cent.\n280\n* Paradoxically, growth rate of employment recorded a much milder escalation from 0.39 percent\nin 1951 to 1.12 per cent in 2012, and further to 3.2 per cent in 2022-23\nThere was a period (late 1990s) when the growth of employment slumped to as low as 0.28 per\ncent, pointing to the staggering gap when GDP growth was of 8.7 per cent.\nIndia is thus facing an expanding gulf between 'GDP growth' and 'employment growth, and is\nconsenuently slipping into the vicious cycle of liobless growth', thanks to technology overwhelmingly\n6.6.2 Why are we relying more on Technology, and less on Labour?\n* our growth process is being increasingly hijacked by MNCs (multinational companies). These\ncompanies specialise in achieving high growth through efficient use of technology rather than\nthrough greater use of manpower.\n• The obvious result is that growth is moving faster than the opportunities of employment. By and\nlarge, it is a 'jobless growth'.\nJAVLR 6.3\nGROWTH SANS EMPLOYMENT\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\n9.8. Secondary sector is taking a lead over tertiary sector as a source of employment in urban\nAns. It is wrong to say that secondary sector is taking a lead over tertiary sector as a source of\nemployment in urban areas. In fact, tertiary sector is taking a lead over secondary sector as a\nsource of employment in urban areas. Secondary sector lagging behind the tertiary sector points\nto the fact that industrialisation has failed to take-off to become the leading sector of growth.\nQ.9. \"The Indian unemployment rate is hovering around 7-8% according to the Centre for Monitoring\nIndian Economy. This is because job growth has not kept pace with GDP growth.\"\nSource: timesofindia.indiatimes.com\nWhat is the situation called when growth of jobs is not in sync with GDP growth?\nAns. Jobless growth, as there is GDP growth in the economy but no substantial growth in the level of\n6.7 CASUALISATION AND INFORMALISATION OF WORKFORCE.\n6.7.1 Casualisation\n• What is casualisation of workforce? It is a situation when more and more workers are hired on\n'casual & contract positions' rather than 'full-time permanent positions'. So that the percentage\nof casually hired workers in the total workforce tends to rise. Casual and contract workers are hired\nfor short period of time to accomplish a specific-task. Often, the casual workers are deprived of\n281","file":"chap6.pdf"},{"id":"ied-6-06","image":"ied-pages/ied-6-06.jpg","text":"282\nIndian Economic Development\nhealth and insurance benefits as available to the permanent full-time employees. Also, due to bo\nof long-term commitments, the casual workers face the risk of job-insecurity.\n• Table 6.4 shows change in employment pattern (status wise) in India during the period 19722\nTable 6.4: Percentage Change in Employment Pattern (Status-wise), 1972-2023\nItem\nPercentage of Workforce\nSelf-employed\nRegular Salaried Employees\nCasual Wage Labourers\nTotal\nSource: NCERT, 2025-26\n1972-73\n61.4\n15.4\n23.2\n100\n1983\n1993-94\n57.3\n13.8\n28.9\n100\n54.6\n13.6\n31.8\n100\n2011-12\n52.0\n18.0\n30.0\n100\n2022-23\n55.6\n21,1\n23.3\n100\n• Table 6.4 shows that over the period 1972-73 to 2022-23, there is a perceptible shift from\nself-employment and regular salaried employment to casual\nwage work, even when\nself-employment continues to be the major avenue of employment in India.\n• The process of moving from self-employment and regular salaried employment to casual wage\nwork is known as casualisation of workforce. This makes the workers highly vulnerable.\n• In India, casualisation of workforce is an emerging challenge, as indicated by the following facts:\n(i) Percentage of self-employed workers has declined from 61.4 per cent in 1972-73 to\n57.3 per cent in 1983 and further to 54.6 per cent in 1993-94. It further decreased to 52 per\ncent in 2011-12, though by 2022-23, it had risen to 55.6 per cent.\n(i) Percentage of regularly-hired workers in total workforce continues to hover between\n14-15 per cent during the period 1972-73 to 1993-94. It was 18 per cent in 2011-12 and\n21.1 per cent in 2022-23.\n(in Percentage of casually hired workers total workfore has ricon form 2g nor cort in\n197to percent in further toner conting aner it shy\ndeclined to 30 percent in 2011-12. In 2022-23 it again declined to 23.3 per cent.\n(iv) In the formal manufacturing sector, percentage of contract workers was recorded to be as\nhigh as 40.7 per cent in the year 2022-23.\nCauses of Increasing Incidence of Casualisation\nThe five main causes of increasing incidence of casualisation in India are as under:\n(i) The bulk of self-employed workforce is found in rural areas.\n(il) People work on their farms and fields, not because everybody is painfully empload not\ngetting non-farm jobs even when they wish to migrate.\nbecause everybody is contributing to output, but simply because many of them are not\nEmployment\nunemployed\".\nThey are employed on their farms only for the name-sake; in fact they are disguisedly\ni taking cance for opportunities, these marginal workers tend to migrate to urban areas,\nand more often than not, they get employment only as casual workers, or as daily-wagers.\nThis is the story of unskilled workers leading to casualisation of employment.\nvi Even skilled workers in the urban areas are to struggle for regular jobs. This is owing to\nmassive unemployment across all sectors of the economy. Unemployment is so huge that\nthe bargaining power of the workers is significantly reduced. On the other hand, bargaining\npower of the employers is very high. They follow the principle of 'hire and fire12\n6.7.2 Informalisation\nEmployment may be classified as formal sector employment, and informal sector employment.\nClassification of Employment\nFormal Sector\nInformal Sector\nEmployment\nEmployment\n(a) Formal Sector: Formal sector refers to organised sector of the economy. It includes all\ngovernment departments, public enterprises and private establishments which hire 10 or\nmore workers. Those working in the organised sector are called 'formal workers'.\n(b) Informal Sector: Informal sector refers to unorganised sector of the economy. It includes all\nsuch private enterprises which hire less than 10 workers, besides farming and self-employment\nventures. Those working in the unorganised sector are called 'informal workers'.\n! HIGHLIGHTS\nFormal Sector Employment\n• The data relating to formal sector employment is collected by the Union Ministry of Labour through\nemployment exchanges located across different parts of the country.\n• In India, public sector is the largest employer in the formal sector.\n• In 2012, out of 30 million formal sector workers, about 18 million workers were employed in public\n• Also, men form the majority, as women constitute only about 17 per cent or one-sixth of the formal\nsector workforce.\n• From the viewpoint of employment status, the underlying difference between formal and informal\nsectors (or between organised and unorganised sectors) is that:\n(i) Workers in the formal sector are entitled to social security benefits (such as provident fund,\ngratuity, pension, etc.) while workers in the informal sector are not.\n11. Disanisediv unemployed - a situation when the person seems to be employed but is not contributing to the output, i.e.,\nhis marginal productivity is zero.\n12. Hire and fire-emnlaving new staff and dismissing existing staff.\n283","file":"chap6.pdf"},{"id":"ied-6-05","image":"ied-pages/ied-6-05.jpg","text":"Indian Economic Development\n(ii) While economic interest of the workers in formal\nsector is protected through various labour laws,\nthere are hardly any protective laws for the\nEntitlement to\nOrganised Unorganis\nSector\nSector\ninformal sector (other than Minimum Wages Act).\nsocial security\nbenefits\n(iii) To protect their economic interest, workers in\nProtection of\nthe formal sector can form trade unions; no such\neconomic interest\nof workers\nunions exist in the informal sector.\nFormation of\n(iv) Informal sector workers are highly vulnerable to\ntrade unions\nuncertainties of the market. They are hired when\nVulnerability to\nthe market sentiments are good and are fired\nuncertainties of\nmarket\nwhen there is economic slowdown.\nTable 6.5: Formal Workers vs. Informal Workers\nBasis of Difference\nFormal Workers\nInformal Workers\n1. Nature of Work\n• Work in organised sector of the\n• Work in unorganised sector of the\neconomy.\neconomy.\n2. Entitlement to Social\n• Are\nentitled\nto social security\n• Are not entitled to social security\nSecurity Benefits\nbenefits (like provident fund, gratuity,\nbenefits.\npension, etc.)\n3. Right to Form Trade\n• Can form trade unions.\n• Cannot form trade unions.\nUnions\n4. Protection against\nUncertainties of the\nMarket\n• Are protected by various labour laws\nagainst uncertainties of the market.\n• Remain unprotected by labour laws,\nand are therefore, highly vulnerable to\nuncertainties of the market.\n! HIGHLIGHTS\n• İn essence, formal workers are like regular workers, while informal workers are like daily-wagers.\n• Uncertainty of employment is always a lingering threat to the informal workers.\n• An informal worker is more likely to be 'casual' as well.\n• Informalisation leads to casualisation.\nInformalisation of Workforce in India\n• Meaning: Informalisation of workforce is defined as a situation where percentage of workforce\nin the formal sector tends to decline and that in the informal sector tends to rise.\n• Evolution: In accordance with socialistic pattern of society13 (as a central goal of Development\nPlanning launched in 1951), 'formal sector employment' should have risen and informal sector\nemployment' should have declined over time. Alas! iust the onposite has hannened in the\ndevelopment since 1991.\n13. Socialist pattern of society - a pattern of society which gives more importance to collective c\nplanning and social welfare.\neconomic\n284\nEmployment\n» Major Cause: A series Of economic reforms were launched in 1991, with liberalisation, privatisation\nand globalisation' as their key elements. Consequently, there is a significant transformation from a\ncontrolled economy' to 'market economy.\n- And, in this process of transformation there has been a significant drift towards informalisation\nbut art pronomy and informalication of workers nerhans, are strongly correlated\nto each other. Informalisation is driven by the forces of supply and demand in the labour market.\n• Data: Presently, formal sector workers comes to merely 30 million, i.e., 6 per cent of the total\nworkforce (473 million workers); rest 443 million, i.e., 94 per cent of the workfore continues to\nbe informal.\n- Out of the 30 million formal sector workers, only 6 million, i..., 20 per cent are women.\n- In the informal sector, 70 percent of the workforce are male and 30 per cent of the workforce\nare women.\nFormal Sector\nInformal Sector\nMale\nFemale\n310\n24\nFig. 6.7: Workers in Formal and Informal Sector, 2011-12 (in million)\n• Implication: This points to growing vulnerability of the workforce to uncertainties of the market\neconomy.\n- However, in view of the rising vulnerability of the workers in the informal sector, the government\nin India has recently initiated several measures (by way of Labour Codes 2025) to improve\nworking conditions of these workers. These measures include:\n(i) universal social security for Gig/Platform workers\"4,\n(i) free health checks for workers beyond 40 years of age,\n(iii) expanded protection for the women on night duties, and\n(iv) mandatory appointment letters for all workers including Gig/Platform workers (defining\ntheir wages and terms & conditions of work).\n! HIGHLIGHTS\n• A staggering number of 44 crore workers working in informal or unorganised sector of the economy\nPoints to growing vulnerability of the workforce to uncertainties of the market.\n• Informalisation causes poverty and fragmentation of social harmony. In the early 1980s, closure of\nmore than 60 textile mills in Ahmedabad (offering employment to nearly 1,50,000 workers) and the\nconsequent riots are a testimony to this assertion.\no 'on demand' or temporary workers.\n14. Gig/Platform wc\n285","file":"chap6.pdf"},{"id":"ied-6-04","image":"ied-pages/ied-6-04.jpg","text":"Indian Economic Development\nAVLR 6.4\nCASUALISATION AND INFORMALISATION OF THE WORKFORCE\n286\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\nQ. 10. Classify the following as formal sector or informal sector workers:\n(i) A nurse at a private hospital which has a staff of 25 people.\n(ii) Rickshaw puller.\n(iii) The owner of a textile shop employing 9 people.\n(iv) Civil engineer employed at a construction company having 10 workers.\n(v) A police constable.\nAns. Formal Sector Workers: (i), (iv), (v)\nInformal Sector Workers: (ii), (i)\nQ. 11. In India, casualisation of workforce has tended to rise over time. Is it true?\nAns. Yes, it is true. In India, casualisation of workforce has tended to rise, particularly during the\nperiod 1972-73 to 1993-94. During this period, casualisation increased from 23.2 per cent to\n31.8 per cent. This is because of massive unemployment in the country and consequently, low\nbargaining power of the employees in relation to the employers.\n6.8 UNEMPLOYMENT\n• Unemployment refers to a situation when\npeople are willing to work at the existing wage\nrate, as well as able to work, but are not getting\nwork.\n• It should be noted with emphasis that such\npeople who are not willing to work at the\nexisting wage rate are not considered as\nunemployed.\nUnemployment: Willing to work but not getting work\n!\nHIGHLIGHTS\nData Source for Unemployment\n• The major sources of data on unemployment are:\n- Reports of Census of India,\n- National Statistical Office's Reports of Employment and Unemployment Situation,\n- Annual Reports of Periodic Labour Force Survey, and\n- Directorate General of Employment and Training Data of Registration with Employment Exchanges.\nEmployment\n6.8.1 Types of Unemployment\nfollowing are the two principal types of unemployment found in India:\nTwo Principal Types of Unemployment in India\nDisguised Unemployment\nSeasonal Unemployment\nDetails are as under:\n(a) Disguised Unemployment: Disguised\nunemployment\noccurs\nwhen\nthe\nnumber of workers engaged in a job\nis much more than actually required\nto accomplish it. If some of them\nare withdrawn from that job, total\nproduction will not fall. The below given\nexample illustrates this situation.\nDisguised Unemployment: Apparently employed\nbut actually not\nExample:\nSuppose a farmer has four acres of land, but he actually needs only two workers and himself\nto carry out various operations on his farm in a year. However, if he employs five workers\nthere, then two persons are disguised unemployed.\n> Disguised unemployment is a common form of unemployment found in rural India.\n> Disguised unemployment is rampant (widespread) in Indian agriculture. A study conducted\nin the late 1950s, revealed that about one-third of agriculture workers in India were\ndisguisedly unemployed.\n> The three principal causes of disguised unemployment are as under:\n(i) On account of joint family system, farming families continue to work on family land, no\nmatter the actual number of workers far exceeds the required number.\n(ii) Per person holding size continues to shrink with the expansion of the family size.\nImplying greater possibility of disguised unemployment.\n(iii) Lack of job opportunities outside agriculture compels the people to work on family\nfarms. It further increases the possibility of disguised unemployment.\n! HIGHLIGHTS\nDisguised Unemployment is not prevalent in Agriculture alone\n• Disguised unemployment is a characteristic feature of the Indian agriculture. It is almost rampant there\nOwing to a heavy pressure of population, joint family system and the lack of vocational avenues outside\nagriculture. But it is a phenomenon not confined to agriculture alone.\n• Have a deeper look into the functioning of the public sector enterprises. It will not be surprising to find\nlots of persons idling all the time. It is a pointer to the fact that more people are employed than actually\ns a sign of disguised unemployment.\nneeded. S\n287","file":"chap6.pdf"},{"id":"ied-6-03","image":"ied-pages/ied-6-03.jpg","text":"Indian Economic Development\n(b) Seasonal Unemployment: Another kind of unemployment is seasonal unempl\nThis is a common form of unemployment prevailing in India. It occurs simply beca\njob. They have no work to do.\nagriculture is a seasonal occupation. During off-season, often the farm workers are out\n› The volume of seasonal unemployment depends upon the conditions and methods a\ncultivation in different states.\n> It is estimated that a farmer who grows one crop in a year usually remains unemployed fo\n5 to 7 months.\n• In rural areas, besides agriculture, there are many other seasonal activities like sugarcane\ncrushing, brick kilns, etc. Workers engaged in these activities remain occupied for af\nmonths in a year. Rest of the period, they remain unemployed.\n! HIGHLIGHTS\nFull Employment does not mean Zero Unemployment\n• No, full employment does not mean a situation of zero unemployment.\n• In an economy, there is always some natural rate of unemployment, which is the minimum rate of\nunemployment caused by structural changes in the system of production.\n6.8.2 Causes of Unemployment in India\nThe main causes of unemployment in India are highlighted through the following flow chart:\nCauses of Unemployment in India\n(a)\nSlow\nEconomic\nGrowth\n(e)\n(f)\n(g)\n(h)\nRapid\nGrowth of\nPopulation\nAgriculture\n-A Seasonal\nOccupation\nLack of\nIrrigation\nFacilities\nJoint\nFamily\nSystem\nDecay of\nCottage and\nSmall\nIndustries\nLow\nSavings and\nInvestment\nLimited\nMobility of\nLabour\nFollowing is a detailed explanation of the main causes of ever-increasing unemployment in India:\n(a) Slow Economic Growth: Economic growth (in terms of annual increase in GDP size) is too\nslow to absorb the expanding supply of labour. Hence, rising unemployment.\n(b) Rapid Growth of Population: Constantly rising population has been a grave nroblem in India.\nIt is one of the principal factors contributing to unemployment.\n15. Seasonal unemployment - a type of unemployment that occurs at particular times of the year when demand for labou\nis lower than usual.\n16. Structural changes - in the context of zero unemployment, structural changes refer primarilto chance on taken\nor pattern of demand.\nTAStew economic crowth COP in india is growing at the rate off% derant which be a\ndollars in USA. In terms of annual increase in GDP size, 6% of 4 trillion dollars is far less than 30% of 25 trillion deltars\nThus, 6% GDP growth rate in India is low in the sense that the existing GDP base is very low, because which\nunemployment.\nexpansion of production (during a year) remains inadequate to absorb the expanding labour supply. Hence, rising\n288\nEmployment\n* Despite the completion of Twelve Five Year Plans, the size of unemployment has continued\n(d) Agriculture- A Seasonal Occupation: Agriculture is underdeveloped in India and so offers\nseasonal employment. No doubt it is the primary occupation of our country and a large size\nof the population depends upon it, but its seasonal character does not provide stable jobs to\nthe farmers throughout the year.\n* Farmers often find no opportunity to work for three to four months in a year. The size\nof disguisedly unemployed is estimated to be nearly 15 per cent of the total working\npopulation in agriculture.\n(d) Lack of Irrigation Facilities: Despite decades of planning, irrigation facilities continue to be\nscant, covering only about 44 per cent of agriculture area.\n› For want of irrigation, mono-cropping (only one crop a year) becomes a compulsion. In the\nabsence of multi-cropping, opportunities of employment remain limited.\n(e) Joint Family System: It encourages disguised unemployment. In joint families, there is a high\ntendency to survive on joint income without work.\n> Joint family system is more prevalent in rural areas; hence a high degree of disguised\nunemployment in these areas.\n(f) Decay of Cottage and Small Industries: Decay of cottage and small industries during the\nBritish regime has given a big blow to the potential of self-employment in India.\n> Emergence of large industry in place of the small industry after independence has failed to\nyield employment opportunities to the desired extent.\n(g) Low Savings and Investment: There is scarcity of capital and even the scarce capital has not\nbeen optimally used to eradicate unemployment.\n> Bulk of the capital has been invested in large-scale industries where there is high capital\nper unit of labour. Employment of labour per unit of capital remains significantly low.\n(h) Limited Mobility of Labour: Mobility of labour in India is very low. Owing to a variety of family\nas well as social constraints, people are reluctant (unwilling) to move to far-off areas even\nwhen jobs are available there.\n> Factors like diversity of language, religion and customs in different states are a major\nhindrance to the mobility of labour. Lesser the mobility, greater the unemployment.\nBriefly, whereas low rate of economic growth is generating\nonly limited employment\nopportunities, high rate of population growth is causing an exponential rise in the demand for jobs.\nConsequently, the gulf between the supply of labour and the demand for labour is tending to be very\nlarge.\nIn fact, unemployment is assuming monstrous proportions and is an emerging threat to social\nharmony in the country.\n289","file":"chap6.pdf"},{"id":"ied-6-02","image":"ied-pages/ied-6-02.jpg","text":"Indian Economic Development\nCRITICAL QUESTIONS\nQ. 12. What are the two ways of tackling disguised unemployment in agriculture?\nAns.\n(i) Integrated rural development offering non-farm occupation in the villages.\n(ii) Development of urban industry that induces rural-urban migration.\nQ. 13. Disguised unemployment lowers efficiency/productivity. Write your views.\nAns. This is absolutely true that disguised unemployment lowers efficiency/productivity. As a mati\nof fact, disguised unemployment is defined as a situation when marginal productivity of labou\nis zero or negative. In a situation of disguised unemployment, number of workers tends to\nlarge in relation to land and capital, that productivity of labour (both marginal as well as average\nis bound to be low.\n6.9 GOVERNMENT AND EMPLOYMENT GENERATION\nOvertime, the Government of India (the central and state governments) has taken a host of\ninitiatives to tackle the problem of unemployment. Some of these policies and programmes are\nlisted below:\nTable 6.6: Existing Employment Generation Programmes\nExisting Programmes (Presently Non-active)\nExisting Programmes (Presently Active)\n1. Prime Minister's Rozgar Yojana (PMRY): Started in 1. Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment\n1993, this was the first programme aimed at creating\nGuarantee Act (MGNREGA)*: Launched in 2005,\nself-employment to educated unemployed in urban\nunder this Act, all those who are willing to work at\nareas.\nthe minimum wage are offered work for a minimum\n- The scheme provides a loan of up to & 1 lakh for\nperiod of 100 days.\nopening one's own enterprise and & 2 lakh for other\nactivities.\n2. Swarna Jayanti Shahri Rozgar Yojana (SJSRY): This\nYojana was launched on December 1, 1997.\n2. National Rural Livelihood Mission (NRLM): This\n- The objective of this yojana is to provide self-\nscheme is also known as 'Aajeevika'.\nemployment or wage employment to urban\n- NRLM is expected to provide self-employment to\nunemployed or underemployed persons.\n- The programme has\nnow been renamed as\n3. Rural Employment Generation Programme (REGP):\nDeendayal Antayodaya Yojana.\nThis programme was lunched on 1 April,\n3. Pradhan Mantri Gram Sadak Yojana: It was launched\nIt mainly aims at self-employment opportunities in\nin December 2000 with the objective of providing\nsmall towns and villages.\nsingle all weather road connectivity to unconnected\n- The scheme provides loans up to & 25 lakh to rural\nartisans/entrepreneurs to set-up their own enterprises.\n- Generation of employment was the underlying\n4. Prime Minister's Employment Generation Programme\ntheme of this programme.\n(PMEGP): This scheme was started in 2008.\n- It aims at providing an assistance of & 25 lakh for\nsetting up a manufacturing enterprise and & 10 lakh\n5. Swarnajayanti Gram Swarozgar Yojana (SGSY): This\nYojana was launched in the villages, in April 1999.\n- Under this programme, 1a large number of small\nenterprises were established in rural areas.\n[Note: From December, 2025 MGNREGA has been renamed as Viksit Bharat-Guarantee for R\n(VB-G RAM G). Now, this Act offers employment for a minimum period of 125 days,\nicevika Mission (Gramin)\n-lier 100 days.]\n290\n69,1 Some Recent Initiatives by the Government\nEmployment\n(a) Aatmanirbhar Bharat Rozgar Yojana (ABRY): Launched in 2020, this Yojana was to incentivise\nemployers for creation of new employment and restoration of loss of employment during\nCOVID-19 pandemic.\n(b) Pradhan Mantri Rozgar Protsahan Yojana (PMRPY): This Yojana was launched with effec\nfrom 1st April, 2016. Like ABRY, this was also meant to incentivise employers for creation of\nnew employment.\n» However, in this programme, the government was to bear the burden of employers' EPS\ncontribution (of 12 per cent) for the first three years of employment. To be noted, this\nscheme was to cover only semi-skilled and unskilled workers.\n› The beneficiaries registered upto 31st March, 2019 will continue to receive the benefit\nfor three years from the date of registration under the scheme, i.e., upto 31st March, 2022.\n(c) Pradhan Mantri Garib Kalyan Rozgar Abhiyaan (PMGKRA): It was a 125-day Abhiyaan\nlaunched by the Prime Minister (on 20th June, 2020) with a mission to address the issues\nof returnee migrant workers and similarly affected rural population by COVID-19 pandemic\nthrough.\n> A multi-pronged strategy was launched of providing immediate employment and\nlivelihood opportunities to the distressed.\n(d) Deendayal Antyodaya Yojana -National Urban Livelihoods Mission (DAY-NULM): This was\nto reduce poverty and vulnerability of the urban poor households by enabling them to access\ngainful self-employment and skilled wage employment opportunities.\n(e) Pradhan Mantri MUDRA Yojana (PMMY): This scheme was launched by the Hon'ble Prime\nMinister on April 8, 2015 for providing loans up to & 10 lakh to the non-corporate, non-farm\nsmall/micro enterprises.\n* These loans are classified as MUDRA loans under PMMY. These loans are given by\nCommercial Banks, RRBs, Small Finance Banks, MFls and NBFCs.\n(f) Pradhan Mantri Kaushal Vikas Yojana (PMKVY): This is the flagship scheme of the Ministry of\nSkill Development and Entrepreneurship (MSDE) implemented by National Skill Development\nCorporation (NSDC).\n> The objective of this Skill Certification scheme is to enable Indian youth to take up industry\nrelevant skill training that will help them in securing a better livelihood.\nHIGHLIGHTS\nMake in India and Start-up India\n• Make in India and Start-up India are the two missionary schemes launched by the government in the\nrecent years.\n• Launched on September 25, 2014, Make in India focuses on developing India as an epicentre of industrial\nproduction. Exnansion of production activity is expected to generate opportunity of employment.\n291","file":"chap6.pdf"},{"id":"ied-6-01","image":"ied-pages/ied-6-01.jpg","text":"Indian Economic Development\n• Start-up India is a flagship initiative by the government to explore entrepreneurial skill in the country. tie\nexpected to generate opportunities of self-employment and consequently, opportunities of employmen\nfor others.\nSkill India\nSTARTUP\nSTAND UP INDIA\nINDIA\nRole of Government in Employment Generation\n• Governments, both at the Central level as well as State level, play an important role in generating\nemployment opportunities, directly and indirectly.\n• Government creates employment directly by employing people in public sector enterprises like\nIOC (Indian Oil Corporation) and ONGC (Oil and Natural Gas Corporation), government run industries,\nhotels, and transport companies.\n• When the output of goods and services from government owned enterprises rises, the output of private\nenterprises (which receive raw material from government enterprises) also tends to rise. Rise in output\nleads to a rise in employment opportunities in the economy. Thus, the government, in this case,\ngenerates employment opportunities indirectly.\n• For example, when a government owned steel company increases its output, it will result in a direct\nincrease in employment in that government company. Simultaneously, private companies, which\npurchase steel from it, will also increase their output and thus employment. This is the indirect generation\nof employment opportunities by the government initiatives in the economy.\nPower Points [Summary of the Chapter]\n• Worker: A worker is an individual who is in some employment to earn a living.\n• Self-employed and Hired Workers:\n• Self-employed workers are those who are engaged in their own business/profession.\n• Hired workers are those who work for a wage/salary.\nCasual and Regular Workers:\n• Casual workers are like daily-wagers; not hired on regular basis.\n• Regular workers are hired on regular basis; are on permanent rolls of the employers.\nLabour Supply: It refers to amount of labour (hours of works that the neonle are willing to sunaluat\ncorresponding to a given wage rate. Labour supply changes in response to change in wage rate.\n• Labour Force: It refers to the number of people who are able to work and willing to work at the existing\n• Workforce: It refers to number of people actually in employment.\nParticipation Rate:\nTotal Workforce\nTotal Population\n× 100\nEmployment\n• Occupational Structure: Primary sector 46.1 per cent, secondary sector 24.9 per cent, tertiary sector\n* Occupational structure in India shows only a modest change over time.\n• High percentage of employment in primary sector points to economic backwardness.\nJobless Growth: It refers to a situation when GDP grows faster than the opportunities of employment\nresulting in unemployment.\nCasualisation and Informalisation of Employment:\n• Casualisation refers to a situation when the percentage of casually-hired workers in the total workforce\ntends to rise over time.\n• Informalisation refers to a situation when people tend to find employment more in unorganised (or\ninformal) sector of the economy, and less in organised (or formal) sector of the economy.\n• Unemployment: It is a situation when people are willing to work at the existing wage rate, and are able\nto work, but are not getting work.\nImportant Types of Unemployment in India:\n• Disguised unemployment is a situation when more people are engaged in a production activity than\nactually required.\n• Seasonal unemployment occurs because of seasonal nature of crop production.\nCauses of Unemployment in India:\n(i) Slow economic growth\n(ii) Rapid growth of population\n(i) Agriculture —a seasonal occupation\n(iv) Lack of irrigation facilities\n(v) Joint family system\n(vi) Decay of cottage and small industries\n(vil) Low savings and investment\n(vili) Limited mobility of labour\nGovernment and Employment Generation:\n• Existing Programmes (Presently Non-active):\n0) Prime Minister's Rozgar Yojana (PMRY)\n(i) Swarna Jayanti Shahri Rozgar Yojana (SJSRY)\n(i) Rural Employment Generation Programme (REGP)\n(iv) Prime Minister's Employment Generation Programme (PMEGP)\n(v) Swarnajayanti Gram Swarozgar Yojana (SGSY)\n• Existing Programmes (Presently Active):\n(i) Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Act (MGNREGA)\n(i) National Rural Livelihood Mission (NRLM)\n(iii) Pradhan Mantri Gram Sadak Yojana\nSome Recent Initiatives by the Government:\n(i)\nAatmanirbhar Bharat Rozgar Yojana (ABRY)\n(ii) Pradhan Mantri Rozgar Protsahan Yojana (PMRPY)\n(ii) Pradhan Mantri Garib Kalyan Rozgar Abhiyaan (PMGKRA)\n(iv) Deendayal Antyadaya Yojana - National Urban Livelihoods Mission (DAY-NULM)\n(v) Pradhan Mantri MUDRA Yojana (PMMY)\n(vi) Pradhan Mantri Kaushal Vikas Yojana (PMKVY)\n293","file":"chap6.pdf"}],"boxes":[{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-6-13","image":"ied-pages/ied-6-13.jpg","text":"A Note of Caution\n\"All those who are engaged in economic activities, in whatever capacity-high or low, are workers.\n- NCERT Text (Page 94)\n• Read this statement with a note of caution.\n- The authors (VK Publications) are of the opinion that economic activities include all those activities\nwhich are related to the use of scarce resources. Like production (value-addition), consumption is\nalso an economic activity, as it is related to the use of scarce resources. The fact is that while all\nproduction activities are economic activities, all economic activities are not necessarily production\nactivities.\n- Accordingly, it would be appropriate to define a worker as the one who is engaged in production\nactivity the activity that leads to value-addition.\n- Consumption is certainly an economic activity, but a person engaged in consumption (called\nconsumer) is certainly not a worker.\n6.1.2 Workers and Employment\n• The nature of employment in India is multifaceted. Some workers get emnloyment\nthroughout the year while some others get employment only for a few months in a year.\nIrrespective of their duration of employment, those engaged in value-addition procees are trested\nas workers.\n• Workers need not necessarily be emploved by others and paid wages as a reward. Even those who\nare self-employed (and not getting wages) are also workers. Simply because they are engaged in\nthe 'value-adding process'. This brings us to the distinction between 'self-employed' and hired\nworkers', as in the following section.\n6.1.3 Self-employed and Hired Workers\nThe distinction between self-employed hired worker chanted\nnext page:\n1. Value-addition - economic value added to a product before it is sold in the\n268\nEmployment\nTypes of Workers\nSelf employed Workers\n• These are those\nworkers who are\nengaged in their\nown business or\nown profession.\n• Examples: A farmer\nworking on his\nown farm, or a\nshopkeeper working\nin his own cement\nshop.\nHired Workers\n• These are those workers who work for\nothers; they render their services to\nothers and as a reward, get ages/salaries.\nOr, may be they are paid in kind.\n• Examples: A proof-reader working in a\npublication house, or a teacher working\nin a school.\nCasual Workers\n• These are the daily wagers. They are not\nhired by their employers on regular basis.\n• They are not given social security\nbenefits, like provident fund, gratuity\nor pension.\n• Causal workers are usually unskilled\nworkers, like a worker working at the\nconstruction site.\nRegular Workers\n• These are on permanent pay-roll of\ntheir employers.\n• They are entitled to all social security\nbenefits including pension, gratuity\nand provident fund.\n• Regular workers are usually skilled\nworkers like an engineer working in a\nfactory.\n6.1.4 Labour Supply, Labour Force, Workforce,\nUnemployment and Participation Rate\n• Labour supply refers to the amount of labour that the workers are willing to offer corresponding\nto a particular wage rate. You may be able to work 10 hours a day, but willing to work only for\n6 hours a day at a particular wage.\n- Labour supply, thus, is measured in terms of man-hours of work (or man-days, considering\n8 man hours equal to one man-day. It is always estimated in relation to wage rate.\n• Labour force, on the other hand, refers to the number of workers actually working or willing to\nwork. It is not related to wage rate.\nTable 6.1: Labour Supply vs. Labour Force\nBasis of Difference\n1. Meaning\nLabour Force\nLabour Supply\n• It refers to supply of labour corresponding\n• It refers to the number of persons actually\nto different wage rates.\nworking or willing to work.\n• Supply of labour is measured in terms of\n• It is not related to wage rate.\nman-days of work and is always related to\nwage rate.\n2. Impact of\nChange in\nNumber of\nWorkers\n• Supply of labour can increase or decrease\n• Size of labour force increases or decreases\neven when the number of workers remains\nonly when the number of persons actually\nworking or willing to work increases or\nconstant.\n• Because the supply of labour is measured\ndecreases.\nin terms of man-days or person-days: one\n• Because it is measured in terms of the number\nperson-day referring to 8 hours of work.\nof persons (not in terms of person-days).\n269"},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-6-12","image":"ied-pages/ied-6-12.jpg","text":"• Low employment among women is a sign of social and economic backwardness of a nation.\n• Economic backwardness, because the economy fails to generate enough jobs.\n• Social backwardness, because job work for the women (outside their homes) is considered as a social\ntaboo.\nAVLR 6.2\nREASONS FOR LOW FEMALE WORKFORCE PARTICIPATION IN INDIA-\nRURAL vs. URBAN"},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-6-11","image":"ied-pages/ied-6-11.jpg","text":"Why do People Work and Why Should We Study about Working People?\n• Three factors explain why do people work:\n- People work to earn a living. It is essential for existence in a system of mutual interdependences.\n- Employment infuses in us a sense of self-esteem, and brings to the fore our sense of self-worth.\n- Employment contributes to GDP of the nation, and contributing to GDP gives us a sense of global pride\nbecause growth of GDP implies growth of the nation.\n• Four factors explain why studying about working people (or studving about employment is\n- Studying about working people offers insights into the quality and quantity of our human resource or\nhuman capital, which is an important determinant of economic growth.\n- Emplovment study helps analyse the significance of different sectore Primary Congra, and Tortia,\ncontributes how much towards GDP.\n- It helps analyse many social issues such as social security of the workers, work environment.\nexploitation of marginalised sections of the society, child labour, etc.\n- It offers inputs for manpower planning. Understandino the nature and autont of evicting amaloument.\nwe can plan for our future requirement of manpower across different sectors of the economy.\n& AVLR 6.1\nBASIC CONCEPTS RELATING TO UNEMPLOYMENT\n2. Unemployment - number of persons who are able to work and willing to work, but not getting work.\n270\nEmployment"},{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"ied-6-11","image":"ied-pages/ied-6-11.jpg","text":"Q. 1. Clacsify the following as self-employed, regular salaried emplovee and casual wage labourer\n(i) Owner of a boutique.\n(i) Labourer working at a jute mill who is a daily wager but hired on regular basis.\n(ii) Clerk at Bank of Maharashtra.\n(iv) Labourer hired from the labour contract site to clean the road.\n(v) Teacher of a private school who gets monthly salary and has been working for the\npast 2 years on permanent roll.\n(vi) An ice cream seller running his own ice cream van near a school.\n(vil) A receptionist working as a full-time employee in an office.\n(vili) Workers hired during Diwali season to fulfil the excess demand of peak season.\n(ix) A lecturer hired as a permanent faculty member of a university.\n(x) Producer of a film working in his own movie.\nAns. Self-employed: (i), (vi), (x)\nRegular Salaried Employee: (ii), (iii), (v), (vii), (ix)\nCasual Wage Labourer: (iv), (viii)\nQ. 2. Self-employed are different from hired workers. Is it true?\nAns. Yes, it is true. Self-employed workers are those workers who are engaged in their own business\nor profession while hired workers are those workers who work for others and get wages and\nQ. 3. Can labour supply change even when the number of workers remain constant? If yes, state why?\nAns. Yes, as labour supply is measured in terms of man-days of work.\n0. 4. State two reasons why people engage themselves in work.\n(i) To earn a living, and"},{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"ied-6-10","image":"ied-pages/ied-6-10.jpg","text":"Q. 5. What does low level of employment among women indicate?\nAns. It indicates economic and social backwardness.\nQ. 6. Why is the rate of participation low in the case of urban women?\nAns. High priority for education in comparison to jobs at an early age results in low participation rate\namong urban women.\nQ. 7. State the principal reason behind high incidence of female workers of rural areas in primary sector.\nAns. Female workers in rural areas are unwilling to migrate for jobs outside their villages."},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-6-09","image":"ied-pages/ied-6-09.jpg","text":"Under-estimation of Women Workers in the Country\n• There is a significant under-estimation of women workers in the country. This is because:\n- Women rendering household services as housewives are not deemed as workers. Because (a) they\nare not paid for it, and (b) it is difficult to ascertain market value of the services rendered by the\nhousewives.\n- Women rendering services on the family farms in rural areas and in the family business in the urban\nareas are often not treated as workers, largely because of the convention of not considering unpaid\nwomen as workers, even when unpaid men working on family farms or in family business are\nconsidered as employed and therefore as workers."},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-6-09","image":"ied-pages/ied-6-09.jpg","text":"• In India, self-employment is a significant source of livelihood. Because:\n- Literacy rate is low, implying low acquisition of skill for wage jobs.\n- Owing to diverse\nvironment across different regions, mobility of workers is low.\nny, generating less jobs owing to low level of investment.\n- India is a dev\n275"},{"title":"The tabular presentation, given below, highlights these observations along with the valid reason","page":"ied-6-08","image":"ied-pages/ied-6-08.jpg","text":"URBAN WORKFORCE\n(bent towards tertiary sector)\nRURAL WORKFORCE\n(bent towards primary sector)\n1. Observation • In India, trade, commerce, banking, insurance, • Rural workforce shows a sinificant bot\nhealth, education, tourism and related services\nare developing faster than manufacturing and\ntowards 'primary sector of employment.\nallied production activities.\n• These activities represent 'Tertiary sector\nof employment. Urban workforce shows a\nsignificant bent towards the tertiary sector of\nemployment.\n2. Reason\n• This may largely be explained in terms of\n(i) Lack of non-farm job opportunities in the\nglobalisation of the economy, besides the\nrural areas.\nfact that people in urban areas are becoming\nincreasingly conscious of their quality of life,\n(ii) Lack of mobility (or limited mobility) owing\nspending the bulk of their income on a variety\nto family/social constraints (particularly for\nwomen workers).\nof services (like health, education and tourism).\n6.5.2 Distribution of Male-Female Workforce across Different Sectors\nFig. 6.5 shows male-female workforce across different sectors of the economy.\nMale Workforce\nFemale Workforce\n32.2%%\n24.7%\nPrimary Sector\nSecondary Sector\nTertiary Sector\n37.5%\n64.4%\n29.39\n19.3%\nFig. 6.5 offers the following two observations:\nFig. 6.5: Distribution of Male-Female Workforce across Different Sectors of the Economy (2022-23)\n(a) Primary activities of production are confined largely to rural areas, and\nMobility of wore in rural areas extremely wOwing the and corl\nconstraints, female workers would prefer to do jobs dose to their homes, even when\nwage rate is low.\nEmployment\n(i) Percentage of men finding employment in primary sector (37.5 per cent) is only next to that of\nwomen. It is also fairly low. It is explained in terms of the lack of jobs outside agriculture in the\nrural areas. Men get more employment opportunities in both secondary and tertiary sectors\n6,5.3 Change in Occupational Structure\nTable 6.3 shows change in occupational structure in India over the period 1972-73 to 2022-23.\nTable 6.3: Change in Occupational Structure (1972-73 to 2022-23)\nSector\nPercentage of Workforce\nPrimary\nSecondary\nTertiary\nTotal\n100\n100\n2022-23\n46.1\n24.9\n29.0\n100\nSource: NCERT, 2025-26\nObservations\n• Table 6.3 shows that between the period 1972-73 to 2022-23:\n- percentage of workforce engaged in primary sector has declined from 74.3 per cent to 46.1 per cent.\n- percentage of workforce engaged in secondary sector has risen from 10.9 per cent to 24.9 per cent.\n- percentage of workforce engaged in tertiary sector has risen from 14.8 per cent to 29.0 per cent.\nDoes it mean that we have become an industrial economy (an essential feature of a developed\neconomy)?\nAnswer is: Not at all.\n- Changes in occupational structure no way indicates the shift in epicentre (hub of economic activity)\nof the economy-from primary to secondary sector. So long as the epicentre does not shift (or so\nlong as primary sector continues to dominate as principal source of employment), Indian economy\nwould continue to marked as agricultural economy?, rather than an industrial economy.\n• Historical experience of the developed countries suggests a predominant shift of workforce from\nprimary sector to secondary and tertiary sectors of employment. The shift has to be so prominent\nthat industrial sector emerges as the principal source of employment, or a principal source of\nlivelihood. But the Indian experience presents only a dismal picture.\n- The shift is so modest that we can at best call ourselves a developing economy rather than a\ndeveloped economy."},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-6-08","image":"ied-pages/ied-6-08.jpg","text":"Work at Home\n• Earlier work at home was associated with traditional work like weaving, lace making, etc.\n• It was only after the technological revolution that work at home' became a diversified term associated\nwith tech-enabled IT sector as well.\n• This has in fact become a common household name ever since the nationwide lockdown was imposed\nin our country amidst the outbreak of COVID-19 pandemic in 2020-21.\n- an economy where agriculture is the principal source of occupation.\n9. Agricultural e\n279"},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-6-07","image":"ied-pages/ied-6-07.jpg","text":"Jobless growth is a situation when the level of output in the economy tends to rise owing to innovative\ntechnology without any perceptible (noticeable) rise in the level of employment.\n6.6.1 The Gulf between GDP Growth and Employment Growth\n• The trend path of GDP and employment during the period 1950-51 to 2022-23 is reflected in\nFig. 6.6 as under:\nGDP\n1o Employment\n0.39\n0.85\n203199184 173189 13 0.98\n3.2\n0.28\n1.12\nFig 6.6 offers the following observations:\nFig. 6.6: Percentage Growth Rate of GDP and Employment (1951-2023)\nGDP growth rate recorded a rise from 3.6 per cent in 1951 to 87 percent in 1000-000 # chowed\n10. Chronic unemployment - persistent unemployment over an extended period of time.\na modest slump during the period 2005-2023, and in 2022-23 it was recorded to be 7 per cent.\n280\n* Paradoxically, growth rate of employment recorded a much milder escalation from 0.39 percent\nin 1951 to 1.12 per cent in 2012, and further to 3.2 per cent in 2022-23\nThere was a period (late 1990s) when the growth of employment slumped to as low as 0.28 per\ncent, pointing to the staggering gap when GDP growth was of 8.7 per cent.\nIndia is thus facing an expanding gulf between 'GDP growth' and 'employment growth, and is\nconsenuently slipping into the vicious cycle of liobless growth', thanks to technology overwhelmingly\n6.6.2 Why are we relying more on Technology, and less on Labour?\n* our growth process is being increasingly hijacked by MNCs (multinational companies). These\ncompanies specialise in achieving high growth through efficient use of technology rather than\nthrough greater use of manpower.\n• The obvious result is that growth is moving faster than the opportunities of employment. By and\nlarge, it is a 'jobless growth'.\nJAVLR 6.3\nGROWTH SANS EMPLOYMENT"},{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"ied-6-07","image":"ied-pages/ied-6-07.jpg","text":"9.8. Secondary sector is taking a lead over tertiary sector as a source of employment in urban\nAns. It is wrong to say that secondary sector is taking a lead over tertiary sector as a source of\nemployment in urban areas. In fact, tertiary sector is taking a lead over secondary sector as a\nsource of employment in urban areas. Secondary sector lagging behind the tertiary sector points\nto the fact that industrialisation has failed to take-off to become the leading sector of growth.\nQ.9. \"The Indian unemployment rate is hovering around 7-8% according to the Centre for Monitoring\nIndian Economy. This is because job growth has not kept pace with GDP growth.\"\nSource: timesofindia.indiatimes.com\nWhat is the situation called when growth of jobs is not in sync with GDP growth?\nAns. Jobless growth, as there is GDP growth in the economy but no substantial growth in the level of"},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-6-06","image":"ied-pages/ied-6-06.jpg","text":"Formal Sector Employment\n• The data relating to formal sector employment is collected by the Union Ministry of Labour through\nemployment exchanges located across different parts of the country.\n• In India, public sector is the largest employer in the formal sector.\n• In 2012, out of 30 million formal sector workers, about 18 million workers were employed in public\n• Also, men form the majority, as women constitute only about 17 per cent or one-sixth of the formal\nsector workforce.\n• From the viewpoint of employment status, the underlying difference between formal and informal\nsectors (or between organised and unorganised sectors) is that:\n(i) Workers in the formal sector are entitled to social security benefits (such as provident fund,\ngratuity, pension, etc.) while workers in the informal sector are not.\n11. Disanisediv unemployed - a situation when the person seems to be employed but is not contributing to the output, i.e.,\nhis marginal productivity is zero.\n12. Hire and fire-emnlaving new staff and dismissing existing staff.\n283"},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-6-05","image":"ied-pages/ied-6-05.jpg","text":"• İn essence, formal workers are like regular workers, while informal workers are like daily-wagers.\n• Uncertainty of employment is always a lingering threat to the informal workers.\n• An informal worker is more likely to be 'casual' as well.\n• Informalisation leads to casualisation.\nInformalisation of Workforce in India\n• Meaning: Informalisation of workforce is defined as a situation where percentage of workforce\nin the formal sector tends to decline and that in the informal sector tends to rise.\n• Evolution: In accordance with socialistic pattern of society13 (as a central goal of Development\nPlanning launched in 1951), 'formal sector employment' should have risen and informal sector\nemployment' should have declined over time. Alas! iust the onposite has hannened in the\ndevelopment since 1991.\n13. Socialist pattern of society - a pattern of society which gives more importance to collective c\nplanning and social welfare.\neconomic\n284\nEmployment\n» Major Cause: A series Of economic reforms were launched in 1991, with liberalisation, privatisation\nand globalisation' as their key elements. Consequently, there is a significant transformation from a\ncontrolled economy' to 'market economy.\n- And, in this process of transformation there has been a significant drift towards informalisation\nbut art pronomy and informalication of workers nerhans, are strongly correlated\nto each other. Informalisation is driven by the forces of supply and demand in the labour market.\n• Data: Presently, formal sector workers comes to merely 30 million, i.e., 6 per cent of the total\nworkforce (473 million workers); rest 443 million, i.e., 94 per cent of the workfore continues to\nbe informal.\n- Out of the 30 million formal sector workers, only 6 million, i..., 20 per cent are women.\n- In the informal sector, 70 percent of the workforce are male and 30 per cent of the workforce\nare women.\nFormal Sector\nInformal Sector\nMale\nFemale\n310\n24\nFig. 6.7: Workers in Formal and Informal Sector, 2011-12 (in million)\n• Implication: This points to growing vulnerability of the workforce to uncertainties of the market\neconomy.\n- However, in view of the rising vulnerability of the workers in the informal sector, the government\nin India has recently initiated several measures (by way of Labour Codes 2025) to improve\nworking conditions of these workers. These measures include:\n(i) universal social security for Gig/Platform workers\"4,\n(i) free health checks for workers beyond 40 years of age,\n(iii) expanded protection for the women on night duties, and\n(iv) mandatory appointment letters for all workers including Gig/Platform workers (defining\ntheir wages and terms & conditions of work)."},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-6-05","image":"ied-pages/ied-6-05.jpg","text":"• A staggering number of 44 crore workers working in informal or unorganised sector of the economy\nPoints to growing vulnerability of the workforce to uncertainties of the market.\n• Informalisation causes poverty and fragmentation of social harmony. In the early 1980s, closure of\nmore than 60 textile mills in Ahmedabad (offering employment to nearly 1,50,000 workers) and the\nconsequent riots are a testimony to this assertion.\no 'on demand' or temporary workers.\n14. Gig/Platform wc\n285"},{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"ied-6-04","image":"ied-pages/ied-6-04.jpg","text":"Q. 10. Classify the following as formal sector or informal sector workers:\n(i) A nurse at a private hospital which has a staff of 25 people.\n(ii) Rickshaw puller.\n(iii) The owner of a textile shop employing 9 people.\n(iv) Civil engineer employed at a construction company having 10 workers.\n(v) A police constable.\nAns. Formal Sector Workers: (i), (iv), (v)\nInformal Sector Workers: (ii), (i)\nQ. 11. In India, casualisation of workforce has tended to rise over time. Is it true?\nAns. Yes, it is true. In India, casualisation of workforce has tended to rise, particularly during the\nperiod 1972-73 to 1993-94. During this period, casualisation increased from 23.2 per cent to\n31.8 per cent. This is because of massive unemployment in the country and consequently, low\nbargaining power of the employees in relation to the employers."},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-6-04","image":"ied-pages/ied-6-04.jpg","text":"Data Source for Unemployment\n• The major sources of data on unemployment are:\n- Reports of Census of India,\n- National Statistical Office's Reports of Employment and Unemployment Situation,\n- Annual Reports of Periodic Labour Force Survey, and\n- Directorate General of Employment and Training Data of Registration with Employment Exchanges.\nEmployment\n6.8.1 Types of Unemployment\nfollowing are the two principal types of unemployment found in India:\nTwo Principal Types of Unemployment in India\nDisguised Unemployment\nSeasonal Unemployment\nDetails are as under:\n(a) Disguised Unemployment: Disguised\nunemployment\noccurs\nwhen\nthe\nnumber of workers engaged in a job\nis much more than actually required\nto accomplish it. If some of them\nare withdrawn from that job, total\nproduction will not fall. The below given\nexample illustrates this situation.\nDisguised Unemployment: Apparently employed\nbut actually not\nExample:\nSuppose a farmer has four acres of land, but he actually needs only two workers and himself\nto carry out various operations on his farm in a year. However, if he employs five workers\nthere, then two persons are disguised unemployed.\n> Disguised unemployment is a common form of unemployment found in rural India.\n> Disguised unemployment is rampant (widespread) in Indian agriculture. A study conducted\nin the late 1950s, revealed that about one-third of agriculture workers in India were\ndisguisedly unemployed.\n> The three principal causes of disguised unemployment are as under:\n(i) On account of joint family system, farming families continue to work on family land, no\nmatter the actual number of workers far exceeds the required number.\n(ii) Per person holding size continues to shrink with the expansion of the family size.\nImplying greater possibility of disguised unemployment.\n(iii) Lack of job opportunities outside agriculture compels the people to work on family\nfarms. It further increases the possibility of disguised unemployment."},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-6-04","image":"ied-pages/ied-6-04.jpg","text":"Disguised Unemployment is not prevalent in Agriculture alone\n• Disguised unemployment is a characteristic feature of the Indian agriculture. It is almost rampant there\nOwing to a heavy pressure of population, joint family system and the lack of vocational avenues outside\nagriculture. But it is a phenomenon not confined to agriculture alone.\n• Have a deeper look into the functioning of the public sector enterprises. It will not be surprising to find\nlots of persons idling all the time. It is a pointer to the fact that more people are employed than actually\ns a sign of disguised unemployment.\nneeded. S\n287"},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-6-03","image":"ied-pages/ied-6-03.jpg","text":"Full Employment does not mean Zero Unemployment\n• No, full employment does not mean a situation of zero unemployment.\n• In an economy, there is always some natural rate of unemployment, which is the minimum rate of\nunemployment caused by structural changes in the system of production.\n6.8.2 Causes of Unemployment in India\nThe main causes of unemployment in India are highlighted through the following flow chart:\nCauses of Unemployment in India\n(a)\nSlow\nEconomic\nGrowth\n(e)\n(f)\n(g)\n(h)\nRapid\nGrowth of\nPopulation\nAgriculture\n-A Seasonal\nOccupation\nLack of\nIrrigation\nFacilities\nJoint\nFamily\nSystem\nDecay of\nCottage and\nSmall\nIndustries\nLow\nSavings and\nInvestment\nLimited\nMobility of\nLabour\nFollowing is a detailed explanation of the main causes of ever-increasing unemployment in India:\n(a) Slow Economic Growth: Economic growth (in terms of annual increase in GDP size) is too\nslow to absorb the expanding supply of labour. Hence, rising unemployment.\n(b) Rapid Growth of Population: Constantly rising population has been a grave nroblem in India.\nIt is one of the principal factors contributing to unemployment.\n15. Seasonal unemployment - a type of unemployment that occurs at particular times of the year when demand for labou\nis lower than usual.\n16. Structural changes - in the context of zero unemployment, structural changes refer primarilto chance on taken\nor pattern of demand.\nTAStew economic crowth COP in india is growing at the rate off% derant which be a\ndollars in USA. In terms of annual increase in GDP size, 6% of 4 trillion dollars is far less than 30% of 25 trillion deltars\nThus, 6% GDP growth rate in India is low in the sense that the existing GDP base is very low, because which\nunemployment.\nexpansion of production (during a year) remains inadequate to absorb the expanding labour supply. Hence, rising\n288\nEmployment\n* Despite the completion of Twelve Five Year Plans, the size of unemployment has continued\n(d) Agriculture- A Seasonal Occupation: Agriculture is underdeveloped in India and so offers\nseasonal employment. No doubt it is the primary occupation of our country and a large size\nof the population depends upon it, but its seasonal character does not provide stable jobs to\nthe farmers throughout the year.\n* Farmers often find no opportunity to work for three to four months in a year. The size\nof disguisedly unemployed is estimated to be nearly 15 per cent of the total working\npopulation in agriculture.\n(d) Lack of Irrigation Facilities: Despite decades of planning, irrigation facilities continue to be\nscant, covering only about 44 per cent of agriculture area.\n› For want of irrigation, mono-cropping (only one crop a year) becomes a compulsion. In the\nabsence of multi-cropping, opportunities of employment remain limited.\n(e) Joint Family System: It encourages disguised unemployment. In joint families, there is a high\ntendency to survive on joint income without work.\n> Joint family system is more prevalent in rural areas; hence a high degree of disguised\nunemployment in these areas.\n(f) Decay of Cottage and Small Industries: Decay of cottage and small industries during the\nBritish regime has given a big blow to the potential of self-employment in India.\n> Emergence of large industry in place of the small industry after independence has failed to\nyield employment opportunities to the desired extent.\n(g) Low Savings and Investment: There is scarcity of capital and even the scarce capital has not\nbeen optimally used to eradicate unemployment.\n> Bulk of the capital has been invested in large-scale industries where there is high capital\nper unit of labour. Employment of labour per unit of capital remains significantly low.\n(h) Limited Mobility of Labour: Mobility of labour in India is very low. Owing to a variety of family\nas well as social constraints, people are reluctant (unwilling) to move to far-off areas even\nwhen jobs are available there.\n> Factors like diversity of language, religion and customs in different states are a major\nhindrance to the mobility of labour. Lesser the mobility, greater the unemployment.\nBriefly, whereas low rate of economic growth is generating\nonly limited employment\nopportunities, high rate of population growth is causing an exponential rise in the demand for jobs.\nConsequently, the gulf between the supply of labour and the demand for labour is tending to be very\nlarge.\nIn fact, unemployment is assuming monstrous proportions and is an emerging threat to social\nharmony in the country.\n289"},{"title":"CRITICAL QUESTIONS","page":"ied-6-02","image":"ied-pages/ied-6-02.jpg","text":"Q. 12. What are the two ways of tackling disguised unemployment in agriculture?\nAns.\n(i) Integrated rural development offering non-farm occupation in the villages.\n(ii) Development of urban industry that induces rural-urban migration.\nQ. 13. Disguised unemployment lowers efficiency/productivity. Write your views.\nAns. This is absolutely true that disguised unemployment lowers efficiency/productivity. As a mati\nof fact, disguised unemployment is defined as a situation when marginal productivity of labou\nis zero or negative. In a situation of disguised unemployment, number of workers tends to\nlarge in relation to land and capital, that productivity of labour (both marginal as well as average\nis bound to be low."},{"title":"HIGHLIGHTS","page":"ied-6-02","image":"ied-pages/ied-6-02.jpg","text":"Make in India and Start-up India\n• Make in India and Start-up India are the two missionary schemes launched by the government in the\nrecent years.\n• Launched on September 25, 2014, Make in India focuses on developing India as an epicentre of industrial\nproduction. Exnansion of production activity is expected to generate opportunity of employment.\n291"},{"title":"Power Points [Summary of the Chapter]","page":"ied-6-01","image":"ied-pages/ied-6-01.jpg","text":"• Worker: A worker is an individual who is in some employment to earn a living.\n• Self-employed and Hired Workers:\n• Self-employed workers are those who are engaged in their own business/profession.\n• Hired workers are those who work for a wage/salary.\nCasual and Regular Workers:\n• Casual workers are like daily-wagers; not hired on regular basis.\n• Regular workers are hired on regular basis; are on permanent rolls of the employers.\nLabour Supply: It refers to amount of labour (hours of works that the neonle are willing to sunaluat\ncorresponding to a given wage rate. Labour supply changes in response to change in wage rate.\n• Labour Force: It refers to the number of people who are able to work and willing to work at the existing\n• Workforce: It refers to number of people actually in employment.\nParticipation Rate:\nTotal Workforce\nTotal Population\n× 100\nEmployment\n• Occupational Structure: Primary sector 46.1 per cent, secondary sector 24.9 per cent, tertiary sector\n* Occupational structure in India shows only a modest change over time.\n• High percentage of employment in primary sector points to economic backwardness.\nJobless Growth: It refers to a situation when GDP grows faster than the opportunities of employment\nresulting in unemployment.\nCasualisation and Informalisation of Employment:\n• Casualisation refers to a situation when the percentage of casually-hired workers in the total workforce\ntends to rise over time.\n• Informalisation refers to a situation when people tend to find employment more in unorganised (or\ninformal) sector of the economy, and less in organised (or formal) sector of the economy.\n• Unemployment: It is a situation when people are willing to work at the existing wage rate, and are able\nto work, but are not getting work.\nImportant Types of Unemployment in India:\n• Disguised unemployment is a situation when more people are engaged in a production activity than\nactually required.\n• Seasonal unemployment occurs because of seasonal nature of crop production.\nCauses of Unemployment in India:\n(i) Slow economic growth\n(ii) Rapid growth of population\n(i) Agriculture —a seasonal occupation\n(iv) Lack of irrigation facilities\n(v) Joint family system\n(vi) Decay of cottage and small industries\n(vil) Low savings and investment\n(vili) Limited mobility of labour\nGovernment and Employment Generation:\n• Existing Programmes (Presently Non-active):\n0) Prime Minister's Rozgar Yojana (PMRY)\n(i) Swarna Jayanti Shahri Rozgar Yojana (SJSRY)\n(i) Rural Employment Generation Programme (REGP)\n(iv) Prime Minister's Employment Generation Programme (PMEGP)\n(v) Swarnajayanti Gram Swarozgar Yojana (SGSY)\n• Existing Programmes (Presently Active):\n(i) Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Act (MGNREGA)\n(i) National Rural Livelihood Mission (NRLM)\n(iii) Pradhan Mantri Gram Sadak Yojana\nSome Recent Initiatives by the Government:\n(i)\nAatmanirbhar Bharat Rozgar Yojana (ABRY)\n(ii) Pradhan Mantri Rozgar Protsahan Yojana (PMRPY)\n(ii) Pradhan Mantri Garib Kalyan Rozgar Abhiyaan (PMGKRA)\n(iv) Deendayal Antyadaya Yojana - National Urban Livelihoods Mission (DAY-NULM)\n(v) Pradhan Mantri MUDRA Yojana (PMMY)\n(vi) Pradhan Mantri Kaushal Vikas Yojana (PMKVY)\n293"}]}],"questions":[{"chapter":"1","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Which is a macroeconomic issue?","options":["One household’s saving","Price of one brand of shoes","Output of one firm","General price level"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The general price level is an economy-wide variable.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q1"},{"chapter":"1","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Studying the equilibrium of the steel industry is:","options":["National-income accounting","Microeconomics","Always macroeconomics","Neither branch"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Aggregation alone does not make a study macroeconomic; one industry remains a microeconomic unit.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q2"},{"chapter":"1","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Assertion: More saving by every household can reduce total income. Reason: Lower consumption can reduce aggregate demand.","options":["Both true; reason explains assertion","Assertion false; reason true","Assertion true; reason false","Both true; reason does not explain assertion"],"answer":0,"explanation":"This is the micro–macro paradox under demand-constrained conditions.","tag":"Assertion–reason","id":"q3"},{"chapter":"1","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Partial equilibrium studies:","options":["Only foreign trade","Only government accounts","All markets simultaneously","One market, holding other markets unchanged"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Partial equilibrium is the textbook’s method of microeconomic analysis.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q4"},{"chapter":"2","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"A bakery buys flour to make bread. The flour is:","options":["Final consumption","Fixed investment","A capital good","An intermediate good"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Its end use is as a raw material in current production.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q5"},{"chapter":"2","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"A new machine used repeatedly by a factory is:","options":["A non-durable consumer good","A final capital good","An intermediate good","A transfer payment"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Capital goods are final goods used repeatedly to produce other goods.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q6"},{"chapter":"2","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Which pair consists entirely of stocks?","options":["Capital and wealth","Consumption and expenditure","Income and saving","Investment and output"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Stocks are measured at a point of time; all the other pairs are flows.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q7"},{"chapter":"2","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"If gross investment is less than depreciation, net investment is:","options":["Equal to gross investment","Positive","Zero","Negative"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Net investment = gross investment − depreciation; the capital stock falls.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q8"},{"chapter":"2","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Which is a real flow from households to firms?","options":["Wages","Consumption expenditure","Rent payments","Factor services"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Households supply factor services; firms pay money incomes in the opposite direction.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q9"},{"chapter":"2","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"A car bought for private household use and an identical car bought to run as a taxi are respectively:","options":["Intermediate and final","Capital and consumer goods","Consumer durable and capital good","Both intermediate"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Classification depends on end use, not physical appearance.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q10"},{"chapter":"2","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Closing stock is 80 and opening stock is 110. Inventory investment equals:","options":["80","190","−30","30"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Change in stock = 80 − 110 = −30.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q11"},{"chapter":"3","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"National income is:","options":["NNPFC","GDPMP","GNPMP","NDPMP"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The textbook defines national income as net national product at factor cost.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q12"},{"chapter":"3","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"To convert GDPMP to NDPFC, subtract:","options":["Depreciation and NFIA","Depreciation and NIT","Subsidies only","NFIA only"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Gross → net removes depreciation; market price → factor cost removes NIT.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q13"},{"chapter":"3","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Factor income paid abroad exceeds factor income received from abroad. NFIA is:","options":["Necessarily equal to GDP","Zero","Negative","Positive"],"answer":2,"explanation":"NFIA = factor income from abroad − factor income to abroad.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q14"},{"chapter":"3","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Which belongs to India’s domestic territory?","options":["Every foreign branch owned by an Indian citizen","US embassy in New Delhi","Indian embassy in Japan","An Indian tourist’s hotel room abroad"],"answer":2,"explanation":"An Indian embassy abroad is part of India’s economic territory.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q15"},{"chapter":"3","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"A gift received from a relative abroad is:","options":["Domestic product","A current transfer","Factor income from abroad","Depreciation"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A gift does not reward factor services; it is not NFIA.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q16"},{"chapter":"3","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Nominal GDP rises 10% while quantities are unchanged. This can be explained by:","options":["A fall in all prices","A fall in the GDP deflator","An increase in real output","An increase in prices"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Nominal GDP uses current prices, so price increases can raise it without more output.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q17"},{"chapter":"3","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Which is a limitation of GDP as a welfare index?","options":["It has an accounting period","It ignores income distribution","It includes final goods","It measures current production"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The same GDP can coexist with very different distributions of income.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q18"},{"chapter":"3","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Statement 1: Citizenship and normal residence are identical. Statement 2: Domestic income can include factor income earned by non-residents.","options":["Both false","Only 1 true","Both true","Only 2 true"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Residence depends on economic interest and residence conventions; domestic income depends on territory.","tag":"Two statements","id":"q19"},{"chapter":"4","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Sales are 500, opening stock 40 and closing stock 70. Value of output is:","options":["530","610","500","470"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Output = sales + change in stock = 500 + 70 − 40 = 530.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q20"},{"chapter":"4","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Which is included in compensation of employees?","options":["Employer contribution to provident fund","Dividend","Old-age welfare pension","Lottery winnings"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Employer social-security contributions form part of compensation of employees.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q21"},{"chapter":"4","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Operating surplus excludes:","options":["Interest","Rent","Wages","Profit"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Wages are compensation of employees; operating surplus includes rent, interest and profit.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q22"},{"chapter":"4","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"A dealer sells a second-hand car and charges commission. Current GDP includes:","options":["Resale value only","Commission only","Full resale value and commission","Neither"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The car was counted when first produced; the dealer’s current service is new production.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q23"},{"chapter":"4","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"GDCF is given in an expenditure-method question along with change in stock. You should normally:","options":["Add both in full","Ignore all investment","Deduct change in stock","Use GDCF without adding stock again"],"answer":3,"explanation":"GDCF already includes inventory investment; check the label before adding components.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q24"},{"chapter":"4","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"The same wheat becomes flour and then bread. Counting every stage’s full sales value causes:","options":["Double counting","A transfer payment","NFIA","Depreciation"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Intermediate value is already embodied in the final product.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q25"},{"chapter":"4","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Which purchase is investment expenditure in GDP?","options":["An old bond","Existing shares","A second-hand house’s full value","A newly built house"],"answer":3,"explanation":"New residential construction adds to the capital stock.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q26"},{"chapter":"4","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Arrange the value-added steps: A add NFIA; B calculate output; C deduct intermediate consumption; D deduct depreciation and NIT.","options":["B → C → D → A","C → A → B → D","A → B → C → D","B → D → A → C"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Output minus intermediate consumption gives GVA MP; then convert to national income.","tag":"Sequence","id":"q27"},{"chapter":"5","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Barter requires:","options":["Paper currency","A central bank","Demand deposits","Double coincidence of wants"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Each party must want the good offered by the other.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q28"},{"chapter":"5","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"A price tag of ₹300 illustrates money as:","options":["Store of value","Measure of value","Standard of deferred payment","Transfer of ownership"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Money supplies a common unit of account.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q29"},{"chapter":"5","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Repaying a loan in fixed rupee instalments illustrates:","options":["Barter","Only store of value","Credit rationing","Standard of deferred payments"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Future obligations are expressed and settled in money.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q30"},{"chapter":"5","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Which is included in M1?","options":["Interbank deposits","Shares held by public","Demand deposits with commercial banks","Time deposits only"],"answer":2,"explanation":"M1 includes currency with public, demand deposits and other deposits with RBI.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q31"},{"chapter":"5","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Statement 1: Money supply is a flow. Statement 2: Money held by the public is part of money supply.","options":["Only 2 true","Only 1 true","Both false","Both true"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Money supply is a stock at a point of time.","tag":"Two statements","id":"q32"},{"chapter":"5","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Liquidity refers to:","options":["Face value of a note","Rate of inflation","Amount of government debt","Convertibility into cash"],"answer":3,"explanation":"An asset is more liquid when it converts to cash more readily.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q33"},{"chapter":"5","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"A cheque is:","options":["A second stock of money in addition to the deposit","Unlimited legal tender in every transaction","A capital good","An instruction to transfer a bank deposit"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Counting the cheque and the underlying deposit separately would duplicate the same money.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q34"},{"chapter":"6","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"With a 20% reserve ratio, the simple deposit multiplier is:","options":["2","20","0.2","5"],"answer":3,"explanation":"1 ÷ 0.20 = 5 under the simple model’s assumptions.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q35"},{"chapter":"6","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"RBI buys government securities from the market. Other things equal, bank liquidity:","options":["Rises","Becomes zero","Is necessarily unchanged","Falls"],"answer":0,"explanation":"RBI purchases inject liquidity, supporting credit creation.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q36"},{"chapter":"6","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Which action can restrict credit?","options":["Purchase securities","Raise margin requirement","Lower CRR","Lower repo rate"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A larger margin means a smaller loan for the same collateral.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q37"},{"chapter":"6","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"CRR reserves are maintained:","options":["Only with the government treasury","Only as gold in each bank","With the borrower","With the RBI"],"answer":3,"explanation":"CRR is the required percentage of deposits kept with the central bank.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q38"},{"chapter":"6","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"An RBI emergency loan to a bank unable to borrow elsewhere illustrates:","options":["Portfolio investment","Lender of last resort","Tax collection","Banker to government"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The central bank supports banks when normal sources of funds fail.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q39"},{"chapter":"6","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Which is a qualitative instrument?","options":["Rationing of credit","SLR","CRR","Repo rate"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Rationing directs credit to specified uses or sectors.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q40"},{"chapter":"6","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"If banks keep excess reserves or borrowers keep cash, actual deposit expansion is:","options":["Potentially less than the simple maximum","Exactly infinite","Unaffected by leakage","Always greater than 1/reserve ratio"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The maximum assumes all lendable reserves are used and loans are redeposited.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q41"},{"chapter":"7","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"C = 60 + 0.75Y. Autonomous consumption is:","options":["0.25","0.75","60","60Y"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The intercept is consumption at Y = 0.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q42"},{"chapter":"7","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"The slope of the consumption curve equals:","options":["APS","MPS","APC","MPC"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Slope = ΔC/ΔY, which is MPC.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q43"},{"chapter":"7","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"At the break-even point:","options":["MPC = 1 and MPS = 0 necessarily","Saving equals investment necessarily","APC = 0 and APS = 1","APC = 1 and APS = 0"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Break-even means C = Y and S = 0; it does not by itself establish macroeconomic equilibrium.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q44"},{"chapter":"7","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"If APC is 1.2, APS is:","options":["0.2","−0.2","2.2","1.2"],"answer":1,"explanation":"APC + APS = 1, hence APS = −0.2.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q45"},{"chapter":"7","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"In an open economy, AD is:","options":["C + S","Y − S","C + I + G + X + M","C + I + G + X − M"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Net exports are exports minus imports.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q46"},{"chapter":"7","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"With positive autonomous consumption and constant MPC below one, APC normally:","options":["Always equals MPC","Falls with income","Always exceeds two","Rises with income"],"answer":1,"explanation":"APC = C̄/Y + MPC; the first term falls as Y rises.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q47"},{"chapter":"7","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"If S = −80 + 0.2Y, consumption is:","options":["80 + 0.8Y","−80 + 1.2Y","80 + 0.2Y","−80 + 0.8Y"],"answer":0,"explanation":"C = Y − S = 80 + 0.8Y.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q48"},{"chapter":"7","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"At zero income APC is:","options":["Equal to MPC","Zero","Undefined","One"],"answer":2,"explanation":"APC = C/Y cannot be calculated with Y = 0.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q49"},{"chapter":"8","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"If planned AD is below output, unplanned inventories:","options":["Decrease","Stay equal to desired inventories","Become exports automatically","Increase"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Unsold goods accumulate, leading firms to cut production.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q50"},{"chapter":"8","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Equilibrium in the two-sector model requires:","options":["C = Y always","Planned S = planned I","Unemployment = zero","APC = MPC"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Planned saving equals planned investment; full employment is not guaranteed.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q51"},{"chapter":"8","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Ex-post saving and investment are equal because:","options":["Every plan succeeds","Unplanned inventory investment is included","Government balances its budget","MPC is zero"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Realised investment includes unintended changes in stocks.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q52"},{"chapter":"8","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"MPC rises from 0.6 to 0.8. The multiplier:","options":["Falls to 1","Stays at 4","Rises from 2.5 to 5","Falls from 5 to 2.5"],"answer":2,"explanation":"k = 1/(1 − MPC).","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q53"},{"chapter":"8","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Assertion: Equilibrium can coexist with unemployment. Reason: Planned expenditure can equal planned output below capacity.","options":["Assertion false; reason true","Both true; reason explains assertion","Assertion true; reason false","Both true; reason does not explain assertion"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Underemployment equilibrium matches plans without full utilisation.","tag":"Assertion–reason","id":"q54"},{"chapter":"8","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"An investment decline of 40 with MPC 0.75 causes an income change of:","options":["−160","−10","160","−40"],"answer":0,"explanation":"k = 4; ΔY = 4 × (−40) = −160.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q55"},{"chapter":"9","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"The inflationary gap is measured at:","options":["Zero output","Full-employment output","Break-even consumption income only","Any arbitrary income"],"answer":1,"explanation":"It is excess AD over AS at full employment.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q56"},{"chapter":"9","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Which policy helps correct deficient demand?","options":["Raise taxes","Buy securities through OMO","Cut government spending","Raise CRR"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Buying securities injects liquidity and supports lending and spending.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q57"},{"chapter":"9","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Beyond full-employment capacity, excess AD principally raises:","options":["The capital stock immediately","Real output without limit","The general price level","Unemployment by definition"],"answer":2,"explanation":"In the simple model real output cannot expand beyond capacity.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q58"},{"chapter":"9","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Which is fiscal policy?","options":["RBI securities sale","SLR change","Government expenditure increase","Repo-rate reduction"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Fiscal policy concerns government revenue and expenditure.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q59"},{"chapter":"9","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Workers willing to work at the existing wage but unable to find work face:","options":["Involuntary unemployment","Only leisure preference","Voluntary unemployment","Full employment"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The inability to obtain work despite willingness distinguishes involuntary unemployment.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q60"},{"chapter":"10","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Which receipt creates a liability?","options":["A fine","Government borrowing","Income tax","A fee"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Borrowing is a capital receipt because it creates a repayment obligation.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q61"},{"chapter":"10","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Recovery of a loan by government is:","options":["Revenue receipt","A tax","Revenue expenditure","Capital receipt"],"answer":3,"explanation":"It reduces the government’s financial asset: outstanding loans.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q62"},{"chapter":"10","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Which is capital expenditure?","options":["Loan-principal repayment","A food subsidy","Interest on past debt","Employee salaries"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Repayment reduces liabilities; interest does not.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q63"},{"chapter":"10","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Fiscal deficit measures:","options":["Exports minus imports","Only revenue receipts","Borrowing requirement","Only interest payments"],"answer":2,"explanation":"It is expenditure minus receipts other than borrowings.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q64"},{"chapter":"10","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"If primary deficit is zero:","options":["Fiscal deficit is zero necessarily","Government debt is zero","Fiscal deficit equals interest payments","Revenue deficit is zero necessarily"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Primary deficit = fiscal deficit − interest payments.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q65"},{"chapter":"10","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"National defence is generally:","options":["Rival and excludable","Non-rival and non-excludable","Rival and non-excludable","Non-rival and excludable"],"answer":1,"explanation":"These are the two characteristics of a public good.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q66"},{"chapter":"10","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Progressive taxation and spending on low-income households primarily serve:","options":["Credit creation","Redistribution","Exchange-rate hedging","Currency issue"],"answer":1,"explanation":"They can reduce inequality in income and wealth.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q67"},{"chapter":"10","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"A grant given to a state government for building a hospital is classified by the granting government as:","options":["A loan-principal repayment","Revenue expenditure","Disinvestment","A capital receipt"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The textbook convention treats grants as revenue expenditure for the grantor, even when used to create assets.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q68"},{"chapter":"11","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"The rate rises from ₹80/$ to ₹84/$ under a flexible system. The rupee has:","options":["Appreciated","Been revalued officially","Become a fixed currency","Depreciated"],"answer":3,"explanation":"More rupees are needed per dollar: the rupee’s external value falls.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q69"},{"chapter":"11","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"More inward FDI raises the supply of dollars. Other things equal, ₹/$:","options":["Must stay fixed","Rises","Becomes negative","Falls"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A rightward supply shift reduces the price of dollars and appreciates the rupee.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q70"},{"chapter":"11","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"An official reduction in the external value of a currency under a fixed system is:","options":["A floating appreciation","Devaluation","Revaluation","Appreciation"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Devaluation is an official change; depreciation is market driven.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q71"},{"chapter":"11","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"When excess demand for dollars threatens a fixed rate, the central bank can:","options":["Buy dollars","Sell dollars from reserves","Ban all domestic saving","Issue foreign shares itself"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Selling dollars supplies the shortage and supports the domestic currency.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q72"},{"chapter":"11","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"A contract today fixing an exchange rate for payment three months later is a:","options":["Currency reserve ratio","Spot contract","Forward contract","Transfer payment"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Forward markets allow future exchange on terms agreed today.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q73"},{"chapter":"11","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"A rise in ₹/$ makes a $100 import cost:","options":["Zero rupees","Fewer rupees","The same rupees always","More rupees"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The foreign-currency price times a larger rupee/dollar rate gives a larger rupee cost.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q74"},{"chapter":"12","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"An export of goods is recorded as:","options":["Capital-account debit","Official-reserve loss automatically","Current-account credit","Current-account debit"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Exports bring foreign-exchange receipts and are current-account credits.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q75"},{"chapter":"12","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"An Indian resident sends a gift abroad. The entry is:","options":["Current-account debit","Domestic factor income","A goods export","Capital-account credit"],"answer":0,"explanation":"An outward current transfer is a debit in the current account.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q76"},{"chapter":"12","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Interest paid on a foreign loan and repayment of its principal belong respectively to:","options":["Current and capital accounts","Both trade balance","Both transfers","Capital and current accounts"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Interest is factor income; principal changes an external liability.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q77"},{"chapter":"12","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"A BoP accounting balance proves that:","options":["All autonomous transactions balance","Counterpart and financing entries close the accounts","Foreign borrowing is zero","There is no external deficit"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Accounting balance can coexist with an autonomous deficit financed through reserves or loans.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q78"},{"chapter":"12","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Accommodating transactions are undertaken:","options":["Only to earn profit","Only as goods exports","To finance or correct an external imbalance","Only by tourists"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Their purpose is adjustment of the BoP imbalance; they are below-the-line items.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q79"},{"chapter":"12","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Balance of trade includes:","options":["All current transfers","Goods only","All capital flows","Goods and all services"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Trade balance is merchandise exports minus merchandise imports.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q80"},{"chapter":"12","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Under the supplied textbook’s convention, direct purchases by foreign tourists in domestic markets are:","options":["Merchandise exports","Domestic borrowing","Capital-account debits","Invisible trade"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The purple box classifies direct tourist purchases as invisible trade.","tag":"Concept MCQ","id":"q81"},{"chapter":"2","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"Gross fixed investment is ₹420 crore. Opening inventories are ₹90 crore and closing inventories ₹120 crore. Depreciation is ₹70 crore. Find inventory investment, gross investment and net investment.","solution":"1. Inventory investment = 120 − 90 = ₹30 crore.\n2. Gross investment = 420 + 30 = ₹450 crore.\n3. Net investment = 450 − 70 = ₹380 crore.\nHeading to remember: Gross Investment, Net Investment and Depreciation.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q82"},{"chapter":"2","type":"numerical","marks":3,"q":"A machine costs ₹12 lakh, has scrap value ₹2 lakh and an expected life of 5 years. Find annual depreciation. If annual gross investment is ₹7 lakh, find net investment.","solution":"Annual depreciation = (12 − 2)/5 = ₹2 lakh.\nNet investment = 7 − 2 = ₹5 lakh.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q83"},{"chapter":"3","type":"numerical","marks":6,"q":"GDPMP = ₹8,000 crore; depreciation = ₹500 crore; indirect taxes = ₹700 crore; subsidies = ₹100 crore; factor income from abroad = ₹180 crore; factor income paid abroad = ₹260 crore. Find NIT, NFIA, NDPFC and national income.","solution":"NIT = 700 − 100 = ₹600 crore.\nNFIA = 180 − 260 = −₹80 crore.\nNDPFC = 8,000 − 500 − 600 = ₹6,900 crore.\nNNPFC = 6,900 − 80 = ₹6,820 crore.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q84"},{"chapter":"3","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"National income is ₹6,200 crore, depreciation ₹300 crore, NIT ₹450 crore and NFIA −₹50 crore. Find GDPMP.","solution":"GDPMP = NNPFC + Depreciation + NIT − NFIA\n= 6,200 + 300 + 450 − (−50) = ₹7,000 crore.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q85"},{"chapter":"3","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"GDPMP is ₹4,800 crore and GNPMP is ₹4,700 crore. Depreciation is ₹200 crore and NIT is ₹300 crore. Find NFIA and national income.","solution":"NFIA = GNPMP − GDPMP = 4,700 − 4,800 = −₹100 crore.\nNNPFC = GNPMP − Depreciation − NIT = 4,700 − 200 − 300 = ₹4,200 crore.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q86"},{"chapter":"3","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"Nominal GDP is ₹7,200 crore and real GDP is ₹6,000 crore. Calculate the GDP deflator. If nominal GDP later becomes ₹8,100 crore with deflator 125, find real GDP.","solution":"GDP deflator = 7,200/6,000 × 100 = 120.\nLater real GDP = 8,100/125 × 100 = ₹6,480 crore.\nAn index of 125 means prices are 25% above the base-year level, not necessarily 25% above the immediately preceding year.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q87"},{"chapter":"3","type":"numerical","marks":6,"q":"An economy produces only rice and cloth. Base prices are ₹20/kg and ₹50/metre. Current prices are ₹25/kg and ₹60/metre. Current quantities are 100 kg and 80 metres. Find nominal GDP, real GDP and the deflator.","solution":"Nominal GDP = 25 × 100 + 60 × 80 = ₹7,300.\nReal GDP = 20 × 100 + 50 × 80 = ₹6,000.\nDeflator = 7,300/6,000 × 100 = 121.67 (approximately).\nUse current quantities in both measures.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q88"},{"chapter":"3","type":"numerical","marks":6,"q":"GDPFC = ₹5,200 crore; NDPFC = ₹4,900 crore; indirect taxes ₹200 crore; subsidies ₹260 crore; NFIA ₹80 crore. Find depreciation, GDPMP and NNPMP.","solution":"Depreciation = 5,200 − 4,900 = ₹300 crore.\nNIT = 200 − 260 = −₹60 crore.\nGDPMP = GDPFC + NIT = 5,200 − 60 = ₹5,140 crore.\nNNPMP = GDPMP − Depreciation + NFIA = 5,140 − 300 + 80 = ₹4,920 crore.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q89"},{"chapter":"4","type":"numerical","marks":6,"q":"A firm has domestic sales ₹900 crore, exports ₹100 crore, opening stock ₹80 crore, closing stock ₹110 crore and intermediate purchases ₹430 crore. Find output and GVA MP. Then find NVA FC if depreciation is ₹50 crore and NIT ₹40 crore.","solution":"Total sales = 900 + 100 = ₹1,000 crore.\nChange in stock = 110 − 80 = ₹30 crore.\nOutput = 1,000 + 30 = ₹1,030 crore.\nGVA MP = 1,030 − 430 = ₹600 crore.\nNVA FC = 600 − 50 − 40 = ₹510 crore.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q90"},{"chapter":"4","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"A farmer sells wheat for ₹100 to a mill; the mill sells flour for ₹170 to a bakery; the bakery sells bread for ₹280 to households. No other intermediate inputs exist. Find each producer’s value added and total GDP.","solution":"Farmer GVA = 100 − 0 = ₹100.\nMill GVA = 170 − 100 = ₹70.\nBakery GVA = 280 − 170 = ₹110.\nTotal = ₹280, equal to final bread value. Adding full sales gives ₹550 and double counts ₹270.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q91"},{"chapter":"4","type":"numerical","marks":6,"q":"Wages in cash = ₹1,000 crore, wages in kind = ₹100 crore, employer social contributions = ₹80 crore, rent and royalty = ₹200 crore, interest on productive loans = ₹120 crore, corporate tax = ₹50 crore, dividends = ₹70 crore, retained earnings = ₹30 crore, mixed income = ₹250 crore and NFIA = −₹40 crore. Find national income.","solution":"Compensation = 1,000 + 100 + 80 = ₹1,180 crore.\nProfit = 50 + 70 + 30 = ₹150 crore.\nOperating surplus = 200 + 120 + 150 = ₹470 crore.\nNDPFC = 1,180 + 470 + 250 = ₹1,900 crore.\nNNPFC = 1,900 − 40 = ₹1,860 crore.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q92"},{"chapter":"4","type":"numerical","marks":6,"q":"PFCE ₹1,200 crore; GFCE ₹400 crore; gross domestic fixed capital formation ₹500 crore; change in stock −₹20 crore; exports ₹180 crore; imports ₹230 crore; depreciation ₹100 crore; NIT ₹80 crore; NFIA ₹30 crore. Find GDPMP and national income.","solution":"GDCF = 500 − 20 = ₹480 crore.\nNet exports = 180 − 230 = −₹50 crore.\nGDPMP = 1,200 + 400 + 480 − 50 = ₹2,030 crore.\nNNPFC = 2,030 − 100 − 80 + 30 = ₹1,880 crore.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q93"},{"chapter":"4","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"For an economy, total output ₹2,500 crore and intermediate consumption ₹700 crore. PFCE ₹800 crore, GFCE ₹300 crore, GDCF ₹600 crore, exports ₹250 crore and imports ₹150 crore. Show that production and expenditure methods agree.","solution":"Value-added method: GDPMP = 2,500 − 700 = ₹1,800 crore.\nExpenditure method: GDPMP = 800 + 300 + 600 + (250 − 150) = ₹1,800 crore.\nBoth measure the same final production from different sides. Do not add inventories separately when already included in GDCF.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q94"},{"chapter":"5","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"Currency held by the public is ₹600 crore; demand deposits ₹900 crore; other deposits with RBI ₹20 crore; net time deposits ₹1,500 crore; post-office savings deposits ₹100 crore. Find M1, M2 and M3 under the textbook definitions.","solution":"M1 = 600 + 900 + 20 = ₹1,520 crore.\nM2 = 1,520 + 100 = ₹1,620 crore.\nM3 = 1,520 + 1,500 = ₹3,020 crore.\nM3 adds time deposits to M1, not to M2.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q95"},{"chapter":"6","type":"numerical","marks":6,"q":"An initial deposit of ₹2,000 enters the banking system. The required reserve ratio is 10%, there are no excess reserves and every loan is redeposited. Find total deposits, additional credit and reserves in the final position.","solution":"Multiplier = 1/0.10 = 10.\nTotal deposits = 2,000 × 10 = ₹20,000.\nAdditional credit = 20,000 − 2,000 = ₹18,000.\nTotal reserves = 10% × 20,000 = ₹2,000.\nDistinguish total deposits from new credit.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q96"},{"chapter":"6","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"Initial deposit is ₹1,000 and reserve ratio falls from 20% to 10%. Find the maximum total deposits before and after, and their increase.","solution":"Before: 1,000/0.20 = ₹5,000.\nAfter: 1,000/0.10 = ₹10,000.\nIncrease = ₹5,000.\nThis assumes the same initial reserves, full lending and complete redepositing.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q97"},{"chapter":"6","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"A bank lends against collateral worth ₹80 lakh. If the margin rises from 20% to 35%, find the maximum loan at each margin and the reduction.","solution":"At 20% margin: loan = 80 × 0.80 = ₹64 lakh.\nAt 35% margin: loan = 80 × 0.65 = ₹52 lakh.\nReduction = ₹12 lakh.\nHigher margin restricts credit against unchanged security.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q98"},{"chapter":"7","type":"numerical","marks":6,"q":"C = 100 + 0.8Y. At Y = ₹500 crore, find C, S, APC, APS, MPC and MPS.","solution":"C = 100 + 0.8 × 500 = ₹500 crore.\nS = 500 − 500 = 0.\nAPC = 500/500 = 1; APS = 0.\nMPC = 0.8; MPS = 0.2.\nThis is the break-even point, but not necessarily equilibrium.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q99"},{"chapter":"7","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"Income rises from ₹400 crore to ₹600 crore and consumption rises from ₹360 crore to ₹510 crore. Find MPC and MPS. Find APC and APS at the new income.","solution":"ΔC = 150; ΔY = 200.\nMPC = 150/200 = 0.75; MPS = 0.25.\nAt Y = 600: APC = 510/600 = 0.85; S = 90; APS = 90/600 = 0.15.\nMPC + MPS = 1 and APC + APS = 1.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q100"},{"chapter":"7","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"Saving function is S = −60 + 0.25Y. Derive consumption function and find break-even income. Calculate saving at Y = ₹160 crore.","solution":"C = Y − S = Y − (−60 + 0.25Y) = 60 + 0.75Y.\nAt break-even S = 0: 0.25Y = 60, so Y = ₹240 crore.\nAt Y = 160: S = −60 + 40 = −₹20 crore (dissaving).","tag":"Numerical","id":"q101"},{"chapter":"7","type":"numerical","marks":6,"q":"At income ₹1,000 crore, APC is 0.9. If income rises by ₹200 crore and MPS is 0.25, find new consumption, saving, APC and APS.","solution":"Initial C = 0.9 × 1,000 = ₹900 crore.\nMPC = 1 − 0.25 = 0.75; ΔC = 0.75 × 200 = ₹150 crore.\nNew C = ₹1,050 crore; new Y = ₹1,200 crore; new S = ₹150 crore.\nNew APC = 1,050/1,200 = 0.875; APS = 0.125.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q102"},{"chapter":"8","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"C = 80 + 0.75Y and autonomous investment is ₹120 crore. Determine equilibrium income, consumption and saving.","solution":"Y = C + I = 80 + 0.75Y + 120.\n0.25Y = 200 ⇒ Y* = ₹800 crore.\nC = 80 + 0.75 × 800 = ₹680 crore.\nS = 800 − 680 = ₹120 crore = I.\nCheck planned S = planned I.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q103"},{"chapter":"8","type":"numerical","marks":6,"q":"S = −150 + 0.3Y and planned investment is ₹210 crore. Find equilibrium income. At Y = ₹1,000 crore, explain inventory adjustment.","solution":"Equilibrium: −150 + 0.3Y = 210 ⇒ 0.3Y = 360 ⇒ Y = ₹1,200 crore.\nAt Y = 1,000: planned S = ₹150 crore < I = ₹210 crore.\nAD exceeds output by ₹60 crore; inventories fall unexpectedly and firms expand production.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q104"},{"chapter":"8","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"Investment rises by ₹80 crore and equilibrium income rises by ₹400 crore. Find multiplier, MPC, MPS and change in consumption.","solution":"k = 400/80 = 5.\nMPS = 1/5 = 0.2; MPC = 0.8.\nΔC = ΔY − ΔI = 400 − 80 = ₹320 crore.\nFinal additional saving is ₹80 crore.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q105"},{"chapter":"8","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"MPC is 0.6 and investment falls by ₹50 crore. Calculate the final changes in income, consumption and saving.","solution":"k = 1/(1 − 0.6) = 2.5.\nΔY = 2.5 × (−50) = −₹125 crore.\nΔC = 0.6 × (−125) = −₹75 crore.\nΔS = −125 − (−75) = −₹50 crore = ΔI.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q106"},{"chapter":"8","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"An investment injection of ₹100 crore occurs with MPC 0.8. State income generated in the first four rounds and the eventual total.","solution":"Rounds: ₹100 crore, ₹80 crore, ₹64 crore, ₹51.2 crore.\nFirst four rounds total = ₹295.2 crore.\nEventual total = 100/(1 − 0.8) = ₹500 crore.\nLater rounds continue; the first four do not equal the final multiplier effect.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q107"},{"chapter":"9","type":"numerical","marks":6,"q":"C = 100 + 0.8Y, I = ₹200 crore and full-employment income is ₹2,000 crore. Find equilibrium income, the deflationary gap and the required increase in autonomous expenditure.","solution":"Y* = (100 + 200)/0.2 = ₹1,500 crore.\nAt Yf = 2,000: AD = 100 + 0.8 × 2,000 + 200 = ₹1,900 crore.\nDeflationary gap = 2,000 − 1,900 = ₹100 crore.\nIncome shortfall = ₹500 crore. With k = 5, required autonomous expenditure increase = 500/5 = ₹100 crore.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q108"},{"chapter":"9","type":"numerical","marks":6,"q":"C = 120 + 0.75Y, I = ₹280 crore and Yf = ₹1,200 crore. Determine the demand situation and the autonomous-expenditure correction.","solution":"Notional Y* = (120 + 280)/0.25 = ₹1,600 crore, above capacity.\nAt Yf: AD = 120 + 0.75 × 1,200 + 280 = ₹1,300 crore.\nInflationary gap = 1,300 − 1,200 = ₹100 crore.\nReduce autonomous expenditure by ₹100 crore. Real output cannot rise indefinitely above full employment in this model.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q109"},{"chapter":"9","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"Full-employment income is ₹2,400 crore; current equilibrium income is ₹1,800 crore; MPS is 0.25. Find multiplier and the autonomous investment increase needed for full employment.","solution":"k = 1/0.25 = 4.\nRequired ΔY = 2,400 − 1,800 = ₹600 crore.\nΔI = ΔY/k = 600/4 = ₹150 crore.\nThe expenditure gap is ₹150 crore, while the income gap is ₹600 crore.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q110"},{"chapter":"10","type":"numerical","marks":6,"q":"Revenue receipts = ₹900 crore; revenue expenditure = ₹1,050 crore; capital expenditure = ₹450 crore; non-debt capital receipts = ₹100 crore; interest payments = ₹120 crore. Calculate revenue, fiscal and primary deficits.","solution":"Revenue deficit = 1,050 − 900 = ₹150 crore.\nTotal expenditure = 1,050 + 450 = ₹1,500 crore.\nFiscal deficit = 1,500 − 900 − 100 = ₹500 crore.\nPrimary deficit = 500 − 120 = ₹380 crore.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q111"},{"chapter":"10","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"Total expenditure is ₹2,000 crore, fiscal deficit ₹500 crore, non-tax revenue ₹200 crore and non-debt capital receipts ₹100 crore. Find tax revenue.","solution":"Fiscal deficit = TE − Tax revenue − Non-tax revenue − Non-debt capital receipts.\n500 = 2,000 − T − 200 − 100.\nT = 2,000 − 200 − 100 − 500 = ₹1,200 crore.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q112"},{"chapter":"10","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"Revenue deficit is ₹80 crore, revenue expenditure ₹600 crore, fiscal deficit ₹200 crore and non-debt capital receipts ₹50 crore. Find revenue receipts and capital expenditure.","solution":"Revenue receipts = RE − RD = 600 − 80 = ₹520 crore.\nTE = FD + RR + NDCR = 200 + 520 + 50 = ₹770 crore.\nCapital expenditure = 770 − 600 = ₹170 crore.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q113"},{"chapter":"10","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"Fiscal deficit is ₹700 crore and primary deficit is ₹460 crore. Find interest payments. If revenue receipts are ₹1,000 crore and non-debt capital receipts ₹150 crore, find total expenditure.","solution":"Interest payments = FD − PD = 700 − 460 = ₹240 crore.\nTE = FD + RR + NDCR = 700 + 1,000 + 150 = ₹1,850 crore.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q114"},{"chapter":"11","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"The exchange rate changes from ₹80/$ to ₹84/$. A $500 import is bought at each rate. Find the rupee cost before and after, and identify the domestic currency movement.","solution":"Before: 500 × 80 = ₹40,000.\nAfter: 500 × 84 = ₹42,000.\nRupee cost rises by ₹2,000. The rupee depreciates under a flexible system.\nThe ₹/$ quote rises 5%; the rupee’s dollar value falls by (1 − 80/84) × 100 ≈ 4.76%.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q115"},{"chapter":"11","type":"numerical","marks":6,"q":"Dollar demand is D = 200 − 2e and supply is S = 20 + e, where e is ₹/$ and quantities are arbitrary units. Find equilibrium. Demand then becomes D = 230 − 2e. Find the new rate and explain the movement.","solution":"Initially: 200 − 2e = 20 + e ⇒ 3e = 180 ⇒ e = ₹60/$; Q = 80.\nAfter shift: 230 − 2e = 20 + e ⇒ 3e = 210 ⇒ e = ₹70/$; Q = 90.\nGreater dollar demand raises ₹/$ and depreciates the rupee.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q116"},{"chapter":"11","type":"numerical","marks":3,"q":"A central bank fixes the rate at ₹70/$. At this rate dollar demand is $100 million and supply is $75 million. Find the intervention needed and its reserve effect.","solution":"Excess dollar demand = 100 − 75 = $25 million.\nThe central bank sells $25 million from reserves to meet the shortage and defend the rate.\nIts foreign-exchange reserves decrease by $25 million. This is not a market-clearing rate without intervention.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q117"},{"chapter":"12","type":"numerical","marks":6,"q":"Goods exports ₹600 crore, goods imports ₹800 crore, services receipts ₹250 crore, services payments ₹150 crore, factor income receipts ₹40 crore, factor income payments ₹70 crore, transfers received ₹90 crore and transfers paid ₹20 crore. Find trade and current-account balances.","solution":"Trade balance = 600 − 800 = −₹200 crore.\nNet services = 250 − 150 = ₹100 crore.\nNet factor income = 40 − 70 = −₹30 crore.\nNet transfers = 90 − 20 = ₹70 crore.\nCurrent-account balance = −200 + 100 − 30 + 70 = −₹60 crore.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q118"},{"chapter":"12","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"An economy has a current-account deficit of ₹180 crore and autonomous net capital inflow of ₹130 crore. Find its autonomous BoP balance and reserve change if reserves provide the only financing.","solution":"Autonomous balance = −180 + 130 = −₹50 crore.\nA ₹50 crore deficit must be financed by drawing down official reserves.\nReserve assets decrease by ₹50 crore; the accommodating financing entry closes the accounting balance.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q119"},{"chapter":"12","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"Exports of goods ₹900 crore, imports ₹850 crore, net services ₹100 crore, net factor income −₹70 crore, net transfers ₹20 crore and autonomous capital-account balance −₹60 crore. Find the current-account and overall autonomous balances.","solution":"Trade balance = 900 − 850 = ₹50 crore.\nCurrent account = 50 + 100 − 70 + 20 = ₹100 crore surplus.\nAutonomous overall balance = 100 − 60 = ₹40 crore surplus.\nIf absorbed solely into reserves, reserve assets rise by ₹40 crore.","tag":"Numerical","id":"q120"},{"chapter":"1","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Meaning, origin and principal concerns’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"1.1 ABOUT MACROECONOMICS\n1. Macroeconomics is defined as that branch of economics which studies economic issues or economic problems at the level of an economy as a whole.\n2. The term Macro comes from Makros, meaning large.\n3. Keynes’ The General Theory of Employment, Interest and Money (1936) established macroeconomics as a separate branch.\n4. Its principal concerns are employment, inflation, growth of output and depression.\n5. Keynes stressed government intervention when inadequate demand reduces output and employment.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q121"},{"chapter":"1","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Microeconomics versus macroeconomics’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"1.2 HOW MACROECONOMICS DIFFERS FROM MICROECONOMICS?\n1. Microeconomics studies scarcity and choice at the level of an individual, household, firm or industry; allocation of resources is its central issue.\n2. Macroeconomics studies these problems for the economy as a whole; determination of overall output and employment is its central issue.\n3. Micro uses partial equilibrium analysis, studying one market while holding other markets constant.\n4. Macro uses general equilibrium analysis, examining simultaneous equilibrium across markets.\n5. An industry is an aggregate, but studying one industry is still microeconomics.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q122"},{"chapter":"1","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Micro–macro paradox and interdependence’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Micro-Macro Paradox · Micro-Macro Interdependence\n1. What is logical at the micro level may not be logical at the macro level.\n2. Saving may improve one person’s future prosperity; if everyone saves more by spending less, aggregate demand, investment, output and employment may fall.\n3. Macro affects micro when an RBI interest-rate change affects one producer’s borrowing.\n4. Micro affects macro when individual increases in spending together raise aggregate demand.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q123"},{"chapter":"1","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Scope and significance’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"1.3 SCOPE AND SIGNIFICANCE OF MACROECONOMICS\n1. Macroeconomics studies national income, employment, general prices, growth, money, banking, government budgets and international transactions.\n2. It helps explain an economy’s functioning and formulate policies for growth with stability.\n3. Keep the level of analysis clear: the price of one product is micro; the general price level is macro.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q124"},{"chapter":"2","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Final goods, intermediate goods and end use’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"2.1 MEANING OF END-USE CLASSIFICATION OF GOODS\n1. Final goods are those goods which have crossed the boundary line of production and are ready for use by their final users.\n2. They lead to final consumption expenditure or investment expenditure.\n3. Intermediate goods are purchased by one firm from another for resale or for use as raw material in producing other goods.\n4. Classification depends on end use, not the physical nature of the good: milk bought by a household is final; milk bought by an ice-cream producer is intermediate.\n5. Unsold additions to inventory count as investment.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q125"},{"chapter":"2","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Consumption goods versus capital goods’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"2.1.1 Final Goods · Consumption Goods · Capital Goods\n1. Consumption goods directly satisfy human wants.\n2. They include durable goods, non-durable goods, semi-durable goods and services.\n3. Capital goods are fixed assets of producers, repeatedly used in production: durable-use producer goods such as plant and machinery.\n4. A household car is a durable consumption good; a taxi used to produce transport services is a capital good.\n5. Both are final goods.\n6. A capital good is not intermediate merely because it helps produce something.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q126"},{"chapter":"2","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Consumption expenditure and investment’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"2.2 CONSUMPTION EXPENDITURE · 2.3 INVESTMENT\n1. Consumption expenditure includes expenditure by households, government and non-profit private institutions.\n2. Investment is a process of adding to the stock of capital.\n3. Fixed investment adds to fixed assets; inventory investment changes stocks of raw materials, semi-finished goods and finished goods.\n4. New residential construction is investment.\n5. Buying existing shares transfers financial ownership; it does not itself create new capital goods.\n6. Inventory investment can be negative when closing stock is less than opening stock.\nFormula / relationship:\nInventory investment = Closing stock − Opening stock\nGross investment = Fixed investment + Inventory investment","tag":"Topic review","id":"q127"},{"chapter":"2","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Gross investment, net investment and depreciation’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"2.3.3 Gross Investment, Net Investment and the Concept of Depreciation\n1. Net investment refers to the increase in the stock of capital during an accounting year.\n2. Gross investment includes replacement of worn-out capital.\n3. Depreciation, also called consumption of fixed capital, refers to loss of value of fixed assets in use through normal wear and tear, normal accidental damage and expected obsolescence.\n4. Unexpected destruction is a capital loss rather than normal depreciation.\n5. A depreciation reserve fund is kept to cope with depreciation losses.\nFormula / relationship:\nNet investment = Gross investment − Depreciation\nDepreciation per year = (Capital value − Scrap value) ÷ Estimated life","tag":"Topic review","id":"q128"},{"chapter":"2","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Stocks and flows’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"2.4 STOCKS AND FLOWS\n1. Stocks are quantities measured at a point of time; flows are quantities measured over a specified period of time.\n2. Wealth, money supply, capital and bank balances are stocks.\n3. Income, consumption, investment and saving are flows.\n4. Capital is a stock; investment is the flow adding to it.\n5. “Money supply on 31 March” is a stock even though money changes hands repeatedly.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q129"},{"chapter":"2","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Four sectors and circular flow of income’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"2.5 FOUR SECTORS · 2.6 INTERSECTORAL FLOWS · 2.7 CIRCULAR FLOW OF INCOME\n1. The household sector supplies factor services and consumes goods.\n2. The producing sector produces goods and services.\n3. Government acts as a welfare agency and producer; the external sector links the domestic economy with the rest of the world.\n4. Real flows are flows of goods and factor services; money flows are payments and receipts in the opposite direction.\n5. Circular flow of income refers to the circularity of production, income and expenditure across sectors.\n6. In a simple two-sector model, production generates income and income generates expenditure.\nFormula / relationship:\nProduction → Income generation → Expenditure → Production\nValue of output = Factor income = Final expenditure","tag":"Topic review","id":"q130"},{"chapter":"3","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘National income, factor incomes and transfer incomes’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"3.1 CONCEPT OF NATIONAL INCOME\n1. National income is the sum total of factor incomes earned by normal residents of a country during the period of an accounting year.\n2. Factor incomes are rewards for factor services: compensation of employees, rent, interest and profit.\n3. Transfer incomes such as gifts, charity and old-age pensions are unilateral receipts without a current productive service in return; they are excluded.\n4. An employer’s retirement pension earned through service differs from an old-age welfare pension.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q131"},{"chapter":"3","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Normal residents and domestic territory’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"3.1.3 Normal Residents · 3.2.2 Domestic Territory\n1. A normal resident ordinarily resides in a country and has a centre of economic interest there.\n2. Citizenship alone does not determine residence.\n3. Tourists and visitors for medical treatment do not become residents merely through travel.\n4. Diplomats remain residents of their home countries; border workers are residents where they live.\n5. Domestic territory is economic territory: Indian embassies abroad belong to India’s domestic territory; foreign embassies in India do not.\n6. Check the Ability Zone for ships, aircraft, fishing vessels and other special cases.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q132"},{"chapter":"3","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Domestic income versus national income’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"3.2 DOMESTIC AND NATIONAL CONCEPTS OF INCOME\n1. Domestic income is the sum total of factor incomes generated within the domestic territory, whether earned by residents or non-residents.\n2. National income belongs to normal residents, wherever earned.\n3. Net factor income from abroad is factor income received from the rest of the world minus factor income paid to the rest of the world.\n4. Remittances that are gifts are transfers, not NFIA.\nFormula / relationship:\nNNP at FC = NDP at FC + NFIA\nNFIA = Factor income from abroad − Factor income to abroad","tag":"Topic review","id":"q133"},{"chapter":"3","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘The three conversion bridges’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"3.3 GROSS AND NET · 3.4 MARKET PRICE AND FACTOR COST\n1. Use three independent adjustments.\n2. Gross to net: subtract depreciation.\n3. Domestic to national: add NFIA.\n4. Market price to factor cost: subtract net indirect taxes, which equal indirect taxes minus subsidies.\n5. Reverse the sign when travelling backwards.\n6. A negative NFIA reduces national income; negative NIT means subsidies exceed indirect taxes.\n7. Do not deduct indirect taxes again if NIT is already supplied.\nFormula / relationship:\nNNPFC = GDPMP − Depreciation − NIT + NFIA\nGDPMP = NNPFC + Depreciation + NIT − NFIA","tag":"Topic review","id":"q134"},{"chapter":"3","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Eight related aggregates’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"3.5 AGGREGATES RELATED TO NATIONAL INCOME\n1. GDPMP, GDPFC, NDPMP and NDPFC are domestic measures; GNPMP, GNPFC, NNPMP and NNPFC are national measures.\n2. Gross measures include consumption of fixed capital; net measures exclude it.\n3. Market-price measures include net indirect taxes; factor-cost measures exclude them.\n4. National income means NNPFC; domestic income means NDPFC.\n5. Identify all three labels before substituting a number.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q135"},{"chapter":"3","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Nominal GDP, real GDP and GDP deflator’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"3.6 NOMINAL AND REAL GDP\n1. Nominal GDP is valued at current-year prices; real GDP is valued at base-year prices.\n2. Real GDP helps isolate changes in physical production from changes in prices.\n3. Nominal GDP may rise with unchanged output simply because prices rise.\n4. The GDP deflator is the ratio of nominal GDP to real GDP multiplied by 100.\n5. Use the price index as a percentage index, not as a decimal, in the formula below.\nFormula / relationship:\nNominal GDP = Σ(Current price × Current quantity)\nReal GDP = Σ(Base-year price × Current quantity)\nGDP deflator = Nominal GDP ÷ Real GDP × 100\nReal GDP = Nominal GDP ÷ GDP deflator × 100","tag":"Topic review","id":"q136"},{"chapter":"3","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘GDP and welfare: why output is an incomplete measure’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"3.7 GDP AND WELFARE\n1. A rise in real GDP can expand available goods and services, but it does not ensure an equal rise in welfare.\n2. The textbook highlights distribution of income, composition of GDP, non-monetary transactions and externalities.\n3. Unequal distribution can leave most people without gains; more military goods may not improve consumption; unpaid household work is omitted; pollution imposes an unpriced cost.\n4. Positive externalities are benefits not paid for; negative externalities are costs not compensated.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q137"},{"chapter":"4","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Value added method: measure production once’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"4.2 VALUE ADDED METHOD (OR PRODUCT METHOD)\n1. Value added is value of output minus intermediate consumption.\n2. Value of output equals sales plus change in stock, with own-account production added where applicable.\n3. Sum GVA at market price across producing units to obtain GDPMP.\n4. Deduct depreciation and NIT, then add NFIA for national income.\n5. Alternatively, sum sectoral NVAFC and add NFIA.\nFormula / relationship:\nValue of output = Sales + Closing stock − Opening stock\nGVA MP = Value of output − Intermediate consumption\nNNPFC = ΣGVA MP − Depreciation − NIT + NFIA","tag":"Topic review","id":"q138"},{"chapter":"4","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Double counting and value-added precautions’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"4.2.2 Precautions · 4.2.3 Problem of Double Counting\n1. Double counting means counting the value of the same output more than once.\n2. Avoid it by counting final goods only or summing value added at each stage.\n3. If wheat sells for 100, flour for 160 and bread for 250, total value added is 100 + 60 + 90 = 250, not 510.\n4. Include own-account construction and imputed rent of owner-occupied houses.\n5. Exclude resale value of second-hand goods, but include the current broker’s service.\n6. Do not count intermediate goods separately from the final product.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q139"},{"chapter":"4","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Income method: compensation, surplus and mixed income’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"4.3 INCOME METHOD\n1. Income method measures domestic income as the sum total of factor incomes generated within domestic territory.\n2. Compensation of employees includes wages and salaries in cash and kind and employers’ social-security contributions.\n3. Operating surplus comprises rent and royalty, interest and profit.\n4. Profit includes corporate tax, dividends and retained earnings; these are components, so do not add them again if total profit is given.\n5. Mixed income of the self-employed combines labour and property income that cannot be separated.\nFormula / relationship:\nNDPFC = Compensation of employees + Operating surplus + Mixed income\nOperating surplus = Rent and royalty + Interest + Profit\nNational income = NDPFC + NFIA","tag":"Topic review","id":"q140"},{"chapter":"4","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Income-method precautions’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"4.3.2 Precautions Regarding Income Method\n1. Include only incomes earned from current productive services.\n2. Exclude transfer receipts, capital gains and windfall gains.\n3. Exclude interest on consumption loans and interest on public debt when it represents a transfer rather than payment for a productive service, following the textbook treatment.\n4. Include imputed rent and production for self-consumption where measured.\n5. Do not count sale proceeds of shares or second-hand goods as factor income; the current commission or brokerage is a service income.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q141"},{"chapter":"4","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Expenditure method and its precautions’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"4.4 EXPENDITURE METHOD\n1. Expenditure method measures final expenditure on domestically produced output.\n2. Add private final consumption expenditure, government final consumption expenditure, gross domestic capital formation and net exports.\n3. Gross domestic capital formation includes fixed capital formation and change in stocks.\n4. Exclude financial-asset purchases, transfers and second-hand purchases; include current brokerage.\n5. Subtract imports because imported content may already be included in consumption or investment.\n6. Do not add change in stock twice if gross domestic capital formation already includes it.\nFormula / relationship:\nGDPMP = PFCE + GFCE + GDCF + (X − M)\nNNPFC = PFCE + GFCE + GDCF + (X − M) − Depreciation − NIT + NFIA","tag":"Topic review","id":"q142"},{"chapter":"4","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Reconciliation and treatment of tricky items’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"4.5 RECONCILIATION OF THREE METHODS · 4.6 TREATMENT OF DIFFERENT ITEMS\n1. The three methods measure the same current production from different sides: value added creates factor income, and income finances final expenditure.\n2. In an exam, classify each item before calculating.\n3. Government services are counted even when provided free; employer provident-fund contributions belong in compensation.\n4. Scholarships and welfare pensions are transfers.\n5. Foreign factor earnings require a residence/territory check; the purchase of a newly produced house is investment.\n6. Read the supplied transaction table for item-by-item domestic and national treatment.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q143"},{"chapter":"5","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Money, barter and the evolution of money’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"5.1 MEANING AND EVOLUTION OF MONEY\n1. Money is generally defined as a thing that is commonly accepted as a medium of exchange.\n2. Barter exchanges goods directly for goods.\n3. Its difficulties include lack of double coincidence of wants, lack of a common measure of value, difficulty storing wealth and difficulty making deferred payments.\n4. Money separates selling from buying: the seller need not want the precise good the buyer offers.\n5. Evolution moves through commodity money, metallic money, paper money and bank/credit money.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q144"},{"chapter":"5","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Fiat, fiduciary and legal-tender money’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"5.2 FORMS OF MONEY\n1. Fiat money is money issued by authority of the government.\n2. Fiduciary money is accepted on trust between payer and payee.\n3. Legal tender must be accepted in discharge of debts within the applicable legal conditions; a cheque depends on acceptance and is not legal tender.\n4. Full-bodied money has face value equal to intrinsic commodity value; credit money has face value greater than intrinsic value.\n5. Follow the textbook distinctions, not the everyday use of the word “cash”.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q145"},{"chapter":"5","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Primary and secondary functions of money’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"5.3 FUNCTIONS OF MONEY\n1. The primary functions are medium of exchange and measure of value (unit of account).\n2. Secondary functions are standard of deferred payments, store of value and transfer of value.\n3. Buying a book illustrates medium of exchange; quoting its price illustrates measure of value; paying a loan next year illustrates deferred payment; keeping purchasing power for later illustrates store of value.\n4. Inflation reduces the purchasing power stored in money.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q146"},{"chapter":"5","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Money supply and its components’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"5.4 SUPPLY OF MONEY\n1. Money supply is the stock of money held by the public at a particular point of time.\n2. Interbank deposits and money held by the government/banking system are excluded from money held by the public.\n3. M1 comprises currency with the public, demand deposits with commercial banks and other deposits with the RBI.\n4. Demand deposits are withdrawable on demand.\n5. A cheque transfers a deposit; it is not an additional independent stock of money.\nFormula / relationship:\nM1 = Currency with public + Demand deposits with banks + Other deposits with RBI","tag":"Topic review","id":"q147"},{"chapter":"5","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Ability Zone: broader measures and liquidity’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"ABILITY ZONE · Measures of Money Supply · Liquidity\n1. The textbook also gives M2, M3 and M4 for reference.\n2. M2 adds post-office savings-bank deposits to M1.\n3. M3 adds net time deposits with commercial banks to M1 and is called aggregate monetary resources.\n4. M4 adds total post-office deposits, excluding National Savings Certificates, to M3.\n5. Liquidity means convertibility into cash: money is the most liquid asset.\n6. The textbook’s monetary-system notes discuss paper currency, limited/unlimited legal tender and the minimum reserve system; the original page is available to read exactly.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q148"},{"chapter":"6","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Commercial banks and their functions’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"6.1 COMMERCIAL BANKS\n1. Commercial banks accept deposits and advance loans with the objective of earning profit.\n2. Deposits include current, savings and fixed deposits.\n3. Primary functions are accepting deposits and lending; other functions include agency services and general-utility services.\n4. Banks mobilise saving and create demand deposits through lending.\n5. A central bank is the apex institution regulating the monetary and financial system; its objective is growth with stability rather than commercial profit.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q149"},{"chapter":"6","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Credit creation: follow every round’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"6.2 MONEY CREATION BY THE COMMERCIAL BANKS\n1. Suppose an initial cash deposit of 1,000 enters the banking system and the reserve ratio is 20%.\n2. The first bank keeps 200 and lends 800.\n3. If 800 is redeposited, the next bank keeps 160 and lends 640.\n4. Repeated lending and redepositing expand total deposits to 5,000.\n5. These results assume no cash leakage, no excess reserves and loans redeposited in the banking system.\n6. Total deposits include the initial deposit; additional credit excludes it.\nFormula / relationship:\nDeposit multiplier = 1 ÷ Reserve ratio\nTotal deposits = Initial deposit ÷ Reserve ratio\nAdditional credit = Total deposits − Initial deposit","tag":"Topic review","id":"q150"},{"chapter":"6","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Central bank: seven functions’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"6.3 THE CENTRAL BANK\n1. The textbook lists issuer of currency, banker to the government, bankers’ bank and supervisor, lender of last resort, custodian of foreign exchange, clearing-house function and control of credit.\n2. As government banker it holds accounts, receives money, makes payments and manages borrowing.\n3. As bankers’ bank it holds bank reserves and supports banks.\n4. Clearing settles interbank claims.\n5. Lender of last resort provides emergency assistance when normal sources cannot meet a bank’s needs.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q151"},{"chapter":"6","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Quantitative instruments: rates and open market operations’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"6.4.1 Quantitative Instruments of Credit Control\n1. Bank rate, repo rate and reverse repo rate influence the cost and placement of funds.\n2. The textbook distinguishes bank-rate lending without collateral from repo lending against securities under a repurchase agreement.\n3. Raising lending rates tends to raise borrowing cost and reduce credit demand; lowering them works in the opposite direction.\n4. RBI sales of securities absorb liquidity and reduce credit creation; RBI purchases release liquidity and increase it.\n5. These are textbook mechanisms, not a claim about today’s policy rates.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q152"},{"chapter":"6","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Reserve ratios and qualitative instruments’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"6.4.1 Quantitative Instruments · Qualitative Instruments\n1. CRR is the percentage of deposits banks must keep with the RBI.\n2. SLR requires banks to maintain prescribed liquid assets such as cash, gold and approved securities.\n3. Higher reserve requirements constrain lending; lower requirements release lending capacity.\n4. Margin requirement is the difference between collateral value and loan value.\n5. Higher margins reduce permitted loans.\n6. Rationing fixes credit quotas, while moral suasion uses persuasion and pressure to influence bank lending.\n7. Quantitative instruments affect overall credit; qualitative instruments target its allocation.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q153"},{"chapter":"6","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Ability Zone: when monetary tools succeed’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"ABILITY ZONE · Success of Repo Rate · Success of Open Market Operations\n1. Repo-rate policy depends on banks’ reliance on RBI loans, responsiveness of credit demand, the wider structure of interest rates and availability of non-bank funds.\n2. Open market operations require a functioning securities market and an effective link between reserve changes and lending.\n3. Explaining a policy requires the transmission chain: policy change → bank funds or borrowing cost → credit → expenditure → aggregate demand.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q154"},{"chapter":"7","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Aggregate demand and aggregate supply’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"7.1 CONCEPT OF AD · 7.2 COMPONENTS OF AD · 7.3 CONCEPT OF AS\n1. Aggregate demand is the sum total of demand for all goods and services during an accounting year, measured as planned expenditure.\n2. In a two-sector economy AD = C + I; a closed economy with government adds G; an open economy adds X − M.\n3. Aggregate supply refers to production planned by producers.\n4. In the textbook’s short-run model with constant prices and spare capacity, AS is identified with income/output and represented by the 45° line.\nFormula / relationship:\nAD = C + I + G + (X − M)\nAS = Y = C + S","tag":"Topic review","id":"q155"},{"chapter":"7","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Consumption function: autonomous and induced consumption’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"7.4 CONSUMPTION FUNCTION\n1. Consumption function is the functional relationship between consumption and income.\n2. Autonomous consumption is consumption when income is zero; induced consumption is the part that varies with income.\n3. In C = C̄ + bY, the vertical intercept is C̄ and the slope b is MPC.\n4. Consumption rises with income, but normally by less than the rise in income.\n5. A change in income moves along a given function; a change in autonomous consumption shifts it.\nFormula / relationship:\nC = C̄ + bY\nMPC = ΔC ÷ ΔY\nInduced consumption = bY","tag":"Topic review","id":"q156"},{"chapter":"7","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘APC and MPC: totals versus increments’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"APC vs. MPC\n1. Average propensity to consume is total consumption divided by total income; marginal propensity to consume is additional consumption divided by additional income.\n2. APC may exceed one when consumption exceeds income.\n3. MPC is the slope of the consumption curve; APC is not.\n4. With positive autonomous consumption and constant MPC below one, APC falls as income rises.\n5. At zero income APC is undefined.\nFormula / relationship:\nAPC = C ÷ Y\nMPC = ΔC ÷ ΔY","tag":"Topic review","id":"q157"},{"chapter":"7","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Saving function and the break-even point’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"SAVING FUNCTION · Break-even Point\n1. Saving is income not consumed.\n2. Derive S by subtracting the entire consumption function from Y.\n3. The saving function has a negative intercept equal to autonomous consumption and a slope equal to MPS.\n4. At break-even income C = Y and S = 0: APC is one and APS is zero.\n5. Below it saving is negative; above it saving is positive.\n6. Break-even is not necessarily equilibrium, because equilibrium also depends on investment.\nFormula / relationship:\nS = Y − C = −C̄ + (1 − b)Y\nBreak-even income = C̄ ÷ (1 − b)","tag":"Topic review","id":"q158"},{"chapter":"7","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘APS, MPS and the two identities’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"APS vs. MPS · Relationship between Propensity to Consume and Propensity to Save\n1. Average propensity to save is S/Y; marginal propensity to save is ΔS/ΔY.\n2. APS can be negative when households dissave.\n3. MPS is the slope of the saving function.\n4. Since income is split between consumption and saving, APC + APS = 1 and MPC + MPS = 1.\n5. Do not confuse ΔS (change in saving) with AS (aggregate supply): similar letters represent different concepts.\nFormula / relationship:\nAPS = S ÷ Y; MPS = ΔS ÷ ΔY\nAPC + APS = 1; MPC + MPS = 1","tag":"Topic review","id":"q159"},{"chapter":"7","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Investment and deriving one function from the other’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"INVESTMENT · ABILITY ZONE: Derivation of C-function from S-function\n1. In the simple model autonomous investment is independent of income, so its curve is horizontal.\n2. Induced investment responds to changes in income or activity.\n3. The expected profitability of investment and rate of interest influence investment decisions.\n4. To obtain C from S, use C = Y − S; to obtain S from C, use S = Y − C.\n5. The two slopes add to one and their vertical intercepts have equal magnitudes with opposite signs.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q160"},{"chapter":"8","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Short run and equilibrium output’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"8.1 CONCEPT OF SHORT RUN · 8.2 CONCEPT OF EQUILIBRIUM OUTPUT\n1. The textbook’s short run assumes constant technology and a given capital stock; changes in employment change output.\n2. Equilibrium output is the level where intended production equals intended expenditure.\n3. Producers have no unplanned change in inventories and no incentive to change output.\n4. In the two-sector economy Y = C + I and Y = C + S, so the same equilibrium condition can be written S = I.\nFormula / relationship:\nY = AD = C + I\nC + S = C + I ⇒ S = I\nY* = (C̄ + I) ÷ (1 − MPC)","tag":"Topic review","id":"q161"},{"chapter":"8","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘How inventories restore equilibrium’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Equilibrium GDP · Inventory Adjustment\n1. When AD is below AS, goods remain unsold and actual inventories exceed desired inventories.\n2. Producers cut output and employment until planned saving equals planned investment.\n3. When AD exceeds AS, inventories fall unexpectedly and producers expand output, provided spare capacity exists.\n4. The direction is easy to reverse by mistake: excess supply causes inventory accumulation; excess demand causes inventory depletion.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q162"},{"chapter":"8","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Ex-ante versus ex-post saving and investment’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Ex-ante Saving and Ex-ante Investment\n1. Ex-ante means planned; ex-post means realised.\n2. Planned saving and planned investment need not be equal away from equilibrium.\n3. Actual saving and actual investment are equal by accounting identity because realised investment includes unintended inventory changes.\n4. Equilibrium requires equality of planned S and planned I, not merely the ex-post identity.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q163"},{"chapter":"8","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Effective demand and underemployment equilibrium’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Effective Demand · Underemployment Equilibrium\n1. Effective demand is aggregate demand at the equilibrium level of income/output.\n2. Equilibrium can occur below full employment: firms’ production plans can match spending plans while workers and productive capacity remain idle.\n3. An increase in autonomous expenditure shifts AD upward and raises equilibrium output in the model with spare capacity; a fall lowers it.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q164"},{"chapter":"8","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Investment multiplier: size and derivation’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"8.5 INVESTMENT MULTIPLIER\n1. Investment multiplier is the ratio of change in income to change in investment.\n2. Starting with ΔY = ΔC + ΔI and ΔC = MPC × ΔY gives ΔY(1 − MPC) = ΔI.\n3. Higher MPC means more income is respent, so the multiplier is larger.\n4. Higher MPS means more income leaks into saving, so the multiplier is smaller.\n5. With MPC zero, k = 1; as MPC approaches one, k becomes very large.\nFormula / relationship:\nk = ΔY ÷ ΔI = 1 ÷ (1 − MPC) = 1 ÷ MPS\nΔY = k × ΔI","tag":"Topic review","id":"q165"},{"chapter":"8","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Multiplier mechanism, assumptions and reverse operation’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Multiplier Mechanism\n1. An investment injection is someone’s income.\n2. They consume a fraction, which becomes someone else’s income; successive rounds generate ΔI, MPC × ΔI, MPC² × ΔI and so on.\n3. Saving reduces each next round.\n4. The simple multiplier assumes constant MPC, available spare capacity, constant prices, and no additional leakages such as imports or taxes.\n5. A fall in investment works in reverse, producing a multiplied fall in income.\n6. The final increase in saving equals the initial increase in investment.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q166"},{"chapter":"9","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Full employment and unemployment’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"9.1 SOME CRITICAL CONCEPTS\n1. Full employment means those able and willing to work at the existing wage obtain work.\n2. Voluntary unemployment reflects unwillingness to work at the existing wage; involuntary unemployment means willing workers cannot find jobs.\n3. The textbook also distinguishes natural unemployment: frictional unemployment during job transitions and structural unemployment caused by changing industries, skills or production techniques.\n4. Underemployment equilibrium has AD = AS with unutilised capacity.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q167"},{"chapter":"9","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Deficient demand and deflationary gap’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"DEFICIENT DEMAND · Deflationary Gap\n1. Deficient demand occurs when aggregate demand is less than the aggregate supply corresponding to full employment.\n2. The deflationary gap is the shortfall of AD from the level required to maintain full employment; measure it vertically at full-employment output.\n3. It reduces output and employment and creates involuntary unemployment.\n4. The gap in expenditure is not the same as the shortfall in equilibrium income: the multiplier links the two.\nFormula / relationship:\nDeflationary gap = Yf − AD at Yf\nRequired increase in autonomous expenditure = (Yf − Y*) ÷ k","tag":"Topic review","id":"q168"},{"chapter":"9","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Excess demand and inflationary gap’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"EXCESS DEMAND · Inflationary Gap\n1. Excess demand occurs when AD exceeds the demand required to sustain full employment.\n2. The inflationary gap is the excess of AD over full-employment AS, measured at Yf.\n3. Once resources are fully employed, real output cannot expand in the simple model; excess spending raises the general price level.\n4. A calculated equilibrium above Yf is a signal of inflationary pressure, not evidence that the economy can actually produce beyond its capacity.\nFormula / relationship:\nInflationary gap = AD at Yf − Yf","tag":"Topic review","id":"q169"},{"chapter":"9","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Fiscal measures: expenditure, taxation and borrowing’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Fiscal Policy\n1. To correct deficient demand, raise government expenditure, reduce taxes, reduce public borrowing and increase borrowing from the central bank, following the textbook framework.\n2. These measures raise spending power or public expenditure.\n3. For excess demand, reverse the direction.\n4. Explain the chain: lower taxes → higher disposable income → more consumption → higher AD.\n5. Borrowing from the public can withdraw purchasing power; borrowing from the central bank can expand it.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q170"},{"chapter":"9","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Monetary measures: cost and availability of credit’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Monetary Policy\n1. For deficient demand, lower bank/repo/reverse-repo rates, lower CRR and SLR, buy securities, lower margins, relax credit rationing and encourage lending.\n2. For excess demand, raise rates and reserve requirements, sell securities, raise margins and restrict lending.\n3. Monetary policy is the central bank’s policy; fiscal policy is the government’s revenue and expenditure policy.\n4. Always connect the instrument to credit and then to aggregate demand.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q171"},{"chapter":"10","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Government budget and its objectives’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"10.1 CONCEPT OF GOVERNMENT BUDGET · 10.2 OBJECTIVES\n1. Government budget is a statement of expected receipts and expected expenditure of the government for the coming financial year, revealing its budgetary policy.\n2. Objectives include GDP growth, allocation of resources, redistribution of income and wealth, economic stability, management of public enterprises and employment generation.\n3. Public infrastructure can encourage private investment; progressive taxation and social spending can reduce inequality.\n4. Fiscal discipline means managing public finances responsibly to avoid unsustainable deficits and debt.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q172"},{"chapter":"10","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Revenue receipts versus capital receipts’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"10.3.1 Budget Receipts\n1. Revenue receipts neither create a liability nor reduce assets.\n2. They include tax revenue and non-tax revenue such as fees, fines, interest, profits/dividends and grants.\n3. Capital receipts create a liability or reduce assets: borrowing creates liabilities, while loan recovery and disinvestment reduce assets.\n4. Do not classify a receipt by size or regularity alone; use the liability/asset test.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q173"},{"chapter":"10","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Direct taxes, indirect taxes and non-tax revenue’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Tax Revenue · Non-tax Revenue\n1. Direct taxes have impact and incidence on the same person, such as income tax and corporation tax.\n2. Indirect taxes allow the burden to be shifted, such as GST.\n3. A fee is a payment for a specific government service; a fine penalises an offence.\n4. A tax is compulsory and does not promise a direct equivalent service.\n5. Progressive taxes take a larger proportion as income rises; proportional taxes keep the same proportion; regressive burdens fall relatively more heavily on lower incomes.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q174"},{"chapter":"10","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Revenue expenditure versus capital expenditure’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"10.3.2 Budget Expenditure\n1. Revenue expenditure neither creates assets nor reduces liabilities.\n2. Salaries, pensions, subsidies and interest payments are examples.\n3. Capital expenditure creates assets or reduces liabilities: construction of a highway, purchase of equipment, lending by government and repayment of loan principal.\n4. Repayment of principal is capital expenditure; interest is revenue expenditure.\n5. Grants to states remain revenue expenditure for the granting government even if the recipient uses them to build assets, following the textbook convention.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q175"},{"chapter":"10","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Revenue deficit, fiscal deficit and primary deficit’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"10.4 BUDGET DEFICIT: REVENUE DEFICIT, FISCAL DEFICIT AND PRIMARY DEFICIT\n1. Revenue deficit is the excess of revenue expenditure over revenue receipts.\n2. Fiscal deficit is total expenditure minus total receipts excluding borrowings; it measures borrowing requirements.\n3. Primary deficit deducts interest payments from fiscal deficit and indicates the current borrowing requirement excluding past-debt interest.\n4. A zero revenue deficit does not guarantee zero fiscal deficit.\n5. A zero primary deficit means borrowing covers interest payments.\nFormula / relationship:\nRevenue deficit = Revenue expenditure − Revenue receipts\nFiscal deficit = Total expenditure − Revenue receipts − Non-debt capital receipts\nPrimary deficit = Fiscal deficit − Interest payments","tag":"Topic review","id":"q176"},{"chapter":"10","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Implications, balanced and unbalanced budgets’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"10.5 BALANCED AND UNBALANCED BUDGET\n1. A revenue deficit indicates government dissaving: borrowing may finance current consumption rather than asset creation.\n2. Fiscal deficits may raise debt and interest burdens and crowd out private borrowing; financing expenditure can also support demand and productive capacity, depending on its use.\n3. A balanced budget has receipts equal to expenditure; a surplus budget has receipts above expenditure and can restrain inflation; a deficit budget can support demand in recession.\n4. Consider both the size and composition of expenditure.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q177"},{"chapter":"10","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Ability Zone: public, private, common and club goods’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"ABILITY ZONE · Public Goods, Private Goods, Common Goods and Club Goods\n1. Public goods are non-rival and non-excludable, such as national defence.\n2. Private goods are rival and excludable.\n3. Common goods are rival but non-excludable, such as fish in an open pond.\n4. Club goods are excludable but non-rival until congestion, such as a subscription service.\n5. Rivalry means one person’s use reduces availability to others; excludability means users can be denied access.\n6. Public expenditure supports infrastructure, redistribution, stability and an investment-friendly environment.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q178"},{"chapter":"11","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Foreign exchange and the quotation convention’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"11.1 FOREIGN EXCHANGE AND FOREIGN EXCHANGE RATE\n1. Foreign exchange refers to foreign currency.\n2. Foreign exchange rate is the price paid in domestic currency for a unit of foreign currency.\n3. Throughout these lessons, quote the rate as ₹ per US dollar.\n4. A rise from ₹80/$ to ₹84/$ means the rupee buys fewer dollars and has depreciated; a fall to ₹76/$ means rupee appreciation.\n5. Always identify which currency is in the numerator before describing a movement.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q179"},{"chapter":"11","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Demand, supply and flexible exchange-rate determination’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"11.2.1 Flexible Exchange Rate\n1. Flexible exchange rate is determined by demand and supply in the foreign exchange market.\n2. Demand for foreign exchange comes from imports, overseas investment, foreign travel, loan repayment, income payments, grants and speculation.\n3. Supply comes from exports, foreign investment, inward loans, tourism receipts, income receipts, grants and remittances.\n4. With ₹/$ on the vertical axis, demand slopes downward and supply upward; their intersection is the equilibrium rate.\n5. A higher exchange rate makes foreign goods costlier in rupees and normally reduces import-related foreign-exchange demand.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q180"},{"chapter":"11","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Movements versus shifts, appreciation versus depreciation’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"Appreciation and Depreciation of Domestic Currency\n1. A change in the exchange rate causes movement along a given demand or supply curve.\n2. A change in imports, investment preferences, income or foreign demand shifts a curve.\n3. Greater demand for dollars shifts demand right, raising ₹/$ and depreciating the rupee.\n4. Greater supply of dollars shifts supply right, lowering ₹/$ and appreciating the rupee.\n5. Depreciation can make exports cheaper for foreigners and imports costlier for residents; actual quantity responses depend on demand conditions.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q181"},{"chapter":"11","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Fixed rate, devaluation and revaluation’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"11.2.2 Fixed Exchange Rate\n1. Fixed exchange rate is set and maintained by the monetary authority.\n2. An official rise in ₹/$ is devaluation of the rupee; an official fall is revaluation.\n3. Depreciation/appreciation describe market-driven changes under a flexible system.\n4. To defend a fixed rate when demand for dollars exceeds supply, the authority sells foreign currency from reserves; when dollar supply exceeds demand it buys foreign currency.\n5. An overvalued domestic currency tends to create excess demand for foreign currency at the official rate.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q182"},{"chapter":"11","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Managed floating and merits/demerits’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"11.3 MANAGED FLOATING · 11.4 MERITS AND DEMERITS\n1. Managed floating combines market determination with occasional central-bank intervention to limit undesirable movements; the textbook also calls it dirty floating.\n2. Fixed rates reduce exchange uncertainty and can support trade but require reserves and may misallocate resources.\n3. Flexible rates need less reserve defence and adjust through markets but create uncertainty and exposure to external shocks.\n4. The original comparison covers the full list of merits and demerits.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q183"},{"chapter":"11","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Foreign exchange market: transfer, credit and hedging’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"11.7 FOREIGN EXCHANGE MARKET\n1. The foreign exchange market is the market for national currencies.\n2. The transfer function moves purchasing power between countries.\n3. The credit function supports trade through financing arrangements.\n4. Hedging reduces exchange-rate risk.\n5. Spot transactions exchange currency for current delivery; forward transactions agree today on a rate for delivery at a future date.\n6. A forward contract fixes an exchange rate; it does not guarantee that the future spot rate will equal that rate.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q184"},{"chapter":"12","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Balance of payments: account and recording rule’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"12.1 BoP (or BoP ACCOUNTS)\n1. Balance of payments is a statement of accounts showing all monetary transactions of a country with the rest of the world during a period of time, generally one year.\n2. It records goods, services, income, transfers and asset-related transactions between residents and non-residents.\n3. Foreign-exchange receipts are credit items; payments are debit items.\n4. The textbook uses current, capital and official-reserves accounts.\n5. Keep its school-level account terminology when answering.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q185"},{"chapter":"12","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Current account, trade balance and invisibles’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"12.2.1 Current Account\n1. Current account records exports and imports of goods and services, factor income and current transfers.\n2. Goods are visible trade or merchandise; services, factor incomes and transfers are invisibles.\n3. Balance of trade covers goods only.\n4. A gift from abroad is a current transfer; wages for a service are factor income.\n5. The supplied purple box treats direct tourist purchases as invisible trade: preserve that convention for this textbook.\nFormula / relationship:\nTrade balance = Goods exports − Goods imports\nCurrent-account balance = Trade balance + Net services + Net factor income + Net current transfers","tag":"Topic review","id":"q186"},{"chapter":"12","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Capital account and asset/liability changes’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"12.2.2 Capital Account\n1. Capital account records transactions that change a country’s assets or liabilities in relation to the rest of the world.\n2. The textbook includes borrowing, foreign investment, NRI deposits, banking capital and short-term debt.\n3. Inward FDI or an external loan is a credit; an outward investment or repayment of foreign loan principal is a debit.\n4. Interest on a foreign loan belongs in the current account; the principal belongs in the capital account.\n5. FDI involves a lasting productive interest/control; portfolio investment purchases financial securities.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q187"},{"chapter":"12","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Official reserves, deficit and surplus’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"12.2.3 Official Reserves Account\n1. If autonomous foreign-exchange payments exceed autonomous receipts, there is a BoP deficit, financed by accommodating transactions such as a drawdown of reserves or official borrowing.\n2. A surplus can increase official reserves.\n3. A decrease in reserve assets finances a shortfall; an increase uses foreign exchange.\n4. Follow the sign convention explicitly stated in a numerical instead of confusing a positive stock increase with a credit entry.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q188"},{"chapter":"12","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Autonomous versus accommodating transactions’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"12.3.1 Autonomous and Accommodating Items of BoP Account\n1. Autonomous transactions are undertaken for independent economic motives such as profit, rather than to correct a BoP imbalance; they are above-the-line items.\n2. Accommodating transactions are undertaken to finance or correct the imbalance and are below the line.\n3. A foreign loan may be autonomous when raised commercially or accommodating when arranged specifically to finance a deficit.\n4. Classification depends on purpose, not merely the type of transaction.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q189"},{"chapter":"12","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Accounting balance versus operational balance’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"ABILITY ZONE · Balance of Payments: Accounting Sense and Operational Sense\n1. BoP balances in the accounting sense because every transaction has a counterpart and financing entries close the accounts.\n2. This does not mean autonomous receipts equal autonomous payments or that the economy has no external problem.\n3. A current-account deficit financed by foreign borrowing is consistent with accounting balance and increasing indebtedness.\n4. Read the Ability Zone worked table to see a current-account deficit offset by capital inflows.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q190"},{"chapter":"12","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain ‘Causes and significance of disequilibrium’. Use the relevant textbook headings and support the explanation with an example or relationship.","solution":"12.3.2 Significance of BoP Accounts · Causes of Disequilibrium\n1. Economic causes include large development imports, business cycles, domestic inflation, import substitution and changes in trading partners’ costs.\n2. Political instability can discourage foreign investment; policy changes can increase imports.\n3. Social causes include changes in tastes and cross-border preferences.\n4. BoP data show external strengths, weaknesses, debt and reserve pressures and help formulate policy.\n5. A trade deficit alone does not tell you the whole BoP position.","tag":"Topic review","id":"q191"},{"chapter":"3","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"“An increase in GDP necessarily raises welfare.” Defend or refute with four textbook points.","solution":"Refute.\n1. Distribution of income: growth concentrated among a few may not improve most people’s welfare.\n2. Composition of GDP: output may rise through goods that do not improve ordinary consumption.\n3. Non-monetary transactions: unpaid household services are omitted.\n4. Externalities: pollution costs and unpaid external benefits are not fully reflected in GDP.\nUse “limitations of GDP as an index of welfare” as the answer heading.","tag":"Theory","id":"q192"},{"chapter":"4","type":"theory","marks":6,"q":"State six precautions when estimating national income by the expenditure method.","solution":"Precautions regarding Expenditure Method\n1. Count final expenditure only; exclude intermediate expenditure.\n2. Exclude purchases of second-hand goods; include current brokerage.\n3. Exclude financial-asset purchases such as existing shares and bonds.\n4. Exclude transfer payments; the recipient’s later purchase of output is separate.\n5. Include own-account investment and imputed services where measured.\n6. Subtract imports and include exports to isolate domestic production.\nAvoid adding change in stock twice if GDCF already includes it.","tag":"Theory","id":"q193"},{"chapter":"6","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain how an increase in CRR can control excess demand.","solution":"Cash Reserve Ratio (CRR)\n1. CRR is the required percentage of bank deposits kept with the RBI.\n2. Higher CRR means more required reserves against the same deposits.\n3. Banks’ capacity to extend credit and create deposits falls.\n4. Lower credit availability restrains consumption and investment expenditure, reducing AD and inflationary pressure.","tag":"Theory","id":"q194"},{"chapter":"8","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"“Ex-post saving equals ex-post investment, so every economy is always in equilibrium.” Is the conclusion valid?","solution":"Refute the conclusion.\nEx-post Saving and Investment: realised saving equals realised investment because actual investment includes unplanned inventory changes.\nEx-ante Saving and Investment: equilibrium requires equality of planned saving and planned investment.\nIf output exceeds planned expenditure, inventories accumulate unexpectedly; firms reduce production. The ex-post identity holds even while firms are adjusting away from disequilibrium.","tag":"Theory","id":"q195"},{"chapter":"9","type":"theory","marks":6,"q":"Distinguish deficient demand and excess demand under the headings meaning, gap, effects and correction.","solution":"1. Meaning: deficient demand is AD below the full-employment requirement; excess demand is AD above it.\n2. Gap: deficient demand creates a deflationary gap; excess demand creates an inflationary gap, each measured at full-employment output.\n3. Effects: deficient demand lowers output and employment; excess demand raises prices when output is at capacity.\n4. Correction: expand public spending and ease credit for deficient demand; restrain public spending and tighten credit for excess demand.","tag":"Theory","id":"q196"},{"chapter":"10","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Distinguish revenue receipts and capital receipts, giving two examples of each.","solution":"Revenue Receipts\nNeither create a liability nor reduce assets. Examples: income tax and government fees.\nCapital Receipts\nCreate a liability or reduce assets. Examples: borrowing creates a liability; recovery of government loans reduces a financial asset.\nThe distinction rests on the asset/liability effect, not the amount or frequency of the receipt.","tag":"Theory","id":"q197"},{"chapter":"11","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"An increase in foreign investment enters an economy with a flexible exchange rate. Explain the foreign-exchange market effect using ₹ per dollar as the quotation.","solution":"Supply of Foreign Exchange\nForeign investment brings an inflow of foreign currency, shifting dollar supply to the right.\nDetermination of Flexible Exchange Rate\nWith dollar demand unchanged, the equilibrium ₹/$ rate falls and the equilibrium quantity of dollars increases.\nAppreciation of Domestic Currency\nFewer rupees are required for one dollar; the rupee appreciates. Draw ₹/$ vertically, dollars horizontally, a downward D curve and S shifting right.","tag":"Case-based","id":"q198"},{"chapter":"12","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"A country finances a current-account deficit through overseas borrowing. Explain why BoP accounting balance does not prove a sound external position.","solution":"Balance of Payments: Accounting Sense and Operational Sense\n1. The loan produces a capital-account inflow that can offset the current-account deficit.\n2. Double-entry recording and financing entries make the accounts balance.\n3. The borrowing increases external liabilities and future interest/principal obligations.\n4. Accounting balance does not establish equality of autonomous receipts and payments or absence of external vulnerability.","tag":"Case-based","id":"q199"},{"id":"q200","chapter":"2","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain Types of consumption goods using the textbook headings and a suitable illustration.","solution":"2.1.1 (a) Consumption Goods or Consumer Goods\nDurable-use consumer goods serve consumers repeatedly over a long period, such as cars and refrigerators. Semi-durable goods serve over a shorter period, such as clothes. Non-durable goods are used up in a single act of consumption, such as food. Services are intangible acts satisfying wants, such as teaching or medical treatment. Durability alone does not make an item a capital good: capital goods are used by producers in production.","tag":"Topic review"},{"id":"q201","chapter":"2","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain Why final goods are counted and intermediate goods are not using the textbook headings and a suitable illustration.","solution":"ABILITY ZONE · Significance of the Distinction between Intermediate Goods and Final Goods\nIntermediate inputs are embodied in final output. Counting both adds the same value more than once. If cloth worth ₹5,000 becomes shirts worth ₹7,000, final output is ₹7,000, not ₹12,000. Under the value-added method, the cloth producer’s value and the shirt producer’s additional ₹2,000 are each counted once. It is incorrect to say intermediate goods contribute nothing: their contribution is included in final value.","tag":"Topic review"},{"id":"q202","chapter":"3","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain Special cases of residents and territory using the textbook headings and a suitable illustration.","solution":"ABILITY ZONE · Normal Residents of a Country · Components of Domestic Territory\nNormal Residents: citizens of another country can be residents here when their centre of economic interest is here; officials and diplomats serving abroad remain residents of their home country; border workers are residents where they live. Domestic Territory: include a country’s embassies, consulates and military establishments abroad, ships and aircraft operated by its residents between countries, and fishing vessels and floating platforms operated by residents in international waters under the accounting conventions. Exclude foreign embassies and international organisations located inside its political borders. Residence and territory are separate tests.","tag":"Topic review"},{"id":"q203","chapter":"4","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain Steps under the three measurement methods using the textbook headings and a suitable illustration.","solution":"4.2.1 Steps · 4.3.1 Steps · 4.4.1 Steps\nValue Added Method: identify and classify producing units; estimate each unit’s output and intermediate consumption; obtain sectoral value added; aggregate and make depreciation, tax and NFIA adjustments to national income. Income Method: identify producing enterprises; classify factor incomes into compensation, operating surplus and mixed income; sum them to obtain domestic income; add NFIA. Expenditure Method: identify final expenditure by sectors; classify consumption, investment and net exports; sum to GDPMP; convert to NNPFC. Keep the starting aggregate clear because the adjustments depend on whether it is gross/net and market price/factor cost.","tag":"Topic review"},{"id":"q204","chapter":"5","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain Why money solves barter’s difficulties using the textbook headings and a suitable illustration.","solution":"5.1.3 Barter System of Exchange · 5.3 Functions of Money\nLack of Double Coincidence of Wants: money is accepted as an intermediary, so you can sell to one person and buy from another. Lack of a Common Measure of Value: prices expressed in money make values comparable. Difficulty in Storing Wealth: money offers a convenient store, though inflation can erode it. Difficulty in Deferred Payments: future payments can be fixed in monetary units. Difficulty in Transfer of Value: purchasing power can be transferred without moving bulky goods. In theory answers, state the barter difficulty first and then the function that addresses it.","tag":"Topic review"},{"id":"q205","chapter":"6","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain Issuer of currency and banker to government using the textbook headings and a suitable illustration.","solution":"6.3 THE CENTRAL BANK · Functions\nIssuer of Currency: the central bank has the principal note-issuing authority; in India, the ₹1 note and coins are issued by the Government of India. Centralised issue promotes uniformity and control. Banker to Government: the RBI keeps government deposits, receives payments, makes disbursements, advances funds within the applicable arrangements and manages public debt. Financial adviser is a related role. Distinguish issuing authority from circulation: government-issued coins enter circulation through the monetary system.","tag":"Topic review"},{"id":"q206","chapter":"6","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain Bankers’ bank, lender of last resort and clearing house using the textbook headings and a suitable illustration.","solution":"6.3 THE CENTRAL BANK · Functions\nBankers’ Bank and Supervisory Role: commercial banks maintain required cash balances with the central bank, which regulates their functioning. Lender of Last Resort: the central bank offers financial accommodation in emergencies when other sources are unavailable. Clearing House: it settles interbank claims by adjusting banks’ accounts and facilitates fund transfers. Custodian of Foreign Exchange: it manages the country’s foreign-exchange reserves and can intervene in the exchange market. Control of Credit: it regulates the cost and availability of credit to support growth with stability.","tag":"Topic review"},{"id":"q207","chapter":"7","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain Ranges of APC, MPC, APS and MPS using the textbook headings and a suitable illustration.","solution":"Comparative Table of APC, MPC, APS and MPS · Critical Questions\nAPC can exceed one when C exceeds Y; it equals one at break-even and is below one when saving is positive. APS is negative when saving is negative, zero at break-even and positive when saving is positive; with positive consumption it remains below one. MPC and MPS range from zero to one in the textbook model and add to one. At the endpoints, MPC = 1 implies MPS = 0 and all additional income is consumed; MPC = 0 implies MPS = 1 and all additional income is saved. Do not infer a marginal ratio from a single total-income observation.","tag":"Topic review"},{"id":"q208","chapter":"8","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain Break-even income versus equilibrium income using the textbook headings and a suitable illustration.","solution":"Consumption Function · Equilibrium Output\nBreak-even Income: consumption equals income, so saving is zero; use C = Y. Equilibrium Income: planned output equals planned expenditure, so saving equals investment; use Y = C + I or S = I. With positive autonomous investment and positive MPS, equilibrium income exceeds break-even income. The two coincide only when autonomous investment is zero in the simple model. If you are asked for dissaving at zero income, solve for autonomous consumption and report saving as its negative.\nBreak-even Y = C̄/MPS\nEquilibrium Y = (C̄ + I)/MPS","tag":"Topic review"},{"id":"q209","chapter":"10","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain Budget objectives: the headings to reproduce using the textbook headings and a suitable illustration.","solution":"10.2 OBJECTIVES OF GOVERNMENT BUDGET\nGDP Growth: create infrastructure and induce private investment through suitable incentives. Allocation of Resources: influence production through taxes, subsidies and public provision. Provision of Public Goods: provide collective services that markets may undersupply. Redistribution of Income and Wealth: use progressive taxation and welfare spending. Balanced Regional Growth: direct infrastructure and incentives to less-developed regions. Employment Opportunities: expand activity and labour demand. Economic Stability: raise spending during deficient demand and restrain it during excess demand. Select and explain the number of objectives requested, using these headings.","tag":"Topic review"},{"id":"q210","chapter":"10","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain Free-rider problem using the textbook headings and a suitable illustration.","solution":"ABILITY ZONE · Free Rider Problem\nA free rider enjoys the benefits of goods and services without contributing the full or partial cost of providing them. Non-excludability makes it difficult to withhold benefits from non-payers. Private providers may be unable to collect enough revenue to cover costs, so the market can fail to provide a needed public good. National defence and street lighting illustrate why public provision can be necessary.","tag":"Topic review"},{"id":"q211","chapter":"10","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain Tax rates and tax burden are different using the textbook headings and a suitable illustration.","solution":"ABILITY ZONE · Progressive and Regressive Nature of Taxation is Different from Progressive and Regressive Rates of Taxation\nProgressive Rate: the tax rate rises with income. Regressive Rate: the tax rate falls with income. Proportionate Rate: the tax rate stays constant as income changes. Progressive Nature: the real burden is greater on the rich. Regressive Nature: the real burden is greater on the poor. The textbook stresses that a proportionate tax can still have a regressive real burden. When answering, specify whether you mean the numerical rate or the economic burden.","tag":"Topic review"},{"id":"q212","chapter":"10","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain Does a balanced budget leave AD unchanged? using the textbook headings and a suitable illustration.","solution":"ABILITY ZONE · Does Balanced Budget Leave Aggregate Demand Unaffected in the Economy?\nNo. Equal tax collection and government expenditure do not necessarily have equal first-round effects on AD. Government expenditure enters AD in full; a tax increase reduces disposable income, and consumption falls by MPC times the tax rise. If G and taxes each rise by ₹100 and MPC is 0.5, expenditure raises AD by ₹100 while the initial consumption loss is ₹50, giving a net initial rise of ₹50. Under the simple lump-sum-tax model, later rounds imply a balanced-budget multiplier of one. Distinguish the initial AD change from the final income change.\nInitial ΔAD = ΔG − MPC × ΔT\nIf ΔG = ΔT: initial ΔAD = (1 − MPC)ΔG","tag":"Topic review"},{"id":"q213","chapter":"11","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain Fixed versus flexible rates: named merits and demerits using the textbook headings and a suitable illustration.","solution":"11.4 MERITS AND DEMERITS OF FIXED AND FLEXIBLE EXCHANGE RATE\nFixed Rate—Merits: market stability and mobility of capital; growth of international trade; stable macroeconomic policies; devaluation as a policy tool; a check on inflation. Fixed Rate—Demerits: large reserve requirements; inefficient allocation of resources; limited forex market; discouraged venture capital; possible divergence from equilibrium. Flexible Rate—Merits: fewer reserves required for rate defence; market allocation of resources; larger forex market; international mobility of liquidity; support for venture capital. Flexible Rate—Demerits: weak-currency marginalisation; market uncertainty; difficulty maintaining stable monetary policy; difficulty with bilateral trade agreements; external shocks. These are the textbook’s headings: explain the relevant mechanism instead of only listing them.","tag":"Topic review"},{"id":"q214","chapter":"8","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"In a two-sector economy, MPS is 0.25, planned investment ₹150 crore and equilibrium income ₹1,000 crore. Find autonomous consumption and dissaving at zero income.","solution":"At equilibrium S = I.\nS = −C̄ + 0.25Y.\n150 = −C̄ + 0.25 × 1,000 ⇒ C̄ = ₹100 crore.\nAt Y = 0, saving = −₹100 crore; the magnitude of dissaving is ₹100 crore.","tag":"Numerical"},{"id":"q215","chapter":"4","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"Two firms A and B have equal GVA MP. B’s output is ₹1,200 crore and its purchases from A are ₹300 crore. A purchases ₹350 crore of inputs from B, exports ₹200 crore and adds ₹100 crore to stock. Assuming no other intermediate inputs, find A’s domestic sales.","solution":"GVA of B = 1,200 − 300 = ₹900 crore.\nGVA of A also equals ₹900 crore.\nOutput of A = GVA + Intermediate consumption = 900 + 350 = ₹1,250 crore.\nSales of A = Output − Change in stock = 1,250 − 100 = ₹1,150 crore.\nDomestic sales = Total sales − Exports = 1,150 − 200 = ₹950 crore.","tag":"Numerical"},{"id":"q216","chapter":"12","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"A business borrows abroad to buy equipment; the government separately obtains an overseas loan specifically to finance a BoP deficit. Classify each as autonomous or accommodating and explain why the account type alone does not decide this.","solution":"Autonomous Items\nThe business loan is autonomous because it is taken for an independent investment/profit motive.\nAccommodating Items\nThe government loan is accommodating because its stated purpose is financing the external imbalance.\nBoth may involve capital-account borrowing, but the purpose determines the autonomous/accommodating classification.","tag":"Case-based"},{"id":"ied-theory-1-0","chapter":"i1","type":"theory","marks":6,"q":"Explain before british rule and the colonial transformation. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Before British rule and the colonial transformation\n1. Before British rule: strong handicrafts, especially textiles, alongside a mainly agricultural economy.\n2. Colonial policy: turn India into a raw-material supplier and a market for British manufactured goods.\n3. Result: slow growth, low productivity, weak industry and widespread poverty.\nThe economy was organised around British needs, which explains the weaknesses in every sector.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-1-1","chapter":"i1","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain why agriculture stagnated. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Why agriculture stagnated\n1. Exploitative land settlement: high rent and insecure tenure left cultivators with little money or reason to improve land.\n2. Low investment: poor irrigation, old techniques and limited inputs kept yields low.\n3. Small, fragmented holdings: scattered plots made efficient cultivation difficult.\nMost people depended on farming, but dependence is not the same as productivity.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-1-2","chapter":"i1","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain commercialisation, subsistence and vulnerability. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Commercialisation, subsistence and vulnerability\n1. Subsistence farming: grow mainly for the family’s own use.\n2. Commercial farming: grow mainly for sale, including colonial cash crops such as cotton and jute.\n3. The problem: pressure to grow cash crops could reduce food cultivation without improving farmers’ incomes.\nCommercialisation describes where the crop goes; it does not guarantee that the farmer benefits.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-1-3","chapter":"i1","type":"theory","marks":6,"q":"Explain decline of handicrafts: causes and consequences. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Decline of handicrafts: causes and consequences\n1. British machine-made imports competed with Indian handmade goods.\n2. Discriminatory duties weakened Indian goods in foreign markets while favouring British goods in India.\n3. Loss of royal patronage reduced demand for specialised crafts.\n4. Displaced artisans moved towards agriculture because modern industry could not absorb them.\nDe-industrialisation = handicrafts decline without enough modern industry to replace them.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-1-4","chapter":"i1","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain a limited modern industrial base. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"A limited modern industrial base\n1. Cotton mills developed mainly in western India; jute mills were concentrated in Bengal.\n2. Iron and steel emerged later, including TISCO in 1907.\n3. Capital-goods industries remained weak, limiting the ability to make machinery.\n4. Modern industry grew too slowly to employ displaced artisans.\nA few successful industries existed, but the overall industrial base remained narrow.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-1-5","chapter":"i1","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain foreign trade and the drain of wealth. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Foreign trade and the drain of wealth\n1. Trade pattern: export raw materials; import manufactured goods.\n2. British control: trade served Britain’s factories and markets; the Suez Canal strengthened these links.\n3. Drain of wealth: export earnings financed British administration, wars and payments abroad.\nExport surplus means exports exceed imports. It helped India only if the resulting resources stayed and supported development.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-1-6","chapter":"i1","type":"theory","marks":6,"q":"Explain population conditions and the year 1921. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Population conditions and the year 1921\n1. Poor health: high death and infant-mortality rates, low life expectancy, disease and inadequate sanitation.\n2. Low literacy: education reached few people, especially women.\n3. 1921: the Year of the Great Divide—population growth became more consistently positive afterwards.\nHigh birth and death rates initially kept population growth modest; falling death rates later widened the gap.\nHistorical indicators in your textbook: birth rate ≈48 per 1,000; death rate ≈40 per 1,000; infant mortality ≈218 per 1,000 live births; life expectancy ≈32 years; literacy below 16%; female literacy ≈7%.\nInfant mortality concerns deaths below age one per 1,000 live births—not per 1,000 total people.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-1-7","chapter":"i1","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain occupational imbalance. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Occupational imbalance\n1. Occupational structure = how workers are distributed across sectors.\n2. About 70–75% worked in the primary sector; industry employed only about 10% in the textbook’s colonial summary.\n3. Weak non-farm opportunities pushed extra workers onto land, lowering average output per worker.\nEmployment share tells you where people work; GDP share tells you where output is produced.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-1-8","chapter":"i1","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain railways and other infrastructure: purpose and effects. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Railways and other infrastructure: purpose and effects\n1. Colonial purpose: move raw materials, sell British goods, transport troops and administer India.\n2. Infrastructure built: railways, ports, post and telegraph; rural roads and social facilities remained inadequate.\n3. Side effects: better transport, communication and links between regions.\nA balanced answer recognises the benefits while explaining that colonial interests determined the priorities.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-2-0","chapter":"i2","type":"theory","marks":6,"q":"Explain capitalist, socialist and mixed economies. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Capitalist, socialist and mixed economies\n1. Capitalist economy: private ownership; markets and profit guide production.\n2. Socialist economy: government ownership and planning guide resource use.\n3. Mixed economy: public and private sectors coexist; India used public leadership with regulated private enterprise.\nIndia wanted industrial growth while also pursuing social welfare and equity.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-2-1","chapter":"i2","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain economic planning and five year plans. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Economic planning and Five Year Plans\n1. Planning = deliberately allocating resources towards stated goals over a fixed period.\n2. India’s First Five Year Plan began in 1951.\n3. Plans coordinated investment in agriculture, industry and infrastructure when resources were scarce.\nCommon goals stayed broadly similar, but the priority given to each sector could change between plans.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-2-2","chapter":"i2","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain growth, modernisation, self-reliance and equity. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Growth, modernisation, self-reliance and equity\n1. Growth: increase productive capacity and output.\n2. Modernisation: adopt better technology and a progressive social outlook, including women’s participation.\n3. Self-reliance: reduce dependence on foreign resources for essential needs.\n4. Equity: ensure benefits reach all sections and basic needs are met.\nMore GDP does not automatically mean fairer distribution; growth and equity are separate goals.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-2-3","chapter":"i2","type":"theory","marks":6,"q":"Explain land reforms: changing agrarian relations. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Land reforms: changing agrarian relations\n1. Abolition of intermediaries: remove rent-collecting middlemen and connect cultivators directly with the state.\n2. Land ceiling: limit holdings and redistribute surplus land.\n3. Consolidation: combine scattered plots; cooperative farming: pool resources.\n4. Limitation: loopholes, litigation and weak implementation reduced the gains.\nInstitutional reforms change ownership and tenure; technical reforms change production methods.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-2-4","chapter":"i2","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain the green revolution package. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"The Green Revolution package\n1. Green Revolution = a sharp rise in agricultural output through HYV technology.\n2. The package: HYV seeds + irrigation + fertilisers + pesticides + credit.\n3. Early gains concentrated in wheat and well-irrigated regions; later gains spread to more crops and areas.\nSeeds alone cannot deliver the promised yield when irrigation and supporting inputs are missing.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-2-5","chapter":"i2","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain green revolution: gains. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Green Revolution: gains\n1. Higher yields raised foodgrain production and reduced dependence on imports.\n2. More marketable surplus supplied people outside agriculture.\n3. Better food supplies supported buffer stocks and price stability.\n4. Higher farm incomes increased demand for industrial goods.\nMarketable surplus = production left for sale after the farmer’s own requirements.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-2-6","chapter":"i2","type":"theory","marks":6,"q":"Explain green revolution: limitations. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Green Revolution: limitations\n1. Crop imbalance: early gains mainly benefited wheat and rice.\n2. Regional imbalance: well-irrigated regions advanced faster.\n3. Income inequality: larger farmers could afford the input package more easily.\n4. Environmental damage: excessive chemicals and groundwater use harmed soil and water.\nEvaluate both the increase in food output and how its gains and costs were distributed.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-2-7","chapter":"i2","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain the subsidy debate. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"The subsidy debate\n1. In favour: subsidies make inputs affordable, encourage new technology and support small farmers.\n2. Against: benefits may favour large farmers; government spending rises; cheap inputs can encourage waste.\n3. Better design: target farmers who need support and discourage excessive input use.\nThe issue is who receives the subsidy and what behaviour it encourages.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-2-8","chapter":"i2","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain why the public sector led industrialisation. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Why the public sector led industrialisation\n1. Basic and heavy industries needed large investments and long waiting periods before returns.\n2. Private firms often lacked the capital or incentive to undertake them.\n3. The public sector supplied infrastructure, strategic industries and investment in backward regions.\nPublic-sector leadership supported a mixed economy; it did not mean private firms disappeared.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-2-9","chapter":"i2","type":"theory","marks":6,"q":"Explain ipr 1956 and industrial licensing. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"IPR 1956 and industrial licensing\n1. IPR 1956: one group reserved for the state; a second with state leadership and private participation; the remainder open to regulated private enterprise.\n2. Industrial licensing controlled new factories and expansion.\n3. Purpose: direct scarce resources and promote regional balance; drawback: too many controls weakened competition.\nRemember the three groups by the degree of state involvement, rather than as an unexplained list.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-2-10","chapter":"i2","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain small-scale industries and the karve committee. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Small-scale industries and the Karve Committee\n1. Karve Committee, 1955: emphasised small industries’ role in rural development.\n2. Why protect them? They create jobs with relatively little capital and spread production across regions.\n3. Support: reserved products, concessions and credit.\n4. Weaknesses: limited finance, outdated technology and marketing difficulties.\nTheir main advantage was labour-intensive, decentralised growth.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-2-11","chapter":"i2","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain import substitution, tariffs and quotas. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Import substitution, tariffs and quotas\n1. Import substitution = make domestically what was previously imported.\n2. Tariff: tax imports, making them costlier. Quota: limit their quantity.\n3. Benefit: protect young domestic industries and conserve foreign exchange.\n4. Cost: prolonged protection weakens pressure to improve quality and efficiency.\nProtection can help an industry get started; permanent shelter can make it complacent.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-2-12","chapter":"i2","type":"theory","marks":6,"q":"Explain appraising the pre-reform strategy. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Appraising the pre-reform strategy\n1. Strategy: public-sector leadership, licensing, small-industry protection and import substitution.\n2. Achievements: a wider industrial base, infrastructure and greater food security.\n3. Limitations: inefficient enterprises, restrictive controls, weak job creation and persistent poverty.\nThe pre-reform strategy built capacity, but its limitations created pressure for change.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-3-0","chapter":"i3","type":"theory","marks":6,"q":"Explain why reforms became necessary in 1991. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Why reforms became necessary in 1991\n1. Fiscal pressure: spending exceeded revenue, increasing borrowing and debt.\n2. External crisis: foreign-exchange reserves fell to roughly two weeks of imports.\n3. Other problems: inflation and weak public-enterprise returns.\n4. Response: assistance from IMF/World Bank and a shift towards a more competitive economy.\n1991 combined an immediate payments crisis with deeper weaknesses in the old growth strategy.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-3-1","chapter":"i3","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain liberalisation: meaning and industrial reforms. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Liberalisation: meaning and industrial reforms\n1. Liberalisation = reduce direct government controls over producing units.\n2. Industrial changes: reduce licensing, open more activities to private firms, ease expansion and imports of capital goods.\n3. Mechanism: easier entry → more competition → pressure to improve efficiency.\nFewer controls does not mean no regulation; safety, competition and other safeguards still matter.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-3-2","chapter":"i3","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain financial-sector reforms. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Financial-sector reforms\n1. Increase competition by allowing more private and foreign participation in finance.\n2. Give banks more operational freedom within safeguards.\n3. Shift RBI’s emphasis from detailed control towards facilitation and regulation.\nRBI becoming a facilitator does not mean it stopped supervising banks.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-3-3","chapter":"i3","type":"theory","marks":6,"q":"Explain tax reforms, gst and demonetisation. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Tax reforms, GST and demonetisation\n1. Tax reforms: simplify taxes, rationalise rates, widen the tax base and improve compliance.\n2. GST, 1 July 2017: brought many indirect taxes into a common framework.\n3. Demonetisation, November 2016: withdrew legal-tender status of the then ₹500/₹1,000 notes, aiming to address unaccounted money and encourage formal/digital activity.\nKeep a policy’s objective separate from whether it fully achieved that objective.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-3-4","chapter":"i3","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain devaluation and trade reforms. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Devaluation and trade reforms\n1. 1991 devaluation: officially lower the rupee’s value to improve export competitiveness and tackle the external crisis.\n2. Trade reforms: reduce tariffs, quantitative restrictions and licensing barriers.\n3. Effect: cheaper exports in foreign-currency terms, costlier imports in rupees and greater competition for domestic firms.\nDevaluation is an official change; depreciation is a market-driven fall in currency value.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-3-5","chapter":"i3","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain privatisation and disinvestment. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Privatisation and disinvestment\n1. Privatisation: greater private ownership or management of public enterprises.\n2. Disinvestment: sale of government equity; a minority sale need not transfer control.\n3. Possible gains: efficiency, commercial discipline and revenue.\n4. Concerns: undervaluation, job losses and loss of future public income.\nAsk whether ownership or control actually changed before calling a sale full privatisation.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-3-6","chapter":"i3","type":"theory","marks":6,"q":"Explain autonomy and public-enterprise status. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Autonomy and public-enterprise status\n1. Selected public enterprises received greater financial and managerial freedom.\n2. Navratna/Maharatna arrangements aim to support quicker commercial decisions.\n3. The enterprise can remain government-owned while gaining autonomy.\nAutonomy changes decision-making freedom; privatisation changes the private sector’s role in ownership or management.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-3-7","chapter":"i3","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain globalisation and outsourcing. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Globalisation and outsourcing\n1. Globalisation = deeper integration through trade, investment, technology and services.\n2. Outsourcing = hiring an external provider for particular work.\n3. India’s advantages: skilled English-speaking workers, relatively low costs, IT links and time-zone differences.\nAn overseas company using an Indian accounting service is outsourcing; the wider cross-border integration is globalisation.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-3-8","chapter":"i3","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain wto: purpose and debate. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"WTO: purpose and debate\n1. WTO began in 1995, succeeding GATT.\n2. Role: multilateral trade rules, negotiations and dispute settlement.\n3. Debate: developing countries face unequal bargaining power and competition from subsidised producers in richer countries.\nExplain its intended rules-based role, then assess whether the gains are distributed fairly.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-3-9","chapter":"i3","type":"theory","marks":6,"q":"Explain reforms: favourable effects. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Reforms: favourable effects\n1. Competition and choice: firms face pressure to improve quality; consumers get more options.\n2. Investment and technology: foreign links can bring finance, knowledge and market access.\n3. Growth and external strength: the textbook highlights faster growth and stronger forex reserves over particular periods.\nUse a cause-and-effect chain, not just “reforms were good”: competition → efficiency → better products.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-3-10","chapter":"i3","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain reforms: criticisms and uneven gains. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Reforms: criticisms and uneven gains\n1. Agriculture: reduced support and foreign competition hurt some producers.\n2. Industry and jobs: import competition and capital-intensive growth limited gains for some firms/workers.\n3. Unequal development: benefits concentrated in certain sectors, cities and income groups.\n4. Other concerns: disinvestment, fiscal priorities, economic dependence and consumerism.\nReforms can raise total output while leaving particular people or sectors behind.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-4-0","chapter":"i4","type":"theory","marks":6,"q":"Explain human resource, human capital and formation. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Human resource, human capital and formation\n1. Human resource: people’s abilities to contribute to production.\n2. Human capital: the stock of their skills, knowledge and health.\n3. Human capital formation: adding to that stock through education, training and health investment.\nPopulation counts people; human capital asks what they are able to do.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-4-1","chapter":"i4","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain human capital versus human development. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Human capital versus human development\n1. Human capital view: education and health are means to higher productivity and income.\n2. Human development view: education, health, freedom and well-being are valuable ends in themselves.\n3. Implication: people deserve health and education even without an immediate measured productivity gain.\nSame services, different ultimate purpose: productive returns versus a better human life.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-4-2","chapter":"i4","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain physical capital versus human capital. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Physical capital versus human capital\n1. Physical capital: tangible, separable from its owner and saleable as an asset.\n2. Human capital: embodied in a person; only its services can be sold.\n3. Both depreciate, but learning and health investment can counter the loss of human capability.\n4. Human capital also creates social benefits, such as informed citizenship.\nA machine can be sold separately; a driver’s skill cannot be separated from the driver.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-4-3","chapter":"i4","type":"theory","marks":6,"q":"Explain education and health expenditure. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Education and health expenditure\n1. Education builds knowledge, skills and earning capacity.\n2. Health increases efficiency and reduces working time lost to illness.\n3. Health spending includes preventive care, treatment, awareness, hygiene and sanitation.\nThey complement each other: a sick worker cannot fully use even excellent skills.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-4-4","chapter":"i4","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain training, migration and information. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Training, migration and information\n1. On-the-job training: learn skills needed in actual work.\n2. Adult study: update knowledge and capabilities after initial schooling.\n3. Migration: bear travel/settlement costs to reach better opportunities.\n4. Information: spend to find suitable education and jobs.\nEach is an investment when its cost is expected to produce future benefits.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-4-5","chapter":"i4","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain how human capital contributes to growth. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"How human capital contributes to growth\n1. Change in emotional/material environment: education encourages new ideas and techniques.\n2. Higher productivity of physical capital: skilled workers use machines better.\n3. Higher participation and equality: more people can join and benefit from growth.\nThe relationship runs both ways: capabilities support growth, and growth finances better education and health.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-4-6","chapter":"i4","type":"theory","marks":6,"q":"Explain problems of human capital formation. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Problems of human capital formation\n1. Rising population stretches education and health resources.\n2. Brain drain reduces domestic access to skilled people.\n3. Deficient manpower planning creates a skills–jobs mismatch.\n4. Insufficient primary-sector training and low academic standards weaken practical capability.\nQualifications improve human capital only when they bring usable knowledge and skills.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-4-7","chapter":"i4","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain education: wider objectives. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Education: wider objectives\n1. Good citizenship and broader horizons: people make more informed choices.\n2. Science, technology and resource use: better knowledge improves production.\n3. Culture and personality: education develops people beyond their jobs.\n4. Participation: more people can contribute to development.\nEducation generates both private returns and benefits for society.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-4-8","chapter":"i4","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain why government intervenes in education and health. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Why government intervenes in education and health\n1. Public intervention widens access when private prices exclude poorer households.\n2. Government also finances costly facilities and sets quality standards.\n3. Kothari Commission: education spending of 6% of GDP.\n4. RTE Act, 2009: free and compulsory education for ages 6–14.\nSpending as a share of GDP and as a share of the government budget have different denominators.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-4-9","chapter":"i4","type":"theory","marks":6,"q":"Explain achievements, knowledge economy and remaining gaps. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Achievements, knowledge economy and remaining gaps\n1. Achievements: more schools, higher education, literacy and technical skills support a knowledge economy.\n2. Access gaps: illiteracy, gender bias, low rural access and expensive private provision.\n3. Quality gap: weak vocationalisation and insufficient public spending.\n4. Steep education pyramid: fewer people reach each higher level.\nAccess gets people into education; quality and relevant skills determine what they gain.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-5-0","chapter":"i5","type":"theory","marks":6,"q":"Explain rural development and the action plan. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Rural development and the action plan\n1. Rural development addresses economic, social and environmental problems together.\n2. Priorities: education/health, land and productive resources, infrastructure, credit and marketing.\n3. Diversification and poverty reduction create livelihoods beyond seasonal crop work.\nImproving rural life is broader than simply increasing farm output.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-5-1","chapter":"i5","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain why rural credit is needed. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Why rural credit is needed\n1. Short-term credit pays for seeds, fertilisers, wages and needs before harvest income arrives.\n2. Long-term credit pays for machinery, irrigation and land improvement.\n3. Institutional sources: cooperatives, banks, RRBs and land-development banks.\n4. Non-institutional sources: moneylenders, landlords and traders.\nThe timing gap between spending on a crop and earning from it explains the need for credit.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-5-2","chapter":"i5","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain nabard, self-help groups and micro-credit. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"NABARD, self-help groups and micro-credit\n1. NABARD, 1982: apex institution supporting and coordinating rural credit/development.\n2. SHGs pool members’ small savings and lend within the group, often linking to banks.\n3. Micro-credit can reach borrowers without conventional collateral and support women’s participation.\nSmall loans help inclusion; viable work, skills and market access determine whether incomes improve.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-5-3","chapter":"i5","type":"theory","marks":6,"q":"Explain weaknesses of rural credit. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Weaknesses of rural credit\n1. Unequal access: small farmers may still depend on informal lenders.\n2. Delays, paperwork and collateral make institutional credit hard to obtain.\n3. Crop failure and unproductive borrowing make repayment difficult.\n4. Overdues weaken lenders and restrict future credit.\nGood credit needs the right timing, terms and productive use—not just a larger loan.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-5-4","chapter":"i5","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain agricultural marketing and its defects. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Agricultural marketing and its defects\n1. Marketing includes gathering, processing, grading, packing, storage, transport and sale.\n2. Defects: faulty weights, poor information, weak transport/storage and large intermediary margins.\n3. Distress sale: urgent cash needs force farmers to sell cheaply after harvest.\nCredit and marketing are linked: debt pressure plus no storage weakens the farmer’s bargaining power.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-5-5","chapter":"i5","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain improving agricultural marketing. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Improving agricultural marketing\n1. Regulated markets improve fairness; roads, storage and information reduce losses.\n2. Cooperatives pool produce and strengthen bargaining.\n3. MSP, procurement, buffer stocks and PDS support the food-marketing system.\n4. Direct markets reduce intermediaries; contracts offer markets but can create bargaining risks.\nDifferent measures fix different problems: storage addresses timing; market information addresses bargaining.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-5-6","chapter":"i5","type":"theory","marks":6,"q":"Explain why rural diversification matters. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Why rural diversification matters\n1. Crop diversification changes the mix of crops; broader diversification adds allied/non-farm work.\n2. Why? Spread risk, earn outside the crop season and absorb surplus farm labour.\n3. Options: animal husbandry, fisheries, horticulture, processing and rural services.\nDiversification turns a single seasonal income source into several livelihood opportunities.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-5-7","chapter":"i5","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain animal husbandry, fisheries and operation flood. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Animal husbandry, fisheries and Operation Flood\n1. Animal husbandry supplies milk, eggs and meat, adding income alongside crops.\n2. Operation Flood: milk cooperatives linked producers and consumers, supporting the White Revolution.\n3. Fisheries create jobs but need finance, equipment, storage and protection from pollution.\n4. Veterinary care, feed and better breeds raise livestock productivity.\nAllied activities reduce dependence on crops, but still require reliable support services and markets.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-5-8","chapter":"i5","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain horticulture and information technology. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Horticulture and information technology\n1. Horticulture: fruits, vegetables, flowers and plantation crops can raise incomes and improve nutrition.\n2. Perishability makes cold chains, transport and processing important.\n3. IT provides weather, prices, advice and links to work/markets.\n4. Electricity, connectivity and skills determine who benefits from IT.\nProducing a valuable crop is only half the task; getting it to buyers without spoilage is the other half.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-5-9","chapter":"i5","type":"theory","marks":6,"q":"Explain organic farming: meaning and advantages. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Organic farming: meaning and advantages\n1. Organic farming uses organic inputs and ecological methods rather than synthetic chemicals.\n2. Benefits: healthier soil, less pollution and lower dependence on non-renewable chemical inputs.\n3. Textbook headings also emphasise healthier food, inexpensive technology and potential export premiums.\n4. India’s abundant labour can support labour-intensive organic methods.\nKeep the environmental benefits clear; premium prices and lower costs are possibilities, not guarantees.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-5-10","chapter":"i5","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain organic farming: constraints and the pranam box. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Organic farming: constraints and the PRANAM box\n1. Constraints: lower transition yields, high cost per unit and shorter shelf life.\n2. Farmers need training, suitable inputs, certification and marketing.\n3. PRANAM box: encourage alternative nutrients and balanced fertiliser use to protect soil health.\nOrganic farming becomes viable when production support is matched by a reliable market.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-6-0","chapter":"i6","type":"theory","marks":6,"q":"Explain workers and economic activity. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Workers and economic activity\n1. A worker participates in an economic activity producing goods or services.\n2. Self-employed and hired workers both count.\n3. Unpaid family helpers in a farm/business can count even without a separate wage.\n4. Own-family domestic services are generally outside measured employment.\nDo not confuse “no wage” with “no economic production”.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-6-1","chapter":"i6","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain self-employed, regular and casual workers. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Self-employed, regular and casual workers\n1. Self-employed: operate their own enterprise, such as a shop owner.\n2. Regular salaried: continuing employment with regular pay.\n3. Casual wage labourer: hired for short periods/tasks, such as a daily-wage construction worker.\nEmployment status tells you the work relationship; construction or agriculture tells you the sector.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-6-2","chapter":"i6","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain labour force, workforce and participation. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Labour force, workforce and participation\n1. Workforce: people actually employed.\n2. Labour force: employed people plus unemployed people willing and available for work.\n3. Labour supply: the hours/services people offer at a wage.\n4. Someone not seeking or available for work is outside the labour force, rather than automatically unemployed.\nWorker-population ratio uses population; unemployment rate uses labour force. Always check the denominator.\nLabour force = Workforce + Unemployed\nWorker-population ratio = Workers ÷ Population × 100\nUnemployment rate = Unemployed ÷ Labour force × 100","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-6-3","chapter":"i6","type":"theory","marks":6,"q":"Explain rural–urban and male–female participation. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Rural–urban and male–female participation\n1. Rural work participation reflects farming, family labour and the need to earn even in low-productivity work.\n2. Urban patterns differ because of education and the available jobs.\n3. Women face care responsibilities, social barriers and under-recording of economic activity.\nA lower measured participation rate does not mean women make no productive contribution.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-6-4","chapter":"i6","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain workforce across sectors. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Workforce across sectors\n1. Primary: agriculture and allied activities.\n2. Secondary: manufacturing and construction. Tertiary: services.\n3. Rural workers are more concentrated in farming; urban work is more concentrated outside it.\n4. A high farm employment share with a low farm GDP share signals low average output per worker.\nDevelopment needs productive non-farm jobs, not just movement out of agriculture.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-6-5","chapter":"i6","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain jobless growth and technology. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Jobless growth and technology\n1. Jobless growth: GDP rises faster than employment opportunities.\n2. Capital-intensive technology and productivity gains can raise output without many new jobs.\n3. Responses: labour-intensive activities, small enterprises, relevant skills and rural diversification.\nIt means inadequate job creation relative to growth—not necessarily zero new jobs in every year.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-6-6","chapter":"i6","type":"theory","marks":6,"q":"Explain casualisation of the workforce. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Casualisation of the workforce\n1. Casualisation: a rising share of casual wage work rather than secure regular jobs/self-employment.\n2. Surplus rural labour migrates; limited jobs and weak bargaining power encourage daily-wage hiring.\n3. Consequences: insecurity and fewer benefits.\nThe concept describes a process; check actual table years before claiming the casual share always rose.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-6-7","chapter":"i6","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain formal and informal employment. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Formal and informal employment\n1. Formal sector: government/public enterprises and private establishments with ten or more workers in the textbook definition.\n2. Informal sector: smaller private enterprises, farming and self-employment.\n3. Informalisation: more employment without formal protection and social-security benefits.\nCasualisation concerns job status; informalisation concerns organisation and protection. They can overlap.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-6-8","chapter":"i6","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain open, disguised and seasonal unemployment. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Open, disguised and seasonal unemployment\n1. Open: willing and able to work, but unable to get a job.\n2. Disguised: extra workers can leave without reducing output; their marginal productivity is zero.\n3. Seasonal: work disappears outside the working season.\n4. Educated/structural: qualifications or skills do not match available opportunities.\nIf two people leave a family farm and output stays the same, those two were disguisedly unemployed.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-6-9","chapter":"i6","type":"theory","marks":6,"q":"Explain why unemployment persists. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Why unemployment persists\n1. Slow job growth and rapid population growth widen the employment gap.\n2. Seasonal farming and inadequate irrigation leave people without year-round work.\n3. Joint-family farming can conceal surplus labour; declining cottage industries reduce alternatives.\n4. Low investment, skills mismatch and limited mobility restrict opportunities.\nExplain the mechanism after the heading: inadequate irrigation → fewer crop seasons → seasonal unemployment.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-theory-6-10","chapter":"i6","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Explain government measures and mgnrega. Use the relevant textbook headings and support your points with an example or distinction.","solution":"Government measures and MGNREGA\n1. Direct employment: government hires workers itself. Indirect: public activity raises demand and jobs in other firms.\n2. MGNREGA: at least 100 days of unskilled manual wage work per financial year for a rural household whose adults volunteer.\n3. Work is to be provided within 15 days of demand; eligible failure attracts unemployment allowance.\n4. Skills, rural livelihoods, roads and small-business support create other opportunities.\nThe MGNREGA entitlement is per household, not 100 days separately for every adult.","tag":"Textbook points · topic review"},{"id":"ied-mcq-1","chapter":"i1","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"The principal purpose of colonial economic policy in India was to:","options":["Achieve Indian industrial self-reliance","Serve Britain’s economic interests","Develop Indian consumer welfare","Eliminate agricultural exports"],"answer":1,"explanation":"India supplied raw materials and became a market for British manufactured goods.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-2","chapter":"i1","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"An export surplus under British rule did not necessarily benefit India because:","options":["India exported only services","All export earnings went to cultivators","The surplus financed colonial payments abroad","India had no imports"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The surplus financed administrative, war and other British expenses: the drain of wealth.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-3","chapter":"i1","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Statement I: Agriculture dominated colonial employment. Statement II: This necessarily implied high agricultural productivity.","options":["I is false; II is true","I is true; II is false","Both are false","Both are true"],"answer":1,"explanation":"High dependence on agriculture coexisted with low productivity and stagnation.","tag":"Two statements"},{"id":"ied-mcq-4","chapter":"i1","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Which is a cause of agricultural stagnation under British rule?","options":["Abundant institutional credit","Exploitative land settlement","Heavy investment in irrigation","Universal ownership rights for tenants"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Insecure tenure and high rent discouraged investment, alongside low technology and inadequate irrigation.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-5","chapter":"i1","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"De-industrialisation means:","options":["A fall in agricultural employment only","Decline of handicrafts without adequate modern industrial replacement","Growth of every manufacturing industry","A ban on all imports"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Artisans lost livelihoods while modern industry grew too slowly to absorb them.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-6","chapter":"i1","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"1921 is called the Year of the Great Divide in the context of:","options":["Industrial licensing","GST","Population growth","Public-sector disinvestment"],"answer":2,"explanation":"It marks the transition from irregular/stagnant population growth towards sustained growth.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-7","chapter":"i1","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Commercialisation of agriculture primarily concerns:","options":["Abolishing land revenue","Producing for market sale","Replacing every crop with machinery","Producing only for family consumption"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Its defining feature is production for sale; improved farmer welfare does not automatically follow.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-8","chapter":"i1","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Assertion: Railways had some beneficial effects on India. Reason: Their primary colonial objective was Indian social welfare.","options":["Assertion true; reason false","Both true; reason explains assertion","Both true; reason does not explain assertion","Assertion false; reason true"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Transport and communication benefits existed, but colonial strategic and commercial interests were the primary motive.","tag":"Assertion–reason"},{"id":"ied-mcq-9","chapter":"i1","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Occupational structure measures:","options":["Distribution of workers across activities","Only exports by commodity","Distribution of tax rates","Distribution of government debt"],"answer":0,"explanation":"It concerns where people work, rather than sectors’ GDP shares.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-10","chapter":"i1","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"The decline of royal courts mainly weakened:","options":["The government’s GST collections","The supply of British machine-made imports","Modern service exports","Demand for traditional handicrafts"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Royal patronage had supported specialised handicrafts.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-11","chapter":"i2","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Which is NOT one of the four common goals of planning?","options":["Growth","Modernisation","Permanent dependence on imports","Equity"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The fourth goal is self-reliance.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-12","chapter":"i2","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Replacing imports with domestically produced goods is:","options":["Import substitution","Disinvestment","Outsourcing","Informalisation"],"answer":0,"explanation":"An inward-looking strategy supported domestic industry through protection.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-13","chapter":"i2","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"A quota differs from a tariff because a quota:","options":["Always makes imports free","Is only a subsidy","Is a tax on income","Sets a quantitative limit on imports"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A tariff is a tax on imports; a quota restricts their quantity.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-14","chapter":"i2","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Abolition of intermediaries is an example of:","options":["Foreign-exchange reform","A monetary-policy instrument","Institutional land reform","Technical reform"],"answer":2,"explanation":"It changes agrarian ownership and tenure relations.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-15","chapter":"i2","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"HYV seeds produced strong results when combined with:","options":["Irrigation, fertilisers and supporting inputs","Only rain and no capital","A ban on farm credit","Removal of all storage"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The Green Revolution was an input package, not seeds alone.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-16","chapter":"i2","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Marketable surplus is:","options":["Only imported food","Government tax revenue","Output remaining for sale after the farmer’s own requirements","Total farm output"],"answer":2,"explanation":"It is the portion available for the market.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-17","chapter":"i2","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"The Karve Committee (1955) is associated with:","options":["Demonetisation","WTO establishment","Currency devaluation","Small-scale industries"],"answer":3,"explanation":"It recognised small industries’ contribution to rural development and employment.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-18","chapter":"i2","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Equity as a planning goal emphasises:","options":["Only imported machinery","Eliminating all private firms","Only a rise in total output","Benefits reaching all sections and meeting basic needs"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Growth and equity are distinct: higher GDP alone does not ensure fair distribution.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-19","chapter":"i2","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Assertion: Initial Green Revolution gains were regionally uneven. Reason: Access to irrigation and complementary inputs varied across regions.","options":["Both true; reason does not explain assertion","Assertion false; reason true","Both true; reason explains assertion","Assertion true; reason false"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Regions with irrigation and resources could adopt the package more effectively.","tag":"Assertion–reason"},{"id":"ied-mcq-20","chapter":"i2","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Which is an argument AGAINST indiscriminate input subsidies?","options":["They eliminate all inequality","They can encourage excessive water and chemical use","They always raise government revenue","They make every crop organic"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Fiscal burdens and ecological damage are central criticisms, alongside unequal benefits.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-21","chapter":"i2","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"A mixed economy has:","options":["Both public and private sectors","No planning or markets","Only private ownership","Only government ownership"],"answer":0,"explanation":"India combined public-sector leadership with regulated private enterprise.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-22","chapter":"i2","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Modernisation includes:","options":["Only land ceilings","Complete withdrawal from foreign trade","Only machinery, with no change in social outlook","Technology and progressive changes in social outlook"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The textbook includes social attitudes, such as women’s participation, alongside technology.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-23","chapter":"i3","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"The severe external crisis of 1991 was marked by:","options":["No import requirements","Very low reserves and a balance-of-payments problem","Foreign-exchange reserves sufficient for decades","The introduction of MGNREGA"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Reserves were barely enough for about two weeks of imports.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-24","chapter":"i3","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"LPG stands for:","options":["Liberalisation, privatisation, globalisation","Licensing, planning, grants","Loans, production, government","Labour, prices, growth"],"answer":0,"explanation":"These are the three main elements of the New Economic Policy.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-25","chapter":"i3","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Selling a minority government shareholding without transferring control is:","options":["Import substitution","Necessarily full privatisation","Nationalisation","Disinvestment"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Disinvestment can occur without a transfer of management control.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-26","chapter":"i3","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Statement I: Liberalisation reduces certain direct controls. Statement II: It eliminates all government regulation.","options":["Both false","I false; II true","I true; II false","Both true"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Prudential, safety and competition regulation can remain.","tag":"Two statements"},{"id":"ied-mcq-27","chapter":"i3","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"An official reduction in currency value in a fixed or managed arrangement is:","options":["Appreciation","Informalisation","Revaluation","Devaluation"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Distinguish devaluation from market-driven depreciation.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-28","chapter":"i3","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"An Indian firm providing accounting services to a foreign company illustrates:","options":["Import substitution","Buffer stocking","Land ceiling","Outsourcing"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The client contracts an activity to an external provider.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-29","chapter":"i3","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"WTO was established in:","options":["1951","1947","1991","1995"],"answer":3,"explanation":"It succeeded GATT and provides a framework for multilateral trade rules.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-30","chapter":"i3","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Which is a criticism of reform outcomes?","options":["The absence of foreign competition","Guaranteed equal benefits for all farmers","The abolition of every private enterprise","Uneven gains across sectors and regions"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Agriculture, employment and regional imbalance are important appraisal points.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-31","chapter":"i3","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"GST was introduced in India on:","options":["8 November 2016","1 January 1991","1 July 2017","26 January 1950"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Demonetisation occurred in November 2016; GST followed in July 2017.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-32","chapter":"i3","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Consumer sovereignty increased with reforms mainly through:","options":["A universal ban on imports","Greater choice and competition","Elimination of product variety","Government ownership of every shop"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Competition and entry expanded consumers’ range of goods and services.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-33","chapter":"i3","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Public-enterprise autonomy necessarily means the enterprise has been sold to private owners. This statement is:","options":["False; autonomy can expand while public ownership remains","True only for schools","True in every case","The definition of a tariff"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Managerial autonomy and ownership transfer are distinct.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-34","chapter":"i4","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Human capital is best described as:","options":["Knowledge, skills and health embodied in people","Only the population count","The number of buildings","Government financial debt"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Capabilities, rather than headcount alone, determine human capital.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-35","chapter":"i4","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Human development treats education and health as:","options":["Physical machinery","Ends valuable for people’s well-being","Only means to raise productivity","A form of indirect tax"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Human capital stresses productive returns; human development values well-being itself.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-36","chapter":"i4","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Which is NOT a source of human capital formation?","options":["Information about jobs","On-the-job training","Health expenditure","Buying an existing machine without training"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The machine is physical capital; the other options improve people’s capabilities.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-37","chapter":"i4","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Vaccination is an example of:","options":["Disinvestment","Seasonal unemployment","Only curative treatment","Preventive medicine"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Preventive care reduces the likelihood of illness.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-38","chapter":"i4","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Assertion: Migration can be an investment in human capital. Reason: Its costs may be undertaken to obtain higher future earnings.","options":["Both true; reason does not explain assertion","Both true; reason explains assertion","Assertion true; reason false","Assertion false; reason true"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Travel and settlement costs are weighed against expected returns.","tag":"Assertion–reason"},{"id":"ied-mcq-39","chapter":"i4","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Which distinction is correct?","options":["Human capital is separable from its owner","Physical capital is intangible","Human capital cannot ever lose value","Human capital is inseparable from its owner"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A person supplies the services of human capital; the capability is embodied in that person.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-40","chapter":"i4","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"The Kothari Commission recommendation discussed in the textbook is education expenditure of:","options":["60% of all wages","6% of GDP","6% of population","0.6% of exports"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Be precise about the denominator: GDP, not total government spending.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-41","chapter":"i4","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"The RTE Act, 2009 concerns free and compulsory education for children aged:","options":["15–21","0–3","6–14","Only above 18"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The age group is 6–14 years.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-42","chapter":"i4","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Years of schooling alone may inadequately measure human capital because:","options":["Education has no benefits","Health is always irrelevant","Quality and acquired skills also matter","Every school is identical"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Quantity of schooling cannot fully capture effective capability.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-43","chapter":"i4","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Brain drain means:","options":["Migration of skilled people abroad","A rise in groundwater","A tariff on books","A fall in machinery imports"],"answer":0,"explanation":"It can reduce the domestic availability of skilled human resources.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-44","chapter":"i5","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Rural development is:","options":["Only the construction of urban roads","The abolition of all farming","Only an increase in wheat output","A comprehensive improvement in rural economic and social conditions"],"answer":3,"explanation":"It includes credit, marketing, infrastructure, human resources and diversification.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-45","chapter":"i5","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"NABARD was established in:","options":["1982","2017","1995","1955"],"answer":0,"explanation":"It is the apex institution discussed for rural credit and development.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-46","chapter":"i5","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Which is a non-institutional source of rural credit?","options":["Commercial bank","Regional rural bank","Moneylender","Cooperative credit society"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Moneylenders, traders and landlords are non-institutional sources.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-47","chapter":"i5","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"SHGs generally begin by:","options":["Selling central-bank reserves","Fixing import quotas","Guaranteeing unlimited corporate loans","Pooling small savings of members"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Pooled savings support small loans and can facilitate bank linkage.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-48","chapter":"i5","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"A distress sale occurs when a farmer:","options":["Receives a guaranteed premium","Sells urgently at an unfavourable price due to immediate needs","Waits indefinitely for the best price","Always exports organic crops"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Debt and lack of storage may force sale immediately after harvest.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-49","chapter":"i5","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Agricultural marketing includes:","options":["Only obtaining a bank loan","Only retail sale","Only growing seeds","Processing, grading, storage, transport and sale"],"answer":3,"explanation":"It covers the chain between production and consumption.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-50","chapter":"i5","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Operation Flood is associated with:","options":["Currency devaluation","Wheat imports","Milk cooperatives and the White Revolution","Coal nationalisation"],"answer":2,"explanation":"It linked milk producers and consumers through cooperative organisation.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-51","chapter":"i5","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Diversification can reduce seasonal unemployment by:","options":["Creating allied and non-farm work","Banning rural services","Eliminating storage","Making every farmer grow only one seasonal crop"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Alternative activities provide work outside the crop season.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-52","chapter":"i5","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Which is a constraint of organic farming?","options":["Guaranteed premium prices for all farmers","Certification and marketing difficulties","No need for any knowledge","Automatic high yields in every transition"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Training, yields, shelf life and market access also matter.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-53","chapter":"i5","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Rythu Bazaars illustrate:","options":["Direct agricultural marketing","Foreign-exchange dealing","Disinvestment","Industrial licensing"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Direct links can reduce intermediaries and improve farmers’ returns.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-54","chapter":"i5","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Horticulture needs cold chains mainly because:","options":["Its output is always metal","It never needs transport","It is unrelated to agriculture","Its output is often perishable"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Storage and transport reduce spoilage and improve market access.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-55","chapter":"i6","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"A family member helping produce crops without a separate wage is:","options":["Always outside the workforce","An unpaid family worker engaged in economic activity","Necessarily openly unemployed","Only a consumer"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A separate wage is not required for participation in economic production.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-56","chapter":"i6","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Labour force equals:","options":["Only people receiving salaries","Workforce plus unemployed people seeking/available for work","Total population in every case","Only casual workers"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Workforce counts employed people; labour force also includes the unemployed.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-57","chapter":"i6","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"A household has six farm workers; two leave and output is unchanged. The two were:","options":["Necessarily formally employed","Seasonally unemployed by definition","Disguisedly unemployed","Not members of the labour force"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Their marginal productivity was zero.","tag":"Case-based"},{"id":"ied-mcq-58","chapter":"i6","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Casualisation concerns:","options":["A rise only in formal-sector social security","Only the growth of exports","An increase in university buildings","Movement towards casual wage work"],"answer":3,"explanation":"It is about employment status; informalisation concerns organisation and protection.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-59","chapter":"i6","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"The textbook’s formal-sector definition includes:","options":["Every household service","Government departments and private establishments with ten or more workers","Only own-account farmers","Only private firms with fewer than ten workers"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Public enterprises are also included in the formal sector.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-60","chapter":"i6","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Jobless growth means:","options":["Every person has a government job","Zero output growth in every year","All workers become farmers","Output growth without adequate corresponding employment growth"],"answer":3,"explanation":"GDP may grow much faster than employment.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-61","chapter":"i6","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"The MGNREGA employment guarantee is primarily:","options":["100 days for every adult separately","A permanent salaried urban job","A subsidy only for machinery","100 days per rural household in a financial year"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Adult household members volunteer for unskilled manual work; the entitlement is household-based.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-62","chapter":"i6","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Worker-population ratio uses which denominator?","options":["Number of factories","Labour force","Only unemployed people","Population"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Workers/population ×100. The unemployment rate instead uses labour force.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-63","chapter":"i6","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"A daily-wage construction worker is classified by employment status as:","options":["Outside all economic activity","Casual wage labourer","Necessarily a regular salaried employee","Always self-employed"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Construction is the sector; casual wage labour is the employment status.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-mcq-64","chapter":"i6","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"Statement I: Informal workers generally lack many formal social-security benefits. Statement II: Informalisation and casualisation mean exactly the same thing.","options":["I true; II false","I false; II true","Both false","Both true"],"answer":0,"explanation":"One concerns protection/organisation; the other concerns employment status.","tag":"Two statements"},{"id":"ied-mcq-65","chapter":"i6","type":"mcq","marks":1,"q":"If agriculture employs a much larger share of workers than its share of GDP, it suggests:","options":["The GDP and employment shares must be equal","Agriculture produces all GDP","Every worker has zero output","Relatively low average output per agricultural worker"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The comparison indicates a productivity gap, not zero productivity for every worker.","tag":"Concept check"},{"id":"ied-written-1","chapter":"i6","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"A village has 2,000 people, 900 employed people and 100 unemployed people seeking work. Calculate its labour force, worker-population ratio and unemployment rate.","solution":"Labour force = 900 + 100 = 1,000.\nWorker-population ratio = 900/2,000 ×100 = 45%.\nUnemployment rate = 100/1,000 ×100 = 10%.\nThe two rates use different denominators.","tag":"Application"},{"id":"ied-written-2","chapter":"i6","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"A region’s population is 5 lakh and its worker-population ratio is 40%. Its labour force is 2.5 lakh. Calculate workers, unemployed people and the unemployment rate.","solution":"Workers = 5 ×0.40 = 2 lakh.\nUnemployed = 2.5 −2 = 0.5 lakh.\nUnemployment rate = 0.5/2.5 ×100 = 20%.","tag":"Application"},{"id":"ied-written-3","chapter":"i2","type":"numerical","marks":3,"q":"A farm produces 120 quintals of wheat, retaining 35 quintals for family consumption and seed requirements. Find the marketable surplus.","solution":"Marketable surplus = Total output − Retained requirements.\n= 120 −35 = 85 quintals.\nMarketable surplus is not total farm production.","tag":"Application"},{"id":"ied-written-4","chapter":"i4","type":"numerical","marks":3,"q":"Education spending is ₹60 crore, GDP is ₹1,000 crore and total government expenditure is ₹300 crore. Express education spending as a percentage of each denominator.","solution":"Share of GDP = 60/1,000 ×100 = 6%.\nShare of government spending = 60/300 ×100 = 20%.\nBoth describe the same spending, but they answer different questions.","tag":"Application"},{"id":"ied-written-5","chapter":"i6","type":"numerical","marks":4,"q":"An economy produces output worth 1,000 units, of which agriculture contributes 200. Agriculture employs 50 of the economy’s 100 workers. Compare average output per worker in agriculture and the rest of the economy.","solution":"Agriculture: 200/50 = 4 output units per worker.\nOther sectors: (1,000−200)/(100−50) = 800/50 = 16 units per worker.\nAverage output per worker outside agriculture is four times as high. Employment shares and output shares are not interchangeable.","tag":"Application"},{"id":"ied-written-6","chapter":"i1","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"“Railways prove that colonial economic policy primarily aimed to develop India.” Evaluate this statement.","solution":"Colonial infrastructure: purpose and effects\nThe statement confuses beneficial effects with the primary objective.\nRailways moved raw materials, expanded markets for British manufactures and served military/administrative needs.\nThey also improved transport, movement of people and commercial links.\nThese benefits should be acknowledged, but colonial interests determined priorities and design.","tag":"Evaluate a statement"},{"id":"ied-written-7","chapter":"i2","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"A small farmer cannot adopt HYV seeds because irrigation and affordable credit are missing. Explain why distributing seeds alone may fail, and identify two suitable measures.","solution":"Green Revolution: complementary inputs\nHYV technology requires irrigation, fertilisers, plant protection and supporting finance. Seeds alone cannot secure the expected yield.\nExpand reliable irrigation and provide timely institutional credit on suitable terms.\nTraining and access to inputs/marketing can support adoption.\nTargeting small farmers can reduce unequal gains.","tag":"Case-based"},{"id":"ied-written-8","chapter":"i3","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"The government sells 20% of its equity in an enterprise but retains control. Another public enterprise receives greater managerial autonomy. Are both necessarily privatisation? Explain.","solution":"Disinvestment, privatisation and autonomy\nThe equity sale is disinvestment. A minority sale with government control retained is not necessarily full privatisation.\nManagerial autonomy increases freedom to make decisions; it does not itself transfer ownership.\nPrivatisation involves a greater private role through ownership or management transfer.\nIdentify the actual change in control rather than relying only on the word reform.","tag":"Case-based"},{"id":"ied-written-9","chapter":"i4","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"A health programme is justified solely by its effect on worker productivity. Explain the human-capital view and add the human-development perspective.","solution":"Human capital versus human development\nThe human-capital view treats health as an investment improving efficiency, attendance and productive earnings.\nThe human-development view regards health and a better quality of life as ends in themselves.\nIt supports health access even when immediate measured productivity gains are limited.\nThe perspectives overlap in valuing health, but differ in the ultimate purpose.","tag":"Case-based"},{"id":"ied-written-10","chapter":"i5","type":"theory","marks":6,"q":"A farmer sells immediately after harvest at a low price to repay a moneylender. Explain the linked credit and marketing problems, and suggest three measures.","solution":"Rural credit and agricultural marketing\nImmediate repayment needs and inadequate storage create a distress sale, weakening bargaining power.\nTimely affordable institutional credit can reduce dependence on the moneylender.\nStorage and warehouse facilities allow the farmer to delay sale.\nCooperative/direct marketing, better price information and fair regulated transactions improve returns.\nExplain how each measure addresses a specific difficulty.","tag":"Case-based"},{"id":"ied-written-11","chapter":"i6","type":"theory","marks":4,"q":"Output rises rapidly while secure jobs grow slowly and more workers take daily-wage work. Identify and distinguish the two processes.","solution":"Jobless growth and casualisation\nJobless growth: output increases without adequate corresponding employment growth. Capital-intensive techniques can contribute.\nCasualisation: movement towards casual wage work rather than regular salaried work or self-employment.\nThe first compares output and job growth; the second concerns employment status and security.\nDo not assume they are identical or that every casual worker is openly unemployed.","tag":"Case-based"}]};
